#: content/install/windows.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Install on Windows"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/install/windows.md content/install/linux.md content/install/macos.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Prerequisites"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Install your OpenCL GPU drivers if you have a GPU.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "On Windows 11, it seems that the system OpenCL drivers for Intel embedded GPU cause issues (black images), especially with newer generation CPU. You may want to [remove Windows drivers](https://community.intel.com/t5/OpenCL-for-CPU/uninstall-Intel-OpenCL/m-p/1134032#M5756) and [install Intel ones](https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/developer/articles/tool/opencl-drivers.html#proc-graph-section).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "See the [caveats section below](#caveats) for more details.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "EXE package (recommended)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The Ansel project provides an official `.exe` package, built for the stable, pre-release and experimental channels. This is the recommended way of installing Ansel, since it is fresh from the repository, always up-to-date and ships updated lens databases for Lensfun.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/install/windows.md content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Downloads"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- [Get the latest EXE package](https://nightly.link/aurelienpierreeng/ansel/workflows/win-nightly/master/ansel.stable.win64.zip)\n- [Find earlier EXE packages](https://github.com/aurelienpierreeng/ansel/releases/tag/v0.0.0).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/install/windows.md content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Building from source code manually"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Step 1:  Install MSYS2 (instructions and prerequisites can be found on the official website: https://www.msys2.org)\n- Step 2:  Using the MSYS terminal - Update the base system until no further updates are available by repeating:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (bash)
#: content/install/windows.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "  $ pacman -Syu\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Step 3:  Using the UCRT64 terminal - Clone the Ansel git repository (in this example into `~/ansel`):\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (bash)
#: content/install/windows.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "  $ cd ~\n  $ git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/aurelienpierreeng/ansel.git\n  $ cd ansel\n  $ git submodule init\n  $ git submodule update\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Step 4:  Using the UCRT64 terminal - Install all build dependencies using the script from the repository:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (bash)
#: content/install/windows.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "  $ ./packaging/install-deps-windows-msys2.sh\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Step 5:  Using the UCRT64 terminal - Update your lensfun database:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (bash)
#: content/install/windows.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "  $ lensfun-update-data\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\nMSYS will initialize a personal Unix-like `/home` folder, by default located in `C:\\\\msys64\\home\\USERNAME` where `USERNAME` is your current Windows username. If your username contains non-latin characters, like accentuated letters, the clashes between Windows encoding and Linux encoding will make the compilation fail on directory pathes issues. In that case, create a new directory in `C:\\\\msys64\\home\\USERNAME_WITHOUT_ACCENTS`, and in the MSYS terminal, do `cd /home/USERNAME_WITHOUT_ACCENT`.\n{{</ note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Step 6:  Using a text editor, eg. MS Notepad - Modify the `.bash_profile` file in your `$HOME` directory and add the following lines:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (bash)
#: content/install/windows.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "  # Added as per http://wiki.gimp.org/wiki/Hacking:Building/Windows\n  export PREFIX=\"/ucrt64\"\n  export LD_LIBRARY_PATH=\"$PREFIX/lib:$LD_LIBRARY_PATH\"\n  export PATH=\"$PREFIX/bin:$PATH\"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Step 7:  By default CMake will only use one core during the build process. To speed things up, using a text editor, eg. MS Notepad, you might wish to add a line like:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (bash)
#: content/install/windows.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "  export CMAKE_BUILD_PARALLEL_LEVEL=\"8\"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "  to your `~/.bash_profile` file. This would use 8 cores.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Step 8:  Using the UCRT64 terminal - Execute the following command to activate profile changes:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (bash)
#: content/install/windows.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "  $ . .bash_profile\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Step 9:  Using the UCRT64 terminal - Build and install Ansel:\n  - Variant 1: __with all contextual optimizations enabled for your hardware__:\n    ```bash\n    $ mkdir build\n    $ cd build\n    $ cmake -G Ninja -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DBINARY_PACKAGE_BUILD=OFF -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=/opt/ansel ../.\n    $ cmake --build .\n    $ cmake --install .\n    ```\n    After this, Ansel will be installed in `/opt/ansel` directory and can be started by typing `/opt/ansel/bin/ansel.exe` in MSYS2 UCRT64 terminal.\n  - Variant 2: __with only generic optimizations and producing an installable EXE package__:\n    ```bash\n    $ mkdir build\n    $ cd build\n    $ cmake -G Ninja -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DBINARY_PACKAGE_BUILD=ON -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=/opt/ansel ../.\n    $ cmake --build . --target package\n    $ cmake --install .\n    ```\n    After this, you will need to double-click and install the `ansel.exe` found in the `build/bin` folder. This package will be portable and be installable on other platforms.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Building with AddressSanitizer (ASan)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "[AddressSanitizer](https://github.com/google/sanitizers/wiki/AddressSanitizer) is a compiler instrumentation that catches memory bugs (use-after-free, heap buffer overflows, double-free, etc.) at the exact moment they happen, with the offending line of code and both the allocation and the faulty-access call stacks. This is invaluable for chasing a crash that otherwise only shows up much later, or not at all, in a normal build — typically, a heap corruption that Windows only notices (and reports as an opaque address) the next time it happens to touch the damaged memory, long after the actual bug.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This is a debug build, meant for hunting a specific bug, not for daily use: keep it in its own prefix, separate from your normal install.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. Install a suitable Clang toolchain"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "MSYS2's own UCRT64 Clang does not ship an ASan runtime for the `x86_64-w64-mingw32` target (only `builtins`/`fuzzer`/`profile` are provided, no `asan_*`). [llvm-mingw](https://github.com/mstorsjo/llvm-mingw) is a separate, prebuilt Clang distribution that does include it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Download the latest `llvm-mingw-<date>-ucrt-x86_64.zip` from the [llvm-mingw releases page](https://github.com/mstorsjo/llvm-mingw/releases).\n- Extract it under `/opt`, e.g. so that `/opt/llvm-mingw-<date>-ucrt-x86_64/bin/clang++.exe` exists.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\nThis is a separate toolchain from the one used for a normal build: it is only ever referenced explicitly, below, through `CC`/`CXX`, so it cannot affect a normal build.\n{{</ note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "2. Create the compiler wrappers"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "llvm-mingw's Clang bundles its own libc++, which is what its ASan runtime is built to work with — but it does not know about MSYS2's own layout, and mixing it with MSYS2's headers/libraries (unavoidable, since Ansel links against MSYS2-built GTK, glib, etc.) causes two separate problems: a `<math.h>`/`isnan`/`signbit` macro clash against libc++'s own headers, and llvm-mingw's Clang defaulting to a C++ standard library (libc++) that is not the one the rest of the build actually links against (MSYS2's own libstdc++). Two small wrapper scripts route it to MSYS2's libstdc++ instead, and add the search paths llvm-mingw has no reason to know about on its own.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Adjust the GCC version in the paths below (`15.2.0`) to whatever `ls /ucrt64/include/c++/` reports on your system.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Create `/opt/llvm-mingw-<date>-ucrt-x86_64/bin/clang-mingwlib.cmd`:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (bat)
#: content/install/windows.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "@echo off\nREM C compiler wrapper: makes bare -lname libraries that live under MSYS2 ucrt64's lib\nREM dir (e.g. -lexchndl, DrMingw's crash handler) resolvable, since llvm-mingw's own\nREM clang.exe has no reason to know that directory exists otherwise.\n\"%~dp0clang.exe\" -L C:/msys64/ucrt64/lib -Wno-unused-command-line-argument %*\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Create `/opt/llvm-mingw-<date>-ucrt-x86_64/bin/clang++-libstdcxx.cmd`:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (bat)
#: content/install/windows.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "@echo off\nREM C++ compiler wrapper: use MSYS2 ucrt64's libstdc++ instead of llvm-mingw's bundled\nREM libc++ (which clashes with mingw-w64's macro-based isnan/signbit in <math.h> once\nREM GTK/glib's own -I flags pull that math.h in ahead of libc++'s own). The -Wno-*\nREM flags silence GCC-only header attributes/extensions/builtins Clang either doesn't\nREM know or evaluates differently -- harmless as plain warnings, but this build uses\nREM CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug, which turns on -Werror.\n\"%~dp0clang++.exe\" -stdlib=libstdc++ ^\n  -isystem C:/msys64/ucrt64/include/c++/15.2.0 ^\n  -isystem C:/msys64/ucrt64/include/c++/15.2.0/x86_64-w64-mingw32 ^\n  -L C:/msys64/ucrt64/lib ^\n  -Wno-invalid-constexpr -Wno-unused-command-line-argument ^\n  -Wno-unknown-attributes -Wno-keyword-compat -Wno-deprecated-builtins ^\n  -Wno-inline-namespace-reopened-noninline -Wno-unknown-warning-option -Wno-user-defined-literals ^\n  %*\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "3. Build"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Using the UCRT64 terminal, from the Ansel source directory:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (bash)
#: content/install/windows.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "$ export CC=/opt/llvm-mingw-<date>-ucrt-x86_64/bin/clang-mingwlib.cmd\n$ export CXX=/opt/llvm-mingw-<date>-ucrt-x86_64/bin/clang++-libstdcxx.cmd\n$ export CFLAGS=\"-fsanitize=address -fno-omit-frame-pointer\"\n$ export CXXFLAGS=\"-fsanitize=address -fno-omit-frame-pointer\"\n$ export LDFLAGS=\"-fsanitize=address\"\n$ mkdir build_asan\n$ cd build_asan\n$ cmake -G Ninja -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug -DBINARY_PACKAGE_BUILD=OFF -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=/opt/ansel_asan ../.\n$ cmake --build .\n$ cmake --install .\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< warning >}}\nUse `-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug`, not `Release`/`RelWithDebInfo`. The latter enable LTO (`-flto=thin`), which produces bitcode-only `.obj` files this cross-compiler setup cannot reliably link. Debug is also the generally-recommended build type for sanitizers regardless: no optimizer reordering to fight with when reading a stack trace.\n{{</ warning >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "4. Copy the ASan runtime DLLs"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`ansel.exe` ends up dynamically linked against six DLLs that are not on `PATH` by default. Copy them next to the installed binaries:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (bash)
#: content/install/windows.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "$ cp /opt/llvm-mingw-<date>-ucrt-x86_64/bin/libclang_rt.asan_dynamic-x86_64.dll /opt/ansel_asan/bin/\n$ cp /opt/llvm-mingw-<date>-ucrt-x86_64/bin/libc++.dll /opt/ansel_asan/bin/\n$ cp /opt/llvm-mingw-<date>-ucrt-x86_64/bin/libunwind.dll /opt/ansel_asan/bin/\n$ cp /ucrt64/bin/libstdc++-6.dll /opt/ansel_asan/bin/\n$ cp /ucrt64/bin/libgcc_s_seh-1.dll /opt/ansel_asan/bin/\n$ cp /ucrt64/bin/libwinpthread-1.dll /opt/ansel_asan/bin/\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The first three are llvm-mingw's own ASan/libc++/unwind runtimes (the ASan runtime itself depends on `libc++.dll` even though Ansel's own code is built against libstdc++, and Clang defaults to llvm-mingw's own libunwind for exception handling); the last three are libstdc++'s own dependencies.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "5. Run"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (bash)
#: content/install/windows.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "$ ASAN_OPTIONS=\"alloc_dealloc_mismatch=0:allocator_may_return_null=1:detect_leaks=0:halt_on_error=0\" /opt/ansel_asan/bin/ansel.exe --cachedir /opt/ansel_asan_cache --configdir \"$HOME/.config/ansel_asan\"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Reproduce whatever you are chasing, then close Ansel normally. If ASan catches anything, its report is printed directly to the terminal — redirect it to a file (`> ansel_asan.log 2>&1`) if you want to keep a copy.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\nGTK and glib are precompiled MSYS2 DLLs, not built with ASan. A pointer crossing that boundary (allocated on one side, freed on the other) trips a `bad-free` that has nothing to do with whatever you are actually chasing — this fires right at startup, from glib's own type system init, before any Ansel code runs. `halt_on_error=0` logs it and keeps going instead of aborting there; `alloc_dealloc_mismatch=0` and `allocator_may_return_null=1` suppress the same class of cross-allocator false positive elsewhere. This is a limitation of instrumenting one executable in a process otherwise made of non-instrumented DLLs, not a bug in Ansel.\n{{</ note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "VS Code setup"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "To make debugging easier with VS Code, you can use the UCRT64 terminal within the program, which is better suited than the standard Windows terminal. For example, you can use GDB and simply `Ctrl + click` on relevant lines in the debugger output to jump directly to the corresponding line in the code editor.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- To add UCRT64 terminal:\n  - Open settings.json\n  - Insert this code in the line before the last `}` :\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "    {{< warning >}} The preceding item must end with a `,` {{</ warning >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "    ```json \n    \"terminal.integrated.profiles.windows\": {\n      \"UCRT64\": {\n        \"path\": \"C:\\\\MSYS2\\\\usr\\\\bin\\\\bash.exe\",\n        \"env\": {\n            \"MSYSTEM\": \"UCRT64\",\n            \"CHERE_INVOKING\": \"1\"},\n        \"args\": [ \"--login\", \"-i\"],\n      }\n    }\n    ```\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- To make it the default terminal in VS Code:\n  - Insert this code in the line before the last `}` :\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "  {{< warning >}} The preceding item must end with a `,` {{</ warning >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (json)
#: content/install/windows.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "  \"terminal.integrated.defaultProfile.windows\": \"UCRT64\"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/install/windows.md content/install/linux.md
#: content/install/opencl.md content/views/toolboxes/scopes.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/blurs.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Caveats"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Starting in command line (with arguments)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In some situations, you will need to start Ansel in command line, with arguments modifying its default behaviour, to test or debug issues:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. If you built yourself, start the MSYS2 MINGW64 terminal (from the applications menu), and execute `/opt/ansel/bin/ansel.exe`,\n2. If you installed from the EXE package, open the Windows terminal (`cmd.exe`) and execute `\"C:\\Programs Files\\ansel\\bin\\ansel\"` (assuming you installed Ansel in the default directory, suggested by the installer).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "For example, if you note issues with OpenCL, you could start Ansel with OpenCL entirely disabled using `\"C:\\Programs Files\\ansel\\bin\\ansel\" --disable-opencl`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The caveat though is the debugging commands (`-d OPTION`) don't output messages to the terminal, as they do on Unix, because of issues with Windows. Instead, the output will be written in an `ansel-log.txt` text file, in your cache directory. Start the help of the software ( runing `\"C:\\Programs Files\\ansel\\bin\\ansel\" -h`), and the last line should tell you where the file will be located after the line `note: debug log and output will be written to this file: PATH`. Typically, it should be `C:\\Users\\USERNAME\\AppData\\Local\\Microsoft\\Windows\\INetCache\\ansel` on Windows 10.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/install/windows.md content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "OpenCL"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Darktable blacklists all Intel OpenCL drivers to prevent issues, because of an history of bad drivers. In practice, since Intel Neo, things are better and reasonably-old Intel platforms are well supported, so the blacklist is removed on Ansel. OpenCL issues (typically: black images) are still regularly reported with brand-new hardware.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If you find yourself in this situation, you have several mitigation options:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "0. Try to install a newer or an older version of your GPU driver.\n1. If you have a discrete GPU (Nvidia or AMD), you can disable the Intel embedded GPU:\n    1. By entirely removing the Intel OpenCL driver (use your software manager to locate it), so Ansel uses only your discrete driver,\n    2. By disabling the Intel GPU from within Ansel: open Preferences → Processing → CPU / GPU / memory, find that GPU's section, and switch off its \"Enable\" checkbox. This only affects that device — your discrete GPU (or the CPU path) keeps working normally, and no file needs editing.\n2. Try to change the build options for OpenCL kernels — this one has no equivalent in Preferences yet, so it still needs the config file:\n    - Locate the `anselrc` text file on your system (typically in `C:\\Users\\USERNAME\\AppData\\Local\\ansel`),\n    - Open it and locate the line starting with `cldevice_v4/N/YOUR_DEVICE/building=`, where `N` is a small index and `YOUR_DEVICE` is the name of your GPU as reported by its driver. The default value is `-cl-mad-enable -cl-no-signed-zeros`; yours may differ if you already changed it,\n    - Clear the building options, so you get `cldevice_v4/N/YOUR_DEVICE/building=` (nothing after `=`)\n3. If you don't have a discrete GPU and the Intel one is your only one:\n    1. Start the application with OpenCL disabled at all with `COMMAND --disable-opencl` (see the previous section for the actual system command to run, depending on your installation)\n    2. Disable the Intel GPU from Preferences (see point 1.2. above)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Memory allocation"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel uses virtual memory pre-allocation for fast memory access. Virtual memory needs to be enabled on your system and the pagefile size required by Ansel should be available on your system. If, at startup, you see errors like :\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (bash)
#: content/install/windows.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "couldn't alloc map (VirtualAlloc error 1455)\nERROR: can't init pixelpipe cache, aborting.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "it means the system refused to allocate the requested memory. This can happen on gamers PC where performance optimizations have been made.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Diagnose "
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Open Task Manager → Performance → Memory and check `Committed: X / Limit`. `X` is the currently-used virtual memory. If it is close to `Limit` you will have to close applications using it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Fix "
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The first approach is to enable or increase the virtual memory on your system. Go to System Properties → Advanced → Performance → Virtual Memory and set pagefile to system managed or several GB.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If this is not enough, the second approach is to reduce Ansel memory consumption. `memory_os_headroom` can be adjusted directly from Preferences → Processing → CPU / GPU / memory, without editing any file. `host_memory_limit` has no equivalent in Preferences yet, so it still needs the config file: open `C:\\%LOCALAPPDATA%\\ansel\\anselrc` with a text editor and locate the lines :\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block
#: content/install/windows.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "host_memory_limit=-1\n...\nmemory_os_headroom=25000\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When set to `-1` or `0`, `host_memory_limit` detects all the RAM available on your system and will allocate to Ansel `host_memory_limit - memory_os_headroom`. Values are in MiB. To manually control this, you can set `host_memory_limit=4000` (4 GiB) as a starting point, then try allocating more if that works.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/windows.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\n`memory_os_headroom` is clamped between 1024 (1 GiB) and a third of the RAM Ansel detects: setting it to `0` will not actually leave zero headroom, it will floor to 1024. If you need to go below that, lowering `host_memory_limit` itself is the only way.\n{{</ note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/install/_index.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Install"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/install/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Dependencies for Source Builds"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If you plan to build from source, the Ansel repository ships per-platform dependency installer scripts in `packaging/`. Use the script matching your OS (for example `./packaging/install-deps-ubuntu.sh`, `./packaging/install-deps-macos.sh`, or `./packaging/install-deps-windows-msys2.sh`).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/install/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Understanding Ansel's releases"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/install/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel channels"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel development and release path follows 3 channels :\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. A __production channel__ :\n\t1. Linked to the Git [master branch](https://github.com/aurelienpierreeng/ansel),\n\t2. Contains tested code supposed to work reliably for daily use,\n\t3. Contains features found under the _Done_ column, in the [project management board](https://github.com/orgs/aurelienpierreeng/projects/1).\n2. A __pre-release channel__ :\n\t1. Backward-compatible with the production channel _(so you can switch from one to another without breaking your image edits)_,\n\t2. Linked to the Git [candidate branch](https://github.com/aurelienpierreeng/ansel/tree/candidate),\n\t3. Contains code to test before integration in production channel but still relatively safe to use,\n\t4. Contains features found under the _Testing_ column, in the [project management board](https://github.com/orgs/aurelienpierreeng/projects/1).\n3. An __experimental channel__ :\n\t1. __non-backward-compatible__ with either the production or pre-release channels,\n\t2. Linked to the Git [dev branch](https://github.com/aurelienpierreeng/ansel/tree/dev),\n\t3. Contains features found under the _In progress_ column, in the [project management board](https://github.com/orgs/aurelienpierreeng/projects/1),\n\t4. This channel needs to be used aside of the production install since it contains prototypes of new modules, new versions of old modules, and other features that will upgrade the version of the [Ansel database](../preferences-settings/storage#database), making it incompatible with previous versions. It can also contain prototypes that will never make it in production.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "At all time, the  [project management Kanban board](https://github.com/orgs/aurelienpierreeng/projects/1) will show what changes are being currently worked on in the non-production channels, with links to their pull request and issue, to help traceability of changes and bug reports.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/install/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel versioning"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The __production channel__ is versioned as follow :\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. All versions having the same major version number (`0.x`, `1.x`, `2.x`) produce editing histories that are compatible with each other and use the same internal [database](../preferences-settings/storage#database) version. Major version numbers are upgraded every time changes are introduced that break this property, such as new modules, database structure changes, or new versions of old modules.\n2. Minor version numbers (`x.0`, `x.1`, `x.2`) are upgraded every time new changes are made that don't break compatibility within the major version, like GUI refactorings and bug fixes.\n3. The major version `0.x` is compatible with darktable 4.0 and 4.0.1 image editing histories, which means it is also compatible with edits made with any darktable version earlier than 4.0. Ansel will not maintain compatibility with upstream darktable for future versions after `0.x`, since darktable 4.2 will introduce nonsensical changes that will stay the burden of the darktable team only.\n4. Versions are tested snapshots of the __production channel__. This channel will keep getting changes in-between versions, notably from the __pre-release channel__, supposed to be safe between versions.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/install/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel testing"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Tests are important because I develop and use Ansel on Linux Fedora, and I have no way of knowing how it works on other OS. A [continuous integration](https://github.com/aurelienpierreeng/ansel/actions/workflows/ci.yml) bot automatically tests building and basic execution (processing a test picture through the [CLI variant](../cli/ansel-cli) of the software), on Windows 10, Linux Ubuntu 20.04 and 22.04 and Mac OS 11.7 and 12.6. On each OS, the CI bot builds using GCC 9 to GCC 12 and Clang 12 to 14.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "But those continuous integration tests are not enough as they don't encompass a real desktop use with a graphical interface and will not show runtime errors. The different channels are meant to gradually push changes to production.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (mermaid)
#: content/install/_index.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "graph TD;\n\tmaster[/Stable/];\n\tcandidate[/Pre-release/];\n\tdev[/Experimental/];\n\tmaster2[/Stable/];\n\tmaster3[/Stable/];\n\n\tmaster --> m1((Merge));\n\tmaster --> m2((Merge));\n\n\tmaster --> dev;\n\tdev --> c3[fa:fa-code Make unsafe changes];\n\tc3 --> t3[fa:fa-clock Testing];\n\tt3 --> r3{Bugs ?};\n\tr3 -- no ---> m2;\n\tr3 -- yes --> f3[Fix];\n\tf3 --> t3;\n\tm2 --> p3([fa:fa-tag Tag new major version]);\n\tp3 --> master2;\n\n\tmaster --> candidate;\n\tcandidate --> c1[fa:fa-code Make safe changes];\n\tc1 --> t1[fa:fa-clock Testing];\n\tt1 --> r1{Bugs ?};\n\tr1 -- no -----> m1;\n\tr1 -- yes --> f1[Fix];\n\tm1 --> p1([fa:fa-tag Tag new minor version]);\n\tf1 ---> t1;\n\tp1 --> master2;\n\n\tmaster2 --> t2[fa:fa-clock Testing];\n\tt2 --> r2{Bugs ?};\n\tr2 -- no ---> p2([fa:fa-tag Tag new maintenance version])\n\tr2 -- yes --> f2[Fix];\n\tf2 --> t2;\n\tp2 --> master3;\n\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This graph shows how tests are dispatched in the general development workflow. Pre-built \"nightly\" packages are provided for all 3 channels (stable, pre-release and experimental) for all the platforms (Linux, Windows, Mac OS) and can be tested directly by anyone.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "After some time without bug reports, the pre-release and experimental channels are successively merged into the stable channel, letting some time between both merges.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "After some time with no bug report in the stable channel, a new maintenance release gets tagged. This means that the `x.0.0` major versions are less tested than the `x.0.1` and `x.1.0`, and should be avoided in environments where stability is the priority.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/install/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Release schedule"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The __production channel__ is updated in a rolling-release fashion. Each change triggers automatic builds on Linux, Windows and Mac OS, producing \"nightly\" packages that can be directly downloaded and installed on each platform. You can subscribe to these nightly builds to update Ansel in a rolling-release fashion.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Major, minor and maintenance versions are released when they are ready.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/install/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ugrading between major releases"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Major releases are defined by the fact that the [Ansel database](../preferences-settings/storage#database) structure will be upgraded and therefore will not be usable by previous major releases. Before the ugrading process starts, Ansel automatically backs up the database in the configuration folder, appending the new version number to the database filename.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The configuration folder of Ansel is located by default at :\n* `$HOME/.config/ansel` for Mac OS and Linux\n* `C:\\%LOCALAPPDATA%\\ansel` on Windows.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In this configuration folder, the image editing histories are stored in the file `library.db` and the presets, styles, and other preferences are stored in the file `data.db`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If you upgrade, for example, from version 2.6 to 3.0, both files are backed up under the respective names `library.db-pre-3.0`  and `data.db-pre-3.0`. If, for some reason, you want to go back to the previous 2.6 version after having upgraded to 3.0, you just need to remove the `pre-3.0` suffixes from the file names to restore your back-ups. Any editing you may have done with the 3.0 version will be lost without a chance of being recovered in that case, unless you manually back up `library.db` and `data.db` for a future where you will use the 3.0 version.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< warning >}}\nThere is no backup mechanism in place for the [XMP sidecar files](../views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/sidecar.md), but they can be restored from the `library` database, which means you need to keep it clean and up-to-date.\n{{</ warning >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/install/linux.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Install on Linux"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Install your GPU OpenCL drivers if you have a GPU :\n- For __Nvidia__ GPUs, OpenCL support is part of the CUDA proprietary driver and is not available with the open-source _Nouveau_ driver:\n\t- [add Nvidia's repository to your package manager](https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-installation-guide-linux/index.html#package-manager-installation),\n\t- [install the CUDA packages](https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-installation-guide-linux/index.html#driver-installation),\n\t- Reboot.\n\t- __It is highly discouraged to manually install drivers through [runfiles (.run)](https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/cuda-installation-guide-linux/index.html#runfile-installation) as it is riddled with problems you will need to manually fix everytime the Linux kernel is updated__.\n- For __AMD__ GPUs, the [AMDGPU-Pro](https://www.amd.com/en/support/kb/faq/amdgpu-installation) driver is recommended:\n\t- [download the relevant driver](https://www.amd.com/en/support),\n\t- [extract the archive](https://www.amd.com/en/support/kb/faq/amdgpu-installation#faq-Prerequisites),\n\t- Run the installation script:\n\t\t- If you want only the OpenCL driver, without video drivers and OpenGL (assuming your system already has some of those working): `./amdgpu-pro-install -y --opencl=pal,legacy --headless`,\n\t\t- If you want the complete GPU stack (OpenCL/OpenGL, Vulkan, video drivers): `./amdgpu-pro-install -y --opencl=pal,legacy`è\n\t- Reboot.\n- For __Intel__ embedded GPUs, the _Neo_ driver provides support for OpenCL 3.0 on relatevly recent\n\t- [official driver releases](https://github.com/intel/compute-runtime/releases/latest) contain the Ubuntu `.deb` packages, you need to install `intel-opencl-icd_xxxx.deb`,\n\t- The safest way is to locate the `intel-opencl-icd` package in your distribution's repository, and install it. It can be part of optional or third-party repositories for distributions leaning a bit too hard toward open-sourcery (Debian/Fedora).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If you don't have a GPU, a manual build is recommended to get the best performance possible (see below).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "For a better GUI, install [Roboto fonts](https://fonts.google.com/specimen/Roboto). It is available as a package in most distributions. It has been chosen because it is clean, designed for software GUI and supports 311 languages, including extended Latin, Cyrillic and Greek alphabets.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "AppImage package (recommended)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The Ansel project provides an official AppImage package, built for the stable channel, every night at 00:00 UTC. This is the recommended way of installing Ansel, since it is fresh from the repository, always up-to-date, contains all necessary libraries and dependencies at their proper version, and ships updated lens databases for Lensfun.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The AppImage is build on an Ubuntu 18.04 machine, which is old enough to support most currently-running Linux distributions.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Install [Fuse2](https://docs.appimage.org/user-guide/troubleshooting/fuse.html),\n- Ensure your Linux distribution runs at least `libc`/`glibc` version 2.27, that is :\n\t- Ubuntu ≥ 18.04,\n\t- Fedora ≥ 28,\n\t- Debian ≥ Buster,\n\t- OpenSuse Leap ≥ 15.3.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In practice, most Linux distributions released after 2018 and all distributions released after 2021 are good to go.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- [Get the latest AppImage](https://nightly.link/aurelienpierreeng/ansel/workflows/lin-nightly/master/ansel.stable.AppImage.zip),\n- [Find earlier AppImage packages](https://github.com/aurelienpierreeng/ansel/releases/tag/v0.0.0).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This will most likely put the AppImage package into your `~/Downloads` folder, you may want to move it to a dedicated folder like `~/bin`, into your personal directory.[^1]\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "[^1]: The `~/bin` folder is natively supported by [appimaged](https://github.com/AppImageCommunity/appimaged).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Run the AppImage"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- [Give it execution permission](https://discourse.appimage.org/t/how-to-run-an-appimage/80/4).\n- Either double-click on the AppImage file from your file browser or launch `./Ansel-xxxx-x86_64.AppImage` in terminal.\n- Command-line arguments are forwarded to the packaged binaries, so `./Ansel-xxxx-x86_64.AppImage --version`, `./Ansel-xxxx-x86_64.AppImage -d dev -d perf -d opencl -d verbose` and `./Ansel-xxxx-x86_64.AppImage /path/to/image.raw` work as expected.\n- Auxiliary command-line tools can be called through the AppImage too, by passing the tool name as the first argument, for example:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (bash)
#: content/install/linux.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "./Ansel-xxxx-x86_64.AppImage ansel-cli input.raw output.tif\n./Ansel-xxxx-x86_64.AppImage ansel-cltest\n./Ansel-xxxx-x86_64.AppImage ansel-cmstest\n./Ansel-xxxx-x86_64.AppImage ansel-generate-cache\n./Ansel-xxxx-x86_64.AppImage ansel-noiseprofile\n./Ansel-xxxx-x86_64.AppImage ansel-gen-noiseprofile\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "To create a system shortcut and have Ansel accessible from your dash/app menu, you can use [AppImageLauncher](https://assassinate-you.net/posts/2020/09/appimagelauncher-2.2.0-released/), available as `.deb`, `.rpm` and `.AppImage` packages ([download](https://github.com/TheAssassin/AppImageLauncher/releases/tag/v2.2.0)). It will integrate the AppImage with your desktop environment with or without running extra services in background, upon request, and can update the Ansel AppImage.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Update the AppImage"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Automatic updates are not available at this point, you may use [AppImageUpdate](https://github.com/AppImageCommunity/AppImageUpdate) to update the Ansel AppImage. The benefit of this method is to allow incremental updates, avoiding to re-download a ~95 MB file every time.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Though the Lensfun database of lens profiles is stored and up-to-date in the AppImage package, if you installed a custom Lensfun database at some point in your home directory (usually, by running the command `lensfun‑update-data`), this database can take precedence over the one shipped in the AppImage.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If you note that a relatively-new lens supposed to be supported by Lensfun doesn't appear to be supported in the [lens correction module](../views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md), either run the `lensfun-update-data` command again, or simply delete the local database, usually located in `~/.local/share/lensfun/updates`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The AppImage intentionally uses the host OpenMP runtime (`libgomp`) instead of bundling its own copy. This avoids mixing a packaged OpenMP runtime with the host OpenCL and GPU drivers, which has been observed to trigger stability issues on some systems.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Packages from Linux distributions repositories"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Pre-built packages are provided by third-party maintainers, may not be up-to-date and may be compiled with some optional features disabled and other distro-centric customizations. They are outside of the scope of the Ansel project and no support is offered. They are mentionned here for what they are worth.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid " - [Arch Linux Repository (AUR)](https://aur.archlinux.org/packages/ansel-git).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Building manually ensures that the software uses all available optimizations for your particular hardware, while using the pre-built package uses generic optimizations that fit all modern 64 bits architectures. Depending on the modules you use and on your CPU, you may experience a 25-30 % speed-up by using a manual build taylored for your hardware, when not using OpenCL.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When using OpenCL, the GPU code is compiled for your particular hardware whether you use a pre-built package, the AppImage or a manual build, so it will make no difference for OpenCL-ready modules, but not all modules have an OpenCL variant and many parts of the software run on CPU (like the picture codecs).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Install `git` through your package manager,\n- In a terminal, run :\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (bash)
#: content/install/linux.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "$ git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/aurelienpierreeng/ansel.git\n$ cd ansel\n$ git submodule init\n$ git submodule update\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Install the dependency packages (see below).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Dependencies"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The repository includes per-distro dependency installers in `packaging/`. From the repo root, run one of:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (bash)
#: content/install/linux.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "./packaging/install-deps-ubuntu.sh\n./packaging/install-deps-debian.sh\n./packaging/install-deps-fedora.sh\n./packaging/install-deps-arch.sh\n./packaging/install-deps-suse.sh\n./packaging/install-deps-nix.sh\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "These scripts install the build/runtime dependencies used by CI and packaging.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Note that optional dependencies don't make the script abort when they are not found, but will disable the corresponding features. You need to read the build script output to find out if everything works as you expect it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\nAnsel builds its own copy of Exiv2 — the library it uses to read image metadata — from the `src/external/exiv2` git submodule, and links it statically. You therefore don't need `libexiv2-dev` (or `exiv2-devel`) installed, and you no longer need to build Exiv2 by hand.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This used to be a real obstacle: Fedora, and some other distributions, build Exiv2 without ISOBMFF support. Canon CR3 raw files are ISOBMFF containers, so an Ansel linked against such a build simply could not open them. Ansel now enables ISOBMFF itself, so CR3 works everywhere.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Make sure you clone with `--recurse-submodules`, or run `git submodule update --init` in an existing clone.\n{{</ note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Compile the application"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Easy way"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "From the source code directory, you need to invoke the build script as follow:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (bash)
#: content/install/linux.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "$ sh build.sh --install --sudo --clean-all\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This will:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Cleanup any remnant of a previous Ansel build and installation (avoiding weird corner-cases),\n- Build the software with the most aggressive level of optimizations for your particular hardware (the produced binary will not be portable to another hardware),\n- Install the software in `/opt/ansel`\n- Install a system-wide command `ansel` that can be invoked in a terminal,\n- Install a system-wide desktop launcher,\n- Update Lensfun database of lenses profiles.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "To enable less-aggressive optimizations (if you discover picture artifacts) and install the software somewhere else, use:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (bash)
#: content/install/linux.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "$ sh build.sh --prefix /opt/YOUR/PATH --build-type RelWithDebInfo --install --sudo --clean-all\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Invoke `sh build.sh --help` for a complete overview of available options. In most cases, you can skip `--clean-all` for a faster build (so it does not rebuild from scratch), but that can cause inconsistencies sometimes, and spotting those is a lot more time-consuming than simply rebuilding from scratch.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Custom way"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If you don't have a good reason to do that, don't, and use the previous method.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (bash)
#: content/install/linux.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "$ cd ansel\n$ mkdir build\n$ cd build\n$ export CXXFLAGS=\"-O3 -fno-strict-aliasing\"\n$ export CFLAGS=\"$CXXFLAGS\"\n$ cmake .. -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=/usr -G Ninja -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -DBINARY_PACKAGE_BUILD=ON -DCMAKE_INSTALL_LIBDIR=lib64\n$ sudo cmake --build . --target install -- -j 8\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Run the self-built application"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "You can run `ansel` or `/opt/ansel/bin/ansel` from the terminal, or use your applications menu to locate Ansel.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Update the self-built application"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "You can run the build script above with the extra argument `--update` like:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (bash)
#: content/install/linux.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "$ sh build.sh --install --sudo --clean-all --update\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The caveat is this will update the source code except the build script itself, which may cause issues if the build script was modified (but that rarely happens). To overcome this, you can execute the script twice, or update manually with:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (bash)
#: content/install/linux.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "$ cd ansel\n$ git pull --recurse-submodules\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Then run the building script above, same as when you installed.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Linking the system Exiv2 instead"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel builds its own Exiv2 by default, and that is the configuration we test and ship. If you are packaging Ansel for a distribution and must link the system library instead, configure with:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (bash)
#: content/install/linux.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "$ cmake -DUSE_BUNDLED_EXIV2=OFF ...\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "That build **requires an Exiv2 compiled with `-DEXIV2_ENABLE_BMFF=ON`**, and the configure step will stop with an error if it isn't. ISOBMFF is what reads the metadata of Canon CR3, AVIF and HEIF files; CR3 is one of the most common formats among Ansel users, and an editor that cannot open its users' raw files is not something we can let build quietly. Fedora in particular still disables it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "It appears that most Linux distributions provide outdated Lensfun databases (even though the binary is up-to-date), so you may want to update it: `sudo lensfun-update-data`\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Containers and sandboxes (Docker, Kubernetes, Windows Subsystem for Linux, etc.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel is a front-end desktop application that needs a CMS (_Color Management System_) having direct access to the GPU (graphic processor).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Indeed, even if you don't use OpenCL hardware acceleration, display color profiles (`.icc` and `.icm`) often contain an \"hardware\" calibration tagged VCGT (_Video Card Gamma Table_) that typically calibrates the gamma and white point of the screen. This VCGT is directly pushed to a special memory onto the GPU, used to color-correct the whole screen framebuffer, whether or not individual applications are actually color-managed internally.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Internally color-managed applications may then apply a second round of color correction, aiming at converting standard RGB spaces to the display RGB space (typically through a matrix conversion). The correctness of this second round is subjected to the correctness of the first round: the VCGT.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This design is of course terrible, because different parts of the OS (operating system) can compete for access to the VCGT (most obvious example: the redshift apps adjusting the color temperature of your screen depending of the time of day, as to reduce blue light at night and help sleep), and overwrite it at different times, making the VCGT unreliable and unpredictable.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When using containers and sandboxed environments like Docker, or WSL2, there is no way of knowing how the framebuffer will be color-corrected, that is no way of knowing what part of the pipeline (host OS or container) will get the last say over what happens in the VCGT. These systems have been designed to run text-based and server-side applications, image production applications have never been a goal.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "__It is highly discouraged to try and run Ansel in Docker/Kubernetes containers or in the Windows Subsystem for Linux as these have no way of ensuring color traceability.__\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "32 bits platforms, Raspberry Pi and non-conventional platforms"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Though the support for 32 bits platforms was officially dropped as of Darktable 2.2, some people have reported to be able to install later Darktable versions (so possibly Ansel) on them. Similarly, people have reported successful installations of Darktable on Raspberry Pi which, despite being a 64 bits platform, doesn't nearly have sufficient power to process 24 to 52 Mpx images.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/linux.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Users are hereby discouraged from trying to install Ansel on anything else than a typical x86_64 desktop computer. Ansel uses heavy image processing and being able to install and start the software on some weak hardware doesn't imply the runtimes will be bearable in a production environment.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/install/macos.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Install on Mac OS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/install/macos.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Build from source (Homebrew, recommended)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/macos.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This method is derived from the repository scripts in `packaging/macosx/`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/macos.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Install Xcode Command Line Tools:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (bash)
#: content/install/macos.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "  xcode-select --install\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/macos.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Install Homebrew from https://brew.sh/ (use the default prefix: `/opt/homebrew` on Apple Silicon, `/usr/local` on Intel).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/install/macos.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Steps"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/macos.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. Clone the repository and submodules:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (bash)
#: content/install/macos.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "   git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/aurelienpierreeng/ansel.git\n   cd ansel\n   git submodule init\n   git submodule update\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/macos.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "2. Install build dependencies:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (bash)
#: content/install/macos.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "   ./packaging/macosx/1_install_hb_dependencies.sh\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/macos.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "   This is the canonical dependency list used by CI and packaging.\n3. Build and install into the local `install/` directory:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (bash)
#: content/install/macos.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "   ./packaging/macosx/2_build_hb_ansel_custom.sh\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/macos.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "   Edit `packaging/macosx/2_build_hb_ansel_custom.sh` if you need custom CMake options.\n4. (Optional) Create a macOS application bundle (can be signed if you have a Developer ID):\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (bash)
#: content/install/macos.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "   export CODECERT=\"your.developer@apple.id\"   # optional\n   ./packaging/macosx/3_make_hb_ansel_package.sh\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/macos.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "   The bundle is generated at `install/package/Ansel.app`.\n5. (Optional) Generate a DMG image from the bundle:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (bash)
#: content/install/macos.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "   ./packaging/macosx/4_make_hb_ansel_dmg.sh\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/macos.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "   The output is `Ansel-<version>-<arch>.dmg` in the `install/` directory.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/install/macos.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Running the build"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/macos.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Development install (after step 3):\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (bash)
#: content/install/macos.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "  ./install/bin/ansel\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/macos.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- From the app bundle (after step 4):\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (bash)
#: content/install/macos.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "  ./install/package/Ansel.app/Contents/MacOS/ansel --configdir ~/.config/ansel --cachedir ~/.cache/ansel/\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/install/macos.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Notes and limitations"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/macos.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- The DMG is not notarized. If Gatekeeper blocks it, remove the quarantine attribute:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (bash)
#: content/install/macos.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "  xattr -d com.apple.quarantine Ansel-*.dmg\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/macos.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- The DMG is only guaranteed to run on the same macOS version it was built on.\n- Homebrew package versions define the library versions bundled into the app.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/install/macos.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "MacPorts method (legacy, Intel-only)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/macos.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A legacy Intel-only method using MacPorts exists in `packaging/macosx/BUILD.txt`. It includes custom patches for `gnutls` and `gtk-osx-application`, and uses `gtk-mac-bundler` to produce a DMG. Use this only if you specifically need a MacPorts-based build.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/install/debug.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Debugging"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/debug.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel is written in the C language. While C __pretends__ to be portable (_runs the same on all operating systems_), many little things need to be contextually adapted to each OS using slightly different code. Also, depending on hardware and on the version of the dependencies, even on Linux, some discrepancies can occur between distributions.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/debug.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "That is to say that bugs are almost impossible to anticipate when programming, and the default state of a computer program is buggy. Reporting bugs properly is the only way to help cleaning up the unforseen pathological cases.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/install/debug.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "What is needed to debug ?"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/debug.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "4 things :\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/debug.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "* The sequence of user operations that lead to the bug (doing this, clicking that, etc.),\n* The nature of the bug (segmentation fault at line xxx, crash with no warning),\n* The hardware and operating system used, OpenCL driver if any, Linux kernel version, etc.\n* The exact version of the software used, that is the full name of the `.exe` Windows package or `.AppImage` Linux package, or the full version string that can be found in the popup by clicking the Ansel logo, in the top panel of the software.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/install/debug.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Unexpected crashes"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/debug.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When Ansel crashes unexpectectdly, an `ansel_bt_xxxxxx.txt` file (where `xxxxxx` is an hexadecimal identifier) is usually written in the `/tmp` directory on Linux, or in `$USER\\appData\\local\\temp\\` on Windows. When you are lucky, it will contain the exact line of the exact file of the sourcecode responsible for the crash. This needs to be reported to the developer.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/debug.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If the file is empty, please move on to the next section.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/install/debug.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Forced crash"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/debug.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When you note a reproduceable crash and are lucky enough to be on Linux, you can force it to crash with better feedback by running the program within `gdb` (_it is typically already installed on most distributions, otherwise find the package providing it on yours and install it_). `gdb` will usually provide more information than the backtrace file, and may also record backtraces in situations where the above method produces an empty file.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/debug.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. Start a `gdb` session, in terminal :\n   * If using a self-built version, run `gdb /opt/ansel/bin/ansel`\n   * If using an AppImage, run `gdb /path-to-appimage/Ansel-xxxxx-gxxxx-x86_64.AppImage`\n2. Start the software with the `run` command, from the same terminal, and reproduce your faulty sequence of operations,\n3. When it crashes, run `bt full > ansel_bt.log` (`bt` is for backtrace), and send the `ansel_bt.log` file just created in your home folder to the developer.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/install/debug.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Typical bugs"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/debug.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "* When there is no space left on the hard drive hosting your home folder, Ansel will crash fair and square at startup with a segmentation fault.\n* When your system is out-of-memory (RAM) or Ansel tries to reserve more memory than what is still available, the OS will kill Ansel fair and square, without backtrace or error message. This typically happens when processing heavy editings. It can be fixed to some extent by reducing the resources used by Ansel, in the [preferences](../preferences-settings/processing.md).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/install/debug.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Warnings"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/debug.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When starting the program in terminal, you will usually see a list of warnings linked to Gtk, like:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (bash)
#: content/install/debug.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Gtk-WARNING **: 19:22:37.893: Theme parsing error: gtk.css:123:31: The :insensitive pseudo-class is deprecated. Use :disabled instead.\nGtk-Message: 17:18:35.117: Failed to load module  \"canberra-gtk-module\"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/debug.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "These can be ignored.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/install/darktable.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Import configuration from darktable"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel is based on darktable 4.0 and configuration files of darktable 4.0.1 and prior can be seemlessly imported by Ansel, such that you recover your tags, styles, presets, and library of images.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< warning >}}\nImporting darktable 4.2 database into Ansel will make you lose the settings of _filmic RGB_, _sigmoid_ and _highlights reconstruction_ modules for all edits using them. _Sigmoid_ is not and will never be supported into Ansel, and the nonsensical changes made to _filmic RGB_ in darktable 4.2 will never be ported to Ansel. All other modules should still work.\n{{</ warning >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/install/darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Importing usual darktable configuration files"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\nThis section applies if you installed darktable from sources or pre-built packages, on Linux, Windows and Mac. It does __not__ apply if you installed darktable from Flatpak packages (_see the next section in this case_).\n{{</ note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The configuration folder of darktable is located by default at :\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- `~/.config/darktable` for Mac OS and Linux\n- `C:\\%LOCALAPPDATA%\\darktable` on Windows.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel re-uses the same folder structure, where `darktable` is replaced by `ansel`. Since Ansel is based on darktable 4.0, you can import your darktable 4.0 and earlier configuration files by simply copying into `/ansel` configuration folder.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/install/darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Terminal"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This works on Mac OS and Linux terminal : \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (bash)
#: content/install/darktable.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "$ cp -R ~/.config/darktable ~/.config/ansel\n$ mv ~/.config/ansel/darktablerc ~/.config/ansel/anselrc\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/install/darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Graphical"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If you are not comfortable with command lines or use Windows, use your file browser to copy-paste the following files from the darktable configuration folder to the Ansel's one :\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- `library.db`,\n- `data.db`,\n- `shortcutsrc`,\n- `darktablerc`, which will need to be renamed `anselrc` after copying.\n- The `color` folder, if any, especially if some of your edits use custom-made ICC color profiles.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "From there, you can continue to work just as before. Ansel can be installed alongside darktable.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/install/darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Importing configuration files from Flatpak"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Flatpak packages work on recent Linux distributions only and are a hacky way of dealing with outdated packages on distributions repositories, but they create many other problems in the process.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Flatpak run in sandboxes and don't store their configuration files at the usual place but in ` ~/.var/app/org.darktable.Darktable/config/darktable`, where `~/` is the current user home folder.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "From this Flatpak configuration folder, you will need to import the files mentionned in the previous section into `~/.config/ansel`. However, the current user will not be the owner of the copied files, which belong to the Flatpak user, and they are copied with their permissions retained. In practice, it means that the copied files will be read-only and Ansel will not be able to write into the database.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "To fix the permissions, you have a console and a graphical method.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Run in terminal :\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (bash)
#: content/install/darktable.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "$ cp -R ~/.var/app/org.darktable.Darktable/config/darktable ~/.config/ansel\n$ mv ~/.config/ansel/darktablerc ~/.config/ansel/anselrc\n$ chown -R USER:USER ~/.config/ansel # may need sudo\n$ chmod -R 644 ~/.config/ansel\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "where `USER` is your OS-wise username.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Use your file browser to navigate to `~/.config/`. Since it is an hidden file (as the `.` prefix suggests), you may need to display hidden files first to find it (the typical shortcut is <kbd>Ctrl + H</kbd>).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\nThe following uses a generic logic. The actual wording of the labels depend on the file browser and desktop environment you are using and need to be adjusted.\n{{</ note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Once in `~/.config/`, right-click on the `ansel` folder and click on the _Properties_ entry of the contextual menu, then on the _Permissions_ tab. From there, set `read` and `write` permissions for the file owner and its group and check the option _Apply to subfolders and their content_.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Note that the graphical method does not allow you to change the file owner, which needs to be done in terminal with the command `chown -R USER:USER ~/.config/ansel`. If you don't know how to do it, and if you are not the owner of the folder, set `read` and `write` permissions to _Others_ too (which means all users of the system). __This is a security threat if you are not the only user of the computer since it will allow anybody to access and write changes in the folder__. It is ok if you are the only user of the computer or you trust the intents __and__ the skills of the other users (at home).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/install/darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Working with Ansel alongside darktable"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Darktable and Ansel can store the images editing histories in [XMP sidecar files](../views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/sidecar.md) when the option is enabled. Since they both use the same XMP tags, the last application to have opened the image will overwrite its own history within the XMP. This is not a real issue for darktable 4.0 and Ansel 0.0 since their [pixel pipelines](../views/darkroom/pixelpipe/_index.md) are compatible, but the compatibility is broken between Ansel and darktable 4.2.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "However, both applications load the editing histories first from their database. Then, if the _look for updated xmp files on startup_ is [enabled in the preferences](../preferences-settings/storage#xmp), they can crawl the image folders to detect if the XMP edits are more recent than the database ones. In that case, they will prompt a window asking if the files should be synchronized and in which direction. Never synchronize from XMP to database if you use both applications on the same files.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/install/opencl.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Install OpenCL drivers"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/opencl.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "OpenCL is a vendor-agnostic and cross-platform way of offloading computations to the GPU. GPUs are designed specifically to handle pixel operations, with an optimized memory layout and a massively multi-core architecture[^2] allowing for fast and energy-efficient image processing.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/opencl.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "[^2]:  At least 720 cores for cheap GPUs, to almost 10.000 cores for the beasts\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/opencl.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "But OpenCL can only be used for background computational (offline rendering) tasks and cannot be used to render the GUI in real-time, which is the realm of OpenGL.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/opencl.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The architecture of Ansel and darktable pixel pipeline is flawed in that regard, because the output of (fast) OpenCL computations has to be sent back to the (slow) Gtk 3 thread, which paints the processed image within the GUI (along with the rest of GUI controls) using only a single CPU core. This is the biggest performance bottleneck in darktable and Ansel, and the reason why it is a rather slow application.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/opencl.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Nevertheless, OpenCL will bring a substantial speed-up to your image processing and exporting.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/install/opencl.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Scope of OpenCL"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/opencl.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "OpenCL is used only by the [image processing modules](../views/darkroom/modules/_index.md) that have an OpenCL kernel available as an alternative to the CPU (plain C) code. This is optional, and not all modules have an OpenCL kernel, depending on the skills, willingness and available hardware of the developer who programmed them. [^1]\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/opencl.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "[^1]: Plain C code can rarely be translated directly to OpenCL kernels and needs extra effort to adapt it to the different memory layout.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/opencl.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "OpenCL kernels are compiled at runtime by [LLVM](https://llvm.org/), when the application is first executed, which may take some time and delay the startup. They are then cached in `~/.config/ansel`, and recomputed every time the OpenCL driver version changes (on upgrades and downgrades).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/opencl.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Apart from some (_most_) image processing modules, the rest of Ansel does not make use of the GPU at all, being through OpenCL or OpenGL.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/install/opencl.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Requirements"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/opencl.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "OpenCL will be available when a GPU and its driver are detected and compatible with OpenCL 1.2 at least. A GPU with at least 2 GB of memory is recommended. LLVM v4 to v15 is needed to build the kernels at runtime (it is included in the official packages – _the drivers are not_).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/install/opencl.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Expected benefits"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/opencl.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "It is impossible to say how much faster a particular GPU will make image rendering, compared to a particular CPU, without actually benchmarking them, especially because image processing is more bound by the speed of I/Os (transfers between hardware memories) than by the raw clock speed of the computing chip (the infamous GHz × number of cores).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/opencl.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "It has been found that crazy-expensive CPUs still perform better than cheap GPUs. However, at similar performance, GPUs will typically drain less electric power and produce less heat than CPUs, which will also prevent [thermal throttling](https://pcguide101.com/cpu/what-is-cpu-throttling/) to some extent.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/opencl.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The overall performance of OpenCL depends on :\n- The GPU raw performance,\n- How well the driver uses the GPU (beware the power-saving modes),\n- How well the OS integrates the driver (noticeably, support in the Linux Kernel),\n- How many applications use the GPU simultaneously,\n- How cleverly the LLVM compiler was able to optimize the OpenCL kernels for your hardware and driver,\n- How well your computer cooling system is able to maintain the casing at a low-enough temperature,\n- How fast your motherboard bus is able to copy pixels from the RAM to the GPU memory, and the other way around.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/opencl.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Anyone pretending to guess the overall performance by just looking at a GPU specifications sheet is a fraud.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/install/opencl.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Vendors"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/install/opencl.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Intel"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/opencl.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel can use the Intel GPU embedded on the CPU chip, provided you use recent [Intel Graphics Compute Runtime drivers](https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/developer/articles/tool/opencl-drivers.html) (dubbed [_Intel Neo_](https://github.com/intel/compute-runtime)). These seem to work fairly on Linux and Windows at least.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/opencl.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The previous generation of Intel OpenCL drivers, dubbed _Intel Beignet_, has never worked reliably. Fortunately, it has gradually disappeared from repositories and distributions since 2018.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/opencl.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Using the embedded GPU will be typically slightly faster than using the CPU. If getting the Intel Graphics Compute Runtime on your system is not too much of a hassle, the boost is definitely worth it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/install/opencl.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Optimus"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/opencl.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "[Optimus](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nvidia_Optimus) is an Intel + Nvidia hardware setting used on laptops since 2011, which consists in a low-power/low-performance Intel embedded GPU (_eGPU_) used for lightweight tasks, coupled with a discrete high-performance/power-hungry Nvidia GPU (_dGPU_) for games and rendering, but turned on only on demand.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/opencl.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "On Linux, this technology has been properly supported only since circa 2020 (_though HDMI still does not work on eGPU for most laptops if the HDMI port is soldered on the dGPU_), but only for the mainstream distributions, and Gnome even allows to start applications explicitly on the eGPU or the dGPU without having to use command line or restart the graphical session.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/opencl.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "However, \"starting an application on dGPU\" only means that the GUI rendering (aka OpenGL) is piped to the dGPU. Since Ansel renders all the GUI on CPU and doesn't use OpenGL at all, it doesn't need to be explicitly started on dGPU. OpenCL will be able to work in background on all detected GPUs no matter which one renders the GUI.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/opencl.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel is able to use both the eGPU and the dGPU at the same time to render image pipelines concurrently.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/install/opencl.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "AMD"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/opencl.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Only the proprietary [amdgpu-pro driver](https://amdgpu-install.readthedocs.io/en/latest/install-installing.html) supports OpenCL.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/opencl.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "On Linux, AMD has been known for several years to be very slow to update their drivers when distributions are updated. Don't upgrade your distribution before you know AMD has working drivers for it, and stick to LTS versions if using Ubuntu.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/install/opencl.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Nvidia"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/opencl.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Only the proprietary driver supports OpenCL.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/opencl.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "On Linux, Nvidia OpenCL depends on the CUDA libraries. There are known issues of kernel panic or system crash when the computer goes in hibernate or suspend mode, because the allocated memory buffers are not preserved. If that happens to you, Linux distributions typically have packages called `nvidia-modprobe`, `nvidia-persistenced` and `nvidia-power(save)d` which should take care or that (or worsen it). In any case, avoid letting your computer go in suspend or hibernate mode while any application using OpenCL is running, and save any work in progress before.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/opencl.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "OpenCL and graphics drivers have the worst track-records and are known to break every next upgrade, mostly because vendors consider them low-priority, compared to their proprietary equivalent (CUDA for Nvidia, Metal for Apple) or the more recent Vulkan.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/install/opencl.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Stay on the production/LTS branches"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/opencl.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If you have an AMD card, you may have no choice : the latest driver will generally be the only one working on your system.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/opencl.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "For Nvidia and Intel cards, there are production branches that ship the yesteryear driver version. It may not support the brand new hardware nor the latest cool upgrades, but will generally show improved stability and fewer bad surprises.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/opencl.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Keep away from the hot new drivers for as long as you can. Linux distributions typically ship a couple of legacy drivers alongside the most recent.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/install/opencl.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Memory use"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/opencl.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "All applications using the GPU, through CUDA, Vulkan, Metal, OpenCL and OpenGL will share the GPU memory, which is typically much smaller than the system RAM. The GPU memory allocation is not as predictable as the RAM allocation, and is mostly vendor/driver-dependent.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/opencl.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "That is to say that running concurrently several applications all using the GPU may result in crashes if they temporarily try to use more memory than available. Try to avoid playing 4K videos in your web browser, playing games or running GPU-accelerated video encoders (Nvenc, VDPAU, etc.) when Ansel is processing 36-52 Mpx pictures.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/install/configuration.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Configuration files"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/configuration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The configuration files of Ansel are located in :\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/configuration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "* `$HOME/.config/ansel` for Mac OS and Linux\n* `C:\\%LOCALAPPDATA%\\ansel` on Windows.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/install/configuration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Default configuration files"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/configuration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`anselrc`\n: contains all user preferences except presets and styles. This may be opened in any text editor for manual edit if any preference makes the software crash at startup.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/configuration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`keyboardrc`\n: contains all user-defined keyboard shortcuts.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/configuration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`data.db`\n: contains user presets for modules, styles, tags and locations. This is an SQLite database. It is backed-up before updating to a new release and automatic snapshots can be configured (see below).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/configuration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`library.db`\n: contains all images known to Ansel, along with their directories (dubbed _film rolls_). Like the previous, it is an SQLite database backed-up before updating to new releases and with optional snapshots.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/configuration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The frequency of snapshots can be defined in the software global _Preferences_ -> tab _Storage_ -> section _Database_ -> entry _Create database snapshots_ and _how many snapshot to keep_. By default, Ansel saves one snapshot every week when closing the software.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/install/configuration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Optional configuration files"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/configuration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`luarc`\n: a text file containing enabled Lua modules. Note that Lua doesn't work yet on Ansel v0.0.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/configuration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`lua` directory\n: containing the tree of all available Lua modules. This directory is actually a local Git repository that can updated through `git pull` from the sources. It is added by the automatic Lua installer or can be addde manually.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/configuration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`user.css`\n: a stylesheet containing user-defined CSS tweaks to the default theme,\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/configuration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`watermark` directory,\n: to add manually, containing SVG files to be used in the [watermark module](../views/darkroom/modules/watermark.md),\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/configuration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`color` directory\n: containing an `in` and an `out` sub-directory in which user-defined ICC v2 or v4 profiles can be dropped for later use respectively in the [input color profile](../views/darkroom/modules/input-color-profile.md) and [output color profile](../views/darkroom/modules/output-color-profile.md) modules. Input profiles will be used to decode images opened in Ansel, output profiles to export images, for softproofing in the darkroom or for display color management.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/install/configuration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "XMP sidecars"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/configuration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel allows to [write XMP sidecar files](../views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/sidecar.md) along the raw images. These files duplicate the content of the local `library.db` in a text file hosted on the same storage as the raw pictures (which may be a server, a NAS or an external hard-drive), while the `library.db` stays locally in the home directory of the current computer user.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/configuration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel always loads the editing histories from the local `library.db` database first, and can optionally [look for updated XMP files on startup_](../preferences-settings/storage#xmp). If the editing version doesn't match between XMP and database, you will prompted with a conflict resolution window allowing to synchronize from XMP to database or from database to XMP, or to keep the most/least recent version. This can be used to synchronize edits between computers, but needs to be used carefully because the content of XMP is not checked, so all computers need to use the same version of Ansel.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/install/configuration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "XMP sidecars can also interfere with darktable if you use both. [Learn more](./darktable.md#working-with-ansel-alongside-darktable)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/data-privacy.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Data privacy"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/data-privacy.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel is a desktop application that runs entirely on your computer. It needs **no account**, and by\ndefault it sends **nothing** anywhere. The only data that ever leaves your machine is the *optional*,\n*anonymous* information described on this page — and only after you have explicitly agreed to it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/data-privacy.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This page documents, from your point of view, exactly **what** can be collected, **how**, **where**\nit goes, **who** processes it and **what for**. The exact source code that collects and sends each\npiece of data is linked at the bottom for independent audit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/data-privacy.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The short version"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/data-privacy.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Everything here is **opt-in**. On first launch Ansel shows a single dialog with one checkbox per\n  data flow, both of which you can leave unticked.\n- Ansel **never** sends your images, your file names, your folder paths, your metadata, your location,\n  your name, your email, your IP-based identity, or anything that could identify you or your photos.\n- You can change your mind at any time in **Preferences ▸ Storage ▸ Privacy**.\n- If you build Ansel yourself, both features can be compiled out entirely.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/data-privacy.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\nThe information collected is *anonymous and aggregate*: it describes the software and the computer it\nruns on, not you and not your work. There is no account and no way to link a report back to a person.\n{{< /note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/data-privacy.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The consent dialog"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/data-privacy.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The first time you run Ansel with its interface, you are shown one dialog titled **\"Help us improve\nAnsel\"**. It contains:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/data-privacy.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- a checkbox to enable **crash reports**,\n- a checkbox to enable **anonymous usage statistics**,\n- a link to this very page,\n- a button to confirm your choice.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/data-privacy.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Whatever you choose is remembered, and the dialog is never shown again. Nothing is sent for any flow\nwhose box you leave unticked. If Ansel is started without its graphical interface (command-line\nprocessing), no dialog can be shown and nothing is collected until you have made a choice in the\ninterface.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/data-privacy.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The two data flows"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/data-privacy.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Crash reporting and usage analytics are **independent**. They have separate checkboxes, separate\non/off switches in the preferences, go to **different** service providers, and can be enabled one\nwithout the other.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/data-privacy.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. Crash reports"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/data-privacy.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**What for** — so that when Ansel crashes, the developers automatically receive the technical details\nneeded to find and fix the bug, instead of relying on you to notice, locate and attach a crash log.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/data-privacy.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**When** — only if a crash actually happens. A crash report is stored locally at the moment of the\ncrash and uploaded the **next** time you start Ansel.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/data-privacy.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**What is sent**\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/data-privacy.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- the *backtrace* — the list of internal program functions that were running when Ansel failed\n  (including, on Linux, a detailed `gdb` backtrace attached as a text file);\n- your operating system and version;\n- your hardware: number of CPU cores, amount of RAM, graphics card / OpenCL device name;\n- on Linux: whether you use X11 or Wayland, and your desktop environment (GNOME, KDE, …);\n- your screen resolution, the Ansel window size, and display scaling (DPI/PPD);\n- the Ansel version, build type, and whether it is an official or self-built binary;\n- how long the session ran before crashing, and counts of recent crash-free sessions;\n- *context about the moment of the crash*: which views, panels and editing modules were in use during\n  that session (by name and count), and the **kind** of image being processed — its file\n  **extension** (e.g. `cr3`, `jpg`), whether it is a raw/non-raw/HDR/monochrome file, whether it\n  still needs demosaicing, and its pixel dimensions (no filename, no path).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/data-privacy.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Who and where** — crash reports are processed by [**Sentry**](https://sentry.io), on their\n**European Union** (Germany) infrastructure. Sentry's privacy policy:\n<https://sentry.io/privacy/>.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/data-privacy.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Turn it off** — untick *Preferences ▸ Storage ▸ Privacy ▸ Send anonymous crash reports*.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/data-privacy.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "2. Anonymous usage statistics"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/data-privacy.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**What for** — so the developers can see which features and platforms are actually used, and focus\ntheir limited time where it helps the most. A small open-source project otherwise has almost no\nvisibility into how its software is used.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/data-privacy.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**When** — a short message the first time you use a given tool or open a given kind of file during a\nsession, plus a summary at the start and (on a clean exit) end of the session. It records *that* a\ntool was used or a kind of file was opened — never the order, exact timing, or content of what you\ndid.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/data-privacy.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- a *random installation identifier* — a meaningless random number generated on your computer, used\n  only to avoid counting the same installation many times. It is **not** derived from your hardware,\n  account or network, and cannot identify you;\n- your operating system, number of CPU cores, amount of RAM, graphics card, and (on Linux) display\n  server and desktop environment;\n- your screen resolution and display scaling;\n- the Ansel version, build type, and whether it is an official or self-built binary;\n- how long the session lasted;\n- which views, panels and editing modules you used, **as counts only** (e.g. \"exposure module\n  enabled 4 times\") — never in what order or at what time;\n- the **kinds** of files you worked on: file **extensions** and broad type flags (raw vs non-raw,\n  needing demosaicing or not) with counts — never file names, paths, contents or metadata.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/data-privacy.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Who and where** — usage statistics are processed by [**PostHog**](https://posthog.com) on their\n**European Union** infrastructure (`eu.i.posthog.com`). PostHog's privacy policy:\n<https://posthog.com/privacy>.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/data-privacy.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Turn it off** — untick *Preferences ▸ Storage ▸ Privacy ▸ Share anonymous usage statistics*.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/data-privacy.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "What is never collected"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/data-privacy.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "To be unambiguous, Ansel **never** transmits any of the following, under any setting:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/data-privacy.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- your photographs or any image data, thumbnails or previews;\n- file names, folder names or paths;\n- image metadata (EXIF, IPTC, GPS/location, capture dates, camera serial numbers, …);\n- your name, email address, or any account information (there is no account);\n- your editing history or the parameter values of your edits;\n- the contents of your catalog/database, tags, ratings or comments;\n- keystrokes, mouse movement, or screen captures.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/data-privacy.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Changing or revoking your choice"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/data-privacy.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Open **Preferences ▸ Storage ▸ Privacy**. There you will find the two independent switches:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/data-privacy.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- **Send anonymous crash reports**\n- **Share anonymous usage statistics**\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/data-privacy.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Changes take effect immediately for usage statistics and from the next launch for crash reporting.\nTurning a switch off stops all corresponding transmission.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/data-privacy.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "For developers and auditors"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/data-privacy.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Both features are free software, like the rest of Ansel, and can be reviewed line by line. The\nrelevant source files in the\n[Ansel repository](https://github.com/aurelienpierreeng/ansel) are:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/data-privacy.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| What | File |\n|---|---|\n| The consent dialog and on/off logic | [`src/common/privacy_consent.c`](https://github.com/aurelienpierreeng/ansel/blob/master/src/common/privacy_consent.c) |\n| Crash reporting: setup, what context is attached, what is sent | [`src/common/sentry.c`](https://github.com/aurelienpierreeng/ansel/blob/master/src/common/sentry.c) |\n| Usage statistics: setup, payload, network sending | [`src/common/telemetry.c`](https://github.com/aurelienpierreeng/ansel/blob/master/src/common/telemetry.c) |\n| Where view usage is recorded | [`src/views/view.c`](https://github.com/aurelienpierreeng/ansel/blob/master/src/views/view.c) |\n| Where panel usage is recorded | [`src/libs/lib.c`](https://github.com/aurelienpierreeng/ansel/blob/master/src/libs/lib.c) |\n| Where editing-module usage is recorded | [`src/develop/imageop.c`](https://github.com/aurelienpierreeng/ansel/blob/master/src/develop/imageop.c) |\n| Where the processed file type is recorded | [`src/develop/pixelpipe_hb.c`](https://github.com/aurelienpierreeng/ansel/blob/master/src/develop/pixelpipe_hb.c) |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/data-privacy.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If you distribute your own build of Ansel, you can disable these features entirely at compile time\nwith the CMake options `-DUSE_SENTRY=OFF` (crash reporting) and `-DUSE_TELEMETRY=OFF` (usage\nstatistics), in which case the corresponding code is not even included in the binary.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/_index.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Documentation"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This documentation is derived from [Darktable 4.0 user manual](https://github.com/darktable-org/dtdocs.git) and further developed under the [Ansel project](https://github.com/aurelienpierreeng/ansel-doc), where comments, suggestions and corrections can be made. It is released under the [GPL 3.0](https://www.gnu.org/licenses/gpl-3.0.en.html) license.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "[Darkroom modules](./views/darkroom/_index.md) that have been deprecated in either Ansel or darktable 4.0 are not documented here and can be found on [darktable user manual](https://docs.darktable.org).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\n\"Deprecated module\" means the GUI widget of the module is hidden from the software interface as to decourage users from using it in new edits. The pixel processing filters for deprecated modules are __still__ in the software and the GUI will show the widgets of the deprecated modules for any old edit using them. This ensures compatibility of old edits since Darktable 1.0 at least.\n{{</ note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The goal of this documentation is to explain the effect and use cases of the features and settings existing in the software. The opposite approach, explaining what features are useful to achieve a certain goal, is provided in the [workflows](../workflows/_index.md) section.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Users may subscribe to the [RSS feed of the documentation](./index.xml) to follow new pages and fresh updates of old pages.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/getting-started/selection.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Image selection"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/selection.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel allows batch-applying editing histories (through copy-pasting, or through styles) or metadata. The target images for those batch-operations are _selected_ images. But even single-image operations (like opening in [darkroom](../views/darkroom/_index.md) to edit, or editing metadata, copy/pasting, etc.) use selections.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/selection.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Selections are particular objects in Ansel, because :\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/selection.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. They are remembered between reboots and across ateliers (views) changes,\n1. They are global in the sofware and used to pass on lists of images __between__ lighttable and other ateliers,\n1. They are disabled and inactive __within__ an atelier (except for the lighttable), which use active images instead (contextual selection),\n2. They are necessarily a subset of the current filtered collection (as displayed in the [lighttable](../views/lighttable/)),\n3. They can be set only from the [lighttable](../views/lighttable/) view (no selection can be performed or used from the [filmstrip](../views/toolboxes/filmstrip.md)),\n4. They can be turned into a temporary collection themselves, by using the _Restrict to selection_ button in the include/filter toolbar of the [lighttable](../views/lighttable/).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/selection.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Selected images will appear highlighted in the lighttable. Because selections are used to perform (possibly harmful) _write_ operations, Ansel forces \"hard\" interactions (pressing on an hardware button) on thumbnails to define them. These allowed interactions are :\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/selection.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- <kbd>Left click</kbd> or <kbd>Space</kbd> : to select a single image,\n- <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Left click</kbd> or <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Space</kbd> : to add/remove an image to the current selection (toggle),\n- <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Left click</kbd> or <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Space</kbd> : to extend the selection range from the nearest-selected image to the current image,\n- Global menu 🡒 _Selection_ : to select all/none images from the current collection, or invert the current selection. Associated keyboard shortcuts will be shown in the menu entries,\n- <kbd>Double left click</kbd> or <kbd>Enter</kbd> will open the hovered image in [darkroom](../views/darkroom/_index.md), but will also create a singleton selection, because the darkroom can only handle one image at a time. This is a particular behaviour.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/selection.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A selection will be needed to perform any property editing, even on a single image, among the following :\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/selection.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Applying star ratings, or rejecting,\n- Applying color labels,\n- Applying tags, title, copyright or any metadata,\n- Changing GPS coordinates or time zone,\n- Copying/pasting image histories, deleting them,\n- Removing images from library, or from the disk,\n- Etc.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/selection.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Unlike Darktable, Ansel doesn't implicitely treats the hovered image as a part of any selection, which prevents unpredictable and often unwanted changes on random images when the mouse cursor is left dangling on the thumbnail grid.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/selection.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If you scrolled far away from the selection and lost it, the global menu _Selection_ 🡒 _Scroll back to selection_ will realign the view on the thumbnail grid to the start of your selection.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/selection.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A message sitting on the right of the top-most toolbar will tell how many images are selected, among how many in the current collection, and, if there is only one selected, will tell the image index after the `#` symbol. This index will match the `#` numbers in the background of the lighttable thumbnails, and is referred to the current collection.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/selection.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In ateliers (views) other than the lighttable, interactions with the filmstrip will define active images, which are contextual selections relevant only to the current atelier. We don't call them selection here is to distinguish with the application-wide, global selection. Selections are inactive in views other than the lighttable, and even disabled in darkroom, which forces digital assets management actions from the global menu to always target the main image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/getting-started/_index.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Getting started"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel is a **non-destructive RAW photo editor**. It never modifies your original files: every change is stored as a recipe (an editing _history_) in a [library](library.md) database, and only written out to a real image file when you [export](../views/toolboxes/export.md). This means your originals stay safe, edits are reversible forever, and the same recipe can be applied to many images at once.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This section is a quick bootstrap. Each step links to a more detailed page and to the relevant [view](../views/_index.md).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/getting-started/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The four-step workflow"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (mermaid)
#: content/getting-started/_index.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "flowchart LR\n    A[Import] --> B[Cull &amp; organize] --> C[Edit] --> D[Export]\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/getting-started/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. Import"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Tell Ansel about your images. From the [global menu](../views/global-menu.md), choose _File → Import…_ (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>I</kbd>). You can either reference images where they already are, or copy them off a memory card into a tidy folder structure as you import.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "More details: [Import images](import.md)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/getting-started/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "2. Cull and organize"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Imported images appear as thumbnails in the [lighttable](../views/lighttable/_index.md). This is where you sort the keepers from the rest: assign **star ratings** (<kbd>0</kbd>–<kbd>5</kbd>), **reject** (<kbd>R</kbd>) or **color labels** (<kbd>F1</kbd>–<kbd>F6</kbd>), add tags and metadata, and narrow the view down to exactly the images you want with the [collection filters](library.md) and the _Library_ tool.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "To act on images — rate them, tag them, copy an edit onto them — you first **select** them with a click or a keystroke. Selection is deliberate and explicit, so nothing changes by accident.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "More details: [Library and collections](library.md) · [Image selection](selection.md)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/getting-started/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "3. Edit"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Double-click a thumbnail (or select it and press <kbd>Enter</kbd>) to open it in the [darkroom](../views/darkroom/_index.md). The image-processing [modules](../views/darkroom/modules/_index.md) sit in the right panel, grouped into workflow tabs that follow the [pixelpipe](../views/darkroom/pixelpipe/_index.md). Editing left-to-right across the tabs, and bottom-to-top within them, walks you through a sound order of operations.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "There is **no save button**: every change is recorded automatically into the history. You can step back at any time with <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd>, or revisit any past state from the [history of changes](../views/toolboxes/history-stack.md).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "More details: [Editing images](editing.md)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/getting-started/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "4. Export"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Apply the history to the original to produce a final file. Select the images and choose _File → Export…_ (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>E</kbd>) to render them to JPEG, TIFF and other formats. You can also reuse an edit across a whole shoot by copying its history or saving it as a [style](../views/toolboxes/styles.md).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "More details: [Export](../views/toolboxes/export.md)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/getting-started/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Finding your way around"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A few things worth knowing before you start:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- **Views.** Ansel is split into [views](../views/_index.md) — _lighttable_ for browsing, _darkroom_ for editing, plus optional map, print and slideshow. Switch views from the **Ateliers** menu; return to the lighttable from anywhere with <kbd>Escape</kbd>.\n- **The menu bar is the backbone.** Most application-wide commands live in the [global menu](../views/global-menu.md) at the top of the window. Press <kbd>Alt</kbd> to reveal each menu's keyboard mnemonic.\n- **Keyboard or mouse, your choice.** The whole interface can be driven from either. The [global action search](keyboard.md#vimkey-like-global-action-search) (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>P</kbd>) finds and triggers any command by name.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "More details: [Shortcuts and keyboard interaction](keyboard.md)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/getting-started/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Coming from another editor?"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If you are migrating from Darktable, the [from Darktable](../from-darktable.md) page summarizes what changed. The short version: selection is explicit (hovering never changes anything), and the GUI is organized around the menu bar and pipeline-ordered module tabs rather than customizable icon groups.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/getting-started/library.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Library and collections"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/library.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel keeps a database of all images it knows, that is all images that were [imported](import.md), until you actively remove them from the library (global menu : _File_ 🡒 _Remove from library_). The library stores the filesystem path of the images, their metadata and their editing history.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/library.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< warning >}}\nAnsel does not listen to the content of filesystem folders, meaning it will not be notified if imported images are (re)moved on the filesystem. You will need to manually update the pathes if that is the case, or you can move those files directly with Ansel (under the global menu _File_).\n{{< /warning >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/library.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel will then perform database extractions based on arbitrary user criteria : those are called _collections_. They are built in [lighttable](../views/lighttable/_index.md) from the _library_ toolbox and from the _include_ toolbar. Ansel has no concept of gallery or virtual folder that you __create__ first, then to which you __add__ arbitrary images. Instead, in Ansel, you __attach__ tags to images, then __collect__ all images having a certain tag (or any kind of metadata, base folder, etc.), possibly mixing filters and search criteria to narrow-down the search : this is what makes a collection.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/library.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The first two tabs of the _library_ toolbox, _folders_ and _collections_, contain simple interfaces to the most-used querying criteria : base folder of the images (called _filmroll_), and tags attached to images. The third tab, _queries_, offers a more complete and somewhat cumbersome interface to perform any kind of advanced query, possibly mixing several criteria with boolean operators (OR/AND/AND NOT), among filmrolls, tags and many more image metadata.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/library.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Once you have built a collection by inputing querying criteria, you can save them into a preset into the _library_ toolbox, by clicking on the menu in its header, then _store a new preset_.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/library.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Once the first stage of querying is performed in the _library_ toolbox, the _include_ toolbar, in the second row of the header, will apply workflow-related refinements (see [lighttable](../views/lighttable/_index.md) for more details).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/library.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A collection can be defined only from the lighttable view, and is then globally shared within the whole software. It defines the content of the lighttable thumbnail grid and of the [filmstrip](../views/toolboxes/filmstrip.md) in all other views.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/getting-started/editing.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Editing images"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/getting-started/editing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Understanding non-destructive editing"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/editing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Before diving deeper into how to use the software, we should stop here to explain its general paradigm : non-destructive editing.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/editing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Once you [imported](./import.md) images to Ansel, what you really did is only to create an entry for them into the [library](../install/configuration.md) database. The modifications you make on these images are never written to the original images, which are actually never changed. This is a strong design requirement aiming at protecting your input RAW images from any kind of corruption.[^1] Ansel will not even let users change the EXIF date and time in the original files.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/editing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "[^1]: Except the natural aging of your storage media that may lead to randomly-corrupted bits in the future.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/editing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Instead, Ansel writes your image-processing and metadata changes to its library database, and possibly duplicates them to [sidecar XMP files](../views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/sidecar.md). These changes are stored as a list of the [modules](../views/darkroom/modules/_index.md) used and the parameters defined for each module. __Said otherwise, a non-destructive editing is stored only as an history stack of changes, which represents the recipe for building the final image.__\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/editing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\nFor some non-destructive RAW editors, an editing is only a snapshot of the parameters set in all the image-processing filters ([modules](../views/darkroom/modules/_index.md)) used, so you have one entry per filter/module. Ansel keeps the whole history as long as you don't compress it (using global menu 🡒 _Edit_ 🡒 _Compress history_), so you can have many entries per filter/module, one entry per user change.\n{{< /note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/editing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "To actually build the final image, you will need to [export](../views/toolboxes/export.md) it to a target file on a target filesystem : this will apply the history stack to the original, input RAW, to create a final file that can be opened in any image viewer.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/editing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "There are many benefits to working in a non-destructive fashion :\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/editing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. The input image stays as safe as possible,\n2. You can apply the same history stack to many images (through copy-pasting or creating [styles](../views/toolboxes/styles.md)), therefore editing in batch,\n3. You can go back at any point in your history and revert selective changes (same as <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd>, but persisting between reboots), even in some years,\n4. You can start from an existing history, duplicate it, and work on a variant while keeping the original untouched,\n5. You can share your histories (as XMP files or as styles) with other users.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/editing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Because we keep all those history steps, they are automatically saved once applied. You will find no \"save\" button in Ansel. Here is the summary of the workflow in Ansel:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/editing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/editing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| Step | Action from user perspective | Action from software perspective |\n| ---- | ---------------------------- | -------------------------------- |\n| 1. Import images | Select input images from the filesystem | Create virgin entries for images in the database |\n| 2. Edit images | Apply corrections to an image             | Add editing history entries for this image into the database |\n| 3. Export images | Build a final JPG/TIFF/etc. image       | Run a pixel pipeline applying the history onto the input image |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/editing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< /table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/getting-started/editing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Editing, retouching, correcting your image"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/editing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "After [import](./import.md), the series of images you imported will be opened in the [lighttable](../views/lighttable/_index.md) view, as a grid of thumbnails. You only have to double-click on any image you want to edit, which will then open the [darkroom](../views/darkroom/_index.md). This view will provide you with image processing [modules](../views/darkroom/modules/_index.md) allowing to apply changes and correction to the appearance of your image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/getting-started/import.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Import images"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/getting-started/import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Basics"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel backbone is a library of images, saved as an SQLite database into `~/.config/ansel/library.db`. Since Ansel is a non-destructive photo editor, editings are saved as histories of settings, along with image metadata, as text[^1] into the library database. The application of editing histories over raw images, as to produce output raster images, is the export step.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The library is also used to keep track of image collections (by tags, folders, EXIF, editing time, etc.). Because of this, we need to make the library aware of new images : it is the import step. This will initialize a new database entry with metadata and a virgin history for each imported picture.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "[^1]: or as binary blobs, but still : we store the recipes, not the results.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The import modal window can be accessed from the global menu _File_ 🡒 _Import..._.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/getting-started/import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Two kinds of imports"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The _file handling_ setting allows to enforce 2 different strategies :\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Import in library\n: This simply makes the library aware of pictures that already exist somewhere on a local or distant storage.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "[^2]: though there are manual way to update file pathes in the library, if you ever move entire folders.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Copy to disk\n: The typical use case for this strategy is when you want to empty your camera memory cards and save the photos to a permanent storage. It adds a copying step, with bulk files and folders renaming, prior to the same insertion to library as in the previous strategy.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/getting-started/import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Importing without copy"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "You will need to ensure that those images stay available on the same storage in the future, at the exact same address, because Ansel needs to access them prior to applying any history or generating exports and thumbnails. Ansel does not keep track of file moves on the filesystem,[^2] meaning displaced images will appear lost in the interface (displayed as skull thumbnails).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/getting-started/import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Importing with copy"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When emptying your memory card to a permanent storage, that is when you copy the files before importing to the library, you can rename files in batch and split them automatically into subfolders. This is done through the _project directory naming pattern_ and _file naming pattern_ fields, using [variables](..//variables.md). The final path of each image imported with copy will be `Base directory / Project directory / Filename`, where `Base directory` will be selected directly from the filesystem (usually the user's `Pictures` default folder), without variables.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Some particular variable's values can be set from the import window :\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Project Date\n: By default, it is (explicitly) set to today's date, and the time defaults to 00:00:00 UTC+0 if not explicitly set. Date and time can be changed to any past of future date, either in plain-text (using ISO 8601 format), or using the calendar widget. This date and time will be used by the variables `$(YEAR)`, `$(MONTH)`, `$(DAY)`, `$(HOUR)`, `$(MINUTE)`, `$(SECOND)`, `$(MSEC)`. If you plan on using time (hour, minute, second and below), you should manually set it in plain-text in the entry.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Jobcode\n: This is the project name, like the subject of your photo session. It is retrieved in patterns with the `$(JOBCODE)` variable.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Those values are constant among images for a whole import session.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "EXIF variables can also be used in file and directory names, like `$(EXIF.YEAR)`, `$(EXIF.MONTH)`, `$(EXIF.DAY)`, etc. for the date and time of image capture. These variables are a property of each image, which means that, if you use them into directory names, images may be split into different directories and later to different _filmrolls_ (see below). EXIF variables are fetched from reading the actual files, which triggers I/O from the filesystem holding them, and can significantly slow-down importation for files hosted on remote storages or USB 2.0 cameras.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "To fast-track per-picture date/time retrieval, you can also use the variables `$(FILE.YEAR)`, `$(FILE.MONTH)`, `$(FILE.DAY)`, `$(FILE.HOUR)`, `$(FILE.MINUTE)` and `$(FILE.SECOND)`. These variables read the file modification date from the filesystem, without reading the actual files, which reduces I/O and brings substantial speed-up when accessing memory cards from within cameras. Provided no third-party application tampered with raw files, the date/time should be the same as EXIF, at least down to the second. Issues may arise if pictures were opened and saved from other applications, and their time of modification changed.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< warning >}}\nDumping all new images into a (few) single, massive directories is discouraged as it doesn't promote sane culling workflows and might need insane filtering options in lighttable to be workable. (See Darktable's 4.0 new collections/filtering monstruosity…).\n{{< / warning >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "While Ansel allows you to perform advanced SQL queries into the library database, to find images using many properties (including date/time, EXIF, rating, editing history, etc.), you should not rely on it to customarily access your images. Opening images from other applications, or uploading them to websites (for example : to send print orders) will use your native filesystem interface. Even within Ansel, accessing large collections of images at once will be slower.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "With this in mind, you should try to keep a tidy file structure, organizing photos into per-project or per-session directories, containing no more than a few hundreds pictures at once. Directories and file names should be self-explanatory, to allow text search into filenames from any file browser. Those are old and low technologies, supported on any platform, and relying on those makes for efficient and simple workflows.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/getting-started/import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Importing from cameras"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "__It is highly recommended to import from memory cards using a card reader__. Mounting the memory card through the camera, using an USB link and PTP/MTP drivers, is possible in most cases but discouraged :\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Read/write performance is bad (in any case, worse),\n- There are many possible camera MTP/PTP drivers issues on Linux, possibly involving the `udev` stack,\n- Recent cameras may not be supported at all for a while,\n- Several applications (and the OS file browser) may compete for access to PTP/MTP cameras and have auto-mount enabled (only one can lock it at a time),\n- The camera will need to be powered on during the whole process, draining batteries for no reason.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If you still want to proceed, here is some important information to do so.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/getting-started/import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "All OS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel uses the [Gio](https://docs.gtk.org/gio/index.html) library to handle files. This library essentially communicates with the operating system to handle local and distant storages, which means the proper drivers need to be installed. Depending on the generation of your cameras, you might look into 3 drivers :\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- [USB Mass Storage Device Class (UMS or MSDC)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/USB_mass_storage_device_class), used by card readers, by some cameras and by USB sticks. This is the most reliable and most widely supported, as the drivers already are in all OS since the early 2000's. Prefer this whenever possible,\n- [Picture Transfer Protocol (PTP)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Picture_Transfer_Protocol), used by cameras, especially DSLR,\n- [Media Transfer Protocol (MTP)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Media_Transfer_Protocol), used by some cameras and by smartphones.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Some cameras will let you choose the USB driver to load, in their menu. When that is the case, choose preferably mass storage or MTP. In any case, check that your camera is not in PictBridge mode (used only for direct connexion between a camera and a printer).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/getting-started/import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Windows"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Mass Storage devices should work out-of-the-box, however PTP and possibly MTP devices will require an USB driver compatible with `libusb` (like [Zadig](https://zadig.akeo.ie/)) to work with Gio. Once the driver is installed and the camera plugged-in, you need to go to the configuration panel and enable the Zadig driver for the relevant USB port. This will prevent the camera to work properly with other Windows applications and with the rest of the OS (file browser and such). You will need to deinstall the USB port and reinstall it with native drivers to regain access to your camera from Windows.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/getting-started/import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Linux"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Gio relies on [GVFs](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GVfs) and [FUSE](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Filesystem_in_Userspace) to mount external (and possibly remote) filesystems using various protocols (FTP, sFTP, Samba, DAV, PTP, MTP, etc.). You will need the following packages installed on your system (_actual package names may vary depending on your distribution_) :\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- For all cameras and card readers : `gvfs`, `gvfs-fuse`,\n- For PTP cameras : `gvfs-gphoto2`\n- For MTP cameras : `gvfs-mtp`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Note that only one application can lock a PTP camera at a time. If your file browser or any part of your OS is automatically mounting the PTP device when hotplugging, it might not be available to Ansel.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "There might also be interactions with the `udev` stack that you might need to debug.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\nBuying a 20 $ memory card reader will save you a lot of headaches and give you much faster I/O. Connection issues with USB cameras are out of the scope of Ansel and will not be debugged.\n{{< /note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/getting-started/import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Mac OS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "No idea.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/getting-started/import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "What next ?"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Photos are imported into _filmrolls_, which are the primary collection type in Ansel. Filmrolls are related to filesystem folders, but they are not equivalent :\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- A filmroll contains images from a single folder ; the folder's name is the identifier (unique name) of the filmroll,\n- A filmroll does not necessarily contain __all__ images from its sibling folder, for example JPEG files from the folder may have been discarded from the filmroll,\n- Filmrolls are not recursive or hierarchical, but it is possible to list all filmrolls attached to the sub-folders of an high-level folder,\n- If 2 sets of images are imported, at different times, from the same folder, they will be added to the same filmroll,\n- Filmrolls will not be automatically updated when new images are added to the filesystem folders.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In other words, filmrolls are _views_ of filesystem folders that exist only in Ansel's database, and containing only images that Ansel is aware of (because they were imported).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When the import completes, the import window will close and the [lighttable](../views/lighttable/_index.md) view will be updated to the last filmroll created during import.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Shortcuts and keyboard interaction"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel interface is designed to support two mutually-exclusive modes of interaction and navigation: through pointing devices (mouse or graphic tablet) and through the keyboard. Compared to Darktable, many interactions that required combinations of both have been removed, which makes the workflows more efficient.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Shortcuts and available actions"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel exposes app-wise actions and GUI controls to keyboard bindings through pathes like `Darkroom/Modules/Exposure/Black point`, where the left-most member of the path is the view and the right-most member is the actual control being bound. A such path defines an human-readable unique identifier that also says where the control is located in the application GUI.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel has two kinds of objects that users can map to keyboard binding through those pathes:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- __GUI controls/widgets__ : buttons, sliders, comboboxes, etc. Shortcuts attached to those will give them the focus,\n- __Actions__ : most of them are attached to the global menu, but some are contextual, depending on which widget has the focus. Shortcuts attached to those will trigger the actions.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The focus is a GUI state in which a widget will capture all keyboard events sent to the application. For text entries, that obviously means recording keystrokes to type text. For other widgets, like sliders, that may be capturing arrow keys events to increase/decrease the value. Only one widget in the whole application can grab the focus at any given time.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Clicking on a widget will automatically give it the focus, but that makes for a terrible workflow where you may have to constantly switch between mouse and keyboard. To allow a full-keyboard workflow, Ansel provides shortcuts to switch the focus to any desired target widget. From there, interacting with widgets (to change their value or their state) is done through generic (non-user-configurable) key bindings.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This means that :\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. Widgets shortcuts are only meant to __select a target widget__ (visible or not) to which further keyboard interactions will be dispatched,\n2. There is __only one shortcut__ to configure (and remember) per widget,\n3. All widgets of the same type (button, slider, combobox) will use the __same keyboard interactions__ once focused (easier to remember).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Focused control widgets will appear in a bolder font that the others. Focused modules will be highlighted.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Actions (other than widget focusing ones) don't target widgets at all, and calling their shortcut directly triggers them, possibly outside the GUI.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Finding default shortcuts, setting new ones"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\n__A popup window__ showing all available actions and their defined keys is available from the __global menu__, under _Edit_ 🡒 _Keyboard shortcuts…_. This window is meant to be at the same time a cheatsheet and a controller to edit shortcuts.\n{{< /note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< figure src=\"/shortcuts-popup.jpg\" caption=\"The shortcuts window\" />}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The shortcut pathes are broken into a hierarchical tree in this window. The first level of the hierarchy defines the shortcuts groups:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- There is one _global group_ (tied to the global menu),\n- There are several _local groups_ (tied to each view : darkroom, lighttable, etc.).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Inside each group, a combination of keys can be used at most once. If you try to re-use a key combination already in use somewhere else in the same group, you will get an error message saying what path is already using it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Several shortcuts groups can include the same combination of keys. The global group is loaded all the time in all views of the application, the local groups are loaded only when entering their respecting view and unloaded when exiting it (only one is loaded at anytime). If the global and a local group have each a shortcut using the same keys, as long as the local group is loaded, its shortcut will take precedence over the global shortcut.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Read-only shortcuts\n: They are signaled with a lock icon, meaning they can't be edited by users. They are described as _contextual interaction on focus_, because they will need a widget of the right type focused before to have any effect. You will find them under :\n    - `Darkroom/Controls` (for sliders and comboboxes interactions),\n    - `Lighttable/Thumbtable` (for navigation into the thumbnails grid).\n: They are given here in a \"cheatsheet\" spirit and are guaranteed to be up-to-date with what the code defines. The values found in the popup supercede any documentation regarding shortcuts defaults. The keys used by the read-only shortcuts will be unusable in user-parametrable shortcuts from the same group.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "User-parametrable shortcuts\n: They are either focusing actions, targeting a control, a module or a toolbox, or direct action triggers, like any action from the global menu.\n    - __Edit a shortcut__ : double click on the shortcut label, or on the _disabled_ label, in the _keys_ column, then type the keys you want to define as a shortcut. To stop recording keystrokes, you can either hit <kbd>Escape</kbd> or click outside the _keys_ column (which may be more reliable).\n    - __Clear a shortcut__ : double click on the trashbin icon, on the right of the shortcut. There is no way to clear a shortcut once in editing mode.\n: After a shortcut has been edited, it will be used without having to restart the application. If the shortcut applies to a menu entry, it should be updated in the menu too.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The shortcuts popup window has __a double search-engine__:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- One __for features__, that performs a case-insensitive search into action pathes,\n- One __for keys__, allowing to search for key modifiers, or keys, or combinations of both:\n    - If only a key modifier or key is given, results will be returned for partial matches,\n    - If both a key and a modifier are given, results will be returned for full matches,\n    - The syntax for key combinations is `<Mod1><Mod2>Key`, where all elements are optional. Auto-completion is provided for modifiers when you start typing `<`.\n- __features and key searches can be combined__ to efficiently narrow results,\n- As long as any search entry is not empty, the shortcut treeview is expanded. To automatically expand all items while not searching anything in particular, you can input `/` into the feature search entry.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Vimkey-like, global action search"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "All the available actions can be searched and triggered from anywhere in the software, using a vimkey-like command with auto-completion. The feature is available from the global menu, under _Help_ 🡒 _Search actions_ (default shortcut : <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>P</kbd>).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< figure src=\"/actions-search.jpg\" caption=\"The action search\" />}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This opens a modal popup with a search entry. Type in there a path (partial or complete) or simply the name of the action you are looking for. The auto-completion will return the list of all available actions matching your search from the currently-active shortcut groups, along with a description and their defined shortcut (if any). From there, by selecting one of the results (with mouse click, or navigating with arrow keys then hitting <kbd>Enter</kbd>), you will trigger the action. To cancel, hit <kbd>Escape</kbd>.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In this context, the action pathes become commands that can be triggered from the search popup without having to define shortcuts, which helps if you know __what__ action you want to trigger but don't remember __where__ its controller is in the GUI, or simply if you can't be bothered remembering shortcuts.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In darkroom, if you select in the global action search the path to a control (slider or combobox) within a module, the focusing action will open the parent module, scroll it such that it gets visible, make sure the control is visible (like changing the notebook page if the control is within a notebook/tabs) and focus the control. From there, you can keep interacting with the control through arrow keys to change its value.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This feature has also replaced the module search bar used since Darktable 3.6, because triggering the focus action on a module achieves the same result as restricting the list of modules to those matching a name query (making this module appear in GUI).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Keyboard workflow"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Global menu"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "All menues have mnemonics that can be shown by hitting <kbd>Alt</kbd> : they will then appear underlined. Hitting the underlined letter along with <kbd>Alt</kbd> will open the target menu. Once one menu is opened, you can navigate through all menues using arrow keys.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#: content/preferences-settings/lighttable.md
#: content/views/lighttable/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Lighttable"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md content/views/toolboxes/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Toolboxes"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "You can collapse/expand the toolboxes, in the left sidebar, by assigning them shortcuts or using the global action search on their focusing action. The effect of the focusing action is a toggle, so expanded toolboxes will be collapsed on the next action trigger.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Once a control has gained focus in a toolbox (which, so far, can only happen with a click), focusing on the next control can be done with <kbd>Tab</kbd> or using the arrow keys (in some situations). Otherwise, the focus is automatically given to the thumbtable (grid of thumbnails).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Thumbtable"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Navigation within thumbnails can be done with arrow keys. <kbd>PageUp</kbd>/<kbd>PageDown</kbd> scroll the view to the previous/next page. <kbd>Home</kbd>/<kbd>End</kbd> move all the way to the start/end of the grid.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Navigation within thumbnails only hovers. To commmit the hovered thumbnail to a selection, press <kbd>Space</kbd> for a singleton selection. To add/remove the hovered thumbnail to/from the current set of selected images, press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Space</kbd>. To extend the current selection up to the hovered thumbnail (range selection), press <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<bkd>Space</kbd>.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A selection is absolutely mandatory to be able to modify image metadata, such as ratings (stars), color labels, etc. or to apply any batch operations. Ansel is designed such that selections can only be made by \"hard\" interactions (pressing an hardware button), as to prevent user errors. Batch selection options, independent from the thumbtable, can be found in the global menu, under _Selection_.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Applying ratings uses the numeric keys (0-5) by default, applying color labels uses the F1-F6 keys.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Opening a picture in darkroom is done with <kbd>Enter</kbd>.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Filters"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "You can filter the current collection by text using the search entry, which will search into all text metadata of the image. To enter the search entry, the default shortcut is <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>F</kbd>.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md content/preferences-settings/darkroom.md
#: content/views/darkroom/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Darkroom"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Modules"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "To collapse/expand and focus a module, you can:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Hit <kbd>Alt</kbd> and see the mnemonic keys that some module have in their name, then type the mnemonic. This works only for visible modules.\n- Use the action search or define a shortcut on the module focusing action. This works also for hidden modules.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The module focusing action will automatically :\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. Open the native module tab of this module,\n2. Expand and focus the module,\n3. Scroll the right sidebar such that at least the header of the module (if not the whole module) is visible,\n4. Collapse and fade out all other modules,\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The user-defined set of favourite modules, that used to be displayed in a separate tab, is replaced by the ability to target a specific module through its focusing action.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "To expand+focus the previous/next module in the current module tab, use <kbd>Page Up</kbd>/<kbd>Page Down</kbd>.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "To switch to the next/previous module tab, use <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Tab</kbd> and <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Tab</kbd>. Once you reached one end of the modules tabs, it will cycle back to the other end.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Modules controls"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "To go directly to a control (slider or combobox), use the action search or define a shortcut to its focusing action. This will automatically:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. Open the native module tab of this module,\n2. Expand and focus the module,\n3. Scroll the right sidebar such that at least the header of the module (if not the whole module) is visible,\n4. Collapse and fade out all other modules,\n4. Open the right notebook (tab) page if the control is insterted into a notebook,\n5. Focus the control.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Once the control is focused:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- On sliders:\n    - Arrow keys <kbd>Left</kbd>/<kbd>Right</kbd> will increase/decrease the value by a normal step,\n    - <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+ arrows change the value using a fine step,\n    - <kbd>Shift</kbd>+ arrows change the value using a coarse step,\n    - <kbd>Insert</kbd> triggers the attached color-picker if any.\n- On comboboxes:\n    - Arrow keys <kbd>Up</kbd>/<kbd>Down</kbd> will cycle through the options,\n    - <kbd>Enter</kbd> validates the selected option and closes the popup,\n    - <kbd>Escape</kbd> exits the popup without validating the selected option.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Once a control is focused, you can focus to the previous/next with <kbd>Up</kbd>/<kbd>Down</kbd> keys, or <kbd>Tab</kbd>. To focus the previous/next control outside a notebook tab, when you reached the end of the current notebook page, use <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Up</kbd>/<kbd>Down</kbd>.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Once a shortcut is attached to a control (slider or combobox) focusing action, as long as the shortcut keys are kept pressed, all mouse scrolling events are sent to this control even when the mouse is not over the widget (as it would normally require to record scrolling events). This allows a 2-hands mixed workflow where one hand, on the keyboard, selects the target control, while the other scrolls on the mouse to adjust the value. It replaces MIDI support that achieves the same through mapping exactly one potentiometer to exactly one control, which will quickly run out of knobs to control Ansel.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Module instances"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When several instances of modules are in use, shortcuts are always attached to the first-added instance, which may not necessarily be the first in the pixel pipeline: the first instance is always the unnamed, base instance, until it gets deleted (then the next replaces it).[^1]\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "[^1]: The recommended workflow always uses the first instance as an average global setting, most likely to be accessed from keyboard for quick adjustments. Later instances are supposed to be local, often using masks, for which keyboard setting will not be enough if possible at all.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "All module instances that have an instance name set will get a private focusing action attached to the module (but none attached to its controls), using a path prefixed with `Modules/Instances/` and followed by `Module name/Instance name`. Since instance names are a property of each editing history, and not constant during the lifecycle of the whole application, this focusing action cannot be assigned a key shortcut and is accessible only from the global action search.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When renaming the base instance of a module, it gets its own `Modules/Instances/` focusing action, but this is only a duplicate of the default one from `Modules/`, and it doesn't change the behaviour of the default actions (focusing the module and its controls).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "System integration"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Certain shortcuts are already reserved by your operating system and desktop environment. It may be a good idea to let the OS listen and decode those before they are passed on to Ansel (in which case, they are consumed and never passed on). To do so, go into the [Preferences](../preferences-settings/miscellaneous.md) popup, to _miscellaneous_, and check the box _let the OS mask Ansel shortcuts_.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Key variants and alternatives"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The numpad key alternatives (digits, enter, delete, home, end, insert, page up/down, arrows) are decoded as the non-numpad alternatives by all shortcuts, which means numbers will be seen as the same object no matter where they are typed. They respond to numlock as they should (numlock on enables digits and arithmetic operators, numlock off enables navigation).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/getting-started/keyboard.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel allows single-key shortcuts, without modifier, which is non-standard and may cause issues with some keys. For this reason, shortcuts are temporarily disabled when a text entry is active. If that causes issues on your system, consider using typical double-key shortcuts (with a modifier).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/preferences-settings/security.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Security"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/security.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Control whether warning messages are shown before undertaking certain activities.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/preferences-settings/security.md
#: content/preferences-settings/lighttable.md
#: content/preferences-settings/general.md
#: content/preferences-settings/darkroom.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/blurs.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "General"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/security.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ask before removing images from the library\n: Always ask before removing image information from Ansel's library database, where the xmp file is retained (default on).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/security.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ask before deleting images from disk\n: Always ask before deleting an image file (default on).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/security.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ask before discarding history stack\n: Always ask before discarding the history stack of an image (default on).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/security.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ask before deleting a mask shape\n: Always ask before a [drawn mask](../views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md) shape is permanently deleted, from the [shape manager](../views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md) or from a module's mask list (default on). The confirmation dialog carries an _Always ask_ checkbox that writes back to this setting.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/security.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Try to use trash when deleting images\n: Instead of physically deleting images from disk, attempt to put them into the system's trash bin (default on).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/security.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ask before moving images from film roll folder\n: Always ask before moving an image file (default on).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/security.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ask before copying images to new film roll folder\n: Always ask before copying an image file to a new location (default on).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/security.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ask before removing empty folders\n: Always ask before removing any empty folder. This can happen after moving or deleting images (default off).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/security.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ask before deleting a tag\n: Always ask before deleting a tag from an image (default on).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/security.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ask before deleting a style\n: Always ask before deleting a style (default on).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/security.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ask before deleting a preset\n: Always ask before deleting a preset (default on).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/security.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ask before exporting in overwrite mode\n: Always ask before [exporting](../views/toolboxes/export.md) images in overwrite mode.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/preferences-settings/security.md content/views/toolboxes/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Other"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/security.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Password storage backend to use\n: The backend to use for password storage. Options: “auto” (default), “none”, “libsecret”, “kwallet”.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/security.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Executable for playing audio files\n: Define an external program which is used in the lighttable view to play audio files that some cameras record to keep notes for images (default “aplay”).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/lighttable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Control functionality in the [lighttable](../views/lighttable/_index.md) view and modules.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/lighttable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "hide built-in presets for utility modules\n: If enabled, only user-defined presets will be shown in presets menu for utility modules -- built-in presets will be hidden (default off).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/lighttable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "show image time with milliseconds\n: Choose whether to include milliseconds when displaying time values (default off). If set, milliseconds are shown in the [image information](../views/toolboxes/image-information.md) module and can also be used in the [geotagging](../views/toolboxes/geotagging.md) module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/preferences-settings/lighttable.md
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/thumbnails.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Thumbnails"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/lighttable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "use raw file instead of embedded JPEG from size\n: When generating thumbnails for images that have not yet been processed in the darkroom, if the thumbnail size is greater than this value, generate it by processing the raw image data. If the thumbnail is below this size, use the JPEG preview image embedded in the raw file. Once an image has been processed in the darkroom, thumbnails will always be generated from raw data (you can revert back to the JPEG preview by discarding history). To render thumbnails with the best quality choose \"always\".\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/lighttable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "high quality processing from size\n: If the thumbnail size is greater than this value and is being generated from raw data, it will be processed using the full quality rendering path, which is better but slower (default 720p). To render thumbnails with the best quality, choose \"always\".\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/lighttable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "delimiters for size categories\n: Size categories are used to allow different thumbnail overlays to be shown depending on the thumbnail size. A pipe delimited set of values defines at what image sizes the category changes. The default value of \"120|400\" means that there will be 3 categories of thumbnail: 0-120px, 120-400px and >400px.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/preferences-settings/_index.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Preferences & Settings"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/_index.md
