msgid "Any altered settings (i.e. those that differ from their default state) are highlighted with a bullet beside them. If you change a setting that needs a restart to take effect, a message will appear as a reminder after you exit the preferences dialog.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Double click on a setting's label to reset to its default value.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The preferences dialog can be closed by clicking on the close button in your window manager or pressing the Escape key.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/_index.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Performance"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Memory & performance tuning"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Memory requirements"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Processing a raw image in Ansel requires a great deal of system memory. A simple calculation makes this clear: For a 20 megapixel image, Ansel requires a 4x32-bit floating point cell to store each pixel, meaning that each full image of this size will require approximately 300MB of memory just to store the image data. In order to actually process this image through a given module, Ansel needs at least two buffers (input and output) of this size, with more complex modules potentially requiring several additional buffers for intermediate data. Without further optimization, anything between 600MB and 3GB of memory might be required to store and process image data as the pixelpipe executes. On top of this is Ansel's code segment, the code and data of any dynamically-linked system libraries, as well as further buffers that Ansel uses to store intermediate states (cache) for quick access during interactive work.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "All in all, Ansel requires _at least_ 4GB of physical RAM plus 4 to 8GB of additional swap space to run but it will perform better the more memory you have.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "As well as executing on your CPU, many Ansel modules also have OpenCL implementations that can take full advantage of the parallel processing offered by your graphics card (GPU). Similarly, the more GPU memory you have, the better Ansel will perform.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Tiling"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If Ansel does not have sufficient memory to process the entire image in one go, modules may choose to use a \"tiling strategy\", wherein the image is split into smaller parts (tiles) which are processed independently, and then stitched back together at the end. While this allows images to be processed with a much smaller memory footprint, it does also come with some down-sides:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Tiling is always slower -- sometimes up to 10x slower, though for some modules the difference is negligible,\n- Tiling is not technically possible for some modules because of the nature of the underlying algorithms\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "For most systems, tiling will probably only be used for full-sized image exports, with interactive work in the darkroom being processed more efficiently. For best performance (and avoidance of tiling modes) you should run Ansel alongside as few other applications as possible and configure Ansel to use as much of your system and GPU memory as you can.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Performance tuning"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "There are a number of configuration parameters that can help you to fine-tune your system's performance. Some of these parameters are available in [Preferences > Processing > CPU, GPU, Memory](../processing.md#cpu-gpu-memory) and others need to be modified directly in Ansel's configuration file (found in `$HOME/.config/Ansel/Anselrc`).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This section provides some guidance on how to adjust these settings.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "How to test"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In order to determine how much your modifications improve (or not) Ansel's performance, you will need one or more sample images to test with, and a method of assessing the speed of the pixelpipe.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "For sample images, you are advised to use some of the more intensive modules, such as [diffuse or sharpen](../../views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md) or [denoise (profiled)](../../views/darkroom/modules/denoise-profiled.md). Exports are likely to have more consistent and comparable timings between pipe runs than interactive work (and will also push your hardware more).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In order to obtain profiling information you need to start Ansel from a terminal with `Ansel -d opencl -d perf`. If you want more information about tiling you should use `Ansel -d opencl -d tiling -d perf`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Each time the pixelpipe is processed (when you change module parameters, zoom, pan, export etc.) you will see (in your terminal session) the total time spent in the pixelpipe and the time spent in each of the OpenCL kernels. The most reliable value is the total time spent the in pixelpipe and you should use this to assess your changes.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Note**: The timings given for each individual module are unreliable when running the OpenCL pixelpipe asynchronously (see _asyncronous mode_ below).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "To allow for efficient processing with OpenCL it is essential that the GPU is kept busy. Any interrupts or a stalled data flow will add to the total processing time. This is especially important for the small image buffers used during interactive work, which can be processed quickly by a fast GPU. However, even short-term stalls of the pixelpipe can easily become a bottleneck.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "On the other hand Ansel's performance during file exports is more or less only governed by the speed of the algorithms and the horse-power of your GPU. Short-term stalls will not have a noticeable effect on the total time of an export.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel resources"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The \"Ansel resources\" preference (in [Preferences > Processing > CPU, GPU, Memory](../processing.md#cpu-gpu-memory)) allows you to choose between four different approaches to allocating your system's resources to Ansel. Each of these options controls multiple individual parameters, which are defined independently in `$HOME/.config/Ansel/Anselrc`. You can amend any of these directly within your Anselrc file to tweak values for your selected resource level, though you cannot add your own custom resource level to the preferences drop-down.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Each of the four \"Ansel resources\" options are defined as follows:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "resource_default=512 8 128 700\nresource_large=700 16 128 900\nresource_small=128 4 64 400\nresource_unrestricted=16384 1024 128 900\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "More generally, these can be represented as `resource_level=a b c d` where `a` - `d` are defined as follows:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "a. system memory for module processing\n: The maximum amount of system memory made available for module processing. Lower values force memory-hungry modules to process images with an increasing number of tiles. This number is a fraction of the total amount of system memory, divided by 1024. For example, on a system with 16GB of total system memory the amount assigned by `resource_default` (in GB) is `16 * 512 / 1024`, or 8GB of system RAM.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "b. minimum tiling buffer size\n: The minimum size of a single tiling buffer, similarly expressed as a fraction of total system memory. For example, on a system with 16GB of total system memory the amount assigned by `resource_default` (in GB) is `16 * 8 / 1024`, or 0.125GB of system RAM. Note that this setting is largely historic and is no longer of much practical use -- you are advised to leave it at its default value.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "c. thumbnail cache memory\n: The amount of memory to use for the thumbnail cache. Again, this is expressed as a fraction of total system memory and, on a 16GB system, the amount assigned by `resource_default` is `16 * 128 / 1024`, or 2GB of system RAM.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "d. OpenCL (GPU) memory\n: The maximum amount of GPU memory made available for module processing. As with system memory, lower values will force memory-hungry modules to process images with an increasing number of tiles. Your GPU memory will likely also be used by other applications on your system. However, in contrast to system memory, your GPU is not able to take advantage of swap files and it can be difficult for Ansel to know exactly how much memory is available at a given time. If this parameter is set too high, Ansel could be forced to fall back to CPU processing (which will be significantly slower). For this reason, the GPU memory parameter fraction also includes an extra 400MB of headroom in an attempt to avoid over-allocation of memory. For example, on a GPU with 6GB of memory, Ansel will use approximately `(6 - 0.4) * 700 / 1024`, or 3.8GB of GPU RAM when using the `resource_default` level.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In addition to the resource levels presented in the UI the following options can be set via the command-line (e.g. `Ansel --conf resourcelevel=\"notebook\"`). These modes are designed for debugging tiling issues and testing performance of common systems on larger development machines. The following options are provided:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- \"mini\" (1GB ram, 2MB single buffer, 128MB thumbnail cache, 200MB OpenCL memory)\n- \"notebook\" (4GB ram, 32MB  single buffer, 512MB thumbnail cache, 1GB OpenCL memory)\n- \"reference\" (8GB ram, 32MB single buffer, 512MB thumbnail cache, 2GB OpenCL memory)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Tuning GPU memory usage"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If you want to make maximal use of your GPU memory for OpenCL, you have three options:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Choose the \"large\" resource level. For a 6GB card, this will use approximately 5GB of GPU memory, leaving 1GB for the rest of your system.\n- Alter Anselrc to increase the last number (the OpenCL memory fraction) for your selected resource level. For example, increasing the OpenCL memory fraction to 950 would increase the available memory on a 6GB GPU to approximately 5.3GB.\n- Set [preferences > processing > cpu / gpu / memory > tune OpenCL performance](../processing.md#cpu-gpu-memory) to \"memory size\", which will use all of your device's memory, less a 400MB headroom. Please see the [section below](#id-specific-opencl-configuration) for other options related to this setting.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Device-specific OpenCL configuration"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The default Ansel settings should deliver a reasonable GPU performance on most systems. However, if you want to try to optimize things further, this section describes the relevant configuration parameters (all of which are set in your Anselrc file).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Since darktable 4.0 most of the OpenCL-related options are managed with a \"per device\" strategy. The configuration parameter for each device looks like:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`cldevice_v4_quadrortx4000=0 250 0 16 16 1024 0 0 0.017853`\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "or, more generally\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`cldevice_version_canonicalname=a b c d e f g h i`\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "An entry will be automatically created in Anselrc for each newly-detected device when you launch Ansel for the first time, with the correct canonical device name and version number. The parameters `a` - `i` are defined as follows and can be manually edited:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "a. avoid atomics\n: _1 = avoid atomics; 0 = use atomics_\n: Atomic operations in OpenCL are a special method of data synchronization and are only used in a few modules. Unfortunately, some old AMD/ATI devices are extremely slow in processing atomics and, on these cards, it is better to process the affected modules on the CPU rather than accepting an ultra-slow GPU codepath. Set this parameter to 1 if you experience slow processing within modules like [_local contrast_](../../views/darkroom/modules/local-contrast.md) or if you get intermittent system freezes. Please note that this should not affect any card manufactured since 2015.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "b. micro nap\n: _default 250_\n: In an ideal case you will keep your GPU busy at 100% when processing the pixelpipe. However, if your GPU is also required to update your screen, and Ansel is using it at 100%, there may not be sufficient time left for this task. This will usually manifest as jerky GUI updates on panning, zooming or when moving sliders. To resolve this issue Ansel can add small pauses into its pixelpipe processing so that the GPU can catch its breath and perform GUI related activities. The \"micro nap\" parameter controls the duration of these pauses in microseconds. On current systems you are pretty safe with the default value, even for integrated graphics cards. If you are using multiple devices or you are not using your discrete GPU for drawing on your screen, this value can be set to 0 for the non-desktop device.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "c. pinned memory\n: _0 = use gui to select mode; 1 = enforce pinned transfer; 2 = disable pinned transfer_\n: During tiling huge amounts of memory need to be transferred between host and device. On some devices direct memory transfers to and from an arbitrary host memory region may give a large performance penalty. This is especially noticeable when exporting large images on smaller graphics cards or while using newer modules like [_diffuse or sharpen_](../../views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md) or the _guided laplacians_ mode in the [_highlight reconstruction_](../../views/darkroom/modules/highlight-reconstruction.md) module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": There is no safe method or general rule to predict whether or not this parameter will provide a performance benefit, so you will have to experiment for yourself. This mode can also be set globally by setting the \"tune OpenCL performance\" option to \"memory transfer\" (in [Preferences > Processing > CPU, GPU, Memory](../processing.md#cpu-gpu-memory)), in which case this parameter should be set to 0. Otherwise, you can enable/disable it at a device level using this parameter.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "d. clroundup wh / e. clroundup ht\n: These parameters should be left at this default value -- testing has not shown any benefit to using other values.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "f. number of event handles\n: Event handles are used by Ansel to monitor the success/failure of kernels and provide profiling info even if the pixelpipe is executed asynchronously. The number of event handles is a limited resource of your OpenCL driver -- while they can be recycled, there is a limited number that can be used at the same time. Unfortunately, there is no way to find out what the resource limits are for a given device, so Ansel uses a very conservative guess of 128 by default. On most current devices and drivers you can expect a number of up to 1024 to be safe and lead to slightly better OpenCL performance. If your driver runs out of free handles you will experience failing OpenCL kernels with error message `CL_OUT_OF_RESOURCES` or even crashes or system freezes.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": A value of 0 will block Ansel from using any event handles. This will prevent Ansel from properly monitoring the success of your OpenCL kernels but saves some driver overhead leading to a better performance. The consequence is that any failures will likely lead to garbled output without Ansel noticing. This is only recommended if you know for sure that your system runs rock-solid.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "g. asynchronous mode\n: _1 = use asynchronous mode; 0 = don't use_\n: This flag controls how often Ansel blocks the OpenCL pixelpipe to get a status on success/failure of the kernels that have been run. For optimum latency set this to 1, so that Ansel runs the pixelpipe asynchronously and tries to use as few interrupts/events as possible. If you experience OpenCL errors like failing kernels, reset the parameter to 0 (the default). This will cause Ansel to interrupt after each module so that you can more easily isolate any problems. Issues have been reported with some older AMD/ATI cards (like the HD57xx) which can produce garbled output if this parameter is set to 1. If in doubt, leave it at its default of 0.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "h. disable device\n: _0 = enable device; 1 = disable device_\n: If Ansel detects a malfunctioning device it will automatically mark it as such by setting this parameter to 1. If you have a device that reports a lot of errors you can manually disable it by setting this field to 1.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "i. benchmark\n: When Ansel detects a new device on your system it will do a small benchmark and store the result here. You can change this back to 0 to force Ansel to redo the benchmark but in most cases **you should not edit this setting**.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Note**: if Ansel detects a \"buggy\" device configuration key it will be rewritten back to default values.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Id-specific OpenCL configuration"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A second device-specific configuration key is also provided, which takes into account both the device name **and** the device id (just in case you have two identical devices). In this case, the usual key name `cldevice_version_canonicalname` is followed by `_idX` with X being the device id. For example, if the above example device was referred to as device 0, the second configuration setting would (by default) be `cldevice_v4_quadrortx4000_id0=400`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This configuration key currently only has a single parameter defined:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "forced headroom (default 400)\n: The amount of memory (in MB) that will **not** be used by Ansel during OpenCL processing. This setting is only valid if you set [preferences > processing > tune OpenCL performance](../processing#cpu-gpu-memory) to \"memory size\".\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": If you set this parameter to zero (`0`) then, on the first run of a pixelpipe, Ansel will attempt to determine how much GPU memory is actually available and use this (with a safety-margin of 100MB) as the maximum amount of memory that Ansel will use, for the remainder of your session. This is usually safe unless you start other applications (that use a reasonable amount of GPU memory) while Ansel is running. Otherwise, use of this option could lead to out-of-memory errors, which will cause Ansel to fall back to CPU, significantly reducing performance. You may switch this option off and on again to prompt Ansel to perform its memory calculation again (at the start of the next pipe run). Note that there are known issues with memory auto-detection on newer Nvidia drivers so auto-detection should be used with care and is therefore disabled by default.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": If you are certain that no apps (or your OS) make use of the specific device you can set this parameter to 1 for the otherwise-unused device so that Ansel will use all of that device's memory.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": The default of 400MB should be fine for most systems. If you find you run into performance problems due to Ansel falling back to CPU, try changing it to 600 or disabling \"tune for memory size\".\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Other configuration keys"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The following additional configuration keys are also available in Anselrc:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "cldevice\\_version\\_canonicalname\\_building\n: This option is used when compiling OpenCL kernels and may be provided for performance tuning or to work around bugs. You must remove any existing kernels in order to recompile them with the new options. Provide an empty string to recompile without any options. Remove the setting entirely to recompile with default options, default is `-cl-fast-relaxed-math`\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "opencl\\_synch\\_cache\n: If set to \"true\", this parameter will force Ansel to fetch image buffers from your GPU after each module and store them in its pixelpipe cache. This is a resource consuming operation, but can make sense depending on your GPU (including if the GPU is rather slow). In this case Ansel might in fact save some time when module parameters have changed, as it can go back to some cached intermediate state and reprocess only part of the pixelpipe. In many cases this parameter should be set to \"active module\" (the default), which will only cache the input of the currently-focused module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/multiple-devices.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Multiple devices"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/multiple-devices.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The scheduling of OpenCL devices can be optimized on most systems using the “OpenCL scheduling profile” settings. However, if your system is equipped with more than one GPU, you might want to set the relative device priority manually. To do this you need to select the “default” scheduling profile and change the settings in the “opencl\\_device\\_priority” configuration parameter.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/multiple-devices.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "It is important to understand how Ansel uses OpenCL devices. Each processing sequence of an image -- to convert an input to the final output using a history stack -- is run in a pixelpipe. There are four different types of pixelpipe in Ansel. One type is responsible for processing the center image view (or full view) in darkroom mode, another pixelpipe processes the preview image (navigation window). There can be one of each of these two pixelpipes running at any time, with the full and preview pixelpipes running in parallel. In addition there can be multiple parallel pixelpipes performing file exports as well as multiple parallel pixelpipes generating thumbnails. If an OpenCL device is available, Ansel dynamically allocates it to one specific pixelpipe for one run and releases it afterwards.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/multiple-devices.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The computational demand varies significantly depending on the type of pixelpipe being executed. The preview image and thumbnails are low resolution and can be processed quickly, whereas processing the center image view is more demanding. A full export pixelpipe is more demanding still.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/multiple-devices.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The configuration parameter “opencl\\_device\\_priority” holds a string with the following structure: `a,b,c.../k,l,m.../o,p,q.../x,y,z...`. Each letter represents one specific OpenCL device. There are four fields in the parameter string separated by a slash, each representing one type of pixelpipe. `a,b,c...` defines the devices that are allowed to process the center image (full) pixelpipe. Likewise devices `k,l,m...` can process the preview pixelpipe, devices `o,p,q...` the export pixelpipes and finally devices `x,y,z...` the thumbnail pixelpipes. An empty field means that no OpenCL device may serve this type of pixelpipe.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/multiple-devices.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel has an internal numbering system, whereby the first available OpenCL device receives the number 0. All further devices are numbered consecutively. This number, together with the device name, is displayed when you start Ansel with `Ansel -d opencl`. You can specify a device either by number or by name (upper/lower case and whitespace do not matter). If you have more than one device with the same name you need to use the device numbers in order to differentiate them.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/multiple-devices.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A device specifier can be prefixed with an exclamation mark `!`, in which case the device is excluded from processing a given pixelpipe. You can also use an asterisk `*` as a wildcard, representing all devices not previously explicitly mentioned in that group.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/multiple-devices.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Sequence order within a group matters -- Ansel will read the list from left to right and whenever it tries to allocate an OpenCL device to a pixelpipe it will scan the devices in that order, taking the first free device it finds.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/multiple-devices.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If a pixelpipe process is about to be started and all GPUs in the corresponding group are busy, Ansel automatically processes the image on the CPU by default. You can enforce GPU processing by prefixing the list of allowed GPUs with a plus sign `+`. In this case Ansel will not use the CPU but rather suspend processing until the next permitted OpenCL device is available.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/multiple-devices.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel's default setting for “opencl\\_device\\_priority” is `*/!0,*/*/*`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/multiple-devices.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Any detected OpenCL device is permitted to process the center view image. The first OpenCL device (0) is not permitted to process the preview pixelpipe. As a consequence, if there is only one GPU available on your system, the preview pixelpipe will always be processed on the CPU, keeping your single GPU exclusively for the more demanding center image view. This is a reasonable setting for most systems. No such restrictions apply to export and thumbnail pixelpipes.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/multiple-devices.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The default is a good choice if you have only one device. If you have several devices it forms a reasonable starting point. However, as your devices might have quite different levels of processing power, it makes sense to invest some time optimizing your priority list.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/multiple-devices.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Here is an example. Let's assume we have a system with two devices, a fast Radeon HD7950 and an older and slower GeForce GTS450. Ansel (started with `Ansel -d opencl`) will report the following devices:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/multiple-devices.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "[opencl_init] successfully initialized.\n[opencl_init] here are the internal numbers and names of\n                          OpenCL devices available to Ansel:\n[opencl_init]           0       'GeForce GTS 450'\n[opencl_init]           1       'Tahiti'\n[opencl_init] FINALLY: opencl is AVAILABLE on this system.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/multiple-devices.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Here, the GeForce GTS 450 is detected as the first device and the Radeon HD7950 ('Tahiti') as the second. This order will normally not change unless the hardware or driver configuration is modified, but it's better to use device names rather than numbers to be on the safe side.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/multiple-devices.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "As the GTS450 is slower than the HD7950, an optimized \"opencl\\_device\\_priority\" could look like: `!GeForce GTS450,*/!Tahiti,*/Tahiti,*/Tahiti,*`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/multiple-devices.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The GTS450 is explicitly excluded from processing the center image pixelpipe; this is reserved to “all” other devices (i.e. the HD7950/Tahiti). Conversely, for the preview pixelpipe, the Tahiti is excluded, so that only the GTS450 is permitted to do the work.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/multiple-devices.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "For file export and thumbnail generation we want all hands on deck. However, Ansel should first check whether the Tahiti device is free, because it's faster. If it is not free, then all other devices -- in fact only the GTS450 -- are checked.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/_index.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Opencl"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/still-doesnt-work.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Opencl still does not run for me"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/still-doesnt-work.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "As has been mentioned, OpenCL systems come with a huge variety of setups: different GPU manufacturers and models, varying amounts of GPU memory, different drivers, different distributions etc..\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/still-doesnt-work.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Many of the potential problems will only appear with very specific combinations of these factors. As the Ansel developers only have access to a small fraction of these variations, please understand that we might not be able to fix your specific problem. There is not much we can do if we are unable to reproduce your issue.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/still-doesnt-work.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If you are having issues with a specific device (and you have other OpenCL devices available) the first thing to do would be to try disabling just that device -- see [memory & performance tuning](../mem-performance.md#device-specific-opencl-configuration) for more information.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/still-doesnt-work.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If nothing else helps, the best option is probably to start Ansel with\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/still-doesnt-work.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`Ansel --disable-opencl`\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/still-doesnt-work.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In the end there is nothing in Ansel that only runs on GPU. Don't let the lack of OpenCL discourage you -- Ansel's CPU code is also highly optimized for performance!\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/scheduling-profile.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Scheduling profile"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/scheduling-profile.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel can use the CPU and one or several OpenCL capable GPUs. Depending on the relative performance of these devices, users can choose among certain scheduling profiles to optimize performance. This is achieved by setting the configuration parameter [Preferences > Processing > CPU, GPU, Memory > OpenCL scheduling profile](../../processing#cpu-gpu-memory), which offers the following choices:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/scheduling-profile.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "default\n: If an OpenCL-capable GPU is found Ansel uses it for processing the center image view while the [navigation preview window](../../../views/toolboxes/navigation.md) is processed on the CPU in parallel. This is the preferred setting for systems with a reasonably fast CPU and a moderately fast GPU. The exact allocation of devices to the various pixelpipe types can be finetuned with the “opencl\\_device\\_priority” configuration parameter (see [multiple devices](./multiple-devices.md)).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/scheduling-profile.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "very fast GPU\n: With this scheduling profile Ansel processes the center image view and the preview window on the GPU sequentially. This is the preferred setting for systems with a GPU that strongly outperforms the CPU.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/scheduling-profile.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "multiple GPUs\n: This setting addresses systems with multiple GPUs whose relative performance does not differ significantly. Whenever a processing job is started Ansel uses any currently idle GPU but not the CPU. Users of systems with a variety of GPUs will need better control on their relative priority. They would be better off selecting the “default” profile and fine-tuning their system with the “opencl\\_device\\_priority” configuration parameter (see [multiple devices](./multiple-devices.md)).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/scheduling-profile.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "On first start-up or after any detected change in the GPU configuration of your system Ansel tries to identify the best suited profile for you. You can change it at any time in [Preferences > Processing > CPU, GPU, Memory](../../processing.md#cpu-gpu-memory).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/setting-up.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Setting up opencl"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/setting-up.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The huge diversity of systems and the marked differences between OpenCL vendors and driver versions makes it impossible to give an comprehensive overview of how to setup OpenCL. We only can give you an example, in this case for NVIDIA driver version 331.89 on Ubuntu 14.04. We hope that this will serve as a basic introduction and will help you to solve any problems specific to your setup.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/setting-up.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The principle OpenCL function flow is like this:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/setting-up.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`Ansel > libOpenCL.so > libnvidia-opencl.so.1 > kernel driver module(s) > GPU`\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/setting-up.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Ansel dynamically loads `libOpenCL.so` -- a system library that must be accessible to the system's dynamic loader (`ld.so`).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/setting-up.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- `libOpenCL.so` reads the vendor-specific information file (`/etc/OpenCL/vendors/nvidia.icd`) to find the library that contains the vendor-specific OpenCL implementation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/setting-up.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- The vendor-specific OpenCL implementation comes as a library `libnvidia-opencl.so.1` (which in our case is a symbolic link to `libnvidia-opencl.so.331.89`).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/setting-up.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- `libnvidia-opencl.so.1` needs to talk to the vendor-specific kernel modules `nvidia` and `nvidia_uvm` via device special files `/dev/nvidia0`, `/dev/nvidiactl`, and `/dev/nvidia-uvm`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/setting-up.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "At system startup the required device special files (`/dev/nvidia*`) need to be created. If this does not happen on your system by default, the easiest way to set them up and make sure all modules are loaded is by installing the `nvidia-modprobe` package.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/setting-up.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A user account that needs to make use of OpenCL from within Ansel must have read/write access to NVIDIA's device special files. On some systems these files allow world read-write access by default, which avoids permission issues but might be debatable in terms of system security. Other systems restrict the access to a user group, e.g. “video”. In this case your user account has to be member of that group.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/setting-up.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "To summarise, the packages that needed to be installed in this specific case were:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/setting-up.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "nvidia-331 (331.89-0ubuntu1~xedgers14.04.2)\nnvidia-331-dev (331.89-0ubuntu1~xedgers14.04.2)\nnvidia-331-uvm (331.89-0ubuntu1~xedgers14.04.2)\nnvidia-libopencl1-331 (331.89-0ubuntu1~xedgers14.04.2)\nnvidia-modprobe (340.24-1)\nnvidia-opencl-dev:amd64 (5.5.22-3ubuntu1)\nnvidia-opencl-icd-331 (331.89-0ubuntu1~xedgers14.04.2)\nnvidia-settings (340.24-0ubuntu1~xedgers14.04.1)\nnvidia-settings-304 (340.24-0ubuntu1~xedgers14.04.1)\nnvidia-libopencl1-331 (331.89-0ubuntu1~xedgers14.04.2)\nnvidia-opencl-dev:amd64 (5.5.22-3ubuntu1)\nnvidia-opencl-icd-331 (331.89-0ubuntu1~xedgers14.04.2)\nopencl-headers (1.2-2013.10.23-1)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/setting-up.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The list of NVIDIA related kernel modules as reported by lsmod was:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/setting-up.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "nvidia\nnvidia_uvm\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/setting-up.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The list of NVIDIA related device special files (`ls -l /dev/nvidia*`) should read like:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/setting-up.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "crw-rw-rw- 1 root root 195,   0 Jul 28 21:13 /dev/nvidia0\ncrw-rw-rw- 1 root root 195, 255 Jul 28 21:13 /dev/nvidiactl\ncrw-rw-rw- 1 root root 250,   0 Jul 28 21:13 /dev/nvidia-uvm\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/setting-up.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Beware that the major/minor numbers (e.g. `250/0` for `/dev/nvidia-uvm` in this example) may vary depending on your system.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/problems-solutions.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Possible problems & solutions"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/problems-solutions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel will detect OpenCL run-time errors automatically. On detecting an error, it will then reprocess everything on the CPU. While this will slow down processing it should not affect the end result.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/problems-solutions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "There can be various reasons why OpenCL might fail during the initialization phase. OpenCL depends on hardware requirements and on the presence of certain drivers and libraries. In addition all these have to fit in terms of maker, model and revision number. If anything does not fit (e.g. your graphics driver -- loaded as a kernel module -- does not match the version of your `libOpenCL.so`) OpenCL support will likely not be available.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/problems-solutions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In this case, the best thing to do is start Ansel from a console with `Ansel -d opencl`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/problems-solutions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This will give additional debugging output about the initialization and use of OpenCL. First, if you find a line that starts with `[opencl_init] FINALLY ...` that should tell you whether OpenCL support is available for you or not. If initialization failed, look at the messages above for anything that reads like `could not be detected` or `could not be created`. Check if there is a hint about where it failed.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/problems-solutions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Here are a few cases that have been observed in the past:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/problems-solutions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Ansel states that no OpenCL aware graphics card is detected or that the available memory on your GPU is too low and the device is discarded. In that case you might need to buy a new card if you really want OpenCL support.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/problems-solutions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Ansel finds your `libOpenCL.so` but then tell you that it couldn't get a platform. NVIDIA drivers will often give error code -1001 in this case. This happens because `libOpenCL.so` is only a wrapper library. For the real work further vendor-specific libraries need to be loaded. This has failed for some reason. There is a structure of files in `/etc/OpenCL` on your system that `libOpenCL.so` consults to find these libraries. See if you can find something fishy in there and try to fix it. Often the required libraries cannot be found by your system's dynamic loader. Giving full path names might help.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/problems-solutions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Ansel states that a context could not be created. This often indicates a version mismatch between the loaded graphics driver and libOpenCL. Check if you have left-over kernel modules or graphics libraries from an older installation and take appropriate action. When in doubt, perform a clean reinstall of your graphics driver. Sometimes, immediately after a driver update, the loaded kernel driver does not match the newly installed libraries. In this case reboot your system before trying again.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/problems-solutions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Ansel crashes during startup. This can happen if your OpenCL setup is completely broken or if your driver/library contains a severe bug. If you can't fix it, you can still use Ansel with option `--disable-opencl`, which will skip the entire OpenCL initialization step.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/problems-solutions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Ansel fails to compile its OpenCL source files at run-time. In this case you will see a number of error messages looking like typical compiler errors. This could indicate an incompatibility between your OpenCL implementation and Ansel's interpretation of the standard. In that case please raise an issue on [github](https://github.com/darktable-org/darktable/issues/new/choose) and we will try to assist. Please also report if you see significant differences between CPU and GPU processing of an image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/problems-solutions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When the GPU result differs from the CPU result"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/problems-solutions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The cases above are about OpenCL failing to start. A rarer and more confusing situation is\nOpenCL starting perfectly and then producing *different pixels* from the CPU. Ansel computes the\nsame thing either way, so a visible difference means something below Ansel is not doing the\narithmetic it claims.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/problems-solutions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The usual culprit is the accuracy of the graphics driver's own math library. Ansel used to ask\nits kernels to be compiled with `-cl-unsafe-math-optimizations`, an option that allows a driver\nto substitute an approximate implementation for any standard mathematical function. Most drivers\ndo this harmlessly. Some do not: on Intel integrated graphics, that option makes `erf()` return\nexactly zero for small arguments and `pow()` wrong by tens of percent, which visibly corrupted\nthe [AI raw denoise](../../../views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md) module and,\nto a lesser degree, anything using gamma curves.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/problems-solutions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Current versions no longer request that option, for any vendor. **However, updating Ansel is not\nenough to fix an affected installation.** The kernel compilation options are stored per device in\n`Anselrc`, written the first time a device is seen and never overwritten afterwards, so an old\nsetting survives an upgrade. Look for:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/problems-solutions.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "cldevice_v4/<number>/<device-name>/building=...\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/problems-solutions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If the value still contains `-cl-fast-relaxed-math` or `-cl-unsafe-math-optimizations`, replace\nit with:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/problems-solutions.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "cldevice_v4/<number>/<device-name>/building=-cl-mad-enable -cl-no-signed-zeros\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/problems-solutions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "then delete the compiled kernel cache so the kernels are rebuilt with the new options:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/problems-solutions.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "rm -rf ~/.cache/ansel/cached_kernels_for_*\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/problems-solutions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Deleting the whole `building` line works too — Ansel will rewrite it with its current default.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/problems-solutions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Two tools help you decide whether this applies to you. `tools/opencl-math-accuracy.c` in the\nAnsel sources is a small standalone program (it does not link against Ansel) that scores every\nOpenCL device on your machine, under every combination of compilation options, against a\nhigh-precision reference, and prints the exact `Anselrc` line for any device that needs one. And\n[`Ansel-nn-parity`](../../../cli/ansel-nn-parity.md) checks the neural denoiser\nspecifically, telling you whether the CPU, the GPU or neither is the one disagreeing.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/problems-solutions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A few on-CPU implementations of OpenCL also exist, coming as drivers provided by INTEL or AMD. We have observed that they do not provide any speed gain versus our hand-optimized CPU code. Therefore Ansel simply discards these devices by default. This behavior can be changed by setting the configuration variable `opencl_use_cpu_devices` (in `$HOME/.config/Anselrc`) to `TRUE`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/activate-opencl.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Activating opencl in Ansel"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/activate-opencl.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Using OpenCL in Ansel requires that your PC is equipped with a suitable graphics card and that it has the required libraries in place. Most modern graphics cards from NVIDIA and AMD come with full OpenCL support. The OpenCL compiler is normally shipped as part of the proprietary graphics driver and is used as a dynamic library called `libOpenCL.so`. This library must be in a folder where it can be found by your system's dynamic linker.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/activate-opencl.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When Ansel starts, it will first try to find and load `libOpenCL.so` and, on success, check if the available graphics card comes with OpenCL support. A sufficient amount of graphics memory (1GB+) needs to be available for Ansel to take advantage of the GPU. If that check passes, Ansel tries to setup its OpenCL environment: a processing context needs to be initialized, a calculation pipeline to be started, OpenCL source code files (extension is `.cl`) needs to be read and compiled and the included routines (OpenCL kernels) need to be prepared for Ansel's modules. If all of that completes successfully, the preparation is complete.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/activate-opencl.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "By default, OpenCL support is activated in Ansel if all the above steps were successful. If you want to de-activate it you can do so in [Preferences > Processing > CPU, GPU, Memory](../../processing#cpu-gpu-memory). This configuration parameter is grayed out if the OpenCL initialization failed.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/activate-opencl.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "You can switch OpenCL support off and on at any time without requiring a restart. Depending on the type of modules you are using, you will notice the effect as a general speed-up during interactive work and export. Most modules in Ansel can take advantage of OpenCL but not all modules are demanding enough to make a noticeable difference. In order to feel a real difference, use modules like [_diffuse or sharpen_](../../../views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md), and [_denoise (profiled)_](../../../views/darkroom/modules/denoise-profiled.md).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/activate-opencl.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If you are interested in profiling statistics, you can start Ansel with command line parameters `-d opencl -d perf`. After each run of the pixelpipe you will be shown details of processing time for each module plus an even more fine-grained profile for all used OpenCL kernels.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/activate-opencl.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Apart from the speed-up you should not see any difference in the results between CPU and GPU processing. Except for some rounding errors, the results are designed to be identical. If, for some reason, Ansel fails to properly finish a GPU calculation, it will normally detect the failure and automatically (and transparently) fall back to CPU processing.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/how-opencl-works.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "How opencl works"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/how-opencl-works.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "As you can imagine, the hardware architecture of GPUs can vary significantly. There are different manufacturers, and even different generations of GPUs from the same manufacturer may not be comparable. At the same time GPU manufacturers don't normally disclose all the hardware details of their products to the public. One of the consequences of this is the need to use proprietary drivers under Linux, if you want to take full advantage of your graphics card.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/how-opencl-works.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Fortunately an industry consortium lead by The Khronos Group has developed an open, standardized interface called OpenCL, which allows your GPU to be used as a numerical processing device. OpenCL offers a C99-like programming language with a strong focus on parallel computing. An application that wants to use OpenCL will need OpenCL source code that it hands over to a hardware-specific compiler at run-time. This way applications can use OpenCL on different GPU architectures (even at the same time). All of the hardware “secrets” are hidden in this compiler and are normally not visible to the user (or the application). The compiled OpenCL code is loaded onto your GPU and -- with certain API calls -- it is ready to perform calculations for you.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/background.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "The background"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/background.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Processing high resolution images is a demanding task requiring a modern computer. In terms of both memory and CPU power, getting the best out of a typical 15, 20 or 25 Megapixel image can quickly take your computer to its limits.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/background.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel's requirements are no exception. All calculations are performed on 4 x 32bit floating point numbers. This is slower than “ordinary” 8 or 16 bit integer algebra, but eliminates all problems of tonal breaks or loss of information.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/background.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A great deal of optimization has been undertaken to make Ansel as fast as possible. If you run a current version of Ansel on a modern computer, you might not notice any “slowness”. However, there are conditions and certain modules where you will feel (or hear from the howling of your CPU fan) how much your poor multi-core processor has to struggle.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/background.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "That's where OpenCL comes in. OpenCL allows Ansel to take advantage of the enormous power of modern graphics cards. Gamers' demand for highly detailed 3D worlds in modern shooters (as well as cryptocurrency mining) has fostered rapid GPU development. AMD, NVIDIA and Co had to put enormous processing power into their GPUs to meet these demands. The result is modern graphics cards with highly parallelized GPUs that can quickly calculate surfaces and textures at high frame rates.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/performance/opencl/background.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "You are not a gamer and you don't take advantage of that power? Well, then you should at least use it in Ansel! For the task of highly parallel floating point calculations modern GPUs are much faster than CPUs. This is especially true when you want to repeat the same few processing steps millions of times. Typical use case: processing high megapixel images.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/general.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Control the overall look and feel of Ansel.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/general.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "interface language\n: Set the language of the user interface. The system default is marked with an * (needs a restart)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/general.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "theme\n: Set the theme for the user interface. Aside from any aesthetic considerations, the recommended interface color for color evaluation is middle gray. Visual perception is affected by ambient brightness, and a low user interface brightness causes all kinds of illusions. Using a dark interface to retouch photos can therefore lead to excessive retouching (abuse of contrast and saturation) and to a photo that is too dark when printed. It is therefore highly recommended that you use one of the \"grey\" themes for retouching work as these are designed so that the user interface approximates middle gray (default \"Ansel\").\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/general.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "use system font size\n: Select this option to use the font size defined by your system. If unchecked, you may enter a custom font size in the box below (default on).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/general.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "font size in points\n: If the \"use system font size\" option is switched off, enter a font size (in points) for Ansel to use. The font size will be changed immediately.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/general.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "GUI controls and text DPI\n: Adjust the global GUI resolution to rescale controls, buttons, labels, etc. Increase for a magnified GUI, decrease to fit more content in the window. Set to -1 to use the system-defined global resolution. The default is 96 DPI on most systems. (needs a restart)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/preferences-settings/general.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "CSS theme modifications"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/general.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In addition to selecting a pre-built theme you can also apply additional CSS customisations of your own to tweak the look-and-feel of Ansel.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/general.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Two different methods are provided for this:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/general.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "create a custom theme\n: If you wish to make a large number of changes to Ansel's UI you may wish to create your own theme (in a `.css` file) and place it in `$HOME/.config/ansel/themes` (or `C:\\%LOCALAPPDATA%\\ansel\\themes` on Windows). Your new theme will automatically appear in the _theme_ selection list the next time you restart Ansel.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/general.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": Please note that the structure of Ansel's internal CSS changes frequently and you may need to make significant changes to your own themes when new versions of Ansel are released. For this reason (among others) we do not recommend creating complex custom themes unless you are willing to devote a lot of time to ongoing maintenance. If your theme loads any of Ansel's pre-built themes using the `@import url` directive, note that your CSS theme file may not be portable between installations (`@import url` uses relative paths and the location of the pre-built themes is system-dependent).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/general.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "create theme tweaks\n: A text box is provided at the bottom of the general tab within which you can enter your own CSS tweaks. When using this option, Ansel will first load your selected theme (the \"base\" theme, chosen in the _theme_ drop-down) and then apply your custom CSS on top. This means that you can easily make minor alterations to the look-and-feel, while still keeping mostly up-to-date with core theme changes when a new version of Ansel is released. It also means that you can usually change your base theme without affecting your custom CSS tweaks.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/general.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": When you have finished entering your CSS, click the \"save CSS and apply\" button. This will save your CSS to `$HOME/.config/Ansel/user.css` (or `C:\\%LOCALAPPDATA%\\Ansel\\user.css` on Windows) and immediately apply it to the current Ansel session.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/general.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": If you notice any issues after applying your CSS, you can uncheck the \"modify selected theme with CSS tweaks below\" box to revert. This will immediately restore the base theme but will leave your tweaks in the editor so that you can re-edit them and try again. Simply press \"save CSS and apply\" again when you are ready to retry. This will automatically re-check the \"modify selected theme with CSS tweaks below\" checkbox and apply the new CSS.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/general.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Note**: If you have any issues while using custom CSS tweaks please retry with the \"modify selected theme with CSS tweaks below\" option unchecked to be certain that they were not caused by any of your alterations.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/preferences-settings/general.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Understanding Ansel's themes"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/general.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "All of Ansel's pre-built themes are provided as CSS files in `$Ansel/share/Ansel/themes/` (where `$Ansel` is Ansel's installation directory). The default theme is `Ansel.css` and this contains the bulk of the code used to control the look-and-feel of Ansel.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/general.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If you choose to create your own custom theme file you are advised import Ansel's existing theme files using `@import url` (this directive expects relative paths) and then apply your own customisations on top. You do not need to do this when using the CSS text box in the preferences dialog -- attempting to use `@import url` in the CSS tweaks text box will not work correctly.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/general.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Themes use the same basic CSS principles as in html browsers (with some minor exceptions -- see the [Gtk documentation](https://developer-old.gnome.org/gtk3/stable/chap-css-overview.html) for details):\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/general.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- The majority of the style properties are assigned to broad groups of UI elements, for example, Gtk buttons and text entry fields\n- Next, related groups of Ansel-specific UI elements are given _class names_ allowing them to be styled as a group\n- Finally, some unique UI elements are assigned a CSS _id_ so that they can be styled independently\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/general.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "You are encouraged to explore the existing theme and to make use of the [Gtk Inspector](https://wiki.gnome.org/Projects/GTK/Inspector) tool to figure out how to select the specific UI element (or class of elements) you wish to modify. Some experimentation will be required.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/general.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Please note that Ansel themes are grayscale by default in order that users not be distracted by strong colors while editing images. You are advised to retain this practice in your own themes and to keep the average shade as close to middle-gray as possible. In addition you are advised to review your custom CSS each time Ansel is updated, to ensure that changes to the application have not adversely affected your tweaks.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/preferences-settings/presets.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/tone-equalizer.md content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Presets"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/presets.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This menu provides an overview of the presets that are defined for Ansel's modules, and allows you to modify some of their properties.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/presets.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Pre-defined presets (those that are included by default within Ansel) are shown with a lock symbol. Their properties cannot be changed.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/presets.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "User-defined presets can be imported from exported `.dtpreset` files using the \"import\" button at the bottom of the screen. You can export _all_ user-defined presets to a single directory using the \"export\" button.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/presets.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Delete a user-defined preset by selecting it and pressing the Delete key.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/presets.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Edit a user-defined preset's properties by selecting it and pressing Enter or double-clicking. This will open a dialog that allows the preset to be edited, exported to an external `.dtpreset` file or deleted.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/preferences-settings/lua-options.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Lua options"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/lua-options.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "lua scripts installer don't show again\n: Check this box to hide the [lua scripts installer](../views/toolboxes/lua-scripts-installer.md) in the lighttable if no lua scripts are installed.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/preferences-settings/other-views.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Other views"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/other-views.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Enable optional views:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/other-views.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "* [map](../views/map/)\n* [slideshow](../views/slideshow/)\n* [print](../views/print/)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/other-views.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Once you restart Ansel the enabled view(s) will be available in the *Ateliers* menu.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/other-views.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "These views are all hidden by default to spare some computing resources.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/preferences-settings/storage.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Storage"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/preferences-settings/storage.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Database"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/storage.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "check for database maintenance\n: Indicates when darktable should check for database fragmentation and perform maintenance. Options are \"never\", \"on startup\", \"on close\" and \"on both\". Each of these is also available with an additional \"(don't ask)\" option to perform the checks automatically without prompting (default \"on close\").\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/storage.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "database fragmentation ratio threshold\n: Fragmentation ratio (in per cent) above which database maintenance should be performed (subject to the selection made in the option above) (default 25).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/storage.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "create database snapshot\n: Specifies how often Ansel should create database snapshots. Options are \"never\", \"once a month\", \"once a week\", \"once a day\" and \"on close\" (default \"once a week\")\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/storage.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "how many snapshots to keep\n: Number of snapshots to keep after creating a new snapshot, not counting database backups taken when moving between Ansel versions. Enter \"-1\" to store an unlimited number of snapshots. (default 10)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/preferences-settings/storage.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Xmp"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/storage.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "write sidecar file for each image\n: XMP files provide a redundant method of saving the changes that you have made to an image, in addition to the changes saved to Ansel's database. This option allows you to choose when to write these files. Choose from:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/storage.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": - _never_: Don't write XMP files. This can be useful if you are running multiple version of Ansel for development/testing purposes but is not normally recommended\n: - _on import_: An XMP file will be written as soon as you add an image to Ansel's library and will be subsequently updated each time you edit it\n: - _after edit_: An XMP file will be written the first time you perform an edit on an image and will be updated on each subsequent edit. Importing will not generate an XMP file by default.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/storage.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": It's strongly recommended that you choose either \"on import\" or \"after edit\". XMP files provide a useful fail-safe so that you don't lose data if your database becomes corrupted. Backing up your raw file plus the accompanying XMP file will allow you to fully restore your work at a later date by re-importing your edit history back into Ansel (default \"on import\").\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/storage.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "store xmp tags in compressed format\n: Entries in XMP tags can get rather large and may exceed the available space to store the history stack in some output files on export. This option allows binary XMP tags to be compressed in order to save space. Available options are “never”, “always”, and “only large entries” (default).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/storage.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "look for updated xmp files on startup\n: Scan all previously-known XMP files on startup and check if any have been updated in the meantime by some other software. If updated XMP files are found, a menu is opened for the user to choose if the XMP content should be used to update the database or the other way around (replacing Ansel's database entries by the XMP file contents). Activating this option also causes Ansel to check for text sidecar files that have been added after import time (default off). This makes the application take more time to load, especially if your files are hosted remotely (on a server or on a NAS).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/preferences-settings/processing.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Processing"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/processing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Control how images are processed.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/preferences-settings/processing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Image processing"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/processing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "always use LittleCMS 2 to apply output color profile\n: If this option is activated, Ansel will use the LittleCMS 2 system library to apply the output color profile instead of its own internal routines. This is significantly slower than the default but might give more accurate results in some cases.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/processing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": If the given ICC is LUT-based or contains both a LUT and a matrix, Ansel will use LittleCMS 2 to render the colors regardless of this parameter's value (default off).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/processing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "pixel interpolator (warp)\n: The pixel interpolator used for rotation, lens correction, liquify, crop and final scaling.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/processing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": Whenever we scale or distort an image we have to choose a pixel interpolation algorithm (see [wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Image_scaling) for details). For warping modules, Ansel offers bilinear, bicubic or mitchell. In general, mitchell is better both at preserving sharpness and at preventing halos.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/processing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "pixel interpolator (scaling)\n: The pixel interpolator used for scaling. The same options are provided as for the warp modules.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/processing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": lanczos3 can cause pixel overshoots leading to artefacts but sometimes gives a more crisp visual appearance. This option is therefore only provided for transforming (scaling) algorithms and is the default value.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/processing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "3D lut root folder\n: Define the root folder (and sub-folders) containing Lut files used by the [_lut 3D_](../views/darkroom/modules/lut-3D.md) module\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/preferences-settings/processing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "CPU, GPU, Memory"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/preferences-settings/processing.md
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Settings"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/processing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "CPU cores\n: Number of CPU cores (physical and virtual) in use. `-1` will use all detected cores (physical and virtual). Depending on your hardware, you may want to try setting this value equal to the number of physical cores of your CPU, which may speed-up memory I/O, since memory transfer usually is our bottleneck in image processing. This will be irrelevant for most users, aside from debugging issues. This value does not affect modules using OpenCL when OpenCL is enabled.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/processing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Background workers\n: Number of background threads used to process thumbnail rendering pipelines, import, export, etc. Increasing this value will not increase memory usage proportionnaly, because only one pipeline at a time is allowed to run, but it may help hiding filesystem I/O latencies when loading image files. The maximum value here is defined by your operating system, and is typically 1024 or 2048.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/processing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Memory headroom for OS/applications (MiB)\n: This is the amount of RAM space that Ansel will __never use__, and leave to the operating system and other applications. To set it, you can reboot your computer and open a system monitoring application, then measure how much RAM space the idle OS is using. If you like to play videos or music in background, while retouching in Ansel, you will need to plan for this too and include the RAM usage of your player into this headroom. __Setting this value too low will result in crashes of the application__ because the OS will kill the Ansel process when it reaches RAM saturation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/processing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Memory reserved for lighttable thumbnail cache (MiB)\n: This is the amount of RAM space that Ansel will allocate to rendered thumbnails displayed in the [lighttable](../views/lighttable/_index.md) and [filmstrip](../views/toolboxes/filmstrip.md). You should set this value according to the typical number of images you have in a folder and how large they appear in the lighttable :\n    - A 360×225 px image uses 0.35 MiB,\n    - A 720×450 px image uses 1.24 MiB,\n    - A 1440×900 px image uses 5 MiB,\n    - A 6000×4000 px image uses 92 MiB\n: The default value of 1000 MiB can hold 2800 small thumbnails, or 805 medium thumbnails, or 200 large thumbnails, or 10 images at 24 Mpx full resolution (for full-resolution preview in lighttable).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/processing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Maximum RAW resolution to edit\n: Set this value to the resolution of your best camera. Ansel will then reserve at all time a sufficient RAM space to hold 4 internal full-resolution buffers, which will be enough to avoid tiling in most modules using intermediate copies (when not using OpenCL).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/processing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< warning >}}\nAnsel uses the total memory available on the system, subtracts the headroom size, the thumbnail cache size, the size of the 4 full-resolution RAW buffers and uses what remains for its pixelpipe cache. This cache is used to store the intermediate output of modules, as to prevent recomputing when not necessary. It will make Ansel RAM consumption increase a lot, which is no issue. The cache will be automatically shrunk if the application has trouble allocating new buffers. However, the operating system will not shrink it itself, so the _memory headroom_ needs to be properly set.\n{{< /warning >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/processing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\nThe pixel pipeline cache can be manually emptied using the global menu _Run_ → _Clear pipeline caches_.\n{{< /note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/processing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Pipe recompute timeout\n: When doing value changes on sliders and comboxes, in darkroom [modules](../views/darkroom/modules/_index.md), the changes are sent to the pixel pipeline to re-render the output once every _N_ milliseconds. _N_ is the _pipe recompute timeout_. It will ensure that intermediate value changes (when dragging a cursor) don't trigger a new (computationnaly-expensive) recomputation, at the expense of making the GUI feel more or less _laggy_ or _responsive_. Powerful hardware can tolerate values as low as 50 ms, but weak hardware (or battery mode) may benefit from values as high as 200 ms to spare useless intermediate recomputations.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/processing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Enable disk backend for thumbnail cache\n: Saves rendered [lighttable](../views/lighttable/_index.md) thumbnails to the disk cache, in the current user's home folder. This will prevent recomputing them on the next startup, but may create privacy issues if you are editing private pictures on a publicly shared computer.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/processing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Activate OpenCL support\n: Process darkroom [modules](../views/darkroom/modules/_index.md) that support it on the GPU using OpenCL. This option will be available only if an OpenCL driver and device were found on your system.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/processing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "GPU vRAM headroom (MiB)\n: Amount of video RAM that Ansel will __never use__ on the GPU, and leave to other applications using hardware graphics acceleration (OpenGL, Vulkan, VDPAU, CUDA, etc.).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/processing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "System library with OpenCL runtime\n: Ansel will typically detect automatically the OpenCL drivers on your system, so you can leave this option empty. But if you have several conflicting OpenCL libraries, or your runtime is installed in an unusual place, you can define here the path to the OpenCL runtime to load.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/preferences-settings/processing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Testing your configuration"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/processing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- To diagnose OpenCL issues, start `ansel -d opencl`\n- To benchmark performance and test different memory configurations, start `ansel -d perf`,\n- To diagnose memory and cache size issues, start `ansel -d memory -d cache -d pipe`\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/preferences-settings/processing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Libraw"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/processing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel uses the Rawspeed library by default to decode raw image files. Rawspeed is flawlessly integrated in Ansel, but does not support Canon `.CR3` files yet. For this reason, a basic support of Libraw has been implemented such that owners of recent Canon cameras can still decode their files. Libraw also tends to support new formats faster than Rawspeed.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/processing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The options of this section allow users to force the use of Libraw for any picture they want, using rules based on file extension and camera/vendor EXIF metadata. The feature is brittle and unsafe in general because we don't check and sanitize every possible flavour of encoding.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/processing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Supported files\n:  * Canon `.CR3`\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/processing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Files that seem to be working\n:   * Olympus `.ORF`\n    * Hasselblad `.3FR`\n    * Nikon `.NEF` __non-compressed__\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/processing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Files that definitely don't work and make the software crash\n:   * Nikon sRAW and compressed `.NEF`\n    * Phase One `.IIQ`\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/processing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< warning >}}\nThe library used to decode files is remembered at the application level, globally, not for each file. Editing pictures decoded by Libraw may not produce the exact same result if you revert to Rawspeed in the future. You should really stick to Rawspeed whenever possible.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/processing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "You are strongly advised to save any edited picture to 16 bits TIFF at full resolution using Rec2020 linear color space as an archival backup of your work, and not rely on the consistency of the non-destructive editing result in the future.\n{{</ warning >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/processing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Raw file extensions to load through Libraw\n: case-insensitive, coma-separated list of the file extensions. Default : `cr3`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/processing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Camera models to load through Libraw\n: case-insensitive, coma-separated list of the camera models as they appear in the [_Display metadata_](../views/toolboxes/image-information.md) module, under the _model_ field. You may have to enable this field using the preferences of the module if it does not appear in the widget.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/processing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Camera makers to load through Libraw\n: case-insensitive, coma-separated list of the camera manufacturers as they appear in the [_Display metadata_](../views/toolboxes/image-information.md) module, under the _maker_ field. You may have to enable this field using the preferences of the module if it does not appear in the widget.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/processing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\nFor Canon files using the `.CR3` format, the _model_ and _maker_ metadata are not decoded properly and left blank. You have to filter them mandatorily by file extension.\n{{</ note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/processing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "To debug this feature : \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/processing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. Start Ansel in command line using `ansel -d imageio`. For each loaded image, it will tell which library was used to decode it,\n1. If the settings you input make the software crash at startup, remove the `libraw/extensions`, `libraw/models`, `libraw/makers` configuration keys in the `anselrc` configuration file, located in `~./config/ansel` folder on Linux and Mac, or `APPDATA\\.config\\ansel` on Windows.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/preferences-settings/miscellaneous.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Miscellaneous"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/preferences-settings/miscellaneous.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Interface"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/miscellaneous.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Invert the direction of the mouse vertical scroll\n: By default, scrolling the mouse up increases the value of sliders and of the shape parameters of [drawn masks](../views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md) alike. Enable this if your OS/desktop environment is configured to scroll the view instead of the content, so that scrolling yields the value you expect. Horizontal scrolling is unaffected (default off).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/miscellaneous.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Invert the direction of the mouse horizontal scroll\n: The same inversion for horizontal scrolling, set independently of the vertical one (default off).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/miscellaneous.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Load default shortcuts at startup\n: When launching the application, Ansel loads default shortcuts first, and then loads user-defined shortcuts on top. This allows default shortcuts to be overridden with a new action but prevents them from being deleted (since the deleted shortcut will be automatically reloaded on the next restart). Deactivate this preference to stop loading default shortcuts on startup -- only load the user-defined ones (including any defaults that you have not subsequently deleted or overridden). This makes deletion easier but also means that you will not benefit from new shortcuts added in future versions without first re-enabling this preference (default on).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/miscellaneous.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Scale slider step with min/max\n: When activated, the default step-size, when altering sliders, will depend on the current min/max values for that slider (default on).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/miscellaneous.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Sort built-in presets first\n: Choose how the presets menu is sorted. If this option is enabled, built-in presets are shown first. If the option is disabled, user presets are shown first (default on).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/miscellaneous.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Always show panels' scrollbars\n: Defines whether the panel scrollbars should be always visible or activated only depending on the panel content (default on). (needs a restart)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/miscellaneous.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Position of the scopes module\n: Choose whether to show the [scopes](../views/toolboxes/scopes.md) module in the left or right panel (default right). (needs a restart)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/miscellaneous.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Method to use for getting the display profile\n: This option allows the user to force Ansel to use a specific method to obtain the current display profile for [color management](../color-management/_index.md). In the default setting “all”, Ansel will choose to query the X display server's xatom or the colord system service. You can set this option to “xatom” or “colord” to enforce a specific method if the two methods give different results. You can run the [Ansel-cmstest](../cli/Ansel-cmstest.md) binary to examine your color management subsystem.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/preferences-settings/miscellaneous.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Tags"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/miscellaneous.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Omit hierarchy in simple tag lists\n: When exporting images any hierarchical tags are also added as a simple list of non-hierarchical tags to make them visible to some other programs. When this option is checked Ansel will only include the last part of the hierarchy and ignore the rest. So `foo|bar|baz` will only add `baz`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/preferences-settings/miscellaneous.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Shortcuts with multiple instances"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/miscellaneous.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "It is possible to create multiple instances of many processing modules. In this scenario it is not always obvious which instance should be controlled by keyboard shortcut operations. The following options control rules that are applied (in order) to decide which module instance keyboard shortcuts should be applied to.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/miscellaneous.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "These rules are also used when clicking and dragging on the scopes module to change exposure.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/miscellaneous.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Prefer focused instance\n: If an instance has focus, apply the shortcut to that instance and ignore any other rules. Note that this option does not impact blending shortcuts, which are always applied to the focused instance (default on).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/miscellaneous.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Prefer expanded instances\n: If instances of the module are expanded, ignore collapsed instances (default off).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/miscellaneous.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Prefer enabled instances\n: After applying the above rule, if remaining instances of the module are active, ignore inactive instances (default off).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/miscellaneous.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Prefer unmasked instances\n: After applying the above rules, if remaining instances of the module are unmasked, ignore masked instances (default off).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/miscellaneous.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Selection order\n: After applying the above rules, apply the shortcut to the first or last instance remaining (default \"last instance\").\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/preferences-settings/miscellaneous.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Map / geolocalisation view"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/miscellaneous.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Pretty print the image location\n: Show a more readable representation of the geo-location in the [image information](../views/toolboxes/image-information.md) module (default on).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/preferences-settings/miscellaneous.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Slideshow view"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/miscellaneous.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Waiting time before each picture in slideshow\n: The number of seconds before displaying the next image (default 5) in the [slideshow view](../views/slideshow/_index.md).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Shortcuts"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Some actions in Ansel can be performed with a keyboard shortcut. A table of key shortcuts is available in _Help > Table of key shortcuts_.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Note:** The table shows different key shortcuts for different Views.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Assigning key shortcuts"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "For the time being shortcuts can only be defined or changed by editing your `$HOME/.config/Ansel/shortcutsrc` file. There is no support for MIDI-devices in Ansel.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Deleting default shortcuts"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When launching the application, Ansel loads default shortcuts first, and then loads user-defined shortcuts on top. This allows default shortcuts to be overridden with a new action but prevents them from being deleted (since the deleted shortcut will be automatically reloaded on the next restart).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "There are two ways to delete default shortcuts:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Prevent default shortcuts from being reloaded"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Disable [Preferences > Miscellaneous > Interface > Load default shortcuts at startup](./miscellaneous.md) to prevent default shortcuts from being reloaded. While this option is disabled, Ansel will only load user-defined shortcuts and any defaults that you have not subsequently deleted or overridden.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Override default shortcut with a no-op action"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "You can _override_ the action of a default shortcut by assigning an identical shortcut to the \"global/no-op\" action (which does nothing). You can do this by directly editing your `$HOME/.config/Ansel/shortcutsrc` file (you must exit Ansel first). For example, the following default shortcuts are defined in `shortcutsrc` for switching views in Ansel:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Return=global/switch views/darkroom\nEscape=global/Ateliers/Lighttable\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "You could disable these shortcuts by changing `shortcutsrc` as follows:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Return=global/no-op\nEscape=global/no-op\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Actions"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Note:** The following two section were used to explain the deprecated shortcut mapping screen and are left here for future reference or development.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Shortcuts are used to initiate _actions_ within Ansel.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "An _action_ is usually (but not always) an operation that you might undertake using Ansel's point-and-click user interface. For example:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Increase, decrease or reset sliders\n- Scroll through dropdown lists\n- Enable, expand or focus modules\n- Click buttons\n- Switch between views\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Such point-and-click type _actions_ are normally defined as the application of an _effect_ to an _element_ of a _widget_, where these terms are defined as follows:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Widget\n: Each visible part of the user interface is known as a _widget_. For example the Ansel application window is a widget, containing side panel widgets, each of which contains module widgets, each of which contains button, slider and dropdown list widgets etc... When assigning a shortcut to an action, you must first decide which widget it is to be applied to.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Element\n: An _element_ is the part of a UI widget that is affected by your shortcut. For example, for a slider that has a color picker, you can make a shortcut activate the color picker _button_ element or change the _value_ element of the slider. For a row of tabs (the row is a single widget) you can select which tab element to activate or use your mouse scroll wheel to scroll through the tabs.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Effect\n: A shortcut can sometimes have multiple possible _effects_ on a given _element_. For example, a button can be activated as if it was pressed with a plain mouse-click or as if it was pressed with Ctrl+click. A slider's value can be edited, increased/decreased or reset.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Common actions"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The following is a list of some of the actions to which you can assign shortcuts, organized by widget type.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Global"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Actions in the \"global\" section can be executed from any Ansel view. Most of these actions do not have specific _elements_ as they are used to perform one-off operations.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md content/views/_index.md
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Views"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Actions in the \"views\" section can only be executed from the specified Ansel view. As with global actions, most do not have specific _elements_ as they are used to perform one-off operations.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Buttons"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A button is a clickable icon in the Ansel interface. The default action, when assigning shortcut to a _button_, is to activate that button as if clicked with the left mouse button. You can modify this action to activate the button as if clicked while holding a modifier key.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Toggles"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A toggle is a button that has a persistent on/off state. It therefore has additional _effects_ to allow you to toggle it or explicitly set its state. As with a normal button the default action, when assigning a shortcut to a toggle, is to activate the toggle as if clicked with the left mouse button (which toggles the button on/off).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Utility modules"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "All utility modules have the following elements:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "_Show_\n: Acts as a _toggle_ that expands and collapses the module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "_Reset_\n: Acts as a _button_ that resets all module parameters when activated. The _ctrl-activate_ action can be used to re-apply any automatic presets for that module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "_Presets_\n: Allows you to select actions from the presets menu (e.g. edit, update, previous, next). The default action, when assigning a shortcut to a _preset_ element, is to display a list of the available presets for selection.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The default action, when assigning a shortcut to a utility module, is to _toggle_ the _show_ element (expand/collapse the module).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In addition, shortcuts are available for all of the controls on each module as well as any stored presets (see below).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Processing modules"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Processing modules have the same elements and defaults as utility modules with the following additional elements:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "_Enable_\n: Acts as a _toggle_ that switches the module on and off.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "_Focus_\n: Acts as a _toggle_ that focuses or defocuses the module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "_Instance_\n: Allows you to select actions from the multiple-instance menu (e.g. move up/down, create new instance). The default action, when assigning a shortcut to the _instance_ element, is to display a list of the available options for selection;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If an action affects a processing module that can have multiple instances, you can choose which instance to adjust with a given shortcut. By default, all actions will affect the \"preferred\" instance, as defined using the settings in [Preferences > Miscellaneous > Shortcuts with multiple instances](./miscellaneous.md#shortcuts-with-multiple-instances).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Dropdowns"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A dropdown is a multi-selection box and has the following elements available:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "_Selection_\n: Allows values to be selected from the dropdown list in various ways. The default action, when assigning a shortcut to a dropdown, is to display a popup _edit_ box with a list of the available values for selection.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "_Button_\n: A standard _button_ element that allows the button to the right of the dropdown (if present) to be activated. For example, the _aspect_ dropdown in the [_Crop_](../views/darkroom/modules/crop.md) module has a button that allows the crop controls to be changed from portrait to landscape and vice versa.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Sliders"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A slider allows you to continuously alter an integer or decimal value, and has the following elements available:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "_Value_\n: Allows the current value of the slider to be altered. The default action, when assigning a shortcut to a slider, is to display a popup _edit_ box so you can enter a value. Value elements are also used for modifying some on-screen graphs. When modifying the _value_ element with a shortcut you may not exceed the bounds set in the visual slider.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "_Force_\n: This is the same as the _value_ element described above, but it allows you to exceed the bounds set in the visual slider.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "_Zoom_\n: Allows you to change the upper and lower bounds of the visual slider without altering the current value.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/shortcuts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "_Button_\n: A standard _button_ element that allows the button to the right of the slider (if present) to be activated. For example, a slider may include a color picker to visually set its value based on selected elements of the image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/darkroom.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Control functionality in the [darkroom](../views/darkroom/_index.md) view and associated modules.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/darkroom.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\nPen pressure mapping and brush-stroke smoothing for drawn-mask brushes are set from the [Brush options](../views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md#brush-options) section of a module's _Drawn_ tab, and the parameter each mouse-wheel combination edits from its [Mouse wheel](../views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md#mouse-wheel) section.\n{{< /note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/darkroom.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Display of individual color channels\n: Control how individual color channels are displayed when activated in the [parametric masks](../views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md) feature: _false color_ or _grayscale_ (default _false color_).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/darkroom.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Pattern for the image information line\n: Set the information displayed in the image information line, in the title bar. You can use any of the [variables](../variables.md), `$(NL)` for a new line, and [formatting](../variables.md#formatting) (bold, italic, colors, etc.) — default `$(EXIF.EXPOSURE) • f/$(EXIF.APERTURE) • $(EXIF.FOCAL.LENGTH) mm • $(EXIF.ISO) ISO`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/preferences-settings/darkroom.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Hide built-in presets for processing modules\n: If enabled, only user-defined presets are shown in the presets and favourites menus of processing modules — built-in presets are hidden (default off).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/cli/purge_non_existing_images_sh.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Purge_non_existing_images.sh"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/purge_non_existing_images_sh.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Find and remove entries from the library database referencing images that no longer exist in the filesystem. You must close Ansel before running this script.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/purge_non_existing_images_sh.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The script can be called with the following command line parameters:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block
#: content/cli/purge_non_existing_images_sh.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "purge_non_existing_images.sh [-c|--configdir <path>]\n                             [-l|--library <path>]\n                             [-p|--purge]\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/purge_non_existing_images_sh.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Run the script with no options to perform a \"dry run\", which generates a report of the missing files without committing any changes to the database.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/purge_non_existing_images_sh.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The available options are:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/purge_non_existing_images_sh.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`-c|--configdir <path>`\n: Specify the path to the Ansel configuration directory to be used by the script. If this option is not provided, the default config directory location will be used.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/purge_non_existing_images_sh.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`-l|--library <path>`\n: Specify the path to the library.db database file to be analysed by the script. If this option is not specified, the default library.db file location will be used.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/purge_non_existing_images_sh.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`-p|--purge`\n: Actually delete any entries in the database that refer to non-existent files. If the option is not provided, a report will be printed without committing any changes to the database.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/purge_non_existing_images_sh.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Notes:**\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/purge_non_existing_images_sh.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. The script must be run in a unix shell, and the `sqlite3` client must be available in the command search path. For Linux systems, this will normally not be an issue.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/purge_non_existing_images_sh.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "2. For Windows systems, you will normally need the MSYS2 environment to be installed, as described in the [instructions for building Ansel in a Windows environment](../install/windows.md). If you installed Ansel using the standard Windows installer package, the location of the script would normally be something like: `C:\\Program Files\\Ansel\\share\\Ansel\\tools\\purge_non_existing_images.sh`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/purge_non_existing_images_sh.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "3. For macOS systems, the Terminal application provides a shell, and the `sqlite3` client is provided by the operating system by default. If Ansel was installed using an application bundle from a dmg image, then the default location for the script would be `/Applications/Ansel.app/Contents/Resources/share/Ansel/tools/purge_non_existing_images.sh`\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/purge_non_existing_images_sh.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "4. The delete operation can't be undone. It is therefore strongly recommended that you take a backup of the database before purging any entries.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/cli/_index.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Command Line Interface"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/cli/ansel-cltest.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel-cltest"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-cltest.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The `Ansel-cltest` binary checks if there is a usable OpenCL environment on your system that Ansel can use. It emits some debug output that is equivalent to calling `Ansel -d opencl` and then terminates.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-cltest.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`Ansel-cltest` is called without command line parameters.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/cli/ansel-generate-cache.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel-generate-cache"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-generate-cache.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The `ansel-generate-cache` binary updates Ansel's thumbnail cache. Invoke this program to generate all missing thumbnails in the background when your computer is idle.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-generate-cache.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`ansel-generate-cache` can be called with the following command line parameters:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block
#: content/cli/ansel-generate-cache.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "ansel-generate-cache\n              [-h, --help]\n              [--version]\n              [--min-mip <0-8>] [-m, --max-mip <0-8>]\n              [--min-imgid <N>] [--max-imgid <N>]\n              [--core <Ansel options>]\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-generate-cache.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "All parameters are optional. If started without parameters `ansel-generate-cache` uses reasonable defaults.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-generate-cache.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`-h, --help`\n: Display usage information and terminate.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-generate-cache.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`--version`\n: Display copyright and version information and terminate.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-generate-cache.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`--min-mip <0-8>, -m, --max-mip <0-8>`\n: Ansel can store thumbnails with up to eight different resolution steps for each image. These parameters define the maximum resolution to be generated (defaults to a range of 0-2). There is normally no need to generate all possible resolutions here -- missing ones will be automatically generated by Ansel the moment they are needed. When asked to generate multiple resolutions at once, the lower-resolution images are quickly downsampled from the highest-resolution image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-generate-cache.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`--min-imgid <N>, --max-imgid <N>`\n: Specifies the range of internal image IDs from the database to work on. If no range is given, `ansel-generate-cache` will process all images.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-generate-cache.md content/cli/ansel-cli.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`--core <Ansel options>`\n: All command line parameters following `--core` are passed to the Ansel core and handled as standard parameters. See the [`Ansel binary`](./Ansel.md) section for a detailed description.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/cli/ansel-cmstest.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel-cmstest"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-cmstest.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The `Ansel-cmstest` binary (Linux only) investigates whether the color management subsystem of your computer is correctly configured and displays some useful information about the installed monitor profile(s).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-cmstest.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`Ansel-cmstest` is called without command line parameters.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/cli/ansel-cli.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel-cli"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-cli.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The `ansel-cli` binary starts the command line interface variant of Ansel which allows images to be exported.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-cli.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This variant does not open any display -- it works in pure console mode without launching a GUI. This mode is particularly useful for servers running background jobs.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-cli.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`ansel-cli` can be called with the following command line parameters:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block
#: content/cli/ansel-cli.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "ansel-cli [<input file or folder>]\n              [<xmp file>]\n              <output file or folder>\n              [--width <max width>]\n              [--height <max height>]\n              [--hq <0|1|true|false>]\n              [--upscale <0|1|true|false>]\n              [--style <style name>]\n              [--style-overwrite]\n              [--apply-custom-presets <0|1|false|true>]\n              [--out-ext <extension>]\n              [--import <file or dir>]\n              [--icc-type <type>]\n              [--icc-file <file>]\n              [--icc-intent <intent>]\n              [--verbose]\n              [--help [option]]\n              [--core <Ansel options>]\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-cli.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The user must supply an input filename and an output filename. All other parameters are optional.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-cli.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`<input file or folder>`\n: The name of the input file or folder (containing images) to be exported. If you wish to process multiple images or multiple folders use the `--import` option instead.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-cli.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`<xmp file>`\n: The optional name of an XMP sidecar file containing the history stack data to be applied during export. If this option is not provided Ansel will search for an XMP file that belongs to the given input file(s).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-cli.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`<output file or folder>`\n: The name of the output file or destination folder. The export file format is derived from the file extension or from the `--out-ext` option. You can also use a number of [variables](..//variables.md) in the output filename. For obvious reasons this parameter is mandatory if you use the program on an image folder containing multiple images. If you specify output folder it is recommended that you also specify the file format with `--out-ext`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-cli.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`--width <max width>`\n: Limit the width of the exported image to the given number of pixels.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-cli.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`--height <max height>`\n: Limit the height of the exported image to the given number of pixels.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-cli.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`--hq <0|1|true|false>`\n: Define whether to use high quality resampling during export (see the [export](../views/toolboxes/export.md) module reference for more details). Defaults to true.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-cli.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`--upscale <0|1|true|false>`\n: Define whether allow upscaling during export. Defaults to false.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-cli.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`--style <style name>`\n: Specify the name of a style to be applied during export. If a style is specified, the path to the Ansel configuration directory must also be specified (i.e. `--core --configdir ~/.config/Ansel`). By default no style is applied.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-cli.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`--style-overwrite`\n: The specified style overwrites the history stack instead of being appended to it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-cli.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`--apply-custom-presets <0|1|false|true>`\n: Whether to load `data.db` which contains presets and styles. Disabling this option allows you to run multiple instances of `Ansel-cli` at the cost of being unable to use the `--style` option. Defaults to true.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-cli.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`--out-ext <extension>`\n: Set the output extension to use. If specified takes precedence over `<output file>`. By default this is extracted from `<output file>`. Defaults to `jpg` if `<output folder>` is specified.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-cli.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`--import <file or dir>`\n: Specify input file or folder, can be used multiple times. This option cannot be combined with `<input file or folder>`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-cli.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`--icc-type <type>`\n: Specify the ICC profile type, which is the same as specifying the \"output profile\" in the [_output color profile_](../views/darkroom/modules/output-color-profile.md) module. Defaults to \"image specified\". Use `--help icc-type` to obtain a list of the supported types. See the _output color profile_ module reference for a more detailed description of the available options.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-cli.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`--icc-file <file>`\n: Specify the ICC profile filename. Defaults to an empty filename.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-cli.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`--icc-intent <intent>`\n: Specify the rendering intent. Defaults to \"image specified\". Use `--help icc-intent` to obtain a list of the supported intents. See [rendering intent](../    color-management/rendering-intent) for a more detailed description of the available options.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-cli.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`--verbose`\n: Enables verbose output.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-cli.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`--help [option]`\n: Prints usage and exits. If `option` is specified, additionally prints usage for the given option.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/cli/ansel-cli.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Export options"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-cli.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Export options for Ansel are defined as configuration items, set from within the [export module](../views/toolboxes/export.md). There are two ways to alter this configuration when using `Ansel-cli`, as described below.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/cli/ansel-cli.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Use the export module"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-cli.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The `Ansel-cli` command will use the last format configuration used in the export module, when run in interactive (gui) mode. You may therefore manually set your desired format options in the Ansel gui and then run `Ansel-cli` to export your files.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/cli/ansel-cli.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Pass options on the command-line"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-cli.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "You can set any export format configuration option using the following syntax:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block
#: content/cli/ansel-cli.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "    --core --conf plugins/imageio/format/<FORMAT>/<OPTION>=<VALUE>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-cli.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "where `<FORMAT>` is the name of the desired output format and `<OPTION>` is any configuration option for that format.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-cli.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "An option set in this way will not be permanently stored but will be used just for this run of `Ansel-cli`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-cli.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The following sections describe the configuration options/values that are available for each export format:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#: content/cli/ansel-cli.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Jpeg"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-cli.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`quality`\n: The compression quality (`5` - `100`)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#: content/cli/ansel-cli.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "J2k (jpg2000)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-cli.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`format`\n: The format of the output\n:  - `0`: J2K\n:  - `1`: jp2\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-cli.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`preset`\n: The DCP mode\n: - `0`: Cinema2K, 24 FPS\n: - `1`: Cinema2K, 48 FPS\n: - `2`: Cinema4K, 24 FPS\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#: content/cli/ansel-cli.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Exr (OpenEXR)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-cli.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`bpp`\n: The bit depth (`16` or `32`)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-cli.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`compression`\n: The compression type\n:  - `0`: uncompressed\n:  - `1`: RLE\n:  - `2`: ZIPS\n:  - `3`: ZIP\n:  - `4`: PIZ\n:  - `5`: PXR24\n:  - `6`: B44\n:  - `7`: DWAA\n:  - `8`: DWAB\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#: content/cli/ansel-cli.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Pdf"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
