#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/cli/ansel-nn-parity.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel-nn-parity"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-nn-parity.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The `Ansel-nn-parity` binary checks that the neural network behind the [AI raw\ndenoise](../views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md) module computes the same\nthing on your CPU and on your graphics card as it did on the machine that trained it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-nn-parity.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "You should not normally need it. It exists for one specific situation: **the denoised image\nlooks wrong — a grid, a mesh, a colour cast — and you suspect the graphics card**. This tool\nanswers that question with a number instead of an opinion, and tells you which of the three\nimplementations disagrees with the other two.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/cli/ansel-nn-parity.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Running it"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block
#: content/cli/ansel-nn-parity.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel-nn-parity <model.anselnn> <fixture-directory> [size] [--core <Ansel options>]\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-nn-parity.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The model is one of the `.anselnn` files shipped with Ansel (typically in\n`/usr/share/ansel/` or `share/ansel/` inside your installation). The fixture directory holds a\nreference input and the output the training framework produced from it; fixtures live in the\n[ansel-denoise](https://github.com/aurelienpierreeng/ansel-denoise) repository under\n`fixtures/models/`, one per model, and must match the model you point at — the tool refuses to\nrun otherwise, because comparing one model against another model's reference produces a large\nerror that looks exactly like a real defect.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-nn-parity.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Anything after `--core` is passed to Ansel itself, which is how you select a device:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block
#: content/cli/ansel-nn-parity.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel-nn-parity share/ansel/denoise-large-single-v1.anselnn fixtures/models/large-single 96 \\\n    --core --conf opencl_devid_export=\"+1\"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/cli/ansel-nn-parity.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Reading the output"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block
#: content/cli/ansel-nn-parity.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "model denoise-large-single-v1.anselnn: in=5 out=1, fixture 96x96\n  torch vs CPU     : max abs err 1.79e-07 (at 440: 0.535022 vs 0.535022)\n  using OpenCL device 0\n  torch vs OpenCL  : max abs err 1.79e-07 (at 440: 0.535022 vs 0.535022)\n  CPU   vs OpenCL  : max abs err 1.19e-07\n  PASS (tolerance 2e-04)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-nn-parity.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Healthy numbers sit around `1e-07`, the precision limit of single-precision arithmetic. The\ntolerance is `2e-04`, so a passing result has roughly a thousandfold margin.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-nn-parity.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "What a failure tells you depends on **which line** fails:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-nn-parity.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- **`torch vs OpenCL` fails while `torch vs CPU` passes** — your graphics driver is the\n  problem, not Ansel and not the model. The usual cause is an inaccurate math library in the\n  driver; see [possible problems & solutions](../preferences-settings/performance/opencl/problems-solutions.md).\n- **Both fail by the same amount** — the fixture does not belong to that model. Check that you\n  paired them correctly.\n- **`torch vs CPU` fails alone** — that would be a genuine bug in Ansel. Please report it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-nn-parity.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If no OpenCL device is available the GPU comparison is skipped and only the CPU is checked.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/cli/ansel-nn-parity.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "What it does not cover"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel-nn-parity.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The reference input is synthetic, so the tool validates the network and the OpenCL kernels — not\nthe whole module. A correct result here means the arithmetic is sound; it does not prove that\nevery rendering path around it is. It is a hardware and driver check, not a substitute for\nlooking at your pictures.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/cli/ansel.md content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The `Ansel` binary starts Ansel with its GUI and full functionality. This is the standard way to use Ansel.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`Ansel` can called with the following command line parameters:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block
#: content/cli/ansel.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel [-d {all,act_on,cache,camctl,camsupport,control,demosaic,\n               dev,fswatch,imageio,input,ioporder,lighttable,lua,\n               masks,memory,nan,opencl,params,perf,print,pwstorage,\n               signal,sql,tiling,undo,verbose}]\n          [<input file>|<image folder>]\n          [--version]\n          [--disable-opencl]\n          [--library <library file>]\n          [--datadir <data directory>]\n          [--moduledir <module directory>]\n          [--tmpdir <tmp directory>]\n          [--configdir <user config directory>]\n          [--cachedir <user cache directory>]\n          [--localedir <locale directory>]\n          [--luacmd <lua command>]\n          [--noiseprofiles <noiseprofiles json file>]\n          [--d-signal <signal>]\n          [--d-signal-act <all,raise,connect,disconnect,print-trace>]\n          [--conf <key>=<value>]\n          [-t <num openmp threads>]\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "All parameters are optional. In most cases Ansel should be started without any additional parameters, in which case Ansel uses suitable defaults.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`-d {all,act_on,cache,camctl,camsupport,control,demosaic,dev,fswatch,imageio,input,ioporder,lighttable,lua,masks,memory,nan,opencl,params,perf,print,pwstorage,signal,sql,tiling,undo,verbose}`\n: Enable debug output to the terminal. There are several subsystems of Ansel and each of them can be debugged separately. You can use this option multiple times if you want to debug more than one subsystem (e.g. `Ansel -d opencl -d camctl`) or debug all of them at once (with `-d all`). Some debug options (like `-d opencl`) can also provide more verbose output, which can be invoked with the additional option `-d verbose`. The verbose option must be explicitly provided, even when using `-d all`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`--d-signal <signal>`\n: If `-d signal` or `-d all` is specified, specify the signal to debug using this option. Specify `ALL` to debug all signals or specify signal using it's full name. Can be used multiple times.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`--d-signal-act <all,raise,connect,disconnect,print-trace>`\n: If `-d signal` or `-d all` is specified, specify the signal action to debug using this option.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`<input file>|<image folder>`\n: Optionally supply the name of an image file or folder. If a filename is given Ansel starts in darkroom view with that file opened. If a folder is given Ansel starts in lighttable view with the content of that folder as the current collection.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`--version`\n: Print the Ansel version number, a copyright notice, some other useful information, and then terminate.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`--disable-opencl`\n: Prevent Ansel from initializing the OpenCL subsystem. Use this option if Ansel crashes at startup due to a defective OpenCL implementation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`--library <library file>`\n: Ansel keeps image information in an sqlite database for fast access. The default location of that database file is `$HOME/.config/Ansel/library.db`. Use this option to provide an alternative location (e.g. if you want to do some experiments without compromising your original `library.db`). If the database file does not exist, Ansel creates it for you. You may also provide `:memory:` as the library file, in which case the database is kept in system memory -- all changes are discarded when Ansel terminates.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": Whenever Ansel starts, it will lock the library to the current user. It does this by writing the current process identifier (PID) into a lock file `<library file>.lock` next to the library specified. If Ansel finds an existing lock file for the library, it will terminate immediately.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`--datadir <data directory>`\n: Define the directory where Ansel finds its runtime data. The default location depends on your installation. Typical locations are `/opt/Ansel/share/Ansel/` and `/usr/share/Ansel/`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`--moduledir <module directory>`\n: Ansel has a modular structure and organizes its modules as shared libraries for loading at runtime. This option tells Ansel where to look for its shared libraries. The default location depends on your installation. Typical locations are `/opt/Ansel/lib64/Ansel/` and `/usr/lib64/Ansel/`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`--tmpdir <tmp directory>`\n: Define where Ansel should store its temporary files. If this option is not supplied Ansel uses the system default.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`--configdir <config directory>`\n: Define the directory where Ansel stores user-specific configuration. The default location is `$HOME/.config/Ansel/`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`--cachedir <cache directory>`\n: Ansel keeps a cache of image thumbnails for fast image preview and precompiled OpenCL binaries for fast startup. By default the cache is located in `$HOME/.cache/Ansel/`. Multiple thumbnail caches may exist in parallel -- one for each library file.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`--localedir <locale directory>`\n: Define where Ansel can find its language-specific text strings. The default location depends on your installation. Typical locations are `/opt/Ansel/share/locale/` and `/usr/share/locale/`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`--luacmd <lua command>`\n: A string containing lua commands to execute after lua initialization. These commands will be run after your “luarc” file.\n: If lua is not compiled-in, this option will be accepted but won't do anything.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`--noiseprofiles <noiseprofiles json file>`\n: Provide a json file that contains camera-specific noise profiles. The default location depends on your installation. Typical locations are `/opt/Ansel/share/Ansel/noiseprofile.json` and `/usr/share/Ansel/noiseprofile.json`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`--conf <key>=<value>`\n: Ansel supports a rich set of configuration parameters defined by the user in `$HOME/.config/Ansel/Anselrc`. You may temporarily overwrite individual settings on the command line with this option -- these settings will not be stored in `Anselrc` on exit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/cli/ansel.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`-t <num openmp threads>`\n: limit number of openmp threads to use in openmp parallel sections\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/color-management/_index.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Color management"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel employs a fully color managed workflow:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Input color specifications are taken from embedded or user-supplied ICC profiles or (in the case of raw files) from a library of camera-specific color matrices.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Ansel automatically reads the display profile of your monitor (if properly configured) for accurate on-screen color rendition. Multiple-screen setups are fully supported as long as a system service like colord is in place and properly configured to inform Ansel of the correct monitor profile.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Output files can be encoded in one of Ansel's built-in profiles, including sRGB and Adobe RGB, or into a color space supplied by the user as an ICC profile.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/color-management/color-spaces.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel's color spaces"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-spaces.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Input images are either RGB files (like JPEGs or TIFFs) or camera RAWs. Both store visual information as a combination of primary colors (e.g. red, green and blue) which together describe a light emission to be recreated by a display.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-spaces.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The following image illustrates this concept.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-spaces.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "![Spectral decomposition of a light emission into 3 RGB intensities](spectral-decomposition.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-spaces.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The left-hand-side of the image depicts a colored light that we need to represent digitally. We can use three ideal color filters to decompose this light into three colored primary lights at different intensities. In order to recreate the original colored light from our ideal decomposition (as illustrated in the center of the image) we simply need to recombine those three primary lights by addition.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-spaces.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "It should be possible to reproduce the original colored light by taking a set of white lights at the correct intensities and projecting those lights through appropriately colored filters. This experiment can be performed at home using gels and dimmable white bulbs. This is roughly what old color CRT displays did and is how video projectors still work.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-spaces.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In photography, the initial decomposition step is performed by the color filter array that sits on top of your camera's sensor. This decomposition is not ideal, so it isn't possible to precisely recreate the original emission with simple addition -- some intermediate scaling is required to adjust the three intensities.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-spaces.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "On screens, the LED bulbs are dimmed proportionally to each intensity, and the emissions of the three lights are physically added to reconstruct the original emission. Digital images store the intensities of these primary lights as a set of three numbers for each pixel, depicted on the right-hand side of the above image as shades of gray.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-spaces.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "While a set of display intensities can be easily combined to recreate an original light on a screen (for example, if we created a synthetic image in-computer) the set of captured intensities from a sensor needs some scaling in order for the on-screen light addition to reasonably reproduce the original light emission. This means that every set of intensities, expressed as an RGB set, must be linked to a set of filters (or primary LED colors) that define a _color space_ -- any RGB set only makes sense with reference to a color space.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-spaces.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Not only do we need to temper the captured intensities to make them summable again, but if we are to recompose the original light on a display that does not have the same colored filters or primaries as the space in which our RGB set belongs, these intensities need to be rescaled to take into account the differing filters on the display. The mechanism for this scaling is described in _color profiles_, usually stored within `.icc` files.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-spaces.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Note**: Color is not a physical property of light -- it exists only in the human brain, as a product of the decomposition of a light emission by the cone cells in the retina, again very similar in principle to the above filtering example. An \"RGB\" value should be understood as \"light emissions encoded on 3 channels connected to 3 primaries\", but the primaries themselves may look different from what humans would call \"red\", \"green\" or \"blue\".\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-spaces.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The filters described here are overlapping band-pass filters. Since they overlap, summing them back together would not preserve the energy of the original spectrum so (long story short) we need to dial them down with regard to the retina cone response\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-spaces.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Most of Ansel's actual image processing takes place in a large RGB \"working profile\" space, with some (mostly older) modules internally working in the CIELab 1976 color space (often just called “Lab”). The final output of the image processing pipeline is once again in an RGB space shaped for either the monitor display or the output file.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-spaces.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This process implies that the pixelpipe has two fixed color conversion steps: [_input color profile_](../views/darkroom/modules/input-color-profile.md) and [_output color profile_](../views/darkroom/modules/output-color-profile.md). In addition there is the [_demosaic_](../views/darkroom/modules/demosaic.md) step for raw images, where the colors of each pixel are reconstructed by interpolation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-spaces.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Each module has a position in the pixelpipe that tells you which color space the module lives in:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-spaces.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Up to [_demosaic_](../views/darkroom/modules/demosaic.md)\n: The raw image information does not yet constitute an \"image\" but merely \"data\" about the light captured by the camera. Each pixel carries a single intensity for one primary color, and camera primaries are very different from primaries used in models of human vision. Bear in mind that some of the modules in this part of the pipe can also act on non-raw input images in RGB format (with full information on all three color channels).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-spaces.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Between [_demosaic_](../views/darkroom/modules/demosaic.md) and [_input color profile_](../views/darkroom/modules/input-color-profile.md)\n: Image is in RGB format within the color space of the specific camera or input file.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-spaces.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Between [_input color profile_](../views/darkroom/modules/input-color-profile.md) and [_output color profile_](../views/darkroom/modules/output-color-profile.md)\n: Image is in the color space defined by the selected working profile (linear Rec2020 RGB by default). As Ansel processes images in 4x32-bit floating point buffers, we can handle large working color spaces without risking banding or tonal breaks.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-spaces.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- After [_output color profile_](../views/darkroom/modules/output-color-profile.md)\n: Image is in RGB format as defined by the selected display or output ICC profile.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/color-management/color-dimensions.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel's color dimensions"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-dimensions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This section defines the perceptual properties of color, both conceptually and quantitatively, in order to characterize and quantify the creative and corrective adjustments made to color in Ansel.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/color-management/color-dimensions.md
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Definitions"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-dimensions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Color properties like \"saturation\", \"brightness\" or \"lightness\" have passed into common usage but are largely misused and often used to mean different things. In color science, each of these terms has a precise meaning.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-dimensions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "There are two frameworks within which color properties may be analyzed and described:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-dimensions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- A scene-linear, physiological, framework, that mostly focuses on the response of the retina cone cells, using color spaces such as CIE XYZ 1931 or CIE LMS 2006,\n- A perceptual, psychological, framework that stacks the brain's corrections on top of the retina signal, using color spaces such as CIE Lab 1976, CIE Luv 1976, CIE CAM 2016 and JzAzBz (2017).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-dimensions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "These two frameworks provide us with metrics and dimensions to analyze color and allow us to change some of its properties while preserving others.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-dimensions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The following dimensions of color are used by Ansel:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-dimensions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "hue\n: An attribute of visual perception in which an area appears to be similar to one of the colors red, yellow, green, or blue, or to a combination of adjacent pairs of these colors considered in a closed ring. [^1] Hue is a shared property between the perceptual and scene-linear frameworks.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-dimensions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "luminance\n: The density of luminous intensity with respect to a projected area in a specified direction at a specified point on a real or imaginary surface. [^2] Luminance is a property of scene-referred frameworks, and is expressed by the Y channel of the CIE XYZ 1931 space.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-dimensions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "brightness\n: An attribute of visual perception according to which an area appears to emit, transmit or reflect more or less light. [^4]\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-dimensions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "lightness\n: The brightness of an area judged relative to the brightness of a similarly illuminated area that appears to be white or highly transmitting. [^3] Lightness is the perceptual, non-linear homologue of luminance (roughly equal to the cubic root of luminance Y). Lightness is expressed by the L channel in CIE Lab and Luv 1976 and the J channel in JzAzBz.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-dimensions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "chroma\n: The colorfulness of an area judged as a proportion of the brightness of a similarly illuminated area that appears gray, white or highly transmitting. [^5] _Warning: chroma is not short for chrominance, which is the color part of a video signal (the Cb and Cr channels in YCbCr, for example)._\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-dimensions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "brilliance\n: The brightness of an area judged relative to the brightness of its surroundings. [^6]\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-dimensions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "saturation\n: The colorfulness of an area judged in proportion to its brightness. [^7]\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-dimensions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "[^1]: CIE definition of _hue_: <https://cie.co.at/eilvterm/17-22-067>\n[^2]: CIE definition of _luminance_: <https://cie.co.at/eilvterm/17-21-050>\n[^3]: CIE definition of _brightness_: <https://cie.co.at/eilvterm/17-22-063>\n[^4]: CIE definition of _lightness_: <https://cie.co.at/eilvterm/17-22-059>\n[^5]: CIE definition of _chroma_: <https://cie.co.at/eilvterm/17-22-074>\n[^6]: Article about _brilliance_ (paywall): <https://doi.org/10.1002/col.20128>\n[^7]: CIE definition of _saturation_: <https://cie.co.at/eilvterm/17-22-073>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-dimensions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Colors can be described in many different color spaces, but no matter the color space, each color needs at least 3 components: some metric of luminance or brightness, and 2 metrics of chromaticity (hue and chroma, or opponent color coordinates).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/color-management/color-dimensions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Illustrations"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-dimensions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "While the previous definitions are useful to give a meaning to the words, they don't tell us what we should be looking at. The following charts show luminance, lightness, chroma, brilliance/brightness and saturation varying from a \"0\" base color and how the resulting colors degrade:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-dimensions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "![swatches](swatches.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-dimensions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "(Lightness + Chroma) or (Brilliance/Brightness + Saturation) are two different ways to encode the same reality. They are orthogonal spaces that can be converted from one to another by a simple rotation of the base. This means that chroma evolves at constant lightness, saturation evolves at constant brilliance/brightness, and vice versa:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-dimensions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "![lightness and chroma to brilliance and saturation](lightness-chroma-to-brilliance-saturation.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-dimensions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Lines of equal chroma are vertical (following the patches grid), meaning that chroma has the same direction for all colors in the gamut (see below). However, lines of equal saturation are oblique (drawn dashed on the graph) and all go from black through each color patch, meaning that their directions are particular to each color.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-dimensions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Increasing the chroma will therefore move all colors uniformly away from the central gray axis horizontally, while increasing the saturation will close or open the angle of the oblique dashed lines like a flower.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-dimensions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Similarly, increasing the lightness will move all colors uniformly up from the horizontal axis, while increasing brilliance/brightness will move them along the lines of equal saturation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-dimensions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "On both of the above charts, lightness, chroma, saturation and brilliance are drawn in the JzAzBz color space, which is a perceptual color space suited for an HDR signal, and is used in parametric masks and the color balance RGB module. Luminance is drawn in the CIE XYZ 1931 color space, and represents the effect of an exposure compensation. It shows the same behaviour as brilliance, except that the step size is not perceptually scaled.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-dimensions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Note**: In this section, _brilliance_ and _brightness_ are both used to describe the same dimension. In all rigor, brightness is an absolute metric, whereas brilliance is the brightness of some surface relative to the brightness of its surroundings (that is, how much a surface \"pops\" out of its surroundings and looks fluorescent). But in image editing, increasing the brightness of some surface will indeed increase its brilliance too, so the term _brilliance_ is preferred in Ansel's user interface for clarity and in reference to its visual effect.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/color-management/color-dimensions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Color dimensions and gamut"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-dimensions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The gamut is the volume of colors that a certain color space can encompass and encode. It is important to note that, once converted to perceptual spaces, the gamut of any RGB space is not uniform along hues.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-dimensions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The following examples show the gamut volume of the sRGB space on hue slices containing the primary red, green and blue lights of the sRGB space, over a lightness-chroma plane with a uniform scale:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-dimensions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "![sRGB-red](sRGB-red.jpg)\n![sRGB-green](sRGB-green.jpg)\n![sRGB-blue](sRGB-blue.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-dimensions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This shows that increasing the chroma (displacement over the horizontal axis) of some quantity can be safe for some hues at some lightness, but can push other hue-lightness coordinates way out of gamut. For example, we have much more margin in green or magenta than in cyan.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-dimensions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Many gamut issues at export are actually user-induced and the result of harsh chroma enlarging. For that reason, using brightness-saturation color models may be safer.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/color-management/color-dimensions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Color dimensions and complementary colors"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-dimensions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Cyan, magenta, yellow (CMY) are complementary colors of red, green, blue (RGB). However, the complementary CMY spaces computed from RGB spaces are not perceptually complementary. To show this, we create a CMY space from sRGB, where cyan has sRGB coordinates (0, 1, 1), magenta (1, 0, 1) and yellow (1, 1, 0), and display it in a lightness-chroma space:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-dimensions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "![sRGB-cyan](sRGB-cyan.jpg)\n![sRGB-magenta](sRGB-magenta.jpg)\n![sRGB-yellow](sRGB-yellow.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-dimensions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "By comparing with the hue slices of the primary colors in the previous section, it is easy to see not only that the gamuts don't have the same shapes, but that the colors do not match.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-dimensions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This is one more reason to avoid using HSL/HSV spaces (derived from RGB spaces) to perform color editing: since these RGB spaces are not perceptually uniform in the first place, the resulting HSV/HSL spaces are not uniform either. While RGB spaces have some merit based on their connection to physical light, any process involving hue should go directly to perceptual spaces.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/color-management/color-dimensions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Color dimensions and settings"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-dimensions.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Many applications, including Ansel, call any settings that affect chroma \"saturation\" (for example, in color balance, \"contrast/brightness/saturation\"). This is a symptom of software trying to be accessible to non-professionals by using a common language. This is misleading, since saturation does exist and is quite different from chroma. In addition, many video specifications improperly call chroma \"saturation\". Whenever Ansel reuses such specifications, it uses the incorrect term from the specification rather than the proper color dimension term.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/color-management/color-artifacts.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Possible color artifacts"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-artifacts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "There are some infrequent situations that still can lead to problematic results unless the user takes some action. Some modules in Lab color space rely on the fact that the L channels carries all lightness information, with the a and b channels purely representing chroma and hue. Unbounded colors with negative L values are especially problematic to these modules and can lead to black pixel artifacts.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-artifacts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "It has been found that highly saturated blue light sources in the image frame are likely candidates for pixels with negative L values. If you are engaged in stage photography you should pay close attention to such lights appearing in images.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/color-artifacts.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In order to mitigate this issue the [_input color profile_](../views/darkroom/modules/input-color-profile.md) module has a gamut clipping option. This option is switched off by default but can be activated if artifacts are observed. Depending on the settings, colors will be confined to one of the available RGB gamuts. In effect black pixel artifacts are prevented at the costs of losing some color dynamics.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/color-management/rendering-method.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Rendering method"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/rendering-method.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel can render colors either with its own internal algorithms or by using the external library LittleCMS2. Ansel's internal method is, by an order of magnitude, faster than the external one. The external option gives you a choice of the rendering intent and might offer a slightly higher accuracy in some cases.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/rendering-method.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "You can change the default method in [preferences > processing > always use LittleCMS 2 to apply output color profile](../preferences-settings/processing.md)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/rendering-method.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Note:** If the given ICC is LUT-based or contains both, a LUT and a matrix, Ansel will use LittleCMS2 to render the colors regardless of the configuration parameter's value.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/color-management/display-profile.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Display profile"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/display-profile.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid " For darktable to faithfully render colors on screen it needs to find the correct display profile for your monitor. In general this requires your monitor to be properly calibrated and profiled, and it needs the profile to be correctly installed on your system. darktable queries your X display server's xatom as well as the system service colord (if available) for the right profile. If required you can enforce a specific method in [preferences > miscellaneous](../preferences-settings/miscellaneous.md).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/display-profile.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "To investigate your display profile configuration you can run the [`ansel-cmstest`](../cli/ansel-cmstest.md) binary (Linux only) which prints out useful information (e.g. profile name per-monitor) and tells you if the system is correctly configured.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/display-profile.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In rare cases you may need to manually select the display profile. This is possible from within the [soft proof](../views/toolboxes/soft-proof.md) and [gamut check](../views/toolboxes/gamut.md) option dialogs in the darkroom view and the display profile dialog in the lighttable view.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/display-profile.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Bear in mind that high-tier consumer-grade screens usually don't need a user-made display profile unless you need to perform soft-proofing with professional expectations, since they are properly calibrated to sRGB in the factory.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/display-profile.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A poorly-made display profile will be more harmful than sticking to the default sRGB profile, since the default might be slightly inaccurate but will at least be reliable. Advanced and professional users are advised to proceed with the production of custom display profiles only if they have the training to assess the quality of the resulting profile and understanding of the profiling options.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/color-management/rendering-intent.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Rendering intent"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/rendering-intent.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If rendering with LittleCMS2 is activated (see [rendering method](./rendering-method.md)) you can define how to handle out-of-gamut colors when converting between color spaces. A selection box in the [export](../views/toolboxes/export.md), [_output color profile_](../views/darkroom/modules/output-color-profile.md), and [soft proof](../views/toolboxes/soft-proof.md) modules gives you a choice of the following rendering intents:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/rendering-intent.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "perceptual\n: Best suited to photographs as it maintains the relative position of colors. This is usually the best choice.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/rendering-intent.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "relative colorimetric\n: Out-of-gamut colors are converted to colors having the same lightness, but different saturation. Other colors remain unmodified.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/rendering-intent.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "saturation\n: Saturation is retained but lightness is slightly changed.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/rendering-intent.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "absolute colorimetric\n: Keep the white point.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/color-management/unbounded-colors.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Unbounded colors"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/unbounded-colors.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Screens and most image file formats can only encode RGB intensities confined within a certain range. For example, images encoded on 8 bits can only contain values from 0 to 255, images on 10 bits from 0 to 1023, and so on… Graphic standards postulate that the maximum of that range, no matter its actual value, will always represent the maximum brightness that the display medium is able to render, usually between 100 and 160 Cd/m² (or nits) depending on the actual standard. We generally call this maximum \"100 % display-referred\". The minimum of the range, encoded 0 no matter the bit-depth used, becomes then \"0 % display-referred\". 100 % encodes pure white, 0 % encodes pure black.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/unbounded-colors.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This is a limitation for image processing applications, because it means that any pixel lying outside of this range will be clipped to the nearest bound, resulting in non-recoverable loss of data (colors and/or textures).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/unbounded-colors.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "For the longest time, image processing software too was bounded to this limitation for technical reasons, and some still is, but now by design choice. As a result, they would clip RGB intensities at 100 % display-referred between image operations.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/unbounded-colors.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel uses floating-point arithmetic inside its color pipeline, which means it can handle any RGB value internally, even those outside the display-referred range, as long as it is positive. Only at the very end of the pipeline, before the image is saved to a file or sent to display, are the RGB values clipped if needed.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/unbounded-colors.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Pixels that can take values outside of the display range are said to have “unbounded colors”. One could choose to clamp (i.e. confine) those values to the allowed range at every processing step or choose to carry on with them, and clamp them only at the last step in the pipeline. However, it has been found that processing is less prone to artifacts if the unbounded colors are not clamped but treated just like any other color data.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/unbounded-colors.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "At the end of the pipeline, modules like [_filmic_](../views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md) can help you to remap RGB values to the display-referred range while maximizing the data preservation and avoiding hard clipping, which is usually not visually pleasing.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/color-management/unbounded-colors.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "However, at all times in the pipeline, you must ensure that you do not create negative RGB values. RGB intensities encode light emissions and negative light does not exist. Those modules that rely on a physical understanding of light to process pixels will fail if they encounter a non-physical light emission. For safety, negative RGB values are still clipped whenever they might make the algorithms fail, but the visual result might look degraded. Negative values can be produced when abusing the _black level_ in [_exposure_](../views/darkroom/modules/exposure.md) or the _offset_ in [_color balance_](../views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md) and care should be taken when using these modules.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/variables.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Variables"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/variables.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel supports variable substitution in a number of modules and preference settings. For example:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/variables.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Defining file names in the [export](./views/toolboxes/export.md) module and [import tool](./getting-started/import.md),\n- Displaying image information in the darkroom's image information line,\n- Displaying image information in the lighttable's overlays and tooltips (see [preferences > lighttable](./preferences-settings/lighttable.md))\n- Placing text on an image in the [_watermark_](./views/darkroom/modules/watermark.md) processing module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/variables.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Most, if not all text entries that use variables implement auto-completion as soon as you start typing `$(`.\n"
msgstr ""

