#. type: Title ##
#: content/variables.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "String substitution"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/variables.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "All of the variables support basic string substitution inspired by bash though some of the details differ.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/variables.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "All patterns are treated as simple string comparisons. There is no regex support.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/variables.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The following string replacement functions are provided, where `var` is one of the variables listed above:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block
#: content/variables.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "$(var-default)                   If var is empty, return \"default\"\n                                 It is possible to use another variable as \"default\", e.g.\n                                 $(WIDTH.CROP-$(WIDTH.RAW))\n\n$(var+alt_value)                 If var is set, return \"alt_value\" else return empty string\n\n$(var:offset)                    Return var starting from offset\n                                 If offset is negative count from the end of the string\n\n$(var:offset:length)             Starting from offset, return at most length characters of var\n                                 If offset is negative the length is counted from the end of var\n                                 If length is negative this indicates the end of the result,\n                                  counted from the end of var, and not an actual length\n\n$(var#pattern)                   Remove \"pattern\" from the start of var\n\n$(var%pattern)                   Remove \"pattern\" from the end of var\n\n$(var/pattern/replacement)       Replace the first occurrence of \"pattern\" in var with \"replacement\"\n                                 If \"replacement\" is empty then \"pattern\" will be removed\n\n$(var//pattern/replacement)      Replace all occurrences of \"pattern\" in var with \"replacement\"\n                                 If \"replacement\" is empty then \"pattern\" will be removed\n\n$(var/#pattern/replacement)      If var starts with \"pattern then \"pattern\" is replaced with \"replacement\"\n\n$(var/%pattern/replacement)      If var ends with \"pattern\" then \"pattern\" is replaced with \"replacement\"\n\n$(var^)                          Make the first character of var uppercase\n\n$(var^^)                         Make all characters of var uppercase\n\n$(var,)                          Make the first character of var lowercase\n\n$(var,,)                         Make all characters of var lowercase\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/variables.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Formatting"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/variables.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The image information patterns support [markup](https://docs.gtk.org/Pango/pango_markup.html). For example, adding the following will provide a clear warning (large, red, bold text) when OpenCL has failed to initialise:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/variables.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`<span alpha='1%'>$(OPENCL_ACTIVATED/no/<span foreground='red' weight='heavy' size='xx-large' alpha='100%'>OPENCL ACTIVATION FAILED</span>$(NL))</span>`\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/map/_index.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Map"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/map/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The Map view is optional and needs to be activated in [Preferences > Other views](../../preferences-settings/other-views.md). It allows you to see where your geo-tagged images were taken, and to add location information to non-geo-tagged images.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/map/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The Map view shows a world map with the images in the current [collection](../../getting-started/library.md) pinned to their geo-tagged location (if available). This requires that images are tagged with location information. Some newer cameras, including smartphones, are already equipped with GPS receivers. Other cameras may need additional GPS hardware to do this.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/map/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Even if your camera doesn't support this feature, there is an alternative method -- Ansel can match the Exif date/time in your image(s) to a separate GPX data tracking file created by a GPS tracker, that has recorded your movements. GPS trackers can be purchased as standalone handheld devices or you can install a GPS tracker app on your smartphone. Location tagging with GPS tracking data can be done using the [geotagging](../toolboxes/geotagging.md) module, in the lighttable and Map views.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/map/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Center Map view"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/map/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In the center of the Map view you will see a world map.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/map/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Map data is taken from open map sources on the internet. As such, new map data is only available if you are connected to the internet -- Ansel keeps a disk cache of previously loaded map data.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/map/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "You can navigate within the map using your mouse. Left-click and drag to move the map. Use the scroll-wheel to zoom in and out.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/map/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "On-screen controls and displays are available to help you find your way. A navigation area is located on top left of the map -- use this as an alternative to mouse-dragging and scrolling. The scale of the map is displayed at the bottom-left. At the bottom-right you can see the geographical coordinates for the center of the map.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/map/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Images that already have geo-location attributes in their metadata are displayed as small icons on the map. Images close to each other are grouped and a count of grouped images is displayed on the bottom-left corner.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/map/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In order to assign geo coordinates to an image, activate the [filmstrip](../toolboxes/filmstrip.md) on the lower panel (press Ctrl+F). You can assign a geo location to an image by dragging the image icon from the film-strip and positioning it on the map -- Ansel will record the new location (latitude and longitude ) as part of the image metadata. This data will be included in exported images.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/map/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In order to remove location data from an image simply drag it from the map and drop it onto the filmstrip.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/map/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Images close to each other are grouped under a single image group. You can use the  [map settings](../toolboxes/map-settings.md) module to control the grouping as needed. The number displayed on the bottom left of the thumbnail is the number of images inside the group. A white number means that all images in the group are at exactly the same location, whereas a yellow number means they are not. Use the mouse scroll wheel while hovering over a group of images to scroll through the thumbnails of the images in that group.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/map/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Normally, images in the center Map view have black borders. If an image is selected in the filmstrip, then the corresponding image on the map will be highlighted with a white border.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/map/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Click+drag to adjust the location of an image. Shift+click to move a complete group of images.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/map/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Undo/Redo"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/map/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "All image movements in the Map view are recorded by Ansel. It is possible to undo or redo such changes to recover a previous state. Note that this undo/redo facility is unlimited while moving images but it is reset each time you leave the Map view.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/map/_index.md content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/undo-redo.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Press Ctrl+Z to undo the last modification and Ctrl+Y to redo the last undone modification (if any).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel separates its functionality into several _views_, each dedicated to one task:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "[Lighttable](lighttable/_index.md)\n: Manage, sort, cull and tag your image collection. This is the default view, loaded at startup, and you can return to it from anywhere by pressing <kbd>Escape</kbd>.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "[Darkroom](darkroom/_index.md)\n: Develop a single image. Open it from the lighttable by double-clicking a thumbnail, or by selecting a picture and pressing <kbd>Enter</kbd>.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "[Map](map/_index.md)\n: Show geo-tagged images on a map and geo-tag new images manually. Hidden by default; enable it in the [preferences](../preferences-settings/other-views.md).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "[Print](print/_index.md)\n: Send images to a printer. Hidden by default; enable it in the [preferences](../preferences-settings/other-views.md). Not available on Windows, as it relies on [CUPS](http://www.cups.org/).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "[Studio Capture](studio-capture/_index.md)\n: Dedicated view for tethered shooting sessions.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "[Slideshow](slideshow/_index.md)\n: Display images full-screen as a slideshow, processing them on the fly. Hidden by default; enable it in the [preferences](../preferences-settings/other-views.md).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "You switch views from the **Ateliers** menu in the top menu bar, which lists every available view and the shortcut to reach it (the current view is greyed out). The darkroom is the exception: because it needs an image to open, you enter it from a lighttable or filmstrip thumbnail, not from the menu.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Window layout"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Every view shares the same window frame: a central work area surrounded by panels. The visibility and size of each panel are remembered independently for each view.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Header (top panel)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The header runs along the top of the window and is common to all views. From left to right it contains:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The [global menu](global-menu.md) bar\n: **File · Edit · Selection · Image · Styles · Run · Display · Ateliers · Help**. This menu bar is the backbone of the interface: it gathers application-wide commands, image operations, view switching and help. Each menu has a keyboard mnemonic — press <kbd>Alt</kbd> to underline the mnemonic letters, then press one to open that menu, and navigate the open menus with the arrow keys.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "_Search actions…_ button\n: Opens the [global action search](../getting-started/keyboard.md#vimkey-like-global-action-search) (default <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>P</kbd>), from which any action can be found and triggered by name, whether or not it has a shortcut.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Message area\n: On the right, a text area where some modules display hints and status messages.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Window buttons\n: A _Go back to lighttable_ button (home icon), plus the minimize and close buttons for the window.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The header can be shown and hidden with <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>T</kbd>.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\nComing from Darktable: the view switcher, the preferences button, the context-help button and the shortcut-mapping button that used to live in the top toolbar are now reached from the global menu (_Ateliers_, _Edit → Preferences…_, _Help → Open contextual help_ and _Edit → Keyboard shortcuts…_ respectively). The separate top toolbar no longer exists.\n{{< /note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Second top row"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Directly below the header, the lighttable shows a single toolbar that combines the [collection filters](toolboxes/collections.md) (rating, color label, edited status, text search, _restrict to selection_) on one side and the [display options](lighttable/_index.md) (columns, zoom, overlays) on the other. Other views leave this row empty.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/_index.md content/views/print/print-view-layout.md
#: content/views/darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Left panel"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Shown and hidden with <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>L</kbd>. It hosts the view's [toolboxes](toolboxes/_index.md):\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- **In lighttable**: the _Library_ collection builder and import/export tools.\n- **In darkroom**: peripheral tools about the picture being edited — navigation, snapshots, color pickers, image information, shape manager, etc.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/_index.md content/views/print/print-view-layout.md
#: content/views/darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Right panel"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Shown and hidden with <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>R</kbd>. Used only in the **darkroom** (and map/print), where it holds the image-processing modules and the scopes. The lighttable has no right panel.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Bottom panel (filmstrip)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Shown and hidden with <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>F</kbd>. Available in the **darkroom**, **map** and **print** views, where it displays the [filmstrip](toolboxes/filmstrip.md): a horizontal strip of the current collection's thumbnails for quick navigation without leaving the view. The lighttable has no filmstrip, since it already shows the whole collection.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Resizing and global layout"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Drag the inner border of the left, right or filmstrip panels to resize them.\n- <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>F11</kbd> toggles the visibility of all panels at once, expanding the work area to fill the window.\n- <kbd>F11</kbd> toggles fullscreen mode.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "All these layout commands are also available from the **Display** menu, under _Panels_ and _Full screen_.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Display menu"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The **Display** menu collects the view-level display settings that previously lived in scattered toolbars:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- _Panels_ — individually toggle the top, left, right and filmstrip panels.\n- _Thumbnail overlays_ — choose whether thumbnail badges (rating, labels, metadata) are _always hidden_, _shown on hover_ or _always shown_.\n- _Thumbnail source_ — choose how thumbnails are generated: _always process the RAW_, _use the embedded JPEG if the image is unedited_, or _always use the embedded JPEG_. This can be changed at runtime.\n- _Collapse grouped images_ and _Show group borders_ — control how [grouped images](lighttable/digital-asset-management/grouping.md) are displayed.\n- _Monitor color profile_ / _Monitor color intent_ — the display [color management](../color-management/_index.md) settings.\n- _Full screen_.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "See the [global menu reference](global-menu.md) for the complete list of menus and their entries.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/studio-capture/_index.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Studio Capture"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/studio-capture/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Studio Capture is a dedicated view for tethered shooting sessions. Instead of\nswitching back to Lighttable every time a new photo lands on disk, this view\nwatches a folder, imports new captures automatically, optionally applies a\nset of styles to them, and always shows you the latest shot full-size — with\nthe filmstrip below to review the rest of the session.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/studio-capture/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{ warning }}\nStudio Capture doesn't support yet control and loading images directly from a camera. You need to use a third party program such as Digikam or Entangle to take and download images  in a local folder.\n{{ /warning }}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/studio-capture/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "It sits alongside Lighttable, Darkroom, Map and Print in the global menu \"Ateliers\".\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/studio-capture/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Quick start"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/studio-capture/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. Open **Studio Capture** in the global menu \"Ateliers\".\n2. In **Auto import**, choose the folder your camera or tethering software writes images into.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/studio-capture/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{ warning }}\nDo not choose the on-camera folder as you either will not be able to take pictures or Ansel will not be able to refresh the folder correctly.\n{{ /warning }}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/studio-capture/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "3. Optionally, build a list of styles in **Auto style** to auto-apply to every incoming shot (e.g. a base look, a black & white conversion...).\n4. Press **Start the session**.\n5. Shoot. Each new image appears automatically in the center, with any configured styles already applied, and gets added to the filmstrip.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/studio-capture/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "You can leave Studio Capture and come back — monitoring keeps running in the background as long as Ansel is open, and Ansel offers to resume it if it was still running when you last closed the application.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/studio-capture/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Auto import"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/studio-capture/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This panel controls what folder is being watched and what happens to each image that shows up in it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/studio-capture/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Session controls"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/studio-capture/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "At the top of the panel:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/studio-capture/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- **Project date** — free-text date used by the `$(YEAR)`, `$(MONTH)`, `$(DAY)` naming variables (see below). Must be typed as `YYYY-MM-DD`, optionally followed by `HH:MM:SS.mmm` — you can type just the leading part you care about (e.g. `2026` or `2026-07`) and the rest is filled in for you (`2026` becomes January 1st, 2026 at midnight). Leave empty to use the current date at scan time.\n- **Jobcode** — a free-text label available as the `$(JOBCODE)` naming variable, handy for tagging a session by client or shoot name.\n- **Scan frequency** — how often (in seconds) the folder is checked for new images. Changes apply the next time you start a session, not to one already running.\n- **Status line** — shows whether a session is running, whether it's ready to start, or (in orange) what's missing before it can start.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/studio-capture/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Source tab"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/studio-capture/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- **Folder to survey** — the folder that receives your camera's images.\n- **Delete original file** — only relevant when copying to another location (see Destination below): removes the source file once the copy has been verified byte-for-byte.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/studio-capture/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\n- Deleting image on-camera is not possible as it could corrupt the memory card.\n- The source folder and scan frequency are locked while a session is running, since the engine compares each scan against a baseline recorded when monitoring started.\n- The source folder cannot contain the destination folder.\n{{< /note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/studio-capture/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Destination tab"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/studio-capture/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- **File handling** — *Add to library* imports images where they already are; *Copy to disk* additionally copies each file to an organized location before importing it. Choosing *Copy to disk* reveals the rest of this tab:\n  - **On conflict** — what to do if the computed destination file already exists:\n    - *Skip*: keep the existing destination file and import that instead of copying over it.\n    - *Overwrite*: replace it with the incoming source.\n    - *Create unique filename* (default): copy the source under a numbered suffix instead of colliding. This is the safest default for tethering, since naming patterns often don't vary from shot to shot.\n  - **Base directory**, **Project directory pattern**, **File naming pattern** — where copies land and how they're named. Both patterns accept `$(...)` variables — start typing `$(` in either field to see the list via auto-completion (things like `$(YEAR)`, `$(JOBCODE)`, `$(FILE_NAME)`...).\n  - A live preview below shows the destination path your current settings would produce, so you can check it before starting.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/studio-capture/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\n- The destination folder cannot be contained in the source folder.\n{{< /note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/studio-capture/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Starting and stopping"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/studio-capture/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Press **Start the session** once your configuration is valid — the button stays greyed out otherwise, with the status line explaining what's missing (an unreadable source folder, an invalid project date, a base directory that doesn't exist or sits inside the surveyed folder, an empty naming pattern...).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/studio-capture/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Every time you press **Start**, Ansel checks whether the surveyed folder already holds images — a folder never surveyed before, new files that appeared while Ansel was closed, or files added since the last time you stopped this session — and if so asks whether to import them right away instead of silently absorbing them into the baseline. Declining just records them as already-known, so a later scan won't pick them up either.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/studio-capture/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A new file is only imported once its size and modification time stop changing for one full scan interval — this avoids importing a file while your camera or tethering software is still writing it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/studio-capture/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Auto style"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/studio-capture/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This panel manages an ordered list of styles (\"the pool\") that gets applied, in order, to every image Studio Capture imports.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/studio-capture/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Pool and Styles"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/studio-capture/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- **Pool** — the styles that are actually applied on import, in the order they're applied. Each row has:\n  - `↑` / `↓` to move it earlier/later in the order.\n  - `-` to remove it from the pool.\n- **Styles** — every style available in your library. Click the **+** icon next to a style to add it to the pool. A style already in the pool is greyed out here so you can see at a glance what's already queued.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/studio-capture/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Styles in the pool all apply in `append` pasting mode on top of each other, in list order, the same way applying several styles manually would. Reorder or remove entries at any time — it takes effect on the next imported image; already-imported photos are not retroactively changed and you would have to apply them manually by clicking the button.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/studio-capture/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Applying styles manually"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/studio-capture/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The **Apply to the displayed image** button re-applies the whole pool, in order, to whichever capture is currently shown in the center, on top of its current history (with the `append` pasting mode) — useful if you change your style pool mid-session and want to bring an earlier shot in line with the rest, without leaving Studio Capture.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/studio-capture/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Viewing captures"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/studio-capture/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The center always shows one capture, full-size, with any styles already baked in. Use the filmstrip at the bottom to look back through the session.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/studio-capture/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- **Zoom**: <kbd>double-click</kbd>, <kbd>middle-click</kbd>, or <kbd>scroll</kbd> the image to toggle between fit-to-window and 100%.\n- **Pan** at 100%: <kbd>drag</kbd>, or use the <kbd>arrow keys</kbd>.\n- **Filmstrip**: <kbd>double-click</kbd> a thumbnail to preview that capture in the\n  center.\n- **Open in Darkroom**: press <kbd>Return</kbd> at any time to open the currently displayed capture for full editing.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/studio-capture/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Color picker"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/studio-capture/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The Scopes module's color picker (point or box sample) works directly on the image shown here, the same way it does in Darkroom.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/studio-capture/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Display toolbox"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/studio-capture/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The bottom-left toolbox buttons — shared with Darkroom — affect how the\nimage is *displayed*:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/studio-capture/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- **Raw overexposed** / **Overexposed (clipping)** — highlight clipped highlights, with the same options (threshold, mode, colors) as in\n  Darkroom.\n- **Soft proof** / **Gamut check** — preview how the image will look under a target output profile, or flag out-of-gamut colors.\n- **ISO 12646** — frame the image on a neutral grey background with a white margin, for more accurate visual judgment of exposure/color.\n- **Picture display** — background brightness and picture margins for the center view.\n- **Guides** — overlay a grid (rule of thirds, etc.) on top of the displayed image; right-click the button for guide options.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/studio-capture/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\nThese only affect the picture while it's shown at fit-to-window zoom; they have no effect at 100%.\n{{< /note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/studio-capture/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Session resume"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/studio-capture/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If Ansel is closed while a session is still monitoring, it offers to resume that session the next time it starts — accepting switches straight to Studio Capture and restarts monitoring on the same folder. Declining stops asking for that session. Stopping a session manually also clears the prompt.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/studio-capture/_index.md content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Tips"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/studio-capture/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Keep the scan frequency low (a few seconds) for a responsive tethering workflow; there's little cost to scanning often.\n- The base directory for copies can never be inside the folder being surveyed, to avoid the copies themselves being picked up as new input.\n- The picker position is remembered between images, so it makes easier to check the value of a particular area. \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/print/_index.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Print"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/print/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The Print view is optional and needs to be activated in [Preferences > Other views](../../preferences-settings/other-views.md). This view allows you to print your images. Because printing is not easy, there are many technical aspects to be taken into account.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/print/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "After selecting an image in the [Lighttable view](../lighttable/_index.md) you can enter the [Print settings](../toolboxes/print-settings.md) module to adjust printer settings and initiate printing.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/print/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This module supports the printer's ICC profile, which is somewhat mandatory if you want to obtain a high quality print close to the image displayed on the screen.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/print/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "It is important to note that ICC profiles provided by the paper and/or printer manufacturers cannot be used on GNU/Linux as they are printer-driver dependent. The Ansel print module uses CUPS and there are no ready-to-use ICC profiles available for this driver.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/print/print-view-layout.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Print view layout"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/print/print-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The central area displays the image layout on the paper (the white area). Some gray borders may be displayed around the image to represent the printable area (the page minus the borders) not filled by the image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/print/print-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The [filmstrip](../toolboxes/filmstrip.md) below the image allows you to select more images.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/print/print-view-layout.md content/views/lighttable/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Overlays"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/print/print-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When the mouse is over the bounding box of an image, its width and height are shown along its top and left, respectively. Margins between the bounding box and the page edge are notated next to the dotted lines extending out from each side of the bounding box. All measurements are shown in the units as chosen in the print settings module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/print/print-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Images are inset along one dimension of their bounding box when they do not match the aspect ratio of the box. The overlaid margin measurements should therefore only be used to understand the layout bounds, not the actual printed size of the image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/print/print-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "[collections](../toolboxes/collections.md)\n: Filter the list of images displayed in the lighttable.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/print/print-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "[image information](../toolboxes/image-information.md)\n: Display image information\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/print/print-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "[print settings](../toolboxes/print-settings.md)\n: Adjust print settings and initiate printing.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/toolboxes/duplicate-manager.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Duplicate manager"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/duplicate-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "View and switch between the versions (duplicates) of the current image. Every version shares the same underlying file but keeps its own independent editing history, stored in its own XMP sidecar, so each can be edited without affecting the others.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/duplicate-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In the darkroom [left panel](../darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md#left-panel), the duplicate manager lists each version of the current image with a preview thumbnail and a version number:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/duplicate-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- **Press and hold** a thumbnail to preview that version in the center view.\n- **Double-click** a thumbnail to switch to that version and edit it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/duplicate-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Creating versions"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/duplicate-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "New versions are created from the [global menu](../global-menu.md), so they also work on a lighttable selection:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/duplicate-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- _Edit → Create new history_ (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>N</kbd>) makes a **virgin** version, with an empty history.\n- _Edit → Duplicate existing history_ (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>D</kbd>) makes an **exact duplicate** of the current edit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/duplicate-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A version's description is stored in the _version name_ metadata field, which you can edit from the [metadata editor](metadata-editor.md) in the lighttable.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/toolboxes/geotagging.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Geotagging"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/geotagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Import and apply GPX track data to a selection of images, and correct image date/time.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/geotagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This module — labelled **Datetime and GPS** in the panel — is common to the [lighttable](../lighttable/_index.md) and [map](../map/_index.md) views. The map view provides an enhanced mode that allows you to preview the position of the images along the GPS tracks while adjusting the images' date/time offset and time zone.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/geotagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Workflow overview"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/geotagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A GPS receiver calculates its current position based on information it receives from satellites, and records it in a GPX file together with the current date and time. The Exif data of the images also contains a time stamp defined by the camera settings. The _geotagging_ module takes the time stamp of the image, looks up the position in the GPX file at that time, and stores the appropriate coordinates (latitude/longitude/elevation) in its database and the image's XMP sidecar file.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/geotagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Two problems may occur during this process:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/geotagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- In contrast to GPS devices, most cameras don't record the time accurately.\n- The time stored in the Exif data does not include the time zone. Most people set their camera to local time, whereas GPS devices store the time in the UTC (Universal Time, Coordinated, i.e. Greenwich (London)) time zone. If the time zones of the camera and GPX file differ, than the related location will be wrong.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/geotagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If your image already shows the correct date/time and carries the UTC time stamp, you can directly apply the GPX track file without further adjustments.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/geotagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Otherwise follow the following process to correlate the time of the images and GPS track files\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/geotagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. Fix the camera time setting for a single image by manually entering the correct date/time for that image into the _date/time_ field. A good way to do this accurately is to take a photograph of a reliable time source. This can be any precise clock or, even better, the time displayed on your GPS device (bearing in mind that GPS devices normally _show_ the local time, even though they _store_ universal time). The difference (offset) between the time entered and the time stored in the image's Exif data will be shown in the _date/time offset_ field.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/geotagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "2. Press the _lock_ button to lock the calculated offset in the module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/geotagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "3. Select all of the images you wish to adjust and click _apply offset_ to apply the calculated offset to those images.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/geotagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "4. If the camera time zone is not UTC, set the time zone in the _camera time zone_ field.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/geotagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "With the time setting corrected, you can now apply GPX tracking data using the _apply GPX track file_ button.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/geotagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Module controls (common)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/geotagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "date/time\n: This field is initialized with the date/time read from the first selected image (format `yyyy:mm:dd hh:mm:ss`) and can be modified to correct the date/time for that image. The individual date/time fields can be altered by scrolling over them with your mouse. If any field reaches its limit, the neighboring fields are automatically updated. For example, if you go over 60 on the minute field, the hour field will automatically be incremented. It is also possible to use milliseconds in this module if you enable [preferences > lighttable > show image time with milliseconds](../../preferences-settings/lighttable.md).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/geotagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "original date/time\n: The original date/time of the image is shown here for reference.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/geotagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "date/time offset\n: The calculated difference between the original date/time and that keyed in the _date/time_ field. If the calculated difference is greater than 99 days 23 hours 59 minutes and 59 seconds, the offset is invalidated.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/geotagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "lock button\n: If the lock button is activated, the offset value is frozen. If you subsequently change the selected image(s), the new image date/time is updated using the locked offset. This allows you to apply the same offset to multiple groups of images.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/geotagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "apply offset button\n: Apply the offset to selected images.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/geotagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "apply date/time button\n: It is sometimes useful to be able to set the absolute date/time for an image, for example where this information is missing. This button allows you to apply the date/time entered in the _date/time_ field to the selected images, without considering the previous value. You can use Ctrl+Z to undo any unwanted changes.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/geotagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "camera time zone\n: Select the camera's time zone. Start typing to show a list of permitted values.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/geotagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "apply GPX track file (lighttable view only)\n: Apply a GPX track file. Click the corresponding button and navigate to the GPX file. You can use Ctrl+Z to undo any unwanted changes. Within the file chooser window, the preview button lists the tracks of the selected GPX file along with the following information: track name, start and end date/time (local time), number of track points and number of selected images that will be geotagged.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/geotagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Module controls (map view)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/geotagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "gpx file\n: The path of the gpx file selected.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/geotagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "track list\n: This table shows the start date/time of each track, along with the number of track points and the number of matching images. When a check button is activated, the related track is displayed on the map. A check button in the table header allows you to select or de-select all of the tracks at once. Hover over a row with your mouse to display the start and end times both in local time (LT) and UTC.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/geotagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "preview images\n: If checked, the matching images are displayed on the map along the visible tracks.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/geotagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "select images\n: If you don't wish to apply an offset to all of the selected images, but only to the matching images, use this button to select images. If you don't want to lose the current offset you may want to lock it before changing the selection.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/geotagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "counts\n: A count of the number of matching images and the number selected is displayed to the right of the _select images_ button.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/geotagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "apply geo-location\n: This button is displayed when the offset is null. The _apply geo-location_ button applies the GPX data to matching images on selected tracks.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/geotagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "apply offset and geo-location\n: This button is displayed once an offset has been entered. As a reminder, the _apply offset_ button applies the offset to all selected images. Unlike _apply offset_, the _apply offset and geo-location_ button applies the offset _and the GPX data_ to matching images on selected tracks.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/geotagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "You can use Ctrl+Z to undo any unwanted changes (twice in case of _apply offset and geo-location_).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/toolboxes/clipping.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Clipping warning"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/clipping.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Highlight areas of the image that may exhibit luminance or gamut clipping.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/clipping.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When an image is sent to a display device, each pixel is normally represented as a set of 3 numbers, representing the intensity of the red, green and blue primary colors in the output color space. Because the output color space is usually closely related to hardware with physical limations, there is a maximum permitted value for the [R,G,B] channels, representing the maximum available intensity for that color space. Similarly, there is also a minimum value below which pixel values will be mapped to zero. When we try to convert from a larger color space to the final output color space, any values exceeding this maximum will be clamped to the maximum value, and any values below the minimum will be clamped to zero. This process is called \"clipping\" and it will lead to lost detail, or \"incorrect\" colors for any pixels with clipped channels.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/clipping.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Click the clipping-warning icon in the [bottom toolbar](../darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md#bottom-panel) to enable the clipping warning.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/clipping.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "There are two ways in which a pixel might become clipped when represented in the output color space.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/clipping.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- _luminance clipping_: This can occur when a pixel is too bright to be represented in the output color space. The pixel luminance is calculated as a weighted average of the [R,G,B] channels. If this average exceeds the maximum allowed value, it is an indication of over-exposure. The overall luminance of a pixel can also be too dark to be represented by an [R,G,B] value in the output color space, in which case it will simply be shown as black. We normally deal with luminance clipping by carefully adjusting tone mappings and exposure levels.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/clipping.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- _gamut clipping_: The output color space defines a set of primary colors that, mixed together in certain ratios, produce the final output color. However, there are only so many colors that can be produced by mixing together a combination of those three primary colors. Highly saturated colors in particular can be difficult to represent, especially for pixels that are very bright or very dark. If there is no set of positive [R,G,B] values that can represent a given color at a given level of brightness, we say that the color is \"out of gamut\", and we need to settle for another color instead that _can_ be represented by permitted [R,G,B] values within the color space. We can handle gamut clipping by being careful not to over-saturate colors in the highlights and shadows, and possibly by using some color grading/color mapping techniques.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/clipping.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The \"clipping warning\" module highlights those pixels that cannot be accurately represented in the output color space, either due to luminance or gamut clipping. It offers several modes to help you differentiate between luminance and gamut clipping, so that you can make better decisions about how to address any issues.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/clipping.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "As the clipping warning runs at the end of the preview pixelpipe, it receives data in display color space then converts it to histogram color space. If you are using a display color space that is not \"well behaved\" (this is common for a device profile), then colors that are outside of the gamut of the display profile will clip or distort.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/clipping.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The clipping warning module, described here, warns you about clipping caused by image processing and the limitations of the output color space. It should not be confused with the following similar tools:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/clipping.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- The [raw overexposed warning](./raw-overexposed.md) indicates where pixels in the original raw file are clipped due to physical limitations in the dynamic range of the camera sensor. This module highlights information that was permanently lost at the point of image capture, and you need to deal with it as best you can using highlight recovery techniques.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/clipping.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- The [gamut check](./gamut.md) module also provides information about clipping arising from image processing. It is based on the external littleCMS library, and is more or less equivalent to the _full gamut_ mode in the clipping warning module. The downsides of the gamut check module are that it doesn't allow you to distinguish between clipping caused by luminance and gamut mapping, and it is much slower than the clipping warning indicator.\n"
msgstr ""

