#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Module controls"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/clipping.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Right-click on the clipping icon to show the following options:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/clipping.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "clipping preview mode\n: Choose the type of clipping that you want to highlight:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/clipping.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": - _any RGB channel_: Provides an over-clipping indication if any one of the three [R,G,B] channels exceeds the maximum permitted value for the histogram color space, or an under-clipping indication if the three [R,G,B] channels are too dark and are all forced to black.\n: - _luminance only_: Indicates any pixels that are clipped because their luminance falls outside of the range set in the \"upper threshold\" and \"lower threshold\" sliders. If this happens, it generally means that tone mapping or exposure settings have been poorly set\n: - _saturation only_: Indicates where over-saturated colors have pushed one or more of the [R,G,B] channels towards a value outside the permitted range of the histogram color space, even though the overall luminance of the pixel may lie within acceptable limits. This means that the pixel's color is impossible to represent in the histogram color space, and can arise from poorly set gamut mapping or saturation settings,\n: - _full gamut_: Shows a combination of the three previous options. This is the default mode, and it gives the most complete indication of potentially problematic pixels.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/clipping.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "color scheme\n: By default, the indicator marks pixels with _red_ where the upper threshold is exceeded (over-clipping) and with _blue_ where the lower threshold is breached (under-clipping). This color scheme can be changed to _black & white_ or _purple & green_ for \"over & under\" indicators, which may be useful to improve visibility for some images.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/clipping.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "lower threshold\n: Expressed in EV relative to the white point (which is nominally EV = 0). If the [R,G,B] channels all fall below this value, an under-clipping indicator is shown warning that the pixel may be end up being crushed to black. Use the following reference to set this threshold, depending on your intended output medium:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/clipping.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": - _8-bit sRGB_ clips blacks at --12.69 EV\n: - _8-bit Adobe RGB_ clips blacks at --19.79 EV\n: - _16-bit sRGB_ clips blacks at --20.69 EV\n: - _fine art matte prints_ typically produce black at --5.30 EV\n: - _color glossy prints_ typically produce black at --8.00 EV\n: - _black & white glossy prints_ typically produce black at --9.00 EV\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/clipping.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "upper threshold\n: How close a pixel should be to the upper limit before being flagged by the clipping warning, expressed as a percentage (default 98%). In the case of gamut checks, this controls how close the saturation of the pixel is allowed to get to the limits of the color space's gamut before a clipping indication is flagged.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Array Element: alias
#: content/views/toolboxes/_index.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Modules/utility-modules/"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A _toolbox_ is a collapsible tool docked in one of the side panels. Unlike the [darkroom processing modules](../darkroom/modules/_index.md), toolboxes do not modify pixels — they help you browse, organize, inspect, export and annotate images, or assess the picture being edited.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Each view shows only the toolboxes relevant to its task, so the same panel position can hold different tools depending on whether you are in the lighttable, the darkroom, the map or the print view.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\nComing from Darktable: many tools that used to be toolbox \"modules\" are now [global menu](../global-menu.md) entries instead, because a grid of buttons is really just a menu in disguise. In particular, _history stack_ copy/paste, _styles_ management, _selection_ helpers, _image_ actions and the maintenance commands now live in the menu bar. The toolboxes below are the ones that remain genuine docked panels.\n{{< /note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Working with toolboxes"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Click a toolbox header to expand or collapse it.\n- A toolbox can be expanded and focused directly through the [global action search](../../getting-started/keyboard.md#vimkey-like-global-action-search) (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>P</kbd>) or an assigned shortcut; triggering its focusing action again collapses it.\n- Drag the inner border of a panel to resize it; panel visibility and size are remembered per view.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Lighttable toolboxes"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Left panel:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- [Library](collections.md) — build the current collection from folders, tags or arbitrary queries.\n- [Metadata editor](metadata-editor.md) — edit title, description, creator and other text metadata.\n- [Tagging](tagging.md) — attach and manage keyword tags.\n- [Geotagging](geotagging.md) — attach GPS coordinates, including from a GPX track.\n- [Image information](image-information.md) — EXIF/IPTC data of the highlighted image.\n- [Notes](notes.md) — free-text notes attached to an image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Second top row:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- [Collection filters](collections.md) — rating, color label, edited status, text search and _restrict to selection_ (see the [lighttable view](../lighttable/_index.md)).\n- Display options — columns, zoom and overlays (see the [lighttable view](../lighttable/_index.md#lighttable-display)).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Reached from the menu bar:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- [Export](export.md) — render the selected images to files (_File → Export…_).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Darkroom toolboxes"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- [Navigation](navigation.md) — pan and zoom the center image from a thumbnail overview.\n- [Scopes](scopes.md) — histogram, waveform and vectorscope, including the [global color picker](global-color-picker.md).\n- [Snapshots](snapshots.md) — freeze and compare edit states.\n- [Duplicate manager](duplicate-manager.md) — manage the versions of the current image.\n- [Shape manager](shape-manager.md) — draw, group and rename every [drawn shape](../darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md) of an image, and choose which modules apply each of them.\n- [History of changes](history-stack.md) — step through, compress or reset the editing history.\n- [Image information](image-information.md) and [Notes](notes.md).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Right panel:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- The [image-processing modules](../darkroom/modules/_index.md), in their [workflow tabs](../darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md#module-workflow-tabs).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Bottom toolbar (visual assessment):\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- [Styles](styles.md), [Color assessment](color-assessment.md), [Raw overexposed](raw-overexposed.md), [Clipping](clipping.md), [Soft proofing](soft-proof.md), [Gamut check](gamut.md), [Guides & overlays](guides-overlays.md), [Focus peaking](focus-peaking.md).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/toolboxes/_index.md content/views/toolboxes/filmstrip.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Filmstrip"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The [filmstrip](filmstrip.md) (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>F</kbd>) is an optional bottom panel available in the darkroom, map and print views. It shows the current collection's thumbnails for quick navigation without returning to the lighttable.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Map toolboxes"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- [Find location](find-location.md) and [Locations](locations.md) — search places and manage location tags.\n- [Geotagging](geotagging.md) and [Map settings](map-settings.md).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Print toolboxes"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- [Print settings](print-settings.md) — page setup, printer, profile and layout for the [print view](../print/_index.md).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- [Lua scripts installer](lua-scripts-installer.md) — manage optional Lua extensions.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/toolboxes/lua-scripts-installer.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Lua scripts installer"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/lua-scripts-installer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This module provides an interface for installing darktable lua scripts. The first time it is run, instructions are displayed in the module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/lua-scripts-installer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "It can be disabled with an option in [preferences > lua options](../../preferences-settings/lua-options.md).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/toolboxes/gamut.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Gamut check"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/gamut.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Highlight areas of the image that may exhibit gamut clipping.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/gamut.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Click the gamut-check icon in the [bottom toolbar](../darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md#bottom-panel) to enable it. Gamut check and [soft proof](./soft-proof.md) are mutually exclusive modes.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/gamut.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Right-click on the icon to open a dialog with configuration parameters -- these are the same as for the [soft proof](./soft-proof.md) option.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/gamut.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "You might also like to consider using the [clipping warning](./clipping.md), which also provides under- and over-exposure warnings as well as a gamut check similar to that offered by this module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Export"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Export the selected images to final files.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Open the export panel from the [global menu](../global-menu.md), _File → Export…_ (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>E</kbd>). Files can be exported to a file on disk, email, various online storage locations, a web album, or a book template.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Storage options"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "target storage\n: The type of location to store your selected images. A number of different back-ends are implemented, including file on disk, LaTeX book template and various web albums. Depending on the selected target, you will be asked to provide additional information, such as filenames or account name and password.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "filename template\n: Define the folder and file to which the image will be exported. This can be automatically generated using several pre-defined variables. See the [variables](../../variables.md) section for details.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "output directory selector\n: The button beside the filename template entry opens a dialog to select the parent directory for export.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "on conflict\n: Choose what to do if the generated filename conflicts with an existing file on export:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": - _create unique filename_: Automatically choose a unique new file name by appending an integer to name of the conflicting file.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": - _overwrite_: Automatically overwrite existing files. This option will present you with a confirmation dialog in order to protect you from accidental data loss -- you can disable this in [preferences > security > ask before exporting in overwrite mode](../../preferences-settings/security.md). _**Note:** This dialog is not presented per-file but as a one-off confirmation before the export job starts._\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": - _skip_: Do not export images where the destination filename already exists.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Format options"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "file format\n: Choose the file format for the exported image. Additional options will appear (below) depending on the selected format.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "quality\n: The quality of the exported file. Higher values lead to larger file sizes. The default quality (95) is a good setting for very high quality exports (e.g. for archiving or printing purposes). If you need a good compromise between size and quality (e.g. for online image display or uploads) you should consider a value of “90” instead.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "bit depth\n: The number of bits used for each color channel. More bits means less posterization/color banding.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "compression\n: The type of compression to use.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "compression level\n: For export formats where _compression_ can be specified, the _compression level_ specifies how much compression to apply. The higher the level, the more the data will be compressed, at the cost of more CPU cycles.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "b&w image\n: For TIFF export format, it is possible to save a monochrome image. This setting controls whether the resulting file encodes the shades of gray as separate RGB channels, or as a single grayscale channel. The latter option will result in smaller files.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Global options"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "set size\n: Choose how to measure the maximum size of your exported image\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": - _in pixels (for file)_: Enter the maximum width and height in pixels.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": - _in cm (for print)_: Enter the maximum width and height in cm and define the image's dpi. The equivalent size in pixels will be automatically calculated.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": - _in inch (for print)_: Enter the maximum width and height in inches and define the image's dpi. The equivalent size in pixels will be automatically calculated.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": - _by scale (for file)_: Enter a multiplier to specify by how much the exported image should be scaled compared to the input image. For example, entering a value of 0.5 will result in an output image with half the width and height (in pixels) of the original image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "dpi\n: If units of cm or inches are chosen, set the dpi of the output image. The dpi will also be stored in the Exif data of the exported image. It will be automatically set to 300 if \"in pixels\" or \"by scale\" is chosen.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "max size\n: Set the maximum width and height of the exported image(s) in pixels, cm or inches (depending on the selected unit) -- zero means that no constraint will be set on that dimension. Exported images will be constrained so as not to exceed either of these values, while retaining the correct aspect ratio. Set both to zero to export with the original dimensions (after cropping). If the entered values exceed the original dimensions Ansel will either export with the original dimensions or upscale the image, depending on the \"allow upscaling\" parameter.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "allow upscaling\n: Set to “yes” to perform an upscaling step if the user-defined maximum width and height exceed the original dimensions of the image. If set to “no” the exported image's dimensions will not exceed the dimensions of the original image (after cropping).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "high quality resampling\n: Set this to 'yes' to perform high quality resampling on the image. The image will be processed in full resolution and only downscaled at the very end. This can sometimes result in better quality, but will always be slower.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "store masks\n: Store masks as layers in exported image. Only works for some image formats (currently TIFF and XCF).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "profile\n: The output color profile. Select “image settings” if you want the settings in the [_output color profile_](../darkroom/modules/output-color-profile.md) module of the individual images to be respected.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "intent\n: This option lets you define the intent -- the way in which Ansel will handle out-of-gamut colors. See [rendering intent](../../color-management/rendering-intent.md) for a more detailed description of the available options.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "style\n: Choose a [style](./styles.md) which Ansel will combine with the existing history stack to generate the output image. These history items are only added temporarily -- the original history stack is not overwritten. You can use this feature to add processing steps and parameters that you want to be applied specifically to images before export. For example you may define a style that adds a stronger level of sharpening when you produce scaled-down JPEG files for the internet or add a certain level of exposure compensation to all of your output images.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "mode\n: When applying a style during export this option defines whether the history stack items of that style replace the original history stack of the image or are appended to it. Technically speaking, in append mode history stack items of the style will constitute separate instances of the respective modules on top of any existing ones. As a consequence the original history stack will remain in effect with the new items being applied in addition. This way you can apply an overall adjustment (e.g. exposure) to a number of exported images while respecting the settings of each individual image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "export\n: Press this button to start a background job to export all selected images. A bar at the bottom of the left hand panel displays the progress of the export job. Furthermore a notification message pops up reporting the completion of each individual export. You may click on the pop-up to make it disappear. You may abort the export job by clicking on the \"x\" icon located close to the progress bar.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Note:** Images that are selected but currently hidden (because they are members of a collapsed group) will not be exported.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Metadata preferences"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The “preferences…” option in the presets menu brings up a dialog where you can configure what metadata to include in exported files. The parameters entered into this dialog are saved along with other export parameters to user presets, and the last entered values are retained when Ansel is closed.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "General settings"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The left-hand-side of this dialog allows you to choose which groups of metadata are to be exported with the image. The following options are available:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "exif data\n: Export the source image's Exif data.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "metadata\n: Export metadata defined in the [metadata editor](./metadata-editor.md) module. Only metadata fields that are tagged as _visible_ and are not tagged as _private_ will be exported.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "geo tags\n: Export geo tags.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "tags\n: Export tags  created in the [tagging](./tagging.md) module (to `Xmp.dc.Subject`). Three additional options can also be selected:\n: - _private tags_: Export private tags\n: - _synonyms_: Export tag synonyms\n: - _omit hierarchy_: Only export the last part of hierarchical tags\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "hierarchical tags\n: Export hierarchical tags (to `Xmp.lr.Hierarchical Subject`)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "develop history\n: Export the entire development history (history stack and shapes) where supported (e.g. JPEG, JPEG2000, TIFF). The development history will be stored as XMP tags within the output file. This information can later be used to reconstruct the parameters and settings that were used to produce the exported image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Caution:** For various reasons embedding XMP tags into output files may fail without notice, e.g. if certain size limits are exceeded. Users are therefore advised to not rely on this feature for their backup strategy. To back up your data always make sure to save your input (raw) file as well as all of Ansel's XMP sidecar files.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Per metadata settings"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The right-hand-side of this dialog allows you to define formulas to populate image metadata. The formulas defined here have priority over the settings in the left-hand-side of the dialog. The first column identifies the entry to be edited. The second column allows you to define how to calculate the value for that metadata entry using a formula.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "See the [variables](../../variables.md) section for details of the variables you can use in your metadata formula. Press Enter to validate the formula. Leave the formula empty to prevent a given metadata entry from being exported (for example `Exif.GPSInfo.GPSVersionID`).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Use the “--” icon to remove a metadata entry from the list and the “+” icon to add a new one from a predefined list of available metadata tags.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Click on the \"add\" button to add a metadata entry to the list.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The formulas allow you virtually define all the metadata you need to qualify your images in tagging and export the values in the XMP or IPTC tags of your choice. The exported tags can be different from one export to the next depending on the destination of the images. Tags and Categories are displayed separately in image information.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "_Remember that a tag set up as a category is never exported._\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- To prevent a specific metadata field from being exported, add it to the list and leave the formula empty.\n- To force a specific exif metadata field to be exported when exif export is disabled, add it to the list and enter `=` into the formula.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-perspective.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Examples"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "example 1\n: A first level tag called places is set as a category, and is followed by four levels of information (or keywords): country, region, city and location (e.g. `places|France|Nord|Lille|rue Nationale`). Each level can be retrieved (when it is defined) by one of the variables `$(CATEGORY0(places))`, `$(CATEGORY1(places))`, `$(CATEGORY2(places))` and `$(CATEGORY3(places))`. In this example, the returned values are \"France\", \"Nord\", \"Lille\" and \"rue Nationale\", respectively. These keywords can also be retrieved as simple tags using the variable `$(TAGS)`. The last keyword level defined (the leaf) is displayed in [image information](./image-information.md), here \"rue Nationale\".\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "example 2\n: A first level tag called creator is followed by the name of the photographer, both set as categories: `creator|firstname lastname`. The formula copyrights (`$(YEAR) $(CATEGORY0(creator))`) builds the text associated with image rights. Here, [image information](./image-information.md) displays \"creator: firstname lastname\" as categories. Neither creator nor \"firstname lastname\" appear in the tags list and they are not exported as simple tags.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/export.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Note:** tagging is not appropriate to define free text metadata, like a title or a description, which may be specific to each image. Use the [metadata editor](./metadata-editor.md) for this type of information.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/toolboxes/image-information.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "EXIF and IPTC"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/image-information.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Display the EXIF and IPTC information (metadata) embedded in an image, plus a number of extra fields Ansel maintains (filmroll, version, local copy, etc.). The module is available in most views, in the left panel.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/image-information.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "As you **hover** thumbnails in the lighttable or filmstrip, the panel updates to show the image under the cursor. This is a read-only preview and changes nothing.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/image-information.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When several images are selected and no single image is highlighted, the module shows only the fields that are identical across the selection; differing fields display _\\<various values\\>_ instead.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/image-information.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In the lighttable, **double-click** the _filmroll_ field to jump to a collection of all images in that film roll.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/image-information.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Choosing the displayed fields"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/image-information.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Open _preferences…_ from the module's presets menu to get the list of all available fields. The **visible** checkbox toggles each field on or off, and you can drag a row to reorder the display. These choices can be saved as module presets. The module's reset button restores all fields, in their default order.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/toolboxes/soft-proof.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Soft proof"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/soft-proof.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "View your image rendered using a selected color profile.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/soft-proof.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Click the soft-proof icon in the [bottom toolbar](../darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md#bottom-panel) to activate soft-proof display mode. This lets you preview your image rendered through a printer profile, to see how colors will end up on the final print. A “soft proof” message at the bottom-left of the image confirms you are in soft-proof mode.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/soft-proof.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Right-click on the icon to open a dialog with the following configuration parameters. For each of these parameters, the list of available profiles is read from `$ANSEL/share/ansel/color/out` and `$HOME/.config/ansel/color/out` (where `$ANSEL` represents Ansel's installation directory and `$HOME` your home directory).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/soft-proof.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "display profile\n: Set the color profile for the display. The option “system display profile” is the preferred setting when working with a calibrated display. The profile is taken either from your system's color manager or from your X display server. The method Ansel uses to detect your system display profile can be changed in [preferences > miscellaneous](../../preferences-settings/miscellaneous.md). For more information see the [display profile](../../color-management/display-profile.md) section.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/soft-proof.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "intent\n: Set the rendering intent for your display -- only available if rendering with LittleCMS2 is activated. See [rendering intent](../../color-management/rendering-intent.md) for a list of available options. This option appears twice -- once for the \"display profile\" and once for the \"preview display profile\".\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/soft-proof.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "softproof profile\n: Set the color profile for soft proofing. Typically these profiles are supplied by your printer or generated during printer profiling.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/soft-proof.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "histogram profile\n: Set the color profile of the histogram. None of the available options are ideal, however, \"system display profile\" is probably the _least bad_ setting, since all other profiles are derived from the display color space and at least the values will conform to what you see on screen.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/toolboxes/global-color-picker.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Global color picker"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/global-color-picker.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The global color picker samples colors from the current image, displays their values in several color spaces, and lets you store and compare samples from different locations. It is part of the [scopes](scopes.md) module (the _Color picker_ and _Live samples_ sections), in the darkroom [left panel](../darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md#left-panel).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/global-color-picker.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Activate it with the color-picker button. It keeps sampling until you turn it off or leave the darkroom.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/global-color-picker.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\nThis **global** color picker is distinct from the **local** color pickers found inside many [modules](../darkroom/modules/_index.md) (e.g. [RGB curve](../darkroom/modules/curve.md)), which set that module's parameters. The two do not work in the same color space: the global picker samples at the **end of the preview pipeline** (display space), whereas a local picker reads the input or output of the module it belongs to.\n{{< /note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/global-color-picker.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/finalscale.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Controls"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/global-color-picker.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Point / area mode\n: **Click** the picker button for point mode (a small spot under the cursor). **<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+click** or **right-click** for area mode (the average over a drawn rectangle).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/global-color-picker.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Statistic (mean / min / max)\n: In area mode, choose whether to display the **mean**, **minimum** or **maximum** channel values of the sampled region. (In point mode the three are identical.)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/global-color-picker.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Color mode\n: Choose the color space in which the sampled values are shown. Values converted to spaces other than the native display space are approximate.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/global-color-picker.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Color swatch\n: A patch of the sampled color is shown next to the numerical values; click it to enlarge it. Right-click the numerical values to copy them to the clipboard.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/global-color-picker.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Hover any value for a tooltip with more detail, including RGB and Lab values and an approximate color name.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/global-color-picker.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Live samples"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/global-color-picker.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Press **add** to store the current sample as a _live sample_. Each live sample keeps its own swatch and values (with its own statistic and color mode).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/global-color-picker.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Live samples are **not locked** by default: they update as you change the edit, which lets you watch how a parameter affects different parts of the image. Click a live sample's swatch to **lock** it (a lock icon appears) so later changes no longer affect it — handy for before/after comparisons.\n- Hover a live sample's **delete** button to highlight its region in the preview.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/global-color-picker.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Display samples on image\n: Show the live-sample locations on the image and on the [vectorscope](scopes.md#vectorscope).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/global-color-picker.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Restrict scope to selection\n: Restrict the [scopes](scopes.md) to only the values inside the picked point/area, to inspect the tones present in that region. When a local color picker is active in a module, this restricts the scope to that module's picked area instead.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/toolboxes/color-assessment.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Color assessment"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/color-assessment.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Assess colors and brightness in your image using ISO 12646:2008 recommended viewing conditions.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/color-assessment.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When developing an image, the way we perceive brightness, contrast and saturation is influenced by the surrounding ambient conditions. If an image is displayed against a dark background, this can have a number of adverse effects on our perception of that image:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/color-assessment.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Exaggeration of the perceived exposure makes the image seems brighter than it really is. This is nicely illustrated by the [Adelson checkerboard shadow effect](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Checker_shadow_illusion).\n- A decrease in the perceived saturation in the image makes the colors seem less rich than they really are (the Hunt effect).\n- A decrease in the perceived contrast in the image makes the tones seem flatter than they really are (Bartleson-Breneman effect 3)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/color-assessment.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The end result is that the final image can end up being too dark and overly-processed in terms of contrast and color saturation. To avoid this, the \"ISO 12646:2008\" standard makes some recommendations about the conditions under which the colors of an image should be assessed. The _color assessment_ module in the darkroom places a frame around the image to help the user better assess the colors in the image, along the lines of those recommendations.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/color-assessment.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "![the color-assessment frame around an image](color-assessment-overview.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/color-assessment.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When the color-assessment button is selected in the [bottom toolbar](../darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md#bottom-panel), the image is zoomed out so that a thick mid-gray border appears around the image to act as a reference against which to compare the image's tones. A thinner white border is placed immediately around the image to give the eyes a basis for comparison when looking at parts of the image that are meant to be a bright white.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/color-assessment.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Although the color assessment mode provides a mid-gray surrounding to the image, it is recommended that you also set your user interface (in [preferences > general](../../preferences-settings/general.md)) to one of the \"grey\" themes. These themes are designed to provide a user interface that is close to middle gray (it is actually slightly darker to allow better contrast with the text in the user interface). When one of these themes is used together with the color assessment mode, this will help to avoid the above perception issues.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/color-assessment.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Color assessment mode has no default keyboard shortcut, but you can assign one to it, or trigger it from the [global action search](../../getting-started/keyboard.md#vimkey-like-global-action-search).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/styles.md content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Styles"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/styles.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A **style** is a named, saved editing recipe — a subset of a history stack that can be applied to any number of images. Styles let you reuse looks, technical corrections, or module configurations without having to redo them from scratch.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/styles.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Styles are accessible from two places:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/styles.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- The **Styles menu** in the menu bar, which lists all your styles and applies them in one click.\n- The **Styles toolbox module**, available as a floating window via **Styles → Manage styles…**\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/styles.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Applying a style"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/styles.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Click any style name in the **Styles menu** to apply it to all currently active images. If **Ask merge settings before apply** is enabled (the default), a [merge settings dialog](#merge-settings-dialog) appears first. See the [copy-paste history](../lighttable/history-copy-paste.md) page for a full explanation of what merge position and pipeline order mean.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/styles.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Double-clicking a style name in the **Styles toolbox module** applies it to all selected images using the currently saved settings, without showing the dialog.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/styles.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\nStyle application and [copy-paste](../lighttable/history-copy-paste.md) share the same underlying merge engine but maintain **independent** default settings. Changing the merge mode for styles has no effect on paste operations, and vice versa.\n{{< /note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/styles.md
#: content/views/lighttable/history-copy-paste.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Merge settings dialog"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/styles.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When **Ask merge settings before apply** is active, clicking a style in the Styles menu opens a dialog with two choices:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/styles.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Merge position\n: how the style's modules are placed relative to the image's existing history:\n    - **Below (Prepend)** — the style becomes the base; your existing edits run afterwards and override conflicts. Use this for decorative looks that sit underneath per-image corrections.\n    - **Above (Append)** — your existing edits become the base; the style runs afterwards and overrides conflicts. Use this for technical corrections (calibration, profiling) that must take precedence.\n    - **Replace** — your existing history is discarded and replaced entirely by the style.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/styles.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Use incoming pipeline order\n: when checked, the module execution order stored in the style replaces yours. This option is **disabled** when the style was saved without a pipeline order; a tooltip explains this when you hover over the greyed-out checkbox.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/styles.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ask me every time\n: when unchecked, the saved defaults are used silently on future applies. You can re-enable the dialog from **Styles → Ask merge settings before apply**.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/toolboxes/styles.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Default settings (Styles menu)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/styles.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The merge mode and pipeline order defaults for style application are controlled at the bottom of the Styles menu:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/styles.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Styles → History pasting mode**\n: Choose **Prepend**, **Append**, or **Replace** as the default merge position.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/styles.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Styles → Nodes pasting mode → Copy module order**\n: Toggle whether the style's pipeline order is applied by default.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/styles.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Styles → Ask merge settings before apply**\n: Toggle whether the dialog is shown each time a style is applied from the menu.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/styles.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Creating a style"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/toolboxes/styles.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "From the history stack module (darkroom)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/styles.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Open the [history stack](./history-stack.md) panel in the darkroom. Click the **create style** button (the icon to the right of \"compress history stack\"). A dialog lets you name the style, add an optional description, and choose which modules from the current history to include.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/toolboxes/styles.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "From the Styles toolbox module"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/styles.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Click **create** in the toolbox. The same dialog appears, drawing from the history stack of the single selected image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/toolboxes/styles.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Hierarchical names"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/styles.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Use the pipe character `|` as a separator in the style name to create sub-categories. For example, a style named `print|tone curve +0.5 EV` appears under a **print** category in both the Styles menu and the toolbox. Categories can be nested.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/toolboxes/styles.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Saving pipeline order"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/styles.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When creating a style, the pipeline order (module execution sequence) is saved alongside the module parameters only if you check the appropriate option in the creation dialog. Styles without a saved order are still fully functional — Ansel will keep the destination image's existing order when applying them.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/styles.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Managing styles"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/styles.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The **Styles toolbox module** is the primary interface for managing styles. Open it via **Styles → Manage styles…** in the menu bar.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/styles.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "edit\n: Open a dialog to include or exclude individual modules from an existing style. Tick **duplicate** to create a new style instead of overwriting the existing one (you will need to supply a new unique name).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/styles.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "remove\n: Delete the selected style immediately, without a confirmation prompt.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/styles.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "import\n: Load a style from a `.dtstyle` XML file. If a style with the same name already exists you will be asked whether to overwrite it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/styles.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "export\n: Save the selected style to disk as a `.dtstyle` file for backup or sharing.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/styles.md
#: content/views/lighttable/history-copy-paste.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Keyboard shortcuts"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/styles.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "You can assign a keyboard shortcut to any style in **Edit → Keyboard shortcuts…** and then press that shortcut from anywhere in the lighttable or darkroom to apply the style to all active images using the current saved merge settings (the dialog is not shown for shortcut-triggered applies).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Array Element: aliases
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "../toolboxes/mask-manager"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Shape manager"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The shape manager is a separate, floating window where you draw, name, group and delete the shapes an image is masked with, and decide which modules each of them applies to. Open or close it from its toggle button in the darkroom [bottom toolbar](../darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md#bottom-panel).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "It works on the same shapes as the per-module [masking & blending](../darkroom/masking-and-blending/_index.md) controls, in the _Drawn_ tab of the [left panel](../darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md#left-panel): a shape drawn here is the same object a module's [drawn mask](../darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md) is built from, and vice-versa. What this window adds is the view across modules — every shape the image carries on one side, every group a module draws with on the other. It is from there that a shape is reused, or handed to a second module, without visiting each module in turn.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The window stays on top of the main window without taking focus, so you can keep drawing on the canvas while it is open. Move and resize it wherever suits your screen: it reopens where you left it, and each list keeps the height you dragged it to.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The shape manager works in the darkroom only. Leave it open while you switch to another view and its lists empty, the whole window greying out until you come back to the darkroom.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "One shape, several uses"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A shape exists only once, and several groups can use it at the same time: the row naming it in a group is a use of the shape, not a copy of it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "What defines the shape — its position, its size, its outline — belongs to the shape itself and so holds everywhere: change it from one module and it changes in all the others. What describes a use — the _set operator_, the _polarity_, the _opacity_ and the order of application — belongs to the group and commits no one else.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Every action on this page reads that way:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Attaching and grouping add a use. Nothing is moved or copied: the shape stays in the groups already using it.\n- Detaching removes one use, and the shape survives with all the others.\n- Deleting ends the shape, and all of its uses go with it.\n- **Duplicate shape** is the only entry that really makes a second shape, independent of the first.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The shape manager window"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< figure src=\"views/toolboxes/shape_manager/shape-manager-window.en.png\" alt=\"The shape manager window: the shape buttons and the All shapes and Module groups lists\" class=\"align-left borderless framed tight\" />}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The window offers the shape buttons and two lists. The lists answer two different questions, and a shape a module uses appears in both — once as itself in _All shapes_, once as a member of that module's group in _Module groups_.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< param-table >}}\n| **Shape buttons** {{< icon src=\"icon/icon-shapes.png\" >}} <div>The five drawing tools — _Circle_, _Ellipse_, _Polygon_, _Brush_ and _Gradient_. See [creating a shape](#creating-a-shape).</div> |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| **All shapes** <div>The image's inventory: every shape and every group drawn on this image, whether or not a module uses it. The groups modules draw with come first, in pipeline order, as single rows that do not expand; a rule separates them from the groups no module uses yet and from every individual shape.</div> |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| **Module groups** <div>The groups the pipeline actually draws with: one row per module carrying a drawn mask, with the shapes it is built from nested inside it. Groups come in the order the [_Pipeline_ tab](../darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md#module-workflow-tabs) lists modules in — and the list follows along when modules are reordered.</div> |\n{{< /param-table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Each new shape gets an automatic name made of its type (_brush_, _circle_, _ellipse_, _polygon_, _gradient_) and an incrementing number, and a group created for a module is named `Group <module name>`, after the module it serves. Double-click a top-level name in either list to rename it — meaningful names help a lot when reusing a shape across several modules. Press <kbd>Enter</kbd> to keep the new name or <kbd>Escape</kbd> to restore the previous one.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Rows"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A row carries some or all of the following, depending on the shape and on where the row sits.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Indications**:\n{{< param-table >}}\n| {{< icon src=\"masks_union.jpg\" alt=\"\" >}} **Set operator**<div>How this row combines with the result built from the rows applied before it — [Union, Intersection, Difference or Exclusion](../darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/set-operators.md). Never on the first row of a group, which has nothing before it to combine with.</div> |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| {{< icon src=\"icon/icon-polarity.png\" alt=\"\" >}} **Polarity**<div>**Invert shape** is on for this row: everything its shape does not enclose is selected instead.</div> |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| **Name**<div>The shape's or group's own name, the same on every row naming it.</div> |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| **Opacity**<div>Follows the name, as a percentage, and only when it is not 100%.</div> |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| {{< icon src=\"icon/icon-paperclip.png\" alt=\"\" >}} **Attached**<div>This shape or group is attached to at least one group. Hovering the icon lists them, one per line. Visible only on a top-level row.</div> |\n{{< /param-table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Buttons**:\n{{< param-table >}}\n| {{< icon src=\"icon/icon-plus.png\" alt=\"\" >}} **Attach**<div>Attaches this row to the group selected in the _Module groups_ list. Only in the _All shapes_ list, and greyed out when it can do nothing — see [attaching to the selected group](#attaching-to-the-selected-group).</div> |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| {{< icon src=\"icon/icon-minus.png\" alt=\"\" >}} **Detach**<div>Detaches this shape from the group it sits in, on a row nested under a group. The shape itself is kept and stays available for reuse.</div> |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| {{< icon src=\"icon/icon-list.png\" alt=\"\" >}} **Module group management**<div>Opens the [module chooser](#the-module-chooser), which manages every module using this shape or group at once. Only in the _All shapes_ list.</div> |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| {{< icon src=\"icon/icon-trash.png\" alt=\"\" >}} **Delete**<div>Deletes this shape or group outright, on a top-level row. It is removed from every group holding it and from the list of shapes.</div> |\n{{< /param-table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Selection"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< figure src=\"views/toolboxes/shape_manager/shape-manager-selecting.en.png\" alt=\"Several rows selected at the same level of the same group\" class=\"align-left borderless framed\" />}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- <kbd class=\"mouse\">Click</kbd> a row to display just that shape, or that whole group, on the canvas with its editing controls. This is the reliable way to grab one shape among many overlapping ones, and it works the other way round too: select a shape on the canvas, or in a module's _Drawn_ tab, and its row is highlighted here.\n- <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd class=\"mouse\">click</kbd> (<kbd>⌘</kbd>+<kbd class=\"mouse\">click</kbd> on macOS) adds a row to the selection or takes it back out.\n- <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd class=\"mouse\">click</kbd> extends the selection to the row you click.\n- <kbd class=\"mouse\">Click</kbd> empty space to select nothing, which hides the shapes again.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\nOnly rows that are peers — at the same level, and under the same group — can be selected together, so anything that does not qualify drops out of the selection.\n{{< /note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Creating a shape"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Click one of the five shape buttons and draw on the canvas, exactly as you would from a module's [_Drawn_ tab](../darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md#creating-a-shape) — the same creation mode, the same gestures, the same parameters. A shape drawn from these buttons belongs to no module: it lands in the _All shapes_ list, ready to be given to whichever modules you decide on afterwards.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "To draw straight into an existing group instead, <kbd class=\"mouse\">right-click</kbd> its row in the _Module groups_ list and pick a shape from **Add new shape …**. The shape is added to that group as soon as you finish drawing it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Attach a shape in a module"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Attaching a shape to a module means adding it to the group that module draws with — one more [use](#one-shape-several-uses), which cannot disturb the modules already applying it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A group cannot take a shape it already holds, at any depth: whatever would attach it there is greyed out for as long as it is in the group, directly or in one of the groups it contains.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "There are two ways to do it, and they differ in what they act on.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Attaching to the selected group"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Select a group in the _Module groups_ list, then click {{< icon src=\"icon/icon-plus.png\" alt=\"Plus\" >}} on any row of the _All shapes_ list. The shape is attached to that group, applied last, combined with **Union**. Selecting a shape nested inside a group works too: the {{< icon src=\"icon/icon-plus.png\" alt=\"Plus\" >}} always attaches to the group that row belongs to.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The module chooser"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- <kbd class=\"mouse\">Click</kbd> {{< icon src=\"icon/icon-list.png\" alt=\"List\" >}} on a row of the _All shapes_ list to open **Modules using this shape** — a window listing every module that can carry a drawn mask, disabled ones included. A module is ticked when the shape sits directly in its group.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< figure src=\"views/toolboxes/shape_manager/shape-manager-module-chooser.en.png\" alt=\"The Modules using this shape window, with a tick box per module\" style=\"width:50%\" class=\"align-left borderless framed\" />}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Tick a module to attach the shape, untick one to detach.\n- Press **Apply** to save changes.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Once applied, a ticked module that has no group yet is given one, named after it, and the shape is put inside it; a module whose group is left empty by an untick loses it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A box that could change nothing is greyed out, with the reason on its row: the shape already reaches the module through a nested group, attaching it would put the module's mask inside itself, or the group on offer brings no shape the module does not already apply.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\nTicking one module and unticking another in the same pass is fine — the removals are applied first, so the two cannot collide. Only the boxes you actually moved are acted on, so a shape a module already carries is never rewritten.\n{{< /note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Building a group"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A group is a container holding shapes and other groups, in order. It is a recipe rather than a picture: what a module renders from its group is its [drawn mask](../darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md), and the mask the module finally blends with is built from that. The shape manager stays on the recipe side — it lists groups and the shapes in them, never masks. Hence the two lists: _All shapes_ holds every group the image carries, _Module groups_ only those a module draws with.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Groups come from two places:\n- A module builds its own the first time you give it a shape, and names it after itself, `Group <module name>` — rename the module and the group follows.\n- The ones you build yourself, with **New group from selection**, are named `Group <number>` and serve no module: they wait in the _All shapes_ list until you hand one to a module, which then renders it like any other shape. A group can serve several modules at once, and a module's own group can sit inside another group.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A group applies its shapes in list order: each one combines with the result of those before it through one of four [set operators](../darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/set-operators.md). Order changes the result, which is what **Move up** and **Move down** in the context menu are for.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Grouping shapes"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Select the rows to group — one or several, in either list — and <kbd class=\"mouse\">right-click</kbd> one of them.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< param-table >}}\n| **New group from selection**<div>Gathers the selection into a new group named `Group <number>`, each member combined with **Union** by default. The group joins the _All shapes_ list, among the groups no module uses yet. Name it after what it gathers: that is what will tell it apart from the other `Group <number>`.</div> |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| **Attach to the group**<div>Lists every group of the image, module groups included, and attaches the selection to the one you pick, applied last, each member combined with **Union**. A group that can take none of the selection — it already holds the whole of it, at any depth, or it sits inside a selected group — is listed greyed rather than left out, so you can see where the shapes already are; picking a group that can take part of the selection attaches that part only. The selected groups themselves are not listed, nor are the groups a retouching module such as [_retouch_](../darkroom/modules/retouch.md) keeps for itself.</div> |\n{{< /param-table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A new group changes nothing in the image until a module uses it.\nNesting a group inside another attaches all of its shapes at once: add one to the nested group and every module using the holding group applies it straight away.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A shape can only repeat within a group through a nested group: the group refuses a shape it already holds, but accepts a group bringing it new ones, even if one of them is already there. In the _Module groups_ list, every application after the first has its name struck through and is marked _Already in '<group>'_, naming the group that holds that first application. It is a remark, not a warning: **Union** and **Intersection** give the same result the second time, but **Difference** and **Exclusion** compound, so a repeat can be exactly what you want.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Detaching and deleting shapes"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Where the row sits decides which of the two you are offered — a row nested under a group is one use of a shape, a top-level row is the shape itself — so the two are never on offer at once.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Detaching a shape from a group"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< param-table >}}\n| {{< icon src=\"icon/icon-minus.png\" alt=\"Minus\" >}}, or **Remove shape from mask**<div>On a row nested under a group. Takes the shape out of that group and no other: the shape survives, still listed in _All shapes_ and still applied by every other module using it.</div> |\n{{< /param-table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Deleting a shape or a group"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< param-table >}}\n| {{< icon src=\"icon/icon-trash.png\" alt=\"Trash\" >}}, or **Delete shape** / **Delete group**<div>On a top-level row. Deletes it for good: it is dropped from every group holding it and from the list of shapes, so every module applying it loses it at once. The _trash_ asks for confirmation first, unless you turn that off — see [Preferences > Security](../../preferences-settings/security.md).</div> |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| **Delete unused shapes**<div>Sweeps the image clean of every shape no group currently holds, in one go.</div> |\n{{< /param-table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Either way, a group left empty is deleted with its last member, and the group holding that one goes too if that empties it. A module left without a group has no drawn mask until you give it a shape again.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\nAnsel keeps every shape ever defined for an image until you explicitly remove it. If you export with the development history, all defined shapes are written to the XMP. A very long list of shapes can exceed the size limit of some file formats and make XMP writing fail on export — prune unused shapes when in doubt.\n{{< /note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The context menu"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "<kbd class=\"mouse\">Right-click</kbd> a row, or the empty space no row occupies, in either list. There is no single menu: it is assembled from the sections below, each separated from the next by a rule. A section you do not see is one whose question does not arise for the row you clicked.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Selecting several rows drops whatever only makes sense one row at a time: the parameter sliders, the four set operators, **Duplicate shape**, and the two entries a group opens with. The rest acts on the whole selection.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Rows deeper than the second level — the shapes of a group nested inside another group — are offered neither the combining and reordering entries nor **Remove shape from mask**: they belong to the nested group, not to the one whose row you opened. Open the menu on the row where they sit directly under their own group instead: under that group's row in the _All shapes_ list, or under its module's row in _Module groups_.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Adjusting, combining and ordering"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< figure src=\"views/toolboxes/shape_manager/shape-manager-context-menu.en.png\" alt=\"The context menu opened on a shape sitting in a group\" class=\"align-left borderless framed tight\" />}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "On a single shape, and only while a group holds it: these sliders read and write the shape's row in a group, so a shape no group holds yet is offered none of them. On a top-level row they act through the first group holding the shape, which matters for **Opacity** — a property of the row rather than of the shape itself.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< param-table >}}\n| **Parameter sliders**<div>**Size** and **Opacity** on every shape, **Fading** on all but a Gradient, which carries a **Curvature** instead, and **Rotation** on an Ellipse and on a Gradient. They set the shape's [parameters](../darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md#shape-parameters) precisely — the values the [mouse wheel](../darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md#mouse-wheel) reaches on the canvas — without selecting the shape there first.</div> |\n{{< /param-table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "On a row sitting in a group, a shape or a nested group alike: these entries set how the row combines with the ones applied before it, and where it comes in that order.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< param-table >}}\n| **Operation** | **Invert shape**<div>Inverts the polarity of each selected row within that group: everything it does not enclose is selected instead.</div> |\n| | **Union** / **Intersection** / **Difference** / **Exclusion**<div>The [set operator](../darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/set-operators.md) combining the row with the result built from the rows before it. On a single row only, combining being a question with no answer for several shapes at once.</div> |\n{{< /param-table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< param-table >}}\n| **Move up** / **down**<div>Moves the selection within the group, which changes what it covers.</div> |\n{{< /param-table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Adding to a group"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< figure src=\"views/toolboxes/shape_manager/shape-manager-context-menu-group.en.png\" alt=\"The context menu opened on a group row of the All shapes list\" class=\"align-left borderless framed tight\" />}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "These two entries appear on a group, wherever it sits. Empty space offers only the first, and what it draws there belongs to no group — see [creating a shape](#creating-a-shape).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< param-table >}}\n| **Add new shape …**<div>Draws a new shape — _Brush_, _Circle_, _Ellipse_, _Polygon_ or _Gradient_ — straight into this group, as soon as you finish drawing it.</div> |\n| **Attach shape …**<div>Lists the image's existing shapes and groups, each followed by the other modules already applying it, and attaches the one you pick to this group. Anything the group cannot take — it already holds it, at any depth, or the group would end up inside itself — is listed greyed rather than left out.</div> |\n{{< /param-table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Grouping the selection"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "On any selection, one row or several, wherever the rows sit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< param-table >}}\n| **New group from selection** / **Attach to the group**<div>Puts the selected rows in a new group, or in an existing one — see [grouping shapes](#grouping-shapes).</div> |\n{{< /param-table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Duplicating, detaching and deleting"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "These entries come last, and there the two levels part ways: a top-level row is the shape, a row inside a group is one use of it — see [detaching and deleting shapes](#detaching-and-deleting-shapes).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< param-table >}}\n| **Duplicate shape**<div>On a single top-level shape. Makes an independent copy of it, belonging to no group; give it to modules as you would any other shape.</div> |\n| **Delete shape** / **Delete group**<div>On top-level rows. Deletes the selection for good — **Delete group** when it is a single group, **Delete shape** otherwise.</div> |\n| **Remove shape from mask**<div>On rows inside a group. Takes them out of that group without deleting them — they stay in _All shapes_, available to reuse elsewhere.</div> |\n| **Delete unused shapes**<div>On every row and on empty space alike. Sweeps the image clean of every shape no group currently holds.</div> |\n{{< /param-table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/toolboxes/metadata-editor.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Metadata editor"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/metadata-editor.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Edit the metadata of selected images.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/metadata-editor.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Metadata is freeformat text (title, description, creator, publisher, rights etc.) that describes your images.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/metadata-editor.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When several images are selected having different values for a given metadata field, the module displays _<leave unchanged>_ for that field -- if you choose to apply changes, these fields will not be changed. If you right-click on the field the different values are listed at the end of the contextual menu. Select one of the values in the menu to apply that value to all of the selected images -- the change will be saved once you press the \"apply\" button or the Enter/Tab key.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/metadata-editor.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "reset\n: Delete visible (see below) metadata from the selected images.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/metadata-editor.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "metadata entry fields\n: A separate field is displayed for each metadata item. Hold Ctrl while scrolling with your mouse to increase the height of a field. Press Ctrl+Enter to insert a new line. Double-click on a field's label to delete the contents of that field.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/metadata-editor.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "apply\n: Write the metadata from the entry fields to the selected images.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/metadata-editor.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The _rights_ field offers a set of ready-made Creative Commons licenses (CC BY, CC BY-SA, CC BY-ND, CC BY-NC, CC BY-NC-SA, CC BY-NC-ND) and _all rights reserved_ as presets, so you can fill it in without typing the full text.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/toolboxes/metadata-editor.md
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Keyboard"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/metadata-editor.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "You may use the keyboard to navigate and apply changes while any of the metadata entry boxes have focus:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/metadata-editor.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- The Tab key saves the current field and moves the cursor to the next field. When the last field is reached, the Tab key returns focus to the first field.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/metadata-editor.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Shift+Tab works the same as Tab, but in the opposite direction.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/metadata-editor.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- The Enter key saves the current field without moving the cursor.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/toolboxes/metadata-editor.md content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Preferences"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/metadata-editor.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The \"preferences…\" option in the presets menu brings up a dialog where you can configure how metadata is handled within Ansel. For each metadata item, two check boxes allow you to restrict how metadata is handled:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/metadata-editor.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "visible\n: Show or hide this metadata field. Hidden fields are not included in exported images.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/metadata-editor.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "private\n: Keep this metadata field private. Private fields are not included in exported images.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/notes.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Notes"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/notes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Write per-image notes that live next to your files and render as Markdown. Such notes can be to-do lists, memos, contacts of the persons in the image, tips or instructions for collaborators, etc.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/notes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The module opens in **preview** by default; switch to **edit** to change the source text. The Markdown parsing needs Ansel to be build with [CMark](https://github.com/commonmark/cmark), which is optional. If Ansel was not built with this library, it will be rendered as plain text.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/notes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Notes are stored as a sidecar text file named `<image basename>.txt` in the same folder as the image. When a local copy exists, the note follows the local copy. When images are imported, copied, moved or renamed with Ansel, the `.txt` sidecars are moved along if present. The text files can be opened with any Markdown or plain-text editor.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/notes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "All duplicates (variants) of an image will share the same sidecar `<image basename>.txt` file, where you can document the differences between them.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/notes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Changes are saved automatically after a short delay and when focus leaves the editor. The content of the sidecar `.txt` can be used through the [variable](../../variables.md) `$(SIDECAR_TXT)` in various places in the software.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/notes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\nThough the sidecar text files are a little-known feature, they have been supported in Darktable since at least 2014 and Ansel has kept supporting them just the same, only extending them.\n{{< /note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/notes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{<gallery cols=\"2\">}}\n{{<figure src=\"text-notes-edit.jpg\" />}}\n{{<figure src=\"text-notes-preview.jpg\" />}}\n{{</gallery>}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/notes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "last modified\n: Shows the last modification time of the note file (hidden when no note exists).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/notes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "edit / preview\n: Toggle between editing the Markdown source and viewing the rendered preview.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/notes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "text editor\n: Multi-line editor with word-wrap and automatic saving.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/notes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "presets\n: Store and apply text templates. A built-in **Default** preset provides a checklist and lifecycle line (see below).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/notes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Markdown preview"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/notes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Supported formatting includes:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/notes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Headings (levels 1–3 are emphasized)\n- Emphasis (italic and bold)\n- Inline code\n- Ordered and unordered lists\n- Links (clickable in preview mode)\n- Images (local or remote)\n- Task lists / checklists\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/notes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Checklist items can be toggled by clicking their checkbox glyphs in preview mode; the source text is updated accordingly.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/notes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Preview spacing matches the edit mode’s blank-line separation between blocks, so switching modes doesn’t shift the layout.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/notes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If Ansel is built **without** CMark, preview falls back to raw text (no Markdown rendering).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/notes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Images"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/notes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Local images referenced with `![]()` are rendered inline.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/notes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Remote HTTP/HTTPS images are downloaded and cached under `~/.cache/ansel/downloads`. If an image is not yet cached, it appears as soon as the download completes.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/notes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Images are color-corrected using the active display profile.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/notes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Variables and auto-completion"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/notes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Type `$(` in edit mode to trigger [variable](../../variables.md) auto-completion (the same variables used in path templates).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/notes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In preview mode, variables are expanded, for example:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block
#: content/views/toolboxes/notes.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Shot on $(EXIF.YEAR)-$(EXIF.MONTH)-$(EXIF.DAY) $(EXIF.HOUR):$(EXIF.MINUTE)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/notes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Example preset (Default)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (markdown)
#: content/views/toolboxes/notes.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "## Todo\n\n- [ ] Normalize illuminant & colors\n- [ ] Normalize contrast & dynamic range\n- [ ] Fix lens distortion and noise\n- [ ] Enhance colors\n\n## Resources\n\n- [Documentation](https://ansel.photos/en/doc)\n\n## Lifecycle\n\nShot on $(EXIF.YEAR)-$(EXIF.MONTH)-$(EXIF.DAY) $(EXIF.HOUR):$(EXIF.MINUTE)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/toolboxes/print-settings.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Print settings"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/print-settings.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Manage settings for the [print view](../print/_index.md) and initiate printing.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/toolboxes/print-settings.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Printer"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/print-settings.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "printer\n: Select one of the installed printers.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/print-settings.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "media\n: The type of media loaded on the printer (Plain Paper, Luster Photo Paper, etc.).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/print-settings.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "profile\n: The printer's ICC profile for the loaded paper. This is the profile specific to the printer and paper. This profile is the last color space transformation applied to the picture whose goal is to create a high quality print.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/print-settings.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "intent\n: The print rendering intent (“perceptual”, “relative colorimetric”, “saturation” or “absolute colorimetric”). See [rendering intent](../../color-management/rendering-intent.md) for more details.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/print-settings.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "black point compensation\n: Whether to adjust the black point of the output profile, which is often lighter than the input profile. This should be “on” when the _intent_ is set to “relative colorimetric”.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/toolboxes/print-settings.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Page"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/print-settings.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "paper size\n: The size of the paper on which to print.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/print-settings.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "orientation\n: Portrait or landscape (note that darktable chooses the best fit by default).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/print-settings.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "units\n: The unit to use for setting the margins: “mm”, “cm”, or “inch”.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/print-settings.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "margins\n: Set each margin separately, or all together by clicking on the middle “lock” button.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/print-settings.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "display grid\n: Select the grid size using the entry field (expressed in the currently selected unit). Tick the option to display the grid on the canvas.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/print-settings.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "snap to grid\n: Help setting the image areas by snapping them to the grid for proper alignment.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/print-settings.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "borderless mode required\n: Indicates whether the printer borderless mode is to be activated. This item is activated when the user's margins are smaller than the printer hardware margins. Note that it is only an indicator as it does not activate the borderless mode automatically.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/toolboxes/print-settings.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Image layout"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/print-settings.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "image width/height\n: This information field displays the actual image width and height (given with the selected units) on the paper.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/print-settings.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "scale factor\n: This information field displays the scaling of the image to fit on the paper. If this value is less than 1 the image is down-scaled, otherwise it is up-scaled. This is an important factor to watch -- a value that is too large (up-scale) may result in a low quality print. The corresponding dpi (dots per inch) is also displayed.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/print-settings.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "alignment\n: Select the alignment of the image on its area.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/print-settings.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "new image area button\n: Create a new image area. Drag and drop on the canvas to place it. If the option snap to grid is activated the area can be easily aligned to the grid lines. An image can be placed into this area by dragging it from the filmstrip and dropping it on the new area.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/print-settings.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "delete image area button\n: Remove the currently selected image area from the composition.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/print-settings.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "clear layout button\n: Remove all the image areas leaving the canvas empty.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/print-settings.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The following four fields represent the position of currently selected area on the page -- the top/left corner on the first line and the width/height of the area on the second line.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/print-settings.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When hovering an image area its position and size are displayed. It is also possible to grab the side and corner of the area to change the size or to drag the whole area to change its position.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/print-settings.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The page layout can be recorded using a preset.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/print-settings.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "profile\n: The export profile to use. This profile is the entry point used for the next transformation using the printer's ICC profile. Usually it is better to prefer a large gamut (e.g. Adobe RGB) rather than a smaller one (e.g. sRGB).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/print-settings.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "intent\n: The rendering intent to use when exporting the image. For more information see [rendering intent](../../color-management/rendering-intent.md).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/print-settings.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "style\n: Choose a style to apply when exporting the image -- defaults to “none”. See the [export](../toolboxes/export.md) module for a more detailed discussion of applying a style during export.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/print-settings.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "mode\n: Whether the chosen style should be appended to the existing history stack or replace it completely. See the [export](../toolboxes/export.md) module for more details.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/toolboxes/print-settings.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Print button"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/print-settings.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When clicked, the images are first exported using the selected options, then composed on the page and finally sent to the printer.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/toolboxes/focus-peaking.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Focus peaking"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/focus-peaking.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Identify which parts of the image contain high-contrast details, like edges and textures, which usually indicates that those areas are in focus.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/focus-peaking.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Enable focus peaking from the [bottom toolbar](../darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md#bottom-panel) in the darkroom, or via _Overlay focus peaking_ in the [lighttable display options](../lighttable/_index.md#overlays). The sharp parts of the image are highlighted with a yellow, green and blue overlay:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/focus-peaking.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Focus peaking works by filtering out most of the image noise, measuring the intensity gradients in the image and calculating average and standard deviation statistics. When the gradient of an edge differs significantly from the mean, the associated pixels are marked with a \"heat map\" indicating how sharp the edge is.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/focus-peaking.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- _yellow_ represents a large (6σ) jump in gradient, indicating a very sharp edge.\n- _green_ represents a medium (4σ) jump in gradient, indicating a reasonably sharp edge.\n- _blue_ represents a small (2σ) jump in gradient, indicating a slightly sharp edge.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/focus-peaking.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "![focus peaking overlaid on a shallow depth-of-field image](focus-peaking-overview.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/focus-peaking.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "On an image shot with a wide aperture and shallow depth of field, the overlay clusters on the plane the lens focused on (and on any other details that happen to fall within the zone of acceptable sharpness around it), which makes it easy to see where focus actually landed.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/focus-peaking.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\nThe focus peaking detects local contrast and uses it as an estimation of sharpness. This is the only known method to do so. While local contrast and sharpness are fairly correlated most of the time, edges between two objects having very different luminances (bright sky and tree leaves) will have a very high local contrast even when blurry, which will make them detected as sharp by focus peaking. A mechanism is in place to mitigate this issue in most cases, but very high-contrast edges will still be detected as sharp no matter their actual sharpness.\n{{</ note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/focus-peaking.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< warning >}}\nThe focus peaking does __not__ detect __if__ the images are sharp, but __where__ the sharpest region of the image is, which is likely to be on the focal plane. This can help to spot front/back-focus, if any. But there is no guaranty that the most sharp region of the picture is actually sharp by some metric of definition of sharpness.\n{{</ warning >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/focus-peaking.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< warning >}}\nDownscaled images have their gradients affected by interpolation methods, where noise is made less visible and local contrast is lost around small details, making focus peaking on small thumbnails inaccurate.\n{{</ warning >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/focus-peaking.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< warning >}}\nThe focus peaking is runs the detection on the final image as it appears in the graphic interface, after all processing steps if any. It will therefore take into account all the blurring and sharpening, as well as contrast enhancements, that may have happened in the processing.\n{{</ warning >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Library"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Filter the images shown in the lighttable view and filmstrip panel using image attributes. This set of filtered images is known as a _collection_.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Importing images into Ansel stores information about them (filename, path, Exif data, data from XMP sidecar files etc.) in Ansel's library database. A collection is defined by applying filtering rules to these attributes, thus creating a subset of images to display in the lighttable view and the filmstrip module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The default collection is based on the _film roll_ attribute and displays all images of the last imported or selected film roll.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\nWe carry the ~~burden~~ legacy of Darktable here, that makes a weird difference between _film rolls_ and _folders_.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When importing an image into Ansel's library, its parent folder is saved as a property of the image named _film roll_. At any given time, when we query the content of a _film roll_, we list all the pictures that Ansel knows of (imported in library) that were imported from that folder.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "It means that Ansel never check the content of the actual filesystem folder after the images have been imported: it may contain more images than what the library is aware of, or the images may have been deleted or moved elsewhere on the filesystem, or the whole folder might have been deleted entirely. So Ansel _film roll_ is not directly equivalent to filesystem folder, even if it's related and linked at import time.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Now, where it gets confusing: what Darktable called _folders_ is actually just _film rolls_ but displayed as a treeview instead of a flat list. Yet _folders_ are listed as a different querying option as _film rolls_, suggesting that it's a different piece of data, though it is only the same content presented differently.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel solves this in the _Folders_ tab: Darktable's _film rolls_ or _folders_ are presented as a _list_ or _treeview_ display option, and that's the end of the story. However, they remain separate objects in the (advanced) _Queries_ tab. \n{{< /note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The three tabs"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The Library module is organised in three tabs, each tuned to a different way of building a collection. The tabs share a single value list (the box at the bottom that shows the available values for the active attribute, along with the image count for each value).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Folders\n: Browse and manage the folders and film rolls known to Ansel. The _View_ selector at the top of this tab switches between a flat **List** of film rolls and a hierarchical **Tree** of folders. This is the tab where you relocate or remove film rolls (one at a time or in batches). It corresponds to a single _film roll_ or _folder_ rule.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Collections\n: Browse and manage your tags, shown as a hierarchical tree. Besides selecting images by tag, this is where you rename or delete tags (in batches). It corresponds to a single _tag_ rule.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Queries\n: Build an arbitrary collection from one or more rules, combining any of the image [attributes](#filtering-attributes) with logical operators. This tab also exposes a [raw SQL](#raw-sql-queries) escape hatch for power users.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Switching tabs only reconfigures the controls and refreshes the value list; it does not re-run the collection query. The collection is only rebuilt when you actually click a value or edit a rule.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Filtering attributes"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The images in a collection can be filtered using the following image attributes. All of them are available in the _Queries_ tab; the _Folders_ and _Collections_ tabs are pre-set to the folder/film-roll and tag attributes respectively.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Files"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "film roll\n: The name of the film roll to which the image belongs (which is the same as the name of the folder in which the image resides). In the _Folders_ tab, choose **List** in the _View_ selector to browse film rolls. Right-click a film roll to remove its contents from the Ansel library, or to tell Ansel that its location has changed in the file system (see [updating the folder path of moved images](#updating-the-folder-path-of-moved-images)).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "folder\n: The folder (file path) where the image file is located. In the _Folders_ tab, choose **Tree** in the _View_ selector to browse the folder hierarchy. Click a folder to select its images; enable the **include sub-folders** checkbox to also include every image located in its sub-folders. Right-click a folder to remove its contents from the Ansel library or to relocate it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "filename\n: The image's filename.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/metadata-tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Metadata"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "tag\n: Any tag that is attached to the image. Untagged images are grouped under the \"not tagged\" entry. Tags are displayed as a hierarchical list.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "title\n: The image's metadata “title” field.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "description\n: The image's metadata “description” field.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "creator\n: The image's metadata “creator” field.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "publisher\n: The image's metadata “publisher” field.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "rights\n: The image's metadata “rights” field.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "notes\n: The image's metadata \"notes\" field.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "version name\n: The image's metadata \"version name\" field.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "rating\n: The image's star rating.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "color label\n: Any color label attached to the image (\"red\", \"yellow\", \"green\", \"blue\", \"purple\").\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "geotagging\n: The geo location of the image (see [locations](./locations.md)).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Times"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "date taken\n: The date the photo was taken, in the format `YYYY:MM:DD`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "date-time taken\n: The date & time the photo was taken, in the format `YYYY:MM:DD hh:mm:ss`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "import timestamp\n: The date/time the file was imported, in the format `YYYY:MM:DD hh:mm:ss`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "change timestamp\n: The date/time the file's history stack was last changed, in the format `YYYY:MM:DD hh:mm:ss`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "export timestamp\n: The date/time the file was last exported, in the format `YYYY:MM:DD hh:mm:ss`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "print timestamp\n: The date/time the file was last printed, in the format `YYYY:MM:DD hh:mm:ss`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Capture details"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "camera\n: The Exif data entry describing the camera make and model.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "lens\n: The description of the lens, as derived from Exif data.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "aperture\n: The aperture, as derived from Exif data.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "exposure\n: The shutter speed, as derived from Exif data.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "focal length\n: The focal length, as derived from Exif data.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "ISO\n: The ISO, as derived from Exif data.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "grouping\n: Choose between \"group followers\" and \"group leaders\".\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "local copy\n: Show files that are, or are not, copied locally.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "history\n: Choose images whose history stacks have been altered or not.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "module\n: Filter based on the processing modules that have been applied to the image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "module order\n: Choose images with \"v3.0\", \"legacy\" or \"custom\" module orders.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The Folders tab"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "view selector\n: The combobox at the top toggles between a flat **List** of film rolls and a hierarchical **Tree** of folders.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "include sub-folders\n: When the _Tree_ view is active, this checkbox controls whether clicking a folder also includes the images contained in all of its sub-folders. Internally it appends a `*` wildcard to the folder path; if you type a path ending in `*` or `%` by hand, the checkbox updates itself to stay in sync.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "sort by / sort direction\n: Choose whether film rolls are ordered by **name** (folder path) or by **id** (roughly the order in which they were first imported), and toggle ascending/descending order. These controls only affect the flat **List** view (the **Tree** is always sorted by path), so they are hidden in the Tree view.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "folder levels\n: The number of folder levels shown in film-roll names, counting from the right. Only meaningful (and only shown) in the **List** view.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "You can **drag images** from the lighttable/filmstrip and drop them onto a folder or film-roll row to physically move the files into that folder (see [drag and drop](#drag-and-drop)).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Right-click a folder or film-roll row for management actions: **remove from library…**, **relocate…**, or **pre-render thumbnails** (fills the on-disk thumbnail cache for every image in the selected folders, as a background task).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The Collections tab"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This tab lists your tags as a hierarchical tree. Click a tag to filter the collection by it:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- **click** a tag to include that tag *and* all of its sub-tags (appends the `*` suffix);\n- **shift+click** to include only the exact tag, not its sub-tags (no suffix);\n- **ctrl+click** to include only the sub-tags, excluding the tag itself (appends the `|%` suffix).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "You can **drag images** from the lighttable/filmstrip and drop them onto a tag row to attach that tag to them (see [drag and drop](#drag-and-drop)).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Right-click a tag to **rename** it, to **delete** one or more selected tags (deleting a tag also detaches it from every image), or to **pre-render thumbnails** of the tagged images.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "no 'uncategorized' group\n: When enabled, tags that have no children are not grouped under a synthetic \"uncategorized\" entry.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The Queries tab"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This tab is the general-purpose collection builder.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Defining filter criteria"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Each rule is made of an attribute selector, an optional comparison operator, and a search field:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "image attribute\n: The combobox on the left chooses which [attribute](#filtering-attributes) the rule filters on.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "comparison operator\n: For numeric, date/time and rating attributes, a small operator selector appears between the attribute and the search field, offering `=`, `<`, `≤`, `>`, `≥` and `≠`. It is hidden for text attributes.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "search pattern\n: In the text field, write a matching pattern. This pattern is compared against all database entries with the selected attribute, matching if the attribute *contains* the pattern. Use `%` as a wildcard. Leave the field empty to match all images that have the attribute. Where applicable, a tooltip appears when you hover over the attribute or the search field.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": Numeric and date/time attributes can also be combined with the comparison operators above, or with a range expressed as `[from;to]` (inclusive on both ends).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "select by value\n: Instead of typing, you can pick from the value list below the search field. It shows the values of the selected attribute that are present in the currently-matching images, with the image count for each, and updates continuously as you type. Clicking a value populates the search field automatically. For numeric and date-time attributes you can select a range of values by clicking the first and last entries.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Combining multiple filters"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "You can combine several rules to build more complex collections. Each rule beyond the first carries a logical operator that defines how it combines with the rules above it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Click the button at the end of a rule row to open a menu:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "clear this rule\n: Remove the current rule, or reset it if it is the only rule defined.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "narrow down search\n: Add a new rule combined with the previous rule(s) using a logical **AND**. An image is kept only if it *also* satisfies the new criteria.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "add more images\n: Add a new rule combined with the previous rule(s) using a logical **OR**. Images that satisfy the new criteria are *added* to the collection.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "exclude images\n: Add a new rule combined with the previous rule(s) using a logical **AND NOT** (except). Images that satisfy the new criteria are *removed* from the collection.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The button of each non-final rule shows its current operator (**AND**, **OR** or **AND NOT**). Click it to change the operator for that rule.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Raw SQL queries"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "For advanced needs that the rule builder cannot express, enable **edit as raw SQL** on the _Queries_ tab. This directly exposes the SQL backend that is actually used underneath the GUI in other modes, where the GUI only builds SQL queries from more user-friendly (and standardized) controls.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The text field then accepts a single SQL `WHERE` expression that is injected directly into the collection query against your *local* library database. Press <kbd>Enter</kbd> to run it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This is a power-user escape hatch:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- The expression is **not** sanitised — it is your responsibility to write valid SQL.\n- It is **read-only**: the expression can only filter rows, it cannot modify the database.\n- A malformed expression makes the underlying query fail gracefully and yields an **empty collection**; it does not crash Ansel.\n- Enabling raw SQL replaces all the rules currently defined in the _Queries_ tab. Disabling it returns to a single empty film-roll rule.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Your expression is evaluated against one row per image. The following columns are available directly (they come from the `images` table); their names, SQL types and meaning are:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| Column             | Type    | Meaning |\n|--------------------|---------|---------|\n| `id`               | INTEGER | Unique image id (primary key). |\n| `group_id`         | INTEGER | Id of the group-leader image. An image is a group leader when `id = group_id`. |\n| `film_id`          | INTEGER | Id of the film roll (folder) the image belongs to. |\n| `filename`         | TEXT    | File name with extension, without the path (e.g. `IMG_1234.CR2`). |\n| `maker`            | TEXT    | Camera manufacturer, from Exif. |\n| `model`            | TEXT    | Camera model, from Exif. |\n| `lens`             | TEXT    | Lens description, from Exif. |\n| `aperture`         | REAL    | f-number, e.g. `2.8`. |\n| `exposure`         | REAL    | Shutter speed **in seconds**, e.g. `0.004` for 1/250 s. |\n| `focal_length`     | REAL    | Focal length in millimetres. |\n| `iso`              | REAL    | ISO sensitivity. |\n| `aspect_ratio`     | REAL    | Width / height after cropping. |\n| `flags`            | INTEGER | Bit-field. Bits 0–2 (`flags & 7`) hold the star rating 0–5; bit 3 (`flags & 8`) marks a rejected image; the remaining bits are internal (local copy, etc.). |\n| `version`          | INTEGER | Duplicate/version number (`0` for the original). |\n| `position`         | INTEGER | Manual ordering index used by the lighttable. |\n| `datetime_taken`   | INTEGER | Capture date/time, as microseconds since `0001-01-01 00:00:00`. `0` means unknown. |\n| `import_timestamp` | INTEGER | Import date/time, same microsecond encoding. `-1` means never. |\n| `change_timestamp` | INTEGER | Last history-change date/time, same encoding. `-1` means never. |\n| `export_timestamp` | INTEGER | Last export date/time, same encoding. `-1` means never. |\n| `print_timestamp`  | INTEGER | Last print date/time, same encoding. `-1` means never. |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Examples:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (sql)
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "iso > 800 AND lens LIKE '%50mm%'\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (sql)
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "aperture <= 2.8 AND exposure < 0.004\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Because the date/time columns are stored as integer microseconds, filtering on them in raw SQL is awkward — for date ranges, prefer the dedicated _date taken_, _import timestamp_, etc. rules of the standard builder, which accept human-readable `YYYY:MM:DD` text.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The columns above are the ones exposed directly to the expression. Attributes that live in other tables (tags, metadata, color labels, edit history, folder paths) are not columns of this row, but you can still reference them through a sub-query on the image `id`. The relevant library tables are:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- `main.tagged_images(imgid, tagid)` joined with `data.tags(id, name, synonyms)` — tags;\n- `main.meta_data(id, key, value)` — title, description and the other text metadata;\n- `main.color_labels(imgid, color)` — color labels (`0`=red, `1`=yellow, `2`=green, `3`=blue, `4`=purple);\n- `main.history(imgid, operation, …)` — applied modules / edit history;\n- `main.film_rolls(id, folder)` — film-roll folder paths.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "For example, to select images tagged under `landscape`:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (sql)
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "id IN (SELECT imgid FROM main.tagged_images ti\n       JOIN data.tags t ON t.id = ti.tagid\n       WHERE t.name LIKE 'landscape%')\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Drag and drop"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "You can drag images out of the lighttable or the filmstrip and drop them directly onto a row of the Library module. What happens depends on the kind of row you drop them on — a folder/film-roll row, or a tag row:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (mermaid)
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "flowchart TD\n    A[\"Select one or more images<br/>in the lighttable or filmstrip\"] --> B[\"Drag them onto a row<br/>of the Library module\"]\n    B --> C{\"Row under the cursor?\"}\n    C -->|\"folder / film roll<br/>(Folders tab)\"| D{\"Confirm the move?\"}\n    C -->|\"tag<br/>(Collections tab)\"| G[\"Attach the tag to the images<br/>(no file is moved)\"]\n    D -->|yes| E[\"Files are physically moved on disk<br/>into that folder, and the<br/>library database is updated\"]\n    D -->|no| F[\"Nothing happens\"]\n    E --> H[\"The film-roll/tag tree and the<br/>lighttable refresh automatically\"]\n    G --> H\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The drop always applies to the single row located **under the mouse cursor** when you release the button, regardless of which rows happen to be selected.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "drop on a folder or film roll\n: The dragged images are **physically moved on disk** into that folder (a film roll is created for the folder if one does not exist yet), and the library is updated to match. Because this touches the file system, you are asked to confirm first. Any duplicates of the moved images follow along.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "drop on a tag\n: The tag of the row is **attached** to the dragged images. This only edits metadata — no file is moved on disk — and the change is written to the images' XMP sidecars.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\nDrag and drop only works from Ansel's own lighttable/filmstrip (it relies on the internal image identifiers). Dragging image files from an external file manager into this module is not supported — use the regular import for that.\n{{< /note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Updating the folder path of moved images"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "While it is best not to touch imported files behind Ansel's back, this module can help you recover when you have moved or renamed image folders after importing them. The process is:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. Open the _Folders_ tab.\n1. A film roll or folder whose location can no longer be found on disk is shown with ~~strikethrough~~ formatting.\n1. Right-click the folder or film roll name and select **relocate…**, then choose the new location of the folder. Selecting several rows first lets you relocate them in one operation by picking their new common parent folder.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This updates Ansel's library database only; it does not move any files on disk.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Returning to a previous collection"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Your recent collections are kept in a history list. You can revert to a previously-defined collection from the **collections** entry in the top menu bar, which lists the most recent collections you have used. The number of remembered collections is configurable from the preferences of that recent-collections list (**number of collections to be stored**).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The Library module no longer hides its settings behind a separate \"preferences…\" popup: every setting now lives directly in the relevant tab.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "do not set the 'uncategorized' entry for tags\n: The **no 'uncategorized' group** checkbox on the _Collections_ tab. When enabled, tags that have no children are not grouped under a synthetic \"uncategorized\" entry (default off).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "number of folder levels to show in lists\n: The **folder levels** spinner on the _Folders_ tab (List view). The number of folder levels to show in film-roll names, counting from the right (default 1).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "sort film rolls by\n: The **sort by** selector on the _Folders_ tab (List view). Sort film rolls by either their **name** (folder path) or **id** (roughly equivalent to the date the film rolls were first imported) (default \"id\").\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/collections.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "sort collection descending\n: The **sort direction** toggle on the _Folders_ tab. Sorts \"film roll\" (when sorted by folder), \"folder\", and date/time attributes (e.g. date taken) in descending order (default on).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/filmstrip.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The filmstrip can be used to quickly switch between images. The images shown are the same as those displayed in the lighttable view and are defined by the currently-selected collection.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/filmstrip.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "![filmstrip](filmstrip.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/filmstrip.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The filmstrip can be switched on and off using the shortcut Ctrl+F. The height of the filmstrip panel can be changed by clicking and dragging its top border.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/filmstrip.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Quickly navigate through the images in the filmstrip by scrolling with the mouse. In the darkroom you can change the photo currently being processed by clicking on another image in the filmstrip.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/filmstrip.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In the darkroom, the image currently being processed is selected and highlighted.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/toolboxes/snapshots.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Snapshots"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/snapshots.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Store snapshots of the center image and compare them against the current edit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/snapshots.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A snapshot is a frozen bitmap of the darkroom center view, taken at any point during editing and later overlaid onto the current view for a side-by-side comparison while you tune a module. It can also be combined with the [history of changes](history-stack.md) to compare a snapshot against different stages of development.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/snapshots.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Taking and comparing snapshots"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/snapshots.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Click **take snapshot** to capture the current center view. Each snapshot is listed above the button; its name reflects the module selected in the history and its position at the time it was taken.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/snapshots.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Click a snapshot's name to display it: this splits the view between the saved snapshot and the current image. Drag the split line to move it. Hover the split line to reveal a small rotation icon at its center; click it to rotate the split orientation (the snapshot and current image cycle between top/bottom/left/right). An arrow marked **S** always indicates which side is the snapshot.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/snapshots.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Click the snapshot name again to hide the overlay and return to editing. Use the module's reset button to clear all snapshots.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/snapshots.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\nSnapshots are kept for the duration of your Ansel session, so you can also use them to compare against a duplicate edit of the same image: take a snapshot, switch to the other version, and enable the snapshot overlay as usual.\n{{< /note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/toolboxes/history-stack.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "History stack"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/history-stack.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The **History of changes** module, in the darkroom [left panel](../darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md#left-panel), shows and lets you navigate the [history stack](../darkroom/pixelpipe/history-stack.md) of the current image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/history-stack.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "It lists every change of state — a module being enabled, disabled, moved, or having a parameter changed — for the current image, newest at the top. Each entry is one user change, so a module can appear several times.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/history-stack.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Navigating the history"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/history-stack.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- **Click** an entry to return the image to that point in its history.\n- **<kbd>Shift</kbd>+click** an entry to expand the corresponding module in the right-hand panel **without** changing the current edit — useful for inspecting a step.\n- **Hover** an entry for a tooltip listing exactly what changed in that step compared to the previous one, which helps track down unintended adjustments.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/history-stack.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The selected history state is remembered: it is safe to quit, leave the darkroom or switch image after stepping back, and you will return to the same state.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/history-stack.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< warning >}}\nIf you select an earlier entry and then make a new change, every step **above** the selected one is discarded. It is easy to lose work this way — <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd> can usually undo it.\n{{< /warning >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/history-stack.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Compressing, resetting and reusing"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/history-stack.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "These operations now live in the [global menu](../global-menu.md), so they work on the whole selection and not just the open image:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/history-stack.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- **Compress history** (_Edit → Compress history_) collapses the stack to the shortest sequence reproducing the current image.\n- **Delete history** (_Edit → Delete history_) discards the stack and resets the image.\n- **Create a style** (_Styles → Create new style…_) turns the current history into a reusable [style](styles.md).\n- **Copy / paste history** (_Edit → Copy history / Paste history_) transfers an edit to other images. See [copy and paste history](../lighttable/history-copy-paste.md) for how the merge works.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/toolboxes/find-location.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Find location"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/find-location.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Search for a location on the map. You must be connected to the internet to use this feature.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/find-location.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "To use this module, type in a place name or address, press Enter and a list of results will be shown. Click on an item in the list and the map will zoom to that location. An outline covering that location or a pin pointing at the location will be displayed.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/find-location.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "An outline (polygon) can be used to create a user location. Check the _max polygon points_ parameter in the [map settings](./map-settings.md) module to ensure that sufficient points are available for a polygon to be displayed.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/toolboxes/raw-overexposed.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Raw overexposed warning"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/raw-overexposed.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Highlight areas of the image where color channels of the raw input file are clipped.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/raw-overexposed.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Clipped color channels imply an overexposed image with loss of information in the affected areas. Some of this information may be recoverable using the [_highlight reconstruction_](../darkroom/modules/highlight-reconstruction.md), [_color reconstruction_](../darkroom/modules/color-reconstruction.md) or [_filmic_](../darkroom/modules/filmic.md) modules.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/raw-overexposed.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Click the raw-overexposed icon in the [bottom toolbar](../darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md#bottom-panel) to show/hide the warning overlay. Right-click the icon to open a dialog containing the following configuration parameters.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/raw-overexposed.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "mode\n: Choose how to mark clipped areas:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/raw-overexposed.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": - _mark with CFA color_: Display a pattern of the respective primary colors (red, green, and blue) to indicate which color channels are clipped.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/raw-overexposed.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": - _mark with solid color_: Mark clipped areas with a user defined solid color (see below) independent of the affected color channels.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/raw-overexposed.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": - _false color_: Set clipped color channels to zero in the affected areas.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/raw-overexposed.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "color scheme\n: Choose the solid color for the _mark with solid color_ mode.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/raw-overexposed.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "clipping threshold\n: Set the threshold defining what values are considered to be overexposed. You can safely leave this slider at its default value of 1.0 (white level) in most cases.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/toolboxes/scopes.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Scopes"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/scopes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The scopes module, at the top of the darkroom [left panel](../darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md#left-panel), provides graphical depictions of the image's tones and [chromaticity](../../color-management/color-dimensions.md#definitions). It also hosts the [global color picker](global-color-picker.md).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/scopes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Drag the bottom edge of the module to resize the scope vertically.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/scopes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\nFor performance, the scopes are computed from the lower-resolution preview pipeline (the same image shown in the [navigation](navigation.md) module), not from the full-resolution center image. Fine detail may therefore not be represented exactly.\n{{< /note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/scopes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Choosing what to show"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/scopes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Use the _hamburger_ menu in the module header to access the following sub-menu items:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/scopes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Show data from\n: Where in the [pixelpipe](../darkroom/pixelpipe/_index.md) the data is sampled, which determines the color space of the plot:\n    - **Raw image** — straight off the sensor, before color processing.\n    - **Output color profile** — at the [output color profile](../darkroom/modules/output-color-profile.md) stage.\n    - **Final display** — after the full pipeline, in display space (what you actually see).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/scopes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Display\n: The type of scope to draw: \n    - **Histogram**, \n    - **Waveform (horizontal)**, \n    - **Waveform (vertical)**, \n    - **Parade (horizontal)**, \n    - **Parade (vertical)**,\n    - **Vectorscope**.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/scopes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Scope types"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/toolboxes/scopes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Histogram"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/scopes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The histogram shows the distribution of pixels by lightness for each RGB channel. The x-axis runs from 0 % to 100 % lightness; the y-axis is the pixel count at each lightness. Values piling up against the left or right edge indicate shadow or highlight clipping.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/scopes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "![histogram](histogram.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/toolboxes/scopes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Waveform"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/scopes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The waveform shows the same tonal distribution as the histogram, but in a spatial context.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/scopes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In the **horizontal** waveform, the x-axis matches the x-axis of the image: the left of the plot corresponds to the left of the image. The y-axis is lightness — the top is 100 % (values above may clip), the middle 50 %, the bottom 0 %. The brightness of each point is the number of pixels at that image position and lightness.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/scopes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "![horizontal waveform](waveform.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/scopes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The **vertical** waveform maps the image's y-axis instead, which can be more readable for portrait-format images.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/scopes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "![vertical waveform](waveform-vertical.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/toolboxes/scopes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Parade"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/scopes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The parade shows the same data as the waveform, but with the red, green and blue channels drawn side by side (again in a horizontal or vertical variant). This is useful for matching the intensities of the three channels and for judging color casts.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/scopes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "![horizontal RGB parade](parade.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/toolboxes/scopes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Vectorscope"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/scopes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The vectorscope shows [chromaticity](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chromaticity) independently of lightness and of spatial position. Distance from the center is chroma; angle is hue. Regions are tinted with the color they represent, and more frequently used colors appear brighter, so the plot conveys color \"volume\". A hue ring marks the maximum chroma of each hue for the current profile, with the RGB primaries and secondaries marked by circles.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/scopes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "![vectorscope](vectorscope.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/scopes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The vectorscope is computed in the [CIELUV](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CIELUV) color space.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/scopes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- The hue ring is not a [gamut check](gamut.md): a color can sit inside the ring yet still be out of gamut because of its lightness.\n- There is deliberately no \"skin tone line\", which is a flawed generalization rather than a standard.\n- The vectorscope is a colorimetric encoding, which inevitably diverges from a viewer's perception.\n- To neutralize a color cast from a reference chart, the color-checker workflow in [color calibration](../darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md#extracting-settings-using-a-color-checker) is faster and more accurate.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/navigation.md content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#: content/views/lighttable/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Navigation"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/navigation.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Zoom and pan the current darkroom image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/navigation.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "At the top of the darkroom [left panel](../darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md#left-panel), the navigation module shows a full preview of the current image with a rectangle marking the area currently visible in the center view. Drag the rectangle to pan the zoomed-in view.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/navigation.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The current zoom level is displayed to the right of the preview. Click it for quick access to common zoom levels.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/navigation.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The module can be expanded/collapsed like any toolbox — from its header, through the [global action search](../../getting-started/keyboard.md#vimkey-like-global-action-search), or via a shortcut you assign to its focusing action. Center-view zoom is also controlled directly: see [zoom and pan](../darkroom/_index.md#zoom-and-pan).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/metadata-tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Tagging"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Attach tags to images, and manage the tag dictionary.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Tags provide a means of adding information to images using a keyword dictionary. You can also manage tags as a hierarchical tree, which can be useful when their number becomes large.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Tags are physically stored in [XMP sidecar files](../lighttable/digital-asset-management/sidecar.md) as well as in Ansel's library database and can be included in [exported](./export.md) images.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In Ansel, attaching tags is also _the_ way to build [collections](./collections.md): a collection is the dynamically-queried set of all images sharing a given tag, always kept up to date. So the everyday job of this module — attaching a tag to images — is the same act as “putting images into a collection”. The tag _dictionary_ itself (creating, renaming, deleting, importing and exporting tags) is a separate, occasional, housekeeping task and lives in its own window.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The following definitions assume that you have set up a single tag named \"`places|France|Nord|Lille`\".\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "tag\n: A tag is a descriptive string that may be attached to an image. A tag can be a single term or a sequence of connected terms forming a path, separated by the pipe symbol. For example, \"`places|France|Nord|Lille`\" defines a single tag, where each term in the path forms a smaller subset of those before it. You can attach as many tags to an image as you like.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": You can assign properties (name, private, category, synonyms and image order) to a tag.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "node\n: Any path that forms part of a tag is a node. In the above example, \"`places`\", \"`places|France`\", \"`places|France|Nord`\" and \"`places|France|Nord|Lille`\" are all nodes. In the hierarchical tree view, the nodes form the branches and leaves of the tree.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "free node\n: Any node that is not explicitly defined as a tag is called a free node. In the above example, \"`places`\", \"`places|France`\" and \"`places|France|Nord`\" are all free nodes. You cannot set any properties, except “name”, for a free node and you cannot add a free node to an image. See the “multiple tags” section below for more information.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "category\n: Any tag can be flagged by the user as being a “category”.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Multiple tags"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The above definitions considered a simple example – a single tag and its properties. Consider instead the scenario where the following four pipe-delimited tags are each separately defined in Ansel.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "places|France|Nord|Lille\nplaces|France|Nord\nplaces|France\nplaces|England|London\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In this case the nodes are\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "places\nplaces|France\nplaces|France|Nord\nplaces|France|Nord|Lille\nplaces|England\nplaces|England|London\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The only free nodes are \"`places`\" and \"`places|England`\". Both of these free nodes are also (by implication) categories.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "You can attach any of these tags to any image. Any tags attached to an image, except category tags, can be included when that image is [exported](./export.md).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If you attach the \"`places|France|Nord|Lille`\" tag to an image, the \"`places|France|Nord`\" and \"`places|France`\" tags are also implicitly attached to that image (you don’t need to attach them manually). Note that this is only true here because those additional tags have been separately defined -- the \"`places`\" node is not included because it is a \"free node\" (not a tag).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Module layout"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The tagging experience is split across two surfaces, matching the two distinct jobs of culling (fast, frequent) and dictionary maintenance (occasional):\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (mermaid)
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "flowchart TB\n    subgraph Sidebar[\"Tags sidebar — lighttable / map (top to bottom)\"]\n        C[\"view (list/tree) · sort (name/count)\"]\n        B[\"Tag entry + ✓ validate button —<br/>type or pick a tag, then Enter or ✓ to attach\"]\n        A[\"Attached tags list — tags on the hovered / selected<br/>images, shown as a list or a tree, trash icon on each row\"]\n        D[\"☑ show system tags\"]\n    end\n    M[\"module gear menu → “manage tags…”\"]\n    subgraph Popup[\"“manage tags…” window\"]\n        E[\"Tag dictionary — list / tree, multiple selection,<br/>no per-image checkboxes\"]\n        F[\"search box · new · import… · export… · tree/list · suggestions\"]\n        G[\"suggestion settings: confidence · recent-tags count\"]\n    end\n    M -->|opens| Popup\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The **sidebar toolbox** (the _Tags_ module in the left panel) is for the “one image → many tags” workflow: it shows the tags already on the current image and lets you attach more. The **manage tags window** is for the dictionary itself (create / rename / delete / import / export) and its suggestion settings. It never attaches anything to your images — it only edits the tags. Open it from the module's gear (preset) menu → **manage tags…**.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Sidebar toolbox"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "From top to bottom, the sidebar contains:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- **Display controls** — a row with two combo boxes:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "  view\n  : Chooses how the attached list below is rendered: **list** shows each tag as its whole `a|b|c` path on one line; **tree** breaks the paths on the pipe `|` and shows them hierarchically, so common parents are grouped.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "  sort\n  : Chooses whether to order the attached tags by **name** (alphabetically) or by **count** (how many of the targeted images carry each tag).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- **Tag entry** — a text box where you type or pick a tag to attach. As you type, an autocompletion list of matching existing tags appears below it; pick one with the arrow keys/mouse, or keep typing a brand-new name. A small clear (✕) icon inside the box empties it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "  **validate** — the check-mark button to the right of the entry attaches the typed (or picked) tag to the target images, exactly like pressing Enter. It is there so that, after choosing a tag from the autocompletion list with the mouse, you do not have to reach for the keyboard.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- **Attached tags list** — the tags attached to the image(s) currently under your mouse cursor (if hovering over an image in the lighttable), or to the currently selected image(s) (if not hovering). Each user tag row carries a trash icon on its right that detaches that single tag in one click. Automatically generated Ansel system tags (with names starting “`Ansel|`”) are shown for information only — only when _show system tags_ is enabled — and have no trash icon. The number in brackets next to a tag indicates how many of the targeted images carry it. You can adjust the height of this list by holding Ctrl and scrolling with your mouse wheel. The list is rendered as a flat list or a hierarchical tree according to the _view_ control above (see [Detach tag](#detach-a-tag) for the right-click actions).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- **show system tags** — a checkbox at the bottom that reveals the system tags Ansel manages automatically (the `Ansel|…` family). They are hidden by default.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The [manage tags window](#manage-tags-window) is opened from the module's gear (preset) menu, not from the sidebar.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Manage tags window"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Open this window from the module's gear (preset) menu → **manage tags…**. It is a separate window dedicated to maintaining the dictionary, and contains:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- a **search box** at the top that filters the tag list as you type (its ✕ icon clears it);\n- the **tag dictionary**, listing every tag known to Ansel, either as a flat _list_ or as a hierarchical _tree_. Multiple tags can be selected at once (Ctrl-click / Shift-click) for bulk operations;\n- a row of buttons:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "  new\n  : Create a new tag using the name typed in the search box.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "  import...\n  : Import tags from a Lightroom keyword file.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "  export...\n  : Export the whole dictionary to a Lightroom keyword file.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "  suggestions\n  : Show a list of suggested keywords based on the keywords already associated with the selected images (see the _suggestion settings_ below). CAUTION: this view queries the database so it might be slow.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "  list / tree\n  : Toggle the dictionary between the flat _list_ view and the hierarchical _tree_ view.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- the **suggestion settings** (formerly a separate _preferences_ dialog), applied immediately:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "  suggested tags level of confidence\n  : Level of confidence used to include a tag in the suggestions list (default 50):\n  : - 0: display all associated tags,\n  : - 99: match tags with a 99% confidence level,\n  : - 100: an essentially unreachable level of confidence, so no matching tags are returned. Use 100% to disable the best-matched suggestions list (faster).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "  number of recently attached tags\n  : Number of recently attached tags to include in the suggestions list (default 20). A value of \"-1\" disables the most-recently-attached suggestions list.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In the hierarchical _tree_ view, a name in italics represents either a free node or a category. You can adjust the height of the dictionary by holding Ctrl while scrolling with your mouse wheel.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "All other dictionary operations (rename, change path, delete, “set as a tag”, navigation to a tag collection, …) are reached by right-clicking a tag — see [Usage](#usage) below.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\nThe management window only edits the tag dictionary; it cannot attach tags to images. To attach a tag, use the sidebar entry, the quick-tag box (Ctrl+T), or drag images onto a tag row in the [collections](./collections.md) tab of the _Library_ module.\n{{< /note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md content/views/slideshow/usage.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lut-3D.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Usage"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Attach a tag"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "To attach an existing or new tag to the image(s) under the cursor (or, failing that, the selected images):\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Type its name in the sidebar **tag entry** — picking from the autocompletion list if it already exists — then press Enter or click the **✓ validate** button. Hierarchical tags are created using the pipe symbol “`|`” to separate nodes. If the tag does not yet exist it is created, then attached.\n- Press **Ctrl+T** to open a small text box at the bottom of the central lighttable view, type a tag name and press Enter. The tag is created if needed and attached to all the selected images.\n- **Drag** an image or group of images from the lighttable/filmstrip and **drop them onto a tag row** in the [collections](./collections.md) tab of the _Library_ module. This attaches that tag to the dragged images (no file is moved).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "For a tag that is attached to only _some_ of the targeted images (shown with a count lower than the number of images), right-click it in the attached list and choose **attach tag to all** to extend it to every targeted image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When hovering over images in the lighttable you can check which tags are attached either in the attached list here, or in the _tags_ attribute of the [image information](./image-information.md) module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Detach a tag"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "From the **attached tags** list in the sidebar:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- click the **trash icon** on the tag's row to detach that one tag;\n- **double-click** a tag to detach it;\n- select one or several tags and press the **Delete** / Backspace key;\n- select one or several tags, **right-click**, and choose **detach tag(s)** to detach them all in one go;\n- **right-click anywhere** in the list (even on empty space) and choose **detach all tags** to remove every tag from the targeted images at once.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Create a tag"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "There are several ways to create a new tag:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- _Type into the sidebar entry and press Enter (or ✓)._ The tag is created and attached to the target images in one step.\n- _Use “create tag…”_ in the dictionary's right-click menu (manage tags window). The tag is created under the selected node (hierarchical) or at the root, and is **not** attached to any image.\n- _Use “set as a tag”_ in the right-click menu to turn a free node (e.g. “`places|England`”) into a real tag, so that it gets implicitly attached to all images carrying its sub-tags (e.g. “`places|England|London`”).\n- _Import a Lightroom keyword text file_ (the _import…_ button). Existing tags are updated, new ones are created. You can export your tags, edit the file, and re-import it.\n- _Import already-tagged images._ Tags found in imported images are added to the dictionary (no opportunity to rename or re-categorize them during import).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A number of tags are generated automatically by Ansel for certain actions — for example “`Ansel|exported`” and “`Ansel|styles|your_style`” identify images that have been exported or had a style applied.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Edit / rename a tag"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In the manage tags window, right-click a single tag:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "edit…\n: Change the tag's name (you cannot move it to another node here — the pipe “`|`” is not allowed in this field), and set its _private_ and _category_ flags and its _synonyms_. The command is aborted if the new name already exists. These attributes are recorded in the `XMP-dc Subject` and `XMP-lr Hierarchical Subject` metadata. Which tags end up in exports is controlled in the [export](./export.md) module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": - A tag set as “private” is, by default, not exported.\n: - A tag set as “category” is not exported in `XMP-dc Subject`, but is exported in `XMP-lr Hierarchical Subject` (which holds the organization of your tags).\n: - “synonyms” enrich the tag information and mainly assist search engines — e.g. “juvenile”, “kid” or “youth” as synonyms of “child”. They can also be used to translate tag names to other languages.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "change path…\n: Available in _tree_ view only. Lets you change the full path of a node, including the nodes it belongs to (use the pipe “`|`” to specify the hierarchy). The dialog shows how many tagged images would be impacted. This is powerful but can significantly rewrite your images' metadata, so use it carefully. The operation is aborted if it would conflict with an existing tag.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A quick way to reorganize the structure is **drag and drop** of nodes in the _tree_ view: drag any node and drop it onto another node to make it (and its descendants) a child of the target. Dragging over a node opens it automatically (drag over the node's selection indicator to avoid opening it). Drop a node onto the top of the window to move it to the root level. Conflicting moves are aborted.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The “copy to entry” right-click item copies the selected tag into the search box, so you can tweak its name and use _new_ to create a similar tag.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Delete a tag"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Deleting a tag removes it from **all** images (selected or not) and from the database. Because this can impact many images, a confirmation dialog shows how many images are affected. **Take this warning seriously — there is no undo** (short of restoring your database and/or XMP sidecars from a backup).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In the manage tags window:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- right-click a tag and choose **delete tag**;\n- select **several** tags (Ctrl-click / Shift-click) and right-click → **delete tags** to remove them all after a single confirmation;\n- right-click a branch node and choose **delete node** to delete that node together with all its child tags.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Tags can also be deleted (and renamed) from the [collections](./collections.md) tab of the _Library_ module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Import / export"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The **import…** button reads a text file in the Lightroom tag file format: existing tags are updated, missing ones are created. The **export…** button writes the entire dictionary to such a file. Round-tripping (export, edit, re-import) is a convenient way to bulk-edit tags.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- In the sidebar entry, **Enter** attaches the typed/picked tag (creating it if needed). **Shift+Tab** moves focus to the first user tag in the attached list.\n- In the attached list, **Tab** returns focus to the entry; **Delete** / **Backspace** detaches the selected tag(s).\n- In the manage tags window, the dictionary's **Left/Right** arrows collapse/expand the selected node in _tree_ view; **Tab** / **Shift+Tab** move focus to/from the search box.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "To see the images carrying a particular tag, right-click it in the manage tags window and choose **go to tag collection**. This opens a [collection](./collections.md) showing all images with that tag.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Choose **go back to work** from the same menu to return to the collection you had open before, as long as you have not selected another collection in the meantime.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/toolboxes/locations.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Locations"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/locations.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Create areas or locations and organize them as hierarchical tags.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/locations.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A location is shown as a shape on the map when selected. Initially each location is represented as a square or circle and can be changed to a rectangle or ellipse by adjusting the shape's width and/or height.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/locations.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A location can also be created from an OpenStreetMap region (city/country) polygon. To achieve this, first make sure the _max polygon points_ parameter is large enough (some country polygons use more than 150,000 points). Then select the desired location in the [find location](./find-location.md) module. When the corresponding region shape is displayed, a polygon symbol becomes available in the \"shape\" control (see below). Select it to create the new location.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/locations.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Each location is stored as tag entry under the geotagging collection in the [collections](./collections.md) module. The pipe “|” character can be used to insert a new level (a group of locations).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/locations.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "shape\n: Select the circle or rectangle symbol to choose the default shape for new locations. A polygon symbol is available when a shape is displayed by the [find location](./find-location.md) module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/locations.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "new location / new sub-location\n: When no location is selected you can use the _new location_ button to create a location at root level. When a location is selected use the _new sub-location_ button to create a sub-location within the selected location.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/locations.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "show all\n: Show or hide all locations that lie within the visible area of the current map.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/toolboxes/locations.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Actions on the locations list"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/locations.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "click\n: Select or de-select a location. If the location is not currently visible on the map, the map is automatically centered on that location.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/locations.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ctrl+click\n: Edit the name of the location. Press Enter to save changes or Esc to close the editing window without saving.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/locations.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "right-click\n: Open a sub-menu which allows you to:\n: - Edit the name of the location.\n: - Delete the location.\n: - Update the filmstrip -- the filmstrip will be populated with all images in the selected location.\n: - Switch to the lighttable view and show a collection that contains all images in the selected location.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/toolboxes/locations.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Actions on locations in the map"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/locations.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "_Note: The following actions have no effect on polygon locations._\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/locations.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "click and drag\n: Move the location shape to a new position on the map.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/locations.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ctrl+click or Ctrl+Shift+click\n: Move an image or a group of images and place them inside a location shape.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/locations.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "mouse scroll\n: When inside a location shape (but not over an image), increase or decrease the size of that shape.\n: When hovering over an image, cycle through the thumbnails of images located at that position on the map.\n: When outside a location shape (and not over an image) zoom the map in or out.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/locations.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Shift+scroll\n: Increase or decrease the width of the location shape.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/locations.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ctrl+scroll\n: Increase or decrease the height of the location shape.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/locations.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "click on a location shape\n: Select a different location when the _show all_ checkbox is selected.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/toolboxes/map-settings.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Map settings"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/map-settings.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Select preferred map data from various providers. Some will provide additional layers (satellite view etc.) which you can toggle.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/map-settings.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "map source\n: Choose the provider to source map information from.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/map-settings.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "max polygon points\n: The [find location](./find-location.md) module doesn't display polygons with more points than this for performance reasons. Usually a country polygon has between 50,000 and 150,000 points.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/map-settings.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "show OSD\n: Choose whether to display the OSD controls at the top-left of the center view.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/map-settings.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "filtered images\n: Check this box to display only the images from the current collection (those shown in the filmstrip) in the center view. Un-check the box to display all images in the current library, where those images have associated GPS data. You can also toggle this option by pressing Ctrl+S.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/map-settings.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "max images\n: The maximum number of thumbnails to display on the map.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/map-settings.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "group size factor\n: Increase or decrease the size of area that causes images to be grouped.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/map-settings.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "min images per group\n: The minimum number of images that need to be placed in the same position in order to automatically create an image group for them.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/map-settings.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "thumbnail display\n: Define what information to show on the map display\n: - _thumbnails_: Display image thumbnails along with a counter.\n: - _count_: Just display the number of images (to free space on the map). Hover over the number of images to show the corresponding thumbnail(s). A count-only marker behaves the same way as a normal image thumbnail, in terms of color coding, scrolling, drag and drop etc.\n: - _none_: Show nothing.\n: You can also cycle through these options by pressing Shift+S.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/toolboxes/guides-overlays.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Guides & overlays"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/guides-overlays.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A number of commonly-used compositional guides can be overlaid on your image while you are editing. These can be enabled either globally (all the time) or locally (when certain modules are active).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/guides-overlays.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Other darkroom functionality also draws colored overlay lines on the image (for example, drawn masks). An option is also provided to change the color of those overlays (see below).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/guides-overlays.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Global guides"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/guides-overlays.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid " Left-click the guides-and-overlays icon in the [bottom toolbar](../darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md#bottom-panel) to globally display guide overlays. The overlays remain on until you click the button a second time to switch them off.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/guides-overlays.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid " Right-click the icon to show the settings dialog (see below).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/guides-overlays.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Local guides"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/guides-overlays.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A more common use is to switch the guides on only when a specific module is activated. A guides control is added by default to all modules that crop or distort the image (currently [crop](../darkroom/modules/crop.md), [orientation](../darkroom/modules/orientation.md), [framing](../darkroom/modules/framing.md), [liquify](../darkroom/modules/liquify.md), [lens correction](../darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md), [retouch](../darkroom/modules/retouch.md) and [rotate and perspective](../darkroom/modules/rotate-perspective.md)).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/guides-overlays.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Tick the box to show guide overlays whenever the module is active. Click the icon on the right to show the settings dialog (see below).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/toolboxes/guides-overlays.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Global guide overlay settings"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/guides-overlays.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Please note that, while you can choose to switch guide overlays on and off either globally or locally, the following settings are stored globally and cannot be set independently for each module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/guides-overlays.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "type\n: The type of compositional guide lines to display.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/guides-overlays.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "flip\n: Some guides are asymmetrical. This option allows you to flip such guides horizontally/vertically.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/guides-overlays.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "horizontal lines, vertical lines, subdivisions\n: When the _grid_ overlay type is selected, set the parameters of the grid.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/guides-overlays.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "overlay color\n: The color of the overlay lines. Note that this impacts _any_ lines that are drawn directly over the image, for example, drawn masks.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/toolboxes/guides-overlays.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "contrast\n: The contrast between the lightest and darkest parts of any overlays -- usually, the contrast between the \"on\" and \"off\" parts of dashed lines.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The lighttable view is the default view loaded when Ansel is started, and allows you to view and manage your image collection. It will noticeably allow you to:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Cull your images, assign then ratings and ultimately decide which images are worth keeping and editing,\n- Filter your images based on ratings, base folder, or any metadata supported by the software (EXIF/IPTC/XMP),\n- Apply metadata, like title, description, tags, GPS coordinates, or correct EXIF date/time,\n- Copy-paste editing histories between images or apply styles, individually or in batches,\n- Export images editing histories to final files.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/lighttable/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Lighttable content"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "All the images known to Ansel are stored in a database, the [library](../../install/configuration.md). The lighttable shows thumbnails of a subset of this library, called a _collection_. A collection is created by filtering the images from the library using criteria, first from the [library toolbox](../toolboxes/collections.md), then from filtering/include toolbar.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Library\n: The library toolbox, in the left sidebar, provides the primary querying criteria to extract a subset of images from the library database. The main criteria are :\n- __Folders__ : the base folder in which the images are stored on your filesystem. They can be shown as a flat list or as a tree. Note that Ansel does not directly read the folder's content, but shows its [imported](../../getting-started/import.md) images (from its database) whose base folder matches this criterion. As such, the actual filesystem folder may contain more images than what Ansel will show, if they have not been imported.\n- __Collections__ : allows to query all images that have a certain tag assigned. This is similar to what other software call \"collections\" or \"virtual folders\", except that, in Ansel, you don't add an image to a virtual folder, you assign a tag to an image, and then query all images having that tag.\n- __Queries__ : enables more advanced querying rules, using metadata and boolean combinations of criteria (or/and/and not). Note that _folders_ and _collections_ (aka _tags_) only provide a simplified interface for the most common fields that can be found in the _queries_ tab. Available metadata to filter can be:\n    - __gear__ : lens name, camera, focal length,\n    - __exposure settings__ : shutterspeed, ISO, aperture,\n    - __date and time__ : of the shot, of last modification, last export, last print, etc.\n    - __user set data__ : title, description, copyright, creator, publisher, etc.\n    - __internal technical tags__ : file format, local copies, etc.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Include\n: The _include_ toolbar, in the second top row, allows to quickly filter in and out images based on their (_in order of appearance_):\n- __rating__ :\n    - Unrated (0 star) : the barred star icon,\n    - _rejected_ state : the circled cross icon (same as on the thumbnails),\n    - 1 to 5 stars : the next 5 star icons. Those are positional, meaning :\n        - Selecting the third starred-button will filter in all images having 3 stars,\n        - To select images rated at least 3 stars, you will need to select the third, fourth and fifth stars, which can be quickly achieved with <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>left click</kbd> on the third star.\n- __color labels__ :\n    - Unlabled images : the white disk,\n    - Color-labeled images : colored disks,\n- __edited state__ :\n    - Images having no editing history : the horizontally-barred circle,\n    - Images having an editing history : the yin-yang +/- circle (same as on the thumbnails),\n- __selection__ : restricting the collection to any arbitrary user-defined selection. This replaces Darktable's _culling_ view.\n- __text search__ : restricting the collection to all images having at least one textual property matching the search query (filename, folder, title, etc.).\n: The controls from the _include_ toolbar are toggle buttons, meaning when they are selected, images matching those criteria will be shown (filtered in/included), and when they are unselected, images matching those criteria will be hidden (filtered out/excluded). To show everything (_include everything_ or _exclude nothing_), you will therefore need to have all buttons checked, which can be quickly achieved by doing a right click on any of the buttons, then selecting _Select all filters_ in the context menu. If no button is toggled, no image is included and the lighttable grid stays empty.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "To summarize, the _library_ toolbox helps filtering images based on their intrinsic properties, while the _include_ toolbar, allows to target workflow-based properties that you can set yourself to keep track of your work in progress :\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Star ratings help culling and narrowing down the set of images you will actually edit. For example :\n    - 5 stars means \"to edit absolutely\",\n    - 4 stars means \"to edit if time permits\",\n    - 3 stars means \"backup plan\",\n- Color labels help tracking where you are in your workflow. For example :\n    - Red label means \"basic/batch editing applied\",\n    - Yellow label means \"fine-tuned/individual editing applied\",\n    - Green label means \"exported\",\n    - Blue label means \"printed\"\n    - Purple label means \"sent to client\".\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Those are, of course, examples that you can adapt to your needs and workflow. Note that you can also use text tags to keep track of all that, but they will not show over thumbnails in the lighttable.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/lighttable/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Selecting and acting on images"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The lighttable enforces a simple, safe interaction model:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Any action that **writes** metadata (ratings, color labels, tags, history…) applies only to **explicitly selected** images.\n- A selection is only ever made by a \"hard\" interaction: a **mouse click** or a **keyboard keystroke**. Hovering an image never changes a selection and never writes anything — hover events are strictly read-only.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This rules out the accidental metadata changes that a hover-driven workflow can cause. Selection is also _What You See Is What You Get_: you can only select images that are actually visible on screen.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Selecting with the mouse:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- **Click** a thumbnail to select it alone.\n- **<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+click** to add/remove a thumbnail to/from the selection.\n- **<kbd>Shift</kbd>+click** to extend the selection up to the clicked thumbnail (range selection).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Selecting with the keyboard is described in [shortcuts and keyboard interaction](../../getting-started/keyboard.md#thumbtable); batch helpers (select all, invert, clear) live in the [_Selection_ menu](../global-menu.md#selection).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Double-click a thumbnail, or select it and press <kbd>Enter</kbd>, to open it in the [darkroom](../darkroom/_index.md).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/lighttable/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Lighttable display"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The display options live in the [second top row](../_index.md#second-top-row), next to the collection filters, and in the **Display** menu. They control the size of the grid, in-image magnification and the overlays drawn over thumbnails.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/lighttable/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Columns (grid size)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The **Columns** spinner sets how many images are shown per row (1 to 12). Fewer columns means larger thumbnails. You can also:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- **<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+scroll** over the grid to change the number of columns,\n- press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>+</kbd> / <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>-</kbd> to add/remove a column.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\nComing from Darktable: \"zoom\" used to mean both the number of images per row and the magnification inside a thumbnail. These are now two distinct controls — **Columns** for the grid size, **Zoom** for the magnification.\n{{< /note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/lighttable/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Zoom (magnification)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The **Zoom** control magnifies the content _inside_ each thumbnail frame: **Fit**, **50 %**, **100 %** or **200 %**. When you zoom in:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- **drag** inside a thumbnail to pan it,\n- **<kbd>Shift</kbd>+drag** to pan across **all** zoomed thumbnails at once,\n- Ansel automatically pans each image toward the barycenter of its details, so faces and subjects roughly line up across pictures with different framing and aspect ratios.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This makes the lighttable a practical tool for comparing sharpness and framing across a series, replacing the dedicated culling/preview views.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Thumbnails can carry overlays: star rating, reject mark, color labels, group borders and metadata. From the **Display → Thumbnail overlays** menu you choose whether overlays are **always hidden** (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>H</kbd>), **shown on hover**, or **always shown** (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>O</kbd>).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Two analysis overlays are available from the display toolbar:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Overlay focus zones\n: Shade each thumbnail to indicate the regions in focus.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Overlay focus peaking\n: Highlight the sharpest edges of each thumbnail, to quickly judge focus across a series. See [focus peaking](../toolboxes/focus-peaking.md).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/lighttable/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Thumbnail source and group display"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Thumbnail generation and group display are also set from the **Display** menu:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- **Thumbnail source** — process the RAW, use the embedded JPEG only when the image is unedited, or always use the embedded JPEG. Changing this takes effect at runtime.\n- **Collapse grouped images** and **Show group borders** (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>P</kbd>) — see [grouping](digital-asset-management/grouping.md).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Scroll the grid with the mouse wheel. From the keyboard, navigate thumbnails with the arrow keys, jump a page with <kbd>Page Up</kbd>/<kbd>Page Down</kbd>, and go to the start/end with <kbd>Home</kbd>/<kbd>End</kbd>. The full keyboard model is documented in [shortcuts and keyboard interaction](../../getting-started/keyboard.md#thumbtable).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/lighttable/history-copy-paste.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Copy and paste history"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/history-copy-paste.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Transfer the editing recipe from one image to one or many others. The result is a **merge** of the source history into each destination image — not a blind overwrite — which means you have fine-grained control over how conflicts between the two histories are resolved.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/history-copy-paste.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Copy-paste is available from the **Edit** menu in any view, and the keyboard shortcuts work in both the lighttable and the darkroom.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/history-copy-paste.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< warning >}}\nThe current darkroom image cannot be a paste target: Ansel applies the paste to the live in-memory pipeline, which would cause conflicts with the undo stack. The darkroom image is silently excluded from the destination list when you paste.\n{{< /warning >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/lighttable/history-copy-paste.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Copying a history"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/lighttable/history-copy-paste.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Copy all (Ctrl+C)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/history-copy-paste.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Edit → Copy history (all)** records the entire editing recipe of the single selected image into a clipboard. The clipboard persists for the duration of the session.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/history-copy-paste.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The source image must be saved to the database before the clipboard is filled. If you are in the darkroom and the image has unsaved in-progress edits, Ansel flushes those to the database first so the clipboard always reflects what you see on screen.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/lighttable/history-copy-paste.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Selective copy (Ctrl+Shift+C)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/history-copy-paste.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Edit → Copy history (parts)** opens a dialog listing all processing modules that are present in the source image's history. Tick or untick individual modules to choose exactly which parts of the recipe you want to transfer. Only the ticked modules will be pasted.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/lighttable/history-copy-paste.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Pasting a history"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/lighttable/history-copy-paste.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Paste all (Ctrl+V)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/history-copy-paste.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Edit → Paste history (all)** applies the entire clipboard to all selected images. If **Ask merge settings before paste** is enabled (the default), a dialog appears first so you can choose how the incoming history is integrated. See [Merge settings dialog](#merge-settings-dialog) below.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/lighttable/history-copy-paste.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Paste parts (Ctrl+Shift+V)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/history-copy-paste.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Edit → Paste history (parts)** opens the same module-selection dialog as selective copy, but this time against the clipboard contents. Tick the modules you want to paste, then click OK. The merge settings dialog appears next (if enabled).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/history-copy-paste.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Because each destination image already has its own editing history, pasting is a **merge** operation. The dialog gives you two independent choices each time you paste (or lets you save a default and skip the dialog).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/lighttable/history-copy-paste.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Merge position"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/history-copy-paste.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Controls how the incoming history is placed relative to the destination's existing history. Since the processing pipeline is applied from the bottom up (early modules run first, later modules override), placement determines which side wins when the same module appears in both histories.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (mermaid)
#: content/views/lighttable/history-copy-paste.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "graph LR\n    subgraph \"Below — Prepend\"\n        direction LR\n        A1[\"Incoming edits (base)\"] --> B1[\"Your existing edits\"]\n    end\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (mermaid)
#: content/views/lighttable/history-copy-paste.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "graph LR\n    subgraph \"Above — Append\"\n        direction LR\n        A2[\"Your existing edits (base)\"] --> B2[\"Incoming edits\"]\n    end\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (mermaid)
#: content/views/lighttable/history-copy-paste.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "graph LR\n    subgraph \"Replace\"\n        direction LR\n        A3[\"Incoming edits only\"] --> C3[\"Output\"]\n    end\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/history-copy-paste.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Below (Prepend)\n: The incoming history is inserted **before** your existing edits in the processing stack. Your current edits run later and therefore **override conflicts**. Use this when you want to apply a baseline recipe while keeping your personal adjustments on top.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/history-copy-paste.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Above (Append)\n: The incoming history is inserted **after** your existing edits. The incoming edits run later and therefore **override conflicts**. Use this when the source image carries corrections (e.g. a colour calibration) that should take precedence over what is already in the destination.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/history-copy-paste.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Replace\n: Your existing history is discarded entirely and replaced with the incoming one. No conflict resolution is needed because nothing is kept from the destination.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/lighttable/history-copy-paste.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Use incoming pipeline order"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/history-copy-paste.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The _pipeline order_ is the spatial order in which processing modules execute — independent from the temporal history stack. When checked, the module execution order is taken from the source image. When unchecked, your current pipeline order is preserved.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/history-copy-paste.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\nThe pipeline order matters when you have reordered modules from their defaults (e.g. moved _colour calibration_ before _exposure_). Copying pipeline order from an image with a different arrangement will resequence your pipeline.\n{{< /note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/lighttable/history-copy-paste.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ask me every time"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/history-copy-paste.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When checked, this dialog appears every time you paste. When unchecked, the current settings are used silently. You can still change the saved defaults at any time via **Edit → History pasting mode** and **Edit → Nodes pasting mode**.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/lighttable/history-copy-paste.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Default settings (Edit menu)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/history-copy-paste.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The merge settings persisted between sessions are controlled by two submenus in the Edit menu:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/history-copy-paste.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Edit → History pasting mode** sets the default merge position:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/history-copy-paste.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- **Prepend** — below mine, my edits win\n- **Append** — above mine, incoming wins\n- **Replace** — discard destination history\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/history-copy-paste.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Edit → Nodes pasting mode → Copy module order** toggles whether the source pipeline order is copied.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/history-copy-paste.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Edit → Ask merge settings before paste** toggles whether the dialog is shown before each paste operation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/history-copy-paste.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "These defaults are used silently when **Ask me every time** is disabled, and are pre-filled in the dialog when it is shown.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/history-copy-paste.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\nCopy-paste and [styles](../toolboxes/styles.md) each maintain **independent** merge settings. Changing the paste defaults here has no effect on style application, and vice versa.\n{{< /note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/lighttable/history-copy-paste.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Workflow examples"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/lighttable/history-copy-paste.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Propagating a colour calibration to a batch"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/history-copy-paste.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. Edit one image: apply _colour calibration_, _exposure_, and _tone equalizer_.\n2. **Edit → Copy history (parts)** and tick only _colour calibration_.\n3. Select the rest of the batch in the lighttable.\n4. **Edit → Paste history (parts)**, choose **Above mine** so the calibration overrides any existing per-image settings.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/lighttable/history-copy-paste.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Sharing a base recipe across a project"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/history-copy-paste.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. Develop one image to the look you want for the whole project.\n2. **Edit → Copy history (all)**.\n3. Select all images from the same session.\n4. **Edit → Paste history (all)**, choose **Below mine** so per-image exposure adjustments you add later will override the shared base.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/lighttable/history-copy-paste.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Resetting to a clean slate"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/history-copy-paste.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. Copy from an image that has only the default pipeline (no edits).\n2. **Edit → Paste history (all)**, choose **Replace**.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/history-copy-paste.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This is equivalent to **Edit → Delete history** but lets you keep the default module activations from a specific image rather than from Ansel's built-in defaults.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/history-copy-paste.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| Action | Shortcut |\n|---|---|\n| Copy history (all) | Ctrl+C |\n| Copy history (parts) | Ctrl+Shift+C |\n| Paste history (all) | Ctrl+V |\n| Paste history (parts) | Ctrl+Shift+V |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/lighttable/undo-redo.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Undo/redo"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/undo-redo.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Most changes made within the lighttable are recorded and can be reverted to a previous state. This includes modifications to color labels, ratings, geo-localization, tags, metadata, orientation, [copy/paste of history](./history-copy-paste.md), image duplication, or [application of a style](../toolboxes/styles.md). The undo/redo facility is unlimited in the number of steps while in the lighttable, but it is reset each time you switch to a different view.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/undo-redo.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd> to undo the last modification and <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Y</kbd> to redo the last undone one (if any). These are also available from the global menu, under _Edit_ 🡒 _Undo_ / _Redo_.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/undo-redo.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The [darkroom](../darkroom/_index.md) has its own, independent undo/redo for image-editing actions; see the [history of changes](../toolboxes/history-stack.md).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/grouping.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Image grouping"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/grouping.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Grouping images helps to improve the structure and clarity of your image collection when displayed in the lighttable view.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/grouping.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "You can combine images into a group by selecting them and using the global menu _Image_ 🡒 _Group images_ (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>G</kbd>). Likewise, remove selected images from a group with _Image_ 🡒 _Ungroup images_ (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>G</kbd>).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/grouping.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Duplicated images are automatically grouped together. Similarly, if you import multiple images from the same directory, having the same base name, but different extensions (eg. `IMG_1234.CR2` and `IMG_1234.JPG`), those images automatically form a group.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/grouping.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Images that are members of a group are denoted by a group icon on their thumbnail.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/grouping.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Whether groups are collapsed is controlled from the global menu _Display_ 🡒 _Collapse grouped images_. When this is off, all images are displayed as individual thumbnails. When it is on, each group is represented by a single thumbnail (the group leader). Press the group icon on a group leader's thumbnail to expand that group (press again to collapse); expanding another group collapses the first.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/grouping.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A group can be outlined by a frame around its member thumbnails, enabled with _Display_ 🡒 _Show group borders_ (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>P</kbd>). The border becomes more visible when you hover over one of the group's images.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/grouping.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "You can define which image is considered to be the group leader by clicking on the group icon of the desired image while that group is expanded. The group icon is shown only if grouping mode is enabled, so to change the group leader, you need to first enable grouping, expand the appropriate group and finally click the group icon of the desired \"group leader\" image. The current group leader is shown in a tooltip when you hover over the group icon of an image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/grouping.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If you collapse an image group and then enter darkroom mode (e.g. by double-clicking on the thumbnail), the group leader image will be opened for developing.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/grouping.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Image groups are also a convenient way to protect an existing history stack against unintentional changes. If you have just finalized an image and want to protect its current version, select the image, duplicate it with _Edit_ 🡒 _Duplicate existing history_ (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>D</kbd>), and make sure grouping is collapsed. Now, whenever you open the image group again in the darkroom, only the group leader will be altered, and the underlying duplicate remains unchanged.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/grouping.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Note:** “duplicating images” only generates a copy of an image's history stack, stored in another small XMP file. There is still only one raw file.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/_index.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Digital asset management"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Beyond editing pixels, Ansel keeps track of your images and the work you do on them. This is _digital asset management_ (DAM): organizing, describing, rating, grouping and safeguarding your collection so it stays searchable and recoverable over time.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The pages in this section cover how that information is stored and used:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- [Sidecar files](sidecar.md) — how edits and metadata are saved alongside your originals as XMP files, and [importing sidecars](sidecar-import.md) from other software.\n- [Star ratings & color labels](star-color.md) — ranking and classifying images for culling.\n- [Metadata and tagging](metadata-tagging.md) — descriptive text and keywords.\n- [Image grouping](grouping.md) — stacking related images together.\n- [Local copies](local-copies.md) — working off-site and re-synchronizing later.\n- [Thumbnails](thumbnails.md) — how previews are generated and cached.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/metadata-tagging.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Metadata and tagging"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/metadata-tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel allows you to store additional information about your images to allow them to be more easily searched and grouped. This information is stored in Ansel's database and XMP sidecar files and can also be included within exported images.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/metadata-tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Metadata (e.g. title, description) is free-format text that usually differs for each image. You can add metadata to images in the [metadata editor](../../toolboxes/metadata-editor.md) module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/metadata-tagging.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Tags are usually shared between multiple images and are used to categorise and group them. You can add tags to images in the [tagging](../../toolboxes/tagging.md) module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/star-color.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Star ratings & color labels"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/star-color.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Star ratings and color labels help you to sort and rank images according to your own criteria. An image's star rating and color labels can be displayed over thumbnails in the lighttable view and filmstrip module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/star-color.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Star ratings"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/star-color.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "You can give an image a rating from zero to five stars. Whenever you import images, each image receives a default rating which you can define in the [import](../../../getting-started/import.md) module.  You can also mark an image as “rejected”.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/star-color.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "There are several ways to change a rating. While hovering the cursor over an image thumbnail, you can press a number key 0 – 5 to define the number of stars, or press R to “reject” an image. This is probably the fastest way to rate your images on first inspection of a film roll.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/star-color.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "You can also click directly on the star icons overlaid on the thumbnails (click the ✕ to reject), or use the global menu _Image_ 🡒 _Ratings_.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/star-color.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "As rejecting an image removes the currently-applied star rating, you can undo the rejection by clicking x or pressing R again.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/star-color.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "To rate multiple images at once, select those images in the lighttable or filmstrip and then press the appropriate shortcut key.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/star-color.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Color labels"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/star-color.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Color labels are another way to classify images, and can be used as an alternative to star ratings or to work alongside them. Each image can carry any combination of one or more color labels (red, yellow, green, blue, or purple).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/star-color.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "You can set the color labels for a single image by hovering your cursor over the thumbnail and pressing the function keys F1 – F5, which correspond with the labels in the order given above. F6 clears all labels.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/star-color.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "To set the color labels of one or more images, select the desired images in the lighttable and then press the appropriate function key, or use the global menu _Image_ 🡒 _Color labels_. A color label is added to all selected images if **any** of them do not currently have it; otherwise the label is removed from all of them. To remove all labels (of any color) from the selected images, press F6 (or _Image_ 🡒 _Color labels_ 🡒 _Clear labels_).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/sidecar.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Sidecar files"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/sidecar.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel is a non-destructive image editor and opens all images in read-only mode. Any data created within Ansel (metadata, tags, and image processing steps) is stored in separate `.XMP` _sidecar_ files. These files are stored alongside the original Raw files and allow Ansel to store information about the images as well as the full editing history without touching the original raw files. When you import an image into Ansel for the first time, an XMP file is automatically generated. The generation of XMP files can be disabled in [preferences > storage > xmp](../../../preferences-settings/storage.md#xmp) but this is not recommended in normal use.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/sidecar.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "For a given source image, multiple editing versions, called _duplicates_, can co-exist, sharing the same input image data but each having their own metadata, tags and processing steps. Each duplicate of a given image (named `<basename>.<extension`) is represented by a separate XMP sidecar file (with a filename constructed in the form `<basename>_nn.<extension>.xmp`, where `nn` represents the version number of that edit). Information for the initial edit -- the “duplicate” with version number zero -- is stored in the sidecar file named `<basename>.<extension>.xmp`. The version number of each duplicate is displayed in the [image information](../../toolboxes/image-information.md) module in each of Ansel's views.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/sidecar.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Your work is automatically synchronised to the sidecar files without the need to press a “save” button. When backing up your data, make sure that you also retain copies of the XMP files, as these are required to fully reconstruct your work in case of a disaster.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/sidecar.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In addition to the sidecar files, Ansel keeps all image-related data in its library database for fast access. An image can only be viewed and edited from within Ansel if its data has first been loaded into the library database. This happens automatically when you first [import](../../../getting-started/import.md) an image. If an image is subsequently re-imported, the database will be updated from the contents of its XMP file.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/sidecar.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Once an image has been imported into Ansel, the database entries take precedence over the XMP file. Subsequent changes to the XMP file by any other software are not visible to Ansel -- such changes will be overwritten the next time Ansel synchronizes the file. On request, Ansel can be configured to search for updated XMP files at startup, offering a choice to update the database or overwrite the XMP file where changes are identified. This configuration can be changed in [preferences > storage > xmp](../../../preferences-settings/storage.md#xmp).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/thumbnails.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Each image in the current collection is represented by a thumbnail in the lighttable view and filmstrip module. A cache of the most recently used thumbnails is stored in a file on disk and loaded into memory at startup. The size of this cache can be adjusted in [Preferences > Processing > CPU, GPU, Memory](../../../preferences-settings/processing.md#cpu-gpu-memory).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/thumbnails.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Thumbnail creation"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/thumbnails.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A thumbnail is created when an image is imported into Ansel for the first time, after an image has been modified in the darkroom, or when revisiting an image whose thumbnail is no longer available.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/thumbnails.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When an image is imported for the first time Ansel can either try to extract an embedded thumbnail from the input image (most raw files contain these, usually in JPEG format) or process the raw image itself using default settings. Choose how Ansel obtains its thumbnails from the global menu _Display_ 🡒 _Thumbnail source_ — _always process the RAW_, _use embedded JPG if unedited_, or _always use embedded JPG_. This can be changed at runtime.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/thumbnails.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Extracting an embedded thumbnail from the input image is usually very fast. However, these thumbnails have been generated by the raw converter of the camera and do not represent Ansel's “view” of that image. You will notice the difference as soon as you open the image in the darkroom mode, at which point Ansel replaces the thumbnail with its own internally processed version.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/thumbnails.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "After import Ansel automatically generates thumbnails for new images as they are needed. When importing a large set of new images, thumbnail generation can slow down navigation in the lighttable view. Alternatively you may terminate Ansel and generate the thumbnail cache separately by running [`ansel-generate-cache`](../../../cli/ansel-generate-cache.md). This program will generate all missing thumbnails in one go.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/thumbnails.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "As the thumbnail cache has a pre-defined maximum size it will eventually get filled up. If new thumbnails are subsequently added, old thumbnails are dropped from the cache. However, Ansel will keep all thumbnails on disk if the corresponding disk backend option is activated in [Preferences > Processing > CPU, GPU, Memory](../../../preferences-settings/processing.md#cpu-gpu-memory). Access to the thumbnails in this secondary cache is slower than the primary cache, but still much faster than reprocessing thumbnails from scratch. The size of the secondary cache is limited only by the available disk space.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/thumbnails.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "You can pre-render or purge cached thumbnails for the selected images at runtime from the global menu _Run_ 🡒 _Preload selected thumbnails in cache_ and _Purge selected thumbnails from cache_.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/thumbnails.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Thumbnails are never removed automatically from the secondary cache. You can manually clean it by recursively deleting all images in the `$HOME/.cache/ansel/mipmaps-xyz.d` folder (where `xyz` denotes an alphanumeric identifier of the cache). After clearing the secondary cache you can simply let Ansel re-generate thumbnails as needed, or generate them all in one go with [`ansel-generate-cache`](../../../cli/ansel-generate-cache.md).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/thumbnails.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If you choose not to activate the disk backend and select too small a cache size, Ansel may become unresponsive, you may experience continuous regeneration of thumbnails when you navigate your collection or flickering of thumbnail images. A good choice of cache size is 512MB or higher (see [Memory and performance tuning](../../../preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md) for more information).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/thumbnails.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "All thumbnails are fully color managed. Colors are rendered accurately on screen as long as your system is properly set up to hand over the right monitor profile to Ansel. For more information see the [color management](../../../color-management/_index.md) section.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/thumbnails.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Skulls"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/thumbnails.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If for some reason Ansel is unable to generate a thumbnail, it displays an image of a skull instead. Don't panic!\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/thumbnails.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "![skull](skull.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/thumbnails.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "There are three main reasons this could happen:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/thumbnails.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- _Missing image file_: Ansel remembers all images it has ever imported, as long as they have not been removed from your database. If Ansel wants to create a thumbnail but is not able to open the input file, a skull is displayed instead. Users are advised to remove images from the database using the selected images module before physically removing them from disk. Alternatively you may occasionally run the script [`purge_non_existing_images.sh`](../../../cli/purge_non_existing_images_sh.md) from Ansel's toolset to clean-up your database.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/thumbnails.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- _Invalid image format_: While the extension of an image may seem to be supported by Ansel, its contents could be either an unsupported image format or a corrupt file.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/thumbnails.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- _Low memory_: If Ansel runs out of memory while generating a thumbnail, it will warn you and display a skull. This can happen if Ansel is run with sub-optimal settings, especially on a 32-bit system. See [memory and performance tuning](../../../preferences-settings/performance/mem-performance.md) for more information.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/sidecar-import.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Importing sidecar files generated by other applications"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/sidecar-import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid " When importing an image, Ansel automatically checks if it is accompanied by a sidecar file. As well as looking for files named `<basename>.<extension>.xmp` and `<basename>_nn.<extension>.xmp` (Ansel's XMP file naming formats) Ansel also checks for the presence of a file in the form `<basename>.xmp` (the naming format for Lightroom's XMP sidecar files). Files with the latter naming format will be read by Ansel but will not be written to. Once the image has been imported, Ansel will generate an additional XMP file using its own naming convention.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/sidecar-import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "At present, Ansel is able to load the following metadata from Lightroom-generated sidecar files during the import process:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/sidecar-import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Tags (including hierarchical tags)\n- Color labels\n- Ratings\n- GPS information\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/sidecar-import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In addition, Ansel has been designed to help migrate some image operations from other specific applications. The aim here is not to make Ansel a drop-in replacement for any other software, but rather to help you to recover part of the work you have already invested into your image. It is important to understand that the import process will never give identical results to other software. The underlying processing engines are very different from application to application, and depend a lot on the individual image. In some cases, the results may be similar but often, further adjustment will be required in Ansel.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/sidecar-import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This migration happens automatically when entering the darkroom view, provided that a corresponding XMP sidecar is found.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/sidecar-import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "At present, Ansel is able to handle the following development steps from Lightroom-generated XMP files (with the corresponding Ansel module in parentheses):\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/sidecar-import.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Black level ([_exposure_](../../darkroom/modules/exposure.md))\n- Exposure ([_exposure_](../../darkroom/modules/exposure.md))\n- Vignette ([_vignetting_](../../darkroom/modules/vignetting.md))\n- Clarity ([_local contrast_](../../darkroom/modules/local-contrast.md))\n- HSL ([_color zones_](../../darkroom/modules/color-zones.md))\n- Grain ([_grain_](../../darkroom/modules/grain.md))\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/local-copies.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Local copies"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/local-copies.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Many users have huge image collections stored on extra hard drives in their desktop computer, or on an external storage medium (RAID NAS, external hard drives etc.).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/local-copies.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "It is a common requirement to develop a number of images while travelling using a laptop and then later synchronize them back to the original storage medium. However, copying images manually from the main storage to the laptop and back is cumbersome and prone to errors. The “local copies” feature of Ansel has been designed to directly support these use cases.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/local-copies.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Create local copies of the selected images from the global menu _File_ 🡒 _Copy distant images locally_. Local copies are always used when present, giving continued access to images even if the external storage is no longer connected. Later, when your primary storage medium has been reconnected, re-synchronize the XMP sidecar files back to it (and delete the local copies) with _File_ 🡒 _Resynchronize distant images_.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/local-copies.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "For safety reasons, if local copies exist and the external storage is available, the local XMP sidecars are automatically synchronized at start up.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/local-copies.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Local copies are stored within the `$HOME/.cache/ansel` directory and named `img-<SIGNATURE>.<EXT>` (where `SIGNATURE` is a hash signature (MD5) of the full pathname, and `EXT` is the original filename extension).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/lighttable/digital-asset-management/local-copies.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Local copies can be identified in the lighttable view by a white marker on the top right of the thumbnail. In addition, all local copies carry the `darktable|local-copy` tag to allow them to be easily selected.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The global menu bar sits at the top-left of the [header](_index.md#header-top-panel) and is available in every view. It gathers the application-wide commands, image operations, history and style management, maintenance tasks, display settings, view switching and help. It is the central reference point of the interface: most commands that are not a per-module setting live here.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\nComing from Darktable: many commands that were previously buried inside side-panel modules, cryptic icon buttons or undocumented keyboard shortcuts have been collected into this menu. \"Modules\" that were really just grids of buttons (image actions, view toolbox, selection helpers…) became menu entries.\n{{< /note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Using the menu"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Click a menu title to open it, or press <kbd>Alt</kbd> to reveal the underlined **mnemonic** letters and press one to open the matching menu. Once a menu is open, navigate all menus with the arrow keys and trigger an entry with <kbd>Enter</kbd>.\n- Entries that have a default keyboard shortcut show it on their right. All these shortcuts are **defaults that you can change** from _Edit → Keyboard shortcuts…_ (see [shortcuts and keyboard interaction](../getting-started/keyboard.md)).\n- Many entries act on the **active images** — that is, the images currently [selected](../getting-started/keyboard.md#thumbtable) in the lighttable or filmstrip. When nothing is selected, those entries are greyed out.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The menus are described below in their bar order.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "File"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Image-file and collection operations.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Import… (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>I</kbd>)\n: Open the [import](../getting-started/import.md) window to add images to the library.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Export… (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>E</kbd>)\n: Open the [export](toolboxes/export.md) settings to render the selected images to files.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Recent collections\n: A submenu listing the collections you have recently browsed; pick one to restore it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Copy files on disk… / Move files on disk…\n: Physically copy or move the selected image files to another folder, updating the library accordingly.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Create a blended HDR\n: Merge the selected bracketed exposures into a single high-dynamic-range DNG.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Copy distant images locally / Resynchronize distant images\n: Manage [local copies](lighttable/digital-asset-management/local-copies.md) of images stored on removable or network drives.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Remove from library (<kbd>Delete</kbd>)\n: Remove the selected images from Ansel's library **without** deleting the files on disk. Available in the lighttable.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Delete from disk (<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Delete</kbd>)\n: Remove the selected images from the library **and** delete (or trash) the files on disk. Available in the lighttable.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Quit (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Q</kbd>)\n: Close Ansel.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Edit"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Editing-history management, undo/redo and application settings. See [history copy/paste](lighttable/history-copy-paste.md) and [undo/redo](lighttable/undo-redo.md).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Undo (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd>) / Redo (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Y</kbd>)\n: Step backwards/forwards through your most recent actions in the current view.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Copy history (all) (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>C</kbd>) / Copy history (parts)… (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>C</kbd>)\n: Copy the whole editing history of the selected image, or pick which modules to copy.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Paste history (all) (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>V</kbd>) / Paste history (parts)… (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>V</kbd>)\n: Paste the copied history onto the selected images, either entirely or selectively.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "History pasting mode\n: How a pasted history combines with the target's existing history:\n    - **Prepend** — the copied history is applied _before_ the current one; current edits win conflicts.\n    - **Append** — the copied history is applied _after_ the current one; copied edits win conflicts.\n    - **Replace** — the current history is discarded and replaced by the copied one.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Nodes pasting mode → Copy module order\n: When enabled, pasting also transfers the module (pixelpipe node) order along with the settings.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ask merge settings before paste\n: When enabled, a dialog asks how to merge the histories each time you paste, instead of using the modes above silently.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Load history from XMP…\n: Apply an editing history read from an external XMP sidecar file to the selected images.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Create new history (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>N</kbd>)\n: Create a fresh, empty duplicate of the selected image to start a new edit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Duplicate existing history (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>D</kbd>)\n: Create a duplicate of the selected image that carries over its current edit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Compress history\n: Collapse the history stack to the minimum set of states producing the current result.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Delete history\n: Discard the editing history of the selected images, resetting them.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Preferences…\n: Open the [preferences & settings](../preferences-settings/_index.md) window.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Keyboard shortcuts…\n: Open the [shortcuts](../getting-started/keyboard.md#finding-default-shortcuts-setting-new-ones) window, which both lists and lets you edit every shortcut.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Batch selection helpers that work on the current collection. Selecting from the thumbnail grid is described in [shortcuts and keyboard interaction](../getting-started/keyboard.md#thumbtable).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Select all (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>A</kbd>)\n: Select every image in the current collection.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Clear selection (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>A</kbd>)\n: Deselect everything.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Invert selection (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>I</kbd>)\n: Select the images that are not currently selected, and vice-versa.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Scroll back to selection\n: Scroll the grid to bring the selected image(s) into view.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Image"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Per-image metadata and grouping actions, applied to the selected images.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Rotate\n: **90° counter-clockwise**, **90° clockwise**, or **Reset rotation**. This sets the image orientation flag.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Color labels\n: Toggle a color label: **Red** (<kbd>F1</kbd>), **Yellow** (<kbd>F2</kbd>), **Green** (<kbd>F3</kbd>), **Blue** (<kbd>F4</kbd>), **Purple** (<kbd>F5</kbd>), or **Clear labels** (<kbd>F6</kbd>).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ratings\n: Set a star rating: **Reject** (<kbd>R</kbd>), **★** (<kbd>1</kbd>) … **★★★★★** (<kbd>5</kbd>), or **Clear rating** (<kbd>0</kbd>).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Reload EXIF from file\n: Re-read the EXIF metadata from the original files, discarding any cached values.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Group images (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>G</kbd>) / Ungroup images (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>G</kbd>)\n: Combine the selected images into a [group](lighttable/digital-asset-management/grouping.md), or break the group apart.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Apply and manage [styles](toolboxes/styles.md) — named, reusable sets of module settings.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "(list of styles)\n: Each available style is listed; click it to apply it to the selected images. Hovering a style name previews it on the current image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "History pasting mode / Nodes pasting mode / Ask merge settings before apply\n: Control how a style merges with the target's existing history, exactly like the equivalent options in the _Edit_ menu.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Create new style…\n: Create a style from the editing history of the selected image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Manage styles…\n: Open the style manager to rename, edit, import, export or delete styles.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Run"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Background and maintenance tasks.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Clear darkroom pipeline caches\n: Free the cached intermediate pipeline results, forcing a full recompute on the next edit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Preload selected thumbnails in cache\n: Pre-render the thumbnails of the selected images into the on-disk cache, up to a chosen resolution (from _360×225 px_ up to _8K_, or _for current grid size_). Runs as a background task.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Purge selected thumbnails from cache\n: Delete the cached thumbnails of the selected images from disk, so they are regenerated next time.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Defragment the library\n: Optimize the library database file.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Backup the library\n: Make a backup copy of the library database.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Resynchronize library and XMP\n: Scan for XMP sidecars that changed outside Ansel and reconcile them with the library.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Save selected developments to XMP\n: Write the editing history of the selected images to their XMP sidecar files.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Resynchronize database with distant XMP for local copies\n: Reconcile the library with the XMP sidecars of images held as local copies.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\nComing from Darktable: preloading and purging the thumbnail cache used to be possible only through hidden shell scripts. They are now first-class menu commands, and the choice between processing the RAW or using an embedded JPEG (in the _Display_ menu) can be changed at runtime instead of only in the preferences.\n{{< /note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/global-menu.md content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Display"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "View-level display settings. These are detailed in the [views overview](_index.md#display-menu).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Monitor color profile / Monitor color intent\n: The display [color-management](../color-management/_index.md) profile and rendering intent (_Perceptual_, _Relative colorimetric_, _Absolute colorimetric_).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Panels\n: Individually toggle the **Top** (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>T</kbd>), **Left** (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>L</kbd>), **Right** (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>R</kbd>) and **Filmstrip** (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>F</kbd>) panels.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Thumbnail overlays\n: When the badges drawn over thumbnails are shown: **Always hide** (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>H</kbd>), **Show on hover**, or **Always show** (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>O</kbd>).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Thumbnail source\n: How thumbnails are generated: **Always process the RAW**, **Use embedded JPG if unedited**, or **Always use embedded JPG**.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Collapse grouped images / Show group borders (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>P</kbd>)\n: Control the display of [grouped images](lighttable/digital-asset-management/grouping.md).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Full screen (<kbd>F11</kbd>)\n: Toggle fullscreen mode. (To toggle all panels at once, use <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>F11</kbd>.)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ateliers"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The view switcher. It lists every available view and switches to the one you pick (the current view is greyed out):\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- **Lighttable** (<kbd>Escape</kbd>)\n- **Darkroom** (<kbd>Enter</kbd> — opens the selected image)\n- **Studio capture** — to survey and import new image automatically to see them directly into darktable\n- **Map**, **Print**, **Slideshow** (when enabled in the [preferences](../preferences-settings/other-views.md))\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Help"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Documentation and support links.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Online documentation\n: Open this documentation website.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ask a question\n: Open the Ansel search engine.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Join the support chat / Join the support forum\n: Open the community chat and forum.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Open contextual help\n: Turn the cursor into a help probe; click a control to open the relevant documentation page.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Search actions… (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>P</kbd>)\n: Open the [global action search](../getting-started/keyboard.md#vimkey-like-global-action-search).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/global-menu.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "About\n: Show the version and credits dialog.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/slideshow/_index.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Slideshow"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/slideshow/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The Slideshow view is optional and needs to be activated in [Preferences > Other views](../../preferences-settings/other-views.md). It allows you to watch a slideshow of your current collection with the associated filtering rules and sort order applied.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/slideshow/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "To learn more about how to define the collection and filtering rules, see the section on [collections](../../getting-started/library.md).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/slideshow/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The next section provides more details on the [usage](usage.md) of the Slideshow view.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/slideshow/usage.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The slideshow view is still in an early stage of development with only a basic set of features.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block
#: content/views/slideshow/usage.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "\nspacebar             start and stop auto-advance mode which automatically switches\n                     to the next images every five seconds by default.\n\nESC                  leave slideshow mode and return to lighttable view.\n\n\n+ or                 increase delay between each image.\nup arrow\n\n- Or                 decrease delay between each image.\ndown arrow\n\nleft-click or\nright arrow or       switch to the next image of the collection.\nright shift-key\n\nright-click or\nleft arrow or        switch to the previous image of the collection.\nleft shift-key\n\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/slideshow/usage.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Hint:** To take full advantage of your screen size, put Ansel into fullscreen mode by pressing F11 and hide the border-controls by pressing the B key.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Darkroom view layout"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The darkroom surrounds the center image with a left panel of peripheral tools, a right panel of image-processing modules, and a bottom toolbar of visual-assessment overlays. An optional [filmstrip](../../views/toolboxes/filmstrip.md) (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>F</kbd>) can be shown along the bottom for quick navigation through the current collection.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Shown and hidden with <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>L</kbd>. It holds the peripheral tools that support editing (but do not themselves process pixels):\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "[Navigation](../../views/toolboxes/navigation.md)\n: Navigate and zoom the center view via a thumbnail overview. Sits at the top of the panel.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "[Scopes](../../views/toolboxes/scopes.md)\n: A graphical depiction of the image's tones and colors (histogram, waveform, vectorscope…). The [global color picker](../../views/toolboxes/global-color-picker.md), used to sample colors from the image, is part of this module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "[Snapshots](../../views/toolboxes/snapshots.md)\n: Take and compare snapshots against the current edit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "[Duplicate manager](../../views/toolboxes/duplicate-manager.md)\n: View and manage the duplicates (versions) of the current image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "[Masking & Blending](masking-and-blending/_index.md)\n: The blend mode, opacity and [masking](masking-and-blending/masks/_index.md) controls for whichever module currently has focus. Shapes themselves are created and managed from the separate [shape manager](../../views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md) window, opened from the [bottom toolbar](#bottom-panel).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "[History of changes](../../views/toolboxes/history-stack.md)\n: The editing history of the current image, where you can step back to any previous state, compress or reset the history.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "[Image information](../../views/toolboxes/image-information.md)\n: Display EXIF and IPTC information about the current image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "[Notes](../../views/toolboxes/notes.md)\n: Free-text notes attached to the image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\nComing from Darktable: the _metadata_ and _tags_ editors are no longer duplicated in the darkroom. Metadata handling now belongs to the [lighttable](../lighttable/_index.md); the darkroom is dedicated to editing.\n{{< /note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Shown and hidden with <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>R</kbd>. It is dedicated to the [image-processing modules](../../views/darkroom/modules/_index.md): the workflow tabs sit at the top, and the modules themselves fill the rest of the panel.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "To inspect or rearrange the order in which modules are applied, see [the pixelpipe and module order](pixelpipe/the-pixelpipe-and-module-order.md).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Module workflow tabs"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The processing modules are grouped into tabs that follow the pixelpipe, so that editing from left to right (across tabs) and bottom to top (within a tab) walks you through a sound workflow. Hover a tab label for its description.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Pipeline\n: Lists every module that is currently **enabled**, in the reverse order of application in the pixelpipe (last-applied on top). This is your overview of what the image actually does.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Basic\n: Modules that adjust brightness, contrast and dynamic range, work with film scans, and perform color-grading.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Repair\n: Modules that repair and reconstruct noisy or missing pixels.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Sharpness\n: Modules that manipulate local contrast, sharpness and blur.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Effects\n: Modules applying special effects.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Technics\n: Technical modules that can be ignored in most situations.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "All\n: Every module available in the software.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\nComing from Darktable: the icon-labelled module groups and the user-customizable group editor are gone. Tabs are named in words, fixed, and ordered to match the pipeline. The \"favourite modules\" tab is replaced by the ability to jump directly to any module through a shortcut or the [action search](../../getting-started/keyboard.md#vimkey-like-global-action-search).\n{{< /note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Navigating tabs and modules"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "With the mouse\n: Click a visible tab; click the arrows to reveal hidden tabs; right-click a tab for the full list; or scroll over the tab bar to cycle. Scroll the panel to move through modules; unfolding a module scrolls it into view.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "With the keyboard\n: <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Tab</kbd> / <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Tab</kbd> move to the next/previous tab (cycling at the ends). <kbd>Page Down</kbd> / <kbd>Page Up</kbd> expand and focus the next/previous module in the current tab. <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Down</kbd> / <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Up</kbd> move focus between a module's controls.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The complete keyboard model — focusing a module or control, then editing it — is in [shortcuts and keyboard interaction](../../getting-started/keyboard.md#darkroom).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Reordering modules"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Hold <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd> and drag a module by its header to move it. Be aware that this changes the **order of the modules in the pixelpipe**, not just their position on screen — it is best done from the _Pipeline_ or _All_ tab, where you can see the whole pipeline. See [the pixelpipe and module order](pixelpipe/the-pixelpipe-and-module-order.md).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Bottom panel"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A toolbar of visual-assessment overlays. From left to right:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "[Styles](../../views/toolboxes/styles.md)\n: Quick-access styles menu. Hover a style name to preview it on the current image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "[Color assessment](../../views/toolboxes/color-assessment.md)\n: Toggle the ISO 12646 color-assessment view (neutral surround and reference frame).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "[Raw overexposed](../../views/toolboxes/raw-overexposed.md)\n: Toggle raw-overexposure indicators (right-click for options).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "[Clipping](../../views/toolboxes/clipping.md)\n: Toggle output clipping warnings (right-click for options).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "[Soft proofing](../../views/toolboxes/soft-proof.md)\n: Toggle soft-proofing (right-click for options).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "[Gamut check](../../views/toolboxes/gamut.md)\n: Toggle gamut checking (right-click for options).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "[Guides & overlays](../../views/toolboxes/guides-overlays.md)\n: Left-click to toggle the guide overlays; right-click to change the guide settings, including the color of all on-image drawing (masks, crop guides, etc.).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Display\n: Opens the _Picture display options_ popover: background brightness and margins, the rendering size of the center view, and the [mask preview settings](masking-and-blending/_index.md#mask-preview-settings).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "[Shape manager](../../views/toolboxes/shape-manager.md)\n: Open or close the separate floating window used to create and manage the [drawn shapes](masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md) used by masks, across all modules at once.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Pipeline node graph\n: Open the [module-order graph](pixelpipe/the-pixelpipe-and-module-order.md#changing-module-order) — a left-to-right view of the pixelpipe where you can inspect and rearrange the order in which modules are applied.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/darkroom-view-layout.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Autoset\n: Run [autoset](_index.md#autoset-auto-developing-an-image) on the chosen modules: each capable module computes its own settings from the image content. Right-click to pick which modules participate.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/_index.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "The pixelpipe"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The **pixelpipe** is the ordered sequence of [processing modules](../modules/_index.md) that turns your original RAW file into the image you see and export. Each module takes the output of the previous one as its input, applies one operation, and hands its result to the next module — exactly like a stack of adjustment layers, applied from the bottom up.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (mermaid)
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/_index.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "flowchart LR\n    RAW([RAW file]) --> M1[module 1] --> M2[module 2] --> M3[module n] --> OUT([output image])\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Nothing is ever written back to your original file: the pixelpipe is re-run on demand from the RAW plus your [history stack](history-stack.md), both for the on-screen preview and for the final [export](../../toolboxes/export.md).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This section explains the concepts behind that process:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- [The anatomy of a processing module](the-anatomy-of-a-module.md) — what a single module is and the controls it shares with every other module.\n- [The pixelpipe & module order](the-pixelpipe-and-module-order.md) — why the order matters, the scene-referred workflow, and how to inspect or change the order.\n- [The history stack](history-stack.md) — how your edits are recorded.\n- [Undo and redo](undo-redo.md).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Why Ansel stays responsive"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "You do not need to understand the internals to use the darkroom, but one design choice is worth knowing because it shapes how the darkroom feels: **Ansel caches the output of every module and only recomputes what actually changed.**\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Each module's output is stored under a fingerprint of everything it depends on (its parameters, its mask, the image region, the modules before it). When you change a setting, only that module and the ones **after** it in the pipe are recomputed — everything before it is reused from the cache.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (mermaid)
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/_index.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "flowchart LR\n    A[module 1] --> B[module 2] --> C[\"module 3 ✏️<br/>(changed)\"] --> D[module 4] --> E[module 5]\n    classDef cached fill:#2d6,stroke:#161,color:#000;\n    classDef recomp fill:#f95,stroke:#a40,color:#000;\n    class A,B cached;\n    class C,D,E recomp;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In the diagram above, editing module 3 only recomputes modules 3 to 5 (orange); modules 1 and 2 (green) are served instantly from the cache. The same logic means that toggling a module on/off, using a [color picker](../../toolboxes/scopes.md), or re-exporting an image you already exported does not rebuild the whole pipeline. In practice this makes Ansel several times to several **dozens** of times faster than recomputing everything, which is what makes a heavy edit still feel interactive.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/the-pixelpipe-and-module-order.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "The pixelpipe & module order"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/the-pixelpipe-and-module-order.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The ordered sequence of [processing modules](../modules/_index.md) operating on an input file to generate an output image is known as the \"pixelpipe\".\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/the-pixelpipe-and-module-order.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The order of the pixelpipe is represented graphically by the order in which modules are presented in the user interface -- the pixelpipe starts with a RAW image at the bottom of the module list, and applies the processing modules one by one, piling up layer upon layer of processing from the bottom up, until it reaches the top of the list, where it outputs the fully processed image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/the-pixelpipe-and-module-order.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Note:** The order in which processing modules are executed exactly matches the order in which the modules appear in Ansel's user interface. **Changing the order of the modules in the user interface changes how your image is processed.**\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/the-pixelpipe-and-module-order.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Module order and workflows"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/the-pixelpipe-and-module-order.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The order in which modules are executed within the pixelpipe has been carefully chosen to give the best output quality. In previous versions of Ansel it was not possible to change the module order. However, there are a number of very specific use cases where the movement of some modules within the pixelpipe is advised.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/the-pixelpipe-and-module-order.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The _scene-referred_ workflow attempts to perform as many operations as possible in a linear RGB color space, only compressing the tones to fit the output medium (with a non-linear tone mapping) at the end of the pixelpipe. This has the advantage of being a more physically-realistic space to do transformations than the traditional _display-referred_ workflow, which attempts to perform operations in a non-linear _perceptual_ color space. Honoring the physical realism (rather than the perceptual realism) makes it much easier to produce predictable processing algorithms with a minimum of artifacts.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/the-pixelpipe-and-module-order.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The following diagram should help you to understand the difference between these workflows. The early, _scene-referred_ part of the pipe works on linear light; a non-linear tone mapping then compresses the dynamic range; the remaining, _display-referred_ part finishes the image for the screen or print:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (mermaid)
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/the-pixelpipe-and-module-order.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "flowchart LR\n    A([RAW]) --> B[exposure] --> C[color calibration] --> D[color balance RGB]\n    D --> TM{{\"tone mapping<br/>(filmic RGB)\"}}\n    TM --> E[sharpening] --> F[output profile] --> G([display / export])\n    classDef scene fill:#cfe,stroke:#284,color:#000;\n    classDef display fill:#fed,stroke:#a52,color:#000;\n    class A,B,C,D scene;\n    class E,F,G display;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/the-pixelpipe-and-module-order.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. _Scene-referred_ modules (green) process linear data that is proportional to the amount of light collected by the camera at the scene. The dynamic range of an image in the scene-referred section of the pixelpipe is often larger than that of the display medium.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/the-pixelpipe-and-module-order.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "2. At some point in the pixelpipe, these pixel values are compressed by a non-linear tone mapping operation into a smaller dynamic range more suitable for display on a monitor or a print.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/the-pixelpipe-and-module-order.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "3. The remaining modules operate in the non-linear _display-referred_ section of the pixelpipe to produce the final output image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/the-pixelpipe-and-module-order.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Changing module order"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/the-pixelpipe-and-module-order.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "It remains highly recommended that users not change the order within the pixelpipe for a number of reasons:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/the-pixelpipe-and-module-order.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- The sequence of modules has been selected with great care in order to give highest output quality. Changes to the sequence often worsen the result rather than improving it.\n- Some processing modules simply don't make sense if they are shifted in the pixelpipe. For example, [_highlight reconstruction_](../modules/highlight-reconstruction.md) needs to be performed on raw data before [_demosaic_](../modules/demosaic.md), which itself needs to be performed before any [_input color profile_](../modules/input-color-profile.md) can be applied. For this reason it is still not possible to move some of the modules that are placed early in the pixelpipe.\n- Most processing modules are designed to work within a specific color space (see the [color management](../../../color-management/_index.md) section for more details). Full flexibility would require modules to support different parallel algorithms depending on the color space they are working in, which would drastically increase complexity.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/the-pixelpipe-and-module-order.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Despite the general recommendation to leave the pixelpipe order alone, it is possible to move modules within the pixelpipe from the darkroom module-order graph. Open it from the node-graph button in the darkroom toolbar, then drag and drop modules directly in the graph to a new location. This should only be done by experienced users who understand the impact this will have on the image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/the-pixelpipe-and-module-order.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The graph popup provides a scrollable left-to-right view of the pipeline, from the base image to the screen output. It shows:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/the-pixelpipe-and-module-order.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- The current execution order of visible modules,\n- The active color-space lifecycle across the pipeline,\n- The input/output runtime descriptors for each module,\n- Raster-mask dependencies between producer and consumer modules,\n- The module-order presets toolbar, used to add presets, reset the order, or apply an existing preset.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/the-pixelpipe-and-module-order.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Modules that cannot cross ordering fences are constrained by the same rules as the processing pipeline itself, so impossible moves are prevented in the graph.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/the-pixelpipe-and-module-order.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The module order can be manually changed back to either the _v3.0_ or _legacy_ versions using the module order popup, which can also be used to define your own custom module order presets.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/the-anatomy-of-a-module.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "The anatomy of a processing module"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/the-anatomy-of-a-module.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The basic element of image processing in Ansel is the [processing module](../modules/_index.md). In order to process a raw image a number of such modules act on the input image in sequence, each performing a different _operation_ on the image data. For those familiar with Adobe Photoshop, the concept of a _processing module_ in Ansel is analogous to that of an _adjustment layer_ in that both make an incremental adjustment to the image, building on top of the adjustments that came before.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/the-anatomy-of-a-module.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "[Utility modules](../../toolboxes/_index.md) are also provided by Ansel, however these are not directly involved in image processing, instead providing a GUI that allows you to manage your images, tag them, export them etc.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/the-anatomy-of-a-module.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Every processing module acts independently of the others, but all modules perform their processing in a similar manner:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (mermaid)
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/the-anatomy-of-a-module.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "flowchart TD\n    IN([module input]) --> OP[1 · operation]\n    OP --> PO([processed output])\n    IN --> BL[2 · blend operator]\n    PO --> BL\n    BL --> BO([blended output])\n    MK[3 · mask:<br/>per-pixel opacity] --> MIX\n    IN --> MIX[4 · mix by mask]\n    BO --> MIX\n    MIX --> FO([final output → next module])\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/the-anatomy-of-a-module.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. Receive the _module input_ from the last executed module and perform an _operation_ on it to produce the _processed output_. This _operation_ is different for every [module](../modules/_index.md).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/the-anatomy-of-a-module.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "2. Combine the _module input_ and _processed output_ using a [blending operator](../masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md) to produce the _blended output_. If no blending is performed, the output of this step is the same as the _processed output_.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/the-anatomy-of-a-module.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "3. Generate a _mask_, which defines an _opacity_ for each pixel in the image. The _opacity_ is later used to control how strongly the module's operation is applied to each part of the image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/the-anatomy-of-a-module.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "   You may define your own mask by drawing shapes over the image or by using pixel properties from the _module input_ or _processed output_ (see [masks](../masking-and-blending/masks/_index.md) for details). This mask may be further modified with a global opacity setting, which affects every pixel equally.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/the-anatomy-of-a-module.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "   If no drawn/parametric mask is used, the output of this step is a mask where every pixel has the same opacity (governed by the global opacity setting). If no opacity is defined (no blending is performed) a global opacity of 1.0 (or 100%) is assumed.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/the-anatomy-of-a-module.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "4. Combine the _module input_ and _blended output_ pixel-by-pixel using the _mask_ as a mixing operator, to produce the _final output_. Writing $o$ for the effective per-pixel opacity (the global opacity multiplied by the mask value at that pixel), the final output of each pixel is:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (math)
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/the-anatomy-of-a-module.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "   \\text{final} = (1 - o)\\,\\text{input} + o\\,\\text{blended}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/the-anatomy-of-a-module.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "   Where the mask opacity is 100% ($o = 1$), the _final output_ is the _blended output_ for that pixel; where the mask opacity is 0 ($o = 0$), it is the _module input_; an intermediate opacity combines the two proportionally. The _final output_ is passed to the next module for further processing.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/the-anatomy-of-a-module.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Steps 2 and 3 are optional and not supported by all modules. For example, the [demosaic](../modules/demosaic.md) module must be applied to the entire raw file in order to produce a legible image so it does not make sense to mask or blend its output.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/the-anatomy-of-a-module.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Each of the above steps is defined in more detail in subsequent sections.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/history-stack.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "The history stack"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/history-stack.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The _history stack_ stores the entire editing history for a given image, in the order in which those edits were applied. It is saved to Ansel's library database and the image's XMP sidecar file and persists between editing sessions.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/history-stack.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Each time a processing module is enabled, disabled, moved or amended a new entry is added to the top of the _history stack_.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/history-stack.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The history stack can be queried and modified within the [history stack](../../toolboxes/history-stack.md) module in the darkroom.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/history-stack.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Note:** The history stack is not a representation of the order in which the modules are **executed** but the order in which they were **amended**. The execution order is represented by the order of the modules in the right-hand panel.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/undo-redo.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Undo and redo"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/pixelpipe/undo-redo.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "While you are editing your image, Ansel records all of the modifications you make to that image. This means that it is possible to undo and redo changes to recover a previous editing state. Note that the undo/redo facility is unlimited in the number of steps while editing an image, but is reset each time the darkroom is switched to a new image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Array Element: aliases
#: content/views/darkroom/_index.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "../darkroom"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The darkroom view is where you develop a single image. The center area shows the picture currently being edited; the [left panel](darkroom-view-layout.md#left-panel) holds peripheral tools (navigation, scopes, snapshots, history…), and the [right panel](darkroom-view-layout.md#right-panel) holds the image-processing modules.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Open the darkroom from the [lighttable](../lighttable/_index.md) by double-clicking a thumbnail, or by selecting an image and pressing <kbd>Enter</kbd>. Return to the lighttable with <kbd>Escape</kbd> (or the home button in the header). You can switch to another image without leaving the darkroom by enabling the [filmstrip](../toolboxes/filmstrip.md) (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>F</kbd>) and clicking a thumbnail in it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "How an image is processed is the subject of the [pixelpipe](pixelpipe/_index.md) and [modules](modules/_index.md) sections; this page and the [darkroom layout](darkroom-view-layout.md) page cover the view itself.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Zoom and pan"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Middle-click the center area to cycle between **fit to screen**, **1:1** and **2:1**.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Scroll with the mouse wheel to zoom between fit-to-screen and 1:1. Hold <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> while scrolling to extend the range from 2:1 up to 1:10. When zoomed in past the window, drag the image to pan.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Working with modules"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The image-processing modules in the right panel are organized into [workflow tabs](darkroom-view-layout.md#module-workflow-tabs) that follow the order of the pixelpipe. The recommended way to edit is to move through the tabs from left to right, and through each module stack from bottom to top.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Modules and their controls are fully navigable from the keyboard, and any module or control can be reached directly through the [global action search](../../getting-started/keyboard.md#vimkey-like-global-action-search) (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>P</kbd>) or an [assigned shortcut](../../getting-started/keyboard.md#darkroom). The [anatomy of a module](pixelpipe/the-anatomy-of-a-module.md) page explains the common module controls (enable, reset, presets, multiple instances, masking & blending).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Autoset: auto-developing an image"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Some modules can compute their own settings from the content of the image instead of relying on a fixed default. The **autoset** button in the [bottom toolbar](darkroom-view-layout.md#bottom-panel) runs this automatic computation on several modules at once, giving you a sensible starting point that you then refine by hand.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Right-click** the autoset button to open a list of the modules that support it, and tick the ones you want it to act on. Capable modules include:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- [Raw black/white point](modules/raw-black-white-point.md) and [exposure](modules/exposure.md) — set the working range and overall brightness;\n- [Highlight reconstruction](modules/highlight-reconstruction.md) — adapt to the clipped channels;\n- [Color calibration](modules/color-calibration.md) — automatic white balance / illuminant detection;\n- [Filmic RGB](modules/filmic.md) — fit the tone mapping to the image's dynamic range;\n- [Tone equalizer](modules/tone-equalizer.md), [color balance RGB](modules/color-balance.md), [color equalizer](modules/color-equalizer.md), [color primaries](modules/color-primaries.md) and [denoise (profiled)](modules/denoise-profiled.md).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (mermaid)
#: content/views/darkroom/_index.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "flowchart LR\n    A([Click autoset]) --> B{For each<br/>ticked module}\n    B --> C[Module analyzes<br/>the image]\n    C --> D[Module writes<br/>its own settings]\n    D --> E([Refine by hand])\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Autoset runs in the background and processes the modules one after another (the button shows a busy state while it works). Each module's result is recorded in the [history](pixelpipe/history-stack.md) like any other change, so you can undo it or tweak it afterwards.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "On-image overlays and assessment"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The [bottom toolbar](darkroom-view-layout.md#bottom-panel) gives quick access to the visual assessment overlays: ISO 12646 [color assessment](../toolboxes/color-assessment.md), [raw overexposed](../toolboxes/raw-overexposed.md) and [clipping](../toolboxes/clipping.md) warnings, [soft-proofing](../toolboxes/soft-proof.md), [gamut checking](../toolboxes/gamut.md), and [guides & overlays](../toolboxes/guides-overlays.md). The quick-access [styles](../toolboxes/styles.md) menu is also there.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/watermark.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Watermark"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/watermark.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Render a vector-based overlay onto your image. Watermarks are standard SVG documents and can be designed using [Inkscape](http://www.inkscape.org/).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/watermark.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "You can also use bitmap (PNG) images.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/watermark.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The SVG processor in Ansel can also substitute strings within an SVG document, allowing image-dependent information to be included in the watermark.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/watermark.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "User-designed watermarks should be placed into the directory `$HOME/.config/Ansel/watermarks`. Once in place, use the reload button update the list of available watermarks.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/watermark.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The following is a list of variable strings that are supported for substitution within an SVG document.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/watermark.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In addition to this list you can also use the variable strings defined in the [variables](../../..//variables.md) section.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/watermark.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "$(WATERMARK_TEXT)             A short free text (max. 63 characters)\n$(WATERMARK_COLOR)            The color to use for $WATERMARK_TEXT\n$(WATERMARK_FONT_FAMILY)      The font family to use for $WATERMARK_TEXT\n$(WATERMARK_FONT_STYLE)       The font style (normal, oblique, italic)\n$(WATERMARK_FONT_WEIGHT)      The font weight (boldness)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/watermark.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "marker\n: Choose the watermark to apply. Use the reload button to update the list to include any newly-added watermarks. The extension of the file (png/svg) is shown in brackets.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/watermark.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "text\n: A free text field in which you can enter up to 63 characters to be printed where referenced by the corresponding watermark. An example is supplied as `simple-text.svg`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/watermark.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "text color\n: The color of the text. Click on the colored field to open a color selector dialog which offers you a choice of commonly used colors, or allows you to define a color in RGB color space.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/watermark.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "text font\n: The font to use (default \"DejaVu Sans Book\"). Click on the field to open a dialog box showing the fonts available on your system. Fonts can be searched by name and a preview is shown next to the font name. You may specify your own sample text.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/watermark.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "opacity\n: The opacity of the watermark's rendering.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/watermark.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "scale\n: The scale of the watermark, with respect to the option selected in the \"scale on\" parameter.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/watermark.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "rotate\n: The rotation angle of the watermark.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/watermark.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "scale on\n: The reference for the scale parameter. The default setting “image” scales the watermark relative to the horizontal image size. Alternatively you can scale the watermark relative to the “larger border” or “smaller border”.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/watermark.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "alignment\n: Use these controls to align the watermark to any edge or the center of the image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/watermark.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "x offset\n: Pixel-independent offset relative to the choice of alignment on the x-axis.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/watermark.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "y offset\n: Pixel-independent offset relative to the choice of alignment on the y-axis.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/sharpen.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Sharpen"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/sharpen.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Sharpen the details in the image using a standard UnSharp Mask (USM).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/sharpen.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This module works by increasing the contrast around edges and thereby enhancing the _impression_ of sharpness of an image. This module is applied to the L channel in Lab color space.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/sharpen.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "_**Note:** The USM algorithm used in this module performs blurs in Lab color space, which can produce undesirable effects, and is no longer recommended. Instead use the presets offered by the [contrast equalizer](./contrast-equalizer.md) module for deblurring or the [local contrast](./local-contrast.md) module for general sharpness._\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/sharpen.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "radius\n: The unsharp mask applies a gaussian blur to the image as part of its algorithm. This parameter controls the radius of that blur which, in turn, defines the spatial extent of the edge enhancement. Very high values will lead to ugly over-sharpening.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/sharpen.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "amount\n: The strength of the sharpening.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/sharpen.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "threshold\n: Contrast differences below this threshold are excluded from sharpening. Use this to avoid amplification of noise.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Array Element: aliases
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/_index.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "../../modules/processing-modules"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/highlight-reconstruction.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Highlight reconstruction"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/highlight-reconstruction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Attempt to reconstruct color information for pixels that are clipped in one or more RGB channel.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/highlight-reconstruction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If these pixels are left partially clipped it can result in unrealistic colors appearing in the image. For example pixels with both green and blue channels clipped will appear to be red without any highlight reconstruction.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/highlight-reconstruction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Three methods of reconstruction are available:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/highlight-reconstruction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "clip highlights\n: Clamp all pixels to the white level (i.e. clip the remaining color channels). This method is most useful in cases where clipped highlights occur in naturally desaturated objects (e.g. clouds).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/highlight-reconstruction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "reconstruct in LCh\n: Analyse each pixel with at least one clipped channel and attempt to correct the clipped pixel (in LCh color space) using the values of the other (3 for Bayer or 8 for X-Trans) pixels in the affected sensor block. The reconstructed highlights will still be monochrome, but brighter and with more detail than with “clip highlights”. This method works fairly well with a high-contrast base curve, which renders highlights desaturated. As with _clip highlights_ this method is a good option for naturally desaturated objects.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/highlight-reconstruction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "reconstruct color\n: Use an algorithm that transfers color information from unclipped surroundings into the clipped highlights. This method works very well on areas with homogeneous colors and is especially useful on skin tones with smoothly fading highlights. Please note that this method can produce maze-like artifacts on highlights behind high-contrast edges, for example well-exposed fine structures in front of an overexposed background.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/highlight-reconstruction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "guided laplacians\n: Use an algorithm (derived from the [_diffuse or sharpen_](./diffuse.md) module) to replicate details from valid channels into clipped channels and to propagate color gradients from valid surrounding regions into clipped regions. This is a slow and computationally-intensive method designed for maximum smoothness and seamless blending of the reconstructed regions into their neighborhood, and is designed primarily to reconstruct spotlights and specular reflections. This mode is available for Bayer sensors only.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/highlight-reconstruction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Note:** When using the highlight reconstruction included with the [_filmic_](./filmic.md) module it may be useful to avoid using this module in _clip highlights_ mode (so that _filmic_ has more information to work with).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/highlight-reconstruction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "method\n: The method used to reconstruct highlights (see above).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/highlight-reconstruction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "clipping threshold\n: Manually adjust the clipping threshold against magenta highlights. You shouldn't need to adjust this.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/highlight-reconstruction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": Click the icon beside the slider to visualise what areas of the image are considered clipped (the clipping mask). If the clipping mask does not match the [RAW over-exposed warning](../../toolboxes/raw-overexposed.md), you may need to correct this value.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/highlight-reconstruction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "noise level (_guided laplacians_ mode only)\n: Add Poisson noise (natural photon noise such as you would find in sensor readings) to the clipped regions. For high-ISO images, the valid regions of the image will be noisy, but the reconstructed clipped areas will be smooth, which may look odd. Adding some noise in the reconstruction helps to visually blend the result with the rest of the image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/highlight-reconstruction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "iterations (_guided laplacians_ mode only)\n: The _guided laplacians_ mode is an iterative process that extrapolates gradients and details from the neighborhood. Each new iteration refines the previous reconstruction but adds more computations that will make the module slower. The default (1 iteration) will typically not be sufficient to completely recover magenta highlights, so you will need to increase this parameter gradually but carefully, to manage the speed/quality trade-off.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/highlight-reconstruction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "inpaint a flat color (_guided laplacians_ mode only)\n: Inpainting a flat color is an algorithmic trick that may help recover magenta highlights in difficult cases (large blown areas) by smoothing RGB ratios. It can be seen as a \"reconstruction booster\" that may reduce the number of iterations required to entirely remove magenta in clipped highlights. However, this also makes the reconstruction less accurate and can lead to non-smooth reconstructed edges and unrelated colors being inpainted (e.g. blue sky or green leaves bleeding into white clouds). Use this setting with caution.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/highlight-reconstruction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "diameter of the reconstruction (_guided laplacians_ mode only)\n: The _guided laplacians_ mode uses a multi-scale algorithm that tries to recover details from each scale independently. The _diameter of the reconstruction_ is the largest scale used by the algorithm. Large scales will increase memory consumption as well as runtimes, and may also cause unrelated colors or details to be inpainted in clipped regions. You are advised to use a diameter roughly twice as large as the largest clipped area to be reconstructed. It is also possible that a given diameter may not suit all clipped areas, in which case you should use several instances at different scales and mask the clipped areas accordingly.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/highlight-reconstruction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Comparison with filmic's highlight reconstruction"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/highlight-reconstruction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "It is important to note that the _highlight reconstruction_ module is quite early in the pixel pipeline -- before [_input color profile_](./input-color-profile.md) and the full chromatic adaptation in [_color calibration_](./color-calibration.md) (if you use the _modern chromatic adaptation_ workflow). A common trick to solve clipped highlights is to simply desaturate them to white but, because white is not defined before the full chromatic adaptation and the input color profiling, it is not possible to use this trick here. Technically, there is no color yet at this point in the pipeline, only an arbitrary 3D signal.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/highlight-reconstruction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The _guided laplacians_ approach has been designed specifically to be immune to white-balance discrepancies and to avoid any concept or method related to color (so there is no explicit desaturation). It only handles gradients (transitions) in the signal and aims at connecting them smoothly, in order to fill the missing parts. This process is quite heavy though, since it falls into the category of supervised machine learning (gradient-based optimization through multi-scale curvature), which is a sub-branch of artificial intelligence.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/highlight-reconstruction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "[_Filmic's highlight reconstruction_](./filmic.md#reconstruct) uses a simpler color propagation algorithm coupled with a desaturation option that can favor an _achromatic_ reconstruction. Not only does it _know_ about color (because it comes after the full color profiling and chromatic adaptation) but it also uses a simplified and faster version of the algorithm used by the _guided laplacians_ approach. Namely, this variant will not try as hard to restore details and will favor a smooth blur instead.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/highlight-reconstruction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The filmic reconstruction is good enough for very large clipped patches and offers the benefit of being able to degrade to white as a last resort. It is also better and faster to inpaint solid color into clipped areas, at the expense of details. Its main drawback is that it is not as selective in the source of the colors being inpainted in clipped parts, so it may inpaint unrelated colors.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/highlight-reconstruction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "All in all, you are advised to use the _guided laplacians_ highlight reconstruction mode to:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/highlight-reconstruction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. Smooth the boundaries of clipped areas,\n2. Recover spotlights and clipped areas of diameter below approximately 256px (on the full-resolution RAW),\n3. Remove chromatic aberrations, which can occur during [demosaicing](./demosaic.md) (the next module in the pipeline) at the boundary between clipped and valid regions.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/highlight-reconstruction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If you find yourself having to increase the _diameter of reconstruction_ past 512px to get a full recovery from magenta, cap the diameter to 512px, do the most you can with this setting, and then **add a second instance of this module** to finish the work, masked to the area that still needs it. This gives more bearable run-times with a very similar result.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/highlight-reconstruction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Older versions of this page advised enabling filmic's highlight reconstruction at that point instead. That is no longer recommended: [filmic's reconstruction is deprecated](./filmic.md#reconstruct) and disabled for new edits, since this module now covers X-Trans and already-demosaiced input too, and its default _harmonic transposition_ method is more accurate than filmic's first-order color propagation. The current recommendation is to stay in this module: keep _harmonic transposition_ as the base, and stack a _guided laplacians_ instance on top of it if harmonic transposition alone is not enough.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/white-balance.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "White balance"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/white-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Adjust the white balance of the image by altering the temperature and tint, defining a coefficient for each RGB channel, or choosing from list of predefined white balance settings.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/white-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The default settings for this module are derived from the camera white balance stored in the image's Exif data.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/white-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "White balance is not intended as a \"creative\" module -- its primary goal is to technically correct the white balance of the image ensuring that neutral colored objects in the scene are rendered with neutral colors in the image. For creative color operations, it is usually better to use other modules such as [_color calibration_](./color-calibration.md) or [_color balance_](./color-balance.md).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/white-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Note:** The [_color calibration_](./color-calibration.md) module now provides a more modern and flexible method of controlling white balance. The _color calibration_ module can be enabled by default for new images by selecting \"modern\" in [preferences > processing > auto-apply chromatic adaptation defaults](../../../preferences-settings/processing.md). Some basic settings are still required (and applied automatically) in the _white balance_ module, so that [_demosaic_](./demosaic.md) works correctly.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/white-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Scene illuminant temp"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/white-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This section provides scene-illuminant _temperature_ and _tint_ controls to adjust the white balace of the image. Click on the 'scene illuminant temp' section label to cycle between 3 different [color modes](#colored-sliders) for the temperature/tint sliders.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/white-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "temperature\n: Set the color temperature in kelvin.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/white-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "tint\n: Alter the color tint of the image, from magenta (tint < 1) to green (tint > 1).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/white-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "White balance presets"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/white-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "setting\n: Choose from a predetermined list of white balances. The available settings are derived from the presets available in the camera used to take the photograph. The following options are provided in addition to any camera-defined white balance presets.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/white-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": - _as shot_ (default): The white balance as reported by the camera\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/white-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": - _from image area_: Draw a rectangle over a neutral color in the image to calculate white balance from that area.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/white-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": - _user modified_: The most recently modified setting. Manual adjustment of temperature, tint or r/g/b channel coefficients will automatically select this option. Choose this setting after selecting any other preset to return parameters to the most recent user-modified state\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/white-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": - _camera reference_: Set the temperature to the camera reference white point, which is assumed to be D65 (or ~6502K). The white balance channel multipliers are calculated such that pure white in the camera colorspace is converted into pure white in sRGB D65 (where pure white means that each color channel has an equal value).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/white-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "For convenience the final four modes can also be set by clicking on one of the buttons in the [button bar](#button-bar) above the _setting_ drop-down.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/white-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "finetune\n: Finetune a camera-specific white balance preset. This is only shown if it is available for the camera in question. The direction of adjustment is dependent on the provided presets. If your camera doesn't have white balance presets available, check [this guide](https://github.com/darktable-org/darktable/wiki/White-balance-presets) to see how you can submit your own.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/white-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Channel coefficients"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/white-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The RGB channel coefficients are automatically calculated from the above parameters and, as such, are hidden by default. You can expand/collapse the channel coefficients section by clicking on either the 'channel coefficients' label or the adjacent triangular button.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/white-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "red/green/blue\n: Set the value of each RGB channel coefficient from 0 to 8\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/white-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Additional functionality"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/white-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Colored sliders"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/white-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "By default the module's sliders are monochrome. However, two flavors of colored sliders can be enabled in [preferences > darkroom > white balance slider colors](../../../preferences-settings/darkroom.md) or by clicking on the 'scene illuminant temp' section label in the module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/white-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "no color (default)\n: The background of the sliders is not colored.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/white-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "illuminant color\n: The slider colors represent the color of the light source (the color you are adjusting to in order to achieve neutral white).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/white-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "effect emulation\n: The slider colors represent the effect the adjustment would have had on the scene. This is how most other raw processors show temperature/tint sliders colors.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/white-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Button bar"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/white-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The button bar is simple addition that allows one-click access to the internal white balance settings. You can disable this by editing your `Anselrc` file. Find the line that says\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/white-balance.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "plugins/darkroom/temperature/button_bar=TRUE\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/white-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "and change `TRUE` to `FALSE`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/white-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Usage warning"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/white-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The only parameters that are used internally by this module's operation are the _rgb channel coefficients_. The _temperature_ and _tint_ sliders are provided as a more user-friendly way to adjust those parameters. The relationship between the channel coefficients and temperature/tint sliders depends on characteristics specific to the camera used to take the photograph. This means that applying white balance settings from an image made with one camera to an image made with another will, in general, not give consistent results.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/white-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The mathematical relationship between the two sets of values is not straightforward. It is possible to set the channel coefficients such that there is no valid equivalent temperature and tint setting (mainly where very high temperature values are calculated from the sliders). Editing white balance using temperature and tint on an image previously edited using channel coefficients may therefore give odd results, at least where high temperature values are involved.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/astrophoto-denoise.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Astrophoto denoise"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/astrophoto-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Remove image noise while preserving structure.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/astrophoto-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This is accomplished by averaging each pixel with some surrounding pixels in the image. The weight of such a pixel in the averaging process depends on the similarity of its neighborhood with the neighborhood of the pixel being denoised. A patch with a defined size is used to measure that similarity.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/astrophoto-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "As denoising is a resource-intensive process, it slows down pixelpipe processing significantly. Consider activating this module late in your workflow.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/astrophoto-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "patch size\n: The radius of the patch used for similarity evaluation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/astrophoto-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "strength\n: The strength of the denoising.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/astrophoto-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "luma\n: The amount of denoising to apply to luma. Select carefully in order not to lose too much structure.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/astrophoto-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "chroma\n: The amount of denoising to apply to chroma. You can be much more aggressive with this parameter.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/negadoctor.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Negadoctor"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/negadoctor.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Process scanned film negatives.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/negadoctor.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "You can obtain an image of a negative using a film scanner, or by photographing it against a white light (e.g. a light table or computer monitor) or off-camera flash.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/negadoctor.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Preparation"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/negadoctor.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If the image of the negative was obtained using a digital camera, then in order to obtain accurate colors in the final image, you will need to take the following points into consideration:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/negadoctor.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- When taking the photograph, adjust the exposure to fully utilise the entire dynamic range of your camera sensor -- i.e. \"expose-to-the-right\", so that the histogram in your camera touches the right hand side without clipping the image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/negadoctor.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Ensure the white balance is correctly set up to compensate for the light source used to illuminate the negative. You can take a profiling picture of the light source with no film negative in front of it, and then use the \"from image area\" feature in the [_white balance_](./white-balance.md) module to obtain a reference white-balance setting. This reference white balance setting can then be made into a style or simply pasted onto the images taken from your film negatives.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/negadoctor.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Apply the standard or enhanced camera matrix in the [_input color profile_](./input-color-profile.md) module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/negadoctor.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When scanning or photographing your film negative, make sure you include some unexposed part of the film within the captured image. This is required to set the _Dmin_ parameter (see below). If this is not possible (e.g. your film holder completely obscures the unexposed parts of the film), you can take a separate image of an unexposed part of the film, measure the _Dmin_ parameter from that image, and then paste that setting to the rest of the images from that film.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/negadoctor.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When developing the scanned/photographed film negatives, it is recommended that you disable any tone mapping modules such as [_filmic_](filmic.md).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/negadoctor.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The _working profile_ parameter in Ansel's [_input color profile_](input-color-profile.md) module should be set to either _linear Rec2020 RGB_, or to an ICC profile representing the actual color space of your film emulsion. Some examples of such ICC profiles may be found in the following forum posts:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/negadoctor.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- [https://discuss.pixls.us/t/any-interest-in-a-film-negative-feature-in-rt/12569/177](https://discuss.pixls.us/t/any-interest-in-a-film-negative-feature-in-rt/12569/177)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/negadoctor.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- [https://discuss.pixls.us/t/input-color-profile-to-use-for-negatives/20271/13](https://discuss.pixls.us/t/input-color-profile-to-use-for-negatives/20271/13)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/negadoctor.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Note:** if you want to use the [_tone equalizer_](tone-equalizer.md) with _negadoctor_, you'll need to move the _tone equalizer_ module **after** _negadoctor_ in the [pixelpipe](..//pixelpipe/the-pixelpipe-and-module-order.md), since the _tone equalizer_ is not designed to work with negatives.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/negadoctor.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "It is strongly recommended that you set the parameters following the order in which they are presented in the GUI. Start by setting the _film stock_, then work through each of the tabs ([_film properties_](#film-properties), [_corrections_](#corrections), [_print properties_](#print-properties)) in order, working from top to bottom in each tab.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/negadoctor.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When using color pickers, be careful to avoid including dust and scratches, which can skew the data taken from the sampled region.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/negadoctor.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "film stock\n: The first step is to choose \"color\" or \"black and white\" in the _film stock_ drop-down. If you select \"black and white\", any sliders that are only used for color will be hidden from view.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/negadoctor.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Film properties"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/negadoctor.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This tab contains a number of basic settings. If, after adjusting these settings, your image is still not quite as you would like it, you can make further adjustments on the [_corrections_](#corrections) tab. These are technical settings, and serve a similar purpose to the _scene_ tab in the [_filmic_](filmic.md) module, in that they adjust the black and white points and hence define the dynamic range of the negative.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/negadoctor.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "color of the film base\n: Sample an area of the base film stock from your scan. This is the area just outside of the image (an unexposed part of the film). If you are working with black and white negatives, you can leave this slider at its default value (white). If working on color film, click the color picker to the right of the color bar, which will create a bounding box covering about 98% of your image. Then, click and drag across an area of your negative which contains only unexposed film stock. This will automatically calculate values for the _D min_ slider(s). It is likely at this point that your image will still look too dark, but you can correct this later.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/negadoctor.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "D min\n: If the _film stock_ is set to \"black and white\", this slider indicates the minimum value corresponding to the unexposed film stock. If the _film stock_ is set to \"color\", this control will consist of 3 separate sliders, one for each of the red, green and blue channels.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/negadoctor.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "D max\n: This slider represents the dynamic range of your film, and it effectively sets the film's white point. Dragging this slider to the left will make the negative brighter. Dragging it to the right will make the negative darker. When adjusting this slider manually, it's a good idea to closely watch your histogram to ensure that you don't clip the highlights (where the histogram has been pushed over too far off the right hand side of the graph). If you click the color picker icon (on the right) negadoctor will automatically calculate this value to ensure maximal use of the histogram without clipping. To use the color picker, click and drag to draw a rectangle across only the exposed parts of the negative. Don't include the unexposed film stock, as this will skew the result.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/negadoctor.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "scan exposure bias\n: This slider allows you to set the black point. It is a technical adjustment that ensures a proper zeroing of the RGB values and a spreading of the histogram between [0, 1] values for robustness in the operations that follow. Dragging this to the left will make the negative brighter. Dragging to the right will make the negative darker. When adjusting this slider manually, it's a good idea to closely watch your histogram to ensure that you don't clip the shadows (where the histogram is pushed too far off the left hand side of the graph). If you click the color picker negadoctor will automatically calculate any required offset. To use the color picker, select a region in the darkest lowlights, or select the entire image without including any unexposed film stock. Double-check the histogram to ensure the left part of it doesn't clip.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/negadoctor.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Corrections"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/negadoctor.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This tab contains sliders that allow you to make color cast corrections within both the shadow and highlight regions.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/negadoctor.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The settings on this tab should not be needed for most well-preserved negatives. It is mostly useful for old and poorly-preserved negatives with a decayed film base that introduces undesirable color casts. Note that the shadows color cast setting will have no effect if the _scan exposure bias_ setting in the [_film properties_](#film-properties) tab is set to a non-zero value.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/negadoctor.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The other case where these color cast corrections may be needed is if the white balance properties of the light used to scan the film negative are significantly different to the light source under which the original film camera took the shot. For example, if you illuminate the film with an LED light, but the original shot was taken under daylight, this may require some additional color cast corrections.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/negadoctor.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "shadows color cast\n: These three sliders allow you to correct for color casts in the shadows by adjusting the _red_, _green_ and _blue_ channels individually. Use the color picker to set the sliders automatically by selecting a neutral gray shadow region requiring correction. Because the shadows sliders can also affect color casts in the highlights, it is important to finish setting the shadows sliders before moving on to the highlights sliders.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/negadoctor.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "highlights white balance\n: These three sliders allow you to correct the white balance in the highlights by adjusting the _red_, _green_ and _blue_ channels individually. Use the color picker to set the sliders automatically by selecting a neutral gray region in the highlights that is not properly balanced.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/negadoctor.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Print properties"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/negadoctor.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This tab contains settings that mimic the tonal effect of the photochemical papers that would have been used to create the hard copy image if you were not developing the photo digitally. These are creative settings, and serve a similar overall purpose to the creative tone curve settings on the _look_ tab of the [_filmic_](filmic.md) module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/negadoctor.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The _print exposure_, _paper black_ and _paper grade_ are analogous to the _slope_, _offset_ and _power_ controls in the [_color balance_](color-balance.md) module (which in turn is based on the ASC CDL standard). These settings define a creative tone curve to enforce your contrast intent after the inversion, at the end of the module. The equation governing this slope/offset/power behaviour is:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/negadoctor.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "_RGB_out_ = ( _RGB_in_ × _exposure_ + _black_ ) ᵍʳᵃᵈᵉ\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/negadoctor.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "paper black (density correction)\n: For this slider, select the color picker and click and drag to select a region that encompasses only the exposed part of the film negative. If you can see unexposed film stock around the edges of your image, ensure that these areas are excluded from the drawn rectangle when calculating the _paper black_ setting. Paper black represents the density of the blackest silver-halide crystal available on the virtual paper. In the analog development process, this black density always results in non-zero luminance, but the digital pipeline usually expects black to be encoded with a zero RGB value. This slider setting lets you remap paper black to pipeline black via an offset. You can use the color picker to select a region of the image that should be mapped to black in the final image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/negadoctor.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "paper grade (gamma)\n: This slider is your gamma (contrast) control, and it defaults to a value of 4. If all has gone well, this value (4) minus the value of D max (from the “film properties” tab) should normally leave you with a value between 2 and 3.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/negadoctor.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "paper gloss (specular highlights)\n: This slider is essentially a highlights compression tool. As you drag this slider to the left, you will see in the histogram that the highlight values are being compressed (pushed to the left). Adjust this accordingly, so that your highlights are not clipped in the histogram. You can also use this to simulate a matte photo print with low-contrast highlights.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/negadoctor.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "print exposure adjustment\n: This slider offers one final opportunity to correct any clipping of the highlights. If you have followed all the previous instructions carefully, you shouldn't need to adjust this setting. Note that you can increase the print exposure while at the same time decreasing the paper gloss, which allows you to brighten the mid-tones without losing any highlights. You can use the color picker to select the brightest highlights, or select the entire image without including any unexposed film stock. This will set the exposure so that the brightest part of the selected region is not clipped. Double-check the histogram to make sure that the right part of the histogram doesn't clip.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Split-toning"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Blend two CAT16 plus RGB mixer corrections across brightness keyframes.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The _split-toning_ module applies two independent color transforms:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- one keyed on the dark tones,\n- one keyed on the bright tones.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Each keyframe combines:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- a chromatic adaptation defined by a correlated color temperature in _CAT16_,\n- a 3×3 RGB channel mixer working in the pipeline working RGB space.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The two transforms are then interpolated according to the scene luminance of each pixel. This makes the module suitable both for creative split toning and for technical correction of mixed illuminants, for example cool bounce light in the shadows combined with a warmer direct illuminant in the highlights.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Scene-referred placement"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The module is designed to run in linear scene-referred RGB and is inserted before [_color primaries_](./color-primaries.md) in the pixelpipe.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The keying metric is the scene luminance \\(Y\\) derived from the current working RGB profile. This means the transition between the two keyframes is driven by brightness rather than by hue.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "General principles"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The user interface contains two tabs:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- _dark_ (shown as _shadows_),\n- _bright_ (shown as _highlights_).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Each tab defines one brightness keyframe in EV. By default:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- the dark keyframe sits at `-16 EV`,\n- the bright keyframe sits at `0 EV`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "For each keyframe, the module first builds a full 3×3 transform:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. a CAT16 chromatic adaptation derived from the selected temperature,\n2. followed by the selected RGB mixer matrix.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The full transform is then blended over brightness as follows:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- below the dark keyframe, the dark transform fades linearly from identity up to full strength,\n- between dark and bright, the module linearly interpolates from the dark transform to the bright transform,\n- above the bright keyframe, the bright transform fades linearly back to identity.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The fade-out distance outside the `[dark ; bright]` segment is equal to the distance between the two keyframes.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This is important because the module does not blend colors after correction. It blends the full transform matrix itself, which generally gives more natural transitions than mixing already-corrected RGB values.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Preview"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "At the top of the module, a preview strip shows a linear gray gradient processed through the current split-toning settings.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This preview is normalized around the current keyframes so you can read at a glance:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- where the dark transform starts to appear,\n- where the interpolation happens,\n- where the bright transform fades out.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The preview colors are projected through the current display profile, so they reflect the actual monitor rendering rather than raw working-space RGB.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Each tab contains three sections:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- a brightness keyframe,\n- a CAT16 temperature,\n- one of four RGB mixer GUI modes.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Brightness"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "brightness\n: Set the brightness keyframe in EV for the current tab.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": The slider includes an area picker. Sample any region in the image and the module will measure the average luminance of that area, convert it to scene \\(Y\\), then map it to EV to place the keyframe automatically.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": Use this to anchor the _dark_ keyframe on a representative shadow or the _bright_ keyframe on a representative highlight.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Temperature"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "temperature\n: Set the color temperature of the illuminant assumed for the current keyframe.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": Internally, this builds a CAT16 chromatic adaptation in the current working RGB space. Lower temperatures bias the correction toward warmer light assumptions, higher temperatures toward cooler ones.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": Because the temperature is only one part of the full transform, it can be used on its own for illuminant correction or combined with the channel mixer for more stylized color remapping.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Mode"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "mode\n: Choose how the RGB mixer matrix is exposed in the GUI.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": The following modes are available:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- _Complete_,\n- _Simple_,\n- _Primaries_,\n- _White-preserving_.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "These are only different parameterizations of the same backend 3×3 mixer. Switching mode does not select a different processing algorithm. It only changes how the matrix is edited.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A 3×3 matrix has 9 coefficients, so a mode exposing 9 controls reaches every mixer, and a mode exposing 6 describes a restricted family. The 3 coefficients a 6-control mode does not expose are not dropped, they are solved to keep something invariant -- which is the property you are really choosing between:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< table >}}\n| mode | controls | what it holds invariant | available when |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| _Complete_ | 9 | nothing | always |\n| _Simple_ | 6 | the neutral axis | the 3 output rows are normalized and their sums are non-zero |\n| _Primaries_ | 9 | nothing | the matrix is non-singular and every column has a non-zero sum |\n| _White-preserving_ | 6 | the neutral axis | the matrix already leaves the neutral unchanged |\n{{< /table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Unlike [_color calibration_](./color-calibration.md), this module always mixes in the pipeline working RGB, never in a CAT space -- the CAT16 adaptation here is a separate step applied before the mixer, not the space the mixer runs in. The neutral is therefore always `(1,1,1)`, and _Simple_ and _White-preserving_ turn out to describe __exactly the same family of matrices__, just through different controls. Choose between them by which set of sliders matches the correction you have in mind, not by what they can reach.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When the current matrix does not satisfy a mode's constraint, that mode is refused and the GUI falls back to _Complete_, rather than showing controls that do not describe the matrix actually in use.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Complete mode"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "_Complete_ is the unconstrained 3×3 mixer representation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "For each output row:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- _output red_,\n- _output green_,\n- _output blue_,\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "you can set the contribution of:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- _input R_,\n- _input G_,\n- _input B_.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Each row also provides a _normalize_ checkbox. When enabled, that row is normalized by the sum of its coefficients.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This is the most general representation and is always available.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Simple mode"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "_Simple_ is an exact geometric representation of a restricted subset of normalized mixer matrices. It exposes 6 parameters:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "global hue rotation\n: A rigid rotation of the normalized chroma plane.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "chroma (u,v) axes orientation\n: The orientation of the principal axes used to express the chroma transform.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "u stretch and v stretch\n: Signed gains along the two chroma axes. A value of `1` is neutral. `0` collapses the selected axis to achromatic.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "achromatic coupling amount and achromatic coupling hue\n: Couple a chroma direction into the achromatic axis while preserving the black-white axis.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This mode is exact only when all three output rows are normalized and have non-zero sums. If the current matrix does not satisfy those constraints, the GUI falls back to _Complete_.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Primaries mode"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "_Primaries_ is another exact GUI representation, this time expressed as a generalized primaries basis in working RGB. It exposes 9 parameters:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- _white hue_,\n- _white purity_,\n- _red hue_,\n- _red purity_,\n- _green hue_,\n- _green purity_,\n- _blue hue_,\n- _blue purity_,\n- _gain_.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This mode is exact only when the current 3×3 mixer can be interpreted as a non-degenerate affine basis change with non-zero affine sums. If not, the GUI falls back to _Complete_.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "It is the only mode of the four that can tint the neutral or change the overall gain of the keyframe. Use it when that is what you want; use one of the 6-control modes when it is not.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "White-preserving mode"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "_White-preserving_ is the primaries picture with the white nailed down. It exposes 6 parameters, 2 per primary:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "red, green and blue rotation\n: Turn that primary around the neutral in the chromaticity plane. This is a per-primary hue rotation: you can swing the reds of a keyframe without touching its blues, which the single global rotation of _Simple_ cannot do.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "red, green and blue saturation\n: Scale the distance between that primary and the neutral. `0%` leaves it alone, `-100%` collapses it onto the neutral, `+100%` doubles its distance.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "All six controls at `0` is the identity matrix. Setting the three _saturation_ sliders together to the same value is a plain saturation control for that keyframe: `+100%` doubles the colorfulness of the keyed tones, and moving them down together desaturates them. A single primary can be taken all the way down to `-100%`, but all three at once stop at about `-86%`: a keyframe with every primary sitting on the neutral maps everything to one color and has no transform left to describe. Past that limit the sliders snap back to the last usable setting.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The property the mode guarantees is that the neutral of the keyframe is left exactly where it is, no matter what the six sliders do. The 3 column magnitudes are solved from that requirement rather than exposed, which is what lets 6 controls describe every neutral-preserving mixer and nothing else. In a split-toning context this gives a clean division of labour: the CAT16 _temperature_ is what tints the neutral of a keyframe, and the mixer then reshapes the colors around that neutral without moving it. If you want the split to come from the temperature alone, this mode guarantees the mixer will not add a cast of its own on top.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This mode requires the current matrix to leave the neutral unchanged, every column to have a non-zero sum, and the 3 primaries not to be collinear -- two primaries turned onto the same direction flatten the model and are refused. If those are not met, the GUI falls back to _Complete_.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Practical guidance"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This module works best when the two keyframes reflect real exposure zones in the scene.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A practical workflow is often:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. pick a representative shadow area on the _dark_ tab to set the dark brightness keyframe,\n2. pick a representative highlight area on the _bright_ tab to set the bright keyframe,\n3. use the temperature sliders first to correct mixed illuminants,\n4. refine the look with the RGB mixer of each tab,\n5. use the preview strip to check the transition region between both anchors.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "For technical correction:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- use the dark keyframe to neutralize colored bounce light in the shadows,\n- use the bright keyframe to compensate the direct illuminant in the highlights,\n- keep the mixer close to identity and rely mostly on CAT16 temperature when you want the most physically grounded result.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "For creative grading:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- place the two keyframes closer together to concentrate the transition in the midtones,\n- push them farther apart to make the blend more gradual,\n- use different mixer modes for exploration, but remember they all write the same backend matrix,\n- prefer _White-preserving_ (or _Simple_) when you want the split to come purely from the keyed blend, since neither can move the neutral of a keyframe.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If the image starts to feel synthetic, simplify the transform first:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- reduce the mixer excursions,\n- rely more on temperature,\n- widen the distance between the two brightness anchors.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/split-toning.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This module is strongest when used as a broad tonal and illuminant shaper, not as a local hue-isolation tool.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/orientation.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Orientation"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/orientation.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Rotate the image 90 degrees at a time or flip the image horizontally and/or vertically.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/orientation.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The module is enabled by default and the orientation (rotation) is automatically set based on the image's Exif data.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/orientation.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "transform\n: Double click the label to reset to the default transformations\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/orientation.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "![rotate counter-clockwise](rotate-counter-clockwise.jpg) rotate counter-clockwise\n: Rotate the image 90 degrees counter-clockwise\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/orientation.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "![rotate clockwise](rotate-clockwise.jpg) rotate clockwise\n: Rotate the image 90 degrees clockwise\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/orientation.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "![flip horizontally](flip-horizontal.jpg) flip horizontally\n: Flip the image (mirror) horizontally\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/orientation.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "![flip vertically](flip-vertical.jpg) flip vertically\n: Flip the image (mirror) vertically\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/orientation.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-perspective.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/framing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "show guides\n: Tick the box to show guide overlays whenever the module is activated. Click the icon on the right to control the properties of the guides. See [guides & overlays](../../toolboxes/guides-overlays.md) for details.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Retouch"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Remove unwanted elements from your image by cloning, healing, blurring and filling using drawn shapes.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This module includes a \"heal\" tool (based on the heal tool from GIMP), as well as \"fill\" and \"blur\" modes. It can also take advantage of wavelet decomposition, allowing the image to be separated into layers of varying detail (from coarse to fine) which can be selectively retouched before being recombined to produce the output image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Clone and heal"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Cloning allows a part of the image (the _target_) to be hidden by replacing it with an area copied from elsewhere in the image (the _source_). For example, you may wish to remove a small cloud from a blue sky:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "![retouch-original](rt-original.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The simplest way to do this is with the _cloning_ tool ![retouch-clone-icon](rt-clone-icon.jpg). The following example uses a circular shape to clone out the cloud using the circle of blue sky beside it:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "![retouch-clone](rt-clone.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In many cases, the edges of the _source_ shape won't precisely match the surroundings of the _target_, leading to unnatural looking results. In this example, the sample of sky we chose as the source was slightly darker than the target, leaving a faint outline of the circular shape used in the cloning process:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "![retouch-clone-nocontrol](rt-clone-nocontrol.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In such cases, the _heal_ tool ![retouch-heal-icon](rt-heal-icon.jpg) is more appropriate. With this tool, the color and luma of the sample is blended to fit better with the surroundings. In this example, using _heal_ instead of _clone_ produces a much more pleasing result:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "![retouch-heal-nocontrol](rt-heal-nocontrol.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Source and target shapes"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Once you have chosen _heal_ or _clone_ mode, you must choose the shape you wish to use (_circle_, _ellipse_, _path_ or _brush_ -- see the [drawn masks](../masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md) section for details). The source and target patches will both use the same shape.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When you hover over the image with your mouse, a plus symbol (+) will appear to indicate where the _source_ shape will be placed by default. Your normal mouse cursor will indicate the position of the target shape:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "![retouch-source-cross](rt-source-cross.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "It is recommended that you choose the position of the source shape first, followed by the position of the target, as follows:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Shift+click to set the position of the source shape in \"relative mode\". The position of the \"plus\" (+) symbol will move to the clicked location and will remain in a fixed position _relative to your cursor_ until you click on the image to begin placing your target shape. If you place subsequent shapes without first changing the target location, the source shape will be placed at the same offset from the target shape as was used for the first stroke.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Ctrl+Shift+click to change the position of the source shape in \"absolute\" mode. As above, the position of the \"plus\" (+) symbol will move to the clicked location, but will _remain in the same absolute position_ even if you move your mouse. You can then click on the image to begin placing the target shape. If you place subsequent shapes without first changing the target location, exactly the same source position will be used, fixed in the absolute coordinate system of the image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Once you have placed your source and target shapes on the image, they can be adjusted manually with your mouse.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Note:** For _circle_ and _ellipse_ shapes only, you can place both the source and target shapes with a single click-and-drag motion: Click on the desired target location and then drag, releasing your mouse button when you reach the desired source location. This operation does not affect the placement of subsequent shapes.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Fill and blur"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The _clone_ and _heal_ tools both require the use of another part of the image to \"fill in\" the region being hidden. Sometimes there is no suitable sample in the image to use for this purpose. In such cases, the _retouch_ module offers two further options:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "![retouch-fill-icon](rt-fill-icon.jpg) fill tool\n: Fill the drawn region with a selected color.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "![retouch-blur-icon](rt-blur-icon.jpg) blur tool\n: Apply a blur to the drawn region, smoothing out any details.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "These two options are most useful when used together with wavelet decomposition, where they can be used to smooth over features within a selected detail layer.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Wavelet decomposition"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Wavelets allow an image to be decomposed into a number of layers each containing varying levels of detail, so that you can work on each detail layer independently and then recombine them at the end. This is particularly useful in portrait photography, where you can deal with skin blotches and blemishes on a coarse layer of detail, leaving the finer skin texture untouched.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This method can be used with the healing tool, for example, to paint over a spot that appears in one of the coarse detail layers, while leaving the hairs in the fine detail layers intact:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "![retouch-beard-preserve](rt-beard-preserve.jpg#w50)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "It can also be used with the blur tool to even out coarse blotches in the skin, again without impacting the finer details.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "![retouch-overview](rt-overview.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Retouch tools"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The _retouch tools_ section consists of two items:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "shapes\n: The number after the _shapes_ label indicates how many shapes have been placed on the image, either directly or within a wavelet layer.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": Click on one of the shape icons to draw a new shape on the image (see [drawn masks](../masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md) for details).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": Ctrl+click on a shape icon to draw multiple shapes continuously (right-click to cancel).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": Click the _show and edit shapes_ ![retouch-shapes-icon](rt-shapes-icon.jpg) button to show and edit any existing shapes for the currently-selected wavelet scale.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "algorithms\n: Choose a retouching algorithm (clone, heal, fill or blur). Ctrl+click to change the algorithm used for the currently-selected shape. Shift+click to set the default algorithm (used for new images or when you reset module parameters).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Wavelet decompose"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The _wavelet decompose_ section centres around a bar graph that shows how the image has been decomposed into detail (scale) layers. The key features of the bar graph are:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- The black square on the left represents the entire image before decomposition.\n- The gray squares show the various wavelet detail layers, with fine details to the left, and coarse details to the right.\n- The white square on the far right represents the residual image (the remainder of the image after the detail layers have been extracted).\n- A light gray dot in a square indicates the currently-selected layer. Click on another square to select a different layer.\n- The light gray bar running along the top indicates which levels of detail are visible at the current zoom level. Zoom in to see finer details.\n- The triangle at the bottom shows how many layers the image has been decomposed into. Drag the triangle to the right to create more layers. Drag it to the left to decrease the number of layers. By default no wavelet decomposition is performed.\n- The triangle at the top shows the current value of the \"merge from\" parameter (see below).\n- The orange lines under the squares indicate which layers have retouch edits applied.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The remaining items in this section are:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "scales\n: Shows how many detail layers the image has been decomposed into. Zero indicates that no wavelet decomposition has been performed.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "current\n: Shows which layer is currently selected (also marked with the light gray dot on the bar graph).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "merge from\n: This setting allows a single edit to be applied to multiple consecutive scales within a group starting from the coarsest scale (excluding the residual image) down to the scale selected. For example, if \"merge from\" is set to 3 and the maximum scale is 5 then all edits that are added to scale 5 will be applied to scales 3, 4 and 5. Edits added to scale 4 will be applied to scales 3 and 4, and edits added to scale 3 will be applied to scale 3 only. If you set _merge from_ to 0, then merging is disabled, and all edits apply only to the scale that they are defined in. Setting _merge from_ to the highest scale available (in this example, 5) also disables merging.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "           merge_from\n               v\n   0   1   2   3   4   5    scale\n               <-------o    scale 5 edits\n               <---o        scale 4 edits\n               o            scale 3 edits\n           o                scale 2 edits\n       o                    scale 1 edits\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "![retouch-display-icon](rt-display-icon.jpg) display wavelet scale\n: Display the currently-selected wavelet layer on the center image. Selecting this option brings up an additional control -- _preview single scale_.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "preview single scale\n: An additional control that allows the black, white and gray points of the wavelet scale preview to be adjusted to make it easier to see. Click the ![auto-levels-icon](rt-auto-levels-icon.jpg) to set these values automatically. This does not affect the module's operation -- only the wavelet scale preview.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "![retouch-cut-icon](rt-cut-icon.jpg) cut\n: Cut all shapes from the currently-selected layer and place them into the clipboard.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "![retouch-paste-icon](rt-paste-icon.jpg) paste\n: Move the shapes on the clipboard to the currently-selected layer.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "![retouch-hide-icon](rt-hide-icon.jpg) temporarily switch off shapes\n: Toggle all shapes (whether on the current layer or not) on or off, temporarily removing the module's effect.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "![retouch-mask-icon](rt-mask-icon.jpg) display masks\n: Show the target shapes associated with the currently-selected layer with a yellow overlay.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Shapes"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This section allows you to modify settings related to the currently-selected shape:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "shape selected\n: Indicates which shape is currently selected, and what type of shape it is.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "fill mode\n: If the _fill_ algorithm has been chosen for the currently-selected shape, choose whether to \"erase\" or fill the selected shape with a chosen \"color\".\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "fill color\n: If a _fill mode_ of \"color\" has been chosen, select the color to fill the shape with. You can click to select or enter a custom rgb value or use the color picker to take a sample from the image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "brightness\n: If the _fill_ algorithm has been chosen for the currently-selected shape, fine-tune the color's brightness. This slider also works in \"erase\" mode.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "blur type\n: If the _blur_ algorithm has been chosen for the currently-selected shape, choose whether to apply a \"gaussian\" or \"bilateral\" blur.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "blur radius\n: If the _blur_ algorithm has been chosen for the currently-selected shape, choose the radius of the blur.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "mask opacity\n: Alter the opacity of the mask associated with the currently-selected shape. An opacity of 1.0 indicates that the shape is completely opaque and the module's effect is fully applied, whereas a value less than 1.0 indicates that the effect applied by the shape is blended with the underlying image to the degree indicated by the slider.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/liquify.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Show guides"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/liquify.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/crop.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Tick the box to show guide overlays whenever the module is activated. Click the icon on the right to control the properties of the guides. See [guides & overlays](../../toolboxes/guides-overlays.md) for details.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Panning and zooming the image"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/retouch.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "While creating or editing a shape, mouse actions are applied to the current shape.  If you need to move or zoom the portion of the image shown in the center view, hold down the 'a' key while dragging the mouse or using the scroll wheel.  While the key is held down, the mouse actions will apply to the entire image rather than the current shape. Holding down the key will also temporarily suppress generating new shapes in continuous-creation mode.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/input-color-profile.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Input color profile"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/input-color-profile.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Define how Ansel will interpret the colors of the image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/input-color-profile.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This module takes the color space used by the image source (e.g. camera, scanner) and converts the pixel encodings to a standardized working color space. This means that subsequent modules in the pipeline don't need to be concerned with the specifics of the input device, and can work with and convert to/from a common working color space.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/input-color-profile.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Where an image has been captured in a raw file, the input color profile module will normally apply either a standard or enhanced color matrix specific for that camera model, which will be used to map the colors into the working profile color space. If color space information is embedded in the image, the _input color profile_ module will use this information when mapping the colors to the working profile color space. The user can also explicitly specify a color space for the incoming image, and can even supply a custom ICC color profile specifically made for the input device.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/input-color-profile.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "As part of the mapping from the input color space to the working profile space, the colors can be confined to a certain gamut using the _gamut clipping_ options, which can help to mitigate some (infrequent) color artifacts. This is also influenced by the chosen [rendering intent](../../../color-management/rendering-intent.md).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/input-color-profile.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Note that the final color profile that will be used when exporting the image is controlled by the [_output color profile_](output-color-profile.md) module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/input-color-profile.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "input profile\n: The profile or color matrix to apply. A number of matrices are provided along with an enhanced color matrix for some camera models. The enhanced matrices are designed to provide a look that is closer to that of the camera manufacturer.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/input-color-profile.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": You can also supply your own input ICC profiles and put them into `$Ansel/share/Ansel/color/in` or `$HOME/.config/Ansel/color/in` (where `$Ansel` is the Ansel installation directory and `$HOME` is your home directory). One common source of ICC profiles is the software that is shipped with your camera, which often contains profiles specific to your camera model. You may need to activate the [_unbreak input profile_](./unbreak-input-profile.md) module to use your own profiles.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/input-color-profile.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": If your input image is a low dynamic range file like JPEG, or a raw file in DNG format, it might already contain an embedded ICC profile, which Ansel will use by default. You can restore this default by selecting “embedded icc profile”.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/input-color-profile.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "working profile\n: The working profile used by Ansel's processing modules. Each module can specify an alternative space that it will work in, and this will trigger a conversion. By default Ansel will use \"linear Rec2020 RGB\", which is a good choice in most cases.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/input-color-profile.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "gamut clipping\n: Activate a color clipping mechanism. In most cases you can leave this control in its default “off” state. However, if your image shows some specific features such as highly saturated blue light sources, gamut clipping might be useful to avoid black pixel artifacts. See [possible color artifacts](../../../color-management/color-artifacts.md) for more information.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/input-color-profile.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": Choose from a list of RGB profiles. Input colors with a saturation that exceeds the permissible range of the selected profile are automatically clipped to a maximum value. “linear Rec2020 RGB” and “Adobe RGB (compatible)” allow for a broader range of unclipped colors, while “sRGB” and “linear Rec709 RGB” produce a tighter clipping. Select the profile that prevents artifacts while still maintaining the highest color dynamics.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/haze-removal.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Haze removal"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/haze-removal.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Automatically reduce the effect of dust and haze in the atmosphere. This module may also be employed more generally to give pictures a color boost specifically in low-contrast regions of the image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/haze-removal.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Haze absorbs light from objects in the scene but it is also a source of diffuse background light. The haze removal module first estimates, for each image region, the amount of haze in the scene. It then removes the diffuse background light according to its local strength and recovers the original object light.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/haze-removal.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Setting both of the module's controls to unity maximizes the amount of haze removal but is also likely to produce some artifacts. Removing the atmospheric light entirely may render the image flat and result in an unnatural looking style. Optimal values are typically below unity and are rather image dependent, but also a matter of personal aesthetic preferences.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/haze-removal.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "strength\n: The amount of haze removal. At unity, the module removes 100 percent of the detected haze up to the specified distance. Negative values increase the amount of haze in the image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/haze-removal.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "distance\n: Limit the distance up to which haze is removed. For small values, haze removal is restricted to the foreground of the image. Haze is removed from the entire image if the distance parameter is set to unity. If the _strength_ is negative the distance control has no effect.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/levels.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Levels"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/levels.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Adjust black, white and mid-gray points in RGB color space.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/levels.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The rgb levels tool shows a histogram of the image, and displays three bars with handles. Drag the handles to modify the black, middle-gray and white points in lightness (in \"RGB, linked channels\" mode) or independently for each of the R, G and B channels (in \"RGB, independent channels\" mode).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/levels.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Moving the black and white bars to match the left and right borders of the histogram will make the output image span the full available tonal range. This will increase the image's contrast.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/levels.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Moving the middle bar will modify the mid-tones. Move it to the left to make the image look brighter and move it to the right to make it darker. This is often referred to as changing the image's gamma.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/levels.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Three color pickers are available for sampling the black, white and gray points from the image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/levels.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Note:** Under certain conditions, especially with highly saturated blue light sources, the _levels_ module may produce black pixel artifacts. See the \"gamut clipping\" option of the [_input color profile_](./input-color-profile.md) module for information about how to mitigate this issue.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/levels.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "mode\n: The mode of operation. \"RGB, linked channels\" (default) provides a single levels tool which updates all channels, taking into account the selected color preservation method (see \"preserve colors\" below). \"RGB, independent channels\" provides separate levels controls for each of the R, G and B channels.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/levels.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "auto\n: Auto-adjust the black and white point and put the gray point exactly in the mean between them. Use the color picker to auto-adjust based on a selected region of the image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/levels.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "preserve colors\n: Choose a color preservation method when using \"RGB, linked channels\" mode (default \"luminance\").\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Color primaries"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Edit RGB/CYM primary control nodes in dt UCS and remap colors through a procedural 3D LUT.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The _color primaries_ module is a secondary color-grading tool built around the six additive and subtractive primaries:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- _red_,\n- _yellow_,\n- _green_,\n- _cyan_,\n- _blue_,\n- _magenta_.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Each of these control nodes is sampled inside the internal RGB gamut and converted to _dt UCS HSB_. The module lets you change the hue, saturation and brightness of those six reference colors, then interpolates the resulting RGB shifts through a cylindrical local field to build a dense 3D LUT.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This gives a tool that sits between a classic primary/secondary corrector and a palette remapper:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- The controls are sparse and easy to understand,\n- The final effect is continuous over the whole RGB volume,\n- Achromatic colors remain fixed on the black-white axis.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The module is designed to run before [_color balance_](./color-balance.md) in the pixelpipe.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Its internal LUT is built and applied in Rec.2020 HLG code values, while the module input and output stay in the pipeline working RGB space. Scene-referred RGB is normalized by the module _white level_ before entering the LUT domain and denormalized afterwards.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The six user controls do not sit directly on the RGB cube corners. Instead, the module starts from the RGB and CYM corners and recesses them toward the achromatic axis. The amount of recession is controlled by _gamut coverage_.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "At `100 %`, the control nodes reach the RGB cube shell. At lower values, the control hull shrinks inward. This setting is critical because it changes what the node edits mean geometrically:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- At `100 %`, negative saturation changes behave like a gamut compression, because the control hull touches the full internal gamut shell and desaturating the nodes pulls extreme colors back inward.\n- Well below `100 %`, the control hull sits inside the gamut. Increasing saturation from there can increase colorfulness by stretching a smaller inner color volume toward the outer gamut.\n- At `100 %`, positive saturation changes are dangerous. Since the control nodes already lie on the gamut shell, adding saturation there can push the interpolation toward very large RGB excursions and quickly explode the gamut.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In practice:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- use `100 %` coverage together with negative saturation shifts when your goal is gamut compression,\n- lower _gamut coverage_ significantly below `100 %` when your goal is to make colors more vivid,\n- avoid using `100 %` coverage together with positive saturation unless you need a very small and carefully controlled effect.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Use the LUT viewer, in the _options_ tab to preview the effect of your changes and validate the gamut is not all squeezed against the cube shell.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The user interface is split into two tabs:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- _colors_,\n- _options_.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Colors tab"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The _colors_ tab contains one section for each primary or secondary color, ordered along the light spectrum:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Each section contains the same three sliders.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "hue\n: Rotate the selected control node around the dt UCS hue ring.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": This changes the hue of the selected primary or secondary and lets neighboring colors follow through interpolation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "saturation\n: Increase or decrease the chroma of the selected control node.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": Negative values pull the node toward the achromatic axis. Positive values push it outward.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": The practical meaning depends strongly on _gamut coverage_:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- with `100 %` coverage, negative saturation is the safe way to compress gamut,\n- with lower coverage, positive saturation can be used to increase colorfulness,\n- with `100 %` coverage, positive saturation can produce excessive out-of-gamut excursions and should be avoided.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "brightness\n: Change the dt UCS brightness of the selected control node.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": This is useful to make a primary or secondary color look lighter or darker without changing all other hues equally.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The slider backgrounds are color-coded from the actual node colors and converted to the current display color profile for preview.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\nWhile we take the chromaticity coordinates of the Rec.2020 RGB primaries, we define their colorshift perceptually in dt UCS HSB space, project the shift back in RGB, and interpolate it in 3D as RGB gradients where the smoothing also happens. This allows a perceptually-grounded color change definition while limiting chroma noise, fringes, and other color artifacts typically associated with manipulating color in perceptual spaces because the 3D interpolation in RGB better preserves image gradients.\n{{< /note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Options tab"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "white level\n: Define the scene-referred white normalization used before the RGB signal enters the LUT domain.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": This is useful when the image contains values above diffuse white and you want the internal LUT to work in a bounded RGB code-value range.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": The slider includes a color picker. Sample a bright reference in the image to set the normalization directly from the scene.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "gamut coverage\n: Control how far the six control nodes reach toward the RGB cube shell.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": This parameter defines the effective color volume controlled by the module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": At `100 %`, the control nodes lie on the gamut boundary and desaturation acts like gamut compression.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": Reducing the value recesses the control hull toward the achromatic axis. This is often the better starting point when your goal is to increase colorfulness.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "brightness smoothing\n: Control how far brightness edits spread along the black-white axis when the deformation field is built.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "saturation smoothing\n: Control how far saturation edits spread away from the achromatic axis. Increase if you start seing chroma noise.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "hue smoothing\n: Control how broadly neighboring hue regions contribute to each local deformation. Increase if you start seeing chroma noise or uneven transitions between hues.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "neutral protection\n: Reduce the strength of color shifts close to the achromatic axis.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": Higher values protect near-neutral colors more strongly from hue and saturation remapping.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "interpolation\n: Choose the 3D LUT interpolation method used at application time.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": The same methods are available as in [_lut 3D_](./lut-3D.md): _tetrahedral_, _trilinear_ and _pyramid_. With a dense LUT the visual differences are usually subtle, but tetrahedral is the default and safest option.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "LUT viewer"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The bottom part of the _options_ tab contains a 3D LUT viewer. It displays the LUT geometry as a projected RGB cube:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- One colored node for the input RGB sample,\n- One colored node for the output RGB sample,\n- An arrow from input to output.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The viewer can display either:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- the interpolated LUT,\n- the sparse control nodes used to build it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "These two modes are mutually exclusive and help answer different questions:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- use the interpolated LUT view to inspect the final color field,\n- use the control-node view to understand how the sparse primaries scaffold is arranged in RGB.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The viewer uses the LUT internal RGB coordinates for geometry, but converts node colors to the display color profile for drawing.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Viewer controls"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "azimuth\n: Rotate the cube around the achromatic axis.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "axis tilt\n: Tilt the achromatic axis relative to the viewing plane. At `90°`, it shows a chromaticity diagram with the achromatic axis orthogonal to the graph plane. At `0°`, it shows the achromatic axis parallel to the graph plane.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "slice depth\n: Move the slicing plane through the cube. On dense LUTs, slicing allows an internal view of the cube at a given depth without displaying all nodes in front of and behind the plane.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "slice thickness\n: Show only node pairs lying within a band around the slicing plane. At `100 %`, slicing is effectively disabled and the graph may become too crowded to read.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "color shift threshold\n: Hide `input -> output` mappings whose input/output distance is below the selected percentage of RGB code values.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "target gamut\n: Restrict the displayed samples to those inside the selected RGB gamut.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "show control nodes\n: Switch the viewer from the interpolated LUT cloud to the sparse control-node cloud.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "save to cLUT\n: Export the currently displayed LUT to a reusable `.cube` file with metadata describing the LUT color space. This file needs to be applied in HLG Rec.2020 color space.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Viewer mouse interaction"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "mouse wheel\n: Zoom in and out, centered on the mouse cursor position.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "left-button drag\n: Pan the view.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "middle-button drag horizontally\n: Change the azimuth angle.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The strongest creative and technical lever in this module is _gamut coverage_. It is not just a reach parameter. It changes whether the module behaves more like a gamut compressor or more like a colorfulness enhancer.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A safe workflow is often:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. set _white level_ correctly,\n2. choose _gamut coverage_ based on the intent,\n3. edit the six node saturations first,\n4. refine hue and brightness afterwards,\n5. inspect the result in the LUT viewer.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "As a rule of thumb:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- for gamut compression, start from `100 %` coverage and pull the relevant nodes inward with negative saturation values (desaturation),\n- for colorfulness enhancement, reduce _gamut coverage_ first and then add saturation cautiously,\n- if colors start to break apart or look synthetic, reduce either the saturation shifts or the coverage before increasing smoothing.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-primaries.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The effect will be stronger in the immediate vicinity of the color control nodes, so defining the _gamut coverage_ also defines where your settings have the most weight. As we move away from the control nodes, the effect becomes weaker, which gracefully blends the colorshifts but can also mute the initial intent.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/surface-blur.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Surface blur"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/surface-blur.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Smooth image surfaces while preserving sharp edges using a bilateral filter.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/surface-blur.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This module _can_ be used to denoise images, however you should be aware that bilateral filters are susceptible to overshoots and Ansel offers much better alternatives. For example, the [_astrophoto denoise_](./astrophoto-denoise.md) module uses a non-local means denoising algorithm, and the [_denoise (profiled)_](./denoise-profiled.md) module provides a choice between non-local means and wavelet denoising algorithms.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/surface-blur.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The _surface blur_ module blurs noise within the surfaces of an image by averaging pixels with their neighbors, taking into account not only their geometric distance but also their distance on the range scale (i.e. differences in their RGB values). It can be particularly useful if one RGB channel is more noisy/needs more smoothing than the others. In such a case, use the [_color calibration_](./color-calibration.md) module to examine the channels one by one, in order to set the blur intensities accordingly.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/surface-blur.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The module can also be used as a creative filter to provide interesting effects, for example to lend an image a cartoon-like appearance. When used with chrominance blending, it can also be used to even out the color of a surface (for example, to remove skin redness).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/surface-blur.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This module is resource-intensive and slows down pixelpipe processing significantly. You should consider activating it late in your workflow.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/surface-blur.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "radius\n: The spatial extent of the gaussian blur.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/surface-blur.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "red, green, blue\n: The blur intensity for each of the RGB channels.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Array Element: tags
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/exposure.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Batch editing"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Color calibration"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A fully-featured color-space correction, white balance adjustment and channel mixer module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This simple yet powerful module can be used in the following ways:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- To adjust the white balance ([chromatic adaptation](#white-balance-in-the-chromatic-adaptation-transformation-cat-tab)), working in tandem with the [_white balance_](./white-balance.md) module. Here, the _white balance_ module makes some initial adjustments (required for the [_demosaic_](./demosaic.md) module to work effectively), and the _color calibration_ module then calculates a more perceptually-accurate white balance after the input color profile has been applied.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- As a simple RGB [channel mixer](#channel-mixing), adjusting the R, G and B output channels based on the R, G and B input channels, to perform cross-talk color-grading.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- To adjust the [color saturation, brightness and B&W output](#outputs-tab), based on the relative strength of the R, G and B channels of each pixel.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- To produce a [grayscale output](#outputs-tab) based on the relative strengths of the R, G and B channels, in a way similar to the response of black and white film to a light spectrum.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- To improve the color accuracy of the input color profile using a [color checker](#extracting-settings-using-a-color-checker) chart.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "White Balance in the Chromatic Adaptation Transformation (CAT) tab"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Chromatic adaptation aims to predict how all surfaces in the scene would look if they had been lit by another illuminant. What we actually want to predict, though, is how those surfaces would have looked if they had been lit by the same illuminant as your monitor, in order to make all colors in the scene match the change of illuminant. White balance, on the other hand, aims only at ensuring that whites and grays are really neutral (R = G = B) and doesn’t really care about the rest of the color range. White balance is therefore only a partial chromatic adaptation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Chromatic adaptation is controlled within the Chromatic Adaptation Transformation (CAT) tab of the _color calibration_ module. When used in this way the _white balance_ module is still required as it needs to perform a basic white balance operation (connected to the input color profile values). This technical white balancing (\"camera reference\" mode) is a flat setting that makes grays lit by a standard D65 illuminant look achromatic, and makes the demosaicing process more accurate, but does not perform any perceptual adaptation according to the scene. The actual chromatic adaptation is then performed by the _color calibration_ module, on top of those corrections performed by the _white balance_ and _input color profile_ modules. The use of custom matrices in the _input color profile_ module is therefore discouraged. Additionally, the RGB coefficients in the _white balance_ module need to be accurate in order for this module to work in a predictable way.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The _color calibration_ and _white balance_ modules can be automatically applied to perform chromatic adaptation for new edits by setting the chromatic adaptation workflow option ([preferences > processing > auto-apply chromatic adaptation defaults](../../../preferences-settings/processing.md)) to \"modern\". If you prefer to perform all white balancing within the _white balance_ module, a \"legacy\" option is also provided. Neither option precludes the use of other modules (such as [_color balance_](./color-balance.md)) for creative color grading further along the pixel pipeline.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "By default, _color calibration_ performs chromatic adaptation by:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Reading the RAW file's Exif data to fetch the scene white balance set by the camera,\n- Adjusting this setting using the camera reference white balance _D65 (daylight)_ preset from the _white balance_ module,\n- Further adjusting this setting with the input color profile in use (standard matrix only).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< warning >}}\nThe default settings initialization, for _color calibration_ chromatic adaptation, __does not work__ if you used an automatic preset changing the RGB coefficients in _white balance_ module. It only reads the defaults settings of _white balance_, which are _D65 (daylight)_. You will need to manually reset the _color calibration_ module (using the reset button) after the first pipeline run so it can read your custom coefficients if you use some.\n{{< /warning >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "For consistency, the _color calibration_ module's default settings always assume that the standard matrix is used in the _input color profile_ module -- any non-standard settings in this module are ignored. However, _color calibration_'s defaults can read any auto-applied preset in the _white balance_ module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "It is also worth noting that, unlike the _white balance_ module, _color calibration_ can be used with [masks](../masking-and-blending/masks/_index.md). This means that you can selectively correct different parts of the image to account for differing light sources.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "To achieve this, create an instance of the _color calibration_ module to perform global adjustments using a mask to exclude those parts of the image that you wish to handle differently. Then create a second instance of the module reusing the mask from the first instance (inverted) using a [raster mask](../masking-and-blending/masks/raster.md).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "CAT tab workflow"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The default illuminant and color space used by the chromatic adaptation are initialised from the Exif metadata of the RAW file. There are four options available in the CAT tab to set these parameters manually:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Use the color picker (to the right of the color patch) to select a neutral color from the image or, if one is unavailable, select the entire image. In this case, the algorithm finds the average color within the chosen area and sets that color as the illuminant. This method relies on the \"gray-world\" assumption, which predicts that the average color of a natural scene will be neutral. This method will not work for artificial scenes, for example those with painted surfaces.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Select \"_(AI) detect from edges_\", which uses a machine-learning technique to detect the illuminant using the entire image. This algorithm finds the average gradient color over the edges in the image and sets that color as the illuminant. This method relies on the \"gray-edge\" assumption, which may fail if large chromatic aberrations are present. As with any edge-detection method, it is sensitive to noise and poorly suited to high-ISO images, but it is very well suited for artificial scenes where no neutral colors are available.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Select \"_(AI) detect from surfaces_\", which combines the two previous methods, also using the entire image. This algorithm finds the average color within the image, giving greater weight to areas where sharp details are found and colors are strongly correlated. This makes it more immune to noise than the _edge_ variant and more immune to legitimate non-neutral surfaces than the naïve average, but sharp colored textures (like green grass) are likely to make it fail.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Select \"_as shot in camera_\" to restore the camera defaults and re-read the RAW Exif.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The color patch shows the color of the currently calculated illuminant projected into sRGB space. The aim of the chromatic adaptation algorithm is to turn this color into pure white, which does not necessarily means shifting the image toward its *perceptual* opponent color. If the illuminant is properly set, the image will be given the same tint as shown in the color patch when the module is disabled.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "To the left of the color patch is the _CCT_ (correlated color temperature) approximation. This is the closest temperature, in kelvin, to the illuminant currently in use. In most image processing software it is customary to set the white balance using a combination of temperature and tint. However, when the illuminant is far from daylight, the CCT becomes inaccurate and irrelevant, and the CIE (International Commission on Illumination) discourages its use in such conditions. The CCT reading informs you of the closest CCT match found:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- When the CCT is followed by \"_(daylight)_\", this means that the current illuminant is close to an ideal daylight spectrum ± 0.5 %, and the CCT figure is therefore meaningful. In this case, you are advised to use the \"_D (daylight)_\" illuminant.\n- When the CCT is followed by \"_(black  body)_\", this means that the current illuminant is close to an ideal black body (Planckian) spectrum ± 0.5 %, and the CCT figure is therfore meaningful. In this case, you are advised to use the \"_Planckian (black body)_\" illuminant.\n- When the CCT is followed by \"_(invalid)_\", this means that the CCT figure is meaningless and wrong, because we are too far from either a daylight or a black body light spectrum. In this case, you are advised to use the _custom_ illuminant. The chromatic adaptation will still perform as expected (see the note below), so the \"_(invalid)_\" tag only means that the current illuminant color is not accurately tied to the displayed CCT. This tag is nothing to be concerned about -- it is merely there to tell you to stay away from the daylight and planckian illuminants because they will not behave as you might expect.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When one of the above illuminant detection methods is used, the module checks where the calculated illuminant sits using the two idealized spectra (daylight and black body) and chooses the most accurate spectrum model to use in its _illuminant_ parameter. The user-interface will change accordingly:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- A temperature slider will be provided if the detected illuminant is close to a _D (daylight)_ or _Planckian (black body)_ spectrum, for which the CCT is meaningful.\n- Hue and chroma sliders in CIE 1976 Luv space are offered for the _custom_ illuminant, which allows direct selection of the illuminant color in a perceptual framework without any intermediate assumption.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Note:** Internally, the illuminant is represented by its absolute chromaticity coordinates in CIE xyY color space. The illuminant selection options in the module are merely interfaces to set up this chromaticity from real-world relationships and are intended to make this process faster. It does not matter to the algorithm if the CCT is tagged \"invalid\" -- this just means that the relationship between the CCT and the corresponding xyY coordinates is not physically accurate. Regardless, the color set for the illuminant, as displayed in the patch, will always be honored by the algorithm.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When switching from one illuminant to another, the module attempts to translate the previous settings to the new illumninant as accurately as possible. Switching from any illuminant to _custom_ preserves your settings entirely, since the _custom_ illuminant is a general case. Switching between other modes, or from _custom_ to any other mode, will not precisely preserve your settings from the previous mode due to rounding errors.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Other hard-coded _illuminants_ are available (see below). Their values come from standard CIE illuminants and are absolute. You can use them directly if you know exactly what kind of light bulb was used to illuminate the scene and if you trust your camera's input profile and reference (D65) coefficients to be accurate. Otherwise, see [_caveats_](#caveats) below.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "CAT tab controls"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "adaptation\n: The working color space in which the module will perform its chromatic adaptation transform and channel mixing. The following options are provided:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": - _Linear Bradford (1985)_: This is accurate for illuminants close to daylight and is compatible with the ICC v4 standard, but produces out-of-gamut colors for more difficult illuminants.\n: - _CAT16 (2016)_: This is the default option and is more robust in avoiding imaginary colors while working with large gamut or saturated cyan and purple. It is more accurate than the Bradford CAT in most cases.\n: - _Non-linear Bradford (1985)_: This can sometimes produce better results than the linear version but is unreliable.\n: - _XYZ_: This is the least accurate method and is generally not recommended except for testing and debugging purposes.\n: - _none (bypass)_: Disable any adaptation and use the pipeline working RGB space.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "illuminant\n: The type of illuminant assumed to have lit the scene. Choose from the following:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": - _same as pipeline (D50)_: Do not perform chromatic adaptation in this module instance but just perform channel mixing, using the selected _adaptation_ color space.\n: - _CIE standard illuminant_: Choose from one of the CIE standard illuminants (daylight, incandescent, fluorescent, equi-energy, or black body), or a non-standard \"LED light\" illuminant. These values are all pre-computed -- as long as your camera sensor is properly profiled, you can just use them as-is. For illuminants that lie near the Planckian locus, an additional \"temperature\" control is also provided (see below).\n: - _custom_: If a neutral gray patch is available in the image, the color of the illuminant can be selected using the color picker, or can be manually specified using hue and saturation sliders (in LCh perceptual color space). The color swatch next to the color picker shows the color of the calculated illuminant used in the CAT compensation. The color picker can also be used to restrict the area used for AI detection (below).\n: - _(AI) detect from image surfaces_: This algorithm obtains the average color of image patches that have a high covariance between chroma channels in YUV space and a high intra-channel variance. In other words, it looks for parts of the image that appear as though they should be gray, and discards flat colored surfaces that may be legitimately non-gray. It also discards chroma noise as well as chromatic aberrations.\n: - _(AI) detect from image edges_: Unlike the _white balance_ module's auto-white-balancing which relies on the \"gray world\" assumption, this method auto-detects a suitable illuminant using the \"gray edge\" assumption, by calculating the Minkowski p-norm (p = 8) of the laplacian and trying to minimize it. That is to say, it assumes that edges should have the same gradient over all channels (gray edges). It is more sensitive to noise than the previous surface-based detection method.\n: - _as shot in camera_: Calculate the illuminant based on the white balance settings provided by the camera.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "temperature\n: Adjust the color temperature of the illuminant. Move the slider to the right to assume a more blue illuminant, which will make the white-balanced image appear warmer/more red. Move the slider to the left to assume a more red illuminant, which makes the image appear cooler/more blue after compensation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": This control is only provided for illuminants that lie near the Planckian locus and provides fine-adjustment along that locus. For other illuminants the concept of \"color temperature\" doesn't make sense, so no temperature slider is provided.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "hue\n: For custom white balance, set the _hue_ of the illuminant color in LCh color space (derived from CIE Luv space).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "chroma\n: For custom white balance, set the _chroma_ (or saturation) of the illuminant color in LCh color space (derived from CIE Luv space).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "gamut compression\n:  Most camera sensors are slightly sensitive to invisible UV wavelengths, which are recorded on the blue channel and produce \"imaginary\" colors. Once corrected by the input color profile, these colors will end up out of gamut (that is, it may no longer be possible to represent certain colors as a valid [R,G,B] triplet with positive values in the working color space) and produce visual artifacts in gradients. The chromatic adaptation may also push other valid colors out of gamut, at the same time pushing any already out-of-gamut colors even further out of gamut.\n: _Gamut compression_ uses a perceptual, non-destructive, method to attempt to compress the chroma while preserving the luminance as-is and the hue as close as possible, in order to fit the whole image into the gamut of the pipeline working color space. One example where this feature is very useful is for scenes containing blue LED lights, which are often quite problematic and can result in ugly gamut clipping in the final image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "clip negative RGB from gamut\n: Remove any negative RGB values (set them to zero). This helps to deal with bad black level as well as the blue channel clipping issues that may occur with blue LED lights. This option is destructive for color (it may change the hue) but ensures a valid RGB output no matter what. It should never be disabled unless you want to take care of the gamut mapping manually and understand what you are doing. In that case, use the _black level correction_ in the _exposure_ module to get rid of any negative RGB (RGB means light, which is energy, and which should always be a positive quantity), then increase the _gamut compression_ until no solid black patches remain in the image. Proper denoising may help getting rid of odd RGB values too. Note that this approach may still be insufficient to recover some deep and luminous shades of blue.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Note 1**: It has been reported that some OpenCL drivers don't play well when negative RGB values are present in the pixel pipeline, because many pixel operators use logarithms and power functions (_filmic_, _color balance_, all the CIE Lab <-> CIE XYZ color space conversions), which are not defined for negative numbers. Although the inputs are sanitized before sensitive operations, it is not enough for some OpenCL drivers, which will output isolated `NaN` (Not a Number) values. These `NaN` values may be subsequently spread by local filters (blurring and sharpening operations, like _sharpness_, _local contrast_, _contrast equalizer_, _low pass_, _high pass_, _surface blur_, and _filmic_ highlights reconstruction), resulting in large black, gray or white squares.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In all these cases, you **must** enable the \"clip negative RGB from gamut\" option in the _color calibration_ module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Note 2**: A common case for failure of the color algorithms in _color calibration_ (especially the gamut compression) is pixels that have a luminance value of 0 (Y channel of the CIE 1931 XYZ space), but non-zero chromaticity values (X and Z channels of the CIE 1931 XYZ space). This case is a numerical oddity that matches no physical reality (a pixel with no luminance should have no chromaticity either), will produce a division by zero in xyY and Yuv color spaces, and will create `NaN` RGB values as a result. This issue is **not** corrected inside _color calibration_ because it is a symptom of a bad input profiling and/or a bad black point level, and needs to be addressed manually either by adjusting the input color profile with the channel mixer or in the _exposure_ module's _black level correction_.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Channel mixing"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The remainder of this module is a standard channel mixer, allowing you to adjust the output R, G, B, colorfulness, brightness and gray of the module based on the relative strengths of the R, G and B input channels.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The _Mixer_ tab now offers several modes, but these are only different GUI representations of the same backend reality. Internally, the processing still uses the same mixer coefficients in the selected _adaptation_ space. Switching between _Complete_, _Simple_, _Primaries_ and _White-preserving_ does not select a different algorithm and does not change the backend by itself. It only changes how the same underlying matrix is exposed in the GUI.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Channel mixing is performed in the color space defined by the _adaptation_ control on the [CAT tab](#cat-tab-controls). For all practical purposes, these CAT spaces are particular RGB spaces tied to human physiology and proportional to the light emissions in the scene, but they still behave in the same way as any other RGB space. The use of any of the CAT spaces can make the channel mixer tuning process easier, due to their connection with human physiology, but it is also possible to mix channels in the RGB working space of the pipeline by setting the _adaptation_ to \"none (bypass)\". To perform channel mixing in one of the _adaptation_ color spaces without chromatic adaptation, set the _illuminant_ to \"same as pipeline (D50)\".\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Note**: The actual colors of the CAT or RGB primaries used for the channel mixing, projected to sRGB display space, are painted in the background of the RGB sliders, so you can get a sense of the color shift that will result from your altered settings.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Channel mixing is a process that defines a boosting/muting factor for each channel as a ratio of all the original channels. Instead of entering a single flat correction that ties the output value of a channel to its input value (for example, \\(R\\_{\\mathrm{out}} = R\\_{\\mathrm{in}} \\times \\mathrm{correction}\\)), the correction to each channel is dependent on the input of _all_ of the channels for each pixel (for example, \\(R\\_{\\mathrm{out}} = R\\_{\\mathrm{in}} \\times R\\_{\\mathrm{correction}} + G\\_{\\mathrm{in}} \\times G\\_{\\mathrm{correction}} + B\\_{\\mathrm{in}} \\times B\\_{\\mathrm{correction}}\\)). Thus a pixel's channels contribute to each other (a process known as \"cross-talk\") which is equivalent to rotating the primary colors of the color space in 3D. This is, in effect, digital simulation of physical color filters.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Although rotating primary colors in 3D is ultimately equivalent to applying a general hue rotation, the connection between the RGB corrections and the resulting perceptual hue rotation is not directly predictable, which makes the process non-intuitive. \"R\", \"G\" and \"B\" should be taken as a mixture of 3 lights that we dial up and down, not as a set of colors or hues. Also, since RGB tristimulus does not decouple luminance and chrominance, but is an additive lighting setup, the \"G\" channel is more strongly tied to human luminance perception than the \"R\" and \"B\" ones. All pixels have a non-zero G channel, which implies that any correction to the G channel is likely to affect all pixels.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The channel mixing process is therefore tied to a physical interpretation of the RGB tristimulus (as additive lights), which makes it well-suited for primary color grading and illuminant corrections, and blends the color changes smoothly. However, trying to understand and predict it from a perceptual point of view (luminance, hue and saturation) is going to fail and is discouraged.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Note**: The \"R\", \"G\" and \"B\" labels on the channels of the color spaces in this module are merely conventions formed out of habit. These channels do not necessarily look \"red\", \"green\" and \"blue\", and users are advised against trying to make sense out of them based on their names. This is a general principle that applies to _any_ RGB space used in any application.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Mixer modes"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "All mixer modes describe the same backend 3×3 transform for the RGB output channels. They differ only by the parameterization used in the GUI and by the mathematical constraints required for that parameterization to exist.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A 3×3 matrix has 9 coefficients, so a mode that exposes 9 controls can reach every possible mixer, and a mode that exposes 6 necessarily describes a restricted family. What each restricted mode gives up in reach, it buys back in predictability: the 3 coefficients it does not expose are not free, they are solved so as to keep some property of the transform invariant. That is the real difference between the modes, and it is what should drive your choice:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< table >}}\n| mode | controls | what it holds invariant | available when |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| _Complete_ | 9 | nothing | always |\n| _Simple_ | 6 | the (1,1,1) axis of the mixer space | the 3 output rows are normalized and their sums are non-zero |\n| _Primaries_ | 9 | nothing | the matrix is non-singular and every column has a non-zero sum |\n| _White-preserving_ | 6 | the neutral of the mixer space | the matrix already leaves that neutral unchanged |\n{{< /table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- reach for _Complete_ when you have actual coefficients to type in, or when no other mode can express what you need;\n- reach for _White-preserving_ when you are grading and want the guarantee that the correction cannot introduce a global cast behind your back;\n- reach for _Simple_ for the same guarantee when _adaptation_ is \"none (bypass)\", where its invariant axis is the neutral -- but note that in a CAT space it pins `(1,1,1)`, which is not the neutral there (see below);\n- reach for _Primaries_ when you deliberately __want__ to tint the white or change the overall gain, which the two 6-control modes cannot do by construction.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Whenever the current matrix leaves a mode's constraint unsatisfied, that mode is refused and the GUI falls back to _Complete_ rather than silently showing you controls that do not describe the matrix in use.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "_Simple_ is an exact geometric representation of a restricted subset of mixer matrices. It has 6 degrees of freedom:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "global hue rotation\n: A rigid rotation of the normalized chroma plane. This is a robust and global hue change.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "chroma (u,v) axes orientation\n: The orientation of the principal axes used to express the chroma transform. This parameter lets you align your UV space on the color primaries you want. It has no effect on the image by itself, it only builds the UV space for the next settings.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "u stretch and v stretch\n: The two signed gains along those principal chroma axes. The neutral value is 1. At 0, it will turn the image achromatic, no matter the axes orientation. Above 1, it will increase the color contrast in the selected axis. Below 0, it will invert colors along the selected axis. Below 1.5, it will increase the color contrast on inverted colors.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "achromatic coupling amount and achromatic coupling hue\n: The magnitude and direction of the chroma vector that is coupled into the achromatic axis, expressed in a fixed chroma basis. When increasing _amount_, the selected hue will be bleached to achromatic (both desaturated and brightened), while its opponent color is saturated and darkened, but in a way that preserves the achromatic axis. All other colors will be more or less saturated/desaturated, depending on their hue angular distance with the _coupling hue_.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This representation is only valid when the 3 output rows are normalized and their sums are non-zero. In practice, _Simple_ is available only if the 3 _normalize channels_ checkboxes of the RGB mixer rows are enabled and the row sums are not zero. If these requirements are not met, Ansel cannot express the current matrix in this coordinate system and the GUI falls back to _Complete_.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "_(U,V) stretches_ can be understood as color contrast along the axis.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A typical use for this would be correcting blue stage lights when the escape gamut and turn magenta : the right angle should be around 0° to put the damaging blue shade on the V axis. Then reduce the _v stretch_ until that blue is not so overwhelming, and adjust _u stretch_ to taste to get a proper color balance.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The _achromatic coupling_ allows to gracefully remap a selected hue to achromatic, while reinforcing the colorfulness of its complementary color. In the case of the stage blue stage lights, after the previous _(u, v) stretch_, you would choose the right blue tint and increase the _coupling amount_ until blue becomes acceptably desaturated.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "_Primaries_ is another exact GUI representation, this time expressed as a generalized primaries model in the current mixer basis. It has 9 degrees of freedom:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- 2 for the achromatic axis: _white hue_ and _white purity_,\n- 2 for each basis vector: _red_, _green_ and _blue hue/purity_ pairs, for a total of 6,\n- 1 _gain_ correction, that accounts for global normalization. \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This mode is exact only for matrices that can be interpreted as a non-degenerate affine basis change in the current adaptation space. In practice, the current 3×3 matrix must be non-singular and the 3 basis vectors plus the white vector must all have non-zero sums. If these requirements are not met, Ansel cannot rebuild the current matrix as primaries parameters and the GUI falls back to _Complete_.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "_White-preserving_ describes the same primaries picture as _Primaries_ above, with the white nailed down. It has 6 degrees of freedom, 2 per primary:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "red, green and blue rotation\n: Turn that primary around the white, within the chromaticity plane of the mixer space. This is a hue rotation, but a per-primary one: you can swing the reds without touching the blues, which the global rotation of _Simple_ cannot do.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "red, green and blue saturation\n: Scale the distance between that primary and the white. `0%` leaves the primary where the mixer space puts it, `-100%` collapses it onto the white, and `+100%` doubles its distance. Negative values desaturate whatever that primary contributes to the image, positive values push it outward.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "All six controls at `0` is the identity matrix, exactly. Setting the three _saturation_ sliders together to the same value is exactly a global saturation control around the neutral: `+100%` doubles the colorfulness of the image, and moving them down together desaturates it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "One asymmetry is worth knowing about. A _single_ primary can go all the way to `-100%`: collapsing one primary onto the neutral still leaves a usable transform. All _three_ at once cannot, because a mixer with every primary sitting on the neutral maps the entire image to a single color and has no inverse to build from. The three-at-once limit measures at about `-86%`, which already leaves only a seventh of the original colorfulness. Past it, the mode reports that the primaries are degenerate and the sliders snap back to the last usable setting -- for a true black-and-white conversion, use the _B&W_ section of the _Outputs_ tab instead.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The distinguishing property is in the name and it is structural, not approximate: __whatever the six sliders do, the neutral of the mixer space comes out unchanged__. This is not a constraint applied on top of the model, it __is__ the model. Only the 3 primary chromaticities are yours to set; the 3 magnitudes that go with them are solved from the requirement that the neutral maps to itself, which is exactly how 6 controls can describe the whole family of neutral-preserving mixers and nothing else.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Which neutral is preserved depends on the _adaptation_ setting of the CAT tab, because that is what defines the mixer space:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- with _adaptation_ set to \"none (bypass)\", the mixer runs in the pipeline working RGB and the preserved neutral is `(1,1,1)` there;\n- with any CAT space (_Bradford_, _CAT16_, _XYZ_), the mixer runs after the chromatic adaptation, where a neutral pixel carries the D50 white expressed in that space -- and that is the vector this mode preserves.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This is worth knowing because it is where _White-preserving_ and _Simple_ part company. _Simple_ pins the `(1,1,1)` axis of whatever space it runs in. In \"none (bypass)\" that __is__ the neutral, and the two modes then describe exactly the same family of matrices with different controls -- pick whichever set of sliders suits the correction you have in mind. In a CAT space, however, `(1,1,1)` is not the neutral the pipeline carries, so a _Simple_ setting can shift neutrals there, while _White-preserving_ cannot.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The mode is available when the current matrix already leaves the mixer neutral unchanged, every column has a non-zero sum, and the 3 primaries are not collinear -- two primaries turned onto the same direction flatten the model and are refused. Note that a matrix with all 3 _normalize channels_ checkboxes enabled preserves `(1,1,1)`, which qualifies in \"none (bypass)\" but generally not in a CAT space.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "At its most basic level, you can think of the _Complete_ mode of the _color calibration_ module as a type of matrix multiplication between a 3x3 matrix and the input RGB vector. This is in fact very similar to what a matrix-based ICC color profile does, except that the user can input the matrix coefficients via the Ansel GUI rather than reading the coefficients from an ICC profile file.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "$$\n\\begin{pmatrix}\nR_{\\mathrm{out}} \\\\\\\nG_{\\mathrm{out}} \\\\\\\nB_{\\mathrm{out}}\n\\end{pmatrix} =\n\\begin{pmatrix}\nR_r & R_g & R_b \\\\\\\nG_r & G_g & G_b \\\\\\\nB_r & B_g & B_b\n\\end{pmatrix}\n\\begin{pmatrix}\nR_{\\mathrm{in}} \\\\\\\nG_{\\mathrm{in}} \\\\\\\nB_{\\mathrm{in}}\n\\end{pmatrix}\n$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This mode therefore has 9 degrees of freedom: every output row has 3 coefficients mixing the 3 input channels. It is the most general representation and is always available. Whenever another mode cannot represent the current matrix exactly, the GUI falls back to _Complete_.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If, for example, you've been provided with a matrix to transform from one color space to another, you can enter the matrix coefficients into the _channel mixer_ as follows:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- In the _Mixer_ tab, select _Complete_ mode\n- In the _output red_ section, set the Rr, Rg and Rb values using the R, G and B input sliders\n- In the _output green_ section, set the Gr, Gg and Gb values using the R, G and B input sliders\n- In the _output blue_ section, set the Br, Bg and Bb values using the R, G and B input sliders\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "By default, the mixing function in _color calibration_ just copies the input RGB channels straight over to the matching output channels. This is equivalent to multiplying by the identity matrix:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "$$\n\\begin{pmatrix}\nR_{\\mathrm{out}} \\\\\\\nG_{\\mathrm{out}} \\\\\\\nB_{\\mathrm{out}}\n\\end{pmatrix} =\n\\begin{pmatrix}\n1 & 0 & 0 \\\\\\\n0 & 1 & 0 \\\\\\\n0 & 0 & 1\n\\end{pmatrix}\n\\begin{pmatrix}\nR_{\\mathrm{in}} \\\\\\\nG_{\\mathrm{in}} \\\\\\\nB_{\\mathrm{in}}\n\\end{pmatrix}\n$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "For a more intuitive understanding of how the mixing sliders in the _output red_, _output green_ and _output blue_ sections behave, consider the following:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- For the _R_ destination channel, adjusting sliders to the right will make the R, G or B areas of the image more \"red\". Moving the slider to the left will make those areas more \"cyan\".\n- For the _G_ destination channel, adjusting sliders to the right will make the R, G or B areas of the image more \"green\". Moving the slider to the left will make those areas more \"magenta\".\n- For the _B_ destination channel, adjusting sliders to the right will make the R, G or B areas of the image more \"blue\". Moving the slider to the left will make those areas more \"yellow\".\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Complete mode controls"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The following controls are shown for each of the _output red_, _output green_ and _output blue_ sections:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "input R/G/B\n: Choose how much the input R, G and B channels influence the output channel relating to the tab concerned.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "normalize channels\n: Select this checkbox to normalize the coefficients to try to preserve the overall brightness of this channel in the final image as compared to the input image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Outputs tab"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The _Outputs_ tab groups the previous _colorfulness_, _brightness_ and _B&W_ controls into a single page with section labels. These controls are independent from the _Mixer_ tab modes above, but they follow the same overall idea: they use the relative strengths of the R, G and B input channels to build output quantities.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The brightness and colorfulness (color saturation) of pixels in an image can also be adjusted based on the R, G and B input channels. This uses the same basic algorithm that the [_filmic_](filmic.md) module uses for tone mapping (which preserves RGB ratios) and for mid-tones saturation (which massages them).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "saturation algorithm\n: This control allows you to upgrade the saturation algorithm to the new 2021 version, for edits produced prior to darktable 3.6 -- it will not appear for edits that already use the latest version.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Colorfulness section controls"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "input R/G/B\n: Adjust the color saturation of pixels, based on the R, G and B channels of those pixels. For example, adjusting the _input R_ slider will affect the color saturation of pixels containing a lot of \"R\" more than pixels containing only a small amount of \"R\".\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "normalize channels\n: Select this checkbox to try to keep the overall saturation constant between the input and output images.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Brightness section controls"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "input R/G/B\n: Adjust the brightness of certain colors in the image, based on the R, G and B channels of those colors. For example, adjusting the _input R_ slider will affect the brightness of colors containing a lot of R channel much more than colors containing only a small amount of R channel. When darkening/brightening a pixel, the ratio of the R, G and B channels for that pixel is maintained, in order to preserve the hue.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "normalize channels\n: Select this checkbox to try to keep the overall brightness constant between the input and output images.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "B&W section"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Another very useful application of _color calibration_ is the ability to mix the channels together to produce a grayscale output -- a monochrome image. In the _Outputs_ tab, use the _B&W_ section and set the R, G and B sliders to control how much each channel contributes to the brightness of the output. This is equivalent to the following matrix multiplication:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "$$\n\\mathrm{GRAY}\\_{\\mathrm{out}} =\n\\begin{pmatrix}\nr & g & b\n\\end{pmatrix}\n\\begin{pmatrix}\nR\\_{\\mathrm{in}} \\\\\\\nG\\_{\\mathrm{in}} \\\\\\\nB\\_{\\mathrm{in}}\n\\end{pmatrix}\n$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When dealing with skin tones, the relative weights of the three channels will affect the level of detail in the image. Placing more weight on R (e.g. [0.9, 0.3, -0.3]) will make for smooth skin tones, whereas emphasising G (e.g. [0.4, 0.75, -0.15]) will bring out more detail. In both cases the B channel is reduced to avoid emphasising unwanted skin texture.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "B&W section controls"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "input R/G/B\n: Choose how much each of the R, G and B channels contribute to the gray level of the output. The image will only be converted to monochrome if the three sliders add up to some non-zero value. Adding more B will tend to bring out more details, adding more R will tend to smooth skin tones.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "normalize channels\n: Select this checkbox to try to keep the overall brightness constant as the sliders are adjusted.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Spot color mapping"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/exposure.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The spot mapping feature is designed to help with batch-editing a series of images in an efficient way. In this scenario, you typically develop a single reference image for the whole batch and then copy&paste the development stack to all of the other images in the batch.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Unfortunately, the light color temperature often changes slightly between shots, even within the same series captured in the same conditions. This can be the result of a cloud passing by the sun in natural light, or a different ratio between colored bounce light and main light. Each image will still need some individual fine-tuning if you want a perfectly even look over the whole series, and this can be both time-consuming and frustrating.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Spot color mapping allows you to define a target chromaticity (hue and chroma) for a particular region of the image (the control sample), which you then match against the same target chromaticity in other images. The control sample can either be a critical part of your subject that needs to have constant color, or a non-moving and consistently-lit surface over your series of images.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/exposure.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The mapping process consists of two steps.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/exposure.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Step 1: set the target"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "There are two ways of setting the target chromaticity for your control sample:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. If you know or expect an arbitrary color for the control sample (for example, a gray card, a color chart, a product or a logo of a specified color), you can set its L, h and c values directly, in Lch derived from CIE Lab 1976 space,\n2. If you simply want to match the development of your reference image, set the _spot mode_ to _measure_, then enable the color picker (to the right of the color patch) and draw a rectangle over your control sample. The _input_ column will then be updated with the L, h, c values of the control sample before the color correction, and the _target_ column will show the resulting L, h, c values of the control sample after the current calibration setting is applied.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If you reset the L, h, c values, the default value is a neutral color at 50% lightness (middle-gray) -- this can be useful to quickly set the average white balance of any image. If you want to match the control sample against neutral gray, you only need to reset the chroma slider because the lightness and hue settings have no effect on chromaticity for neutral grays.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/exposure.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Note that the target value is not reset when you reset the module itself, but is stored indefinitely in Ansel's configuration and will be available on next launch as well as for the next image you develop.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The _take channel mixing into account_ option lets you choose where the target is sampled. If disabled, the target color is measured immediately after the _CAT_ (Chromatic Adaptation Transform) step, which takes place before any channel mixing. This means that if you have a calibrated profile in effect within the channel mixer, this profile will be discarded. If enabled, the target color is measured after the _CAT_ and the channel mixing steps, including any calibrated profile. This is the recommended option for most use cases.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Note**: If you are defining your target from a gray patch, you should know that the gray patch on color checkers is never entirely neutral. For example, Datacolor Spyder has a slightly warm gray (hue = 20°, chroma = 1.2) while X-Rite pre-2014 has a colder but more neutral gray (hue = 240°, chroma = 0.3) and X-Rite post-2014 is almost perfectly neutral (hue = 133°, chroma = 0.2). In general, it is not desirable to match the control sample against a perfectly neutral gray target, and it is actually wrong to do so when using gray cards and color checkers as a control sample.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/exposure.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Step 2 : match the target"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When you open a new image, the _spot mode_ is automatically reset to _correction_. Using the color picker attached to the color patch, you can then directly reselect your control sample in the new image. The proper illuminant settings required for the control sample to match the memorized target chromaticity will be automatically computed, and the setting will be updated in the same operation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The _take channel mixing into account_ option will need to be set the same as when the measurement of the target was performed to ensure consistent results. Note that the target matching only defines the illuminant settings used in the Chromatic Adaptation Transform  -- it does not alter the channel mixer settings, since the calibration is handled in the color checker calibration tool. However, the channel mixer settings can be used or discarded in the computation of the illuminant settings, depending on this option.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/exposure.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This operation can be repeated as many times as you have images in your series with no further work.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Note:** Perfectly matching your control sample against the target chromaticity may still not yield a similar perceptual result, even if the numbers are exactly the same. The ratio of lightness between the control sample and its surrounding, as well as the color contrasts at play in the frame, will alter the perception of colors in ways that are very difficult to model. To build an intuition of this problem, see the [gray strawberries illusion](https://www.sciencealert.com/in-spite-of-what-your-eyes-tell-you-these-strawberries-aren-t-red).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Extracting settings using a color checker"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Since the channel mixer is essentially an RGB matrix (similar to the [_input color profile_](./input-color-profile.md) used for RAW images) it can be used to improve the color accuracy of the input color profile by computing ad-hoc color calibration settings.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "These computed settings aim to minimize the color difference between the scene reference and the camera recording in a given lighting situation. This is equivalent to creating a generic ICC color profile but here, the profile is instead stored as module settings that can be saved as presets or styles, to be shared and reused between images. Such profiles are meant to complement and refine the generic input profile but do not replace it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This feature can assist with:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "* Handling difficult illuminants, such as low [CRI](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Color_rendering_index) light bulbs, for which a mere white balancing will never suffice,\n* Digitizing artworks or commercial products where an accurate rendition of the original colors is required,\n* Neutralizing a number of different cameras to the same ground-truth, in multi-camera photo sessions, in order to obtain a consistent base look and share the color editing settings with a consistent final look,\n* Obtaining a sane color pipeline from the start, nailing white balance and removing any bounced-light color cast at once, with minimal effort and time.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Supported color checker targets"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Users are not currently permitted to use custom targets, but a limited number of verified color checkers (from reputable manufacturers) are supported:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- X-Rite / Gretag MacBeth Color Checker 24 (pre- and post-2014),\n- Datacolor SpyderCheckr 24 (pre- and post-2018),\n- Datacolor SpyderCheckr 48 (pre- and post-2018).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Users are discouraged from obtaining cheap, off-brand, color targets as color constancy between batches cannot possibly be asserted at such prices. Inaccurate color checkers will only defeat the purpose of color calibration and possibly make things worse.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "IT7 and IT8 charts are not supported since they are hardly portable and not practical for use on-location for ad-hoc profiles. These charts are better suited for creating generic color profiles, undertaken using a standard illuminant, for example with [Argyll CMS](https://encrypted.pcode.nl/blog/index.html%3Fp=594.html).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Note**: X-Rite changed the formula of their pigments in 2014 and Datacolor in 2018, which slightly altered the color of the patches. Both formulas are supported in Ansel, but you should be careful to choose the correct reference for your target. If in doubt, try both and choose the one that yields the lowest average delta E after calibration.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In order to use this feature you will need to take a test shot of a supported color checker chart, on-location, under appropriate lighting conditions:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "* Frame the chart in the center 50% of the camera's field, to ensure that the image is free of vignetting,\n* Ensure that the main light source is far enough from the chart to give an even lighting field over the surface of the chart,\n* Adjust the angle between the light, chart and lens to prevent reflections and gloss on the color patches,\n* For the best quality profile you should capture an image with the appropriate brightness. To achieve this, take a few bracketed images (between -1 and +1 EV) of your color checker and load them into Ansel, ensuring that all modules between _color calibration_ and _output color profile_ are disabled. Choose the image where the white patch has a brightness L of 94-96% in CIE Lab space or a luminance Y of 83-88% in CIE XYZ space (use the global color picker). This step is not strictly necessary -- alternatively you can take a single image and apply the exposure compensation as recommended in the profile report.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If the lighting conditions are close to a standard D50 to D65 illuminant (direct natural light, no colored bounced light), the color checker shot can be used to produce a generic profile that will be suitable for any daylight illuminant with only a slight adjustment of the white balance.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If the lighting conditions are peculiar and far from standard illuminants, the color checker shot will be only usable as an ad-hoc profile for pictures taken in the same lighting conditions.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The settings used in color calibration depend on the chosen CAT space and on any color settings defined earlier in the pipe within the _white balance_ and _input color profile_ modules. As such, the results of the profiling (e.g. the RGB channel mixing coefficients) are valid only for a rigid set of _CAT space_, _white balance_ and _input color profile_ settings. If you wish to create a generic style with your profile, don't forget that you will need to include the settings from these modules as well.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Use the following process to create your profile preset/style:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. Enable the [_lens correction_](./lens-correction.md) module to correct any vignetting that might mislead the calibration,\n2. On the bottom of the _color calibration_ module, click on the arrow near the _calibrate with a color checker_ label, to show the controls,\n3. Pick the correct model and manufacturer of your color checker from the _chart_ drop-down,\n4. In the image preview, an overlay of the chart's patches will appear. Drag the corners of the chart so that they match the visual references (dots or crosses) around the target, to compensate for any perspective distortion,\n5. Click the _refresh_ button to compute the profile,\n6. Check the _Profile quality report_. If it is \"good\", you can click on the _validation_ button. If not, try changing the optimization strategy and refresh the profile again.\n7. Save the profile in a preset or style, or simply copy & paste the module settings to all of the pictures taken under the same lighting conditions, from within the lighttable view or filmstrip.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Note:** You don't _need_ to use the standard matrix in the _input color profile_ module when performing a calibration, but be aware that the \"as shot in camera\" default white balance will not work properly with any other profile, and that you will need to always use the same input profile whenever you reuse such calibration settings.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Reading the profile report"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The profile report helps you to assess the quality of the calibration. The settings in color calibration are only a \"best fit\" optimization and will never be 100% accurate for the whole color spectrum. We therefore need to track \"how inaccurate\" it is in order to know whether we can trust this profile or not.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Bad profiles can happen and will do more harm than good if used.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Delta E and the quality report"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The [CIE delta E 2000](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Color_difference#CIEDE2000) (ΔE) is used as a perceptual metric of the error between the reference color of the patches and the color obtained after each step of calibration:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- ΔE = 0 means that there is no error -- the obtained color is exactly the reference color. Unfortunately, this will never happen in practice.\n- ΔE = 2.3 is defined as the Just Noticeable Difference (JND).\n- ΔE < 2.3 means that the average observer will not be able to tell the difference between the expected reference color and the obtained color. This is a satisfactory result.\n- ΔE > 2.3 means that the color difference between the expected reference and the obtained color is noticeable for the average observer. This is unsatisfactory but sometimes unavoidable.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The quality report tracks the average and maximum ΔE at the input of the module (before anything is done), after the chromatic adaptation step (white balance only), and at the output of the module (white balance and channel mixing). At each step, the ΔE should be lower than at the previous step, if everything goes as planned.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Profile data"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The data generated by the profiling process comprises the RGB 3×3 matrix and the detected illuminant. These are expressed in the CAT _adaptation_ space defined in the _CAT_ tab and are provided in case you want to export these coefficients to other software. If the detected illuminant is _daylight_ or _black body_, the matrix should be fairly generic and reusable for other _daylight_ and _black body_ illuminants with the addition of a small white balance adjustment.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Normalization values"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "These are the settings that you should define, as-is, for the _exposure_ and _black level correction_ parameters in the [_exposure_](./exposure.md) module, in order to obtain the lowest possible error in your profile. This step is optional and is useful only when the utmost precision is required, but beware that it can produce negative RGB values that will be clipped at various points in the pipeline.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Overlay"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "![color checker](color-checker.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The chart overlay displays a disc in the center of each color patch, which represents the expected reference value of that patch, projected into the display RGB space. This helps you to visually assess the difference between the reference and the actual color without having to bother with ΔE values. This visual clue will be reliable only if you set the _exposure_ module as instructed in the _normalization values_ of the profile report.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Once the profile has been calibrated, some of the square patches will be crossed in the background by one or two diagonals:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "* Patches that are not crossed have ΔE < 2.3 (JND), meaning they are accurate enough that the average observer will be unable to notice the deviation,\n* Patches crossed with one diagonal have 2.3 < ΔE < 4.6, meaning that they are mildly inaccurate,\n* Patches crossed with two diagonals have ΔE > 4.6 (2 × JND), meaning that they are highly inaccurate.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This visual feedback will help you to set up the optimization trade-off to check which colors are more or less accurate.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Enhancing the profile"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Because any calibration is merely a \"best fit\" optimization (using a weighted least-squares method) it is impossible to have all patches within our ΔE < 2.3 tolerance. Some compromise will therefore be required.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The _optimize for_ parameter allows you to define an optimization strategy that attempts to increase the profile accuracy in some colors at the expense of others. The following options are available:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- _none_: Don't use an explicit strategy but rely on the implicit stategy defined by the color checker manufacturer. For example, if the color checker has mostly low-saturation patches, the profile will be more accurate for less-saturated colors.\n- _neutral colors_: Give priority to grays and less-saturated colors. This is useful for desperate cases involving cheap fluorescent and LED lightings, having low CRI. However, it may increase the error in highly-saturated colors more than not having any profile.\n- _saturated colors_: Give priority to primary colors and highly-saturated colors. This is useful in product and commercial photography, to get brand colors right.\n- _skin and soil colors_, _foliage colors_, _sky and water colors_: Give priority to the chosen hue range. This is useful if the subject of your pictures is clearly defined and has a typical color.\n- _average delta E_: Attempt to make the color error uniform across the color range and minimize the average perceptual error. This is useful for generic profiles.\n- _maximum delta E_: Attempt to minimize outliers and large errors, at the expense of the average error. This can be useful to get highly saturated blues back into line.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "No matter what you do, strategies that favor a low average ΔE will usually have a higher maximum ΔE, and vice versa. Also, blues are always the more challenging color range to get correct, so the calibration usually falls back to protecting blues at the expense of everything else, or everything else at the expense of blues.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The ease of obtaining a proper calibration depends on the quality of the scene illuminant (daylight and high CRI illuminants should always be preferred), the quality of the primary _input color profile_, the _black point compensation_ set in the _exposure_ module, but first and foremost on the mathematical properties of the camera sensor's filter array.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Profile checking"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "It is possible to use the _color space check_ button (first on the left, at the bottom of the module) to perform a single ΔE computation of the color checker reference against the output of the _color calibration_ module. This can be used in the following ways:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. To check the accuracy of a profile calculated in particular conditions against a color checker shot in different conditions.\n2. To evaluate the performance of any color correction performed earlier in the pipe, by setting the _color calibration_ parameters to values that effectively disable it (CAT _adaptation_ to _none_, everything else set to default), and just use the average ΔE as a performance metric.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The ability to use standard CIE illuminants and CCT-based interfaces to define the illuminant color depends on sound default values for the standard matrix in the _input color profile_ module as well as reasonable RGB coefficients in the _white balance_ module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Some cameras, most notably those from Olympus and Sony, have unexpected white balance coefficients that will always make the detected CCT invalid even for legitimate daylight scene illuminants. This error most likely comes from issues with the standard input matrix, which is taken from the Adobe DNG Converter.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "It is possible to alleviate this issue, if you have a computer screen calibrated for a D65 illuminant, using the following process:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-calibration.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. Display a white surface on your screen, for example by opening a blank canvas in any photo editing software you like\n2. Take a blurry (out of focus) picture of that surface with your camera, ensuring that you don't have any \"parasite\" light in the frame, you have no clipping, and are using an aperture between f/5.6 and f/8,\n3. Open the picture in Ansel and extract the white balance by using the spot tool in the _white balance_ module on the center area of the image (non-central regions might be subject to chromatic aberrations). This will generate a set of 3 RGB coefficients.\n4. Save a preset for the _white balance_ module with these coefficients and auto-apply it to any color RAW image created by the same camera.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/liquify.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Liquify"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/liquify.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Move pixels around by applying freestyle distortions to parts of the image using points, lines and curves.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/liquify.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Nodes"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/liquify.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Each of liquify's tools is based on nodes. A point consists of a single node and a line or curve consists of a sequence of linked nodes defining a path.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/liquify.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Each instance of the liquify module is limited to a maximum of 100 nodes -- for more nodes, use additional instances. However, please note that the liquify module consumes a lot of system resources.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/liquify.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Drag the central point of a node to move the node around. The radius describes the area of the effect (distortion occurs only within this radius). To change the radius drag the handle at the circumference. A strength vector starting from the center describes the direction of the distortion, and its strength is depicted by the length of the vector. Change the vector by dragging its arrow head.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/liquify.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Points"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/liquify.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A point consists of a single node and strength vector.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/liquify.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Click the point icon to activate the point tool and then click on the image to place it. Hold Ctrl while clicking on the point icon to add multiple points without having to click the icon again each time. Right-click to exit creation mode.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/liquify.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Point modes"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/liquify.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The strength vector of a point has three different modes. These can be toggled by holding Ctrl and clicking on the arrowhead of the strength vector.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/liquify.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "linear\n: A linear distortion inside the circle, starting from the opposite side of the strength vector and following the vector's direction. This is the default mode.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/liquify.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": ![linear](liquify_ex1.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/liquify.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "radial growing\n: The strength vector's effect is radial, starting with a strength of 0% in the center and increasing away from the center. This mode is depicted by an additional circle with the arrow pointing outwards.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/liquify.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": ![radial growing](liquify_ex4.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/liquify.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "radial shrinking\n: The strength vector's effect is radial, starting with a strength of 100% in the center and decreasing away from the center. This mode is depicted by an additional circle with the arrow pointing inwards.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/liquify.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": ![radial shrinking](liquify_ex3.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/liquify.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Feathering"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/liquify.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "default mode\n: By default the strength varies linearly from 0% to 100% between the center and the radius of the control point. It is possible to modify the feathering effect by clicking on the center of the circle.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/liquify.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": ![default](liquify_ex1.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/liquify.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "feathered mode\n: In \"feathered\" mode, two control circles are displayed, which can be modified independently to feather the strength of the effect. Note that clicking the center of the circle again hides the feathering controls but does _not_ return to the default mode.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/liquify.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": ![feathered](liquify_ex2.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/liquify.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Removing points"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/liquify.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A point can be removed by right-clicking on the center of the node.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/liquify.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Lines and curves"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/liquify.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Lines and curves are sequences of points linked together by straight or curved lines. The effect is interpolated by a set of associated strength vectors.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/liquify.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Click the appropriate icon to activate the line or curve tool and then click on the image to place a sequence of points forming the path. Right-click anywhere when the last point has been placed in order to finish drawing the line/curve.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/liquify.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Hold Ctrl while clicking on the line/curve icon to add multiple lines/curves without having to click the icon again each time. Right-click a second time to exit creation mode after the final line or curve has been completed.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/liquify.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "lines\n: ![lines](liquify_ex5.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/liquify.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "curves\n: ![curves](liquify_ex6.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/liquify.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ctrl+click on a line or curve segment to add a new control point. Ctrl+right-click on the center of a node to remove a control point.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/liquify.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Right-click on a segment to remove the shape completely. Ctrl+Alt+click on a segment to change that segment from a line to a curve and vice versa.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/liquify.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Link modes"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/liquify.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ctrl+click on the center of a node to change the way the points of a curve are linked together. There are four modes, which correspond to different ways of handling the steepness of the bezier curve using control handles:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/liquify.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "autosmooth\n: This is the default mode, in which control handles are not displayed -- controls are automatically computed to give a smooth curve.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/liquify.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "cusp\n: Control handles can be moved independently. This mode is depicted by a triangle symbol in the node's center.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/liquify.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "smooth\n: Control handles always give a smooth curve. This mode is depicted by a diamond symbol in the node's center.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/liquify.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "symmetrical\n: Control handles are always moved together. This mode is depicted by a square symbol in the node's center.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/liquify.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "View and edit nodes"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/liquify.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Click the node tool icon to activate or deactivate the node edit tool. This displays all currently-defined distortion objects and their controls. Alternatively you can right-click on the image at any time for the same effect.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/liquify.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Warps and nodes count"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/liquify.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This information field displays the number of warps (individual distortion objects) and nodes currently in use.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/raw-denoise.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Raw denoise"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Perform denoising on raw image data before it is [demosaiced](./demosaic.md).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This module has been ported from [dcraw](https://www.dechifro.org/dcraw/).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "noise threshold\n: The threshold for noise detection. Higher values lead to stronger noise removal and greater loss of image detail.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "coarse/fine curves\n: The noise of an image is usually a combination of fine-grained and coarse-grained noise. These curves allow the image to be denoised more or less depending on the coarseness of the visible noise. The left of the curve will act on very coarse grain noise, while the right of the curve will act on very fine grain noise.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": Raising the curve will result in more smoothing, while lowering it will result in less smoothing. As an example, you can preserve very fine-grained noise by pulling down the rightmost point of the curve to the minimum value.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": If you are tackling chroma noise with a [blend mode](../masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md), you can raise the rightmost part of the curve quite high, as colors do not change a lot on fine grain scales. This will help especially if you see some isolated pixel left un-denoised.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": The best way to use the R, G, and B curves is to examine each of the channels in turn using the [_color calibration_](./color-calibration.md) module in gray mode, denoise that channel, and then repeat for the other channels. This way, you can take into account the fact that some channels may be noisier than others. Beware that guessing which channel is noisy without actually seeing the channels individually is not straightforward and can be counterintuitive. A pixel which is completely red may not be caused by noise on the R channel, but actually by noise on G and B channels.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/graduated-density.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Graduated density"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/graduated-density.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Simulate a graduated density filter in order to correct exposure and color in a progressive manner.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/graduated-density.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A line is shown on screen allowing the position and rotation of the gradient to be modified with the mouse.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/graduated-density.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This module is known to provoke banding artifacts under certain conditions. You should consider activating the [_dithering_](./dithering.md) module to alleviate these issues.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/graduated-density.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "density\n: Set the density of the filter (EV). A low value underexposes slightly whereas a high value creates a strong filter.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/graduated-density.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "hardness\n: The progressiveness of the gradient. A low value creates a smooth transition, whereas a high value makes the transition more abrupt.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/graduated-density.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "rotation\n: The rotation of the filter. Negative values rotate clockwise. The rotation can also be set by dragging the end of the gradient line with the mouse.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/graduated-density.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "hue\n: Choose a hue to add a color cast to the gradient.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/graduated-density.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "saturation\n: The saturation of the color cast to add to the gradient (defaults to a neutral color cast of 0)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/dithering.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Dithering"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/dithering.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This module eliminates some of the banding artifacts that can result when Ansel's internal 32-bit floating point data is transferred into discrete 8-bit or 16-bit integer output format for display or export.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/dithering.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Although not an inherent problem in any of Ansel's modules, some operations may provoke banding if they produce a lightness gradient in the image. To mitigate possible artifacts you should consider activating dithering when using the [_vignetting_](./vignetting.md) or [_graduated density_](./graduated-density.md) modules. This is especially relevant for images with extended homogeneous areas such as cloudless sky. Also watch out for banding artifacts when using a gradient [drawn mask](../masking-and-blending/masks/drawn).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/dithering.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Viewing an image dithered into a very low bit depth from some distance (e.g. “Floyd-Steinberg 1-bit b&w”) will give the impression of a homogeneous grayscale image. Ansel attempts to mimic this impression when rendering zoomed-out images in the center view, the navigation window and thumbnails. This is accomplished by dithering those images into a higher number of grayscale levels. Note that, as a consequence, the [scopes](../../toolboxes/scopes.md) module -- the data for which is derived from the navigation window -- will show this increased number of levels and is therefore not a full match to the output image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/dithering.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "method\n: Choose the dithering method to use.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/dithering.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": _Floyd-Steinberg_: Systematically distribute quantization errors over neighboring pixels. This method can be selected with some typical output bit depths. Alternatively, you can select _Floyd-Steinberg auto_, which automatically adapts to the desired output format.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/dithering.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": _random dithering_: This method just adds some level of randomness to break sharp tonal value bands.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/dithering.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "damping (\"random\" method only)\n: Controls the level of added random noise expressed as a damping factor in a 10*log 2 basis. A value of -80 is a good fit for 8-bit output formats; -160 for 16-bit output.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/vignetting.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Vignetting"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/vignetting.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Apply a vignetting effect to the image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/vignetting.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Vignetting is a modification of the brightness and saturation at the borders of the image in a specified shape. Many of the parameters listed below can also be modified with a graphical control that overlays the image when the module has focus, showing the shape and extent of the effect.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/vignetting.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Note:** This module is known to provoke banding artifacts under certain conditions. You should consider activating the [_dithering_](./dithering.md) module to alleviate this.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/vignetting.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "_**Also note:** This module can produce unnatural-looking results and should be used with care. Instead, use the [exposure](./exposure.md) module with an [elliptical mask](../masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md) with a large transition area and, if necessary, use the [color balance rgb](./color-balance.md) module with the same mask to reduce saturation at the edges._\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/vignetting.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "scale\n: The radius of the vignetting area.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/vignetting.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "fall-off strength\n: The progressiveness of the fall-off. Lower values cause a steeper transition.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/vignetting.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "brightness\n: The intensity of brightening (positive values) or darkening (negative values).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/vignetting.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "saturation\n: Control how strong colors become when desaturated/saturated in the darkened/brightened vignetting area.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/vignetting.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "horizontal/vertical center\n: Shift the center of the vignetting area horizontally/vertically.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/vignetting.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "shape\n: The shape of the vignetting effect. The default value of 1 creates a circular or elliptical area. Smaller values shift the shape to being more square; higher values turn it into a cross-like shape.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/vignetting.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "automatic ratio\n: Automatically adjust the width/height ratio of the vignetting area to match the aspect ratio of the underlying image. This typically causes the vignetting area to become elliptical.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/vignetting.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "width/height ratio\n: Manually adjust the width/height ratio of the vignetting area.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/vignetting.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "dithering\n: Activate random noise dithering to alleviate banding artifacts caused by vignette gradients. Select “8-bit output” to prevent banding on monitor display and for JPEGs. When set to “16-bit output”, only a small amount of dithering is applied, just strong enough to compensate for banding on the fine grained 16-bit level. It is now recommended that you instead use the [_dithering_](./dithering.md) module to alleviate banding artifacts.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/output-color-profile.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Output color profile"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/output-color-profile.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Manage the output profile for export and the rendering intent to be used when mapping between color spaces.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/output-color-profile.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel comes with pre-defined profiles _sRGB_, _Adobe RGB_, _XYZ_ and _linear RGB_. You can provide additional profiles by placing them in `$Ansel/share/Ansel/color/out` and `$HOME/.config/Ansel/color/out` (where `$Ansel` is the Ansel installation directory and `$HOME` is your home directory).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/output-color-profile.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The output color profile may also be defined within the [export](../../toolboxes/export.md) module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/output-color-profile.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "output intent\n: The rendering intent for output/export. Rendering intent can only be selected when using LittleCMS2 to apply the output color profile (this can be changed in [preferences > processing](../../../preferences-settings/processing.md)). If Ansel's internal rendering routines are used, this option is hidden. For more details see [rendering intent](../../../color-management/rendering-intent.md).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/output-color-profile.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "output profile\n: The profile used to render colors for output/export. The profile data will be embedded into the output file (if supported by the file format) allowing other applications to correctly interpret its colors. As not all applications are aware of color profiles, the general recommendation is to stick to sRGB unless you know what you are doing and have a good reason to do otherwise.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/blurs.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Blurs"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/blurs.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Simulate physically-accurate blurs in scene-referred RGB space.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/blurs.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Blur types"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/blurs.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Three types of blur are provided:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/blurs.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. _lens blur_: Simulates a lens diaphragm with a configurable number of blades and blade curvature to create synthetic bokeh.\n2. _motion blur_: Simulates the effect of camera motion with a configurable path.\n3. _gaussian blur_: This is not really an optical blur but can be used for denoising or for creative effects using [blend modes](../masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/blurs.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A diagram at the top of the module shows the shape of the blurring operator (known as the [_point spread function_](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Point_spread_function)). The module will turn each luminous point from the scene into a blot shaped like the displayed blurring operator, with the size of the blot defined by the _blur radius_.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/blurs.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "blur radius\n: The spreading size of the blur.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/blurs.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "blur type\n: Choose between the different blur variants (above).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/blurs.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Controls specific to lens blur"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/blurs.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "diaphragm blades\n: The number of blades that the diaphragm is composed of. Older lenses used typically 5 or 7 blades, newer lenses typically use 9 or 11 blades. In any case, real lenses have an odd number of blades and any number greater than 11 blades comes very close to producing a perfect disc. If you degenerate the diaphragm settings with the _concavity_ to create a star or an asterisk, this control defines how many branches it has.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/blurs.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "concavity\n: - a concavity of 1 ensures the diaphragm is a [regular convex polygon](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Regular_polygon#Regular_convex_polygons) (triangle, pentagon, heptagon, etc.).\n: - a concavity greater than 1 but lower than `number of blades - 1` turns the shape into a star.\n: - a concavity greater than `number of blades - 1` but lower than `number of blades` turns the shape into an asterisk, when decreasing _linearity_ below 1.\n: - a concavity greater than or equal to `number of blades` degenerates the shape into a \"burst pattern\".\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/blurs.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "linearity\n: - a linearity of 0 creates a disc, no matter the number of blades or the concavity.\n: - a linearity of 1 makes all the outer bounds of the shape straight.\n: - a linearity between 0 and 1 makes the outer bounds of the shape more or less curved.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/blurs.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "rotation\n: Allows the shape to be rotated with respect to its center -- mostly useful with a small number of blades, when a particular orientation is needed.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/blurs.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Controls specific to motion blur"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/blurs.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "direction\n: The orientation of the motion's path in angular degrees. 0° is horizontal motion.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/blurs.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "curvature\n: The curvature of the motion. Zero produces a straight line, a negative value produces a concave curvature, a positive value produces a convex curvature.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/blurs.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "offset\n: Shifts along the motion path following its curve. This is useful to select a portion of the curved path that is symmetrical, which produces a coma shape (example 1: direction = -45°, curvature = +2, offset = +0.5 ; example 2 : direction = -45°, curvature = +1, offset = +1).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/blurs.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This module is implemented using a \"naive\" convolution, which is a slow algorithm. Faster approaches are available (using FFT) but not yet implemented. The GPU implementation, through OpenCL, should hide this issue somewhat. In any case, the runtime of the module will increase with the square of the blur radius.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/blurs.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The blurring process does not take scene depth and depth-of-field into account, but blurs the whole image as a flat object. It is therefore not suitable for creating fake depth-of-field. Using Ansel's general masking will only partially work to isolate the foreground of an image, since it will still be blurred into the background.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/blurs.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Tips and tricks"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/blurs.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "All images are usually (even a tiny bit) noisy. If you blur only a part of the image, the blurred region will look suspiciously clean compared to the rest of the image. It is therefore a good idea to add a bit of noise on top of the blurred part to blend it with the rest, using either the [grain](./grain.md) or the [censorize](./censorize.md) modules.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-reconstruction.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Color reconstruction"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-reconstruction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Recover color information in blown-out highlights.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-reconstruction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Due to the nature of digital sensors, overexposed highlights lack valid color information. Most frequently they appear neutral white or exhibit some color cast, depending on what other image processing steps are involved. This module can be used to “heal” overexposed highlights by replacing their colors with better fitting ones. The module acts on pixels whose luminance exceeds a user-defined threshold. Replacement colors are taken from neighboring pixels. Both the spatial distance and the luminance distance (range) are taken into account for color selection.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-reconstruction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Due to a limitation of the underlying algorithm, reconstructed colors may sometimes be displayed incorrectly if you zoom into the image in the darkroom view. If this happens you might observe a magenta shift in highlight areas close to high contrast edges, or you might see colorless highlight areas when used alongside the “reconstruct color” method of the [_highlight reconstruction_](./highlight-reconstruction.md) module. These artifacts only influence on-screen image display -- the final output remains unaffected. It is recommended that you finetune the parameters of this module only when viewing the fully zoomed-out image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-reconstruction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Note that similar functionality is also available in the _reconstruct_ tab of the [_filmic_](./filmic.md#reconstruct) module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-reconstruction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "threshold\n: The color reconstruction module replaces the color of all target pixels having luminance values above this threshold. Only pixels with luminance values below the threshold are taken as valid source pixels for replacement colors. Setting this parameter too high will cause the module to have no effect on any pixels. Setting it too low will minimize the “pool” of replacement colors -- if no suitable colors are available, the original colors are retained. This parameter therefore exhibits a “sweet spot” characteristic with an optimum setting depending on the individual image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-reconstruction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "spatial extent\n: The spatial distance that source pixels may have from target pixels in order for them to contribute to color replacement. Higher values cause ever more distant pixels to contribute. This increases the chance of finding a replacement color but can make the replacement color less well suited for the reconstruction.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-reconstruction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "range extent\n: The range difference (in luminance) that source pixels may have from target pixels in order for them to contribute to color replacement. Higher values cause more pixels to contribute even if their luminance differs more strongly from the target pixels. This again increases the chance of finding a replacement color but at the same time increases the risk of unfitting colors creeping in.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-reconstruction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "precedence\n: This combobox defines whether certain replacement colors shall take precedence over others as follows:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-reconstruction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": - _off_ (default): All pixels contribute equally\n: - _saturated colors_: Pixels contribute according to their chromaticity -- the more highly-saturated a color, the more it contributes\n: - _hue_: Give precedence to a specific hue (see below)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-reconstruction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "hue\n: This slider is only visible if you set the _precedence_ to “hue”. It allows you to select a preferred hue for replacement colors. This only has an effect if the preferred hue is actually present within the selected spatial and range distance of the target pixels (see above). A typical use case is repairing highlights on human skin in situations where diverging colors are in close proximity (e.g. textiles or hair with a luminance close that of to skin). Setting a hue preference on skin tones prevents these other colors from creeping in.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Array Element: tags
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/finalscale.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Sampling"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/finalscale.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Final resampling"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/finalscale.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This module has always been in the pixel pipeline but was hidden in darktable GUI. Ansel 0.0 exposes it to allow users to fix a mistake in the default pipeline order of darktable.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/finalscale.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Action"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/finalscale.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When exporting images to save them to a file, the picture is processed at full resolution and then rescaled to the specified output size by this module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/finalscale.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "_Final resampling_ is never used when producing previews, for the lighttable thumbnails or in the darkroom, because in this case images are resampled earlier in the pipeline, for better rendering speed. It has no effect if the image is exported at its full (original) resolution either.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/finalscale.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Problem"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/finalscale.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In darktable legacy pipeline order as well as in the darktable v3.0 default pipeline order, the final resampling is positionned after the [output color profile](./output-color-profile.md), which means it resamples RGB data encoded with an [OETF](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transfer_functions_in_imaging) (improperly called \"gamma\" in ICC color profiles), meaning it processes non-linear RGB.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/finalscale.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Resampling non-linear RGB is known to create artifacts such as fringes around sharp and contrasted edges, and should be avoided in general. However, in a majority of cases, it makes no perceptible difference, especially when processing low-dynamic-range images.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/finalscale.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Solution"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/finalscale.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel exposes the GUI widget of the _final resampling_ module to give users the opportunity to correct themselves the position of the module in the pipeline, pending a new default pipeline order.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/finalscale.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "It is recommanded to place the _final resampling_ before the _output color profile_ module, and even before [_filmic_](./filmic.md) for best results.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/finalscale.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The type of interpolation used for this final rescaling is defined globally in the software, in the [_preferences_](../../../preferences-settings/processing.md), under the _pixel interpolator (scaling)_ parameter.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/finalscale.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Enhancements"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/finalscale.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A common practice is to apply some sharpening after the downsampling. Ansel does not do it by default, but you can by duplicating an instance of [_diffuse and sharpen_](./diffuse.md), [_sharpen_](./sharpen.md) or [_contrast equalizer_](./contrast-equalizer.md) and moving it after (above) the _final resampling_ module. In that case, ensure _final resampling_ is before the display view transform (_filmic_ or the _base curve_) such that your sharpening instance can go in-between. You should keep your sharpening at the pixel level, with a radius of 1-2 px maximum.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Drawing"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Paint premultiplied RGBA layers directly in darkroom and save them as pages inside a TIFF sidecar.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This module is a true painting layer system. It is not a local-adjustment mask editor and it is not a clone/heal tool like [retouch](./retouch.md). Instead, it creates and edits independent raster layers that are composited over the current image in the pixelpipe.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The intended use cases are:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Hand painting,\n- Dodging and burning with a brush,\n- Color glazing,\n- Painterly overlays,\n- Matte painting on top of a photograph,\n- Texture painting,\n- Soft masking or erasing with a pressure-sensitive tablet.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The TIFF sidecar is saved as 16 bits floating point, compressed losslessly, in scene-linear working RGB space (the colorspace ICC profile is included in the file), with premultiplied alpha. It can be modified in any software that supports this. \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Each instance of the module is saved as a different layer in this file. This sidecar can also be used to import any arbitrary image and compose it over the photograph, under the display view transform. Typical masking features are available too.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "What the module does"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The module stores one or more drawing layers in a TIFF sidecar linked to the current image. Each layer is a premultiplied RGBA image in the module working space. The layer is loaded into memory, edited live in darkroom, composited over the image preview, and written back to the sidecar when needed.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The module works in three distinct spaces at the same time:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- A full-resolution authoritative layer cache, used for persistence,\n- A view-dependent cropped and scaled process tile, used for realtime display while painting,\n- The darkroom display buffer, where the layer is blended over the current image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In practice, this means the brush can stay responsive while painting, even on large images, because the module paints live only into the visible process tile. The finished stroke is then replayed asynchronously at full resolution into the authoritative cache.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "General workflow"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A typical workflow is:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. Create a new drawing layer.\n2. Choose a paint mode, fall-off, color and brush parameters.\n3. Paint in darkroom.\n4. Adjust or switch layers.\n5. Save the sidecar explicitly if needed, or let the module flush it when leaving darkroom or closing the application.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The module can manage several layers inside the same sidecar TIFF. Each module instance is linked to one layer name and one layer order. You can therefore stack several drawing module instances if you want several independent painted layers in the history.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "How realtime painting works"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The painting engine is split in two stages.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. GUI stroke sampling worker"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The first worker receives raw input events from the GUI:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Button press starts a stroke,\n- Pointer motion adds raw samples,\n- Button release ends the stroke.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Those raw events include:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Position,\n- Pressure,\n- Tilt,\n- Acceleration,\n- Current brush settings,\n- Stroke id and event id.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This worker converts raw input into evenly spaced dabs. It is responsible for:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Distance-based resampling,\n- Smoothing,\n- Interpolation of dab properties,\n- Pressure/tilt/acceleration mapping,\n- Stroke-local opacity and flow behavior,\n- Smudge carry state.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The emitted dabs are immutable records. Once a dab has been emitted into the stroke history, later stages are expected to consume it as-is.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "2. full-resolution replay worker"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "While you paint, the module updates only the current process tile for display. When the stroke is finished, a second background worker replays the immutable dab history into the full-resolution layer cache.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This design avoids paying the cost of full-resolution rasterization at every live dab. It is the main reason the module can remain interactive on large images.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Compositing model"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The drawing layer is stored as premultiplied RGBA and composited over the incoming image buffer.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "That matters for three reasons:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. Color and opacity are stored together in a way that avoids fringe artifacts at soft edges.\n2. Erasing reduces alpha and therefore removes previously-painted contribution cleanly.\n3. Layer blending remains stable through the pixelpipe and through OpenCL processing.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Brush tab"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The _Brush_ tab contains the painting controls.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Paint mode"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "paint\n: Paint the selected color over the current layer.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "erase\n: Reduce or remove previously-painted layer content by attenuating alpha.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "blur\n: Sample a local neighborhood and blend a blurred result back into the layer.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "smudge\n: Pick up already-painted pixels and drag them along the stroke.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Color"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "color\n: Sets the brush color in display RGB.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "pick from\n: Choose whether the color picker samples from the module input or output.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "HDR exposure\n: Raises the brush color intensity above display white. This is useful if the layer is meant to live in a scene-referred HDR pipeline rather than as a simple display-referred overlay.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Geometry"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "fall-off\n: Chooses the brush alpha profile. The module currently provides four profiles:\n: - linear,\n: - gaussian,\n: - quadratic,\n: - sigmoidal.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": The preview widget shows each profile rasterized with the current hardness and sprinkle settings.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "size\n: Brush radius in pixels.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "sampling distance\n: Controls dab spacing along the stroke.\n: - low values produce very dense stamping,\n: - high values space dabs farther apart,\n: - very high values are faster but can reveal the individual stamp structure.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": Internally this goes from approximately one-pixel spacing at 0% to roughly one brush diameter at 100%.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "smoothing\n: Applies stroke smoothing before the dab is emitted. This reduces jitter from the raw pointer stream, at the cost of slightly more predictive motion.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "hardness\n: Controls the inner solidity of the brush.\n: - high values produce a hard center and steeper edge,\n: - low values produce a soft brush with a larger transition zone.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Thickness"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "opacity\n: The target opacity of the stroke.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "flow\n: Controls how overlapping dabs build up within the same stroke.\n: - at 100%, the stroke behaves like watercolor or an airbrush with overlap normalization: overlapping dabs in the same stroke are capped so they do not keep building toward full opacity under normal sampling. Self-intersecting brush strokes will have a constant opacity.\n: - at 0%, the stroke behaves more like a marker or highlighter: overlaps accumulate. Self-intersecting brush strokes will build up more opacity at the intersection.\n: - anything else is a weighted mix of both strategies.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": This is one of the most important controls to understand. _Opacity_ defines the intended strength of the stroke. _Flow_ defines how that strength is distributed across overlapping dabs.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Texture"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "sprinkles\n: Adds multiplicative grain/noise to the brush alpha.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "sprinkle size\n: Controls the spatial size of the grain in layer-space pixels.\n: - low values give fine grain,\n: - high values give larger speckles or blotches.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "coarseness\n: Controls the octave mix of the sprinkle noise.\n: - 0% favors the coarsest octave,\n: - 50% balances the octaves evenly,\n: - 100% favors the finest octave.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Layer tab"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The _Layer_ tab manages sidecar layers and preview background.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Background"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "image\n: Shows the normal image under the drawing layer.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "white, grey, black\n: Replaces the image background in the preview with a flat color. This is useful to inspect painted alpha and edge quality independently of the photograph.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Layer management"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "layer name\n: Name of the current drawing layer in the sidecar TIFF.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "source layer\n: Select an existing layer from the sidecar.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "create new layer\n: Creates a new blank layer with the current name.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "delete layer\n: Deletes the linked layer from the sidecar TIFF.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "create background from input\n: Creates a background layer initialized from the module input. This is useful if you want to paint destructively on a rasterized snapshot rather than on transparency, or if you want to open the sidecar TIFF in another drawing application while still having the reference image in background. Both layers are positionned as they appear.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Fill"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "white\n: Fills the current layer with opaque white.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "black\n: Fills the current layer with opaque black.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "transparency\n: Clears the current layer to full transparency.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "History buttons"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "At the top of the module, outside the notebook tabs, there are three important buttons:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "undo\n: Restores the previous full-layer snapshot.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "redo\n: Reapplies the last undone snapshot.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "save sidecar\n: Forces the current in-memory layer state to be written immediately to the TIFF sidecar.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This is explicit persistence, not just a history item. It is useful if you want to secure long painting sessions before leaving darkroom.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Input tab"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The _Input_ tab maps tablet or pointer data to brush parameters.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "It supports three input sources:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Pressure,\n- Tilt,\n- Acceleration.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Each source can modulate four targets:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Size,\n- Opacity,\n- Flow,\n- Hardness.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "For each source, you can also choose a mapping profile:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Linear,\n- Quadratic,\n- Square root,\n- Inverse linear,\n- Inverse square root,\n- Inverse quadratic.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "These mappings are evaluated per raw input event, before the event is turned into a resolved dab. The cursor preview uses the mapped values too, so the visible cursor should reflect what will actually be emitted into the stroke.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Stroke sampling details"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The stroke engine does not simply stamp one dab per mouse event. Instead, it tries to build a stable sampled path.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The raw pointer path is converted into evenly spaced dabs according to the selected sampling distance. If GUI events arrive too sparsely because of system load or fast mouse motion, the missing dabs are interpolated along the path. If smoothing is enabled, the final dab position and properties are blended with a prediction from the previously-emitted dabs.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The important consequence is that the visible stroke should depend much more on the chosen brush spacing than on the operating system event rate.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Why this module can be slow"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This module is more expensive than an ordinary color adjustment module, because it maintains and updates raster images interactively.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The main cost sources are:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Large brush footprints"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Brush cost grows with the damaged area. A very large soft brush can touch hundreds of thousands of pixels per dab.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Blur and smudge"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "These modes are slower than ordinary paint and erase because they need extra neighborhood sampling and, for smudge, a carried runtime state.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Sprinkles"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Sprinkles add procedural grain evaluation to the alpha path. Large sprinkle size and high-strength grain can become expensive, especially with large brushes.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Full-resolution replay"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Live painting updates only the view-dependent process tile. The finished stroke is then replayed asynchronously at full resolution into the authoritative cache. This moves cost away from the live stroke, but it does not remove it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "On very long or very large strokes, the replay worker can remain busy for a while after the stroke ends.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Saving and leaving darkroom"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The sidecar TIFF must reflect the authoritative full-resolution layer. If there are still deferred replay jobs pending when you:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Save the sidecar,\n- Leave darkroom,\n- Close the application,\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "then Ansel must wait for those jobs to finish before the sidecar can be written safely.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The module therefore shows a blocking modal wait dialog when leaving or closing while a layer still needs to be finalized and saved.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Performance architecture and limitations"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The module uses several caches and workers to remain interactive. The first one being that layers are saved at the resolution of the RAW input, so this is a considerably larger painting matte than in most drawing applications.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Process tile"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "While painting, the module rasterizes into a process tile representing the current visible area plus a safety margin. This is what makes realtime interaction possible.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The consequence is that the immediate preview is view-dependent. If you pan or zoom, the tile must be rebuilt from the authoritative cache.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Authoritative full-resolution cache"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The real saved layer lives in a full-resolution in-memory cache. The sidecar TIFF is only the persistence format.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This is necessary because rebuilding a full-resolution layer from a previously-scaled preview tile would destroy fine texture and blur procedural details such as sprinkles.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Deferred replay worker"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Finished strokes are replayed into the full-resolution cache in a second worker thread. This reduces live latency, but it introduces a second source of work that must eventually complete.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The module supports OpenCL in the compositing path. That accelerates the darkroom display path, but the brush rasterizer itself remains CPU-driven.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Practical consequences:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- The pixelpipe can blend the layer on GPU,\n- Stroke generation and dab rasterization are still CPU tasks,\n- If OpenCL is disabled for the module, the compositing path will run on CPU,\n- If OpenCL is enabled but later modules need the GPU at the same time, total responsiveness can still depend on system load and GPU contention.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "First-use costs"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The first stroke in a session can still be slightly slower than later ones because some caches, threads and buffers are warmed lazily. Several first-use penalties have been reduced, but a completely cost-free first stroke is unrealistic in a raster painting system that initializes preview and replay resources on demand.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Practical advice"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "For best performance:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Keep brush size reasonable, paint with short strokes,\n- Avoid excessive smoothing unless needed,\n- Use lower sprinkle values for large brushes,\n- Reserve blur and smudge for smaller localized work,\n- Let the background replay worker catch up before saving or leaving darkroom if you painted a very long stroke.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "For best visual consistency:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Think of opacity as stroke strength,\n- Think of flow as overlap behavior,\n- Use the preview background colors to inspect edges and alpha,\n- Save the sidecar after important milestones if you are building complex painted layers.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This module is not designed for geometric retouching, source-target healing, or patch-based object removal. Use [retouch](./retouch.md) for that.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This module also does not replace masking-and-blending logic. It paints real pixels into a persistent layer. If you need parametric masks or drawn masks that remain fully procedural, use Ansel masking instead.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Because the layer is stored in a TIFF sidecar and replayed at full resolution, complex sessions can generate a non-trivial amount of CPU work and memory traffic compared with classic adjustment modules.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Summary"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The _drawing_ module is a raster painting layer system for darkroom. It converts stylus or mouse input into sampled dabs, paints them live into a process tile for immediate display, then replays finished strokes asynchronously into a full-resolution authoritative layer cache that is saved in a TIFF sidecar.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Its strengths are:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Direct painting,\n- Tablet-aware input mapping,\n- Persistent paint layers,\n- Scene-referred HDR-aware color painting,\n- Responsive live preview.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Its main limits are:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/drawing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Large-brush cost,\n- Slower blur and smudge modes,\n- Deferred full-resolution replay after strokes,\n- Blocking waits when pending work must be saved before leaving darkroom.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/local-contrast.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Local contrast"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/local-contrast.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Enhance the image's local contrast.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/local-contrast.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This is achieved using either a _local laplacian_ (default) or _unnormalized bilateral_ filter. Both modes work exclusively on the L channel from Lab. The _local laplacian_ filter has been designed to be robust against unwanted halo effects and gradient reversals along edges.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/local-contrast.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "mode\n: Choice of _local laplacian_ filter or _bilateral grid_. The following sections define the controls available for each of these modes.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/local-contrast.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Bilateral grid"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/local-contrast.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "coarseness\n: Adjust the coarseness of the details to be adjusted.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/local-contrast.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "contrast\n: Control how strongly the algorithm distinguishes between brightness levels. Increase this parameter for a more contrasty look.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/local-contrast.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "detail\n: Add or remove detail. Higher values increase local contrast.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/local-contrast.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Local laplacian"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/local-contrast.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "To understand the parameters of the local laplacian filter, one can think of it as applying a curve to the image, similar to the following graph:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/local-contrast.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "![local laplacian curve](local-laplacian-curve.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/local-contrast.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This curve is applied to the image in a way that works locally and avoids halo artifacts.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/local-contrast.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The local laplacian mode also supports shadow lifting and highlight compression.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/local-contrast.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "detail\n: Add or remove detail. Higher values increase local contrast. This inserts an S shaped element in the center of the curve, to increase or decrease local contrast.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/local-contrast.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "highlights\n: This affects one end of the S shaped contrast curve, effectively increasing or compressing contrast in the highlights. A low value pulls the highlights down.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/local-contrast.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "shadows\n: Similar to the highlights parameter, this affects the other end of the contrast curve, and increases or decreases contrast in the shadows. A higher value gives more contrast in the shadows. A lower value lifts the shadows and can effectively simulate a fill light. Note that this is done with local manipulation of the image. However, this means that a completely dark image cannot be brightened in this way -- only dark objects in front of bright objects are affected.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/local-contrast.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "mid-tone range\n: This controls the extent of the S shaped part of the contrast curve. A larger value makes the S wider, and thus classifies more values as mid-tones and fewer values as highlights and shadows. In higher dynamic range settings it can be useful to reduce this value to achieve stronger range compression, by lowering the contrast in the highlights and the shadows.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/local-contrast.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": This setting can cause banding artifacts in the image if pushed to extreme values. This is due to the way in which Ansel computes the fast approximation of the local laplacian filter.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/crop.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Crop"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/crop.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Crop an image using on-screen guides.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/crop.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This module appears late in the pipeline, meaning that the full image can remain available for source spots in the [_retouch_](./retouch.md) module. For best results, you are advised to use the [_rotate and perspective_](./rotate-perspective.md) module to perform rotation and perspective correction (if required), and then perform final creative cropping with this module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/crop.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Whenever this module is in focus, the full uncropped image will be shown, overlaid with crop handles and optional guiding lines.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/crop.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "![screen controls](screen-controls.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/crop.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Resize the crop by dragging the border and corner handles.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/crop.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Move the crop rectangle by clicking and dragging inside the crop area. Constrain movement to the horizontal/vertical axis by holding Ctrl/Shift, respectively while dragging. Commit changes by giving focus to another module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/crop.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The _crop_ module controls are split into two sections as follows:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/crop.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Crop settings"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/crop.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "aspect\n: Set the aspect ratio of the crop, constraining the width:height ratio of the crop rectangle to the chosen aspect. Many common numerical ratios are pre-defined. A few special aspect ratios deserve explanation:\n: - _freehand_: Crop without any restrictions\n: - _original image_: Retain the aspect ratio of the original image\n: - _square_: Constrains the aspect ratio to be 1:1\n: - _golden cut_: The golden ratio (1.62:1)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/crop.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": You can also enter any other ratio after opening the combobox by typing it in the form of “x:y” or as a decimal (e.g. \"0.5\" to apply a ratio of 2:1).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/crop.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": The button beside the aspect combobox allows you to switch between portrait and landscape orientation if you have selected a rectangular aspect ratio.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/crop.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": If you want to add an aspect ratio to the pre-defined drop-down list you can do this by including a line of the form \"`plugins/darkroom/clipping/extra_aspect_ratios/foo=x:y`\" in Ansel's configuration file `$HOME/.config/Ansel/Anselrc`. Here “`foo`” defines the name of the new aspect ratio and “`x`” and “`y`” the corresponding numerical values (`x` and `y` must be integers). Note that you can only add new entries for ratios not already present in the drop-down list.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/crop.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Note:** When resizing an image in freehand mode you may retain the currently-set aspect ratio by holding Shift while dragging on any of the resize controls.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/crop.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Margins"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/crop.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "These sliders allow you to directly set how much of the image to crop from each side. They are automatically updated if you move or resize the crop area on the image using the mouse.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/crop.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "As this section is rarely used, it is collapsed by default.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/crop.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "left\n: The percentage of the image that should be cropped from the left side.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/crop.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "right\n: The percentage of the image that should be cropped from the right side.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/crop.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "top\n: The percentage of the image that should be cropped from the top.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/crop.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "bottom\n: The percentage of the image that should be cropped from the bottom.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/crop.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Guides"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-perspective.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Rotate and perspective"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-perspective.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Automatically correct for converging lines, a form of perspective distortion. The underlying mechanism is inspired by Markus Hebel's [_ShiftN_](http://www.shiftn.de/) program. This module also allows for the rotation of the image to be adjusted.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-perspective.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Perspective distortions are a natural effect when projecting a three dimensional scene onto a two dimensional plane and cause objects close to the viewer to appear larger than objects further away. Converging lines are a special case of perspective distortions frequently seen in architectural photographs -- parallel lines, when photographed at an angle, are transformed into converging lines that meet at some vantage point within or outside of the image frame.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-perspective.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This module is able to correct converging lines by warping the image in such a way that the lines in question become parallel to the image frame. Corrections can be applied in a vertical and horizontal direction, either separately or in combination. In order to perform automatic correction the module is able to analyze the image for suitable structural features consisting of line segments. You can also set the line structures manually by drawing a \"perspective rectangle\" or drawing multiple horizontal and vertical lines onto the image. Based on these (automatic or manually-drawn) line segments a fitting procedure is initiated, which determines the best values for the module's parameters.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-perspective.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "As the most common use case for this module is for rotation, the perspective correction controls are hidden by default. Click the \"perspective\" header to expand the controls.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-perspective.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "While the module is active (and none of the structure buttons are selected) you can right-click and drag anywhere on the image to define a horizontal or vertical line. This will cause the rotation parameter to be automatically adjusted to make the drawn line horizontal/vertical with respect to the image frame.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-perspective.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Perspective correction workflow"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-perspective.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Structure"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-perspective.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The first step is to obtain details about the horizontal and/or vertical structures in the image. Three alternative methods are provided to do this:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-perspective.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Manually draw structure lines"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-perspective.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Click on the ![draw-structure-lines-icon](icon-draw-structure-lines.jpg) and then click on one of the \"fit\" icons to complete the process. You can re-enter this mode to edit your drawn lines at any time. Edit a line by clicking and dragging on the line or the end nodes, and right-click a line to delete it. Once you are happy with your changes, re-select a \"fit\" icon to complete the process.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-perspective.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Manually define a perspective rectangle"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-perspective.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Click on the ![draw-structure-rectangle-icon](icon-draw-structure-rectangle.jpg)icon to enable perspective rectangle drawing mode. This will draw a rectangle on the screen and you can grab and move the corners of the rectangle so that the left and right sides fall on lines you wish to make vertical, and the top and bottom fall on lines you wish to make horizontal. Once you are happy with your rectangle, click one of the \"fit\" icons to complete the process. You can re-enter this mode to edit your drawn rectangle at any time. Once you are happy with your changes, re-select a \"fit\" icon to complete the process.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-perspective.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Automatically detect structure"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-perspective.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Click the ![get-structure-icon](icon-get-structure.jpg) icon to analyze the image for structural elements -- Ansel will automatically detect and evaluate line elements. Shift+click to apply a contrast enhancement step before performing further analysis. Ctrl+click to apply an edge enhancement step before performing further analysis. Both variations can be used alone or in combination if the default analysis is not able to detect a sufficient number of lines.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-perspective.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Only lines that form a set of vertical or horizontal converging lines are used for subsequent processing steps. Line segments are displayed as overlays on the image canvas, with the type of line identified by color as follows:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-perspective.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "green\n: Vertical converging lines\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-perspective.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "red\n: Vertical lines that do not converge\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-perspective.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "blue\n: Horizontal converging lines\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-perspective.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "yellow\n: Horizontal lines that do not converge\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-perspective.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "gray\n: Other lines that are not of interest to this module\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-perspective.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Lines marked in red or yellow are regarded as outliers and are not taken into account during the automatic fitting step. This outlier elimination involves a statistical process using random sampling which means that each time you press the \"get structure\" button the color pattern of the lines will look slightly different.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-perspective.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "You can manually change the status of line segments: Left-Click on a line to select it (turn the color to green or blue) and Right-click to deselect it (turn the color to red or yellow). If you keep the mouse button pressed, you can use a sweeping action to select/deselect multiple lines in a row. The size of the select/deselect brush can be changed with the mouse wheel. Hold down the Shift key and keep the left or right mouse button pressed while dragging to select or deselect all lines in the chosen rectangular area.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-perspective.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Once you are happy with the detected lines, select a \"fit\" icon to complete the process.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-perspective.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Fit"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-perspective.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Once you are happy with the identified horizontal and vertical lines, using one of the methods above, click on one of the \"fit\" icons to automatically set the module's parameters based on the defined structure. The image and the overlaid lines are then displayed with perspective corrections applied.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-perspective.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "You may choose to automatically apply just the vertical corrections ![vertical-icon](icon-vertical.jpg). Ctrl+click on any of the icons to apply a rotation without the lens shift. Shift+click on any of the icons to apply the lens shift without any rotation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-perspective.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Rotate"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-perspective.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Once you are happy with the applied perspective corrections, you may wish to perform a final rotation correction either by adjusting the rotation parameter or right-clicking and dragging the image to define a horizontal/vertical line.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-perspective.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "rotation\n: Control the rotation of the image around its center to correct for a skewed horizon. To rotate by more than the default soft limit of ten degrees, right click and enter the desired value up to 180 degrees.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-perspective.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "automatic cropping\n: When activated, this feature crops the image to remove any black areas at the edges caused by the distortion correction. You can either crop to the \"largest area\", or to the largest rectangle that maintains the original aspect ratio (\"original format\"). In the latter case you can manually adjust the automatic cropping result by clicking in the clip region and moving it around. The size of the region is modified automatically to exclude any black areas.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-perspective.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "lens shift (horizontal)\n: Correct converging horizontal lines (i.e. to make the _blue_ lines parallel).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-perspective.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "lens shift (vertical)\n: Correct converging vertical lines (i.e. to make the _green_ lines parallel). In some cases you can obtain a more natural looking image if you correct vertical distortions to an 80 ~ 90% level rather than to the maximum extent. To do this, reduce the correction slider after having performed the automatic correction.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-perspective.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "shear\n: Shear the image along one of its diagonals. This is required when correcting vertical and horizontal perspective distortions simultaneously.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-perspective.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "guides\n: When activated, a grid is overlaid on the image to help you judge the quality of the correction.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-perspective.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "lens model\n: This parameter controls the lens focal length, camera crop factor and aspect ratio that used by the correction algorithm. If set to \"generic\" a lens focal length of 28mm on a 35mm full-frame camera is assumed. If set to “specific”, the focal length and crop factor can be set manually using the sliders provided.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-perspective.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "focal length\n: If the _lens model_ is set to \"specific\", set the lens focal length. The default value is taken from the image's Exif data, and can be overridden by adjusting the slider manually.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-perspective.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "crop factor\n: If the _lens model_ is set to \"specific\", set the camera crop factor. You will normally need to set this value manually.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-perspective.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "aspect adjust\n: If the _lens model_ is set to \"specific\", this parameter allows for a free manual adjustment of the image's aspect ratio.  This is useful for \"unsqueezing\" images taken with an anamorphic lens (which changes the ratio of image height to width).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-perspective.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "structure\n: Define horizontal and vertical lines in the image using a manual or automatic method (see workflow section for details).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-perspective.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "fit\n: Set the distortion correction sliders automatically based on the identified structure (see workflow section for details).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-perspective.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Here is an image with a skewed horizon and converging lines caused by directing the camera upwards:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-perspective.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "![prespective-correction-example-before](perspective-correction-example-before.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-perspective.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Here is the image after having corrected for vertical and horizontal perspective distortions using automatic structure detection. Note the framing adjustment made by the automatic cropping feature and the still-visible overlay of structural lines:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-perspective.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "![prespective-correction-example-after](perspective-correction-example-after.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Photographic grain"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Simulate photographic grain from a stochastic stack of silver-halide crystal layers.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This module implements a production version of the model presented in [Stochastic photographic grain synthesis from crystallographic structure simulation](https://eng.aurelienpierre.com/2023/07/stochastic-photographic-grain-synthesis-from-crystallographic-structure-simulation/). The goal is not to add generic noise, but to discretize the scene-referred image into finite crystal footprints that capture and deplete light through an emulsion stack.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Unlike simple additive noise, the module works with crystal-shaped grains, a per-layer filling ratio, and a remaining-light model. Each crystal captures a flat tone averaged over its own footprint, so image detail finer than the grain size is not preserved inside the grain.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This way of simulating photographic grain is inspired by physics but is not a 1:1 simulation. The sensitometry aspect was deliberately left since that part is covered by other modules already, and chemical effects of developers over the grain size has not been simulated.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< warning >}}\nAs with any pixel-level modification, the previews are accurate only for a 100% zoom (1:1), and provided in a best-effort manner for any other zoom level.\n{{< /warning >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "_Photographic grain_ is a scene-referred module and is meant to run before _filmic_ in the pixelpipe.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "That matters because the module simulates light being captured by crystals in the emulsion. It therefore belongs before display rendering and before output-referred tone mapping. Grain statistics are built from the scene-referred signal, then later modules shape the final display contrast as usual.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Reference model"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The emulsion is modeled as a stack of elementary crystal layers. For each layer:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Crystal seeds are placed stochastically from a target filling ratio,\n- Each accepted seed randomly picks one crystal footprint from a small precomputed bank,\n- The current light field is averaged over that grain footprint,\n- The captured amount is limited by the layer sensitivity,\n- That flat crystal tone is written to the output over the whole grain surface,\n- The same energy is subtracted from the remaining light field before deeper layers are processed.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The implementation keeps the physically important part of the article:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Light is split in depth through layers,\n- Grain is built from finite crystal footprints,\n- Capture is limited by the remaining available light,\n- The final stack is normalized for average exposure.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Crystal model"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Crystal footprints are regular polygons rasterized on the pixel grid with sub-pixel boundary coverage:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Average size is controlled by the _crystal average size_ slider,\n- Crystal size follows a log-normal distribution,\n- Polygon vertex count follows a gaussian distribution centered around six,\n- Orientation is randomized,\n- Grains with more than five sides are approximated as circles for speed,\n- Non-integer radii keep a partial-occlusion boundary instead of collapsing to a hard binary edge.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Each layer precomputes a bank of random crystal footprints, then accepted seeds randomly pick one bank entry. This avoids recomputing crystal geometry inside the hot pixel loop while keeping enough variation inside each layer.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/denoise-profiled.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Modes"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "B&W"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The image is reduced to one luminance-like scalar light field, one grain stack is synthesized from it, and the resulting luminance modulation is reapplied to RGB while preserving the original color ratios.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This is the closest mode to the scalar reference model and the most neutral starting point.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Color mode models film as sequential blue-, green-, then red-sensitive sub-stacks. The remaining-light field is updated in depth order, so deeper color-sensitive layers only see the light not already captured above them.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This is not a full chemical simulation of color negative film, but it is more physical than three independent parallel RGB noise syntheses. The geometry can also be partly shared across channels to control how correlated the grain structure is between color-sensitive layers.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "mode\n: Choose between one monochrome grain stack and one sequential color grain stack.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "filling\n: Surface ratio occupied by crystals in each layer. Ilford B&W film emulsions from the 1960's have been documented to have around 15% of the layer surface covered by silver crystals. A 25% filling ratio visually match better modern film stock.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": This is a per-layer crystal coverage target, not the final visible grain coverage after the full stack has been accumulated.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": Pushing this value to 100% essentially removes all grain.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "crystal average size\n: Average crystal footprint in pixels at 100% zoom.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": Below 100% zoom, the footprint follows the effective raster scale of the current processing grid. Above 100% zoom it is clamped, so zooming in does not invent larger grains.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": The silver halide crystal is the actual unit sensor of film emulsions, which means details finer than the grain size cannot be recorded. This parameter therefore affects both the coarseness of the grain and the sharpness of the image. \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "layers\n: Number of crystal layers stacked through the emulsion. In color mode, this is understood as the number of layers per channel.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": More layers generally average the result and reduce the visibility of individual grains. Fewer layers make the grain more abrupt and coarser.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": Real film emulsions have theoritically :\n: - around 14 crystal layers for B&W film using non-tabular grains, \n: - 14 crystal layers per color layer for color film using tabular grains,\n: - up to 83 layers for B&W film using tabular grains (Kodak T-Max, Iford Delta).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "crystal size variability\n: Log-normal standard deviation of crystal sizes.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": At `0`, grain size stays very regular. Increasing this produces a broader size distribution and a less synthetic texture.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "layer sensitivity\n: Per-layer capture bias in EV.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": `0 EV` is the neutral setting. It means one layer captures its nominal `1 / layers` share once normalized by the average rasterized grain surface. Positive values make each layer capture more energy. Negative values make each layer capture less.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "inter-channel grain correlation\n: In color mode only.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": Probability that blue-, green- and red-sensitive sub-layers reuse the same crystal births and shapes at matching depths. Lower values give more independent chromatic grain. Higher values produce more shared, film-like grain structure.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "grain colorfulness\n: In color mode only.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": Scales only the chromatic amplitude of the RGB grain residual while keeping its achromatic grain strength unchanged. Lower values mute fluorescent color speckle without desaturating the underlying image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The module ships with two built-in presets:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- _color grain_: filling `85%`, crystal average size `4 px`, `30` layers, layer sensitivity `0 EV`, inter-channel grain correlation `67%`, grain colorfulness `67%`, crystal size variability `0.25`.\n- _B&W grain_: filling `25%`, crystal average size `4 px`, `30` layers, layer sensitivity `0 EV`, crystal size variability `0.25`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "These are meant as practical starting points rather than measured film stocks.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Practical workflow"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Start in this order:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. Choose _mode_.\n2. Set _crystal average size_ to establish the grain scale.\n3. Tune _filling_ to control how much of each layer is occupied by crystals.\n4. Adjust _layers_ and _layer sensitivity_ to control how much energy the stack captures.\n5. Add _crystal size variability_ if the structure feels too regular.\n6. In color mode, reduce _grain colorfulness_ or increase _inter-channel grain correlation_ if the grain feels too fluorescent.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- If the grain is too strong, reduce _filling_ or _layer sensitivity_, or increase _layers_,\n- If the grain is too fine, increase _crystal average size_,\n- If the pattern is too regular, increase _crystal size variability_,\n- If color grain looks synthetic, lower _grain colorfulness_ first, then increase _inter-channel grain correlation_ if needed.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Preview, zoom and thumbnails"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The user-facing grain size is defined relative to the 100% image view, but the implementation compensates for the actual processing grid:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Zooming in above 100% does not enlarge grains beyond their 100% reference size,\n- Zooming out adapts the grain to the current raster scale,\n- Reduced mipmap inputs, such as the thumbnail pipeline, are compensated so grain size stays closer to the darkroom reference instead of becoming artificially coarse.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Critical tuning should still be judged near the final viewing scale.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Masking and blending"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This module supports Ansel masks and blending operators.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "That lets you:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Apply different grain sizes or fillings to different regions,\n- Keep faces and skin cleaner than backgrounds,\n- Stack several instances for more complex emulsions,\n- Reduce the visible contribution of one instance with opacity while preserving its internal grain statistics.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This module is significantly more expensive than synthetic additive noise because it simulates many layers of crystal capture.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Processing cost mainly increases with:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Larger crystal average size,\n- More layers,\n- Larger output images.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The module supports OpenCL. CPU and OpenCL both use the same light-depletion model, but performance still depends strongly on image size and grain settings.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/photographic-grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- The result is intentionally stochastic. Two parameter sets with similar average strength may still produce different local clustering.\n- The module simulates grain structure, not full film sensitometry or print chemistry.\n- Color mode is not a complete color-film emulsion simulator, but a sequential color-sensitive grain model built on the same remaining-light framework.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-zones.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Color zones"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-zones.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Selectively adjust the lightness, saturation and hue of pixels based on their current lightness, saturation and hue.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-zones.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This module works in CIE LCh color space, which separates pixels into _lightness_, chroma (_saturation_) and _hue_ components. It allows you to manipulate the lightness, saturation and hue of targeted groups of pixels through the use of curves.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-zones.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "You first need to choose whether you wish to adjust (select) pixels based on their lightness, saturation or hue. You can then use three curves, on their respective tabs, to adjust the lightness, saturation and hue of ranges of pixels selected via this method.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-zones.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "_**Note:** This module should be used with care, as transitions between colors may not be graceful. Instead, use the [color balance rgb](./color-balance.md) module with a [parametric mask](../masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md)._\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-zones.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Pixel selection method"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-zones.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The _color zones_ module offers three different methods for selecting which pixels you want to adjust. They are:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-zones.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "select by hue (default)\n: Select pixels to manipulate based on their hue. For example, you may want to darken a blue sky, or to change a red porche into a yellow one. The following image shows the full range of hues that you can choose to operate on:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-zones.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": ![color zones choose hue](color-zones-choose-hue.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-zones.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "select by lightness\n: Select pixels to manipulate based on their lightness. For example, you may want to make your shadows brighter, or to make your highlights more yellow. The following image shows the range of lightness levels that you can choose to operate on, from dark to light:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-zones.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": ![color zones choose lightness](color-zones-choose-lightness.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-zones.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "select by saturation\n: Select pixels to manipulate based on their saturation. For example, you may want to tone down the saturation of some already highly saturated pixels, or to change their hue. The following image shows the range of saturation levels that you can choose to operate on, from a completely unsaturated monochrome gray through to the most highly saturated color:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-zones.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": ![color zones choose saturation](color-zones-choose-saturation.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-zones.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Pixel manipulation curves"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-zones.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Once you have chosen a pixel selection method, the selected range of lightness, saturation or hue levels will appear along the horizontal axis of the three pixel manipulation curves, which can be viewed and adjusted by choosing the appropriate tab (lightness, saturation, hue).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-zones.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If, for example, you were to choose to _select by hue_ (the default), the horizontal axis (below the manipulation curves) would show the range of hues you can work with, and the three pixel manipulation curves would appears as follows:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-zones.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "lightness\n: By adjusting the lightness curve up or down in a given (hue) location, you can brighten or darken pixels matching hues where the curve has been raised or lowered, respectively. In the example below the blue sky in an image has been darkened for dramatic effect:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-zones.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": ![color zones adjust lightness](color-zones-adjust-lightness.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-zones.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "saturation\n: By adjusting the saturation curve up or down in a given (hue) location, you can desaturate (make less colorful) or resaturate (make more colorful) pixels matching hues where the curve has been raised or lowered, respectively. In the example below, a red object in the background has been desaturated so it is less of a distraction to the main subject of the photo:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-zones.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": ![color zones adjust saturation](color-zones-adjust-saturation.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-zones.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "hue\n: By adjusting the hue curve up or down in a given (hue) location, you can shift the hue of pixels matching hues where the curve has been raised or lowered, allowing you to replace one color with another. In the example below, a pink toy in an image has been changed to blue:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-zones.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": ![color zones adjust hue](color-zones-adjust-hue.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-zones.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The curves work similarly in the lightness- and saturation-based selection modes as well.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-zones.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Note that these examples are somewhat contrived in order to illustrate the module's usage. In practical use, they would likely need to be combined with [drawn](..//masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md) and/or [parametric](../masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md) masks to further isolate their effect.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-zones.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Range selection"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-zones.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When adjusting the pixel manipulation curves, it can sometimes be difficult to judge exactly where on the horizontal axis pixels will fall. To the right of the tab controls are a pair of color pickers that can be used to assist with this.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-zones.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If you click the left-hand color picker and choose a pixel in the image, you will see a dark vertical line showing where that pixel falls on the horizontal axis. If you Ctrl+click or right-click on the same color picker you can choose a rectangular area from the image -- the range of values represented within the selected rectangle will be shaded vertically, with a similar dark line showing the median value.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-zones.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If you click on the right-hand color picker, you can similarly choose a rectangular area on the image and the display will be shown as described above (a shaded area with a dark vertical line). However, in this case the color picker will also automatically add some control points to the curve for you, representing the highlighted range (see below). Simply drag on the center node to raise or lower the curve within the selected range. Alternatively, hold Ctrl while selecting a range to automatically create a positive curve (push up the selected range) or hold Shift while selecting to create a negative curve (pushed down).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-zones.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "![color zones overview](color-zones-overview.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-zones.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The following controls are available in the _color zones_ module:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-zones.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "lightness, saturation & hue tabs\n: Each tab displays a pixel manipulation curve to allow you to alter “lightness”, “saturation”, or “hue” based on the pixel selection method.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-zones.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "edit by area\n: Choose how to interact with the curve. This setting is disabled by default, allowing the control points for the curve to be freely placed. Check the box to fall back to the legacy \"edit by area\" mode, which functions in a similar way to the spline curve controls used in wavelet modules.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-zones.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid " ![mask-icon](icon-mask.jpg) mask display\n: Enable the _mask display_ to highlight pixels that have been affected by _color zones_ adjustments in yellow.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-zones.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "select by\n: Define the horizontal axis (the range of values to work on). You can choose between “lightness”, “saturation”, and “hue” (the default). Changing this parameter resets all pixel manipulation curves to their default state (horizontal straight lines). If you want to work on multiple ranges, you need to create additional instances of the module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-zones.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "process mode\n: Choose between a “smooth” (default) or “strong” processing mode. The default mode is less likely to cause artifacts.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-zones.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "mix\n: Use this parameter to tune the strength of the overall effect.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-zones.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "interpolation method\n: Define how the curve is interpolated using the user-defined control points.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "AI raw denoise"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Removes sensor noise from the raw mosaic with a neural network, **before\nwhite balance, chromatic-aberration correction and demosaicing** — so every\nlater stage of the pipeline inherits clean data, and demosaicing interpolates\nreal detail instead of weaving noise into maze and zipper artifacts.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Unlike general-purpose AI denoisers, these models are trained **specifically\non non-demosaiced RAW sensor data, for the exact point of the Ansel pipeline\nwhere the module runs**, using Ansel's per-camera noise profiles to\nsynthesize physically accurate Poisson-Gaussian noise. One set of weights\ncovers every camera Ansel has a noise profile for, **Bayer and X-Trans\nalike** — a newly profiled camera is supported without retraining. The\nmodels weigh **between 7 and 36 MB** each and run **entirely locally**, on\nCPU (OpenMP) or GPU (OpenCL); nothing ever leaves your machine.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The training corpus is a public collection of base-ISO raw tiles. **You can\ncontribute your own pictures to help train better models** — see\n[contributing training data](https://ansel.photos/en/contribute/training-data/):\nthe whole workflow takes about ten minutes and requires no machine-learning\nknowledge.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "strength\n: Opacity of the noise removal: blends between the original image (0 %) and\n  the fully denoised result (100 %). Lower it to keep some residual grain.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "model version\n: Pins the trained network a history entry uses, so edits keep rendering\n  identically across application updates.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "model size\n: Network width. **large** is the reference quality, practical on GPU;\n  **half** is roughly four times cheaper; **quarter** is four times cheaper\n  again, for weak hardware or near-realtime editing.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "  The default depends on your machine, because enable-and-see should never\n  freeze a computer: **half** when OpenCL is available, **quarter** when\n  Ansel runs on the CPU alone. Both default to *multiscale*. Changing the\n  default only affects new edits — existing ones keep the model they were\n  made with.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "model variant\n: **single-scale** runs the full-resolution denoising pass only — fast, but\n  no dedicated handling of low-frequency chroma noise. **multiscale** adds a\n  coarse chroma pass on a downscaled image plus a low-band fusion that, on\n  smooth areas, pins the largest scales to the sensor's own averaged\n  measurement. How much that is worth depends on the size: it matters most\n  for the **quarter** and **half** networks, which lack the capacity to\n  handle low-frequency chroma alone, and very little at **large** size —\n  see [model quality](#model-quality-and-cost) below.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "custom model\n: Runs a network of your own instead of the shipped ones — see\n  [using your own model](#using-your-own-model) below. Leave it on\n  *(shipped model)* unless you have trained one.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "noise profile correction"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The per-pixel noise amplitude fed to the network comes from the camera's\nnoise profile at the image ISO. The feedback line under the section header\nshows which profile was matched (a generic profile is used for unprofiled\ncameras). The shipped profiles were measured on demosaiced data and\nsystematically understate the true sensor noise; the correction is carried\nentirely by the controls below — what the sliders show is exactly what\nmultiplies the profile's noise amplitude, nothing is applied behind the\nscenes.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "global correction\n: Scales the assumed noise amplitude for all channels (100 % trusts the\n  sliders as-is). Raise it if noise remains, lower it if fine detail is\n  eaten.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "red / green / blue correction\n: The per-channel correction factors (defaults 282 %, 394 %, 296 %). They\n  are calibrated against raw-mosaic noise measurements over 253 cameras;\n  the demosaicing loss the profiles suffered is channel-dependent\n  (strongest on the dense green lattice) and varies somewhat between camera\n  models, so per-image adjustment can pay off at very high ISO.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Cameras whose noise profile needs the correction removed"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel's noise profiles are **community-contributed** and were measured\nover many years with varying rigour. For some camera families the values\nare not consistent with the rest of the database, and the calibrated\ndefaults above — which assume a consistent profile — then over-state the\nnoise enough that the network produces a **large hue and saturation\nshift** over the whole image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Identified so far: **entry-level Canon DSLRs** and **Panasonic Micro Four\nThirds** bodies. The list is not exhaustive; the symptom is the reliable\nsignal.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Very high ISO is a second, separate case, on any camera.** A profile\ncovers the ISO settings that were actually measured, and above the highest\none the last measurement is simply reused. Of the 433 profiled cameras,\nthe median stops at **ISO 25 000** and only a quarter reach 51 200 — so\npast roughly ISO 64 000 most bodies are running on a value measured far\nbelow, describing a sensor regime that no longer applies.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The error there can point either way, so read the symptom before reaching\nfor a slider: grain left behind means the assumed noise is too low (raise\n*global correction*), while a tint means the balance *between* channels is\noff (trim the offending channel). Unlike the camera families above, this is\na property of those frames, not of the body — correct the high-ISO images,\nnot the camera.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**The fix, for the camera families above:** set **red, green and blue\ncorrection back to 100 %** — or lower if a cast remains. That removes the\ncalibration bump entirely and feeds the network the profile's own values.\nSave it as a [preset](../../../preferences-settings/presets.md)\nauto-applied to that camera model and you will not have to think about it\nagain.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This cannot be corrected centrally. Those profiles are legacy data shared\nwith [_denoise (profiled)_](./denoise-profiled.md) and the rest of the\nnoise-aware modules: rewriting them would silently change how existing\nedits render everywhere else in Ansel, which is a worse outcome than a\nslider you set once per camera.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Using your own model"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The module is not tied to the networks Ansel ships. Any `.anselnn` file placed\nin your Ansel configuration directory appears in the **custom model** menu and\nis used instead of the shipped matrix.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The configuration directory is:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| system | path |\n| ------ | ---- |\n| Linux | `~/.config/ansel/` |\n| macOS | `~/.config/ansel/` |\n| Windows | `%LOCALAPPDATA%\\ansel\\` |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Drop the file in, reopen the module, and it is listed by filename. Nothing\nneeds restarting and nothing needs installing system-wide. While a custom\nmodel is selected, *model version*, *model size* and *model variant* are\ngreyed out: they describe the shipped matrix, and no longer say anything about\nwhat is running.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Your edit records the file's name, not its position in the menu.** Add or\nremove other models and your existing edits keep pointing at the same network.\nThe trade-off is that if you delete or rename a file an edit refers to, that\nedit stops denoising rather than silently switching to another model — the\nnetworks are not interchangeable, and quietly substituting one would change\nyour picture without telling you. The menu keeps showing the missing name so\nyou can see what happened; put the file back, or pick another model.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Using a filename that matches a shipped model — `denoise-half-multi-v1.anselnn`\nand friends — *replaces* that shipped model instead of adding an entry. That is\nthe way to A/B a retrained network against the one it is meant to supersede\nwithout touching any edit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Training one"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Training happens in the companion repository,\n[ansel-denoise](https://github.com/aurelienpierreeng/ansel-denoise), which\nholds the corpus tooling, the training loop and the exporter. Its README is\nthe reference; the shape of it is:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (sh)
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "# 1. get the training corpus (public, base-ISO raw tiles)\n./scripts/fetch_shards.sh\n\n# 2. train — this is the long part, hours to days depending on the GPU\npython3 -m ansel_denoise.train --shards shards/train-all --out runs/mine \\\n    --arch unet-ms --base 32 --depth 4 --steps 200000\n\n# 3. export the checkpoint to the format Ansel loads\npython3 -m ansel_denoise.export runs/mine/ckpt-best.pt --out mine.anselnn\n\n# 4. install it\ncp mine.anselnn ~/.config/ansel/\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`--arch unet` trains a single-scale network, `--arch unet-ms` the multiscale\none; `--base` sets the width (32 is *large*, 16 *half*, 8 *quarter*). The\nexporter writes the architecture, widths and the training-code revision into\nthe file, so Ansel configures itself from the model rather than from your\nmenu selection — a file trained at any width and either architecture loads\nwithout further ceremony.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "You do not need a GPU cluster to experiment, but you do need patience: the\nshipped models are 200 000 steps each. A shorter run produces a working model\nthat simply denoises less well.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Contributing pictures is far cheaper than training.** The corpus is what\nlimits quality, and adding your camera to it helps every user of every model —\nsee [contributing training data](https://ansel.photos/en/contribute/training-data/).\nAbout ten minutes, no machine-learning knowledge required.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "What a model must satisfy"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel validates the file when it loads and refuses anything it cannot run, so\na wrong file is a message, not a crash. The contract is:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- the `ANSELDN1` container written by `ansel_denoise.export` — not a raw\n  PyTorch checkpoint, not ONNX;\n- one of the two architectures the executor implements, `unet` or `unet-ms`;\n- trained on the **raw mosaic** in Ansel's post-`raw settings` domain\n  (black-subtracted, normalised), conditioned on the same per-pixel sigma map\n  the module builds from the camera's noise profile.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "That last point is the one that bites: a network trained on demosaiced RGB, or\non a different noise parameterisation, will load and run and produce\nnonsense — the file format cannot detect a semantic mismatch. Start from the\ntraining repository's own configuration rather than adapting a general-purpose\ndenoiser.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A multiscale model additionally declares an `anchor` scale and is fused\nagainst the sensor's own binned measurement at 16/32/64 px; that fusion\nexpects a network trained with the DC-ownership loss the repository uses. A\nmultiscale file trained some other way will show drift in deep shadows.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Model quality and cost"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Quality is measured as **PSNR gain** (peak signal-to-noise ratio, in\ndecibels): PSNR is the log-scale ratio between the maximum signal and the\nresidual error against a known clean reference. Absolute PSNR mostly tracks\nthe ISO (noisier input, lower numbers everywhere) and says little about the\nreconstruction itself, so the tables report the **gain over the noisy\ninput** — how far, from the noisy capture toward the clean target, the\ndenoiser actually took the image. **Higher is better**; +3 dB halves the\nresidual error energy. Measured on held-out cameras with physically\ncalibrated synthetic noise, ISO 3200–51200 — cameras the models never saw\nin training.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| PSNR gain (dB), all ISO | single-scale | multiscale |\n| ----------------------- | ------------ | ---------- |\n| large                   | +10.6        | +10.5      |\n| half                    | +9.9         | +9.9       |\n| quarter                 | +8.2         | +8.4       |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| PSNR gain (dB), ISO > 12000 | single-scale | multiscale |\n| --------------------------- | ------------ | ---------- |\n| large                       | +12.2        | +12.1      |\n| half                        | +11.5        | +11.5      |\n| quarter                     | +9.5         | +9.7       |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**PSNR barely separates the two variants**, because it averages over the\nwhole image while their difference lives in *low-frequency chroma*: broad\ncoloured blotches that account for little error energy but are very\nvisible. A metric that isolates them — residual chroma after binning\n16×16 — shows what the tables above cannot, and it depends strongly on the\nmodel size:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| multiscale's chroma advantage | all ISO | ISO > 12000 |\n| ----------------------------- | ------- | ----------- |\n| large                         | 0.2 dB  | 0.3 dB      |\n| half                          | 1.3 dB  | 1.6 dB      |\n| quarter                       | 2.6 dB  | 2.9 dB      |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**The smaller the network, the more the coarse chroma pass is worth.** A\nlarge network has enough capacity to handle low-frequency chroma on its\nown, so multiscale buys it almost nothing; a quarter-size one does not, and\nthe coarse pass is what keeps it free of blotches. Practically: **at large\nsize prefer single-scale** — it is marginally ahead on PSNR and cheaper\n(×3.5 against ×3.9 on CPU, ×3.8 against ×5.0 on GPU). **At half and\nespecially quarter size, prefer multiscale** whenever the image is noisy\nenough to blotch: it costs 20–40 % more at half size and 50–75 % more at\nquarter size, and it is what those sizes need to stay clean.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "X-Trans sensors and small models"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The tables above are averages over many cameras. On **X-Trans** sensors (Fujifilm) the smaller\nmodels have one specific weakness they do not have on Bayer sensors: they leave a faint regular\npattern, aligned with the sensor's 6×6 colour filter array, visible in smooth areas at high\nmagnification.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "It scales strongly with model size. Measured on a Fujifilm X100F frame, as the strength of\nsensor-aligned structure in a flat area relative to the same frame rendered without denoising:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| model   | residual sensor pattern |\n| ------- | ----------------------- |\n| quarter | ≈ 970×                  |\n| half    | ≈ 84×                   |\n| large   | ≈ 16×                   |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This is a limit of what a small network can learn, not a defect in the CPU or GPU code — the two\ndevices agree to three significant figures, and the effect falls by a factor of sixty purely by\ngiving the network more parameters. An X-Trans array has 36 distinct site types to treat\nconsistently against a Bayer array's four, and a small network does that less well.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**On X-Trans, prefer `large` when the processing time is acceptable, and `half` as the sensible\ncompromise.** On Bayer sensors the smaller models are much better behaved and the advice in the\nprevious section applies unchanged.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If you see a *strong* grid rather than a faint one — coarse, or clearly tied to rectangular\ntiles — that is a different problem and is not about model size; see [when the GPU result differs\nfrom the CPU result](../../../preferences-settings/performance/opencl/problems-solutions.md).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Compared to *denoise (profiled)*"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel's classical [_denoise (profiled)_](./denoise-profiled.md) module solves\nthe same problem with hand-designed filters (non-local means or wavelets),\nafter demosaicing. The two were measured **through the same pipeline**: a\nreal base-ISO photograph is the reference, physically calibrated noise for\na target ISO is injected into its raw mosaic, and the result is rendered\nwith one module or the other — so the numbers include everything the two\napproaches do differently, not just the denoising step.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The classical module was given every advantage: its algorithm, colour mode\nand strength were **swept per picture and per ISO, and only its best run\ncounted** — a setting you can only find if you already have the clean\nimage. The AI models ran at their shipped defaults.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| PSNR gain (dB) | ISO 3200 | ISO 12800 |\n| -------------- | -------- | --------- |\n| denoise (profiled), default settings | +3.4 | +3.5 |\n| denoise (profiled), **best per-image settings** | +8.8 | +12.0 |\n| AI, quarter single-scale | +7.3 | +9.9 |\n| AI, quarter multiscale | +7.9 | +10.5 |\n| AI, half single-scale (the default) | +9.1 | +12.4 |\n| AI, half multiscale | +9.3 | +13.0 |\n| AI, large multiscale | +9.7 | +13.5 |\n| AI, large single-scale | +9.8 | +13.7 |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Read the second row with caution — it is not a setting you can dial in.**\nThose numbers took an exhaustive parametric sweep: 21 combinations of\nalgorithm, colour mode and strength, re-rendered for every picture and\nevery ISO, scored against a clean reference that only exists because the\nnoise was synthetic. And the winner moved from picture to picture — strength\nbetween 100 % and 200 %, and on one image wavelets in RGB beat non-local\nmeans in Y0U0V0, which won everywhere else. There is no single \"good\"\nconfiguration to recommend, and on a real photograph you have no clean\nreference to score against: you are tuning by eye, on the same image whose\ncorrect appearance you are trying to recover.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Its **default** settings — the realistic case — land 5 to 8 dB lower,\nmostly because the shipped noise profiles understate the true sensor noise.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "So the fair summary depends on the size you run. **Half and large beat the\nbest the classical module can be made to do** — by 0.3 to 1.0 dB at ISO\n3200 and 0.4 to 1.8 dB at 12800 — and they do it with no tuning at all,\nidentically on every picture tested. **The quarter models do not**: a\nperfectly tuned *denoise (profiled)* is 0.9 to 2.1 dB ahead of them. That\nis the honest price of a network eight times smaller, and it is worth\nknowing before choosing that size — though against the classical module's\n*realistic* settings, quarter is still 4 to 7 dB ahead.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The classical module also remains useful when you want manual control over\nthe trade-off, or on images the models handle poorly.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Both measurements are reproducible from the\n[training repository](https://github.com/aurelienpierreeng/ansel-denoise):\n`scripts/compare_denoisers.py` writes the synthetic raws and drives Ansel\nitself, `scripts/speckle_bench.py` produces the model tables above, and\n`scripts/report_doc_tables.py` re-derives every number on this page from\nthe committed results.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Processing cost"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Every denoising option on one scale, ×1 being the **half-size,\nsingle-scale** model — the default. Measured on a 24 Mpx raw with Ansel's\nown per-module timer, keeping the fastest of three exports:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| relative cost                              | CPU   | GPU   |\n| ------------------------------------------ | ----- | ----- |\n| AI, large multiscale                       | ×3.9  | ×5.0  |\n| AI, large single-scale                     | ×3.5  | ×3.8  |\n| AI, half multiscale                        | ×1.2  | ×1.4  |\n| **AI, half single-scale** (the default)    | **×1**| **×1**|\n| AI, quarter multiscale                     | ×0.5  | ×0.5  |\n| AI, quarter single-scale                   | ×0.34 | ×0.29 |\n| *denoise (profiled)*, non-local means      | ×0.5  | ×0.9  |\n| *denoise (profiled)*, wavelets             | ×0.13 | ×0.2  |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The classical module in **wavelet** mode is the cheapest thing here by a\nwide margin: about eight times below the default, and still two to three\ntimes below even the quarter-size networks. Its **non-local-means** mode —\nthe setting the quality sweep usually preferred — is a different matter. It\ncosts about half the default on CPU, roughly what quarter multiscale costs,\nand on GPU it lands near the default itself: nearly twice quarter\nmultiscale and three times quarter single-scale. So on a GPU the classical\nmodule's *good* mode is no longer the cheap choice; a quarter-size network\nruns faster than it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/ai-raw-denoise.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Two caveats on the GPU column. The differences there are *steeper* than on\nCPU, not flatter: the models were tiled to fit a 4 GB card, and tiling\noverlap costs proportionally more for the wider networks — on a card with\nmore memory the large models close some of that gap. And CPU-to-GPU\ncomparisons are deliberately absent, because that ratio depends entirely on\nwhich CPU and which GPU. As a rough order of magnitude, a mid-range\ndiscrete GPU runs the large model faster than a desktop CPU runs the half\nmodel; on laptops without a discrete GPU, prefer the half or quarter size.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Diffuse or sharpen"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Diffusion is a family of physical processes by which particles move and spread gradually with time, from a source that generates them. In image processing, diffusion mostly occurs in two places:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Diffusion of photons through lens glass (blur) or humid air (hazing),\n- Diffusion of pigments in wet inks or watercolors.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In both cases, diffusion makes the image less sharp by \"leaking\" particles and smoothing local variations.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The _diffuse or sharpen_ module uses a generalized physical model to describe several kinds of diffusion, and can be used by image makers to either simulate or revert diffusion processes.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "As it is a highly technical module, several presets are provided to demonstrate its use for various purposes.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Diffusion can be removed in order to:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Recover the original image from sensors with an anti-aliasing filter or mitigate the blur created by most demosaicing algorithms (use one of the _sharpen demosaicing_ presets and move the module before the _input color profile_ module in the pipeline),\n- Reverse static lens blurring/defocusing (use one of the _lens deblur_ presets),\n- Remove atmospheric haze (use the _dehaze_ preset),\n- Add extra acutance for better legibility (use the _local contrast_ preset).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Note that motion blurs cannot be reverted by undoing the diffusion process, as they are not diffusive in nature.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Diffusion can be added in order to:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Create a bloom or Orton effect (use the _bloom_ preset),\n- Inpaint missing or damaged parts of an image (use the _inpaint highlights_ preset),\n- Denoise in an edge-preserving way (use one of the _denoise_ presets)\n- Apply a surface blur (use the _surface blur_ preset).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Since the process is physical, even its glitches may be used for creative purposes. For example, you can:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Simulate line drawing or watercolor (use the _simulate line drawing_ and _simulate watercolor_ presets),\n- Create random patterns and textures by increasing noise (over time, with iterations, noise will connect with neighbours to create random specks).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Note:** This module is highly resource-intensive, as it is actually an anisotropic, multiscale, partial differential equation solver. The module's runtime increases with the number of iterations and OpenCL is therefore strongly recommended. Some \"fast\" presets are also provided for use on systems without OpenCL.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Concepts"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Time"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Diffusion is a time-dependent process: the more time it has, the further the particles can spread. In this module, time is simulated using the number of iterations (the number of times the algorithm runs on top of itself). More iterations can make reconstruction (deblurring, denoising, dehazing) more accurate if properly set, but can also cause it to degenerate.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Direction"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Natural diffusion usually takes place from points with a high potential (high energy or high concentration of particles) to those with a low potential (low energy or low concentration of particles). In an image, this means that diffusion always occurs from the brightest pixels to the darkest.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This particular implementation can simulate natural diffusion, using what is called an isotropic diffusion (all directions have the same weight, like heat diffusion), but can also force a weighted direction parallel to the gradients (forcing diffusion across object edges and creating ghost edges), or a weighted direction perpendicular to the gradients, called isophote (forcing diffusion to be contained inside edges, like in a droplet of watercolor). The relative weight of each direction (gradient and isophote) is user-defined and can be found in the [_direction_](#direction-1) section of the module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Speed"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Depending how fluid the environment is, particles can move more or less freely and therefore more or less fast. The speed of diffusion can be set in the [_speed_](#speed-sharpen--diffuse) section of the module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When performing reconstruction (denoising, deblurring, dehazing), it is advisable to use smaller speeds for better accuracy. This prevents numerical overshoots (and therefore degeneration of the solution) and may require more iterations. For small numbers of iterations, higher speeds may be used. Note that large blurs need many iterations for proper reconstruction, so the speed should be adjusted to avoid degenerating the solution.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "All speeds are added (first to fourth orders), and the sums \"`first order + second order`\" and \"`third order + fourth order`\" should never exceed ±100%, unless you want to produce [glitch art](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glitch_art).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Scale"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Natural diffusion is supposed to happen only to the closest neighbouring coordinates. That is, at each iteration, each pixel should only interact with its 9 nearest neighours.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Here, we fast-track things a bit to save time and reuse the multi-scale wavelets scheme from the [_contrast equalizer_](./contrast-equalizer.md) module, so that we can diffuse at different scales. The maximal scale of diffusion is defined by the _radius span_ parameter.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Regardless of the diffusion, a _sharpness_ parameter allows you to increase or decrease the details at each scale, much like the spline controls of the _contrast equalizer_. Along with the _edge sensitivity_ slider, this provides the same features as the _contrast equalizer_ module (_luma_ and _edges_ tabs) but in a scene-referred RGB space.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Properties"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "iterations\n: The number of times the algorithm should run on top of itself. High values slow the module down but allow more accurate reconstructions, provided that the diffusion speeds are low enough.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "central radius\n: The main scale of the diffusion. Zero causes the diffusion to act more heavily on fine details (used for deblurring and denoising). Non-zero values define the size of details to be heavily diffused (used to increase local contrast).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "radius span\n: This allows you to select the band of details radii to act on, around the central radius. The span of diffusion defines a range of detail scales (between `center - span` and `center + span`) within which the diffusion is confined. High values diffuse on a large band of radii, at the expense of computation time. Low values diffuse closer around the central radius. If you plan to deblur, the radius span should be approximately the width of your lens blur and the central radius should be zero. If you plan to increase the local contrast, but don't want to affect sharpness or noise, the radius span should be 3/4 of your central radius maximum.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The radii are expressed in pixels of the full-resolution image, so copy+pasting settings between images of different resolution may lead to slightly different results, except for pixel-level sharpness.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "For electrical engineers, what is set here is a band-pass filter in wavelets space, using a gaussian frequential window centered on `central radius` with a fall-off (standard deviation) of `radius span`. Wavelet scales are analogous to harmonic frequencies and each wavelet scale defines the radius of the details to act on.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Speed (sharpen ↔ diffuse)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In the following controls, positive values apply diffusion, negative values undo diffusion (i.e. sharpen) and zero does nothing.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1st order speed (gradient)\n: The speed of diffusion of the low-frequency wavelet layers in the direction defined by the _1st order anisotropy_ setting.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "2nd order speed (laplacian)\n: The speed of diffusion of the low-frequency wavelet layers in the direction defined by the _2nd order anisotropy_ setting.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "3rd order speed (gradient of laplacian)\n: The speed of diffusion of the high-frequency wavelet layers in the direction defined by the _3rd order anisotropy_ setting.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "4th order speed (laplacian of laplacian)\n: The speed of diffusion of the high-frequency wavelet layers in the direction defined by the _4th order anisotropy_ setting.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In the following controls, positive values cause diffusion to avoid edges (isophotes), negative values make diffusion follow gradients more closely, and zero affects both equally (isotropic).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1st order anisotropy\n: The direction of diffusion of the low-frequency wavelet layers relative to the orientation of the gradient of the low-frequency (_1st order speed_ setting).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "2nd order anisotropy\n: The direction of diffusion of the low-frequency wavelet layers relative to the orientation of the gradient of the high-frequency (_2nd order speed_ setting).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "3rd order anisotropy\n: The direction of diffusion of the high-frequency wavelet layers relative to the orientation of the gradient of the low-frequency (_3rd order speed_ setting).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "4rd order anisotropy\n: The direction of diffusion of the high-frequency wavelet layers relative to the orientation of the gradient of the high-frequency (_4th order speed_ setting).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Edge management"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "sharpness\n: Apply a gain on wavelet details, regardless of properties set above. Zero does nothing, positive values sharpen, negative values blur. This is mostly useful as an adjustment variable when blooming or blurring, to retain some sharpness while adding a glow around edges. You are not advised to use this for sharpening alone, since there is nothing to prevent halos or fringes with this setting.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "edge sensitivity\n: Apply a penalty over the diffusion speeds when edges are detected. This detection uses the local variance around each pixel. Zero disables the penalty, higher values make the penalty stronger and more sensitive to edges. Increase if you notice edge artifacts like fringes and halos.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "edge threshold\n: Define a variance threshold, which affects mostly low-variance areas (dark or blurry areas, or flat surfaces). Positive values will increase the penalty for low-variance areas, which is good for sharpening or increasing local contrast without crushing blacks. Negative values will decrease the penalty for low-variance areas, which is good for denoising or blurring with a maximal effect on black and blurry regions.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Diffusion spatiality"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "luminance masking threshold\n: This control is useful if you want to in-paint highlights. For values greater than 0%, the diffusion will only occur in regions with a luminance greater than this setting. Note that gaussian noise will be added in these regions to simulate particles and initialize the in-painting.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/censorize.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/chromatic-aberrations.md
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Workflow"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The main difficulty with this module is that while its output can vary dramatically depending on its input paramaters, these parameters have no intuitive link to everyday life. Users are likely to be overwhelmed, unless they are already familiar with Fourier partial differential equations. This section proposes some ways to approach this module without the burden of having to understand the underlying theory.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "General advice"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If you intend to deblur your image using this module, always start by properly correctiong any chromatic aberrations and noise in the image, since the deblurring may magnify these artifacts. It is also important that you don't have clipped black pixels in your image. These can be corrected with the _black level correction_ of the [_exposure_](./exposure.md) module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Since it works on separate RGB channels, it is better to apply this module after [_color calibration_](./color-calibration.md), so that you start with a fully neutral, white-balanced, input image. Note that increasing local contrast or sharpness will also lead to a slight color contrast and saturation boost, which is usually a good thing. Since it uses a variance-based regularization to detect edges, it is also better to put this module before any non-linear operation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Starting with presets"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The provided presets have been tuned by the developer and tested on a range of images for typical purposes. The easiest way is simply to start from the presets, and then tweak them as needed:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- If the effect seems too strong, decrease the number of iterations,\n- If edge artifacts appear, increase the edge sensitivity,\n- If deblurring starts to affect valid blurry parts (bokeh), reduce the radius,\n- If deblurring seems correct in bright areas but excessive in dark areas, increase the edges _threshold_,\n- If deblurring clips black pixels, lower the _black level correction_ in _exposure_ module,\n- Fine-tune the _sharpness_ to your taste.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Starting from scratch"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The module's default settings are entirely neutral and will do nothing to your image. The spirit of the module is that each order affects the texture of the image in a particular way.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Start by tuning the first order parameters (speed and anisotropy) to get an initial base. Then adjust the radius. This will affect coarser textures (either blur or sharpen them). Remember that the first order acts on the low frequencies of the wavelet scale and follows a direction parallel or perpendicular to the gradient of the low frequencies.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Next, start to tune the second order parameters (speed and anisotropy). The second order also acts on the low frequencies of the wavelet scale but this time follows a direction parallel or perpendicular to the gradient of the _high_ frequencies, which can either be the direction of maximal sharpness or of noise. This can be used to reduce noise (using the second order in diffusion mode, with positive values) when you used the first order in sharpening mode (with negative values).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "These two steps can be performed on the zoomed-out image. Remember that, while great care has been taken to make the algorithm's visual result fairly scale-invariant, the preview will be exact only when zoomed 1:1. In any case, anything happening at pixel level (radius < 2px) will not be visible for zoom levels lower than 50%.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "At this point, you may want to tweak the edge sensitivity to take care of any edge artifacts. In theory, diffusing in the isophote direction ensures that diffusion is contained inside edges, but this is not sufficient when corners and sharp convex shapes are present in the image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When the edge sensitivity control has been adjusted to produce satisfying results, the image usually becomes quite soft. In most cases it will be necessary, at this point, to increase the number of iterations in order to compensate. This will come with a performance penalty so tread carefully with the performance/quality trade-off depending on your hardware. If you can't increase the number of iterations, you will have to increase the diffusing speed.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The final step is to fine-tune the third and fourth order, which take care of the high frequencies of each wavelet scale. You will need to be a lot more gentle with these settings than for the first and second orders, as they can cause noise to blow-up really fast.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The third order follows the gradient or isophote direction of the low frequency layer, so can be used to guide the high frequency diffusion in a direction that is more likely to be legitimate regarding real edges (and less prone to catch noise).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The fourth order follows the gradient or isophote direction of the high frequency layer and is more likely to catch noise. Diffusing on the fourth order is the best way to reduce noise without affecting sharpness too much, either as a stand-alone denoiser, or as a regularization step in a deblurring process.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Using multiple instances for image reconstruction"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Noise post-filtering may benefit from **introducing** a diffusion process -- this can be applied as an extra step after the [_denoise (profiled)_](./denoise-profiled.md) module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Conversely, the following optical issues may benefit from reconstruction by **undoing** the diffusion process:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. Blur introduced by a sensor's low-pass filter (LPF) and/or anti-aliasing performed by the [_demosaic_](./demosaic.md) module,\n2. Static lens blur,\n3. Haze/fog,\n4. Light diffusion (using a diffuser that is too large), leading to even lighting and lack of local contrast on the subject.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "While more than one of these issues can affect the same picture at the same time, it is better to try to fix them separately using multiple instances of the module. When doing so, ensure the issues are corrected from coarse scale to fine scale, and that denoising always happens first. That is, your instances should appear in the following [pipe order](../pixelpipe/the-pixelpipe-and-module-order.md):\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. Denoise,\n2. Local contrast enhancement,\n3. Dehaze,\n4. Lens blur correction,\n5. Sensor and demosaic correction.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Starting with the coarser-scale reconstructions reduces the probability of introducing or increasing noise when performing the finer-scale reconstructions. This is unintuitive because these processes don't happen in this order during the formation of the image. For the same reason, denoising should always happen before any attempt at sharpening or increasing acutance.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Notes and warnings"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "While this module is designed to be scale-invariant, its output can only be guaranteed at 100% zoom and high quality or full-size export. Results at lower zoom levels or export dimensions may or may not match your expectations.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When setting a deblurring algorithm, try to bear in mind that many of the greatest images in the history of photography were taken with lenses that were not remotely as sharp as those available today. Although the current trend is to build and sell increasingly sharp lenses and have software apply insane amounts of sharpening on top, this fashion does _not_ lead to better imagery and makes the retouching process more tedious. Soft focus and a bit of blurriness have some poetic merits too, which surgically-sanitized HD images may fail to convey.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "It should be noted that global contrast (using simple tone curves or black/white levels) also affects our perception of sharpness, which is quite different from optical sharpness (optical resolution). Human eyes are only sensitive to local contrast, which may come from optical sharpness (e.g absence of diffusion -- thin edges) as well as from amplified tonal transitions. If some global tone mapping is in place to increase the contrast, the image will look sharper. If a tone mapping is used to decrease the contrast, the image will look more blurry. In none of these cases are the actual edges of objects affected in any way, and the perceptual consequences are pure illusion.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Part of the aging process is a loss of eyesight. The amount of sharpening that people over 50 find pleasing may not be the same as for people in their 20s. It is worth considering sharpening to obtain a _plausible_ result (matching your everyday perception) rather than a _pleasing_ result (that may look good only to people with the same eyesight as yours).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/diffuse.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Finally, assessing the sharpness of images zoomed to 1:1 (100%) or more is a foolish task. In museums, exhibitions and even on screen, the general audience looks at images as a whole, not with a magnifying glass. Moreover, in most practical uses, photographs rarely exceed a resolution of 3000×2000 pixels (roughly a 300 DPI print at A4/letter dimensions) which, for 24 Mpx sensors, means downscaling by a factor of 4. When examining a 24 Mpx file at 1:1, you are actually looking at an image that will never exist. Sharpening at pixel level, in this context, is a waste of time and CPU cycles.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Lens correction"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Correct the distortion, transversal chromatic aberration (TCA) and vignetting your lens leaves in the picture.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Each of those three flaws is corrected from measurements of your lens, not from anything Ansel infers from the image. Most of the time there is nothing to do: the module reads the camera and lens from the image's Exif data, finds the best measurements available for each flaw, and applies them. The controls are there for the cases where it cannot — a lens with no electronics, a body the database has never heard of, a projection you want changed, or a profile you calibrated yourself.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< param-table >}}\n| **Camera model**<div>The body matched from the image's Exif data. Click it to pick another from every body the calibration database knows; only lenses fitting that body's mount are then offered.<br>\nWorth overriding when your own body is missing from the database: an earlier version of the same camera usually carries the same mount and, on a fixed-lens compact, often the very same lens, so picking it deliberately gets you a correction the automatic match cannot find.</div> |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| **Lens model**<div>The lens matched from the image's Exif data. Click it to pick another from the lenses fitting the selected body.<br>\nThis is what a lens with no electronic contacts needs: it tells the camera nothing, so nothing reaches the Exif data to match on. Off-brand lenses whose reported name does not resemble the catalogue's need it too.</div> |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| **Focal length, aperture and focus distance** (_mm_, _f_ and _d_)<div>The shooting conditions the measurements are interpolated for, each read from the image's Exif data: distortion and chromatic aberration are corrected against focal length, chromatic aberration and vignetting against aperture, and vignetting against focus distance.<br>\nOverride any of them by picking a value from its drop-down, or by typing one with the drop-down still open. Focus distance is the one that regularly needs it, since many cameras record none at all.</div> |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| **Vignetting**<div>Correct the darkening towards the corners of the frame. The value chosen is the source the correction is taken from — see [where corrections come from](#where-corrections-come-from).</div> |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| **Distortion**<div>Correct the bending of straight lines, barrel or pincushion. The value chosen is the source the correction is taken from.</div> |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| **Geometry**<div>The projection the corrected frame is rendered in — _rectilinear_, _fish-eye_, _panoramic_, _equirectangular_, _orthographic_, _stereographic_, _equisolid angle_ or _thoby fish-eye_. Rendering a fish-eye frame as rectilinear is what it is for. Offered only while distortion is taken from the _community profile_, the one source that describes the projection the lens was built for. To correct the aspect ratio of an anamorphic lens, use [_rotate and perspective_](./rotate-perspective.md) instead.</div> |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| **Scale**<div>The zoom applied after the distortion correction, which pushes part of the frame beyond its own borders and pulls empty corners in. Press the slider's refresh button to have Ansel find the smallest zoom that just removes those corners. Offered whenever distortion is corrected, from either source.<br>\nA _vendor profile_ already carries the manufacturer's own zoom, so against it this slider starts at 1 and is what you want *on top of* what the profile did.</div> |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| **Chromatic aberrations**<div>Correct the coloured fringes at high-contrast edges, left by the lens focusing colours at slightly different magnifications. The value chosen is the source the correction is taken from. Not offered on images your camera recorded as monochrome, which have no colour channels to misalign.</div> |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| **TCA red** / **TCA blue**<div>The two correction amounts, offered while chromatic aberrations are set to _manual correction_. Look along high-contrast edges for coloured seams and adjust until they are least visible.</div> |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| **Mode**<div>Whether to _correct_ the lens's flaws or to _distort_ an image that does not have them.</div> |\n{{< /param-table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\n_Mode_ is kept for backward compatibility only, so that edits which already set it keep rendering as they did, and it is shown only on those images. It inverts every correction at once rather than one flaw at a time, and it has no current equivalent: the module exists to remove lens flaws, and no use was found for the opposite.\n{{< /note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Where corrections come from"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Vignetting, distortion and chromatic aberration are each corrected from a *source*, and you pick one per flaw:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< param-table >}}\n| **Community profile**<div>From the calibration database Ansel ships. Measured by the [lensfun](https://lensfun.github.io/) community for your lens *model* — someone else's copy of the same lens, which is normally close enough.</div> |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| **Vendor profile**<div>From the profile your camera wrote into the raw file. Sony, Fujifilm and Olympus bodies publish one, as do many cameras that shoot DNG. The manufacturer measured the actual lens on a bench, so this is usually the better of the two, and Ansel picks it wherever the file offers it.</div> |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| **Manual correction**<div>From values you type. Offered for chromatic aberration only, that correction being two numbers; the others would need a whole polynomial.</div> |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| **No correction**<div>Leave this flaw alone.</div> |\n{{< /param-table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**A source is offered only where it can actually be used on the image in front of you.** A lens missing from the database offers no _community profile_; a file carrying nothing from its maker offers no _vendor profile_. So what you are shown *is* what will work — there is nothing to try in order to find out.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "That is settled per flaw rather than per file, because manufacturers do not all publish the same set. Olympus bodies, for instance, record distortion and chromatic aberration but no vignetting: on those images the first two take the camera's own profile while vignetting stays on the database, which is what you want and needs nothing from you.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\nA manufacturer describes distortion and chromatic aberration as a single measurement, so _vendor profile_ is offered for chromatic aberration only while distortion is taking it too. The other direction is free: a vendor distortion works with a community or hand-typed aberration, which is what you want when you are chasing fringing the camera's own profile leaves behind.\n{{< /note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. **Check what was matched.** Look at _Camera model_ and _Lens model_ first. Everything else follows from them, and a wrong lens produces a confidently wrong correction rather than no correction.\n2. **Set them by hand if they are wrong or empty.** Pick the body first, since it decides which lenses are then offered.\n3. **Fill in the focus distance** if vignetting matters on this image and your camera recorded none.\n4. **Take the sources as offered, then reconsider one at a time.** The defaults are already the best measurements available per flaw. Override where you have a reason — a vendor profile that overcorrects, a community profile you trust more.\n5. **Auto-scale the distortion.** Press the refresh button on _Scale_ once distortion is settled, so the frame loses as little as possible.\n6. **Judge at 1:1.** Chromatic aberration, and the softness at a corrected corner, are invisible at fit-to-screen.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Do this before any [crop](./crop.md), so the corrections are measured against the full frame the lens actually drew.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When your lens is not recognised"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If neither source has anything for your lens, every flaw offers only _no correction_. Look for the right profile yourself in the _Camera model_ and _Lens model_ menus before anything else — a lens is often present under a name that does not resemble what the camera reported.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If the lens is in the list but was not matched automatically, the name in the Exif data is the likely culprit, and adjusting it within the exiv2 program may fix the match (see [this discussion](https://dev.exiv2.org/boards/3/topics/2854)). Re-import the images afterwards: the lens name is read at import.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "By default only lenses fitting your camera's mount are listed and matched. Lenses mounted through an adapter — a Four Thirds lens on a Micro Four Thirds body, say — have to be enabled with the [`lensfun-add-adapter`](https://lensfun.github.io/manual/v0.3.2/lensfun-add-adapter.html) tool, then picked up by [rebuilding the database](#updating-the-lens-database).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If your lens is on lensfun's list of [currently supported lenses](https://lensfun.github.io/lenslist/) but absent from Ansel, your copy of the database predates it — again, [rebuild it](#updating-the-lens-database).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If there is no profile anywhere, a [lens calibration service](https://www.darktable.org/2013/07/have-your-lens-calibrated/) is offered by Torsten Bronger, or you can [measure your own](https://lensfun.github.io/lenslist/) and [make Ansel see it](#updating-the-lens-database). Do share what you measure with the lensfun project.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Updating the lens database"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel ships `lenses.db`, a calibration database built from the lensfun profiles published when the version you installed was packaged. It does not change by itself, so there are three reasons to rebuild it:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- the lensfun community has published a profile for your lens since your Ansel was built;\n- you ran `lensfun-add-adapter` to enable lenses mounted through an adapter;\n- you calibrated a lens yourself, and its profile is in your personal lensfun directory.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "None of those reach Ansel until the database is rebuilt, because the profiles live in lensfun's directories as XML while Ansel reads its own database.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The rebuild is a command rather than a menu entry: it is a rare operation, and not an interactive one.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (sh)
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "# only for a profile published since your Ansel was built\nlensfun-update-data\n\n# rebuild Ansel's database from every profile installed on this machine\nansel-lens-db-update\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\n`lensfun-update-data` and `lensfun-add-adapter` belong to the lensfun project and are not installed with Ansel. On Linux they come with your distribution's lensfun package; on Windows and macOS you would have to install lensfun yourself to use them.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Reading those XML profiles is lensfun's own job, so `ansel-lens-db-update` is built only where lensfun was available when Ansel was packaged. If the command is missing from your installation, that is why — and the module still works, on the database Ansel ships.\n{{< /note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< warning >}}\n**Close Ansel before running** `ansel-lens-db-update`. The new file is written safely — created under a temporary name and moved into place, so a running Ansel can never read a half-finished database — but each running instance keeps its own copy open until it exits, and would go on using the old one. Nothing breaks; the update simply would not appear until you restart.\n{{< /warning >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The rebuild **adds to** the lenses Ansel shipped with rather than replacing them. It starts from the lensfun profiles installed beside the database — the very ones the shipped `lenses.db` was built from — then merges over them every profile lensfun can find on this machine: the system-wide database, the system and user update directories, and your own profiles in `~/.local/share/lensfun`. Where two describe the same lens the one found later wins, so yours always beats the rest. Those are lensfun's own precedence rules. Pass `--no-baseline` if you want only what this machine has, and `--help` for the remaining options, which mostly matter if you keep profiles in unusual places.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "So calibrating one lens yourself gives you that lens *plus* the fifteen hundred already there, not a database holding only yours.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The result is written to your configuration directory:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< param-table >}}\n| **Linux and macOS**<div>`~/.config/ansel/lenses.db`</div> |\n| **Windows**<div>`%LOCALAPPDATA%\\ansel\\lenses.db`</div> |\n{{< /param-table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel reads that file before the copy it shipped with, so **you never replace the database Ansel installed** — the new one shadows it. The shipped copy stays in the program's own data folder, `share/ansel/lenses.db` beside the binary: `/usr/share/ansel/lenses.db` on a system-wide Linux install, `Ansel.app/Contents/Resources/share/ansel/lenses.db` on macOS, the `share\\ansel` folder of your install directory on Windows. Leave it alone: the baseline the rebuild starts from lives there too. Delete the file `ansel-lens-db-update` wrote and Ansel is back on the shipped database at the next start.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< warning >}}\nRun `ansel-lens-db-update` again after updating Ansel. A database records the schema it was built for, and an Ansel that reads a different one refuses it: corrections keep working, because Ansel falls back to the database it shipped with, but the lenses you had added are missing until you rebuild. Ansel names the file it refused, and says why, in its log.\n{{< /warning >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "What this module cannot do"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Worth knowing before you go looking for a control that is not there.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Vendor profiles are all-or-nothing.** A manufacturer's own software lets you dial a profile's strength down — half the distortion, three quarters of the vignetting. Ansel applies the profile as measured. For less than the full correction, the _community profile_ is the source that gives you a scale to play with, or shape the distortion by hand with [_rotate and perspective_](./rotate-perspective.md).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Not every manufacturer publishes every correction.** Olympus bodies record distortion and chromatic aberration but no vignetting; a DNG carries whichever corrections its writer chose to record, sometimes none at all. Ansel invents none of the missing ones — the source is simply not offered, and the _community profile_ covers that flaw if it knows your lens.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Only Sony, Fujifilm, Olympus and DNG vendor profiles can be read.** This is a gap in Ansel rather than in your camera: Canon raws, for instance, do carry a vignetting correction block of their own and Ansel has no reader for it yet. Files from other makers use the _community profile_, which for popular lenses is usually well populated. If your camera's profile matters to you, say so — knowing which bodies people actually want is what decides which reader gets written next.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Chromatic aberration cannot borrow a profile it was not measured against.** A manufacturer measures the colour fringing as a departure from *their* distortion figures, so pairing it with the community profile's distortion would combine two different measurements of the same lens.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Corrections are read from the raw file each time it is opened.** Nothing about the vendor profile is stored in your library, so if the file is replaced, or edited on another machine running an older Ansel, the correction follows the file rather than your edit. Ansel says so in its log when it falls back to the _community profile_ because a vendor profile could not be found or read.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Very old edits still follow the database of the day.** Images corrected automatically by much older versions of Ansel did not record which corrections they used — they asked the database again on every open. An edit of that age rendering differently after a database update is why. Anything you edit or touch from now on writes its choice down and is reproducible.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lens-correction.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< warning >}}\nCorrections pull pixels in from beyond the frame, and Ansel fills what is missing at the borders by repeating the edge pixels. On a strong correction that filling is visible, especially on a noisy image — crop it away if it shows.\n{{< /warning >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/censorize.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Censorize"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/censorize.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Degrade parts of the image in an aesthetically pleasing way, in order to anonymize people/objects or hide body parts.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/censorize.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Censorize works in linear RGB color space to apply a physically-accurate gaussian blur and gaussian luminance noise.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/censorize.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Aside from anonymization, this module can also be used for a wide range of creative purposes, for example:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/censorize.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Combine a simple blur with a multiply [blend mode](../masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md) to create a realistic bloom (Orton effect).\n- Combine a simple blur with a subtract blending mode and low opacity to create an [unsharp mask](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unsharp_masking), similar to the [sharpen](./sharpen.md) module but in an RGB scene-referred space.\n- Add noise to create artificial grain.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/censorize.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Note:** The anonymizing methods provided by this module are not forensically safe in order to favor aesthetics. Some forensic techniques may still be able to reconstruct the censorized content based on its structure, especially for simple shapes and text (e.g. license plates, street numbers).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/censorize.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If forensically safe anonymization is required, the only way to achieve this is to paint the surfaces with a solid color.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/censorize.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**The Ansel team does not accept responsibility for poorly anonymized pictures leading to the identification of individuals or personal property.**\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/censorize.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "You are advised to leave the module's controls at their default values while you [mask](../masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md) the areas of the image that you wish to censorize, in order that the details of the image remain visible.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/censorize.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "input blur radius\n: The strength of the first pass of the gaussian blur.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/censorize.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "pixellation radius\n: The size of the \"big pixels\" created after the first pass of gaussian blur.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/censorize.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "output blur radius\n: The strength of the second pass of the gaussian blur, applied after the pixellation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/censorize.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "noise level\n: The strength (standard deviation) of the luminance gaussian noise applied after the second pass of the gaussian blur. Adding noise can fake details in the blurred regions and make content detection more difficult for artificial intelligence algorithms.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-pixels.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Rotate pixels"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-pixels.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The sensors of some cameras (such as the Fujifilm FinePix S2Pro, F700, and E550) have a diagonally oriented Bayer pattern instead of the usual orthogonal layout.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-pixels.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Without correction this would lead to a tilted image with black corners. This module applies the required rotation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-pixels.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/scale-pixels.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel detects images that need correction using their Exif data and automatically activates this module where required. For other images the module always remains disabled.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/rotate-pixels.md
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/scale-pixels.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The module has no controls.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/contrast-equalizer.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Contrast equalizer"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/contrast-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Adjust luminance and chroma contrast in the wavelet domain.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/contrast-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This versatile module can be used to achieve a variety of effects, including bloom, denoise, clarity, and local contrast enhancement.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/contrast-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "It works in the wavelets domain and its parameters can be tuned independently for each wavelet detail scale. The module operates in CIE LCh color space and so is able to treat luminosity and chromaticity independently.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/contrast-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A number of presets are provided, which should help you to understand the capabilities of the module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/contrast-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The _contrast equalizer_ module decomposes the image into various detail scales. On each detail scale, you can independently adjust the contrast and denoise splines for lightness (\"luma\") and chromaticity (“chroma”, or color saturation), as well as adjusting the edge-awareness (“edges”) of the wavelet transform. The luma, chroma and edges splines are provided on separate tabs, and some examples of their usage are given in the following sections.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/contrast-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Below the spline graphs is a _mix_ slider, which can be used to adjust the strength of the effect, and even to invert the graph (with negative values). While your mouse is over the spline graph, the curve will be displayed as if the _mix_ slider was set to 1.0, to allow for easier editing. When you move your mouse away, the graph will be re-adjusted to account for the _mix_ slider.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/contrast-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In the background of the curve you can see a number of alternating light and dark stripes. These represent the levels of detail that are visible at your current zoom scale -- any details without these stripes are too small to be seen your current view. Adjustments made to control points within the striped section _may_ produce a visible effect (depending on the strength of the adjustment). Adjustments outside of the striped region _will not_. Zoom in to see higher levels of detail and make adjustments to the more detailed areas of the image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/contrast-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Tip:** if you are having trouble visualising which parts of the curve will affect which details in the image, you can set the [blend mode](../masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md) to \"difference\". This will make the image go black except for those areas where the output of the module differs from the input. By raising the curve at one of the control points, you will be able to see which details in the image are represented by that point.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/contrast-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Luma tab"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/contrast-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The luma tab allows you to adjust the local contrast in the image's luminance (brightness). Adjustments are represented by a white spline that begins as a horizontal line running across the centre of the graph (indicating that no change will be made). Raise or lower this spline at the left end of the graph to increase or decrease the local contrast of coarse detail in the image. Perform similar adjustments towards the right side of the graph to adjust the local contrast of the fine details in the image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/contrast-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When you hover the mouse pointer over the graph, a white circle indicates the radius of influence of the mouse pointer -- the size of this circle can be adjusted by scrolling with the mouse wheel. The larger the circle of influence, the more control points will be affected when you adjust the curve. A highlighted region in the background shows what the spline would look like if you pushed the currently-hovered control point all the way to the top or bottom on the graph -- see the screenshot below for examples of these features.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/contrast-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The following image shows the default state of the contrast equalizer module before any adjustments have been made:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/contrast-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "![contrast-equalizer-luma-orig](contrast-equalizer-luma-orig.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/contrast-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Raising the two control points at the right-hand end of the graph will increase the sharpness of the fine details (the eye and feathers of the bird) while leaving coarser details (the rocks in the background) largely unaffected. The example below has been exaggerated to better illustrate the effect.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/contrast-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "![contrast-equalizer-luma-high](contrast-equalizer-luma-high.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/contrast-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Increasing the local contrast can also amplify the luma noise in the image. A second spline located at the bottom of the graph can be used to denoise the selected detail scales. Raise this spline (by clicking just above one of the triangles at the bottom of the graph and dragging the line upwards) to reduce noise at the given wavelet scale. In the example above, the dark denoising spline has been raised at the fine-detail end of the graph.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/contrast-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Chroma tab"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/contrast-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The chroma tab allows the color contrast or saturation to be adjusted at the selected wavelet scales. See the following example:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/contrast-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "![contrast-equalizer-chroma-orig](contrast-equalizer-chroma-orig.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/contrast-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Say you wanted to bring out the green color of the anthers at the end of the stamen. The pink petals of the flowers are already quite saturated, but using contrast equalizer you can selectively boost the saturation on the small scale of the anthers without impacting the saturation of the petals. By raising the third control point from the right, you can target the saturation of the anthers only:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/contrast-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "![contrast-equalizer-chroma-high](contrast-equalizer-chroma-high.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/contrast-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "As in the luma tab, the chroma tab also has a denoising spline at the bottom of the graph. This can be used to handle chroma noise at different scales within the image. Chroma denoising can generally be more aggressive on larger wavelet scales and has less effect on a smaller scale.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/contrast-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Edge tab"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/contrast-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The basic wavelet _à trous_ transform has been enhanced in the _contrast equalizer_ to be \"edge-aware\", which can help to reduce the gradient reversals and halo artifacts that the basic algorithm can produce. The _edges_ tab does not directly act on the edges in an image; rather it adjusts the edge awareness feature of the wavelet transform. If you have not adjusted the luma or chroma splines, adjusting the edge awareness spline will have no effect.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/contrast-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "To see the sorts of artifacts that the _edges_ curve tries to combat, here is an example taken from the original paper \"Edge-Optimized À-Trous Wavelets for Local Contrast Enhancement with Robust Denoising\" (Hanika, Damertz and Lensch 2011):\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/contrast-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "![contrast-equalizer-edge](contrast-equalizer-edge.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/contrast-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In the image on the left, the edges spline has been reduced to a minimum, effectively disabling the edge awareness and resulting in halos. In the middle image, the edges spline has been increased too much, resulting in gradient reversals. In the image on the right the edges spline has been set somewhere in between the two extremes, resulting in nice clean edges.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/contrast-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Usually the default central position of the spline is a good starting point, but if there are objectionable artifacts around the edges, this control can be helpful in mitigating them. Some experimentation will be required.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/tone-equalizer.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Tone equalizer"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/tone-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Dodge and burn while preserving local contrast.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/tone-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "It works in linear RGB space and utilizes a user-defined mask to guide the dodging and burning adjustments, helping to preserve local contrast within the image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/tone-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The following diagram describes how the tone equalizer works:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/tone-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "![tone-equalizer-mask](tone-equalizer-overview.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/tone-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. Create a monochrome [_guided mask_](#masking-tab) that divides the input image into regions of similar luminosity. The resulting mask should blur the fine details within the image so that pixels within each region are all treated similarly, preserving local contrast.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/tone-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "2. Adjust the sliders in the [_simple_](#simple-tab) tab or the equalizer graph in the [_advanced_](#advanced-tab) tab to alter the brightness of the underlying image, based on the brightness of the mask. Exposure can also be adjusted by scrolling with the mouse while hovering the cursor over the preview image (see [_cursor indicator/control_](#cursor-indicatorcontrol) for details).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/tone-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "   In the simple tab, each slider corresponds to a single brightness zone (EV) in the mask, which can be raised or lowered to adjust the exposure of the image where the mask's brightness lies in that zone. Similarly, in the equalizer tab, the horizontal axis of the graph corresponds to the brightness level of the mask, and the vertical axis specifies the required exposure adjustment to pixels where the mask matches that brightness level.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/tone-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "3. The exposure of each pixel of the input image is adjusted using the mask and the equalizer graph. For each pixel, the module looks up the brightness of the mask at that point, finds the matching brightness on the horizontal axis of the equalizer graph, and increases or decreases the exposure accordingly (using the vertical axis of the graph).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/tone-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "It is important that the mask separates the image into regions of similar brightness, and that a suitable amount of blur is applied within those regions. This means that all of the pixels in each region will have their exposure adjusted similarly, without adversely affecting local contrast. Examine your image beforehand to identify which regions you wish to dodge and burn, and use the controls on the [_masking_](#masking-tab) tab to ensure that those areas are reasonably separated in tone within the final mask. This will allow those regions to be adjusted independently.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/tone-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The controls of the _tone equalizer_ module are divided between three tabs.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/tone-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "display exposure mask\n: Click on the icon to the right of this label to show/hide the module's guided mask over the top of the image. This control is available in all three tabs.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/tone-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Simple tab"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/tone-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This tab splits the brightness of the guided mask into nine zones (from --8 to 0 EV) and allows you to alter each zone independently. This is a simplified interface and is used to generate the same tone adjustment curve as shown in the [_advanced_](#advanced-tab) tab.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/tone-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "--8 EV ... 0 EV\n: Each slider adjusts the exposure of all pixels where the _guided mask_ has the given brightness. If the mask's histogram is evenly spread over the entire tonal range, sliders towards the top generally affect the shadows, whereas sliders towards the bottom generally affect the highlights. You can check the spread of the histogram within the [_advanced_](#advanced-tab) tab.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/tone-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Advanced tab"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/tone-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This tab allows you to control the same intensity levels as in the simple tab, though here they are represented as control points on a curve. Behind the curve is a histogram representing the intensity levels of the _guided mask_ (not the underlying image). If the histogram is too bunched up, this means your mask doesn't have a good spread of intensity levels, which makes it harder to independently control the brightness of the different parts of your image. It is therefore recommended that the histogram be adjusted so that it extends the entire range, covering as many control points as possible for maximum flexibility. You can adjust the mask using the controls in the [_masking_](#masking-tab) tab.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/tone-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Click+drag the control points on the curve to adjust the brightness of all pixels where the mask has the given intensity. If the mask's histogram is evenly spread over the entire tonal range, control points to the left will generally affect the shadows, and control points to the right will generally affect the highlights. Moving a single control point will also affect control points on either side to ensure the curve remains smooth. This behaviour can be adjusted using the _curve smoothing_ control.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/tone-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "curve smoothing\n: Control how the curve is interpolated between control points. Move the slider to the right to make the transitions between the control points more gradual, but beware that going past about 0.6 can introduce some instability (oscillations) in the curve due to mathematical constraints. Move the slider to the left for a more well-behaved curve, but beware that this can result in harsher tonal transitions that may damage local contrast.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/tone-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Masking tab"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/tone-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This tab contains controls for adjusting the guided mask.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/tone-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The purpose of the guided mask is to separate out areas with different tonal ranges so that they can be independently brightened or darkened by the tone equalizer. The masking filters are designed to allow sharp edges between these areas to be preserved, while blurring details within a given tonal range, so that the brightness can be adjusted without adversely impacting local contrast. Ideally the mask histogram shown in the _advanced_ tab should be spread out across all of the control points.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/tone-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "To avoid having to switch back and forth between the _advanced_ and _masking_ tabs, a gray bar under the \"mask post processing\" label displays a representation of the middle 80% of the histogram. By using the controls in this tab to center and spread out this gray bar, you can expect to have a nicely shaped histogram when you return to the \"advanced\" tab. If you see orange at either end of the gray bar, this means that part of the histogram is outside of the 9 EV range of the mask, and needs to be further adjusted.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/tone-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "![tone-equalizer-mask-histogram](tone-equalizer-mask-histogram.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/tone-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When setting up the guided mask you will need to strike a balance between obtaining a smooth blur within tonal regions (to preserve local contrast) and preservation of the boundaries between those regions. Some experimentation will be required to find the best settings. Often the key controls to adjust are the _exposure/contrast compensation_ sliders at the bottom of the module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/tone-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Displaying the guided mask while making these adjustments will help you to understand these controls and better judge the quality of the mask.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/tone-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "luminance estimator\n: Choose the method by which the luminance of a pixel will be estimated when mapping it to a mask intensity value (default _RGB euclidean norm_).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/tone-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "preserve details\n: Choose the smoothing algorithm used to blur the mask:\n - _no:_ Do not smooth the mask (the effect is the same as using normal tone curves). When the module is used to compress dynamic range, this option can cause compression of local contrast. This can be useful when increasing (local and global) contrast.\n - _guided filter:_ Use the original guided filter algorithm to blur the mask while attempting to preserve edges. One of the limitations of this algorithm is that the guided filter is exposure-sensitive, meaning that shadows tend to be blurred more than highlights. Note that this limitation can sometimes be an asset: if one wants to lighten shadows a lot, the guided filter can provide very good local contrast preservation.\n - _average guided filter:_ Use this option in cases where the effect of the guided filter is too strong. In this mode, a geometric mean is taken between the output of the original _guided filter_ algorithm and the output given by the _no_ option.\n - _eigf (default):_ The _exposure-independent guided filter_ solves the problem of the original _guided filter_, in that it makes the degree of blurring independent of the exposure. This means the degree of blurring applied to the highlights and the shadows regions should be about the same. This improved algorithm is now the default option.\n - _averaged eigf:_ This option takes the geometric mean between the _eigf_ mask and the mask generated by the _no_ option, and is useful in cases where the degree of blurring in the mask needs to be mitigated.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/tone-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "filter diffusion\n: By default this is set to a value of 1, meaning that the filtering algorithm is run once on the input image to produce the blurred monochrome mask.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/tone-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": If you increase this to 2, the filtering algorithm will be run once on the input image to produce an intermediate mask, and then a second time on the intermediate mask. As a result, the final mask will be more blurred than when using a single iteration. Progressively higher values will diffuse the mask even further. However, because the mask filtering algorithm is run multiple times, each iteration will increase the processing time.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/tone-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "smoothing diameter\n: This controls how much of the surrounding image to take into account when calculating the mask's blur at a particular point, defined as a percentage of the length of the image's longer side (default 5%). With lower values, the transition between darker and lighter areas of the mask will be more pronounced. As the value is increased, transitions become smoother/softer. For the default _exposure-independent guided filter (eigf)_, you should typically use blurring radii around 1-10%. With the original _guided filter_, blurring radii around 1-25% will typically yield better results.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/tone-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "edges refinement/feathering\n: Higher values force the mask to follow high contrast edges more closely. Lower values give smoother gradients, but may introduce halos. If required, feathering can be set to values as high as 10,000.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/tone-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "mask post-processing\n: This bar provides a representation of the current span of the mask's histogram. It covers the middle 80% of the histogram, dropping the first and last decile to prevent outliers from skewing the indicator too much. Orange indicators at either end mean that the histogram exceeds the upper or lower bounds of its 9 EV range.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/tone-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "mask quantisation\n: Apply a degree of posterisation to the mask, so that it tends to centre round a few discrete levels. In some cases, this may be useful to help separate out areas of your image into distinct masking levels.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/tone-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "mask exposure compensation\n: Adjust the mask's histogram to the left or right. If you have used the _exposure_ module to adjust the image brightness, you may need to offset that adjustment by using this slider to re-centre the mask's histogram. Click on the wand icon to the right of the slider to set the exposure compensation such that the average of the mask's histogram will coincide with the central --4EV control point. The slider can then be fine-tuned as required.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/tone-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "mask contrast compensation\n: Dilate (spread out) or compress the mask's histogram. The wand icon to the right of the slider will propose a reasonable starting point, which can then be fine-tuned to optimize the spread of the histogram under the tone equalizer control points.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/tone-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Cursor indicator/control"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/tone-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When the _tone equalizer_ module is enabled and expanded, you can move the mouse pointer over the preview image to show a cursor that displays information about the pixel under the pointer. When this cursor is shown, the mouse wheel can be used to brighten or darken the areas of your image that match the mask intensity level at that point. This provides a convenient way to quickly brighten or darken specific parts of the image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/tone-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "![tone-equalizer-simple](tone-equalizer-cursor.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/tone-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- The cross-hairs indicate the position of the pixel under the cursor\n- The text label shows the intensity of the guided mask at that point, in EV\n- The shade of the outer circle indicates the intensity of the mask at that point\n- If the tone equalizer has brightened or darkened pixels matching this mask intensity, the magnitude of the adjustment is indicated by an arc on the left-hand-side. The longer the arc, the greater the brightness adjustment,\n- If there has been an exposure adjustment, the shade of the inner circle indicates the amount of brightening or darkening, relative to the mask's intensity at that point (as indicated by the outer gray circle). That is to say, if the pixel under the crosshairs has been brightened, the inner circle will be a lighter shade of gray than the outer circle; if the pixel has been darkened, the inner circle will be a darker shade of gray than the outer circle.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/tone-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If you need to move or zoom the portion of the image shown in the center view while the module is expanded, hold down the 'a' key while dragging the mouse or using the scroll wheel.  As long as the key is held down, mouse actions adjust the viewport rather than adjusting the tone curve.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/tone-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The _tone equalizer_ comes with several presets that can use used to compress shadows and highlights. Each comes in two variants, using either the guided filter (gf) or the exposure-independent guided filter (eigf). The variants using the guided filter tend to preserve local contrast in the shadows better those that use the exposure-independent guided filter, but at the price of reducing the local contrast in the highlights. Either of these variants may lead to better results, depending on the image. In both cases, the presets preserve middle-gray, so you shouldn't need to adjust the global exposure after activating the tone equalizer.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/demosaic.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Demosaic"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/demosaic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Control how raw files are demosaiced.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/demosaic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Bayer filters"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/demosaic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The sensor cells of a digital camera are not color-sensitive -- they are only able to record different levels of lightness. In order to obtain a color image, each cell is covered by a color filter (red, green or blue) that primarily passes light of that color. This means that each pixel of the raw image only contains information about a single color channel.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/demosaic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Color filters are commonly arranged in a mosaic pattern known as a Bayer filter array. A demosaic algorithm reconstructs the missing color channels by interpolation with data from the neighboring pixels. For further reading see the Wikipedia articles on [demosaicing](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Demosaicing) and the [Bayer filter](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bayer_filter).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/demosaic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel offers several demosaic algorithms, each with it's own characteristics. The differences between them are often very subtle and might only be visible while pixel-peeping. However, as the program works on a pixel-by-pixel basis and demosaic generates the base data for the other modules, the choice of the algorithm can have a visually significant effect on the quality of very fine details in the image. This can include the appearance of false maze patterns as well as the rendering quality of colored edges.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/demosaic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Demosaic interpolation algorithms are often prone to produce artifacts, typically visible as [Moiré patterns](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moire_pattern) when zooming into the image. The chosen algorithm might handle _pre-existing_ Moiré- or Maze-like patterns in the raw data in a better or worse way. In these circumstances _VNG4_ and _LMMSE_ are often more stable.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/demosaic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The following demosaic algorithms are available for sensors with Bayer filters:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/demosaic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- _PPG_ used to be Ansel's default demosaic algorithm. It is fast, but other algorithms generally yield better results.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/demosaic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- _AMaZE_ and _RCD_ offer better reconstruction of high-frequency content (finer details, edges, stars) but might struggle with color reconstruction overshoots or added noise in areas of low contrast. While _AMaZE_ often retains more high-frequency details it is also more prone to color overshoots than _RCD_. Since _RCD_ now offers similar performance to _PPG_, but with better results, it is now the default algorithm.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/demosaic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- _LMMSE_ is better suited for use on high ISO and noisy images than _AMaZE_ or _RCD_, both of which tend to generate overshooting artefacts when applied to such images. It can also be useful to manage images that exhibit Moiré patterns with other algorithms.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/demosaic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- _VNG4_ is better suited for use on images with low-frequency content (e.g. low contrast regions such as sky) but, compared to _AMaZE_ and _RCD_, it often causes loss of some high-frequency details and can sometimes add local color shifts. VNG is no longer really recommended -- for most images, other available algorithms usually provide better results.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/demosaic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- _downscale_ is a last-resort alternative that will actually not demosaic, but interpolate by nearest-neighbour and reduce the resolution by 4 (2 on each side). The rationale behind is that many sensors now have 36, 45, 52… Mpx while we still consume images mostly in Full HD (1920×1080 px = 2 Mpx).[^1] For very noisy pictures, the demosaicing step will actually worsen the noise, by creating patterns, blotches and shifting colors toward green or magenta. Downscaling those very-high-resolution images, when noisy, prevents aggravating the problem and also makes denoising less computationally-intensive, while still producing at least 9 usable Mpx at the end. The _downscale_ option has an iterative color filtering step that uses the guided Laplacian (same as [_highlights reconstruction_](./highlight-reconstruction.md)) to limit moiré, fringes and noise altogether in a multi-scale setup. As a side effect, downscaling without post-filtering is the only method that fully preserves the sensor noise statistics, which is useful when using [noise profiles](./denoise-profiled.md).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/demosaic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "[^1]: Even a 8.5×11 inch print at 300 DPI is still only 8.7 Mpx…\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/demosaic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Note:** The performance of the demosaic algorithms differs significantly, _AMaZE_ being by far the slowest.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/demosaic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Sensors without bayer filters"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/demosaic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "There are a few cameras whose sensors do not use a Bayer filter. Cameras with an \"X-Trans\" sensor have their own set of demosaic algorithms. The default algorithm for X-Trans sensors is _Markesteijn 1-pass_, which produces fairly good results. For slightly better quality (at the cost of much slower processing), choose _Markesteijn 3-pass_. Though _VNG_ is faster than _Markesteijn 1-pass_ on some computers, it is more prone to artifacts.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/demosaic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Special algorithms"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/demosaic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "_passthrough (monochrome)_ is only useful for cameras that have had the color filter array physically removed from the sensor (e.g. scratched off). Demosaic algorithms usually reconstruct missing color channels by interpolation with data from the neighboring pixels. However, if the color filter array is not present, there is nothing to interpolate, so this algorithm simply sets all the color channels to the same value, resulting in a monochrome image. This method avoids the interpolation artifacts that the standard demosaic algorithms might introduce.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/demosaic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "_photosite_color_ is not meant to be used for image processing. It takes the raw photosite data and presents it as red, blue or green pixels. This is designed for debugging purposes in order to see the raw data and can assist with analysis of errors produced by the other demosaic algorithms.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/demosaic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Dual demosaic algorithms"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/demosaic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Some images have areas best demosaiced using an algorithm that preserves high frequency information (like _AMaZE_ or _RCD_) and other areas that might profit from an algorithm more suited to low frequency content (like _VNG4_).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/demosaic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In dual demosaic algorithms (e.g. _RCD + VNG4_) the sensor data is demosaiced twice, first by _RCD_, _AMaZE_ or _Markesteijn 3-pass_ and then by _VNG4_. Both sets of demosaiced data are retained for subsequent processing.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/demosaic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The data from the high frequency algorithm is then analysed for _local data change_ and, using a threshold (there is a bit more of maths involved here), the output image is written pixel-by-pixel for each color channel using data from each demosaic algorithm weighed by the local data change.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/demosaic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In general, areas with greater detail are demosaiced by the algorithm best suited to that purpose (_RCD, AMaZe, Markesteijn 3-pass_) and any flat areas (like blue sky) are demosaiced using the second algorithm (_VNG4_).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/demosaic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "_The 'local data change' is technically implemented as a gaussian-blurred single channel selection mask calculated from a combination of the threshold value and the pixels' luminance._\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/demosaic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Selecting the threshold"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/demosaic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "An automatically-calculated threshold is difficult to implement. Instead, the \"display blending mask\" button can be used to display the _selection mask_ so you can control the selection of the algorithm manually. The brighter the pixel in the displayed mask, the more the output is taken from the high-frequency algorithm.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/demosaic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "method\n: The demosaic algorithm to use (see above).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/demosaic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "edge threshold (_PPG_ only)\n: The threshold for an additional median pass. Defaults to “0” which disables median filtering.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/demosaic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "lmmse refine (_LMMSE_ only)\n: Refinement steps for use with the LMMSE demosaic algorithm. Median steps average the output. Refinement steps add some recalculation of red and blue channels. While the refinement options work well for luma noise, they may decrease quality on images with heavy chroma noise.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/demosaic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "color smoothing\n: Activate a number of additional color smoothing passes. Defaults to “off”.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/demosaic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "match greens\n: In some cameras the green filters have slightly varying properties. This parameter adds an additional equalization step to suppress artifacts. Available options are “disabled”, “local average”, “full average” and “full and local average”. This option is not shown for X-Trans sensors.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/demosaic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "switch dual threshold _(dual demosaic modes only)_\n: Set the contrast threshold for dual demosaic modes. Lower values favor the high frequency demosaic algorithm and higher values favor the low frequency algorithm.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/demosaic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "display blending mask _(dual demosaic modes only)_\n: Show the blending mask that is used to differentiate between high and low frequency areas (adjusted by the \"switch dual threshold\" parameter). For each pixel, the brighter the mask, the more the module's output is taken from the high frequency demosaic algorithm.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/framing.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Framing"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/framing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Generate a frame around the image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/framing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The frame consists of a border (with a user-defined color) and a frame line within that border (with a second user-defined color). Various options are available to control the geometry and color of the frame.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/framing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "border size\n: The size of the frame as a percentage of the underlying full image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/framing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "aspect\n: The aspect ratio of the final module output (i.e. the underlying image plus the frame)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/framing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "orientation\n: The orientation of the frame (portrait/landscape). Select 'auto' for Ansel to choose the most reasonable orientation based on the underlying image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/framing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "horizontal/vertical position\n: Select from a set of pre-defined ratios to control where the underlying image will be positioned on the horizontal/vertical axis. You can also right click and enter your own ratio as \"x/y\".\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/framing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "frame line size\n: The percentage of the frame line size, relative to the border size at its smallest part.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/framing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "frame line offset\n: The position of the frame line, relative to the underlying image. Choose 0% for a frame line that touches the image. Choose 100% for a frame line that touches the outer border.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/framing.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "border color / frame line color\n: A pair of color selectors which allow the border and frame line colors to be defined. Clicking on the colored field will open a color selector dialog which offers a choice of commonly-used colors, or allows you to define a color in RGB color space. You can also activate a color picker to take a color probe from the image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/unbreak-input-profile.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Unbreak input profile"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/unbreak-input-profile.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Add a correction curve to image data. This is required if you have selected certain input profiles in the [_input color profile_](./input-color-profile.md) module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/unbreak-input-profile.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If you decide to use an ICC profile from the camera manufacturer in the [_input color profile_](./input-color-profile.md) module, a correction curve frequently needs to be pre-applied to image data to prevent the final output from looking too dark. This extra processing is not required if you use Ansel's standard or enhanced color matrices.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/unbreak-input-profile.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The correction curve is defined with a linear part extending from the shadows to some upper limit and a gamma curve covering mid-tones and highlights. For further information please see Ansel's neighboring project [UFRaw](http://ufraw.sourceforge.net).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/unbreak-input-profile.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "linear\n: The upper limit for the region counted as shadows and where no gamma correction is performed. Typically values between 0.0 and 0.1 are required by the profile.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/unbreak-input-profile.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "gamma\n: The gamma value required to compensate the input profile. Often the required value is 0.45 (the reciprocal of the 2.2 gamma used by some manufacturer's profiles).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/raw-black-white-point.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Raw black/white point"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/raw-black-white-point.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Define camera-specific black and white points.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/raw-black-white-point.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This module is activated automatically for all raw images. Default settings are applied for all supported cameras. Changes to the defaults are not normally required.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/raw-black-white-point.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "black level 0-3\n: The camera-specific black level of the four pixels in the RGGB Bayer pattern. Pixels with values lower than this level are not considered to contain valid data.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/raw-black-white-point.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "white point\n: The camera-specific white level. All pixels with values above this are likely to be clipped and will be handled accordingly in the [_highlight reconstruction_](./highlight-reconstruction.md) module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/raw-black-white-point.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "flat field correction\n: Use flat field correction to compensate for lens shading. This field only appears for applicable Raw files and will automatically use any GainMap embedded in the Raw. You can choose to disable this correction if desired.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/chromatic-aberrations.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Chromatic aberrations"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/chromatic-aberrations.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Correct chromatic aberrations.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/chromatic-aberrations.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In contrast to the [_raw chromatic aberrations_](./raw-chromatic-aberrations.md) module, this module does not require raw data as input.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/chromatic-aberrations.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "To obtain the best result, you are advised to proceed as follows:\n1. Attenuate the chromatic aberrations as much as possible in the [_lens correction_](./lens-correction.md) module using the TCA sliders.\n2. Increase the strength slider in this module to better see its effect.\n3. Increase the radius until the chromatic aberrations disappear. If this is insufficient, try enabling the \"very large chromatic aberrations\" setting.\n4. Choose the guide that gives the best result in term of sharpness and artifacts.\n5. Reduce the strength to avoid washing out the colors too much.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/chromatic-aberrations.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "For more complicated cases you could also try the following:\n- Use several instances with different correction modes -- for example, a first instance in \"brighten only\" mode, and a second in \"darken only\" mode.\n- Use several instances with low strength to correct the chromatic aberrations a little at a time without degrading colors too much.\n- Use the module with parametric or drawn masks.\n- Use RGB red, green and blue blend modes to restrict the effect to a particular channel.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/chromatic-aberrations.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "guide\n: The color channel that will be used as a reference for the correction.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/chromatic-aberrations.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "radius\n: The radius of the effect. Increase until chromatic aberrations are eliminated. This is the most important slider of the module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/chromatic-aberrations.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "strength\n: This slider acts as a safeguard and can help to preserve colorful areas that do not suffer from chromatic aberrations. Increase for stronger correction, decrease for stronger preservation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/chromatic-aberrations.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "correction mode\n: Allows you to restrict the effect to brighten or darken pixels only. For full control, this can be used in combination with R, G, B blend modes and multiples instances.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/chromatic-aberrations.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "very large chromatic aberrations\n: Makes the algorithm iterative to help in reducing very large chromatic aberrations.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/exposure.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Exposure"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/exposure.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Increase or decrease the overall brightness of an image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/exposure.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This module has two modes of operation:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/exposure.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "manual\n: Set the _exposure_, _black level_ and _clipping threshold_ manually\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/exposure.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "automatic (RAW images only)\n: Use an analysis of the image's histogram to automatically set the exposure. Ansel automatically selects the exposure compensation that is required to shift the selected _percentile_ to the selected _target level_ (see definitions below). This mode is particularly useful for automatically altering a large number of images to have the same exposure. A typical use case of automatic mode is deflickering of time-lapse photographs.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/exposure.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "mode\n: Choose the mode of operation (automatic/manual).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/exposure.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "compensate camera exposure (manual mode)\n: Automatically remove the camera exposure bias (taken from the image's Exif data).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/exposure.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "exposure (manual mode)\n: Increase (move to the right) or decrease (move to the left) the exposure value (EV). To adjust by more than the default limits shown on the slider, right click and enter the desired value up to +/-18 EV.\n: The color picker tool on the right sets the exposure such that the average of the selected region matches the target lightness defined in [spot exposure mapping](#spot-exposure-mapping) options.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/exposure.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "clipping threshold (manual mode)\n: Define what percentage of bright values are to be clipped in the calculation of the _exposure_ and _black level correction_. Use the color picker to sample a portion of the image to be used for this calcuation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/exposure.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "percentile (automatic mode)\n: Define a location in the histogram to use for automatic exposure correction. A percentile of 50% denotes a position in the histogram where 50% of pixel values are above and 50% of pixel values are below that exposure.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/exposure.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "target level (automatic mode)\n: Define the target level for automatic exposure correction (EV) relative to the white point of the camera.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/exposure.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "black level correction (manual and automatic modes)\n: Adjust the black level point to unclip negative RGB values.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/exposure.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Note:** Do not use the black level correction to add more density in blacks as this can clip near-black colors out of gamut by generating negative RGB values. This can cause problems with some modules later in the pixelpipe. Instead, use a tone mapping curve to add density to the blacks. For example, you can use the _relative black exposure_ slider on the _scene_ tab of the [filmic rgb](filmic.md#scene) module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/exposure.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Spot exposure mapping"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/exposure.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Unfortunately, the light often changes slightly between shots, even within the same series captured in the same conditions. This can be the result of a cloud passing by the sun in natural light, surface reflections having less \"shine\" from a different angle, or simply due to unavoidable variability in the mechanical diaphragm aperture. Each image will still need some individual fine-tuning if you want a perfectly even look over the whole series, and this can be both time-consuming and frustrating.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/exposure.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Spot exposure mapping allows you to define a target brightness, in terms of exposure, for a particular region of the image (the control sample), which you then match against the same target brightness in other images. The control sample can either be a critical part of your subject that needs to have constant brightness, or a non-moving and consistently-lit surface over your series of images.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/exposure.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "There are two ways of setting the target brightness for your control sample:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/exposure.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. If you know or expect an arbitrary lightness for the control sample (for example, a gray card, a color chart, a product or a logo of a specified brightness), you can set its L value directly, in CIE Lab 1976 space,\n2. If you simply want to match the development of your reference image, set the _spot mode_ to _measure_, then enable the color picker (to the right of the _exposure_ slider) and draw a rectangle over your control sample. The _input_ column will then be updated with the lightness value of the control sample before the exposure correction, and the _target_ column will show the resulting lightness of the control sample after the current exposure setting is applied.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/exposure.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If you reset the lightness value, the default value is 50% (middle-gray) -- this can be useful to quickly set the average exposure of any image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/exposure.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When you open a new image, the _spot mode_ is automatically reset to _correction_. Using the color picker attached to the exposure slider, you can then directly reselect your control sample in the new image. The proper exposure setting required for the control sample to match the memorized target lightness will be automatically computed, and the setting will be updated in the same operation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/exposure.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Note 1:**  Trying to match lightness of moving parts of a subject across frames can prove tricky because they can have legitimate changes in their illumination as their orientation changes with respect to the main light source. For example, a part of the face can be fully lit in some frames and partially shaded in others. An inconsistently-lit control sample will generally not provide a robust reference for lightness matching across a series and may result in more work than manually matching it with visual feedback.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/exposure.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Note 2:** The exposure module works (by default) in the scene-referred, linear, camera RGB part of the pixel pipeline, before the input color profile is applied. However, the conversion from camera RGB to CIE Lab 1976 space relies on the input color profile. All of the lightness L metrics given in the _spot mapping_ settings will use the input profile defined later in the [_input color profile module_](./input-color-profile.md) to perform the conversion accurately, but the conversion itself assumes a linear (RAW) signal and will not work for JPEG and PNG images (which are non-linearly encoded before the _input color profile_ module). If you want to use this feature on non-RAW images, you will need to move the exposure module to after _input color profile_ or to use the [_module order_](../pixelpipe/the-pixelpipe-and-module-order.md) preset _v3.0 for JPEG/non-RAW input_.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/exposure.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Note 3:** Perfectly matching your control sample against the target lightness may still not yield a similar perceptual result, even if the numbers are exactly the same. For example, if your subject sits in front of a background made of some bright parts and some dark parts, the ratio of bright areas / dark areas will affect the perception of contrast and brightness. If this ratio changes across your series, the subject brightness will not appear constant even though the lightness value is exactly constant. For more details, see the [checker shadow illusion](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Checker_shadow_illusion) and the [Chubb illusion](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chubb_illusion).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/denoise-profiled.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Denoise (profiled)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/denoise-profiled.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "An easy to use and highly efficient denoise module, adapted to the individual noise profiles of a wide range of camera sensors.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/denoise-profiled.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "One issue with a lot of denoising algorithms is that they assume that the variance of the noise is independent of the luminosity of the signal. By profiling the noise characteristics of a camera's sensor at different ISO settings, the variance at different luminosities can be assessed, and the denoising algorithm can be adjusted to more evenly smooth out the noise.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/denoise-profiled.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Currently, Ansel has sensor noise profiles for over 300 popular camera models from all the major manufacturers. If you generate your own noise profile for a camera that is not yet supported by Ansel, be sure to share it with the Ansel development team so they can include it in the next release! Please see Ansel's [camera support](https://github.com/darktable-org/darktable/wiki/Camera-support) page for more information.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/denoise-profiled.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The _denoise (profiled)_ module implements two algorithms, each of which is available in either an easy-to-use \"auto\" mode, or a more advanced manual mode with additional controls:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/denoise-profiled.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "non-local means\n: This algorithm works in the spatial domain in much the same way as the [_astrophoto denoise_](./astrophoto-denoise.md) module. It averages each pixel with some surrounding pixels in the image. The weight of such a pixel in the averaging process depends on the similarity of its neighborhood with the neighborhood of the pixel being denoised. A patch with a defined size is used to measure that similarity.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/denoise-profiled.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": Note that this algorithm is quite resource-intensive.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/denoise-profiled.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "wavelets (default)\n: This algorithm works in the wavelet domain, and provides a simplified user interface. Wavelet decomposition allows you to adjust the denoise strength depending on the coarseness of the noise in the image. This mode can be used in either _Y0U0V0 color mode_ (which allows you to independently control luminance and chroma noise) or _RGB color mode_ (which allows you to independently control noise for each RGB channel).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/denoise-profiled.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": The wavelet algorithm is less resource-intensive than _non-local means_.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/denoise-profiled.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Luma versus chroma noise"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/denoise-profiled.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Both “non-local means” and “wavelet” algorithms can efficiently tackle luma (lightness) noise and chroma (color) noise.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/denoise-profiled.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In the past, it was suggested that you use two separate instances of this module to tackle _chroma_ and _luma_ noise independently (using _chroma_ and _lightness_ blending modes). This is no longer recommended, since the _denoise (profiled)_ module is placed before the _input color profile_ module in the pixelpipe (so that the profile parameters are accurate) and color blending modes should only be used after the input color profile has been applied.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/denoise-profiled.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The new algorithms in this module now provide their own methods to separately handle luma and chroma noise, and in both cases this can be handled with a single module instance.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/denoise-profiled.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The _denoise (profiled)_ module provides some controls that are independent of the algorithm used. These are described first, before moving on to the algorithm-specific controls.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/denoise-profiled.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When describing the controls specific to an algorithm, we will first cover the simplified interface, and then move on to the more advanced controls for that algorithm.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/denoise-profiled.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Note that sliders are provided with minimum and maximum values by default. However, these are only soft limits and, where needed, higher values can be entered by right-clicking on the slider and typing a new value.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/denoise-profiled.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Common controls"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/denoise-profiled.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "profile\n: Ansel automatically determines the camera model and ISO based on the Exif data of your raw file, and searches for a corresponding profile in its database. If your image has an intermediate ISO value, settings will be interpolated between the two closest datasets in the database, and this interpolated setting will show up as the first line in the combo box. You can also manually override this selection if necessary. Re-selecting the top-most entry in the combo box will return you to the default profile.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/denoise-profiled.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "mode\n: Choose which denoising algorithm to use (see above), and whether to present the simplified (\"auto\") or full manual interface for that algorithm.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/denoise-profiled.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "whitebalance-adaptive transform\n: As the white balance amplifies each of the RGB channels differently, each channel exhibits different noise levels. This checkbox makes the selected algorithm adapt to the white balance adjustments. This option should be disabled on the second instance if you have applied a first instance with a color blend mode.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/denoise-profiled.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "adjust autoset parameters (auto modes only)\n: Automatically adjust all the other parameters on the current denoising algorithm using a single slider. This is particularly useful when you have had to increase the exposure on an under-exposed image, which normally introduces additional noise (as if you had taken the shot with a higher ISO). This control compensates for that by using settings similar to those used for a higher ISO image. The \"effective ISO\" used by the denoise algorithm is the actual ISO used, multiplied by the value of this slider.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/denoise-profiled.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "strength\n: Fine-tune the strength of the denoising. The default value has been chosen to maximize the peak signal-to-noise ratio. It's mostly a matter of taste -- whether you prefer a low noise level at the cost of fine details, or you accept more noise to better preserve fine detail.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/denoise-profiled.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "preserve shadows (advanced mode only)\n: Lower this control to denoise the shadows more aggressively. Usually, as noise increases, you will need to decrease this parameter.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/denoise-profiled.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "bias correction (advanced mode only)\n: Correct any color cast that may appear in the shadows. Increase this value if dark shadows appear too green, decrease if they appear too purple.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/denoise-profiled.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Non-local means auto sliders"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/denoise-profiled.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "central pixel weight (details)\n: Control the amount of detail that should be preserved by the denoising algorithm. By default, this will have a low value, meaning that the algorithm will treat both luma and chroma noise equally. Move this slider to the right to reduce the amount of luma denoising, so that the algorithm will primarily affect chroma noise. By adjusting this slider together with the _strength_ slider, you can find a good balance between luma and chroma denoising.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/denoise-profiled.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Non-local means advanced sliders"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/denoise-profiled.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When you take non-local means out of auto mode, the _adjust autoset parameters_ slider is replaced with the following controls. You can use the auto-adjust slider to arrive at some initial settings then, when you switch to manual mode, the sliders will show the equivalent manual settings. You can then continue to fine-tune the manual settings from the auto-set starting point.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/denoise-profiled.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "patch size\n: Control the size of the patches being matched when deciding which pixels to average -- see [_astrophoto denoise_](./astrophoto-denoise.md) for more information. Increase this for images with more noise but be aware that high values may smooth out fine edges. The effect of this slider on processing time is minimal.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/denoise-profiled.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "search radius\n: Control how far away from a pixel the algorithm will attempt to find similar patches. Increasing the value can give better results for very noisy images when coarse grain noise is visible, but the processing time is hugely impacted by this parameter (the processing time increases with the square of this parameter). A lower value will make execution faster, a higher value will make it slower. It most cases it is better to use the scattering parameter, which has a similar effect but without the heavy processing cost.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/denoise-profiled.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "scattering (coarse-grain noise)\n: Like the _search radius_, this slider controls how far from a pixel the algorithm will attempt to find similar patches. However, it does this without increasing the number of patches considered. As such, processing time will stay about the same. Increasing the value will reduce coarse grain noise, but may smooth local contrast. This slider is particularly effective at reducing chroma noise.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/denoise-profiled.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Wavelet curves"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/denoise-profiled.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Wavelet curves are shown when one of the “wavelet” modes is selected.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/denoise-profiled.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The noise in an image usually consists of both fine grain and coarse grain noise, to varying degrees. The wavelet curves allow the strength of the denoising to be adjusted depending on the coarseness of the visible noise. The left end of the curve will act on very coarse grain noise, while the right of the curve will act on very fine grain noise. Raising the curve will increase the amount of smoothing, while lowering the curve will decrease it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/denoise-profiled.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "For example, you can preserve very-fine-grain noise by pulling the right-most part of the curve down. When tackling chroma noise (e.g. on a U0V0 curve, or on a second module instance with a color blend mode) you can safely raise the right side of the curve, since colors do not change a lot on fine scales. This can be useful if you see some noisy isolated pixels with the wrong color.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/denoise-profiled.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Wavelets _Y0U0V0_ color mode"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/denoise-profiled.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The preferred way to use wavelets is with the _Y0U0V0_ color mode. This mode separates the denoising curves into luminance (_Y0_) and color (_U0V0_) components. You can then use the _Y0_ curve to control the level of luma denoising, and the _U0V0_ curve to control the level of chroma denoising.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/denoise-profiled.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "![denoise-y0u0v0](denoise-y0u0v0.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/denoise-profiled.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Wavelets _RGB_ color mode"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/denoise-profiled.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Before the _Y0U0V0_ color mode was introduced, wavelet-based denoising could only be performed directly on the _R_, _G_ and _B_ channels, either together or individually.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/denoise-profiled.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "![denoise-rgb](denoise-rgb.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/denoise-profiled.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If you want to denoise the RGB channels independently, the best way to do this is to use an instance of the [_color calibration_](./color-calibration.md) module placed immediately before the _denoise (profiled)_ module so that it outputs a gray channel based on the _red_ channel only, then denoise that monochrome image using the _red_ wavelet curve. Repeat this procedure for the blue and green channels. This procedure is time-consuming, but gives the best result because looking at the color of a noisy pixel is not a reliable way to determine which channel to adjust. For example, a noisy red pixel could be due to a noise peak on the red channel, but could also be due to a noise lull on the blue and green channels.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/denoise-profiled.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The issue with independently denoising the RGB channels is that there can still be some residual chroma noise at the end that requires excessive smoothing to eliminate. This was in fact one of the key motivations behind implementing the _Y0U0V0_ color mode.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/denoise-profiled.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Wavelets advanced sliders"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/denoise-profiled.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When you take _wavelets_ out of _auto_ mode, the _adjust autoset parameters_ slider is replaced with the _preserve shadows_ and _bias correction_ controls listed above in the [common controls](#common-controls) section.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Color balance"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "An advanced module which brings color-grading tools from cinematography into the photographic scene-referred pipeline.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This module is not suitable for beginners with no prior knowledge of color theory, who might want to stick to the _global chroma_ and _global vibrance_ settings until they have a good understanding of the [dimensions of color](../../../color-management/color-dimensions.md).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Introduction"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Color-grading is an important part of image editing. It can help to remove unwanted color casts and can also deliver a creative color twist that will add atmosphere to your images. In the days of film photography, most of the color ambiance was obtained with the film emulsion and the developing chemicals, with some color timing being performed under the enlarger with color heads. This consumed expensive resources and was mostly reserved for the cinema industry, where the job was performed by a colorist.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In the digital age, where raw images look flat and even, color-grading assumes the same role that film emulsions did, by re-introducing color shifts for aesthetic purposes. It can also serve to harmonize the color palette of a series of images (which may have been shot under different conditions) to achieve a consistent global look. For this task, the [vectorscope](../../toolboxes/scopes.md#vectorscope) is also extremely useful.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Colorists usually split color-grading into two distinct steps:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. _Primary color-grading_ aims to fix unwanted color casts and create a neutral starting point,\n2. _Secondary color-grading_ gives the image its final look and atmosphere.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Primary color-grading is best left to the [_color calibration_](./color-calibration.md) module, which operates in a physical framework better suited to illuminant correction. _color balance_, on the other hand, is mostly concerned with secondary color-grading. Performing a truly neutral primary color-grading should make the secondary color-grading easy to transfer between images (via styles, presets or copy & paste) with a similar effect.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The _color balance_ module is an improvement over the [American Society of Cinematographers Color Decision List](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ASC_CDL) (ASC CDL), and uses alpha masks to allow the effect to be properly split between shadows and highlights. The classic CDL acts on the entire luminance range, and each of its parameters is given more weight on some parts of the image only as a side-effect of the mathematics.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This module works, for the most part (_4 ways_, _chroma_, _vibrance_, _contrast_), in a linear RGB color space designed specifically for color-grading. This color space exhibits a uniform spacing of perceptual hues while retaining a physically-scaled luminance[^1]. The perceptual part of the module (_saturation_ and _brilliance_) works in the JzAzBz[^2] color space, which provides a perceptual scaling of both lightness and chromaticity suitable for HDR images. Both color spaces ensure that saturation and chroma changes take place at constant hue, which is not the case for most other saturation operators in Ansel (notably in the older [_color balance_](./color-balance.md) module).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "[^1]: Richard A. Kirk, Chromaticity coordinates for graphic arts based on CIE 2006 LMS with even spacing of Munsell colours, 2019.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "[^2]: Safdar et al., Perceptually uniform color space for image signals including high dynamic range and wide gamut, 2017. <https://doi.org/10.1364/OE.25.015131>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The _color balance_ module expects a scene-referred linear input and produces a scene-referred RGB output, which may or may not be linear, depending on the module settings (_contrast_ and _power_ will delinearize the output).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "At its output, _color balance_ checks that the graded colors fit inside the pipeline RGB color space (Rec 2020 by default) and applies a soft saturation clipping at constant hue, aiming to retarget out-of-gamut color to the nearest in-gamut color by scaling both chroma and lightness. This prevents the chroma and saturation settings from pushing colors outside of the valid range and allows more drastic adjustments to be safely used.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "_Note that this module abides by the CIE definitions of chroma and saturation, as explained in the [dimensions of color](../../../color-management/color-dimensions.md) section._\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Master tab"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "hue shift\n: Rotate all colors in the image by an angle over the chromaticity plane, at constant luminance and chroma. You can use this control to remove spilled colored light on a subject or to quickly change the color of some object. This setting is usually best applied locally, using [masks](../../../views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/_index.md).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "global vibrance\n: This affects the chroma dimension of color over the entire image, prioritizing those colors with low chroma. This allows the chroma of neutral colors to be increased without exaggerating already-colorful pixels.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "contrast\n: This setting is applied on the luminance channel at constant hue and chroma. The fulcrum setting (in the [_masks_](#masks-tab) tab, under _contrast gray fulcrum_) allows you to set the neutral point of the contrast curve:\n: - at the fulcrum, the contrast curve leaves the luminance unchanged,\n: - below the fulcrum, the contrast curve decreases the luminance for positive contrast values, or increases it for negative values,\n: - above the fulcrum, the contrast curve increases the luminance for positive contrast values, or decreases it for negative values.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": The fulcrum has a value of 18.45% by default, which is consistent with the current scene-referred workflow and should fit most use cases (assuming that global brightness has been fixed as recommended using the [_exposure_](./exposure.md) module).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": The contrast algorithm gives natural results that mimic the central part of the contrast curve of analog film. However, it will also increase the image's dynamic range, which may void _filmic_ settings in the pipe. For global contrast adjustments, you should normally use the [_tone equalizer_](./tone-equalizer.md) module -- the _color balance_ contrast slider is best used with masks, e.g. for selective corrections over the foreground or background.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Linear chroma grading"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Linear chroma grading affects the chroma dimension proportionally to its input value, at constant hue and luminance. It does this globally, with a flat coefficient (using the _global chroma_), as well as on each of the _shadows_, _mid-tones_ and _highlights_ masks (defined in the [_masks_](#masks-tab) tab under _luminance ranges_).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Perceptual saturation grading"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Perceptual saturation grading affects both the luminance and the chroma dimensions, in a perceptual space, proportionally to its input value, at constant hue. It does this globally, with a flat coefficient (using the _global saturation_), as well as on each of the _shadows_, _mid-tones_ and _highlights_ masks (defined in the [_masks_](#masks-tab) tab under _luminance ranges_).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Perceptual brilliance grading"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Perceptual brilliance grading affects both the luminance and the chroma dimensions, in a perceptual space, proportionally to its input value, at constant hue, and in a direction orthogonal to the saturation. Its effect is close to that of changing exposure, but scaled perceptually. It does this globally, with a flat coefficient (using the _global saturation_), as well as on each of the _shadows_, _mid-tones_ and _highlights_ masks (defined in the [_masks_](#masks-tab) tab under _luminance ranges_).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "4 ways tab"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Each of the settings in the 4 ways tab is composed of the same three components, which define a color using independent coordinates:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. _luminance_,\n2. _hue_,\n3. _chroma_.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Color input like this defines a color shift applied to the image globally or over the specified luminance range.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Each hue slider has a color picker, which may be used to compute the opponent color of the selected region. This is useful to revert unwanted color casts (e.g. skin redness), since shifting the color to its opponent cast neutralizes it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Global offset"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This is equivalent to the ASC CDL _offset_ and falls back to adding a constant RGB value to all pixels, quite like the _black offset_ in the _exposure_ module. This control does not use masking.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Shadows lift"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This is conceptually equivalent to the _lift_ from _lift/gamma/gain_, although implemented differently, and falls back to multiplying the masked pixels by a constant RGB value. It is applied using the _shadows_ mask.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Highlights gain"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This is equivalent to the ASC CDL _slope_, and falls back to multiplying a the masked pixels by a constant RGB value. It is applied using the _highlights_ mask.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Global power"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This is equivalent to the ASC CDL _power_, and falls back to applying a constant RGB exponent. It is not masked and needs to be normalized, since the power function has a different behaviour above and below 1, and we are in an unbounded pipeline where white is typically greater than 1. The normalization parameter is available in the [_masks_](#masks-tab) tab under _white fulcrum_.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Masks tab"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This tab defines auxiliary controls for the previous tabs. Masking controls typically don't require any user modification since the defaults are calibrated to suit most needs and fulfil the normal scene-referred pixel pipeline expectations. You should only need to change these settings in specific scenarios.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Luminance ranges"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The graphs show the opacity (on the _y_ axis) of the 3 luminance masks relative to the pixel luminance (on the _x_ axis). The darkest curve represents the _shadows_ mask, the brightest represents the _highlights_ mask, and the third curve represents the _mid-tones_ mask.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Only the _shadows_ and _highlights_ masks can be controlled directly -- the _mid-tones_ mask is computed indirectly from the others and acts as an adjustment variable.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "shadows fall-off\n: Control the softness or hardness of the transition from fully opaque (100%) to fully transparent (0%) for the shadows mask.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "mask middle-gray fulcrum\n: Set the luminance value where all three masks have 50% opacity. In practice, this is used to define how the image is separated into shadows and highlights.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "highlights fall-off\n: Control the softness or hardness of the transition from fully opaque (100%) to fully transparent (0%) for the highlights mask.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "For each of these settings, a mask button, provided to the right of the slider, displays the appropriate mask (shadows, mid-tones, highlights), overlaid as a checker board. The still-visible area of the image (not hidden by the mask) is the area that will be affected by the shadows, mid-tones and highlights sliders in the other tabs.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "All mask previews display the output of the module, including any color changes made, so you can also activate them while editing, to see only the affected part of the image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Luminance masks are computed at the input of the module, which means that they are insensitive to any luminance changes made inside the module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Thresholds"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "white fulcrum\n: Set the white point luminance in EV. This is used to normalize the _power_ setting in the [_4 ways_](#4-ways-tab) tab. Display-referred implementations of power functions assume that white is at 100%, which removes the need for normalization. For scene-referred purposes this needs to be taken into account.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": The color picker to the right of the slider automatically sets the white fulcrum to the maximum luminance from the selected region, which should be sufficient in most cases.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "contrast gray fulcrum\n: Set the fulcrum for the _contrast_ setting in the [_master_](#master-tab) tab. This corresponds to the luminance value that will be left unchanged by the contrast adjustment. This setting usually matches the middle-gray linear value. If you followed the scene-referred workflow recommendations and set the global brightness early in the pipeline, using the _exposure_ module, the correct value should usually be around 18-20%.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": The color picker to the right of the slider automatically sets the contrast gray fulcrum to the average luminance from the selected region. This relies on the assumption that the average luminance is usually close to middle-gray, which is not true if you have specular highlights or primary light sources in the frame, or for low/high-key images.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Saturation formula"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Note that this setting is not really appropriate for the _masks_ tab (since it is not technically related to the masks) but is placed here because it is not meant to be used regularly and in the spirit of saving some display real-estate. Two options are provided:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "JzAzBz (2021)\n: This mode is the original saturation algorithm. It uses the JzAzBz uniform color space (UCS) to compute the saturation. This color space is not meant for color changes and its lightness does not account for the [Helmholtz-Kohlrausch effect](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Helmholtz%E2%80%93Kohlrausch_effect), which states that colorful colors will look brighter than neutral or near-neutral colors (greys and pastels) having the same luminance. It also suffers from non-smooth behaviour near black, with colors being darkened too much.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel UCS (2022)\n: The Ansel Uniform Color Space has been [designed from the ground up](https://eng.aurelienpierre.com/2022/02/color-saturation-control-for-the-21th-century/), using psychoperceptual measurement datasets, for the sole purpose of the color manipulation (saturation) performed by this module. This color space does account for the [Helmholtz-Kohlrausch effect](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Helmholtz%E2%80%93Kohlrausch_effect) and has a built-in gamut mapping formula that is more accurate and efficient than can be achieved in JzAzBz. It displays a smoother behaviour which makes saturation changes more even across the lightness range.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Mask preview settings"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "These settings apply to the mask previews, displayed by clicking the mask buttons in the _luminance ranges_ section. These settings are saved globally, so will be applied to all subsequent images unless changed.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "checker board color 1 and 2\n: Set the two colors for the background checker board mask underlay. You can set them to opponent colors of the current image to aid legibility.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "checker board size\n: Set the width of the checker board cells in pixels (adjusted according to the display's DPI setting).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "FAQ"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Saturation or chroma?"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "As described in the [dimensions of color](../../../color-management/color-dimensions.md) section, saturation and chroma roam the (lightness, chroma) plane in different directions. In addition, the chroma of _color balance_ uses a scene-referred linear space, while the saturation uses a perceptual space, which rescales color for even spacing.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In practice, you should use the chroma setting if you want to preserve the scene-linearity of the light emission and/or keep the luminance unchanged. However, these changes might affect some hues more heavily than others, due to the fact that the color space is not fully perceptually-scaled.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Saturation is closer to the effect of mixing white paint with some base color. Reducing the saturation of red will degrade it to pink, while reducing its chroma will degrade to a gray shade at the same luminance. Saturation is perhaps a more intuitive way to interact with color, due to its connection with painting.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Choosing one or the other is mostly a matter of deciding where on the (lightness, chroma) graph you want to push your colors, and where they are to begin with. To reach pastel colors, saturation is the way to go. To reach laser-like colors (almost monochromatic), at the risk of looking synthetic, chroma is the way to go.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "What is the connection with lift/gamma/gain?"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The _lift/gamma/gain_ algorithm relies on a display-referred color space, since it assumes a bounded and symmetric dynamic range, with white point at 100% and gray at 50%. As such, it is simply unusable in a scene-referred space. However, the only incompatible part is the _lift_. The _gamma_ is exactly the ASC CDL _power_, and the _gain_ is exactly the ASC CDL _slope_.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The _color balance_ module simply has two slopes instead of one: the _gain_, applied on the highlights extracted from the whole image by a mask, and the _lift_, applied similarly but on the shadows.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Changing contrast"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "While _color balance_ is mostly about color (other modules handle the global contrast in chromaticity-preserving ways) luminance is as much a part of color as hue or chroma, and it needs to be dealt with here too, because the perception of saturation relies on it. If you wish to turn red into pink, for example, reducing its chroma will turn it gray, so you need to increase its luminance as well.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "There are several ways to change the contrast in _color balance_, either locally (with masks) or globally (without):\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- In the [_master_](#master-tab) tab, use the _contrast_ setting (possibly alongside the _contrast gray fulcrum_ in the [_masks_](#masks-tab) tab). Be aware that this will raise the white point and therefore increase the dynamic range of the image, which may void filmic settings later in the pipeline.\n- In [_perceptual saturation grading_](#perceptual-saturation-grading), desaturate highlights and resaturate shadows to produce a luminance contrast boost,\n- In [_perceptual brilliance grading_](#perceptual-brilliance-grading), add brilliance in the highlights and remove brilliance in the shadows to produce to a luminance contrast boost,\n- In the [_4 ways_](#4-ways-tab) tab, set the _shadows lift_ luminance to negative values and the _highlights gain_ luminance to positive values, which also produces a luminance contrast boost.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The difference between these methods is how the effect will be weighted relative to the input of the module. You are advised to do the majority of luminance contrast adjustments in the _filmic_ and _tone equalizer_ modules, and then undertake final changes in _color balance_ while examining the colors.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Internal processing"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The following is the internal order of operations within the module:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. Transform from pipeline RGB to Kirk/Filmlight Ych space,\n2. Apply hue shift at constant chroma and constant luminance,\n3. Compute luminance masks with Y,\n4. Apply the _linear chroma_ and _vibrance_ settings at constant hue and luminance,\n5. Transform to Kirk/Filmlight RGB space,\n6. Apply the _4 ways_ settings (except _luminance power_),\n7. Transform to Kirk/Filmlight Yrg space,\n8. Apply _luminance power_ and _contrast_ on Y,\n9. Transform to JzAzBz space,\n10. Apply the _perceptual saturation_ and _perceptual brilliance_ settings,\n11. Soft-clip the chroma using pipeline RGB gamut at constant hue and lightness,\n12. Transform back to pipeline RGB.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Setting the global chroma to -100% will not produce a real monochrome image, as is customary with other algorithms. The reason for this is that the RGB space used has a D65 white point defined in CIE LMS 2006 space, while Ansel uses a white point defined in CIE XYZ 1931 space, and there is no exact conversion between these spaces. The result will therefore be a slighly tinted black & white image. If your intent is to get a real black & white image using the luminance channel, the _color calibration_ module offers a _B&W : luminance-based_ preset that does exactly the same thing but without the white-point discrepancy.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This module has its gamut-mapping (against pipeline RGB) permanently enabled. This means that if your original image contains some largely out-of-gamut colors to start with, simply enabling _color balance_ with no particular setting will slightly alter its colors. This is probably for the best.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The maximum saturation allowed in the pipeline working RGB space is recorded for each hue when initializing the module, and is later cached in a LUT (look-up table) to save performance. If the working profile is later changed, _color balance_ is not notified, meaning that it will not update its cached hue/saturation LUT. To force a LUT update, you can simply change any setting in the _color balance_ module, then change it back again. It is not recommended that you change the working RGB space half-way through an editing session, as this could result in unexpected chroma and hue changes.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-balance.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "For performance reasons, the non-linear conversions from and to the working RGB space are bypassed, meaning that the internal colorimetry will be wrong when using non-linear color spaces. Note that there is no reason to use non-linear spaces as working RGB since they make alpha blending more challenging for no benefit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lut-3D.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Lut 3D"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lut-3D.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Transform RGB values with a 3D LUT file.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lut-3D.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A 3D LUT is a tridimensional table that is used to transform a given RGB value into another RGB value. It is normally used for film simulation and color grading.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lut-3D.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This module accepts `.cube`, `.3dl` and `.png` (haldclut) files. Uncompressed 3D LUT data is not saved in the database or the XMP file, but is instead saved to the 3D LUT file path inside the 3D LUT root folder. It is therefore important to back up your 3D LUT folder properly -- sharing an image with its XMP is pointless if the recipient doesn't also have the same 3D LUT file in their own 3D LUT folder.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lut-3D.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The compressed G'MIC format `.gmz` **is no longer supported**. If you have `.gmz` files, see [Converting `.gmz` files](#converting-gmz-files) below.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lut-3D.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Note**: the module clips all values outside of the range [0,1]. You may have to reduce the range of the input before applying.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lut-3D.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "LUTs are most commonly used in Ansel for color grading or film look simulation. For this reason, by default, the module is placed after the [_filmic_](./filmic.md) module in the pixelpipe and should be applied to a neutral image (without first applying a specific look). While you can find hundreds of free LUTs on the internet, you should note that not all of them are compatible with the Ansel environment and workflow -- incompatible LUTs will not produce the advertised look. To limit the risk, a color grading LUT should have been created to work with one of the available \"application color spaces\" (see below), for both the input and the output of the module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lut-3D.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Camera log LUTs (as F-log or S-Log3) are different to color-grading and film-look-simulation LUTs, and are intended to convert the camera log raw data into something (linear raw data or other color space) that Ansel is able to understand. In this case the _lut 3D_ module should be manually placed between the [_demosaic_](./demosaic) and [_input color profile_](./input-color-profile.md) modules. Once you have done this, you can no longer choose an \"application color space\". The \"input profile\" of input color profile module should be aligned with the output of the LUT. _Please note that this use case has not yet been tested._\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lut-3D.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "file selection\n: Choose the 3D LUT file to use. File selection is inactive if the 3D LUT root folder has not been defined in [preferences > processing](../../../preferences-settings/processing.md).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lut-3D.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "application color space\n: A 3D LUT is defined relative to a specific color space. Choose the color space for which the selected 3D LUT file has been built. Cube files are usually related to REC.709 while most others are related to sRGB.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lut-3D.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "interpolation\n: This defines how to calculate output colors when input colors are not exactly on a node of the RGB cube described by the 3D LUT. There are three interpolation methods available: tetrahedral (default), trilinear and pyramid. Usually you won't see any difference between interpolation methods except with smaller sized LUTs.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lut-3D.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Converting `.gmz` files"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lut-3D.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "What changed, and why"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lut-3D.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel used to read `.gmz`, G'MIC's own compressed LUT container. Support for it has been removed, along with the G'MIC dependency itself.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lut-3D.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The reason is proportion. Ansel called exactly three functions from G'MIC — read a `.gmz`, decompress its keypoints, cache the result — and linking those three functions meant linking all of G'MIC: a 7.4 MB shared library that in turn pulls in around fifty more, among them libcurl, OpenSSL, libssh, LDAP, Kerberos and SASL — a complete HTTP and TLS stack, in a photo editor — plus an X11 display stack and G'MIC's own private copies of libpng, libjpeg, libtiff, OpenEXR, WebP and FFTW, duplicating decoders Ansel already links directly. G'MIC also required Ansel to raise the stack size of *every* thread it creates to 2 MiB, and it published its own OpenMP thread count process-wide, which silently overrode Ansel's \"CPU cores\" preference for anyone who set it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lut-3D.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "That was the cost. The benefit, measured over 61 days of anonymous usage statistics: the whole 3D LUT module accounts for 0.2 % of module activations, and `.gmz` is only a fraction of that — most people who use LUTs use `.cube`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lut-3D.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Nor is `.gmz` merely a container that could have been parsed in a few lines. Its keypoints are reconstructed either by a dense radial-basis-function solve or by a multiscale diffusion PDE, both written in G'MIC's own scripting language. Reimplementing and then maintaining two numerical solvers in Ansel, forever, to read a format that ships alongside a `.cube` equivalent, is not a trade worth making.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lut-3D.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "So the format is deprecated, and converting is a one-off.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lut-3D.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The easy way: download the `.cube` version"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lut-3D.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Most `.gmz` files in circulation are the G'MIC film-emulation CLUT collections, and **the same collections are published as `.cube`**. Before converting anything, check whether the pack you use offers a `.cube` download — for most people that is the whole procedure.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lut-3D.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Converting with the G'MIC command line"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lut-3D.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "G'MIC's own command-line tool reads `.gmz` and writes `.cube`. Install it:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lut-3D.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| Platform | Command |\n|---|---|\n| Debian / Ubuntu | `sudo apt install gmic` |\n| Fedora | `sudo dnf install gmic` |\n| Arch | `sudo pacman -S gmic` |\n| macOS (Homebrew) | `brew install gmic` |\n| Windows | Binaries from [gmic.eu](https://gmic.eu/download.html) |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lut-3D.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A `.gmz` can hold a single LUT or a whole library of them, each with a name. This command converts **every** LUT in the file, one `.cube` per LUT, keeping the original names:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (sh)
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lut-3D.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "mkdir -p converted\ngmic input.gmz foreach { nm={n} decompress_clut 33,33,33 output_cube converted/$nm.cube }\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lut-3D.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Reading it left to right: load the `.gmz`; for each LUT it contains, remember the LUT's name, decompress its keypoints into a 33×33×33 cube, and write that cube out under the remembered name. Capturing `{n}` *before* `decompress_clut` matters — the decompression step replaces the image name with an internal expression, so a naive `output_cube {n}.cube` produces files called things like `(256;0^256;255^256;1).cube`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lut-3D.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "`33,33,33` is the output resolution, and 33 is the conventional size for a `.cube`. You can ask for more (`65,65,65`) at the cost of a much larger file and a slower conversion; the keypoints hold no more detail either way, so this only changes how finely the reconstruction is sampled.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lut-3D.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "For a single-LUT file where you do not care about the name, the short form is enough:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (sh)
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lut-3D.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "gmic input.gmz decompress_clut 33,33,33 output_cube output.cube\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lut-3D.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Decompression is not instant — it is solving a scattered-data interpolation problem — so expect a few seconds per LUT.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lut-3D.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Converting with the GIMP plugin"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lut-3D.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If you would rather not use a terminal, the G'MIC-Qt plugin for GIMP can do it: open the `.gmz`, and in the CLUT filters set the output mode to *Save CLUT as .cube or .png File*.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lut-3D.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Afterwards, in Ansel"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lut-3D.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Put the converted `.cube` files in your 3D LUT root folder (set in [preferences > processing](../../../preferences-settings/processing.md)), then select the LUT again in the module. The result is identical: the `.cube` holds the same reconstructed LUT that Ansel used to compute from the `.gmz` on every load, so this is also slightly faster to open.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lut-3D.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Existing edits that referenced a `.gmz` **will not be silently altered**. Ansel detects them and applies no LUT at all, rather than guessing; in the darkroom you get a dialog naming the file, and elsewhere a line in the log identifying the image. Those edits need the LUT re-selected once, pointing at the converted `.cube`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/lut-3D.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This applies as well to edits made with a much older version, which stored the compressed LUT data *inside* the edit rather than referencing a file. Ansel recognises those too, and the remedy is the same.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-look-up-table.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Color look up table"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-look-up-table.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A generic color look up table implemented in Lab space.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-look-up-table.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The input to this module is a list of source and target points and the complete mapping is interpolated using splines. The resulting look up tables (LUTs) are editable by hand and can be created using the darktable-chart utility to match given input (such as hald-cluts and RAW/JPEG with in-camera processing pairs).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-look-up-table.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "color board\n: The color board grid shows a list of colored patches. The colors of the patches are the source points. The target color of the selected patch is shown as offsets which are controlled by sliders beneath the color board. An outline is drawn around patches that have been altered (where the source and target colors differ).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-look-up-table.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": Click a patch to select it, or use the combo box or color picker. The currently-selected patch is marked with a white square, and its number is displayed in the combo box below.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-look-up-table.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": By default, the module will load the 24 patches of a classic color checker and initialise the mapping to identity (no change to the image). Configurations with more than 24 patches are shown in a 7x7 grid.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-look-up-table.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "interaction\n: To modify the color mapping, you can change the source and target colors, though the main use is to change the target colors.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-look-up-table.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": Start with an appropriate palette of source colors (either from the presets menu or from a style you have downloaded). You can then change the lightness (L), green-red (a), blue-yellow (b), or saturation \\(c\\) of the patches' target values with the sliders.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-look-up-table.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": To change the source color of a patch you can select a new color from your image by using the color picker and Shift+click on the patch you want to replace.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-look-up-table.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": Double-click a patch to reset it; Right-click a patch to delete it; Shift+click on empty space to add a new patch (with the currently picked color as the source).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/curve.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Curve"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/curve.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A classic digital photography tool to alter an image's tones using curves.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/curve.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Activate the color picker on the left to show the picked values in the graph (Ctrl+click or right-click to use the picker in area mode). Numerical (Lab) values of the input and output (see below) at the selected spot or area are shown at the top left of the widget.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/curve.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A second color picker to the right can be used to automtaically create new nodes based on the sampled area. Ctrl+click+drag to alter the created nodes to have a positive curve for the selected area; Shift+click+drag to create a negative curve.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/curve.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "mode\n: RGB is a linear color space designed to capture and display images in additive synthesis. It is related to the capture and display media and does not isolate color and lightness information in the same way that Lab and XYZ color spaces do. This module works in ProPhoto RGB. Adding contrast in RGB space is known to desaturate highlights and boost saturation in the shadows, but this has been proven to be the most reliable way to edit contrast, and is the standard method in most software\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/curve.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": Depending on the desired intent you can apply the RGB curve in two different modes\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/curve.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": - _RGB, linked channels_: Apply the L-channel curve to all three channels in the RGB color space.\n: - _RGB, independent channels_: R, G and B curves can be adjusted independently.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/curve.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "compensate middle gray\n: Select this to change the histogram display in the module. This option does not alter the processing but may assist when editing the curve.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/raw-chromatic-aberrations.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Raw chromatic aberrations"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/raw-chromatic-aberrations.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Correct chromatic aberrations of raw images.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/raw-chromatic-aberrations.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "As this module operates on non-demosaiced raw data, the underlying model assumes an uncropped photographic image as input. It is therefore likely to fail when you zoom in, as it is only able to operate on the visible portion of the image. The module will therefore be bypassed at high zoom levels, and a \"bypassed\" message will appear at the top of the module. This limitation only applies to interactive work and not to the final export.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/raw-chromatic-aberrations.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This module currently only works for raw images recorded with a Bayer sensor (the sensor used in the majority of cameras) -- for other types of image, you should use the [_chromatic aberrations_](./chromatic-aberrations.md) module instead.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/raw-chromatic-aberrations.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The module will also not apply any corrections to any photos that have been identified as monochrome by their metadata, like the files produced by the Leica M10 Monochrom.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/raw-chromatic-aberrations.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "iterations\n: The number of iterations. For most images, \"twice\" is sufficient, and is the default value. Occasionally, increasing this control can give better results.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/raw-chromatic-aberrations.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "avoid colorshift\n: If the module causes purple tinting, tick this box to apply a correction.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Filmic"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Remap the tonal range of an image by reproducing the tone and color response of classic film.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This module can be used either to expand or to contract the dynamic range of the scene to fit the dynamic range of the display. It protects colors and contrast in the mid-tones, recovers the shadows, and compresses bright highlights and dark shadows. Highlights will need extra care when details need to be preserved (e.g. clouds).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The module is derived from another module of the same name in [Blender 3D modeller](https://www.blender.org/) by T. J. Sobotka. While it is primarily intended to recover high-dynamic-range images from raw sensor data it can be used with any image. The following video (by the developer of this module) provides a useful introduction: [filmic: remap any dynamic range in darktable 3](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zbPj_TqTF880).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "_filmic_ is the successor to the _filmic (legacy)_ module from darktable 2.6. While the underlying principles have not changed much, the default settings and their assumptions have, so users of the previous version should not expect a 1:1 translation of their workflow to the new version.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Note**: Despite the technical look of this module, the best way to set it up is to assess the quality of the visual result. Do not overthink the numbers that are presented in the GUI to quantify the strength of the effects.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In order to get the best from this module, your images need some preparation:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "capturing (ETTR)\n: In-camera, it is recommended that you use a technique known as \"Expose To The Right\" (ETTR). This means exposing the shot so that the exposure is as bright as possible without clipping the highlights. It is called \"exposing to the right\" because the in-camera histogram should be touching all the way up to the right hand side without peaking at the right hand side (which could indicate clipping). This technique ensures you make maximum use of the dynamic range of your camera's sensor.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": The default auto-exposure metering mode in your camera will normally expose the image so that the average brightness in the image tends towards middle-gray. Sometimes, for scenes dominated by light tones, the camera will underexpose the image to bring those light tones more towards middle-gray. For scenes dominated by dark tones, it may over-expose the image and end up clipping the highlights. In such cases you can use the exposure compensation dial in your camera to raise or lower the exposure -- the Ansel exposure module can automatically take this into account when processing your image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": In some cases (e.g. specular highlights reflecting off shiny objects) it may be acceptable to have some clipping, but be aware that any clipped data in your image is irrevocably lost. Where data has been clipped, _filmic_ offers a \"highlight reconstruction\" feature to help mitigate the effects of the clipping and blend it smoothly with the rest of the image. The settings for this feature are on the [_reconstruct_](#reconstruct) tab. Some cameras also offer a \"highlight priority\" exposure metering mode that can help to maximise exposure while protecting the highlights, and many offer features such as \"zebras\" or \"blinkies\" in the live view to alert the photographer when parts of the image are being clipped.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "adjust for the mid-tones\n: In the [_exposure_](./exposure.md) module, adjust the exposure until the mid-tones are clear enough. Don't worry about losing the highlights at this point -- they will be recovered as part of the filmic processing. However, it is important to avoid negative pixels in black areas else the computations performed by _filmic_ may produce unpredictable results. For some camera models (Canon, mainly), rawspeed (the raw decoding library of Ansel) may set an exaggerated black level, resulting in crushed blacks and negative pixel values. If so, brighten the blacks by setting a negative black level correction value in the [_exposure_](./exposure.md) module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "white balance, denoise, demosaic\n: If you plan on using _filmic_'s auto-tuners, use the [_white balance_](./white-balance.md) module to first correct any color casts and obtain neutral colors. In RGB color spaces, luminance and chrominance are linked, and _filmic_'s luminance detection relies on accurate measurements of both. If your image is very noisy, add an initial step of denoising to improve the black exposure readings, and use a high quality [_demosaic_](./demosaic.md) algorithm. You don't need to worry about noise if you are planning to set up filmic manually, without using the auto-tuners.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The _filmic_ module is designed to map the dynamic range of the photographed scene (RAW image) to the dynamic range of the display.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This mapping is defined in three steps, each handled in a separate tab in the interface:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid " - The [_scene_](#scene) tab contains the “input” settings of the scene, defining what constitutes white and black in the photographed scene.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid " - The [_reconstruct_](#reconstruct) tab offers tools to handle blown highlights.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid " - The [_look_](#look) tab contains the artistic intent of the mapping that is applied to the input parameters (as defined in the scene tab). This part of the module applies an S-shaped parametric curve to enhance the contrast of the mid-tones and remap the gray value to the middle-gray of the display. As a general guideline, you should aim to increase the latitude as much as possible without clipping the extremes of the curve.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid " - The [_display_](#display) tab defines the output settings required to map the transformed image to the display. In typical use cases, the parameters in this tab rarely require adjustment.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid " - The [_options_](#options) tab includes some optional advanced settings and parameters.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "_filmic_ tends to compress local contrast, so after you have finished adjusting settings here, you may wish to compensate for this using the [_local contrast_](local-contrast.md) module. You may also want to increase the saturation in the [_color balance_](color-balance.md) module, and perhaps to further adjust the tones using the [_tone equalizer_](tone-equalizer.md).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The ranges of _filmic_'s sliders are limited to typical and safe values, but you can enter values outside of these limits by right-clicking and entering values with the keyboard.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Note**: _filmic_ cannot be set with entirely neutral parameters (resulting in a \"no-operation\") -- as soon as the module is enabled, the image is always at least slightly affected. You can, however, come close to neutral with the following settings:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- In the [_look_](#look) tab, set contrast to 1.0, latitude to 99 % and mid-tones saturation to 0 %,\n- In the [_options_](#options) tab, set contrast in shadows and in highlights to a _soft_ (polynomial) curve type.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In this configuration, filmic will only perform a logarithmic tone mapping between the bounds set in the [_scene_](#scene) tab.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Graphic display"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The graphic display at the top of the _filmic_ module offers multiple views to help you to understand its functionality. You can cycle through these views using the ![view-icon](view-icon.jpg) icon.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The following views are available:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "look only\n: This is the default view. The main bright curve shows how the dynamic range of the scene (in EV) is compressed into the display-referred output range. The orange dot shows the middle-gray point, the white dots either side mark out the latitude range, and the orange part of the curve at the bottom and top indicates an overshoot problem with the spline (the [_look_](#look) tab has some controls to deal with this).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": ![filmic-rgb-look-only](filmic-look-only.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "look + mapping (linear)\n: This view shows the mapping of input values [0,1] to output values in linear space, including the dynamic range mapping and the output transfer function. Note that in a scene-referred workflow, input values are allowed to exceed 1, however the graph only shows in/out values in the interval [0,1] in order to make the shape of the graph comparable to other tone curve mapping tools such as _base curve_ or _tone curve_. The actual value of the scene white point is shown in brackets on the _X axis_ (expressed as a percentage of an input value of 1).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": ![filmic-rgb-look-mapping-lin](filmic-look-mapping-lin.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "look + mapping (log)\n: The same as the previous view, but plotted in log space.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": ![filmic-rgb-look-mapping-lin](filmic-look-mapping-log.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "dynamic range mapping\n: This view is inspired by the Ansel Adams Zone System, showing how the zones in the input scene (EV) are mapped to the output. Middle gray from the scene is always mapped to 18% in the output (linear) space, and the view shows how the tonal ranges towards the extremes of the scene exposure range are compressed into a smaller number of zones in the display space, leaving more room for the mid-tones to be spread out over the remaining zones. The latitude range is represented by the darker gray portion in the middle.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": ![filmic-rgb-look-mapping-lin](filmic-dynamic-range-map.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Note:** When some parameters are too extreme, resulting in an unfeasible curve, _filmic_ will sanitize them internally. Sanitizing is illustrated in two ways on the look views:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- A dot becoming red indicates that the linear part of the curve is pushed too far towards the top or the bottom. In the [_look_](#look) tab, reduce the _latitude_ or recenter the linear part using the _shadows ↔ highlights balance_ parameter.\n- A dot becoming a half circle indicates that contrast is too low given the dynamic range of the image. Increase _contrast_ in the [_look_](#look) tab, or the _dynamic range_ in the [_scene_](#scene) tab.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Scene"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The controls in the _scene_ tab are similar in principle to those of the typical _levels_ tools, as found in other software. The difference is that _levels_ assumes display-referred pixels values (between 0 and 100%), whereas _filmic_ allows you to work on scene-referred pixels (between --infinity EV and +infinity EV), which forces the use of a different interface.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "middle-gray luminance (hidden by default)\n: This setting allows you to decide what luminance in the scene should be considered the reference middle-gray (which will be remapped to 18% in display). Use the color picker tool to read the average luminance over the drawn area. If you have a photograph of a gray card or a color chart (IT8 chart or colorchecker) shot in the scene lighting conditions, then the gray color picker tool can be used to quickly sample the luminance of the gray patch on that image. In other situations, the color picker can be used to sample the average luminance of the subject.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": This has an effect on the picture that is analogous to a brightness correction. Values close to 100% do not compress the highlights but fail to recover shadows. Values close to 0% greatly recover the shadows but compress the highlights more harshly and result in local-contrast losses.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": When modifying the middle-gray luminance, the white and black exposures are automatically adjusted accordingly, to prevent the dynamic range from clipping and to help you set the right parameter faster. If you are not happy with the auto adjustment performed by the gray slider, you can correct the white and black exposure parameters afterwards.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Note:** You are not advised to use this control to set middle-gray, hence it is now hidden by default. You should instead use the _exposure_ module to set the middle-gray level (see [_usage_](#usage), above). However, if you wish to make this slider visible, you can enable it with the _use custom mid-gray values_ checkbox in the [_options_](#options) tab.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "white relative exposure\n: The number of stops (EV) between the scene middle-gray luminance and the scene luminance to be remapped to display white (peak-white). This is the right bound of the scene dynamic range that will be represented on the display -- everything brighter than this value on the scene will be clipped (pure white) on the display. The color picker tool reads the maximum luminance in RGB space over the drawn area, assumes it is pure white, and sets the white exposure parameter to remap the maximum to 100% luminance.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "black relative exposure\n: The number of stops (EV) between the scene middle-gray luminance and the scene luminance to be remapped to display black (maximum density). This is the left bound of the scene dynamic range that wll be represented on the display -- everything darker than this value on the scene will be clipped (pure black) on the display. The color picker tool reads the minimum luminance in RGB space over the drawn area, assumes it is pure black, and sets the black exposure parameter to remap the minimum to 0% luminance. The black color picker measurement is very sensitive to noise, and cannot identify whether the minimum luminance is pure black (actual data) or just noise. It works better on low ISO pictures and with high quality demosaicing. When the color picker puts the black exposure at --16 EV, this is a sign that the measurement has failed and you will need to adjust it manually.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": The black relative exposure allows you to choose how far you want to recover lowlights.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "dynamic range scaling and auto-tune\n: The auto-tune color picker combines the above color pickers, and allows you to set the white and black exposures at the same time, using the maximum of the drawn region as the white and the minimum as the black. This gives good results in landscape photography but usually fails for portraits and indoor scenes.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": When no true white and black are available on the scene, the maximum and minimum RGB values read on the image are not valid assumptions any more. Dynamic range scaling symmetrically shrinks or enlarges the detected dynamic range and the current parameters. This works with both color pickers, and adjusts the current values of white and black relative exposures.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Note**: There is no direct relationship between your camera sensor's dynamic range (to be found in DxoMark.com or PhotonsToPhotos.org measurements) and the dynamic range in filmic (scene white EV -- scene black EV). Many things happen before _filmic_ in the pipeline (for example a black raw offset that could map black to 0) such that _filmic_ sees a theoretically infinite dynamic range at its input. This has to do only with pixel encoding manipulation in software, not actual sensor capabilities.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The _scene-referred_ workflow forces a black level correction of --0.0002, in the _exposure_ module, which ensures that the dynamic range seen by _filmic's_ input is around 12.3 EV most of the time. Decrease this value even more if setting the black relative exposure in _filmic_ to --16 EV fails to unclip blacks.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Reconstruct"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**This tab is deprecated and is hidden for new edits.** Use the dedicated [_highlight reconstruction_](./highlight-reconstruction.md) module instead.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Filmic's highlight reconstruction dates from a time when the dedicated module could only handle mosaiced Bayer raws. That module now reconstructs X-Trans sensors and already-demosaiced input (sRAW, linear DNG) as well, using _harmonic transposition_ by default, which is a far more accurate method than the first-order color propagation implemented here. There is therefore no longer any reason to reconstruct highlights in filmic, and doing it here has a real cost: it happens late in the pipeline, after color profiling, so it works on data whose highlights have already been clipped and demosaiced.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The recommended approach, in order:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. Leave [_highlight reconstruction_](./highlight-reconstruction.md) on its default _harmonic transposition_ method. This is enough for the large majority of images.\n2. If harmonic transposition alone does not fully recover a difficult area, add a **second instance** of _highlight reconstruction_ set to _guided laplacians_, stacked on top of the harmonic one, and mask it to the area that needs the extra work.\n3. Only if you are re-editing an existing image that already relies on the controls below should you keep using them.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "What happens to your existing edits:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- **New edits** get the _threshold_ below at its maximum, +16 EV, which disables the whole feature and hides this tab. The reconstruction code is skipped entirely, so it costs nothing.\n- **Existing edits** keep whatever _threshold_ they were saved with, this tab stays visible for them, and they render exactly as before. Nothing you have already developed will change.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The remainder of this section documents the controls as they behave for edits that still use them.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This tab provides controls that blend transitions between unclipped and clipped areas within an image and can also help to reconstruct colors from adjacent pixels. It is designed to handle spotlights that could not possibly be unclipped when taking the shot (such as naked light bulbs or the sun disc in the frame) and aims at diffusing their edges as film would do. It is not designed to recover large areas of clipped pixels or in-paint missing parts of the image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Firstly, a mask needs to be set up to identify the parts of the image that will be affected by the highlights reconstruction. There are then some additional controls to fine-tune some of the trade-offs made by the reconstruction algorithm.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "_highlights clipping_"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "These controls allow you to choose which areas of the image are impacted by the highlight reconstruction algorithms.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "threshold\n: Any pixels brighter than this threshold will be affected by the reconstruction algorithm. The units are in EV, relative to the white point set in the _scene_ tab. **The default is now +16 EV, the maximum, which disables the feature entirely** and hides this tab -- see the deprecation note above. Edits saved with an older version keep their own value (typically +3 EV). To use the feature on such an edit, click the _display highlight reconstruction mask_ icon to show the mask, and lower this threshold until the highlight areas you want to reconstruct are selected in white by the mask. It may be useful to first review the image using the [raw overexposed warning](../../toolboxes/raw-overexposed.md) to show you which pixels in the raw file have been clipped, and whether those pixels are clipped on just one RGB channel or all of them.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "transition\n: Use this control to soften the transition between clipped and valid pixels. Moving this control to the right will increase the amount of blur in the mask, so that the transition between clipped and non-clipped areas is softer. This allows for a smoother blending between the clipped and non-clipped regions. Moving this control to the left will reduce the blur in the mask, making the transition in the mask much sharper and therefore reducing the amount of feathering between clipped and non-clipped areas.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "display highlight reconstruction mask\n: Click on the icon to the right of this label to toggle the display of the highlight reconstruction mask. It is recommended that you turn this on while adjusting the above controls.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "_balance_"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "These controls allow you to balance the trade-offs between the various reconstruction algorithms.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "structure ↔ texture\n: Use this to control whether the reconstruction algorithm should favor painting in a smooth color gradient (structure), or trying to reconstruct the texture using sharp details extracted from unclipped pixel data (texture). By default, the control is in the middle at 0%, which favors both strategies equally. If you have lots of areas where all three channels are clipped, there is no texture detail available to reconstruct, so it is better to move the slider to the left to favor color reconstruction. If you have lots of areas where only one or two channels are clipped, then there may be some texture detail in the unclipped channel(s), and moving the slider to the right will place more emphasis on trying to reconstruct texture using this unclipped data.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "bloom ↔ reconstruct\n: Use this to control whether the algorithm tries to reconstruct sharp detail in the clipped areas (reconstruct), or apply a blur that approximates the blooming effect you get with traditional film (bloom). By default, this is set to 100%, which tries to maximise the sharpness of the detail in the clipped areas. Move this slider to the left if you want to introduce more blur in these areas. Introducing more blur will usually tend to darken the highlights as a by-product, which may lead to a more colorful reconstruction.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "gray ↔ colorful details\n: Use this to control whether the algorithm favors the recovery of monochromatic highlights (gray) or colorful details. Move the slider to the right if you want more color in the highlights. Move the slider to the left if you want to reduce the saturation of the highlights. It can be helpful to reduce the saturation in the highlights if you start seeing magenta or out-of-gamut colors.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Look"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When working on the _look_ tab, it is recommended that you monitor the S-curve spline on the _look only_ graph. This curve starts from the scene/display black levels at the bottom left of the graph, and should smoothly increase up to the scene/display white levels at the top right. Sometimes, if the constraints on the S-curve are too tight, the splines in the shadows and/or highlights regions can \"overshoot\" the limits of the display, and an orange warning is shown on those parts of the spline.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If you see the orange warning indicator at either end of the S-curve, corrective actions should be performed to bring the S-curve back to a smooth monotonically increasing curve. This may involve:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Reducing the latitude and/or contrast,\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Adjusting the shadows/highlights slider to shift the latitude and allow more room for the spline,\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Ensuring that the scene-referred black and white relative exposure sliders on the _scene_ tab have been properly set for the characteristics of the scene,\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Choosing a different curve type for the shadows/highlights contrast on the [_options_](#options) tab.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If the _target black luminance_ setting on the [_display_](#display) tab is non-zero, this can also make it difficult for _filmic_ to find a smooth monotonic spline, and reducing this can also help to relax the constraints. See the [_display_](#display) section to understand the implications of this.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "contrast\n: The _filmic_ S-curve is created by computing the position of virtual nodes from the module parameters and interpolating them. This is similar to how the tone curve module operates, but here, the nodes cannot be moved manually. The curve is split into three parts -- a middle linear part, and two extremities that transition smoothly from the slope of the middle part to the ends of the exposure range.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": The contrast slider controls the slope of the middle part of the curve, as illustrated in the graph display. The larger the dynamic range, the greater the contrast should be set to, in order preserve a natural-looking image. This parameter mostly affects the mid-tones. Note that global contrast has an impact on the acutance (perceived sharpness) -- a low-contrast image will look unsharp even though it is optically sharp in the sense of the [Optical Transfer Function (OTF)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Optical_transfer_function).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": Setting the contrast to 1 almost completely disables the S-curve, though there will be a very small residual effect from the splines in the highlights and shadows.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "hardness (previously _target power factor function_)\n: Known as the _target power factor function_ slider in older versions of _filmic_, this slider is hidden by default, and is adjusted automatically based on values in the [_scene_](#scene) tab. To make this slider visibile, you need to uncheck _auto adjust hardness_ in the [_options_](#options) tab.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": This parameter is the power function applied to the output transfer function, and it is often improperly called the _gamma_ (which can mean too many things in imaging applications, so we should stop using that term). It is used to raise or compress the mid-tones to account for display non-linearities or to avoid quantization artifacts when encoding in 8 bit file formats. This is a common operation when applying ICC color profiles (except for linear RGB spaces, like REC 709 or REC 2020, which have a linear “gamma” of 1.0). However, at the output of _filmic_, the signal is logarithmically encoded, which is not something ICC color profiles know to handle. As a consequence, if we let them apply a gamma of 1/2.2 on top, it will result in a double-up, which would cause the middle-gray to be remapped to 76% instead of 45% as it should in display-referred space.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "shadows / highlights\n: These two sliders directly set the position of the toe node (_shadows_) and of the shoulder node (_highlights_) of the S-curve: the points where the central linear portion of the curve ends and the roll-off toward black or white begins. Each is expressed as a percentage of the available room between middle-gray and the point where the current slope would hit the display black (respectively white) level. They replace the _latitude_ and _shadows ↔ highlights balance_ controls of older versions, which set the same two nodes but in linked coordinates (a global width plus an offset) that made adjusting one end without disturbing the other cumbersome. Internally, the module still stores latitude and balance for compatibility -- the sliders are a pure GUI-layer conversion, and old edits are unaffected.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": The range enclosed between the two nodes -- the latitude -- is the luminance range that is remapped in priority, at the constant slope defined by the contrast parameter. With the default _perceptual_ curve type (see _contrast in shadows/highlights_ in the [_options_](#options) tab), the nodes also act as **tension** controls: values close to 0 % hand the whole curve to the smooth sigmoid roll-off, while large values force the roll-off into a short, sharp turn near the extremes. With the older polynomial curve types it was advisable to keep the latitude as large as possible; with the perceptual sigmoid the logic is reversed and the small default is the appearance-matched optimum -- raise the nodes only if you deliberately want a harder transition.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": The latitude also defines the range of luminances that are not desaturated at the extremities of the luminance range (See _mid-tones saturation_).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "mid-tones saturation / extreme luminance saturation\n: At extreme luminances, the pixels will tend towards either white or black. Because neither white nor black have color associated with them, the saturation of these pixels must be 0%. In order to gracefully transition towards this 0% saturation point, pixels outside the mid-tone latitude range are progressively desaturated as they approach the extremes. The darker curve in the _filmic_ graph indicates the amount of desaturation that is applied to pixels outside the latitude range. Moving the slider to the right pushes the point where desaturation will start to be applied towards the extremes, resulting in a steeper desaturation curve. If pushed too far, this can result in fringing around the highlights. Moving the slider to the left brings the point at which color desaturation will start to be applied closer to the center, resulting in a gentler desaturation curve. If you would like to see more color saturation in the highlights, and you have checked that the white relative exposure in the [_scene_](#scene) tab is not yet clipping those highlights, move the mid-tones saturation slider to the right to increase the saturation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": Please note that this desaturation strategy has changed compared to previous versions of _filmic_ (which provided a different slider control labelled _extreme luminance saturation_). You can revert to the previous desaturation behaviour by selecting \"v3 (2019)\" in the _color science_ setting on the [_options_](#options) tab. Since _filmic_ _v6_ and _v7_ use accurate gamut mapping to the output color space, the desaturation curve is removed and the extreme luminance desaturation becomes in practice an highlights bleaching control.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": This control is set to 0 by default and it is now recommended that saturation is handled earlier in the pipeline. A preset \"add basic colorfulness\" has been added to the [_color balance_](./color-balance.md) module for this purpose.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": With the _v8 (AgX)_ color science, this slider is relabelled _color preservation_ and controls **how much of the per-channel hue drift to keep** -- it does not affect saturation. How much saturation the rendering keeps is set by the chosen _v8_ variant, not by this slider: the _no bleach_ variant preserves the saturation of valid diffuse colors -- skin tones, product colors, blue skies -- completely, because mandatory bleaching of valid midtone colors whitens non-Caucasian skin tones, which is a bias no variant is allowed to inflict on skin (the more-bleached variants trade saturation on non-skin colors deliberately -- see [variants](#variants)). Strongly compressed colors (speculars, clipped lights) bleach at any setting. At **-100 %** the transform runs as pure AgX: the full per-channel hue drift is present (the \"film\" character). At **0 %** (the default) half of that hue drift is removed. At **+100 %** the original hues are restored exactly, while the tonal bleaching of extreme highlights is unchanged. See the [background](#background) section.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The parameters in this tab should rarely require adjustment.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "target black luminance\n: The destination parameters set the target luminance values used to remap the tones. The default parameters should work 99% of the time, the remaining 1% being when you output in linear RGB space (REC709, REC2020) for media handling log-encoded data. These settings should therefore be used with caution because Ansel does not allow separate pipelines for display preview and file output.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": The target black luminance parameter sets the ground-level black of the target medium. By default it is set to the minimum non-zero value that can be encoded by the available number of bits in the output color space. Reducing it to zero means that some non-zero luminances will be mapped to 0 in the output, potentially losing some detail in the very darkest parts of the shadows. Increasing this slider will produce raised, faded blacks that can provide something of a \"retro\" look.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "target middle-gray\n: This is the middle-gray of the output medium that is used as a target for the S-curve's central node. On gamma-corrected media, the actual gray is computed with the gamma correction (middle-gray^(1/gamma)), so a middle-gray parameter of 18% with a gamma of 2.2 gives an actual middle-gray target of 45.87%.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "target white luminance\n: This parameter allows you to set the ceiling level white of the target medium. Set it lower than 100% if you want dampened, muted whites to achieve a retro look.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": To avoid double-ups and washed-out images, _filmic_ applies a “gamma” compression reverting the output ICC gamma correction, so the middle-gray is correctly remapped at the end. To remove this compression, set the destination power factor to 1.0 and the middle-gray destination to 45%.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Options"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "color science\n: This setting defaults to _v7 (2023)_ for new images, and defines the algorithms used by the _filmic_ module (e.g. the extreme luminance desaturation strategy). To revert to the behavior of previous versions of _filmic_, set this parameter to _v3 (2019)_, _v4 (2020)_, _v5 (2021)_ or _v6 (2022)_. The difference between these methods lies in the way in which they handle desaturation close to pure black and pure white (see the [background](#background) section for details). If you have previously edited an image using older versions of _filmic_, the color science setting will be kept at the earlier version number in order to provide backward compatibility for those edits. The _v7 (2023)_ method removes the _preserve chrominance_ option, and the _v8 (AgX)_ method applies the tone curve to each RGB channel separately inside a dedicated rendering color space (see the [background](#background) section for details on both). _v8 (AgX)_ comes in **five variants** -- _no bleach_, _low bleach_, _medium bleach_, _high bleach_ and _extra bleach_ -- which sit on a single trade-off between keeping saturation and keeping hue accurate; see the table in the [background](#background) section to choose one.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "preserve chrominance\n: _(This setting is not available with the v7 and v8 color sciences)_. Define how the chrominance should be handled by _filmic_ -- either not at all, or using one of the three provided norms.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": When applying the S-curve transformation independently on each color, the proportions of the colors are modified, which modifies the properties of the underlying spectrum, and ultimately the chrominance of the image. This is what happens if you choose \"no\" in the preserve chrominance parameter. This value may yield seemingly “better” results than the other values, but it may negatively impact later parts of the pipeline, for example, when it comes to global saturation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": The other values of this parameter all work in a similar way. Instead of applying the S-curve to the R, G and B channels independently, _filmic_, divides all the three components by a norm (N), and applies the S-curve to N. This way, the relationship between the channels is preserved.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": The value of the preserve chrominance parameter indicates which norm is used (the value used for N):\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": - _no_ means that the ratios between the RGB channels are not preserved. This will tend to saturate the shadows and desaturate the highlights, and can be helpful when there are out-of-gamut blues or reds.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": - _max RGB_ is the maximum value of the R, G and B channels. This is the same behaviour as the original version of the _filmic_ module. It tends to darken the blues, especially skies, and to yield halos or fringes, especially if some channels are clipped. It can also flatten the local contrast somewhat.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": - _luminance Y_ is a linear combination of the R, G and B channels. It tends to darken and increase local contrast in the reds, and tends not to behave so well with saturated and out-of-gamut blues.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": - _RGB power norm_ is the sum of the cubes of the R, G, and B channels, divided by the sum of their squares (R³ + G³ + B³)/(R² + G² + B²). It is usally a good compromise between the max RGB and the luminance Y values.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": - _RGB euclidean norm_ has the property of being RGB-space-agnostic, so it will yeild the same results regardless of which working color profile is used. It weighs more heavily on highlights than the power norm and gives more highlights desaturation, and is probably the closest to a color film look.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": There is no \"right\" choice for the norm, and the appropriate choice depends strongly on the image to which it is applied. You are advised to experiment and decide for yourself which setting gives the most pleasing result with the fewest artifacts.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "spline handling\n: This setting selects how the _latitude_, _balance_ and _contrast_ place the toe and shoulder **nodes** of the curve -- not the shape of the segments between them, which is chosen by _contrast in shadows/highlights_ below. _v3 (2021)_ is recommended; _v1_ and _v2_ are kept for backward compatibility with older edits. (The shape of the roll-off, including the modern sigmoid, used to live here as a mislabelled \"v4\"; it now belongs to the curve-type controls.)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "contrast in highlights\n: This control selects the shape of the highlights roll-off of the curve. **_perceptual_** (the default) leaves the straight mid-tone section at exactly the mid-tone slope and then glides smoothly to white -- a \"slope-matched\" roll-off with no fixed strength: it adapts to the scene, staying nearly straight for a low-dynamic-range studio shot (little to compress) and rolling off more firmly for a wide-range landscape (more to compress), and never over-compresses the brightest stop. The other three are the legacy segment types: _safe_ (rational, guaranteed not to over- or under-shoot but muted near white), _hard_ (sharper, more tonal compression, can overshoot) and _soft_ (gentler). Pick a legacy type only if you want a fixed roll-off character instead of the adaptive perceptual default.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "contrast in shadows\n: The same control for the shadows end of the curve. **_perceptual_** (the default) keeps shadow gradients open down to the deepest exposures (tuned for a dim room and a low-flare display); the legacy _hard_/_soft_/_safe_ segment types behave as for highlights.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "use custom middle-gray values\n: Enabling this setting makes the _middle-gray luminance_ slider visible on the [_scene_](#scene) tab. With the current version of _filmic_, you are advised to use the _exposure_ module to set the middle-gray level, so this setting is disabled by default (and the _middle-gray luminance slider_ is hidden).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "auto-adjust hardness\n: By default, this setting is enabled, and _filmic_ will automatically calculate the power function (aka \"gamma\") to be applied on the output transfer curve. If this setting is disabled, a _hardness_ slider will appear on the [_look_](#look) tab so that value can be manually set.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "iterations of high-quality reconstruction\n: Use this setting to increase the number of passes of the highlight reconstruction algorithm. More iterations means more color propagation into clipped areas from pixels in the surrounding neighbourhood. This can produce more neutral highlights, but it also costs more in terms of processing power. It can be useful in difficult cases where there are magenta highlights due to channel clipping.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": The default reconstruction works on separate RGB channels and has only one iteration applied, whereas the _high quality_ reconstruction uses a different algorithm that works on RGB ratios (which is a way of breaking down chromaticity from luminance) and can use several iterations to graduately propagate colors from neighbouring pixels into clipped areas. However, if too many iterations are used, the reconstruction can denegenerate, which will result in far colors being improperly inpainted into clipped objects (color bleeding) -- for example white clouds being inpainted with blue sky, or the sun disc shot through trees being inpainted with leaf-green.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "add noise in highlights\n: This artificially introduces noise into the reconstructed  highlights to prevent them from looking too smooth compared to surrounding areas that may already contain noise. This can help to blend the reconstructed areas more naturally with the surrounding non-clipped areas.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "type of noise\n: This specifies the statistical distribution of the added noise. It can be helpful to match the look of the artificially generated noise with the naturally occurring noise in the surrounding areas from the camera's sensor. The _poissonian_ noise is the closest to natural sensor noise but is less visually pleasing than _gaussian_, which is probably closer to film grain. Also note that most denoising modules will turn the sensor noise from poissonian to slightly gaussian, so you should pick the variant that blends better into the actual noise in your image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The _color science_ parameter (in the _options_ tab) defines the strategy that is used to desaturate colors near pure white (maximum display emission) and pure black (minimum display emission). The problem can be explained with the graph below, which represents the gamut of the sRGB color space at the constant hue of its green primary, with varying lightness (vertical axis) and chroma (horizontal axis):\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "![Gamut cone](sRGB-green.jpg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "As we approach pure black and pure white, the chroma available in gamut shrinks considerably until it reaches zero for lightness = 0 and lightness = 100% of the medium emission. This means that very bright (or very dark) colors cannot be very saturated at the same time if we want them to fit in gamut, with the gamut being imposed by the printing or displaying device we use.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "If colors are left unmanaged and are allowed to escape gamut, they will be clipped to valid values at the time of conversion to display color space. The problem is that this clipping is generally not hue-preserving and definitely not luminance-preserving, so highlights will typically shift to yellow and appear darker than they should, when evaluated against their neighborhood.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "To overcome this, filmic has used various strategies over the years (the so-called _color sciences_) to desaturate extreme luminances, forcing a zero saturation at minimum and maximum lightness and a smooth desaturation gradient. These strategies were all intended to minimize the hue shifts that come with gamut clipping.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Since all of these strategies were approximations (and often over-conservative ones) _v6 (2022)_ introduces a more accurate and measured approach. It performs a test-conversion to display color space, checks if the resulting color fits within the [0; 100]% range, and if it doesn't, computes the maximum saturation available in gamut at this luminance and hue, finally clipping the color to this value. This ensures a minimal color distortion, allowing for more saturated colors and better use of the available gamut, but also enforces a constant hue throughout the whole tone mapping **and** gamut mapping operation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This gamut mapping uses the output color profile as a definition of the display color space and automatically adjusts to any output space. However, only _matrix_ or _matrix + curve(s)_ ICC profiles are supported. _LUT_ ICC profiles are not supported and, if used, will make the gamut mapping default to the pipeline working space (Rec 2020 by default).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Note that the hue used as a reference for the gamut mapping is the hue before any tone mapping, sampled at the input of filmic. This means that even the _none_ chrominance preservation mode (applied on individual RGB channels regardless of their ratios) preserves hue in _v6_. This mode will only desaturate highlights more than the other modes, and a mechanism is in place to prevent it from resaturating shadows -- this behaviour can be bypassed by increasing the _extreme luminance saturation_ setting.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The _v7 (2023)_ color science improves over _v6_ and simplifies the chroma preservation options, by removing them. The chroma preservation modes aim at anchoring saturation and hue across the tone-mapping operation, by preserving RGB ratios compared to a norm. The choice of the norm is important when it comes to managing how the gamut is used and how the contrast of bright objects relatively to their neighbourhood is rendered by the tone-mapper. Several norms have been proposed since filmic _v1_, in 2018: none of them have been found to be a clear winner, and only one of them (max RGB) has some theoritical justification (allowing to reach display peak primary colors after the transform).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The _v7_ approach is to offer a mix between the _max RGB_ norm and the no-preservation option (where the output hue and saturation are still forced to their input values). The proportions of the mix are driven by the _extreme luminance saturation_ setting:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- 0% is an average of both,\n- -50% is strictly equivalent to the _v6_ no-preservation option,\n- +50% is strictly equivalent to the _v6_ _max RGB_ option,\n- Intermediate values are weighted averages between both,\n- Values beyond ±50% (up to ±200%) are linear extrapolations.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Positive values will favour saturated highlights and will be suitable for skies but need to be handled with care for portraits (producing accurate skin tones… which is not what people actually find too saturated and \"beefy\"[^1]), negative values will favour highlights bleaching, which is the preconceived idea many people have of \"film look\" (which is disproved by positive film slides and Technicolor movies, in addition of being highly questionnable to render black and tanned skin, as it removes ethnical features and whitens them).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "[^1]: D. L. MacAdam, \"Quality of Color Reproduction,\" in _Journal of the Society of Motion Picture and Television Engineers_, vol. 56, no. 5, pp. 487-512, May 1951, doi: 10.5594/J06314.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The saturation control gives a fine control over the amount of saturation vs. bleaching expected in highlights. In any case, the saturation algo will not allow the output saturation to be higher than the input one, and it should be made very clear that this setting is not designed for creative purposes, but only to drive the complicated trade-off coming from remapping RGB values from one color space to another, having different gamut and dynamic range.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The _v8 (AgX)_ color science implements the one genuinely useful idea popularized by Blender's AgX view transform and its darktable port: applying the tone curve to each RGB channel **separately**, inside a rendering color space whose primaries have been slightly compressed and rotated. Per-channel curves couple color to tonality -- highlights bleach toward white and shadows sink toward black *as a function of the tonal compression itself*, which produces the smooth, progressive desaturation of bright saturated subjects (flames, LEDs, stained glass) that norm-based tone mapping renders as flat colored patches. The rendering-space compression controls how fast that bleaching happens and steers the direction of the hue drifts that per-channel curves inevitably produce.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Where _v8_ differs from darktable/Blender AgX:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- **The rendering space is derived, not hand-tuned.** The compression and rotation constants in AgX are unexplained numbers inherited from a forum thread. In Ansel they are computed by an optimization with stated objectives -- neutral (zero-average) hue drift measured in a perceptual hue metric, guaranteed positivity, bounded worst-case drift -- against the module's default curve, and the derivation scripts ship with the source code. Notably, there is **no built-in warm/yellow shift**: AgX's skew toward yellow is a creative decision hard-coded in its constants; in Ansel, if you want warmth, you add it yourself where it belongs (see the emulation section below).\n- **Valid colors keep their saturation, by construction (in the _no bleach_ variant).** AgX (and any per-channel tone mapper with a neutral or under-expanding outset) mandatorily desaturates every color the curve touches, midtones included -- olive skin tones get bleached toward Caucasian-looking, which is a racial bias, not a style. In _v8_, the rendering is fitted (against a published human skin-tone database and diffuse reflectances) so that valid diffuse colors recover their chroma through the transform itself, for any dynamic range; only strongly compressed colors (speculars, clipped lights) bleach. The default _no bleach_ variant preserves skin-tone saturation completely (see the [variants table](#variants)); the _low_, _medium_ and _high bleach_ variants deliberately trade a little of it for tighter hue. The _color preservation_ slider then dials the per-channel hue drift continuously (full at -100 %, half at 0 %, none at +100 %); it is a hue control only and leaves the tonal bleaching untouched. The hue handling is performed in a perceptual color space, not HSV.\n- **The output is gamut-mapped.** _v8_ keeps filmic's _v6/v7_ gamut mapping against the export color profile; AgX has none, and its output can leave the display gamut freely.\n- **Everything else is regular filmic.** Scene white/black exposures, contrast, the shadows/highlights nodes, highlight reconstruction, and the display targets work exactly as in the other color sciences -- _v8_ only changes the color handling, not the tone machinery.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The five _v8_ variants {#variants}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Per-channel curves inevitably produce three coupled side effects on every color they touch, and the rendering-space compression cannot flatten all three at once -- pushing one down pushes another up:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- **desaturation** -- colors bleach toward white as they brighten (chroma is lost);\n- **hue drift** -- the classic blue-to-purple, red-to-orange rotation;\n- **apparent-brightness drift** -- a change in how *bright a color looks for its luminance* (the Helmholtz--Kohlrausch effect, below), which is what makes an over-cooked red read \"self-luminous\".\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The five variants are five points on this trade-off, from _no bleach_ (maximum saturation, largest hue drift) to _extra bleach_ (maximum hue and skin fidelity, most muted, most \"film-like\"). The crucial asymmetry: **hue drift is recoverable** -- the _color preservation_ slider restores it exactly -- but **lost saturation is not**; nothing downstream puts back chroma the transform bled away. So picking a variant is mostly deciding *how much saturation to keep versus how hue-stable and film-like* the render should be, knowing you can always pull hue back with the slider.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "What the numbers mean"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Beyond plain saturation and hue, two perceptual measures are used to fit and describe the variants:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- **$\\Delta E$ (color-shift distance)** -- one number for \"how far did this color move\", folding chroma loss and hue drift together. It is **not** the CIE 1976 or CIE 2000 $\\Delta E$: those are built for small differences between reflective print colors and misbehave on the very bright, very saturated colors a tone mapper pushes around. Ours is measured in filmic's own perceptual working space and is *chroma-weighted* -- a hue error on a near-grey color barely counts, while the same error on a vivid color counts fully, the way the eye actually weighs it. Read it as **$0$ = color unchanged, $\\approx 1$ = fully bleached to grey.**\n- **H-K apparent-brightness drift** -- the Helmholtz--Kohlrausch effect: a saturated color looks *brighter* than a grey of the same luminance (strongest for blue, red and magenta). Per-channel tone mapping changes this \"extra glow\" unevenly from hue to hue, which is what makes some renders look garish. \"H-K drift\" measures how much a variant shifts that glow relative to the original scene; **near $0$ means the render keeps the scene's natural brightness balance between colors** -- no hue popping out or sinking relative to its neighbours.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Measured behaviour"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Measured over a human skin-tone database and a circle of diffuse \"memory\" colors (foliage, sky, skin, products) swept across the exposures a photographer would give them. **Saturation drift** is the fraction of chroma lost; **hue drift** is the raw rotation *before* the _color preservation_ slider is applied; averages and maxima are over the whole set.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "_Skin tones_\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< table >}}\n| variant | saturation drift (avg / max) | hue drift ° (avg / max) | $\\Delta E$ (avg / max) | H-K drift (avg / max) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| no bleach | 0.0% / 0.0% | 10.5 / 15.4 | 0.18 / 0.27 | +0.030 / +0.081 |\n| low bleach | 0.0% / 0.0% | 7.8 / 11.8 | 0.14 / 0.21 | +0.028 / +0.079 |\n| medium bleach | 0.0% / 0.5% | 5.3 / 8.7 | 0.09 / 0.15 | +0.026 / +0.076 |\n| high bleach | 0.1% / 3.9% | 2.8 / 5.8 | 0.05 / 0.10 | +0.023 / +0.071 |\n| extra bleach | 1.0% / 7.1% | 1.1 / 3.4 | 0.02 / 0.08 | +0.021 / +0.064 |\n{{< /table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "_Reflective colors_\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< table >}}\n| variant | saturation drift (avg / max) | hue drift ° (avg / max) | $\\Delta E$ (avg / max) | H-K drift (avg / max) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| no bleach | 5.0% / 58.9% | 5.0 / 23.1 | 0.12 / 0.61 | +0.031 / −0.260 |\n| low bleach | 6.2% / 54.8% | 3.9 / 19.7 | 0.11 / 0.56 | +0.027 / −0.244 |\n| medium bleach | 7.6% / 56.6% | 2.9 / 18.9 | 0.11 / 0.57 | +0.023 / −0.245 |\n| high bleach | 8.9% / 58.9% | 2.1 / 18.3 | 0.11 / 0.60 | +0.020 / −0.248 |\n| extra bleach | 10.1% / 62.1% | 1.7 / 17.4 | 0.12 / 0.65 | +0.016 / −0.255 |\n{{< /table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The single trade-off is visible across every column: from _no_ to _extra bleach_, hue drift and $\\Delta E$ fall while saturation drift rises. The high reflective **max** saturation drift (55--62 %) in every variant is the intended bleaching of near-clipping bright colors -- flames, LEDs, speculars -- the effect you chose AgX for; it is roughly the same in all five. Skin is protected in every variant (≤ 1 % average drift), so **no variant whitens skin** the way raw AgX does. Note that skin hue drift is *large* in _no bleach_ (10.5°) and small in _extra bleach_ (1.1°): _no bleach_ spends hue accuracy -- the recoverable quantity -- to protect skin *chroma*, and you buy the hue back with the slider.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Per-hue behaviour"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "For twelve reference hues, the raw hue drift (degrees, before the slider) and the rendered chroma (saturation of the output). Every column is **monotone** from _no_ to _extra bleach_: choosing a stronger variant moves every hue the same way, so the five are consistent renderings of one look, not five different looks.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "_Signed hue drift (°), before the slider_\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< table >}}\n| hue | no | low | medium | high | extra |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| red | 5.5 | 3.8 | 2.3 | 0.7 | −0.8 |\n| red-orange | 6.1 | 4.4 | 2.8 | 1.3 | −0.2 |\n| orange | 4.5 | 3.5 | 2.6 | 1.7 | 0.7 |\n| yellow-green | 1.2 | 0.9 | 0.6 | 0.3 | −0.1 |\n| green | 3.2 | 2.3 | 1.5 | 0.7 | 0.0 |\n| cyan | 7.8 | 5.4 | 3.1 | 0.7 | −1.5 |\n| cyan-blue | 6.1 | 4.1 | 2.0 | −0.2 | −2.4 |\n| blue | 1.5 | 1.4 | 1.2 | 1.0 | 0.8 |\n| blue-magenta | −1.1 | −0.7 | −0.4 | −0.2 | −0.1 |\n| magenta | 2.4 | 1.6 | 1.0 | 0.4 | −0.1 |\n{{< /table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "_Rendered chroma (decreases as bleach increases)_\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< table >}}\n| hue | no | low | medium | high | extra |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| red | 0.163 | 0.158 | 0.154 | 0.148 | 0.143 |\n| red-orange | 0.156 | 0.147 | 0.139 | 0.132 | 0.126 |\n| orange | 0.212 | 0.199 | 0.187 | 0.175 | 0.165 |\n| yellow-green | 0.277 | 0.263 | 0.251 | 0.239 | 0.226 |\n| green | 0.159 | 0.153 | 0.148 | 0.143 | 0.137 |\n| cyan | 0.126 | 0.121 | 0.116 | 0.111 | 0.106 |\n| cyan-blue | 0.228 | 0.223 | 0.216 | 0.208 | 0.199 |\n| blue | 0.246 | 0.242 | 0.237 | 0.230 | 0.223 |\n| blue-magenta | 0.275 | 0.272 | 0.269 | 0.267 | 0.264 |\n| magenta | 0.343 | 0.340 | 0.338 | 0.337 | 0.336 |\n{{< /table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "How each variant was made, and its strengths and flaws"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**None of these is \"correct\" and none is best in every situation.** They are deliberate compromises on a trade-off with no free lunch; the fitting favoured a different priority for each end and interpolated the middle:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- **_no bleach_ -- keep every bit of color, fix hue later.** Fitted to lose the *least* chroma and $\\Delta E$ possible, protecting saturation absolutely (skin 0 %, reflective 5 % avg). *Strength:* the most vivid, punchy render; nothing is washed out. *Flaw:* the largest hue drift (skin 10.5°, reds swing orange), which you must correct with the _color preservation_ slider. *Choose it* when losing saturation is the worst outcome and you don't mind using the slider.\n- **_extra bleach_ -- maximum hue and skin fidelity, film wash-out.** Fitted to minimize hue drift and skin $\\Delta E$ and to hold every hue's *apparent brightness* steady, spending chroma to do it. *Strength:* hue-accurate and calm even with the slider at −100 % (the pure \"film\" character); reds/magentas never read self-luminous. *Flaw:* the most muted colors, and the loss is permanent. *Choose it* for the film-like highlight roll-off as a look, or when hue accuracy without touching the slider matters most.\n- **_low_, _medium_, _high bleach_ -- the interpolated middle.** Each is built as the perceptual **midpoint** of two neighbours (so apparent brightness, hue *and* saturation all step evenly): _medium_ bisects _no_ and _extra_, _low_ bisects _no_ and _medium_, _high_ bisects _medium_ and _extra_. *Strength:* a smooth, even ramp -- pick the point on the saturation-versus-fidelity line you like and every color follows consistently. *Flaw:* none is a specialist; each is a compromise by construction. _medium_ is the neutral all-rounder; _low_ leans vivid; _high_ leans film-like.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Choosing a variant"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- **Losing saturation is the worst outcome** (vivid subjects, sunsets, product shots, colorful fashion) → _no_ or _low bleach_, and pull hue back with the slider.\n- **Hue accuracy without touching the slider matters most** (skin-critical portraits, mixed lighting, neutral reproduction) → _high_ or _extra bleach_.\n- **You want the pronounced \"film\" highlight wash-out as a look** → _extra bleach_.\n- **Unsure / general-purpose** → _medium bleach_, the neutral middle (the shipped default is _low bleach_).\n- Remember the asymmetry: any variant's hue drift is reversible with _color preservation_; its saturation loss is not. When in doubt, err toward *less* bleach.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Color artifacts"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "As filmic v6 (then v7) is so far the best version to retain saturated colors at constant hue, it gets also much less forgiving to __invalid__ colors like chromatic aberrations and clipped magenta highlights, that are much better hidden (albeit __not solved__) by simple curves applied on individual channels (no chrominance preservation) with no care given to their ratios.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "It is not the purpose of a tone mapping and gamut mapping operators to reconstruct damaged signals, and these flaws need to be corrected earlier in the pipeline with the specialized modules provided. However, there is a mechanism in filmic v6 that ensures that any color brighter than the _white relative exposure_ degrades to pure white, so a quick workaround is to simply set the _white relative exposure_ to a value slightly lower than the exposure of the clipped parts. In other words: if it is clipped at the input, let it be clipped at the output. Chrominance preservation options that work the best for this purpose are the _luminance_ and _euclidean_ norms, or simply _none_.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ####
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Inconsistent output"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "With filmic v6, if you export the same image to sRGB and Adobe RGB color spaces, and then compare both images side by side on a large-gamut screen (that can cover Adobe RGB), the sRGB export _should_ have more desaturated highlights than the Adobe RGB version. Since the sRGB color space is shorter than Adobe RGB, its gamut boundary is closer to the neutral grey axis, and therefore the maximum allowed chroma is lower for any given luminance. This is by no means a bug but rather is proof that the gamut mapping is actually doing its job.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Emulating darktable AgX in Ansel"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The darktable _AgX_ module packs 33 parameters into a single module: a tone curve, a channel mixer applied before and after it, an ASC CDL color grading stage (\"look\"), a gamut compression, and exposure heuristics. This is a pipeline within the pipeline, and it contradicts Ansel's design: one module, one job, so that every job can benefit from masking, blending and multiple instances. Everything AgX does is available in Ansel through dedicated modules -- usually with better color science, and always with more control. Here is the mapping:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "tone curve, white/black relative exposure, pivot, contrast\n: _filmic_ itself, [_scene_](#scene) and [_look_](#look) tabs. AgX's pivot corresponds to middle-gray, its \"curve y gamma\" to filmic's hardness (auto-computed), its toe/shoulder powers to the _contrast in shadows/highlights_ presets combined with the _shadows_/_highlights_ node sliders. Set the color science to _v8 (AgX)_ for the per-channel rendering.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "per-channel bleaching and hue drift (\"primaries inset/rotation\")\n: Built into the _v8_ color science with derived constants; the _color preservation_ slider scales the strength (negative half) or recovers the original colors (positive half). If you want *creative* control over primaries beyond that -- what AgX's twelve inset/outset/rotation sliders attempt -- use [_color calibration_](./color-calibration.md) in its **primaries** GUI mode, placed before _filmic_ in the pipeline. It is mathematically the same operation (a 3×3 matrix on RGB), presented with the same primaries-style controls, and it supports masks and multiple instances, which AgX's built-in version does not.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "selectively bleaching a region of the chromaticity plane\n: What AgX's inset does globally, _color calibration_'s **simple** GUI mode does surgically: rotate the chroma axes onto the hue you need, compress the U or V axis, and use the _achromatic coupling_ to remap a chosen hue toward the achromatic axis -- desaturating and brightening it at once. This recovers overwhelming stage lights or brings saturated highlights back into gamut with far more precision than a global primaries compression.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "the \"look\" block (slope / offset / power / saturation)\n: [_color balance_](./color-balance.md), which implements the full ASC CDL in a proper perceptual space, with per-range (shadows/mid-tones/highlights) controls, masks and instances -- AgX's look block is a reduced copy of it computed in a worse space.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "the baked-in warm shift\n: AgX skews brights toward yellow by construction; Ansel's _v8_ is neutral by design. To add warmth deliberately: a white-balance nudge in _color calibration_ (chromatic adaptation), or a per-range shift in _color balance_, or -- for the mixed-lighting look where highlights warm up while shadows stay cool -- the [_split-toning_](./split-toning.md) module, which applies two chromatic adaptations weighted by luminance. The point: the warm shift becomes an explicit, adjustable, maskable decision instead of an unlabeled constant.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "hue-specific adjustments\n: For color shifts confined to the saturated vertices of the gamut (deepening blues without touching neutrals, taming oranges), use [_color primaries_](./color-primaries.md); for hue-wise shifts driven by tonal range, use the [_color equalizer_](./color-equalizer.md). Both blend in RGB and preserve gradients.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "gamut compression of out-of-gamut input\n: Handled inside _v8_ (negatives compression, generalized to the working profile) plus filmic's gamut mapping to the export profile -- which AgX lacks entirely. For difficult cases (deep blue LEDs), prefer fixing the input with _color calibration_'s gamut compression, which is where the problem actually lives.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/filmic.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The workflow difference is philosophical: AgX invites you to fix color inside the tone mapper, at the end of the pipeline, with controls that cannot be masked and whose interactions are opaque. Ansel's approach is divide and conquer -- calibrate color first (_color calibration_), grade it (_color balance_, _split-toning_, _color equalizer_, _color primaries_), then let _filmic_ do one job: compress the dynamic range, with the _v8_ color science reproducing the per-channel rendering AgX is known for, minus its hard-coded look. The same results are reachable step by step, and each step is inspectable, maskable and reversible on its own.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/scale-pixels.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Scale pixels"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/scale-pixels.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Some cameras (such as the Nikon D1X) have rectangular instead of the usual square sensor cells. Without correction this would lead to distorted images. This module applies the required scaling.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Color equalizer"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Remap colors through a procedural 3D LUT driven by perceptual hue-ring controls.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The _color equalizer_ module is a secondary color-grading tool. It lets you define color shifts on three hue rings expressed in _dt UCS HSB_ space, then converts those shifts into a dense RGB 3D LUT that is applied to the image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The user interface is meant to stay perceptually even:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- The editable graphs work in _hue_, _saturation_ and _brightness_ dimensions of _dt UCS HSB_,\n- The underlying LUT is built in RGB code values and applied as a 3D cube transform,\n- The interpolation between user nodes is smooth and periodic over hue.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Because the final effect is applied through a 3D LUT, hue, saturation and brightness edits can interact in a more global way than in curve-only modules. This makes the module well suited to creative color remapping and palette design, but more robust across dramatic changes.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The module is designed to run after [_color balance_](./color-balance.md) in the pixelpipe.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Its internal LUT is built and applied in Rec.2020 HLG code values, while the input and output of the module stay in the pipeline working RGB space. Scene-referred RGB is normalized by the module _white level_ before entering the LUT domain and denormalized afterwards.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The _color equalizer_ is built around three brightness ranges:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- _shadows_,\n- _midtones_,\n- _highlights_.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "For each range, three inner tabs define how colors should be remapped around the hue ring:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- _saturation_,\n- _brightness_,\n- _hue_.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Each graph is defined by editable nodes connected with a periodic monotone Hermite interpolation. The horizontal axis always represents hue. The vertical axis represents the selected correction dimension:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- _saturation_ scales chroma,\n- _brightness_ scales the dt UCS brightness,\n- _hue_ rotates hue around the ring.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "From these user-defined ring deformations, the module builds a 64×64×64 RGB 3D LUT. The LUT is constrained to remain inside the RGB gamut cube.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Primary tabs"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "shadows, midtones, highlights\n: These tabs choose which brightness ring you are editing.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": Each ring corresponds to a fixed brightness anchor in dt UCS HSB space. In practice, the three rings let you define different color remappings for dark, medium and bright colors.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "options\n: This tab contains the global parameters controlling LUT generation and application.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Graph tabs"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "For each primary brightness tab, the following three inner tabs are available:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "saturation\n: Increase or decrease chroma around the hue ring.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "brightness\n: Increase or decrease the dt UCS brightness around the hue ring.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "hue\n: Rotate hues around the ring.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Graph interaction"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "left-click + drag\n: Move an existing node.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ctrl+left-click\n: Add a new node on the curve.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "right-click on a node\n: Delete the selected node.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "double-click\n: Reset the current graph to its default flat state.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The graphs also display a vertical marker when the module color picker is active, showing the hue of the sampled color.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Live preview interaction"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When the module has focus in darkroom, the mouse cursor over the image preview becomes a direct editing control for the currently visible graph:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "move the mouse over the image\n: Sample the module input through the preview pixelpipe cache at the cursor position.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": The sampled color is projected back to the bounded dt UCS hue-ring control space, then the currently visible graph shows:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- A vertical hue marker at the sampled hue,\n- A circular swatch on the curve,\n- The original sampled display color in the inner disk,\n- The corrected display color in the outer ring,\n- An outer intensity arc showing the interpolated correction amount for the active setting.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "mouse wheel over the image\n: Adjust the currently visible graph around the sampled hue.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": The change is distributed to neighboring nodes with a gaussian window, so edits stay smooth and local on the hue ring.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": On the _saturation_ and _brightness_ graphs, the wheel changes the gain of nearby nodes.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": On the _hue_ graph, the wheel rotates nearby nodes around the hue ring.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Shift+mouse wheel\n: Use a larger step.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ctrl+mouse wheel\n: Use a finer step.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "right-click over the image\n: Add a new node on the currently visible graph at the hue sampled under the cursor.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This preview interaction always targets the active combination of:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- brightness tab: _shadows_, _midtones_ or _highlights_,\n- graph tab: _saturation_, _brightness_ or _hue_.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The smoothing parameters are important when doing dramatic local changes : they will help blending color shifts to avoid hash transitions and halos between objects, as well as chroma noise. Increasing all smoothing parameters will make the actual color results drift away from user-set parameters at the benefit of better preserving gradients into the image and limiting edge artifacts.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": This is useful when the image uses values above diffuse white and you want the LUT to work inside a bounded `[0,1]` RGB code-value range.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "brightness smoothing\n: Control how far brightness edits spread along the achromatic axis when the local deformation field is built.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "saturation smoothing\n: Control how far saturation edits spread away from the neutral axis.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "hue smoothing\n: Control how many neighboring hue anchors contribute to the local deformation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": The same methods are available as in [_lut 3D_](./lut-3D.md): _tetrahedral_, _trilinear_ and _pyramid_. The difference is usually subtle with a dense LUT, but tetrahedral is the default and safest option.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Module color picker"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A module-wide color picker is available below the graph notebooks.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "It samples the module input in RGB, averages the selected area, normalizes the result by the module _white level_, and converts it to dt UCS HSB for feedback.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The picker is used to:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Draw a vertical hue marker on all graphs,\n- Display whether the sampled color lies mostly in _shadows_, _midtones_ or _highlights_.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When the sampled brightness lies between two rings, the label displays the relative distance between them, for example `35% shadows, 65% midtones`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The module color picker is separate from the live preview cursor editing described above:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- the picker is used to inspect one area and report its hue/brightness position in the controls,\n- the live preview cursor is used to edit the currently visible graph directly from the image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The bottom part of the module contains a 3D LUT viewer. It displays the LUT geometry as a projected RGB cube:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "axis tilt\n: Tilt the achromatic axis relative to the viewing plane. At 90°, it shows a chromaticity diagram with the achromatic axis orthogonal to the graph plane. At 0°, it shows the achromatic axis parallel to the graph plane.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "slice depth\n: Move the slicing plane through the cube. On dense LUTs, slicing allows to get an \"internal\" view of the cube at a certain depth, for legibility purposes, without displaying color nodes behind and in front of the slicing plane ± slice thickness. \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "slice thickness\n: Show only node pairs lying within a band around the slicing plane. At 100 %, it effectively disables the effect of slicing and the graph may be too crowded to be legible.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "color shift threshold\n: Hide `input -> output` mappings whose input/output distance is below the selected percentage of RGB code values. At 0 %, it shows all mappings, which may crowd the graph beyond legibility.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "save to cLUT\n: Export the currently displayed LUT to a reusable `.cube` file with metadata describing the LUT color space. This file will need to be applied in HLG Rec2020 colorspace.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "middle-button drag vertically\n: Change the axis tilt angle.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "double-click\n: Reset zoom, pan and view orientation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "These interactions affect only the visualization. They do not modify the LUT itself.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Typical use"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A common workflow is:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. Use _color balance_ first to establish the global color mood.\n2. In _color equalizer_, choose one of the three brightness tabs.\n3. Use the module color picker to locate the hue and brightness range you want to target.\n4. Use the live preview cursor over the image to inspect how the active graph affects a real sampled color.\n5. Adjust _hue_, _saturation_ and _brightness_ graphs for that range, either by dragging nodes in the graph or by scrolling over the image preview.\n6. Add nodes from the image preview with right-click when you need a control point exactly at the sampled hue.\n7. Inspect the resulting RGB deformation in the LUT viewer.\n8. Fine-tune the smoothing parameters in the _options_ tab if the remapping spreads too much or not enough.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The module is most effective for creative palette remapping, hue replacement, and selective saturation/brightness shaping of already-balanced images.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Limitations"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Changing greys"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The module is not allowing to modify the achromatic axis, as to tint greys. This is on purpose because the LUT building steps uses a cylindrical 3D interpolation that guarantees its numerical stability. The grey (achromatic) axis is used as the spine on which we base the whole interpolation process. \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Tinting greys is otherwise known as _white balancing_. It can also be achieved in [_color balance_](./color-balance.md) with regard to luminance. That should be achieved prior to using the _color equalizer_\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Compatibility with Darktable color equalizer"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The Darktable \"team\" chose to steal from Ansel an [earlier prototype](https://github.com/aurelienpierreeng/ansel/pull/283) of _color equalizer_ that was brittle and judged unworthy of going into production by its author, Aurélien PIERRE. This prototype applies color shifts in dt UCS HSB space directly, and uses several guided filter instances to deal with edge artifacts and chroma noise. This is a broken attempt at fixing a broken design, because the issue is trying to do color shifts in another color space than RGB. Because of that, preserving image gradients is not possible and edge artifacts can't be avoided, and no amount of guided filters will make it ok.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/color-equalizer.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel _color equalizer_ is not compatible at all with the Darktable one, which should never have been pushed to production. But again, different projects, different quality standards.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/grain.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Grain"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Simulate the grain of analog film. The grain is processed on the L channel of Lab color space.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "coarseness\n: The grain size, scaled to simulate an ISO number.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/grain.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "strength\n: The strength of the effect.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/hot-pixels.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Hot pixels"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/hot-pixels.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Automatically detect and eliminate hot pixels.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/hot-pixels.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Hot pixels are pixels which have failed to record a light level correctly. Detected hot pixels are replaced by an average of their neighbors.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/hot-pixels.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "threshold\n: How strong a pixel's value needs to deviate from that of its neighbors to be regarded as a hot pixel.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/hot-pixels.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "strength\n: The blending strength of the hot pixels with their surrounding.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/hot-pixels.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "detect by 3 neighbours\n: Extend the detection of hot pixels -- regard a pixel as hot if a minimum of only three (instead of four) neighbor pixels deviate by more than the threshold level.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/modules/hot-pixels.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "mark fixed pixels\n: Visually mark the corrected pixels on the image and display a count of hot pixels that have been fixed.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/_index.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Masking & blending"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Masking and blending decide how much of a processing module's effect actually reaches the image, and where -- from applying it uniformly across the whole frame down to targeting a handful of pixels.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Each module takes its input from the preceding module in the pixelpipe, performs its operation, and hands the output to the next module. Blending is the optional step in between: a module's output data can be reprocessed (combined) with its input data using algorithms called blending operators, or [blend modes](./blend-modes.md), before being handed onward.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Each blend mode is further controlled by the _opacity_ parameter (having a value between 0% and 100%) which defines how much the input or output images contribute to the final result. Typically an opacity of 0% outputs an image which is identical to the input image (the module has no effect) whereas an opacity of 100% delivers the maximum effect of the module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This opacity can be the same for every pixel (using the global _Opacity_ slider), in which case blending acts uniformly over the entire image. Alternatively the opacity values can vary depending on the properties or location of each pixel. This local modification of opacity is called a _mask_, and it is what gives you fine control over which parts of an image a module affects, and to what extent.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "These controls are shown in the _Masking & Blending_ tool in the darkroom [left panel](../darkroom-view-layout.md#left-panel): focus a module that supports blending (click its header) and this panel switches to show that module's blending controls. With no module focused, or a module that doesn't support blending, the panel says so instead.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The drawn shapes you create there are the same objects managed by the [shape manager](../../toolboxes/shape-manager.md), a separate floating window, and can be reused across modules.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Enabling blending and masking"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Blending is switched on per module by the _Enable_ checkbox at the top of the panel. While it is unchecked, blending and masking are off entirely: the module's output goes straight to the next module in the pixelpipe with no additional reprocessing, and the rest of the panel stays grayed out.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Check it and blending applies _uniformly_ over the whole image, which is the state you get before any mask is involved: input and output are combined with the chosen blend mode, in an amount set by the global _Opacity_ slider. The defaults -- a blend mode of “Normal” at 100% opacity -- reproduce the module's plain effect.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Adding a mask is what makes that opacity vary from pixel to pixel instead of being the same everywhere. Each masking method has its own tab, and all three -- _Raster_, _Drawn_ and _Parametric_ -- are enabled by default: switching blending on is enough to start using any of them, and they stay uniform until you actually draw a shape, move a parametric slider or pick a raster source. Use as many at once as you need (see [combining masks](#combining-masks) below); each masking tab has its own _Disable_ checkbox to turn that method off when you want it out of the way.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "[Drawn mask](./masks/drawn.md)\n: The opacity is based on pixel location, as defined by one or more shapes drawn on the image. Until you draw a shape, every pixel keeps the same opacity, as set by the global _Opacity_ slider.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "[Parametric mask](./masks/parametric.md)\n: The opacity is based on the properties of individual pixels, computed per-pixel from their values in the color channels you choose.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "[Raster mask](./masks/raster.md)\n: The opacity is based on a mask that was generated by another module earlier in the pixelpipe.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A fourth tab, _Contours_, carries no _Disable_ checkbox of its own because it defines no mask: it refines whichever mask the other tabs produce, blurring and feathering it so its edges follow the details of the image. See [mask contours](./masks/refinement-controls.md).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Blending options"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< figure src=\"mask/mask-blend-option.jpg\" />}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The button {{< icon src=\"icon/icon-hamburger.jpg\" alt=\"\" >}} at the right end of the tab row opens a menu of options for the [parametric mask](./masks/parametric.md); it stays grayed out on modules that don't support one. Use it to pick the color space the parametric mask is computed in, and to show the output-channel sliders (a parametric mask is normally built from the input channels coming into the module):\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< param-table >}}\n| **Reset to default blend colorspace**<div>Goes back to the module's own default color space.</div> |\n| **Lab**<div>Uses the Lab color space. Offered only for modules that natively work in Lab, so that Lab blending is not applied where it doesn't belong.</div> |\n| **RGB (display)**<div>Uses the display-referred RGB/HSL color space.</div> |\n| **RGB (scene)**<div>Uses the scene-referred RGB/J<sub>z</sub>C<sub>z</sub>h<sub>z</sub> color space.</div> |\n| **Show output channels**<div>Shows the output channel sliders, so the parametric mask can also be defined in terms of the module's output. Once they are shown, this entry becomes **Reset and hide output channels**.</div> |\n{{< /param-table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\nNot every blend mode or blending option is available for every module -- the choices on offer depend on the color space the module works in.\n{{< /note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Combining masks"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The three masking methods are **independent toggles that can be combined**. A [raster mask](./masks/raster.md) serves as the base when one is active, and the [drawn](./masks/drawn.md) and [parametric](./masks/parametric.md) masks refine it from there.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "How those masks are folded together is set by _Combine masks_, whose two modes are mirror images of each other:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< param-table >}}\n| **Exclusive** (default)<div>Multiplies the masks together before applying the global _Opacity_ slider. A pixel excluded by any one mask (opacity 0) stays excluded, so each mask you add can only ever _restrict_ the affected area.</div> |\n| **Inclusive**<div>Inverts each mask, multiplies, then inverts the result. A pixel fully included by any one mask (opacity 1.0) stays included, so each mask you add can only ever _extend_ the affected area.</div> |\n{{< /param-table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Deciding which of the two you want before you start is the easiest way to keep a complex mask predictable — see [combining drawn & parametric masks](./masks/drawn-and-parametric.md) for the polarity settings that go with each.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Previewing the mask"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< figure src=\"mask/masked-image.jpg\" height=\"500\" caption=\"Masked image\" />}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A mask is easier to judge when you can see it. The mask icon, both in the module's header and in the blending panel, replaces the center view with a preview of the mask the module is currently using.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Read it as a cut-out rather than as a highlight:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- Where the image **shows through**, the mask is at full opacity and the module's effect is **applied**.\n- Where the **checkerboard** shows, the mask is at zero and the module is **not applied** — that part of the picture is masked out, so it is hidden from view.\n- In between, the image and the checkerboard mix in proportion, so a soft edge fades from one to the other.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The checkerboard pattern is deliberate: it makes a partly transparent mask legible in a way a flat color cannot, since you can tell a half-hidden area from a fully hidden one at a glance.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< figure src=\"mask/options-display-masking.png\" />}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The look of the preview is set in the _Picture display options_ popover, opened by the _Display_ button {{< icon src=\"lighttable-bottom-panel_display.jpg\" alt=\"\" >}} in the toolbox at the bottom of the darkroom [left panel](../darkroom-view-layout.md#left-panel). Its _Mask preview settings_ section holds:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< param-table >}}\n| **Checkerboard color 1** and **Checkerboard color 2**<div>The two alternating squares. Picking colors that clash with the image you are working on is what makes the masked areas obvious — a neutral pair disappears against a busy picture.</div> |\n| **Checkerboard size**<div>The size of a square, from 2 to 32 px (default 8). Larger squares are easier to see; smaller ones obscure less of a finely detailed mask edge.</div> |\n| **Show a greyscaled mask image**<div>Converts the visible part of the image to grayscale during the preview, so the only colors on screen are the checkerboard's. Useful when the picture's own colors make the mask boundary hard to follow.</div> |\n{{< /param-table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The same popover carries _Fast drawn mask rasterization_, which trades a little accuracy in how brush and polygon masks are drawn on screen for speed when zoomed out. Exports, thumbnails and snapshots stay pixel-accurate either way.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/raster.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Raster masks"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/raster.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "As described in the previous sections, the final output of a module's mask (the combined effect of any drawn and parameteric masks) is a grayscale raster image representing the extent to which the module's effect should be applied to each pixel. This raster image is stored internally for active modules and can be subsequently reused by other modules in the pixelpipe.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/raster.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "As with any mask, if the opacity value for a pixel in a raster mask is zero the module's input passed through the module unchanged. If the opacity is 1.0 the module has its full effect. For each value between 0 and 1.0 the module's effect is applied proportionally at that location.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/raster.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Enable the raster mask from its tab and choose a source from the combobox. Raster masks can be identified by the name of the module against which they were originally generated.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/raster.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Combining with drawn and parametric masks"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/raster.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "In Ansel, a raster mask is no longer a mutually-exclusive mode: it can be **combined with a [drawn](drawn.md) and/or [parametric](parametric.md) mask on top**. When you do, the raster mask serves as the **base**, and the drawn and parametric masks refine it — their opacities are multiplied together pixel-by-pixel. Because masks combine multiplicatively, adding a drawn or parametric mask on top of a raster mask can only restrict its area further, never extend it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/raster.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This lets you reuse a mask computed elsewhere in the pipeline (for instance a luminance or edge mask produced by another module) and then trim it to a region with a drawn shape, or restrict it by color with a parametric mask, without having to rebuild it from scratch. In Darktable, selecting a raster mask disabled the other mask types entirely.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/raster.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Note:** Raster masks are generated as part of a module's internal processing. Once a module's processing is complete its mask then becomes available to subsequent modules in the pixelpipe.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/raster.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This has two implications:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/raster.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. Raster masks cannot be generated by disabled modules since they do not participate in pixelpipe processing. As soon as you disable a module, its mask is no longer available for use.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/raster.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "2. Raster masks are passed _up_ the pixelpipe after module processing -- they can only be used by modules that come later in the pipe than the generating module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/set-operators.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Set operators"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/set-operators.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A set operator decides how one shape combines with the mask already built from the shapes applied before it: the shape adds to the selection, narrows it down, or cuts a region out of it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/set-operators.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Every shape in a [drawn mask](./drawn.md) carries one, except the shape applied first, which has nothing before it to combine with. It is set from the **Operation** submenu of the shape's context menu, whether you open it on the canvas or on the shape's row in the shape list, and the [shape manager](../../../toolboxes/shape-manager.md) shows the one each row uses. The operator belongs to the row the shape occupies in that mask rather than to the shape itself, so the same shape can combine differently in every module using it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/set-operators.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The four operators"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/set-operators.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "An operator reads a pixel as \"selected\" when its opacity is greater than zero. The examples below combine a Gradient with a Polygon, showing the effect of each operator applied to the Polygon:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/set-operators.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< gallery cols=\"2\" >}}\n{{< figure src=\"mask-manager_ex1.jpg\" caption=\"A gradient shape\" />}}\n{{< figure src=\"mask-manager_ex2.jpg\" caption=\"A polygon shape\" />}}\n{{< /gallery >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/set-operators.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< param-table image-height=\"9rem\" >}}\n| {{< icon src=\"masks_union.jpg\" alt=\"\" >}} **Union**<div>The default. The result selects pixels that are in the existing mask **or** in the added shape (the maximum value is taken where they overlap).</div> | ![](mask-manager_ex3.jpg) |\n| {{< icon src=\"masks_intersection.jpg\" alt=\"\" >}} **Intersection**<div>The result selects only pixels that are in **both** the existing mask **and** the added shape (the minimum value is taken where they overlap) — useful to \"imprint\" one shape onto another.</div> | ![](mask-manager_ex4.jpg) |\n| {{< icon src=\"masks_difference.jpg\" alt=\"\" >}} **Difference**<div>The result keeps pixels that are in the existing mask but **not** in the added shape — useful to cut a region out of a selection.</div> | ![](mask-manager_ex5.jpg) |\n| {{< icon src=\"masks_exclusion.jpg\" alt=\"\" >}} **Exclusion**<div>The result selects pixels that are in the existing mask **or** the added shape but **not both** (an exclusive or).</div> | ![](mask-manager_ex6.jpg) |\n{{< /param-table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/_index.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Masks"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Masks limit a module's effect to certain parts of the image, so that a correction lands where you want it and leaves the rest of the picture untouched.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A mask is best pictured as a grayscale image laid over the picture: each pixel holds a value between 0 and 1.0 (0% to 100%), and that value is the _opacity_ handed to [blending](../_index.md). An opacity of 0 leaves a pixel exactly as the module received it, 1.0 gives it the module's full effect, and values in between apply it proportionally.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\nA mask decides _where_ a module applies, and how strongly. _How_ its input and output are mixed at that strength is the job of the [blend mode](../blend-modes.md).\n{{< /note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ways to build a mask"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Three methods can set that per-pixel opacity. Each works on its own, or [combined](../_index.md#combining-masks) with the others:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- [Drawn masks](./drawn.md) — select areas by location, with shapes drawn directly on the image.\n- [Parametric masks](./parametric.md) — select pixels by their own color and tonal values.\n- [Raster masks](./raster.md) — reuse a mask that another module already computed earlier in the pixelpipe.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Combining and refining masks"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/_index.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- [Set operators](./set-operators.md) — how each shape of a drawn mask combines with the ones applied before it.\n- [Combining drawn & parametric masks](./drawn-and-parametric.md) — how polarity and the _Combine masks_ setting merge a drawn mask with a parametric one.\n- [Mask contours](./refinement-controls.md) — blur and feather a finished mask so that its edges follow the details of the image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn-and-parametric.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Combining drawn & parametric masks"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn-and-parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Drawn and parametric masks can be used in combination to form a single mask that can be applied to a module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn-and-parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "There are two main elements which control how individual masks are combined: the _polarity_ setting of each individual mask (defined by the plus or minus buttons) and the setting in the “combine masks” combobox.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn-and-parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The \"combine masks\" combobox contains the following options, defining how the drawn and parametric masks will be combined:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn-and-parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "exclusive\n: A straightforward method of combining masks, exclusive mode multiplies together the individual pixel values from each of the component masks.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn-and-parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": For a given pixel, the final mask will have value of 0 if _any_ of the individual masks are 0 at that location and it will only have a value of 1.0 if _all_ masks have a value of 1.0 at that location.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn-and-parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": Any individual mask can _exclude_ a pixel by setting its value to 0, regardless of what the other masks do. Once a pixel is excluded by a mask there is no way for another mask to include it again.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn-and-parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "inclusive\n: Inclusive mode first inverts each individual mask (subtracts its value from 1.0), multiplies the inverted masks together, and finally inverts the combined mask once again.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn-and-parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": For a given pixel, the final mask will have a value of 1.0 if _any_ of the individual masks are 1.0 at that location and it will only have a value of 0 if _all_ masks have a value of 0 at that location.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn-and-parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": Any individual mask can _include_ a pixel by setting its value to 1.0, regardless of what the other masks do. Once a pixel is fully included by a mask (its value is 1.0) there is no way for another mask to exclude it again.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn-and-parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "exclusive and inclusive inverted modes\n: Using the above combination methods alone would still be rather limiting. We gain maximum flexibility by allowing an additional inversion step for each individual mask. This is governed by the polarity buttons that you find close to the individual channels.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn-and-parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": Toggling the polarity button of a mask inverts its values (subtracts the original value from 1.0).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn-and-parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": Finally within the “combine masks” combobox you may select the _exclusive & inverted_ or _inclusive & inverted_ options. Each of these options is equivalent to the _exclusive_ and _inclusive_ modes, respectively, but with a final step that inverts the resulting mask.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn-and-parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Typical use cases"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn-and-parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "inclusive mode\n: For this mode you set the “combine masks” combobox to inclusive mode and make sure that all polarity buttons of all the individual channels and of the drawn mask are set to negative (-). Your starting point is a mask where all pixels have a value of zero (no pixel is selected). You now adjust the parametric mask sliders to bring more and more pixels into the selection or you draw shapes on the canvas to select specific areas of your image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn-and-parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "exclusive mode\n: In the opposite case you set the “combine masks” combobox to exclusive mode and make sure that all polarity buttons are set to positive (+). Your starting point is a mask with all values at 1.0 (all pixels are selected). You now adjust the parametric mask sliders to exclude parts of your image as needed or directly draw shapes on the canvas to exclude those areas.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn-and-parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "For your convenience the parametric masks GUI provides a toggle button that inverts all channel polarities and toggles between inclusive and exclusive mode in the “combine masks” combobox.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn-and-parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "For novice users it is recommended that you stick to the above two use cases. This means that you should decide beforehand how you want to construct your mask.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/refinement-controls.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Mask contours"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/refinement-controls.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When a parametric or drawn mask is active, several additional sliders are shown which allow the mask to be further refined.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/refinement-controls.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Details threshold (_hidden by default_)\n: This control allows you to alter the opacity of the mask based on the amount of detail in the image. Use this slider to select either areas with lots of detail (positive values) or areas that are flat and lacking in detail (negative values). The default (zero) effectively bypasses details refinement. This is mostly useful to apply sharpening and blurring effects that ignore out-of-focus (bokeh) regions or to sharpen only blurry parts, preventing over-sharpening of in-focus regions.\n{{< note >}}\nThis slider is hidden in new edits and only reappears if an image's history already has a non-zero value for it (e.g. imported from an older edit). It is authored from the RGBA buffer right after demosaicing, so it works identically on RAW and non-RAW (JPEG, TIFF, etc.) images alike.\n{{< /note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/refinement-controls.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Blurring radius\n: Blurring the mask creates a softer transition between blended and unblended parts of an image and can be used to avoid artifacts. The blurring radius slider controls the radius of a gaussian blur applied to the final blend mask. The higher the radius, the stronger the blur (set to 0 for an unblurred mask). Mask blur is always applied after feathering if both kinds of mask adjustment are activated. This allows any resulting sharp edges or artifacts to be smoothed.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/refinement-controls.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Feathering guide\n: Mask feathering smooths a drawn or parametric mask such that the mask's edges automatically align with the edges of features in the image. The smoothing is guided either by the module's input or output (before blending), and may happen before or after the mask blurring, depending on what is selected in the “Feathering guide” combobox. Feathering is particularly sensitive to the choice of guide image when used with edge-modifying modules (modules for sharpening or blurring an image).\n: - _Output before blur_: feathering is guided using the _output_ image of the module and takes place _before_ the mask is blurred\n: - _Input before blur_: feathering is guided using the _input_ image of the module and takes place _before_ the mask is blurred\n: - _Output after blur_: feathering is guided using the _output_ image of the module and takes place _after_ the mask has been blurred\n: - _Input after blur_: feathering is guided using the _input_ image of the module and takes place _after_ the mask has been blurred\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/refinement-controls.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Feathering radius\n: Adjust the strength of the feathering effect. Feathering works best if the mask's edges already approximately match some edges in the guiding image. The larger the “Feathering radius” the better the feathering algorithm can align the mask to more distant edges. If this radius is too large, however, the feathered mask may overshoot (cover regions that the user wants to exclude). Feathering is disabled when the feathering radius is set to 0.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/refinement-controls.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Feathering mask opacity\n: The strength of the module's effect is determined by the mask's local opacity. Feathering and blurring the mask may reduce the opacity of the original mask. The “Feathering mask opacity” slider allows you to readjust the mask opacity to compensate. If the mask opacity is decreased (negative slider values) less opaque parts are affected more strongly. Conversely, if the mask opacity is increased (positive slider values) more opaque parts are affected more strongly. As a consequence, completely opaque portions of the mask always remain opaque and completely transparent portions always remain transparent. This is to ensure that regions that have been fully excluded from or included in a module's effect (by setting the mask's opacity to 0% or 100%) remain fully excluded or included.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/refinement-controls.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Feathering mask contrast\n: This slider increases or decreases the mask contrast. This allows you to adjust the transition between the opaque and transparent parts of the mask.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/refinement-controls.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Display mask (mask icon)\n: Click this icon to preview the current mask in the center view: the image shows through where the module is applied, and a checkerboard covers what the mask leaves out. Click it again to turn the preview off. See [previewing the mask](../_index.md#previewing-the-mask) for how to read it and where its colors are set.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/refinement-controls.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd class=\"mouse\">click</kbd> (<kbd>⌘</kbd>+<kbd class=\"mouse\">click</kbd> on macOS) always shows the mask specifically; <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd class=\"mouse\">click</kbd> shows the color channel currently selected by hovering a [parametric mask](./parametric.md) slider instead; holding both together shows both at once. The same icon also appears next to the module's name in its header, so you can check its mask without opening the masking tabs.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/refinement-controls.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Example: feathering a drawn mask"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/refinement-controls.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< figure src=\"feathering.jpg\" caption=\"feathering a rough drawn mask onto a subject, step by step\" />}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/refinement-controls.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "It can be rather tedious to create a drawn mask that precisely covers a particular feature in an image — say, to enhance the color contrast of a foreground subject without affecting the background. Feathering does most of the work:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/refinement-controls.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. Start from a rough, fuzzy drawn shape that only approximately follows the subject's outline.\n2. Raise the **Feathering radius** (e.g. to 50) so the mask snaps to the nearest strong edges, and add a small **Blurring radius** (e.g. 5) to smooth it.\n3. Adjust the **Feathering mask opacity** and **Feathering mask contrast** (e.g. to 0.3 and 0.5) to tighten the transition.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/refinement-controls.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The effect then stays restricted to the subject. Feathering works best when the subject is well separated from an out-of-focus background, because the distinct edge at its border guides the mask to match its shape.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Parametric masks"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A parametric mask builds the per-pixel opacity handed to [blending](../_index.md) from the pixels' own color and tonal values, so a module's effect lands on the tones or colors you single out rather than on an area you draw.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "You single them out one color channel at a time: on each channel you set a range of values to keep and a range to drop, which gives that channel an opacity of its own for every pixel. Those per-channel opacities are then multiplied together, along with the global _Opacity_ slider, into the finished parametric mask. A pixel the mask leaves at 0 reaches the next module exactly as this one received it; a pixel at 1 (100%) gets the module's full effect.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Parametric masks are handled from the _Parametric_ tab of the _Masking & Blending_ tool, in the darkroom [left panel](../../darkroom-view-layout.md#left-panel), for whichever module currently has focus. The tab works alongside the [_Drawn_](./drawn.md) and [_Raster_](./raster.md) ones, since any combination of masking methods can be active at once (see [combining masks](../_index.md#combining-masks)).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Blending has to be enabled for the module before any of it responds: the checkbox reading _Enable in_ followed by the module's name is what arms the tool, and the rest of the tab stays grayed out until you check it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\nA module whose color space provides no compatible channels cannot carry a parametric mask at all. The tab then says so in place of its controls, naming the module: _Parametric masking is disabled because the **&lt;module&gt;** module does not provide compatible color channels._\n{{< /note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The Parametric panel"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< figure src=\"mask/parametric/mask-parametric.en.png\" class=\"align-left borderless framed tight\" />}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< param-table >}}\n| **Disable**<div>Turns parametric masking back off when you want it out of the way; the channel settings stay attached and come back with it.</div> |\n| **Reset**<div>Returns every channel of every tab, input and output alike, to its default range, and the [_Details threshold_](./refinement-controls.md) with them. The _Boost factor_ of each channel is left as you set it.</div> |\n| **Channel tabs**<div>One tab per data channel the module's blending color space offers. Selecting one shows that channel's sliders — see [channel tabs](#channel-tabs).</div> |\n| {{< icon src=\"color-picker.jpg\" >}} **Color picker**<div>Reads values off the image and marks them on the sliders without changing the mask. See [color pickers](#color-pickers).</div> |\n| **Range picker**<div>Reads an area of the image and sets the current channel's range from it, fitting the _Boost factor_ as it goes. See [color pickers](#color-pickers).</div> |\n| {{< icon src=\"icon/icon-polarity.png\" >}} **Invert**<div>Turns a mask that selects into one that deselects, and back. See [invert](#invert).</div> |\n| **Output**<div>The trapezoidal opacity function read on the data the module produces, before blending. Hidden until you ask for it with **Show output channels** in the [blending options](../_index.md#blending-options) menu. See [channel sliders](#channel-sliders).</div> |\n| **Input**<div>The same function, read on the data the module receives. This is the one a parametric mask is normally built on. See [channel sliders](#channel-sliders).</div> |\n| **Log scale** / **Linear scale**<div>Respaces that one slider's travel without touching the image or the mask — see [linear and log scale](#linear-and-log-scale). Each slider carries its own.</div> |\n| {{< icon src=\"icon-eye.jpg\" >}} **Channel display**<div>Replaces the center image with the current channel's data, and keeps it there until you press the button again. Each slider carries its own, and the two are exclusive: switching on the output one switches off the input one. See [inspecting channels and masks](#inspecting-channels-and-masks).</div> |\n| **Boost factor**<div>Rescales the range of values the current channel's sliders span, in EV. See [boost factor](#boost-factor).</div> |\n{{< /param-table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "How a parametric mask is computed"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Each channel turns a pixel's value on that channel into an opacity between 0 and 1, through a trapezoidal function set by the slider's four markers:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- below the first marker, and above the fourth, the opacity is 0;\n- between the second and third, it is 1;\n- over the two stretches in between, it ramps proportionally from one to the other.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A pixel therefore carries one opacity per channel, and the channels are **multiplied together**: a pixel has to satisfy every channel you have constrained to survive. The global _Opacity_ slider multiplies in last. Adding a constraint can only ever narrow what the parametric mask keeps.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A channel whose two inner markers sit at the extremes spans the whole range of values, so it constrains nothing and is skipped altogether — which is the state every channel starts in. A tab whose channel has been moved away from its default shows its letter in **bold**, so you can see at a glance which channels a mask is actually built on without opening each tab.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Channel tabs"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Which channels you get depends on the color space the module blends in, set by the [blending options](../_index.md#blending-options) menu. Each tab is one _data channel_: a real one the pixel is stored in, or a virtual one derived from it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< param-table >}}\n| **Lab**<div>_L_ (lightness), _a_ (green/red), _b_ (blue/yellow), _C_ (chroma of LCh) and _h_ (hue of LCh).</div> |\n| **RGB (display)**<div>_g_ (gray), _R_, _G_, _B_, _H_ (hue of HSL), _S_ (saturation of HSL) and _L_ (lightness of HSL).</div> |\n| **RGB (scene)**<div>_g_ (gray), _R_, _G_, _B_, _Jz_ (lightness of J<sub>z</sub>C<sub>z</sub>h<sub>z</sub>), _Cz_ (chroma) and _hz_ (hue).</div> |\n{{< /param-table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The _g_ (gray) channel is the luminance of the pixel, a weighted average of R, G and B whose exact weightings come from the working color space in use.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "J<sub>z</sub>C<sub>z</sub>h<sub>z</sub> is the polar form of J<sub>z</sub>a<sub>z</sub>b<sub>z</sub>, as LCh is the polar form of Lab. Like the L of Lab, J<sub>z</sub> tracks the way we perceive brightness, but J<sub>z</sub>a<sub>z</sub>b<sub>z</sub> holds up far better on high dynamic range images and shifts hues much less than Lab does — which is why it is the scene-referred choice.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "_See [Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Color_space) for more about these color spaces._\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Boost factor"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Each slider lays its markers out over a fixed range of values. In scene-referred processing pixel values routinely run past that range — a specular highlight can sit at several times \"100%\" — and without a correction every one of those pixels would pile up against the end of the slider with no room to tell them apart.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "_Boost factor_ solves this by rescaling the range the slider spans, expressed in EV (stops). Raising it by 1 EV doubles the range: every value printed under the markers doubles, and highlights that sat past the end of the slider's range come back within reach, where the markers can be placed precisely between them. It is a scale problem it fixes, not a color one — the pixel data and the resulting mask are untouched, only how much of the value range the markers can address. The slider starts at 0 EV and only goes up, so a channel's range can be widened but never narrowed.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "It is shown on every tab but only responds where rescaling means something: it is grayed out on the hue channels, which already cover a full turn, and in the _RGB (display)_ color space on _S_ and _L_ as well, whose values are bounded by definition.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "You rarely need to set it by hand. Using the _Range picker_ to set a channel from an area of the image fits the _Boost factor_ to that area at the same time, raising or lowering it in quarter-stop steps until the area's brightest value just fits on the visible slider. Move the _Boost factor_ yourself afterwards and your value is kept instead, until you pick a different area.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Linear and log scale"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Each slider carries its own _Log scale_ button; press it and the label reads _Linear scale_, for the way back. The scale is remembered per channel and separately for the input and the output slider, and the heading of the slider it applies to gains a _(log)_ or _(zoom)_ suffix while it is on.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This only changes how values are laid out along the slider, for the sake of mouse precision — the image and the mask are unaffected. On a linear slider the shadows occupy a tiny fraction of the travel, which makes fine values there impractical to reach with a mouse; the logarithmic scale spreads that same range out and gives you access to them.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The _a_ and _b_ channels of Lab get a magnified scale instead of a logarithmic one, since a logarithm is not defined across the zero their values straddle. It stretches the middle of the range, where those two channels do most of their work, and compresses the extremes.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Inspecting channels and masks"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Seeing the values you are selecting on makes the sliders far easier to place. Three displays are available, all of them replacing the center image:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< param-table >}}\n| {{< icon src=\"icon-eye.jpg\" >}} **Channel display**<div>Press a slider's eye button to show that channel's data and keep it there. The input and the output button are exclusive — switching one on switches the other off — and the display follows you from tab to tab as you change channel. The data is rendered either in false colors or in gray scale, as set by [preferences > darkroom > Display of individual color channels](../../../../preferences-settings/darkroom.md).</div> |\n| **Hover**<div>For a look you don't need to press anything for, hold a key while the pointer is over a slider: <kbd>Shift</kbd> shows that channel's data, <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> (<kbd>⌘</kbd> on macOS) shows the resulting mask over the image, and both together show both at once. Release and the image comes back.</div> |\n| **Mask view** (<kbd>M</kbd>)<div>With the pointer over a slider, press <kbd>M</kbd> to toggle the mask view on and off. See [previewing the mask](../_index.md#previewing-the-mask) for how to read it.</div> |\n{{< /param-table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Channel sliders"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Each channel slider carries four markers, which set the trapezoidal opacity function described under [how a parametric mask is computed](#how-a-parametric-mask-is-computed). The two **filled** triangles mark the closed, usually narrower edge of the trapezoid, where the opacity reaches 1; the two **open** triangles mark its open, usually wider edge, where the opacity reaches 0. The stretch between an open marker and the filled one beside it is the transition, so moving them apart softens the selection and bringing them together sharpens it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The four markers keep their sequence whatever you do: they can touch, but they cannot pass one another.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< param-table >}}\n| <kbd class=\"mouse\">Click</kbd> and <kbd class=\"mouse\">drag</kbd><div>Grabs a marker and moves it to the pointer. Which of the two pairs you grab depends on the side of the slider you press on: the filled markers are picked up from the side they are drawn on, the open ones from theirs.</div> |\n| <kbd class=\"mouse\">Right-click</kbd><div>Pins the nearest marker, so that the wheel and the arrow keys act on it whatever the pointer does next. Right-click again to release it.</div> |\n| <kbd class=\"mouse\">Scroll</kbd>, <kbd>←</kbd> <kbd>→</kbd> <kbd>↑</kbd> <kbd>↓</kbd><div>Nudges the pinned marker, or the one nearest the pointer, by one step of the channel's own increment — the precise way to place one.</div> |\n| <kbd class=\"mouse\">Double-click</kbd><div>Returns all four markers to their defaults, and sets the channel's polarity to match the _Combine masks_ mode currently in use.</div> |\n{{< /param-table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Each marker prints the value it stands for, in the channel's own units and scaled by the [_Boost factor_](#boost-factor).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Polarity"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Each slider carries a polarity button {{< icon src=\"icon/icon-polarity.png\" >}} that switches the channel between selecting a range and deselecting one. The markers change glyph to confirm it: what was the filled pair becomes the open pair, and the trapezoid turns upside down.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< param-table image-height=\"9rem\" >}}\n| **Range select**<div>With the filled markers at the extremes, the whole range of values is selected and the mask is uniformly at 100%. Moving them inwards excludes more and more of the image, keeping only the range left between them; the open markers set how soft each edge of that range is.</div> | ![](blendif_2b.jpg \"range select opacity function\") |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| **Range deselect**<div>Toggling the polarity turns that around: the whole range starts out deselected, at an opacity of zero, and moving the markers inwards brings more and more of the image back in.</div> | ![](blendif_3b.jpg \"range deselect opacity function\") |\n{{< /param-table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Which of the two you want follows from the _Combine masks_ mode you are working in — see [combining drawn & parametric masks](./drawn-and-parametric.md), which pairs each mode with the polarity that suits it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Invert"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The _Invert_ button {{< icon src=\"icon/icon-polarity.png\" >}} flips the polarity of every channel at once, and it does not stop there: it also flips the polarity of the [drawn mask](./drawn.md) and switches _Combine masks_ between _Exclusive_ and _Inclusive_. It is therefore the one-press way to move a finished mask from the \"start from everything and exclude\" approach to the \"start from nothing and include\" one, and back, rather than a per-channel control — the polarity button on each slider is what inverts a single channel.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Color pickers"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Two pickers read values off the image, and they do quite different things with them. Neither carries a written label, so each is named here for what it does, with its tooltip in parentheses.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< param-table >}}\n| {{< icon src=\"color-picker.jpg\" >}} **Color picker** (_Pick GUI color from image_)<div>A plain probe. <kbd class=\"mouse\">Click</kbd> a point, or <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd class=\"mouse\">click</kbd> (<kbd>⌘</kbd>+<kbd class=\"mouse\">click</kbd> on macOS) or <kbd class=\"mouse\">right-click</kbd> and drag out an area. The values it finds are drawn as markers inside every channel slider, with the mean printed in parentheses, and nothing about the mask changes. Use it to find out where the tones you are after actually sit before you move anything.</div> |\n| **Range picker** (_Set the range based on an area from the image_)<div>Picks and applies in one gesture. Press it, then <kbd class=\"mouse\">drag</kbd> a rectangle on the image to set the current channel's input range from that area, or <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd class=\"mouse\">drag</kbd> (<kbd>⌘</kbd>+<kbd class=\"mouse\">drag</kbd> on macOS) to set its output range instead — the latter only being useful once the output slider is shown.</div> |\n{{< /param-table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The _Range picker_ does more than drop four markers. It sets the inner pair just inside the area's darkest and brightest values and the outer pair just outside them, leaving a small transition on each side; it fits the [_Boost factor_](#boost-factor) to the area; and it sets the channel's polarity so that the area you picked ends up selected rather than dropped, whichever _Combine masks_ mode you are in.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "On a hue channel it goes one step further. Hues wrap around, and a range straddling the point where the scale wraps cannot be expressed by two markers on a straight slider — so the picker takes the range from the opposite side of the hue circle and inverts the channel's polarity, which picks out the same hues in a form two markers can hold.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/parametric.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "1. **Decide which way you are building the mask before you start**, and set _Combine masks_ accordingly: _Exclusive_ with positive polarities to start from the whole image and carve pixels away, _Inclusive_ with negative ones to start from nothing and bring pixels in. Mixing the two halfway through a complex mask is what makes parametric masks feel unpredictable. The _Invert_ button switches the whole mask between the two if you change your mind.\n2. **Pick the channel that separates your subject from its surroundings most cleanly** — a hue channel for a colored object, _g_ or _L_/_J<sub>z</sub>_ for a tonal range, a chroma channel for something saturated against a neutral background. Switch the eye button on for a channel or two and look: the one where subject and background differ most is the one to work on.\n3. **Set the range with the _Range picker_** rather than by hand. Dragging a rectangle over a representative patch of the subject gets you the markers, the _Boost factor_ and the polarity in one gesture, and leaves you with adjustments rather than a blank slider.\n4. **Refine the markers with the mask in view**, holding <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> (<kbd>⌘</kbd> on macOS) over the slider or pressing <kbd>M</kbd>. Move the inner markers to change what is selected and the outer ones to change how abruptly the selection ends; scroll for the last small steps.\n5. **Add a second channel only if the first leaves something in you don't want.** Channels multiply, so each one you constrain narrows the result — two well-chosen channels usually beat five fiddled ones.\n6. **Soften the result in the _Contours_ tab.** A parametric mask follows values, not edges, and often comes out noisy at the boundaries; blurring and feathering it there settles it onto the details of the image. See [mask contours](./refinement-controls.md).\n7. **Restrict it by location if it fires elsewhere in the frame.** A [drawn mask](./drawn.md) on top confines the parametric selection to the part of the picture you meant it for, which is usually quicker than making the color selection itself more exact.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Drawn masks"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A drawn mask builds the per-pixel opacity handed to [blending](../_index.md) from shapes you draw directly on the image, so a module's effect lands exactly where you place them.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Shapes are stored internally as vectors, rendered at whatever resolution the pixelpipe needs, and expressed in the coordinate system of the original image. A shape therefore keeps covering the same part of the picture no matter what you change elsewhere in the edit -- with one visual side effect worth knowing about, see [shape distortions](#shape-distortions) below. Once drawn, a shape can be adjusted, removed, or reused by any other module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Drawn masks are handled from the _Drawn_ tab of the _Masking & Blending_ tool, in the darkroom [left panel](../../darkroom-view-layout.md#left-panel), for whichever module currently has focus. The tab works alongside the [_Parametric_](./parametric.md) and [_Raster_](./raster.md) ones, since any combination of masking methods can be active at once (see [combining masks](../_index.md#combining-masks)).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Blending has to be enabled for the module before any of it responds: the checkbox above the row of tabs, reading _Enable in_ followed by the module's name, is what arms the tool, and everything below it stays grayed out until you check it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The Drawn panel"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< figure src=\"mask/drawn/mask-drawn.en.png\" class=\"align-left borderless framed tight\" />}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< param-table >}}\n| **Disable**<div>Turns drawn masking back off when you want it out of the way; the shapes stay attached and come back with it.</div> |\n| **Mask name**<div>A text field naming this module's mask. It starts out showing `Group <module name>` as a placeholder; type your own name and press <kbd>Enter</kbd> to replace it, or empty the field to go back to that default. Meaningful names pay off as soon as several modules carry masks.</div> |\n| {{< icon src=\"icon/icon-polarity.png\" >}} **Polarity**<div>Reverses the polarity of the whole drawn mask. A circle, by default, restricts the module to the area inside it; flipping the polarity applies the module everywhere _except_ inside that circle.</div> |\n| {{< icon src=\"icon/icon-edit-shape.png\" >}} **Show and edit mask elements**<div>Displays the mask's shapes on the canvas so you can edit them. Selecting this module's group in the [shape manager](../../../toolboxes/shape-manager.md) switches it on too.<br>\n<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd class=\"mouse\">click</kbd> (<kbd>⌘</kbd>+<kbd class=\"mouse\">click</kbd> on macOS) enters _restricted_ edit mode instead, in which a shape's overall position and size are locked -- neither dragging nor scrolling over it moves or resizes it -- and only its individual nodes and segments respond. This is the safe way to fine-tune Polygon and Brush shapes.</div> |\n| **Shape list**<div>The shapes making up this module's mask, described in [the shape lists](#the-shape-lists) below.</div> |\n| **Attach shapes**<div>Switches the list above to every shape defined for the image, so you can attach or detach them. See [reusing a shape](#reusing-a-shape).</div> |\n| **Shape buttons** {{< icon src=\"icon/icon-shapes.png\" >}} <div>Five buttons at the bottom right create a new shape: Circle, Ellipse, Polygon, Brush and Gradient. See [creating a shape](#creating-a-shape).</div> |\n| **Mouse wheel**<div>A collapsible section holding the grid that decides which parameter of a shape each wheel combination edits. It is shared by every mask of every module -- see [mouse wheel](#mouse-wheel).</div> |\n| **Brush options**<div>The collapsible section below it, holding the two settings that govern painted strokes -- see [brush options](#brush-options).</div> |\n{{< /param-table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The shape lists"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The panel has two lists that share the same slot; the _Attach shapes_ button picks which one is showing.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< param-table image-height=\"7rem\" >}}\n| **Shape list** ![](mask/drawn/mask-drawn-shape-list.en.png?class=framed) | <div>The default view, holding the shapes this module's mask is built from, applied in list order from top to bottom.<br>\nEach row carries, from left to right:\n- the [set operator](./set-operators.md) combining that shape with the ones applied before it, with an icon if **Invert** is on,\n- the shape's name,\n- an _unlink_ icon that detaches the shape from this mask while keeping it available elsewhere,\n- and a _trash_ icon that deletes the shape outright.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Right-clicking a row opens the shape-level part of [the context menu](#the-context-menu) — its parameter sliders, the **Operation** submenu and **Move Up** / **Move Down** — which is handy for a shape that is hard to reach on the canvas, or hidden behind others.</div> |\n{{< /param-table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< param-table image-height=\"9rem\" >}}\n| **All-shapes list** ![](mask/drawn/mask-drawn-shape-list-all.en.png?class=framed) | <div>Shown while _Attach shapes_ is pressed, it lists every shape defined for the current image.<br>\nA checkbox on each row attaches it to this module's mask or detaches it, and its name can be edited in place by double-clicking.</div> |\n{{< /param-table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Mouse wheel"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< param-table image-height=\"11rem\" >}}\n| ![](mask/drawn/mask-drawn-wheel.en.png?class=framed) | Turning the wheel with the pointer over a shape edits one of its parameters, and which combination edits which parameter is your call. The _Mouse wheel_ collapsible section, at the bottom of the _Drawn_ tab beside _Brush options_, holds a grid with one row per wheel combination and one column per parameter it can reach; ticking a cell maps that row to that parameter. No combination carries a meaning of its own, so any of them can be pointed at any parameter, or at none.|\n{{< /param-table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "There is one column per parameter -- _Size_, _Fading / Curvature_, _Opacity_ and _Rotation_, all described under [shape parameters](#shape-parameters) -- and a last one, _Nothing_, which unmaps the row so that combination is ignored over a shape. The column named twice is a single cell to tick: it edits the fading of a shape that has one, and the curvature of a Gradient.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The grid is application-wide. Which parameter the wheel edits is a habit of yours rather than a property of one shape or of the module owning the mask, so a change made from one module's _Drawn_ tab applies to every mask of every module, and it is remembered between sessions.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A shape only answers for the parameters it actually has, so a combination mapped to _Rotation_ does nothing over a Circle, a Polygon or a Brush, just like a row set to _Nothing_.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\nBecause the mapping is yours to set, the listings below name the parameter each shape reads, not the keys that reach it: read _Size_ as \"whichever combination is mapped to _Size_\" -- <kbd class=\"mouse\">scroll</kbd> alone, unless you changed it.\n{{< /note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Brush options"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< param-table image-height=\"4.5rem\" >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| ![](mask/drawn/mask-drawn-brush-opt.en.png?class=framed) | The last collapsible section of the _Drawn_ tab holds two settings for painted strokes. Both are application-wide, as the wheel grid is: they apply to every Brush painted in any module, and are remembered between sessions. |\n{{< /param-table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< param-table >}}\n| **Brush strokes smoothing** (_low_, _medium_ or _high_)<div>How many nodes the stroke is converted into when you release the button: more smoothing means fewer nodes, which eases later editing at the expense of accuracy. _Medium_ by default.</div> |\n| **Pen pressure mapping**<div>Applies a graphics tablet's recorded pressure to the stroke's size, fading or opacity, one of the three at a time. _Relative_ scales the attribute between zero and the value the stroke would otherwise have, _absolute_ maps pressure straight onto the 0% to 100% range; size only offers the relative form. Off by default, the pressure reading being ignored.</div> |\n{{< /param-table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A mask is built from shapes, of five types, and any number of them can be combined into one.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The five shapes"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "They differ in how much of the outline you draw yourself, and the effort is worth spending only where the subject asks for it: the further down this list, the more precise the mask and the longer it takes to place.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< param-table >}}\n| **Circle**<div>A disc with a soft edge, placed in a single click and the quickest mask there is. It fits anything roughly round -- a face, a lamp, a bright patch of sky -- and its feathering makes the correction blend on its own.</div> |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| **Ellipse**<div>The same disc, stretched by the four nodes on its outline and turned to any angle. Reach for it as soon as the area is longer than it is wide, or round but seen at an angle.</div> |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| **Polygon**<div>A closed free-form outline through three or more nodes, joined by smooth curves by default: the mask covers the whole area it encloses. This is the shape for an area with a contour of its own to follow -- a building against the sky, a silhouette, a window -- and the one whose nodes let you follow it as closely as you like.</div> |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| **Brush**<div>An open stroke painted freehand, converted into connected nodes when you release the button; those nodes are then edited exactly like a Polygon's. Nothing is enclosed: the mask is the stroke itself, as wide as its _Size_ all along the path. It suits areas that are thin or irregular enough that a closed outline would be tedious to trace -- strands of hair, branches, a trail of light -- and it is the shape a graphics tablet pays off on, its pressure driving the stroke (see [brush options](#brush-options)).</div> |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| **Gradient**<div>A linear ramp running from a line you place out to the edge of the image: a single click places the 50% opacity line, and dashed lines mark where the opacity reaches 100% and 0%. Use it for a correction that has to fade across the frame rather than stop anywhere -- darkening a sky down to the horizon, balancing a light falling from one side. Each new gradient reuses the size, curvature and rotation of the last one you placed or edited.</div> |\n{{< /param-table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\nDepending on the module and the image, a gradient can provoke banding artifacts. Activating the [_dithering_](../../modules/dithering.md) module alleviates this.\n{{< /note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Anatomy of a shape"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Every editing gesture acts on whatever sits under the pointer, so the elements a shape is made of are worth naming apart. Which of them a shape has depends on its type: they all draw their lines, but a Circle has nothing else to grab, an Ellipse carries four nodes, and a Polygon and a Brush carry nodes, segments and handles alike.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "An element is only drawn once its parent is selected, so they appear in turn as you go: a displayed mask shows the shape lines of all its shapes, the selected shape -- the one you clicked adds its border line and its nodes, and the selected node adds its own handles. The hint at the right of the title bar names the element the pointer is over and what a drag there will do.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< param-table image-height=\"4rem\" gutter=\"0rem\" image-crop=\"square\" >}}\n| **Lines** | **Solid** (shape) ![](mask/drawn/mask-drawn-shape-anat-line.png) | <div>The solid line, the outline proper: on a Circle, an Ellipse and a Polygon it encloses the area the mask covers at full strength, and the feathering starts there. A Brush draws it down the middle of its stroke instead, and a Gradient draws it as the 50% opacity line you placed.</div> | \n| | **Dashed** (border)![](mask/drawn/mask-drawn-shape-anat-border.png) | <div>The dashed line running alongside the shape line, marking the far end of the feathering: the mask fades from full strength at the shape line to nothing here, and the distance between the two is what [_Fading_](#shape-parameters) sets. A Gradient has two of them, one where the opacity reaches 100% and one where it reaches 0%, spaced by its [_Size_](#shape-parameters). It is part of the shape rather than a handle, so dragging it moves the whole shape -- except on an Ellipse, where it is the ring that rotates the shape.</div> |\n| | **Dotted** (guide) ![](mask/drawn/mask-drawn-shape-anat-dotted.png) | <div>A guide rather than a part of the shape: it contributes nothing to the mask, has no opacity of its own, and there is nothing on it to grab or drag. Most are drawn only while a gesture is under way, to show where it is heading -- a Polygon draws the stretch running from the last node you placed to the pointer this way while you create it, a Gradient draws its axis right across the image while you turn it, and each goes as soon as the gesture ends..</div> |\n{{< /param-table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< param-table image-height=\"4rem\" image-crop=\"square\" >}}\n| **Arrow** ![](mask/drawn/mask-drawn-shape-anat-arrow.png) | <div>The tie between a clone or heal shape and its _source_ -- the patch of the image it copies its pixels from -- and so [_retouch_](../../modules/retouch.md) shapes only. It runs from the source to the shape and its head points at the shape, which is what tells you which way the pixels travel. It lights up while the source is under the pointer, where <kbd class=\"mouse\">drag</kbd> moves the sampled area without moving the shape itself. Where the two overlap there is no room for the shaft, so only the head is drawn, halfway between them. Being a guide, it is dotted and adds nothing to the mask.</div> |\n{{< /param-table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "What follows applies mainly to **Polygon** and **Brush** shapes:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< param-table image-height=\"4rem\" image-crop=\"square\" >}}\n| **Nodes** ![](mask/drawn/mask-drawn-shape-anat-nodes.png) | <div>A point the outline runs through, drawn round where the outline curves through it and square where it turns a corner -- a _cusp_. <kbd class=\"mouse\">click</kbd> it to select it, which is what reveals its handles. <kbd class=\"mouse\">Drag</kbd> one to move it.</div> | \n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| **Segments** ![](mask/drawn/mask-drawn-shape-anat-segments.png) | <div>The stretch of outline between two consecutive nodes. Its shape is not its own: each of the two nodes it joins lends it one curvature handle, and so it leaves a round node on a curve and a cusp node in a straight line. Between two round nodes it curves along its whole length, between two cusps it runs dead straight, and between one of each it does both -- curved at the round end, straightening into the cusp.<br>\n<kbd class=\"mouse\">Drag</kbd> a segment to move both of those nodes together, which slides it without changing its curve, and <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd class=\"mouse\">click</kbd> (<kbd>⌘</kbd>+<kbd class=\"mouse\">click</kbd> on macOS) to insert a node into it. </div> |\n{{< /param-table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< param-table image-height=\"4rem\" gutter=\"0rem\" image-crop=\"square\" >}}\n| **Handle** | **Curvature** ![](mask/drawn/mask-drawn-shape-anat-handle-curve.png) | <div>A small round handle joined to its node by a line, setting the direction and the strength of the curve running through that node, and so bending the two segments that meet there at once. There is one per node, not one per segment: both sides are derived from it symmetrically, which is what keeps the outline smooth across the node. It belongs to the nodes of a Polygon and of a Brush, shows on the selected node only, and a cusp node has none until it is switched back to a round one.<br>\n<kbd class=\"mouse\">Drag</kbd> it to bend the outline; **Reset round node** in [the context menu](#over-a-node) discards it and restores the automatically computed curve.</div> |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| | **Border** ![](mask/drawn/mask-drawn-shape-handle-border.png) | <div>A small square handle sitting on the border line, on the axis running outwards from its node; dragging it slides along that axis and sets the border of that one node instead of the whole shape's -- how wide the feathering is there on a Polygon, how wide the stroke is there on a Brush. Like the curvature handle it belongs to the nodes of a Polygon and of a Brush, and shows on the selected node only.</div> |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< /param-table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Click one of the five shape buttons to enter creation mode for that shape. The mouse cursor turns into a cross over the image, confirming that the next click will place a shape rather than interact with the picture.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\nCreation mode then stays active: as soon as one shape is finished, another of the same type begins, so you can place several in a row without going back to the button.\n{{< /note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Two shapes depart from that cursor. The Brush hides the cursor entirely and draws its own filled circle instead, showing the current brush size and fading at the pointer. The Polygon switches to a pointing hand as you come back within reach of your first node, telling you that clicking there will close the outline.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "To leave creation mode, click the shape's button again, press <kbd>Escape</kbd>, or right-click the canvas and choose **Done shape creation**. Any of these drops you into edit mode with the shapes you have placed.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The [wheel](#mouse-wheel) adjustments made on a shape being created also update the _defaults_ for that shape type, which the next shape you create will start from. The gestures each shape answers to while you place it are listed with its editing ones under [gestures by shape](#gestures-by-shape).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\nScrolling up increases the value being adjusted. [Preferences > Invert the direction of the mouse vertical scroll](../../../../preferences-settings/darkroom.md) reverses this -- a general scroll-direction setting that affects every slider in Ansel, not just masks.\n{{< /note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Editing a shape"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Selecting and moving"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The {{< icon src=\"icon/icon-edit-shape.png\" alt=\"Show and edit mask elements\" >}} button displays the mask's shapes on the canvas.<br>\n<br>\n<kbd class=\"mouse\">Click</kbd> on a shape on the canvas to select it -- it is highlighted in the [shape list](#the-shape-lists) at the same time. Selection works both ways: click an entry in the list and the view centers on the matching shape.\nYou can then <kbd class=\"mouse\">Click</kbd> on any of [the elements](#anatomy-of-a-shape) the shape can contain.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "<kbd class=\"mouse\">Drag</kbd> a shape to move it as a whole, or one of its elements -- segment, node or handle -- to reshape it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Shape parameters"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Beyond its outline, a shape carries a handful of numeric parameters, and most of the editing comes down to setting them.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< param-table>}}\n| **Size**<div>How large the shape is, grown or shrunk about its own center without changing its outline. On a Gradient it is the extent of the transition instead, the distance between the two dashed lines.</div> |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| **Fading**<div>How abrupt the edge of the shape is: the width of the feathering, the distance between the shape line and its dashed border, over which the mask fades from full strength down to nothing.</div> |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| **Curvature** (_gradient only_)<div>Bends the gradient line into a curve, from -100% to 100%, the line running straight at zero.</div> |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| **Rotation** (_ellipse and gradient only)_<div>The orientation of the shape, turned in precise increments. An Ellipse and a Gradient only -- a Circle is round, and a Polygon and a Brush are turned by moving their nodes.</div> |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| **Opacity**<div>How strongly this one shape contributes to the mask, from 0% to 100%. It scales what the shape paints before that is combined with the shapes above it, so a shape at 50% gives the module half its effect where it covers the image.</div> |\n{{< /param-table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The wheel edits whichever parameter the combination you turn it with is mapped to in [the mouse wheel](#mouse-wheel) grid. It acts in creation as well as in edit mode, wherever the pointer sits over the shape.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "To set a parameter precisely instead, <kbd class=\"mouse\">right-click</kbd> on the shape and use the sliders at the top of [the context menu](#the-context-menu) -- the same ones the [shape manager](../../../toolboxes/shape-manager.md) offers. They all read an absolute value, save _Size_ on a shape other than a Gradient: that one opens at zero and scales the shape by however far you move it, in either direction, rather than reading a size.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< param-table image-height=\"5rem\" image-crop=\"square\" >}}\n| ![](mask/drawn/mask-drawn-shape-node-fading.png) | On a shape built from nodes -- a Polygon or a Brush -- _Size_ and _Fading_ act on the selected node alone when one is selected, and on every node otherwise, which is how one part of an outline is given a wider feathering than the rest. |\n{{< /param-table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Size, fading and rotation describe the shape itself, so they follow it into every mask it is [attached to](#reusing-a-shape). Opacity does not: like the [set operator](./set-operators.md) and **Invert**, it belongs to the row the shape occupies in one mask, and the same shape can therefore carry a different opacity in each module using it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Invert and set operators"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Invert** flips the shape over, the mask then covering everything but what it encloses, and the **set operator** -- _Union_, _Intersection_, _Difference_ or _Exclusion_ -- decides what the shape adds to, or takes from, the mask built by the shapes applied before it. Both are set from the **Operation** submenu of [the context menu](#over-the-shape-itself), whether you open it on the canvas or on the shape's row in [the shape list](#the-shape-lists), and each operator is shown on an example in [set operators](./set-operators.md).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Because an operator works on what sits above, order is part of the result: **Move Up** and **Move Down** in the same menu change the outcome, not just the display.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\nThe shape at the top of the list has nothing to combine with, so the four operators are greyed out for it -- **Invert**, which needs no second operand, stays available.\n{{< /note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Gestures by shape"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Each shape answers to gestures of its own, some of them while you are still placing it. A Circle has none: it is dragged to move it and set through its parameters, and that is all.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< param-table indent=\"0\" >}}\n| **Ellipse** | <div>\n- <kbd class=\"mouse\">Drag</kbd> a node: eccentricity\n- <kbd class=\"mouse\">Drag</kbd> the border ring: rotation, with no modifier needed\n- <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd class=\"mouse\">click</kbd> inside the shape: switches the feathering between equidistant and proportional decay\n</div> |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| **Polygon** | While creating:\n<div>\n- <kbd class=\"mouse\">Click</kbd>: adds a node\n- <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd class=\"mouse\">click</kbd> (<kbd>⌘</kbd>+<kbd class=\"mouse\">click</kbd> on macOS): adds a sharp corner instead\n- <kbd>Backspace</kbd>: removes the node you just placed\n- <kbd class=\"mouse\">Click</kbd> near the first node, or <kbd>Enter</kbd>: closes the shape\n</div> | |\n| | While editing:\n<div>\n- <kbd class=\"mouse\">Drag</kbd> a node or a segment: reshapes the outline\n- <kbd class=\"mouse\">Click</kbd> a node: reveals its curvature handle, which can then be dragged\n- <kbd class=\"mouse\">Drag</kbd> a node's [border handle](#anatomy-of-a-shape): adjusts the feathering at that node only\n</div> | |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| **Brush** | <div>\n- <kbd class=\"mouse\">Left-click</kbd> and <kbd class=\"mouse\">drag</kbd>: paints the stroke\n- Once released, its nodes are edited exactly like a Polygon's\n</div> |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "| **Gradient** | <div>\n- <kbd class=\"mouse\">Double-click</kbd>: resets the curvature\n- <kbd class=\"mouse\">Drag</kbd> the pivot at the center of the line: rotation\n- <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd class=\"mouse\">click</kbd>, while still in creation mode: switches the opacity transition between a _linear_ ramp and a _sigmoidal_ (S-curve) one, which concentrates the transition closer to the center line. The choice sticks for the gradients you create afterwards\n</div> |\n{{< /param-table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Removing a shape"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "There are two distinct outcomes, and the menu keeps them apart:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- **Remove shape from mask** detaches the shape from this module's mask. The shape survives, still listed for the image and still usable by other modules — this is what the _unlink_ icon in the Shape list does too.\n- **Delete shape** removes it altogether, everywhere it was used. The _trash_ icon in the lists does the same.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "With the pointer over one of a Polygon's or Brush's nodes, the menu offers **Delete node** instead, which removes that single node rather than the shape.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Reusing a shape"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "A shape is a single object shared by every mask that uses it: edit it in one module and every other module using it follows.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Press **Attach shapes** to swap the panel's list for every shape defined on the image, then tick a shape's checkbox to add it to the current module's mask, or untick it to detach it. Right-clicking a row here offers only **Duplicate** and **Rename**.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< figure src=\"mask/drawn/mask-drawn-shape-list-all2.en.png\" style=\"width:19rem\" class=\"align-left borderless tight framed\" />}}\nA row reading `Already in '<group>'` is greyed out because the shape is reached through a group that is itself attached, so it is already spoken for.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The [shape manager](../../../toolboxes/shape-manager.md) covers the same shapes from a separate window, which lists every mask the image carries beside them: a shape can be grouped, reordered and renamed there, and handed to several modules at once.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< param-table image-height=\"13rem\" >}}\n| ![](mask/drawn/mask-drawn-contextmenu.en.png?class=framed) | Right-clicking on the canvas opens a menu headed by the shape's name. What follows depends on one thing: what sits under the pointer. There are four cases.<br>\n<br>\nThe same menu is reachable without the canvas: right-clicking a row in the panel's [Shape list](#the-shape-lists) opens its shape-level part, described under [over the shape itself](#over-the-shape-itself) below, minus the removal entries — the row's own _unlink_ and _trash_ icons cover those.|\n{{< /param-table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "While creating a shape"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< param-table >}}\n| **Close path** (or <kbd>Enter</kbd>)<div>Closes the outline, once at least three nodes are placed. Polygon only.</div> |\n| **Remove last point** (or <kbd>Backspace</kbd>)<div>Deletes the node you just placed. Polygon only.</div> |\n| **Done shape creation** (or <kbd>Escape</kbd>)<div>Leaves creation mode.</div> |\n{{< /param-table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Over the shape itself"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The menu opens with sliders for the shape's parameters, all described under [shape parameters](#shape-parameters): **Size** and **Opacity** on every shape, **Fading** on all but a Gradient, which carries a **Curvature** instead, and **Rotation** on an Ellipse and on a Gradient. They set the same values [the mouse wheel](#mouse-wheel) reaches directly on the canvas; the menu is simply the precise way in.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< param-table >}}\n| **Operation** | **Invert**<div>Flips this shape over within the mask: everything it does not enclose is masked instead.</div> |\n| | **Union**, **Intersection**, **Difference**, **Exclusion**<div>How this shape combines with the ones applied before it in the mask. See [set operators](./set-operators.md).</div> |\n{{< /param-table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< param-table >}}\n| **Move up** / **Move down**<div>Reorders the shape within the mask.</div> |\n| **Remove shape from mask** (or <kbd>Delete</kbd>)<div>Detaches it, leaving it available to other modules.</div> |\n| **Delete shape**<div>Deletes it for good.</div> |\n{{< /param-table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Over a node"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Polygon and Brush only. The parameter sliders appear as above, followed by the entries below. The **Operation** submenu and the reordering entries are not offered here, and **Delete node** takes the place of **Remove shape from mask** and **Delete shape** — so a slip of the mouse deletes a node, never the whole shape.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< param-table >}}\n| **Switch to round node** / **cusp node** (or <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd class=\"mouse\">click</kbd>) (<kbd>⌘</kbd>+<kbd class=\"mouse\">click</kbd> on macOS)<div>Turns a corner into a smooth curve, or the reverse.</div> |\n| **Reset round node**<div>Discards curvature handles you dragged by hand and restores the automatically computed curve.</div> |\n| **Delete node** (or <kbd>Delete</kbd>)<div>Removes that node only, leaving the rest of the shape.</div> |\n{{< /param-table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Over a segment"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Polygon and Brush only. This adds one entry to the shape-level ones (**Operation**, **Remove shape from mask** and **Delete shape**); the parameter sliders and the reordering entries are not shown.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< param-table >}}\n| **Add a node here** (or <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd class=\"mouse\">click</kbd>) (<kbd>⌘</kbd>+<kbd class=\"mouse\">click</kbd> on macOS)<div>Inserts a node at that point of the outline.</div> |\n{{< /param-table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Shape distortions"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< param-table image-height=\"7rem\" image-crop=\"square\" >}}\n| ![](mask/drawn/mask-drawn-distortion.png) | Because a shape is stored in the coordinate system of the original image, it travels up the pixelpipe before the module uses it and draws it on screen. Any distorting module active in between -- [_lens correction_](../../modules/lens-correction.md) or [_rotate and perspective_](../../modules/rotate-perspective.md), for instance -- therefore distorts the shape too, on screen and in the exported image alike: circles can render as ellipses, and gradient lines can end up curved.<br>\n_A Circle near the edge of the frame, deformed into an ellipse under the effect of the lens correction module._ |\n{{< /param-table >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "To draw a shape that follows the subject accurately despite this, prefer a Polygon over a Circle or an Ellipse, since its extra nodes let you compensate for the distortion. For a Gradient, bending its line counteracts the simpler distortion that lens correction introduces.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Panning and zooming"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Mouse actions over a shape, or over its nodes and handles, apply to that shape. Away from it, dragging and scrolling pan and zoom the center view as usual, so there is no need to leave creation or edit mode to reposition the canvas.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/masks/drawn.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "On a zoomed-in image, dragging a shape towards the edge of the viewport pans the view in that direction on its own, and keeps panning while you hold the pointer there, so a shape can be moved past the visible area without interrupting the drag. The pan accelerates the closer to the edge you get. It also works while placing a new shape, but not while painting a Brush stroke, where dragging is the drawing action itself.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "Blend modes"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Blend modes define how the input and output of a module are combined (blended) together before the module's final output is passed to the next module in the pixelpipe.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Classic blending modes, designed for display-referred RGB (constrained to 0-100%), implicitly define a fulcrum at 50% (gray) or 100% (white) in their algorithms, depending on the blend mode. Because scene-referred is not subject to these restrictions, this fulcrum needs to be explicitly defined by the user when performing blending operations in the \"RGB (scene)\" color space. The additional _Blend fulcrum_ parameter will be presented to the user when using one of these blend modes in this color space. The effect depends on the operator used. For example, values above the fulcrum might be brightened and values below darkened, or vice versa.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The final output of a module is computed 'per-pixel' as follows:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (math)
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "final_output = (1.0 - opacity) * module_input + opacity * blended_output\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "where the `blended_output` is a combination of the input and output images, depending on the blend mode (below), and the `opacity` is defined 'per-pixel' by a combination of the mask and global opacity parameter. An opacity of 0% outputs an image that is identical to the input image of the module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The _Compositing_ combobox, right below the blend-mode selector, chooses which image is treated as the base layer. Its default, _Output over input_, matches the formula above. Switching it to _Input over output_ effectively reverses the roles of the input and output images in the 'per-pixel' computation:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (math)
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "final_output = (1.0 - opacity) * module_output + opacity * blended_input\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "where the `blended_input` is a combination of the output and input images, depending on the blend mode below where _output_ and _input_ image references are reversed. With _Input over output_ compositing, an opacity of 0% outputs an image that is identical to the _output_ image of the module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Normal modes"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Normal\n: The most commonly used blend mode, \"Normal\" simply mixes input and output to an extent determined by the opacity parameter. This mode is commonly used to reduce the strength of a module's effect by reducing the opacity. This is also usually the blend mode of choice when applying a module's effect selectively with masks. This mode is also known as the \"over\" Porter-Duff alpha blending operator (see [alpha compositing](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alpha_compositing) for more details).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Normal bounded\n: _not available in the \"RGB (scene)\" color space_\n: This blend mode is the same as “Normal”, except that the input and output data are clamped to a particular min/max value range. Out-of-range values are effectively blocked and are not passed to subsequent modules. Sometimes this helps to prevent artifacts. However, in most cases (e.g. highly color-saturated extreme highlights) it is better to let unbound values travel through the pixelpipe to be properly handled later. The “Normal” blend mode is therefore usually preferred.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Arithmetic modes"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Addition\n: Add together the pixel values of the input and output images, lightening the output. When blending in the \"RGB (scene)\" color space, the pixel values of the output image are multiplied by a value proportional to the \"Blend fulcrum\".\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Subtract\n: Subtract the pixel value of the _output_ from the _input_. When blending in the \"RGB (scene)\" color space, the pixel values of the output image are multiplied by a value proportional to the \"Blend fulcrum\". Pixel values less than 0 are set to 0.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Multiply\n: Multiply the pixel values of the input and output together. When blending in display-referred color spaces, pixel values are between 0 and 1.0, the final output will be clamped and will always be darker. When blending in the \"RGB (scene)\" color space, this value is further multiplied by a value proportional to the \"Blend fulcrum\". In this case, values may be greater than 1.0 and therefore brighten the base image. This may have other side-effects, such as updating the white point in the filmic module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": Multiply blending simulates an optical variable density filter, where the density is defined by the output of the module. It has many applications, from blooming and local contrast enhancements (when used with a blur or low-pass filter) to dodging/burning and global contrast enhancements (when used with exposure). The fulcrum sets the output intensity threshold between darkening and brightening (any RGB value below fulcrum will darken).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Divide\n: Divide the pixel values of the input by the output. When blending in the \"RGB (scene)\" color space, the pixel values of the output image are multiplied by a value proportional to the \"Blend fulcrum\".\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": Since this is the inverse of the Multiply mode, it will darken where Multiply brightens and vice versa. Everything else works in essentially the same way.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Screen\n: _not available in the \"RGB (scene)\" color space_\n: Invert the input and output pixel values, multiply those values together and invert the result. This yields approximately the opposite effect to \"Multiply\" mode -- the resulting image is usually brighter, and sometimes “washed out” in appearance.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Average\n: Return the arithmetic mean of the input and output pixel values.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Difference\n: Return the absolute difference between the input and output pixel values.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Geometric mean\n: Return the square root of the product of the input and output pixel values.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Harmonic mean\n: Return the product of the input and output pixel values, multiplied by 2 and divided by their sum.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Contrast enhancing modes"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The following modes are not available in the \"RGB (scene)\" blending color space as they rely on an assumption of \"50% mid gray\" which only applies to display-referred and non-linear color spaces.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Overlay\n: This mode combines the \"Multiply\" and \"Screen\" blend modes: The parts of the input where the output is brighter, become brighter; The parts of the image where the output is darker, become darker; Mid-gray is unaffected.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Softlight\n: This mode is similar to \"Overlay\", except the results are softer and less bright.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Hardlight\n: This mode is not related to \"Softlight\" in anything but name. Like Overlay mode it is a combination of \"Multiply\" and \"Screen\" modes and has a different effect above and below mid-gray. The results with Hardlight blend mode tend to be quite intense and usually need to be combined with a reduced opacity.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Vividlight\n: This mode is an extreme version of Overlay/Softlight. Values darker than mid-gray are darkened; Values brighter than mid-gray are brightened. You will probably need to tone down its effect by reducing the opacity\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Linearlight\n: This mode is similar to the effect of \"Vividlight\".\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Pinlight\n: This mode performs a darken and lighten blending simultaneously, removing mid-tones. It can result in artifacts such as patches and blotches.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Color channel modes"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Lab channels"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The following are available for blending in the Lab color space only\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Lab lightness\n: Mix the lightness from the input and output images, while taking the color channels (a and b) unaltered from the input image. In contrast to “Lightness” this blend mode does not involve any color space conversion and does not clamp any data. In some cases this blend mode is less prone to artifacts than “Lightness”.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Lab a-channel\n: Mix the Lab \"a\" color channel from the input and output images, while taking the other channels unaltered from the input image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Lab b-channel\n: Mix the Lab \"b\" color channel from the input and output images, while taking the other channels unaltered from the input image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Lab color\n: Mix the Lab color channels (a and b) from the input and output images, while taking the lightness unaltered from the input image. In contrast to “Color” this blend mode does not involve any color space conversion and does not clamp any data. In some cases this blend mode is less prone to artifacts than “Color”.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "RGB channels"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The following are available when blending in RGB color spaces only.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "RGB red channel\n: Mix the \"red\" channel from the input and output images, while taking the other channels unaltered from the input image. When blending in the \"RGB (scene)\" color space, the \"red\" channel from the output image is multiplied by a value proportional to the \"Blend fulcrum\".\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "RGB green channel\n: Mix the \"green\" channel from the input and output images, while taking the other channels unaltered from the input image. When blending in the \"RGB (scene)\" color space, the \"green\" channel from the output image is multiplied by a value proportional to the \"Blend fulcrum\".\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "RGB blue channel\n: Mix the \"blue\" channel from the input and output images, while taking the other channels unaltered from the input image. When blending in the \"RGB (scene)\" color space, the \"blue\" channel from the output image is multiplied by a value proportional to the \"Blend fulcrum\".\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "HSV channels"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The following are available when blending in the \"RGB (display)\" color space only.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "HSV value\n: Mix the lightness from the input and output images, while taking color unaltered from the input image. In contrast to “Lightness” this blend mode does not involve clamping.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "HSV color\n: Mix the color from the input and output images, while taking lightness unaltered from the input image. In contrast to “Color” this blend mode does not involve clamping.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Others"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Lightness\n: Mix lightness from the input and output images, while taking color (chromaticity and hue) unaltered from the input image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Chromaticity\n: Mix chromaticity from the input and output images, while taking lightness and hue unaltered from the input image. This blend mode uses RGB ratios, divided by a Euclidean norm.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Lighten\n: _not available in the \"RGB (scene)\" color space_\n: Compare the pixel values of the input and output images, and output the lighter value.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Darken\n: _not available in the \"RGB (scene)\" color space_\n: Compare the pixel values of the input and output images, and output the darker value.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Hue\n: _not available in the \"RGB (scene)\" color space_\n: Mix hue (color tint) from the input and output images, while taking lightness and chroma unaltered from the input image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Color\n: _not available in the \"RGB (scene)\" color space_\n: Mix color (chroma and hue) from the input and output images while taking lightness unaltered from the input image.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ": _Caution: When modules drastically modify hue (e.g. when generating complementary colors) this blend mode can result in strong color noise._\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Coloradjustment\n: _not available in the \"RGB (scene)\" color space_\n: Some modules act predominantly on the tonal values of an image but also perform some color saturation adjustments. This blend mode takes the lightness from the module's output and mixes colors from input and output, enabling control over the module's color adjustments.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< note >}}\nThe \"RAW\" blending color space (used by a small number of modules that act before demosaicing) offers the same blend-mode list as Lab and \"RGB (display)\".\n{{< /note >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/blend-modes.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "{{< warning >}}\nThree entries appear suffixed \"(deprecated)\" in the blend-mode selector: _Difference (deprecated)_, _Subtract inverse (deprecated)_ and _Divide inverse (deprecated)_. They are kept so an edit created with an older version keeps rendering identically, and are only ever offered when that edit's history already uses one of them -- don't pick them for new edits. Their current equivalents: for _Difference (deprecated)_, use _Difference_ (a corrected version of the same math); for _Subtract inverse (deprecated)_ and _Divide inverse (deprecated)_, use _Subtract_ or _Divide_ with the _Compositing_ combobox set to _Input over output_, which reverses the roles of input and output the same way the old \"inverse\" variants did.\n{{< /warning >}}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "title: Transitioning from darktable to Ansel\ndate: 2023-01-18\nlastmod: 2026-06-17\nweight: 10\ndraft: false\nlatex: true\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "This page was first written in January 2023, right after the fork, and listed what had already diverged from darktable 3.8/4.0. A lot has happened on both sides since. The sections below summarize the major changes; the gory technical details live in the [news](https://ansel.photos/en/news/) section of the website.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "What changed in Ansel since 2023"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Performance and reliability"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The pixel pipeline has been [completely overhauled](https://ansel.photos/en/news/complete-pipeline-overhaul/): modules run about 1.8× faster on CPU and 1.6× faster on GPU than darktable, and — more importantly — the pipeline cache was rewritten so that disabling a module, using a color picker or re-exporting an already-exported image does not recompute the whole thing. That makes Ansel 4 to 100× faster than darktable to refresh the image. Memory management was rewritten too: Ansel knows exactly how much RAM it uses and stays within the budget you set, instead of darktable's hand-wavy \"performance profiles\" that have no predictability.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Lighttable, thumbnails and selection"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The whole [lighttable, thumbnail and mipmap-cache stack was rewritten](https://ansel.photos/en/news/redesigning-lighttable-and-mipmap-cache/). Concretely: starting the app and drawing a full collection is roughly 3.5× faster, switching between lighttable and darkroom is near-instant, and scrolling a large collection no longer stutters. The right sidebar was removed (its content moved to the global menu and the left panel), culling became the _Restrict to selection_ filter, the filtering toolbar was rebuilt as plain inclusive toggle buttons, the zoomable lighttable came back (Columns + Zoom with barycentric auto-pan), and selection became explicit and _What You See Is What You Get_ — no more metadata loss from a dangling mouse cursor.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "GUI controls"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The home-made Bauhaus sliders and comboboxes were [largely rewritten](https://ansel.photos/en/news/undarktable-ing-gui-controls/): clicking the combobox chevron now works (imagine that), values are dispatched to the pipeline only when you stop dragging instead of on every intermediate step, and widgets redraw immediately for instant feedback even on slow machines.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Color tools"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The color-grading tools were [reworked so their color models are actually correct](https://ansel.photos/en/news/color-controls-finally-correct/), rather than relying on broken assumptions papered over with magic numbers.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Masking"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Drawn, parametric and raster masks are now independent, combinable toggles instead of a single exclusive mode. In particular, a raster mask can be combined _on top_ of a drawn and/or parametric mask — see [combining masks](views/darkroom/masking-and-blending/_index.md#combining-masks).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Global menu and keyboard"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The global menu and the from-scratch rewrite of the keyboard shortcuts handler both shipped since this page was written; they are described in the relevant sections below.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "What darktable added that Ansel did not follow"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "darktable kept piling on features through versions 4.2 to 5.4. Ansel deliberately did not adopt most of them, either because they duplicate existing tools or because they don't fit the design:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "- **Sigmoid** and **AgX** tone mappers — Ansel keeps a single, well-understood tone mapper (filmic). Bolting on two more display transforms that do the same job differently is exactly the kind of redundant clutter the fork exists to avoid.\n- **Enlarge canvas** and **Overlay** compositing modules — extending the canvas is handled by the _framing_ module, and compositing several images is a job for a compositor, not a RAW developer.\n- **Camera-specific \"JPEG-matching\" styles** for 500+ camera models — chasing the out-of-camera JPEG per body is the opposite of a camera-agnostic, scene-referred workflow.\n- **Multiple workspaces** with separate databases — one library, kept fast, is enough.\n- **The startup splash screen** — a loading screen is a confession that startup is slow, not a feature. Ansel made startup fast instead.\n- **The swap-sidebars preference, drag-and-drop utility-module headers, and the right-click \"which modules to show\" menu** — Ansel removed the whole problem by moving button-only toolboxes into the global menu, so there is nothing left to rearrange.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Ansel does have its own _color equalizer_ and _color primaries_ modules, so those are not in this list.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "What was removed from mainstream darktable"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Features"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "[The tethering view](https://docs.darktable.org/usermanual/3.8/en/tethering/)\n: It relies on the lib GPhoto2 which is super brittle and buggy. The tethering view is merely a GUI over Gphoto2, much like [Entangle](https://entangle-photo.org/). The problem is GPhoto2 is known to break something different at each new release, so it's simply not worth the trouble to maintain a GUI over a Schrödinger cat. Use Entangle if you need to.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The easter egg video game\n: Not funny if you happen to be with a client on April 1st and your working app opens on a silly game that can't even be closed with <kbd>Ctrl+Q</kbd>. (Yes, it happened to me).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The histogram overlay control for [exposure and black level](https://docs.darktable.org/usermanual/3.8/en/module-reference/utility-modules/shared/scopes/#exposure-adjustment)\n: It's too easy to inadvertently mess up exposure when cycling through scopes (especially with a Wacom tablet) and it adds unnecessary GUI motion events and redraws. In cases of multiple instances of exposure module, it is automatically linked to the controls of the last instance, which is generally wrong because it is typically masked.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The new filtering option\n: A beautiful shit made of 6300 lines of bloat and still counting, that needs a screen-reader to understand WTF those icons mean, supposed to fix a problem nobody had with something that adds bugs and trouble. That thing is the poster case of everything that's wrong in Geektable 4.0. It should be called a regression. The same feature is achieved in Ansel with about 580 lines of code. And to say that the new filtering started as a refactoring project… Never let the darktable idiots refactor anything, it's too dangerous.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The [stars rating](https://docs.darktable.org/usermanual/3.8/en/lighttable/digital-asset-management/star-color/#star-ratings) toolbar in lighttable\n: It's redundant with overlays and requires to travel too far with the cursor to set it. Use overlays with cursor or key shortcuts to set stars.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The [timeline](https://docs.darktable.org/usermanual/3.8/en/module-reference/utility-modules/lighttable/timeline/)\n: terrible code and a huge loss of CPU cycles for a niche feature that essentially serves no purpose. This was responsible for way too many SQL requests and removing it is responsible for most of the GUI responsiveness improvement.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The main window borders with collapsing arrows / silly cycling through headers\n: The borders marked with arrows allow to collapse or expand the GUI panels. But they waste space, are typically too narrow for many displays, and tend to reappear when you don't want them because their state is defined per view. Collapsing panels is done with <kbd>Ctrl + Shift + L(eft)</kbd>, <kbd>Ctrl + Shift + R(ight)</kbd>, <kbd>Ctrl + Shift + T(op)</kbd> for the header, and <kbd>Ctrl + Shift + F(ilmstrip)</kbd> for the bottom panel. <kbd>Shift + F11</kbd> toggles all panels at once, <kbd>F11</kbd> toggles fullscreen. And the global menu (you know, like in any app since 1990) now exists and takes care of all that from its _Display_ menu, along with everything else darktable buried inside cryptic icon-buttons.\n: The silly cycling through all the possible combinations of headers and bottoms that happened when hitting these shortcuts in darktable has been completly removed: each shortcut toggles exactly one panel and nothing else.\n: Removing the borders allowed to increase the width of the sidebars scrolling sliders for better interaction.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The [second darkroom window](https://docs.darktable.org/usermanual/3.8/en/darkroom/darkroom-view-layout/)\n: darktable is slow enough with 2 pipelines (thumbnail & preview), a third one is not realistic. This should have been the main preview redirected to another window but the code has been hastily hacked by copy-pasting, it's terrible. Also there is no traceability on which display profile will be used, good luck with that.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The block and timeout overlay mode in lighttable\n: terrible code for another niche feature that nobody tests until bug appear on the issue tracker that will be answered with \"it works on my computer\".\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The extended overlays mode for thumbnails\n: each of the EXIF metadata display triggered its own SQL request per-thumbnail, because no cache was used, because the guy who coded that doesn't learn or doesn't care, but such a loss of CPU cycles is inacceptable.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The non-dynamic culling mode and the zoomable lighttable view\n: Both were ripped out in 2023 because they were a pile of spaghetti code duplicating what the lighttable already did, in a worse way. Both came back later, done right: culling is now simply the _Restrict to selection_ filter button (no separate view, no static/dynamic nonsense nobody understood), and the zoomable lighttable returned as a proper _Columns_ + _Zoom_ pair, with barycentric auto-panning so faces roughly line up across a series. Turns out the features were fine; darktable's implementation was the problem.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The dark and icon themes\n: dark themes trigger all sorts of unwanted visual illusions that make color assessment impossible. Their use is discouraged everywhere on darktable's doc but they are still there.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The ability to assign keyboard shortcuts to Gtk notebook tabs, as well as switching tabs by mouse scroll.\n: Just because you can doesn't mean you should, and capturing mouse scroll on inner tabs messes up with sidebar scrolling (you know, the regular scolling over content that doesn't fit in the viewport).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The VIM-style shortcuts\n: I bet you never knew, but if you start typing `:` in darktable, followed by a command, you can trigger stuff. This feature has never documented and the available features are really limited, but that still listens to all keystrokes to check if it should capture them. In Ansel, you can trigger commands by typing <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>P</kbd>, and the whole UI is mapped to it, so it's basically a shortcuts search engine that doubles with a command palette.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The histogram profile\n: That thing is coded by copy-pasting the display code, instead of writing reusable functions. It wouldn't be that bad if it wasn't completely misleading and actually broke the over-exposure alert (because, yes, it's used there too). Because the histogram is captured at the far end of the pipeline, in display color space, it might be already clipped to display gamut. Say you use Rec2020 space as the histogram profile, because it's the working space, what you see is RGB clipped to display space then rescaled to Rec2020, which means your clipping peak will not be at the far end of the graph, where you expect it. The result is highly misleading and voids the whole principle of a scope. That's just a whole pile of crap coded by people who have no idea of what a graphic pipeline is and hack small bits here and there because the concept of \"modules\" fooled them. They might be modular, but they still apply in sequence in the pipeline, so better check your inputs.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Warning messages in modules\n: I'm responsible for that stupidity. The warning messages were supposed to tell users they are doing things not really intended by the designer (for example, using 2 different steps of white balance correction) and to ask them to double-check if that's really what they wanted. That backfired badly because users don't get the difference between an error and an alert, so even when instructed to use 2 white balances (for example, a custom-corrected raw WB), they would get afraid by the \"error\" and annoy me with questions. Given that this feature is clearly not helping anybody, and did not trigger the expected behaviour (that is, RTFM), there is no point in keeping it. The option to hide them is removed as well.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Favourite presets button\n: There was this favourite presets button at the left of the bottom panel in darkroom, and I found nobody who ever used it. In fact, most people who I asked did not ever know it even existed. So it's gone.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Importing from camera\n: darktable has a way to mount the camera file system (SD or CF memory cards) through USB, using the library GPhoto2. But the desktop environment is also be able to mount the camera as a local hard drive using PTP and/or MTP protocol, possibly also through Gphoto2. In that case, darktable and the OS may compete for access to the USB media, so you need to unmount it first from the OS to be able to open it from darktable. On Windows, you even need to install custom USB drivers to make it work with GPhoto2, but these drivers will prevent the camera to work with the OS until you completly remove and reset them. So this feature is completely removed, and Ansel loads memory cards as USB media through the OS mounts. To enable this, you may have to install Linux packages such as `libmtp`, `linuxptp`, `libptp`, `libgphoto2` and possibly `gphoto2`. For your desktop environnment, you may install `kio-mtp` and `kio-extras` on KDE or `gvfs-mtp` and `gvfs-gphoto2` on Gnome. In any case, you are advised to simply use an SD of CF card reader, it's cheap and prevents most issues that can arise from udev and cameras connected through USB.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Overall, 5 to 6 SQL requests per thumbnail per \"mouse over\" event are now spared, due to the features removed (which still lets 3 requests…, aka 2 more than what should be).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "* The option to display a mask button in module's header is removed and the button is always shown,\n* The option to display the scroll bars in lighttable and darkroom is removed, scrollbars will always appear in lighttable and will never appear in darkroom,\n* The option to favour speed at the expense of quality is removed (process downscaled thumbnails), quality is favoured. This will produce previews consistent with the main preview and will prevent much confusion among users, at the expense of speed.\n* The option to expand modules on \"enable\" event and collapse on \"disable\" event is removed, collapsed state is independent from enabled state (I was the author of that stupidity and even I grew weary of this).\n* The option to scroll to the expanded module is removed, expanded modules will always trigger a scroll to be fully visible in viewport.\n* The option to open only one module at a time is removed. Modules will never be auto-collapsed when a new module is expanded. You may use <kbd>Shift + Click</kbd> on a module header to open this one while collapsing all the others.\n* The option allowing to immediately rename a new instance of a module has been removed and users will always be prompted to rename a new instance right after adding it. This simply encourages sane workflows because everybody adding unnamed instances has always hated themselves shortly after. Be nice to your future self people (no, you will not remember).\n* The option to disable the silly behaviour removing the star rating if you hit 1 star twice has been removed because WTF ? To remove the star rating, hit 0 and be done with it. 1 means 1.\n* The option allowing to compute a downscaled preview pipeline in darkroom is removed, and preview pipelines will always be computed at their correct size. This is a can of worms since neighbourhood filters (local contrast, contrast equalizer, diffuse and sharpen, blur, etc.) are accurate only when zoomed-in at 100%. If you zoom-out at screen size, as to fit the image within the viewport, you already introduce a visual inconsistency that produces misunderstanding among users and lots of false-positive bugs reports. But if you even downscale that, you are really pushing your luck and looking for trouble. You don't pay for that software, at least buy decent hardware to run it properly.\n* The option to use single click to open images in darkroom from thumbnail has been removed. That's just not how file managers work and it creates too many opportunities to accidentally open images.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Modules deprecated"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "All the remaining Lab modules that I have warned against for years are deprecated, namely:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "* Bloom, *(replaced by diffuse or sharpen, preset blooming)*\n* Raw chromatic aberrations, *(replaced by RGB chromatic aberrations)*\n* Contrast, lightness, saturation, *(replaced by filmic and color balance)*\n* Colorize, *(replaced by color balance)*\n* Color contrast, *(replaced by color balance)*\n* Color correction, *(replaced by color balance)*\n* Color mapping, *(replaced by color mapping features in exposure and color calibration)*\n* Color reconstruction, *(replaced by filmic color reconstruction, \"high quality reconstruction\" method)*\n* High-pass, *(replaced by diffuse or sharpen, various deblurring presets)*\n* Levels, *(replaced by filmic, white and black relative exposures settings)*\n* Low light, *(replaced by color balance)*\n* Low-pass, *(replaced by blurs)*\n* Monochrome, *(replaced by color calibration, \"grey\" tab, and various film-emulation presets)*\n* Raw denoise, *(replaced by denoise profiled)*\n* Shadows and highlights, *(replaced by tone equalizer and local contrast)*\n* Sharpen, *(replaced by diffuse or sharpen, various deblurring presets)*\n* Soften, *(replaced by diffuse or sharpen, blooming preset)*\n* Split-toning, *(replaced by color balance)*\n* Tone curve, *(replaced by tone equalizer, filmic and color balance)*\n* Velvia. *(replaced by color balance)*\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The display-referred RGB modules have been deprecated too:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "* Base curve, *(replaced by filmic)*\n* RGB levels, *(replaced by filmic, white and black relative exposures settings)*\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The scene-referred and display-referred processing workflows have been removed too, the scene-referred workflow is the only one and default. The chromatic adaptation workflows are removed too, the modern workflow will always be used.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "One of the most common questions, since the big scene-referred change, was \"what modules should I avoid\". Now, you don't need to ask. Despite what some people say, \"deprecated\" means the modules can't be found in UI for new edits, but they are still very much in the program and ready to serve for old edits that use them. Deprecating is just GUI cleaning.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The *output color profile* module has been removed from the darkroom GUI. It is **still** applied in the pipeline, so only the GUI widget is hidden, but since the output profile is set either from the display options (for the thumbnails and previews) or in the export module (for file exports), the profile defined in the darkroom module was overwritten anyway, and the duplicated setting confused some users. In the export module, the former \"same as image\" profile option (that used the options set in the output color profile module) has been replaced by \"same as original\", which will reuse the embedded color profile if any or fall back to sRGB otherwise (noticeably for raw files). All in all, this only changes **where** the output profile is set in the GUI and de-duplicate this setting, it does not affect the pipeline.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "What was changed from mainstream darktable"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Behaviours"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Mouse scroll\n: The mouse scroll will scroll the content of the container (sidebars) and will not be captured by sliders, unless sliders got clicked over first (or used through keyboard shortcuts). This restores the intuitive and common behaviour of a mouse scroll and protects from unwanted setting changes. See [#11](https://github.com/aurelienpierreeng/ansel/issues/11). The preference to decide how mouse scroll should be treated is consequently removed.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "\"Mouse over\" event\n: In lighttable, the \"mouse over\" event now does not select images for writing and possibly harmful operation (writing metadata, copying history stack, deleting/moving files, applying styles, rating, labelling, tagging etc.). These \"mouse over\" events trigger only safe read-only events  in the metadata display module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Modules listening to \"mouse over\"\n: Similarly, the modules like tagging, geotagging, etc. that reacted to the \"mouse over\" event (by updating their content) now only react to hard selection (click or key stroke). This saves many SQL requests to read metadata when moving the mouse in lighttable and improves responsiveness.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Copy all\n: The \"copy all\" history stack now copies and pastes white balance too. Meaning it does what it says. That used to be the case, but another hasty hack got that changed for madness 2 years ago.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "CSS animations\n: Animations are basically videos that need to be computed at 30 FPS while Gtk is only single-threaded on CPU, that's just a stupid loss of CPU cycles.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Filmstrip thumbnails\n: The thumbnails interaction have been synchronized with the lighttable :\n    * Opening a new image will need double-click\n    * Copy/pasting editing histories in filmstrip view over arbitrary selections will need a interaction with a knobby interface (mouse over event will be disregarded),\n    * The weird selection modes relying on key modifiers are removed. Selection is done with regular single click, as in lighttable.\n    * Rating/rejecting on overlays in filmstrip is prevented because the controls are too small to prevent misclicking accidents.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Keyboard shortcuts\n: The great MIDI input thingy of 2021 broke basic functionality and user expectations : the numeric pad events did not listen to the state of `numlock` and were always recorded as `Home`, `End`, `PageDown` etc. Also, on AZERTY and BÉPO keyboards, the regular number keys are accessed through `Shift`. This broke the picture ratings because numbers from the numeric pad were not recorded as numbers, and the regular numbers did nothing. When reconfiguring the rating shortcuts with regular numbers, you would then see things like `Shift + &` on AZERTY or `Shift + \"` on BÉPO, instead of the expected `1`.\n: Then, to get the numpad numbers to work, you had to manually add them as alternative shortcuts. That's a backstep of more than 25 years regarding UI, all because the MIDI developer thought it was clever to disregard OS key mapping and handle it internally. This has been fixed in a way that gets the OS-decoded key (including modifiers like `Shift` and `numlock` when needed), and then remaps all numpad key strokes to regular key strokes, so the `Enter`, `*`, `/`, `Delete` and all the numeric keys will be treated equally no matter where they are input, without having to duplicate shortcuts for the numpad. It is crazy that this should be announced as a new feature in 2023 and is another proof that darktable development went south thanks to die-hard geeks.\n: Triple-click has also been removed from shortcuts because promoting repetitive stress injury by design is totally stupid.\n: Since then, the whole shortcuts handler — the infamous \"Great MIDI turducken\" of 2021 — has been [rewritten from scratch](getting-started/keyboard.md), in a fifth of the code and a sixth of the complexity. MIDI and gamepad support are gone (2% of users, for a tertiary feature), and in their place the interface is fully navigable from the keyboard without memorizing a single shortcut, with a global action search (<kbd>Ctrl + P</kbd>) that finds and triggers any command by name. The full story is in the [news](https://ansel.photos/en/news/).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The less-used views like _Map_, _Print_ and _Slideshow_ are hidden by default, but can be re-enabled from the _Preferences_ -> _Other views_. This prevents GUI bloat since the `.so` objects of the views are simply not loaded at startup (they are not hidden from the GUI, they completely don't exist). This has the drawback of deleting user-defined shortcuts if any, for these views. But the issue is Gtk initializes and redraws all GUI widgets, whether they are visible or not, so even hidden and unused views will drain your CPU.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Users who want to preserve their shortcuts for the 3 removed views may edit the `~./config/ansel/anselrc` file by adding the following lines __before__ starting the software :\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Fenced code block (ini)
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, no-wrap
msgid "/views/map/enable=TRUE\n/views/print/enable=TRUE\n/views/slideshow/enable=TRUE\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Widgets"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Capitals\n: Modules names, buttons and section labels have their initial letter capitalized programmatically as grammar and typography rules suggests for better visual cues and for consistency with the rest of the world. This does not break translations but may trigger weird behaviours with accented characters. This is a work in progress and not all places have capitals yet. It uses sentence capitalization because the code to do so is 4 lines, while using title capitalization would require tokenization and is far less robust to achieve across a variety of languages.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Export\n: The \"export\" module is moved to the left sidebar in lighttable, for consistency with import, collect, etc.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "DAM modules removed from darkroom\n: The metadata, tags and export modules are removed from darkroom, since they are DAM features. Exporting an image cannot be done from the darkroom to prevent running 3 concurrent pipelines, which can produce crashes and loss of data if the available RAM is exceeded (the RAM allocation code was still broken back then, especially on GPU; it has since been rewritten from scratch, so Ansel now knows exactly how much memory it uses and never exceeds the user-defined budget). This also frees some space for the image processing widgets in darkroom.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Modules reorganized\n: Darktable's \"modules\" lumped two different things behind the same look: image-processing filters (which live in the pipeline) and arbitrary toolboxes (which are just panels of buttons). The toolboxes have been renamed to plain functional names and sorted by where they belong.\n: In the lighttable, a single **left** sidebar holds the file and metadata tools — \"Library\" (the old \"collect\"/\"collections\"), the metadata editor, tagging, \"Datetime and GPS\" (geotagging) and image information. The whole **right** sidebar of the lighttable has been removed: once the toolboxes-that-are-really-menus moved out, there was nothing worth keeping there, and the freed space went to the thumbnails.\n: In the darkroom, the **right** sidebar is reserved for the image-processing modules, and the **left** sidebar holds the editing helpers (scopes, snapshots, history of changes, duplicates, shape manager…).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Toolboxes moved to the global menu\n: The \"modules\" that took no user input and only displayed buttons — selection helpers, image actions, history copy/paste, styles management, the maintenance scripts — have been moved into the global menu, where hierarchical lists of buttons have belonged since graphic designers invented drop-down menues in the 1980's. Another thing darktable failed at, for years, while shipping grids of cryptic icons instead.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Exposure module\n: The exposure boost has been changed from +0.5 to +0.7 EV,\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Filmic\n: In filmic module, the contrast is changed to 1, latitude is set to 0.01% (which practically disables it — doesn't matter for v6), and the interpolation spline is set to back to \"hard\" (fourth order polynomial with $C^2$ connectivity condition).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Color balance RGB\n: This module is now enabled by default with the \"standard colorfulness\" preset, as to give a base rendition closer to the infamous out-of-camera JPEG.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Lens correction\n: This module is now enabled by default because I couldn't think of a photographer __not__ wanting to correct lenses.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: content/from-darktable.md
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Dithering\n: This module is now enabled by default because it does no harm but protects JPG and other low-bit-depth exports from posterization.\n"
msgstr ""
